y. These symptoms could indicate low blood sugar or another issue that needs medical attention."},{"id":"s9","title":"Every step counts","body":"You do not need to walk a specific number of steps or hit a target on a fitness tracker. Even a short, slow walk after eating is doing meaningful work inside your body. You are helping your muscles absorb glucose, reducing the workload on your pancreas, and protecting your blood vessels, kidneys, and eyes from the damage that high blood sugar causes over time. This is one of the most powerful tools you have, and it costs nothing but a few minutes of your day."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'light-exercise'),
  (E'simple-compound-exercises-that-improve-insulin-sensitivity', E'Simple compound exercises that improve insulin sensitivity', E'DIABETES', '{"DIABETES"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Your muscles are the largest consumers of glucose in your body. When you strengthen them, you are essentially building bigger storage tanks for blood sugar.', E'Why muscle matters for blood sugar\n\nYour muscles are the largest consumers of glucose in your body. When you strengthen them, you are essentially building bigger storage tanks for blood sugar. Larger, more active muscles pull more glucose from your bloodstream even when you are resting — this is what doctors call improved insulin sensitivity. The best part is that you do not need a gym, weights, or any equipment. Three simple exercises done in your sitting room can make a real difference to your blood sugar numbers within weeks.\n\nThe three exercises you need\n\nThe routine is straightforward: squats, wall push-ups, and standing calf raises. These are compound exercises, meaning each one works several large muscle groups at once. Together, they target your thighs, glutes, chest, arms, shoulders, and calves — covering most of the major muscles in your body. Each session takes about 10 to 15 minutes.\n\nHow to do squats correctly\n\nStand with your feet shoulder-width apart, toes pointing slightly outward. If you need support, stand in front of a sturdy chair. Slowly bend your knees and lower your body as if you are about to sit down. Go as low as is comfortable — even a quarter of the way down counts when you are starting out. Keep your back straight and your weight in your heels. Push through your heels to stand back up. That is one repetition. Start with 8 repetitions if 10 feels too difficult. Rest for 30 seconds, then do a second set. Over time, work toward three sets of 10 to 12 repetitions.\n\nHow to do wall push-ups correctly\n\nStand facing a wall, about an arm''s length away. Place your palms flat against the wall at shoulder height and shoulder width apart. Keeping your body in a straight line from head to heels, slowly bend your elbows and lean toward the wall until your nose almost touches it. Push back to the starting position. That is one repetition. Start with 8 to 10 repetitions and two sets. As you get stronger over the weeks, you can move your feet further from the wall to increase the difficulty. When wall push-ups become easy, you can progress to doing them against a kitchen counter, which is lower and more challenging.\n\nHow to do standing calf raises\n\nStand behind a chair and hold the backrest lightly for balance. Rise up onto your toes as high as you can, hold for two seconds at the top, then slowly lower back down. That is one repetition. Do 15 repetitions, rest for 20 seconds, and repeat for a second set. Your calf muscles act as a second heart — pumping blood back up from your legs and improving circulation, which is especially important for people with diabetes.\n\nWhen to do the routine\n\nThe best times are before breakfast and before dinner. Doing the exercises before a meal means your muscles are primed to absorb glucose from the food you are about to eat, which reduces the post-meal blood sugar spike. Set a simple reminder on your phone or tie the routine to something you already do — for example, do your squats and push-ups right after you brush your teeth in the morning and right before you start cooking dinner in the evening.\n\nWeek by week progression\n\nWeeks one and two: do two sets of each exercise, twice a day. Focus on learning the correct form rather than rushing. Weeks three and four: increase to three sets of each exercise. You may also start to increase repetitions — try 12 squats, 12 wall push-ups, and 20 calf raises. Weeks five and beyond: your muscles will feel stronger and the routine will feel easier. This is when you can add variations like holding a filled water bottle during squats for extra resistance, or doing counter push-ups instead of wall push-ups.\n\nSafety precautions\n\nAlways warm up with two minutes of marching in place before starting. If you feel sharp pain in your joints — especially your knees — stop and try a smaller range of motion. Mild muscle soreness the next day is normal and means the muscles are adapting. However, sharp pain, dizziness, or chest tightness means stop immediately and rest. If you are on insulin or sulfonylurea medications, check your blood sugar before exercising. If it is below 5.5 mmol/L, have a small snack first to prevent a drop during the routine.\n\nWhat results to expect\n\nWithin the first two weeks, most people notice they feel less tired and sleep better at night. By week four, your post-meal blood sugar readings may start dropping by 1 to 2 mmol/L. Over two to three months, your fasting blood sugar and even your HbA1c may improve. The exercises cost you nothing — no gym fees, no special clothes, no equipment — just 10 to 15 minutes of your day, twice.\n\nYou are building long-term protection\n\nEvery squat, push-up, and calf raise is an investment in your future health. Stronger muscles mean better blood sugar control, healthier bones, improved balance, and more energy for the things you love — playing with your grandchildren, running your business, or walking to church on Sunday. This simple routine is one of the most cost-effective health interventions that exists.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Why muscle matters for blood sugar","body":"Your muscles are the largest consumers of glucose in your body. When you strengthen them, you are essentially building bigger storage tanks for blood sugar. Larger, more active muscles pull more glucose from your bloodstream even when you are resting — this is what doctors call improved insulin sensitivity. The best part is that you do not need a gym, weights, or any equipment. Three simple exercises done in your sitting room can make a real difference to your blood sugar numbers within weeks."},{"id":"s2","title":"The thre