second set. Your calf muscles act as a second heart — pumping blood back up from your legs and improving circulation, which is especially important for people with diabetes."},{"id":"s6","title":"When to do the routine","body":"The best times are before breakfast and before dinner. Doing the exercises before a meal means your muscles are primed to absorb glucose from the food you are about to eat, which reduces the post-meal blood sugar spike. Set a simple reminder on your phone or tie the routine to something you already do — for example, do your squats and push-ups right after you brush your teeth in the morning and right before you start cooking dinner in the evening."},{"id":"s7","title":"Week by week progression","body":"Weeks one and two: do two sets of each exercise, twice a day. Focus on learning the correct form rather than rushing. Weeks three and four: increase to three sets of each exercise. You may also start to increase repetitions — try 12 squats, 12 wall push-ups, and 20 calf raises. Weeks five and beyond: your muscles will feel stronger and the routine will feel easier. This is when you can add variations like holding a filled water bottle during squats for extra resistance, or doing counter push-ups instead of wall push-ups."},{"id":"s8","title":"Safety precautions","body":"Always warm up with two minutes of marching in place before starting. If you feel sharp pain in your joints — especially your knees — stop and try a smaller range of motion. Mild muscle soreness the next day is normal and means the muscles are adapting. However, sharp pain, dizziness, or chest tightness means stop immediately and rest. If you are on insulin or sulfonylurea medications, check your blood sugar before exercising. If it is below 5.5 mmol/L, have a small snack first to prevent a drop during the routine."},{"id":"s9","title":"What results to expect","body":"Within the first two weeks, most people notice they feel less tired and sleep better at night. By week four, your post-meal blood sugar readings may start dropping by 1 to 2 mmol/L. Over two to three months, your fasting blood sugar and even your HbA1c may improve. The exercises cost you nothing — no gym fees, no special clothes, no equipment — just 10 to 15 minutes of your day, twice."},{"id":"s10","title":"You are building long-term protection","body":"Every squat, push-up, and calf raise is an investment in your future health. Stronger muscles mean better blood sugar control, healthier bones, improved balance, and more energy for the things you love — playing with your grandchildren, running your business, or walking to church on Sunday. This simple routine is one of the most cost-effective health interventions that exists."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'yoga-pose'),
  (E'myth-diabetes-can-be-cured-with-herbal-remedies', E'Myth: Diabetes can be cured with herbal remedies', E'DIABETES', '{"DIABETES"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'Almost every person living with diabetes in Kenya has been told by someone — a neighbour, a colleague, a relative, a WhatsApp group — that there is a herbal cure for diabetes.', E'The myth that will not go away\n\nAlmost every person living with diabetes in Kenya has been told by someone — a neighbour, a colleague, a relative, a WhatsApp group — that there is a herbal cure for diabetes. The claims vary: bitter gourd juice taken every morning, cinnamon in hot water, aloe vera gel, neem leaves, or a special mixture sold by a traditional healer. Some of these products are marketed aggressively, with testimonials from people who say they threw away their medication and were healed. The promise is tempting. The reality is dangerous.\n\nWhat science actually says about herbal remedies and blood sugar\n\nSome plants and spices do have compounds that can mildly affect blood sugar. Bitter gourd (karela), for example, contains substances that mimic insulin in laboratory studies. Cinnamon has been shown to improve insulin sensitivity by small amounts in some research trials. Aloe vera, fenugreek, and moringa have also shown modest effects in controlled studies. But here is what the sellers do not tell you: these effects are small — typically reducing blood sugar by 0.5 to 1 mmol/L at best. Compare that to metformin, a standard diabetes medication, which reduces blood sugar by 2 to 4 mmol/L. Herbal remedies cannot do the heavy lifting that prescribed medication does.\n\nWhy stopping medication is so dangerous\n\nWhen someone with type 2 diabetes stops their medication to rely on herbal remedies, their blood sugar begins to rise — sometimes within days. The problem is that high blood sugar often does not cause obvious symptoms right away. You might feel fine for weeks or even months while your glucose levels are silently damaging your body. Persistently high blood sugar harms the tiny blood vessels in your eyes, leading to diabetic retinopathy which can cause blindness. It damages the filters in your kidneys, potentially leading to kidney failure. It injures the nerves in your feet, causing numbness that can lead to infections and, in severe cases, amputation. These consequences are not rare or extreme — they are what happens when diabetes goes uncontrolled.\n\nThe financial trap\n\nMany herbal diabetes cures sold in Kenya are not cheap. Some traditional healers charge KES 2,000 to KES 10,000 for courses of treatment. Packaged herbal supplements in shops can cost KES 500 to KES 3,000 per month. Meanwhile, metformin — the most commonly prescribed diabetes medication — costs between KES 200 and KES 500 per month at most Kenyan pharmacies, and is often available for free or at subsidized rates through public health facilities under NHIF or the new SHA programme. The herbal remedy that promises to replace your medication may actually cost you more money while delivering less benefit.\n\nWhy these myths spread so easily\n\nDiabetes is a lifelong condition, and hearing that there is no cure is hard to accept. When someone on WhatsApp or social media shares a story about being healed through herbs, it offers hope. We also have a strong cultural tradition of herbal medicine in Kenya, and many people trust traditional remedies more than pharmaceutical drugs. Additionally, some people experience real improvements in their blood sugar when they start taking herbal supplements — but this is usually because they have also started eating better, exercising, and paying more attention to their health at the same time. They credit the herbs when the lifestyle changes are doing the actual work.\n\nWhat to do if you want to try herbal supplements\n\nYou are not wrong for being curious about herbal options — that curiosity shows you are actively engaged in managing your health. The important rule is this: never stop or reduce your prescribed medication without your doctor''s knowledge. If you want to try a herbal supplement alongside your medication, tell your doctor at your next visit. Some herbal products can interact with diabetes drugs and cause your blood sugar to drop too low, which is a medical emergency. Your doctor can check for these interactions and monitor your blood sugar to see if the supplement is actually making any difference.\n\nHow to spot a fake diabetes cure\n\nBe sceptical of any product that claims to cure diabetes completely. Be wary of testimonials that are not backed by medical records. Question any seller who tells you to stop your prescribed medication. Look out for products with no clear ingredient list or dosage instructions. A genuine health product will never ask you to abandon proven medical treatment.\n\nThe real path to better health\n\nManaging diabetes effectively requires a combination of medication, dietary changes, regular physical activity, and consistent monitoring. These are not glamorous solutions, and they require daily effort. But they are the strategies that are proven to reduce complications, improve energy levels, and extend life. Your doctor, your clinic, and your own daily choices are your best medicine — not a promise in a bottle.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The myth that will not go away","body":"Almost every person living with diabetes in Kenya has been told by someone — a neighbour, a colleague, a relative, a WhatsApp group — that there is a herbal cure for diabetes. The claims vary: bitter gourd juice taken every morning, cinnamon in hot water, aloe vera gel, neem leaves, or a special mixture sold by a traditional healer. Some of these products are marketed aggressively, with testimonials from people who say they threw away their medication and were healed. The promise is tempting. The reality is dangerous."},{"id":"s2","title":"What science actually says about herbal remedies and blood sugar","body":"Some plants and spices do have compounds that can mildly affect blood sugar. Bitter gourd (karela), for example, contains substances that mimic insulin in laboratory studies. Cinnamon has been shown to improve insulin sensitivity by small amounts in some research trials. Aloe vera, fenugreek, and moringa have also shown modest effects in controlled studies. But here is what the sellers do not tell you: these effects are small — typically reducing blood sugar by 0.5 to 1 mmol/L at best. Compare that to metformin, a standard diabetes medication, which reduces blood sugar by 2 to 4 mmol/L. Herbal remedies cannot do the heavy lifting that prescribed medication does."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why stopping medication is so dangerous","body":"When someone with type 2 diabetes stops their medication to rely on herbal remedies, their blood sugar begins to rise — sometimes within days. The problem is that high blood sugar often does not cause obvious symptoms right away. You might feel fine for weeks or even months while your glucose levels are silently damaging your body. Persistently high blood sugar harms the tiny blood vessels in your eyes, leading to diabetic retinopathy which can cause blindness. It damages the filters in your kidneys, potentially leading to kidney failure. It injures the nerves in your feet, causing numbness that can lead to infections and, in severe cases, amputation. These consequences are not rare or extreme — they are what happens when diabetes goes uncontrolled."},{"id":"s4","title":"The financial trap","body":"Many herbal diabetes cures sold in Kenya are not cheap. Some traditional healers charge KES 2,000 to KES 10,000 for courses of treatment. Packaged herbal supplements in shops can cost KES 500 to KES 3,000 per month. Meanwhile, metformin — the most commonly prescribed diabetes medication — costs between KES 200 and KES 500 per month at most Kenyan pharmacies, and is often available for free or at subsidized rates through public health facilities under NHIF or the new SHA programme. The herbal remedy that promises to replace your medication may actually cost you more money while delivering less benefit."},{"id":"s5","title":"Why these myths spread so easily","body":"Diabetes is a lifelong condition, and hearing that there is no cure is hard to accept. When someone on WhatsApp or social media shares a story about being healed through herbs, it offers hope. We also have a strong cultural tradition of herbal medicine in Kenya, and many people trust traditional remedies more than pharmaceutical drugs. Additionally, some people experience real improvements in their blood sugar when they start taking herbal supplements — but this is usually because they have also started eating better, exercising, and paying more attention to their health at the same time. They credit the herbs when the lifestyle changes are doing the actual work."},{"id":"s6","title":"What to do if you want to try herbal supplements","body":"You are not wrong for being curious about herbal options — that curiosity shows you are actively engaged in managing your health. The important rule is this: never stop or reduce your prescribed medication without your doctor''s knowledge. If you want to try a herbal supplement alongside your medication, tell your doctor at your next visit. Some herbal products can interact with diabetes drugs and cause your blood sugar to drop too low, which is a medical emergency. Your doctor can check for these interactions and monitor your blood sugar to see if the supplement is actually making any difference."},{"id":"s7","title":"How to spot a fake diabetes cure","body":"Be sceptical of any product that claims to cure diabetes completely. Be wary of testimonials that are not backed by medical records. Question any seller who tells you to stop your prescribed medication. Look out for products with no clear ingredient list or dosage instructions. A genuine health product will never ask you to abandon proven medical treatment."},{"id":"s8","title":"The real path to better health","body":"Managing diabetes effectively requires a combination of medication, dietary changes, regular physical activity, and consistent monitoring. These are not glamorous solutions, and they require daily effort. But they are the strategies that are proven to reduce complications, improve energy levels, and extend life. Your doctor, your clinic, and your own daily choices are your best medicine — not a promise in a bottle."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'fact-check'),
  (E'the-guilt-trap-when-you-eat-something-wrong', E'The guilt trap: when you eat something ''wrong''', E'DIABETES', '{"DIABETES"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'You are at a work event, a family gathering, or a birthday party. Someone passes you a plate of mandazi, freshly fried and golden.', E'It happens to everyone\n\nYou are at a work event, a family gathering, or a birthday party. Someone passes you a plate of mandazi, freshly fried and golden. You take one without thinking. Then the guilt hits. You start calculating how much sugar was in it, wondering if you have undone weeks of careful eating. That inner voice gets loud: you should have said no, you should have more discipline, you are failing at this.\n\nTake a breath. Eating one mandazi at a celebration does not erase months of good choices. It does not cancel out the mornings you chose boiled eggs and fruit instead of white bread and sweet tea. It does not undo the walks you have been taking after dinner. One moment is not a pattern, and beating yourself up actually makes things worse.\n\nWhy guilt is bad for your blood sugar\n\nHere is something most people do not know: guilt and shame trigger your body to release cortisol, which is a stress hormone. Cortisol tells your liver to release stored glucose into your blood. So the guilt you feel about eating that mandazi can literally raise your blood sugar on top of whatever the mandazi itself did. You end up with a double spike, one from the food and one from the stress. The emotional punishment does more harm than the food ever could.\n\nThis is not just theory. Research has shown that people who practise self-compassion around food choices have better long-term blood sugar control than people who are harsh with themselves. The reason is simple: when you forgive yourself quickly, you return to your healthy routine faster. When you spiral into guilt, you are more likely to think the whole day is ruined and keep eating poorly.\n\nWhat to do after an off-plan meal\n\nIf you ate something you did not plan for, here is a simple recovery routine that works. First, go for a 10 to 15 minute walk. Walking after eating helps your muscles absorb glucose from your blood, reducing the spike. You do not need to walk fast. A gentle stroll around your compound, to the nearby duka, or along your street is enough. Even standing and doing gentle movements like marching in place helps.\n\nSecond, drink a full glass of water. This supports your kidneys in processing the extra sugar and helps you feel settled. Third, eat your next meal as you normally would. Do not skip it to compensate. Skipping meals causes your blood sugar to drop too low and then spike again when you finally eat, creating a roller coaster that is harder on your body than the original mandazi ever was.\n\nThe social pressure is real\n\nIn Kenya, food is love. When your mother serves you a heaping plate of pilau at a family gathering, refusing or asking for a smaller portion can feel disrespectful. When colleagues order nyama choma and chips after work, saying you will just have a salad draws attention you do not want. These social moments are genuinely difficult, and pretending they are not does not help.\n\nHere are some strategies that work in real life. At family events, serve yourself so you can control portions. Take a small amount of everything so nobody feels their cooking was rejected. Eat slowly so you finish around the same time as everyone else. If someone pushes more food on you, a warm smile and a hand on your stomach with the words I am so full, it was delicious works wonders.\n\nAt restaurants with colleagues, order grilled meat or fish with vegetables instead of fried options. Most Kenyan restaurants will grill your nyama choma without extra marinade if you ask. Choose water or unsweetened tea instead of soda. Nobody notices what you are drinking when the conversation is flowing.\n\nWhen to seek extra support\n\nIf you find that guilt around food is constant, that you dread every meal, or that you feel anxious whenever you eat with other people, that is worth talking about with a professional. Diabetes distress is a real condition that affects up to 40 percent of people living with diabetes. It is not a character flaw. It is your mind struggling under the weight of managing a chronic condition every single day.\n\nAsk your doctor for a referral to a counsellor, or check whether your county hospital offers mental health services, many now do. Some facilities covered under SHA provide counselling sessions. You can also call the Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 if you need someone to talk to right away.\n\nRemember: you are not failing. You are human, living with a condition that asks a lot of you every day. One mandazi, one chapati, one plate of chips does not define your journey. What defines it is that you keep showing up, keep making the next good choice, and keep being gentle with yourself along the way.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"It happens to everyone","body":"You are at a work event, a family gathering, or a birthday party. Someone passes you a plate of mandazi, freshly fried and golden. You take one without thinking. Then the guilt hits. You start calculating how much sugar was in it, wondering if you have undone weeks of careful eating. That inner voice gets loud: you should have said no, you should have more discipline, you are failing at this.\\n\\nTake a breath. Eating one mandazi at a celebration does not erase months of good choices. It does not cancel out the mornings you chose boiled eggs and fruit instead of white bread and sweet tea. It does not undo the walks you have been taking after dinner. One moment is not a pattern, and beating yourself up actually makes things worse."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why guilt is bad for your blood sugar","body":"Here is something most people do not know: guilt and shame trigger your body to release cortisol, which is a stress hormone. Cortisol tells your liver to release stored glucose into your blood. So the guilt you feel about eating that mandazi can literally raise your blood sugar on top of whatever the mandazi itself did. You end up with a double spike, one from the food and one from the stress. The emotional punishment does more harm than the food ever could.\\n\\nThis is not just theory. Research has shown that people who practise self-compassion around food choices have better long-term blood sugar control than people who are harsh with themselves. The reason is simple: when you forgive yourself quickly, you return to your healthy routine faster. When you spiral into guilt, you are more likely to think the whole day is ruined and keep eating poorly."},{"id":"s3","title":"What to do after an off-plan meal","body":"If you ate something you did not plan for, here is a simple recovery routine that works. First, go for a 10 to 15 minute walk. Walking after eating helps your muscles absorb glucose from your blood, reducing the spike. You do not need to walk fast. A gentle stroll around your compound, to the nearby duka, or along your street is enough. Even standing and doing gentle movements like marching in place helps.\\n\\nSecond, drink a full glass of water. This supports your kidneys in processing the extra sugar and helps you feel settled. Third, eat your next meal as you normally would. Do not skip it to compensate. Skipping meals causes your blood sugar to drop too low and then spike again when you finally eat, creating a roller coaster that is harder on your body than the original mandazi ever was."},{"id":"s4","title":"The social pressure is real","body":"In Kenya, food is love. When your mother serves you a heaping plate of pilau at a family gathering, refusing or asking for a smaller portion can feel disrespectful. When colleagues order nyama choma and chips after work, saying you will just have a salad draws attention you do not want. These social moments are genuinely difficult, and pretending they are not does not help.\\n\\nHere are some strategies that work in real life. At family events, serve yourself so you can control portions. Take a small amount of everything so nobody feels their cooking was rejected. Eat slowly so you finish around the same time as everyone else. If someone pushes more food on you, a warm smile and a hand on your stomach with the words I am so full, it was delicious works wonders.\\n\\nAt restaurants with colleagues, order grilled meat or fish with vegetables instead of fried options. Most Kenyan restaurants will grill your nyama choma without extra marinade if you ask. Choose water or unsweetened tea instead of soda. Nobody notices what you are drinking when the conversation is flowing."},{"id":"s5","title":"When to seek extra support","body":"If you find that guilt around food is constant, that you dread every meal, or that you feel anxious whenever you eat with other people, that is worth talking about with a professional. Diabetes distress is a real condition that affects up to 40 percent of people living with diabetes. It is not a character flaw. It is your mind struggling under the weight of managing a chronic condition every single day.\\n\\nAsk your doctor for a referral to a counsellor, or check whether your county hospital offers mental health services, many now do. Some facilities covered under SHA provide counselling sessions. You can also call the Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 if you need someone to talk to right away.\\n\\nRemember: you are not failing. You are human, living with a condition that asks a lot of you every day. One mandazi, one chapati, one plate of chips does not define your journey. What defines it is that you keep showing up, keep making the next good choice, and keep being gentle with yourself along the way."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'peaceful-moment'),
  (E'diabetes-does-not-mean-your-life-is-over-it-means-it-is-different', E'Diabetes does not mean your life is over — it means it is different', E'DIABETES', '{"DIABETES"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'The day you were diagnosed with diabetes, the world probably felt like it shifted under your feet. Maybe you sat in the doctor''s office hearing words like chronic, lifelong, and medication, and your mind went blank.', E'The day everything changed\n\nThe day you were diagnosed with diabetes, the world probably felt like it shifted under your feet. Maybe you sat in the doctor''s office hearing words like chronic, lifelong, and medication, and your mind went blank. Maybe you felt angry, thinking why me, I eat well, I am not even that old. Maybe you felt numb and only let the fear in later, lying awake at night. All of those reactions are completely normal. A diabetes diagnosis is a life event, and it deserves the emotional weight you are giving it.\n\nBut here is what that diagnosis is not: it is not a death sentence. It is not a punishment. It is not proof that you did something wrong. Diabetes has genetic, environmental, and lifestyle components, and many people who do everything right still develop it. What matters now is not how you got here but what you do next.\n\nMillions of Kenyans walk this road\n\nYou are not alone in this. Diabetes affects an estimated 3.6 million Kenyans, and many more are living with it undiagnosed. The matatu driver who takes his metformin before his first trip. The teacher who checks her blood sugar during lunch break. The grandmother in Kisumu who has managed her condition for fifteen years and still tends her shamba every morning. These are ordinary people living full, active, meaningful lives while managing diabetes. Their secret is not perfection. It is consistency and acceptance.\n\nAcceptance does not mean giving up. It means you stop fighting the reality and start working with it. You stop asking why me and start asking what do I need today. That shift frees up energy you can direct toward the practical steps that actually improve your health.\n\nThe adjustments that feel heavy at first\n\nThe early weeks are the hardest. Suddenly you are thinking about everything you eat. A plate of ugali that you used to enjoy without a second thought now comes with mental arithmetic about portion sizes. Your morning chai needs less sugar. You might need to take medication at specific times, which means carrying tablets in your bag or setting phone alarms. Clinic visits that used to happen once a year now happen every three months.\n\nAll of this is real, and it is a lot. But here is what people who have lived with diabetes for years will tell you: it gets lighter. Not because the disease goes away but because the habits become automatic. After a few months, reaching for a fist-sized portion of ugali instead of a mountain-sized one is just what you do. Checking your blood sugar becomes as routine as charging your phone. Taking your medication becomes like brushing your teeth: you barely think about it.\n\nPractical daily adaptations\n\nStart small. You do not need to overhaul your entire life in one week. Pick one meal to adjust first, most people start with breakfast because it sets the tone for the day. Swap sweet white bread and sugary tea for boiled eggs, a small portion of sweet potatoes, and tea with just a little milk and no sugar. Once that feels normal, adjust lunch. Then dinner. Then snacks.\n\nKeep your medication somewhere visible so you do not forget it. Many people in Kenya keep their tablets next to their toothbrush or on the dining table. If you take insulin, talk to your pharmacist about proper storage. Most local pharmacies in Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu, and other towns stock insulin and can advise on keeping it cool even without a fridge, such as using a clay pot method.\n\nTell the people close to you. Diabetes management is easier when your household understands why the cooking has changed and why you take walks after meals. Many families discover that the dietary changes benefit everyone: less sugar, more vegetables, smaller portions.\n\nFinancial realities\n\nThe cost of managing diabetes in Kenya is a real concern. Test strips, medication, and clinic visits add up. Metformin is relatively affordable at KES 200 to KES 500 per month, but insulin can cost KES 1,000 to KES 3,000 or more. Register with SHA if you have not already, as it covers some diabetes medications and consultations at accredited facilities. Some counties also offer free or subsidised diabetes screening through public health centres. Ask at your nearest Level 4 or Level 5 hospital about their diabetes clinic.\n\nYour life is not over, it is different\n\nYou can still cook for your family, enjoy meals together, travel, work, celebrate, and dream. Diabetes asks you to pay more attention to your body, but that attention is a form of self-respect, not a burden. Many people say managing diabetes made them healthier overall than before the diagnosis, because it forced changes they had been putting off.\n\nTake it one day at a time. Some days your blood sugar will be perfect, other days it will spike for no clear reason. That is the nature of this condition. What matters is the trend over weeks and months, not any single reading on any single day. You are learning a new way of living, and you are doing better than you think.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The day everything changed","body":"The day you were diagnosed with diabetes, the world probably felt like it shifted under your feet. Maybe you sat in the doctor''s office hearing words like chronic, lifelong, and medication, and your mind went blank. Maybe you felt angry, thinking why me, I eat well, I am not even that old. Maybe you felt numb and only let the fear in later, lying awake at night. All of those reactions are completely normal. A diabetes diagnosis is a life event, and it deserves the emotional weight you are giving it.\\n\\nBut here is what that diagnosis is not: it is not a death sentence. It is not a punishment. It is not proof that you did something wrong. Diabetes has genetic, environmental, and lifestyle components, and many people who do everything right still develop it. What matters now is not how you got here but what you do next."},{"id":"s2","title":"Millions of Kenyans walk this road","body":"You are not alone in this. Diabetes affects an estimated 3.6 million Kenyans, and many more are living with it undiagnosed. The matatu driver who takes his metformin before his first trip. The teacher who checks her blood sugar during lunch break. The grandmother in Kisumu who has managed her condition for fifteen years and still tends her shamba every morning. These are ordinary people living full, active, meaningful lives while managing diabetes. Their secret is not perfection. It is consistency and acceptance.\\n\\nAcceptance does not mean giving up. It means you stop fighting the reality and start working with it. You stop asking why me and start asking what do I need today. That shift frees up energy you can direct toward the practical steps that actually improve your health."},{"id":"s3","title":"The adjustments that feel heavy at first","body":"The early weeks are the hardest. Suddenly you are thinking about everything you eat. A plate of ugali that you used to enjoy without a second thought now comes with mental arithmetic about portion sizes. Your morning chai needs less sugar. You might need to take medication at specific times, which means carrying tablets in your bag or setting phone alarms. Clinic visits that used to happen once a year now happen every three months.\\n\\nAll of this is real, and it is a lot. But here is what people who have lived with diabetes for years will tell you: it gets lighter. Not because the disease goes away but because the habits become automatic. After a few months, reaching for a fist-sized portion of ugali instead of a mountain-sized one is just what you do. Checking your blood sugar becomes as routine as charging your phone. Taking your medication becomes like brushing your teeth: you barely think about it."},{"id":"s4","title":"Practical daily adaptations","body":"Start small. You do not need to overhaul your entire life in one week. Pick one meal to adjust first, most people start with breakfast because it sets the tone for the day. Swap sweet white bread and sugary tea for boiled eggs, a small portion of sweet potatoes, and tea with just a little milk and no sugar. Once that feels normal, adjust lunch. Then dinner. Then snacks.\\n\\nKeep your medication somewhere visible so you do not forget it. Many people in Kenya keep their tablets next to their toothbrush or on the dining table. If you take insulin, talk to your pharmacist about proper storage. Most local pharmacies in Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu, and other towns stock insulin and can advise on keeping it cool even without a fridge, such as using a clay pot method.\\n\\nTell the people close to you. Diabetes management is easier when your household understands why the cooking has changed and why you take walks after meals. Many families discover that the dietary changes benefit everyone: less sugar, more vegetables, smaller portions."},{"id":"s5","title":"Financial realities","body":"The cost of managing diabetes in Kenya is a real concern. Test strips, medication, and clinic visits add up. Metformin is relatively affordable at KES 200 to KES 500 per month, but insulin can cost KES 1,000 to KES 3,000 or more. Register with SHA if you have not already, as it covers some diabetes medications and consultations at accredited facilities. Some counties also offer free or subsidised diabetes screening through public health centres. Ask at your nearest Level 4 or Level 5 hospital about their diabetes clinic."},{"id":"s6","title":"Your life is not over, it is different","body":"You can still cook for your family, enjoy meals together, travel, work, celebrate, and dream. Diabetes asks you to pay more attention to your body, but that attention is a form of self-respect, not a burden. Many people say managing diabetes made them healthier overall than before the diagnosis, because it forced changes they had been putting off.\\n\\nTake it one day at a time. Some days your blood sugar will be perfect, other days it will spike for no clear reason. That is the nature of this condition. What matters is the trend over weeks and months, not any single reading on any single day. You are learning a new way of living, and you are doing better than you think."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'journaling'),
  (E'when-and-how-to-check-your-blood-sugar-at-home', E'When and how to check your blood sugar at home', E'DIABETES', '{"DIABETES"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'Checking your blood sugar at home is one of the most powerful things you can do to manage your diabetes. It gives you real-time information about how your body responds to different foods, exercise, stress, and medication.', E'Why home monitoring matters\n\nChecking your blood sugar at home is one of the most powerful things you can do to manage your diabetes. It gives you real-time information about how your body responds to different foods, exercise, stress, and medication. Without these numbers, you and your doctor are guessing. With them, you can make informed decisions that keep you healthier and help prevent complications.\n\nYou do not need to check your blood sugar ten times a day. For most people with type 2 diabetes, two to three checks per day provide enough information to spot patterns and make adjustments. The key is consistency and knowing when to check.\n\nGetting a glucometer\n\nIf you do not have a glucometer yet, they are available at most pharmacies across Kenya. Popular brands like Accu-Chek, OneTouch, and Glucoplus are widely stocked. The meter itself costs between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000, and a box of 50 test strips runs KES 1,500 to KES 3,000 depending on the brand. Lancets, the small needles used to prick your finger, cost around KES 300 to KES 500 for a box of 100.\n\nMake sure the test strips are compatible with your specific meter and check the expiry date. Expired strips give inaccurate readings. Store strips in a cool, dry place away from direct sunlight. If you are on a tight budget, ask your pharmacist which brand has the most affordable strips. Some SHA-accredited facilities provide glucometers and strips as part of diabetes management programmes, so ask at your next clinic visit.\n\nWhen to check\n\nThe two most important times to check are fasting and after meals. Your fasting reading is taken first thing in the morning before eating or drinking anything other than water. This tells you what your blood sugar does overnight while your body is at rest. A good fasting target for most people with type 2 diabetes is between 4 and 7 mmol/L, which is 72 to 126 mg/dL.\n\nYour after-meal reading is taken exactly 2 hours after the first bite of your largest meal, usually lunch or dinner. This shows you how your body handled the food you ate. A good post-meal target is below 10 mmol/L, which is 180 mg/dL. If your readings are consistently above these targets, your doctor may need to adjust your medication or you may need to look at your portions.\n\nYou should also check your blood sugar any time you feel unwell or experience symptoms like dizziness, excessive thirst, frequent urination, blurred vision, or unusual tiredness. These can signal that your blood sugar is too high or too low.\n\nHow to check properly\n\nWash your hands with warm water and soap before testing. Food residue on your fingers can give a falsely high reading. If you just peeled an orange or handled a mango, the sugar on your skin will show up in the test. Dry your hands completely.\n\nPrick the side of your fingertip rather than the pad. The sides have fewer nerve endings, so it hurts less. Rotate between different fingers so no single spot gets too sore. Gently squeeze your finger for a drop of blood. Do not squeeze too hard, as this can mix tissue fluid with the blood and affect accuracy. Touch the blood drop to the test strip and wait for the reading.\n\nKeeping a record\n\nWrite down every reading in a notebook with the date, time, and what you ate beforehand. A simple school exercise book works perfectly. Some people use the notes app on their phone. Whatever method works for you is the right one.\n\nAfter two weeks of consistent recording, you will start to see patterns. You might notice that your blood sugar spikes higher after ugali than after githeri. You might see that days when you walk after lunch have lower afternoon readings. You might discover that stress from a difficult week pushes your fasting numbers up. These patterns are incredibly valuable.\n\nBring your notebook to every doctor visit. Your doctor can spot trends across two or three months that a single clinic test cannot reveal, helping them make better decisions about your medication.\n\nWhen to call your doctor immediately\n\nSome readings need urgent attention. Contact your doctor or go to the nearest hospital if your fasting blood sugar is above 15 mmol/L (270 mg/dL) on two consecutive readings, if it drops below 3.5 mmol/L (63 mg/dL) and you feel shaky or confused, or if you are vomiting and cannot keep food or medication down.\n\nLow blood sugar needs immediate treatment. If you feel shaking, sweating, dizziness, or confusion, eat or drink something sugary right away: a tablespoon of honey, half a glass of regular soda, or three glucose tablets. Then eat a small snack like a slice of bread to keep your levels stable. If you experience frequent lows, tell your doctor because your medication may need adjusting.\n\nRemember, your glucometer is a tool, not a judge. A high number is not a failure; it is a signal that something needs attention. Over time, managing your diabetes will feel less like guesswork and more like a skill you are steadily building.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Why home monitoring matters","body":"Checking your blood sugar at home is one of the most powerful things you can do to manage your diabetes. It gives you real-time information about how your body responds to different foods, exercise, stress, and medication. Without these numbers, you and your doctor are guessing. With them, you can make informed decisions that keep you healthier and help prevent complications.\\n\\nYou do not need to check your blood sugar ten times a day. For most people with type 2 diabetes, two to three checks per day provide enough information to spot patterns and make adjustments. The key is consistency and knowing when to check."},{"id":"s2","title":"Getting a glucometer","body":"If you do not have a glucometer yet, they are available at most pharmacies across Kenya. Popular brands like Accu-Chek, OneTouch, and Glucoplus are widely stocked. The meter itself costs between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000, and a box of 50 test strips runs KES 1,500 to KES 3,000 depending on the brand. Lancets, the small needles used to prick your finger, cost around KES 300 to KES 500 for a box of 100.\\n\\nMake sure the test strips are compatible with your specific meter and check the expiry date. Expired strips give inaccurate readings. Store strips in a cool, dry place away from direct sunlight. If you are on a tight budget, ask your pharmacist which brand has the most affordable strips. Some SHA-accredited facilities provide glucometers and strips as part of diabetes management programmes, so ask at your next clinic visit."},{"id":"s3","title":"When to check","body":"The two most important times to check are fasting and after meals. Your fasting reading is taken first thing in the morning before eating or drinking anything other than water. This tells you what your blood sugar does overnight while your body is at rest. A good fasting target for most people with type 2 diabetes is between 4 and 7 mmol/L, which is 72 to 126 mg/dL.\\n\\nYour after-meal reading is taken exactly 2 hours after the first bite of your largest meal, usually lunch or dinner. This shows you how your body handled the food you ate. A good post-meal target is below 10 mmol/L, which is 180 mg/dL. If your readings are consistently above these targets, your doctor may need to adjust your medication or you may need to look at your portions.\\n\\nYou should also check your blood sugar any time you feel unwell or experience symptoms like dizziness, excessive thirst, frequent urination, blurred vision, or unusual tiredness. These can signal that your blood sugar is too high or too low."},{"id":"s4","title":"How to check properly","body":"Wash your hands with warm water and soap before testing. Food residue on your fingers can give a falsely high reading. If you just peeled an orange or handled a mango, the sugar on your skin will show up in the test. Dry your hands completely.\\n\\nPrick the side of your fingertip rather than the pad. The sides have fewer nerve endings, so it hurts less. Rotate between different fingers so no single spot gets too sore. Gently squeeze your finger for a drop of blood. Do not squeeze too hard, as this can mix tissue fluid with the blood and affect accuracy. Touch the blood drop to the test strip and wait for the reading."},{"id":"s5","title":"Keeping a record","body":"Write down every reading in a notebook with the date, time, and what you ate beforehand. A simple school exercise book works perfectly. Some people use the notes app on their phone. Whatever method works for you is the right one.\\n\\nAfter two weeks of consistent recording, you will start to see patterns. You might notice that your blood sugar spikes higher after ugali than after githeri. You might see that days when you walk after lunch have lower afternoon readings. You might discover that stress from a difficult week pushes your fasting numbers up. These patterns are incredibly valuable.\\n\\nBring your notebook to every doctor visit. Your doctor can spot trends across two or three months that a single clinic test cannot reveal, helping them make better decisions about your medication."},{"id":"s6","title":"When to call your doctor immediately","body":"Some readings need urgent attention. Contact your doctor or go to the nearest hospital if your fasting blood sugar is above 15 mmol/L (270 mg/dL) on two consecutive readings, if it drops below 3.5 mmol/L (63 mg/dL) and you feel shaky or confused, or if you are vomiting and cannot keep food or medication down.\\n\\nLow blood sugar needs immediate treatment. If you feel shaking, sweating, dizziness, or confusion, eat or drink something sugary right away: a tablespoon of honey, half a glass of regular soda, or three glucose tablets. Then eat a small snack like a slice of bread to keep your levels stable. If you experience frequent lows, tell your doctor because your medication may need adjusting.\\n\\nRemember, your glucometer is a tool, not a judge. A high number is not a failure; it is a signal that something needs attention. Over time, managing your diabetes will feel less like guesswork and more like a skill you are steadily building."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'medication-tracking'),
  (E'your-hba1c-dropped-here-is-what-that-means', E'Your HbA1c dropped — here is what that means', E'DIABETES', '{"DIABETES"}', E'MILESTONE', E'If your doctor told you that your HbA1c has dropped, take a moment to appreciate what that means. HbA1c, sometimes called glycated haemoglobin, measures your average blood sugar over the past two to three months.', E'What HbA1c actually measures\n\nIf your doctor told you that your HbA1c has dropped, take a moment to appreciate what that means. HbA1c, sometimes called glycated haemoglobin, measures your average blood sugar over the past two to three months. Unlike a single glucometer reading that shows a snapshot of one moment, HbA1c tells the story of your daily management over an entire quarter. It is the most reliable indicator of how well your diabetes is being controlled.\n\nThe test works by measuring how much sugar has attached to your red blood cells. Because red blood cells live for about three months, the amount of sugar stuck to them reflects your average blood sugar during that period. A drop in your HbA1c means that across all those days, the meals you ate, the walks you took, the medication you remembered, your blood sugar was genuinely lower on average.\n\nWhy every percentage point matters\n\nHere is the part that should make you proud: research has shown that every 1 percent drop in HbA1c reduces your risk of serious complications by significant amounts. The risk of damage to the small blood vessels in your eyes and kidneys drops by about 25 percent. The risk of nerve damage, which causes numbness and tingling in your feet, drops by a similar amount. Your risk of heart attack drops by about 18 percent, and diabetes-related death drops by about 21 percent.\n\nThese are not small numbers. A 1 percent improvement, say from 9 percent to 8 percent, translates into years of healthier life. It means your eyes are more likely to stay sharp. Your kidneys are more likely to keep working well. Your feet are more likely to keep feeling sensations normally. These are the parts of your body that diabetes tends to damage silently over time, and you are actively protecting them.\n\nThe daily choices that got you here\n\nThis improvement did not happen by accident. It is the result of hundreds of small decisions you made every day. The mornings you chose a boiled egg and fruit instead of mandazi and sweet tea. The evenings you went for a walk around the neighbourhood after dinner instead of sitting down immediately. The times you measured a fist-sized portion of ugali instead of piling your plate high. The days you remembered your medication even when you were busy or tired.\n\nEach of those choices felt small in the moment. You might have wondered whether any of it was making a difference. Now you have the proof. Your HbA1c result is the sum total of those daily efforts, measured in a single number. The walks mattered. The portion control mattered. The medication mattered. You mattered.\n\nWhat your numbers mean\n\nFor most people with type 2 diabetes, doctors aim for an HbA1c below 7 percent, which corresponds to an average blood sugar of about 8.6 mmol/L or 154 mg/dL. Below 5.7 percent is normal. Between 5.7 and 6.4 percent is pre-diabetes. Between 6.5 and 7 percent means well controlled. Between 7 and 8 percent means fair control with room to improve. Above 8 percent signals your blood sugar needs more attention.\n\nIf your number dropped from one range to a lower one, that is a major achievement. Even if you are still above 7 percent, any downward movement is protecting your body.\n\nHow to keep the momentum going\n\nContinue the habits that got you here. Consistency is more important than perfection. Ask your doctor when to retest. Most recommend an HbA1c test every three months. In Kenya, it costs between KES 800 and KES 2,000 at private labs like Lancet or PathCare. Some public hospitals and SHA-accredited facilities include it in your diabetes check-up at lower cost.\n\nSet a new mini-goal. Maybe you want to bring your HbA1c down by another half percent, add 10 minutes to your daily walk, or check your blood sugar at home more consistently. Small goals keep you moving forward without feeling overwhelmed.\n\nCelebrate this win properly\n\nIn Kenyan culture, we celebrate achievements, and this one deserves recognition. Tell your family what the number means. Let your spouse or children know that the changes in the kitchen are working. If you have a support group at your clinic or in your community, share your result. Your success can encourage someone who is struggling.\n\nTreat yourself to something that is not food-related. Buy that book you have been eyeing, take a walk in a park you enjoy, or sit with the knowledge that you are taking care of yourself in a way that will pay dividends for years.\n\nYou earned this result. Your body is thanking you in the language of numbers, and the message is clear: what you are doing is working. Keep going.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"What HbA1c actually measures","body":"If your doctor told you that your HbA1c has dropped, take a moment to appreciate what that means. HbA1c, sometimes called glycated haemoglobin, measures your average blood sugar over the past two to three months. Unlike a single glucometer reading that shows a snapshot of one moment, HbA1c tells the story of your daily management over an entire quarter. It is the most reliable indicator of how well your diabetes is being controlled.\\n\\nThe test works by measuring how much sugar has attached to your red blood cells. Because red blood cells live for about three months, the amount of sugar stuck to them reflects your average blood sugar during that period. A drop in your HbA1c means that across all those days, the meals you ate, the walks you took, the medication you remembered, your blood sugar was genuinely lower on average."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why every percentage point matters","body":"Here is the part that should make you proud: research has shown that every 1 percent drop in HbA1c reduces your risk of serious complications by significant amounts. The risk of damage to the small blood vessels in your eyes and kidneys drops by about 25 percent. The risk of nerve damage, which causes numbness and tingling in your feet, drops by a similar amount. Your risk of heart attack drops by about 18 percent, and diabetes-related death drops by about 21 percent.\\n\\nThese are not small numbers. A 1 percent improvement, say from 9 percent to 8 percent, translates into years of healthier life. It means your eyes are more likely to stay sharp. Your kidneys are more likely to keep working well. Your feet are more likely to keep feeling sensations normally. These are the parts of your body that diabetes tends to damage silently over time, and you are actively protecting them."},{"id":"s3","title":"The daily choices that got you here","body":"This improvement did not happen by accident. It is the result of hundreds of small decisions you made every day. The mornings you chose a boiled egg and fruit instead of mandazi and sweet tea. The evenings you went for a walk around the neighbourhood after dinner instead of sitting down immediately. The times you measured a fist-sized portion of ugali instead of piling your plate high. The days you remembered your medication even when you were busy or tired.\\n\\nEach of those choices felt small in the moment. You might have wondered whether any of it was making a difference. Now you have the proof. Your HbA1c result is the sum total of those daily efforts, measured in a single number. The walks mattered. The portion control mattered. The medication mattered. You mattered."},{"id":"s4","title":"What your numbers mean","body":"For most people with type 2 diabetes, doctors aim for an HbA1c below 7 percent, which corresponds to an average blood sugar of about 8.6 mmol/L or 154 mg/dL. Below 5.7 percent is normal. Between 5.7 and 6.4 percent is pre-diabetes. Between 6.5 and 7 percent means well controlled. Between 7 and 8 percent means fair control with room to improve. Above 8 percent signals your blood sugar needs more attention.\\n\\nIf your number dropped from one range to a lower one, that is a major achievement. Even if you are still above 7 percent, any downward movement is protecting your body."},{"id":"s5","title":"How to keep the momentum going","body":"Continue the habits that got you here. Consistency is more important than perfection. Ask your doctor when to retest. Most recommend an HbA1c test every three months. In Kenya, it costs between KES 800 and KES 2,000 at private labs like Lancet or PathCare. Some public hospitals and SHA-accredited facilities include it in your diabetes check-up at lower cost.\\n\\nSet a new mini-goal. Maybe you want to bring your HbA1c down by another half percent, add 10 minutes to your daily walk, or check your blood sugar at home more consistently. Small goals keep you moving forward without feeling overwhelmed."},{"id":"s6","title":"Celebrate this win properly","body":"In Kenyan culture, we celebrate achievements, and this one deserves recognition. Tell your family what the number means. Let your spouse or children know that the changes in the kitchen are working. If you have a support group at your clinic or in your community, share your result. Your success can encourage someone who is struggling.\\n\\nTreat yourself to something that is not food-related. Buy that book you have been eyeing, take a walk in a park you enjoy, or sit with the knowledge that you are taking care of yourself in a way that will pay dividends for years.\\n\\nYou earned this result. Your body is thanking you in the language of numbers, and the message is clear: what you are doing is working. Keep going."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'celebration'),
  (E'reducing-salt-without-losing-flavour', E'Reducing salt without losing flavour', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'DIETARY', E'Salt is not the enemy, but too much of it is. Your body needs a small amount of sodium to function properly, about 2,000 milligrams per day, which is roughly one teaspoon of table salt.', E'Salt and blood pressure: understanding the connection\n\nSalt is not the enemy, but too much of it is. Your body needs a small amount of sodium to function properly, about 2,000 milligrams per day, which is roughly one teaspoon of table salt. The problem is that most Kenyans consume two to three times that amount without realising it. Excess sodium causes your body to hold onto more water, which increases the volume of blood in your vessels and pushes your blood pressure up. Over time, this extra pressure damages your arteries, heart, and kidneys.\n\nThe good news is that reducing your sodium intake can lower your blood pressure by 5 to 10 mmHg within a few weeks. That is a significant drop, sometimes enough to reduce or delay the need for additional medication. And the best part: reducing salt does not mean eating bland, tasteless food. Kenyan cooking has a rich tradition of spices and aromatics that can make your meals even more flavourful than before.\n\nYour new flavour toolkit\n\nThe key to cooking with less salt is building flavour from other sources. Start with the aromatics that are already staples in most Kenyan kitchens. Garlic, ginger, and onions form a flavour base that makes almost anything taste rich and satisfying. Add them early in the cooking process and let them cook down slowly. When garlic and onions caramelise in a little oil, they develop a deep sweetness that reduces the need for salt.\n\nDhania (coriander) and tomatoes are your best friends. A generous handful of chopped dhania stirred into a stew at the end of cooking adds brightness and freshness that salt cannot replicate. Diced tomatoes or tomato paste add umami, a savoury depth that tricks your tongue into thinking there is more salt than there actually is. A squeeze of lemon or lime juice just before serving lifts the entire dish.\n\nFor your spice shelf, stock up on black pepper, cumin, turmeric, paprika, and chilli flakes. You can buy all of these at any local market for under KES 200 total. Try this homemade blend: mix equal parts garlic powder, onion powder, black pepper, and paprika. Keep it in a jar in your kitchen and use it wherever you would normally reach for salt.\n\nMeal-by-meal salt reduction\n\nBreakfast is the easiest meal to fix. Switch to plain bread or chapati made at home with minimal salt rather than processed sliced bread, which contains more sodium than you might expect. If you eat eggs, season them with black pepper and turmeric instead of salt.\n\nFor lunch and dinner, where most of the salt typically hides, try these swaps. When cooking sukuma wiki, use half your usual salt and add a squeeze of lemon juice after cooking. The acidity brightens the greens and your tongue perceives it as more flavourful. When making beef or chicken stew, build your flavour base with onions, garlic, ginger, and tomatoes cooked down well, then add cumin and black pepper. You will find you need very little salt.\n\nGitheri is a naturally low-sodium meal because the beans and maize provide their own subtle flavour. Season it with onions, tomatoes, and dhania rather than salt and Royco. Pilau gets its signature taste from the spice mix, not from salt. Focus on cinnamon, cardamom, cumin, and cloves, and reduce the salt by half. Most people cannot tell the difference.\n\nThe hidden sodium traps\n\nSome of the biggest sodium sources in Kenyan kitchens are not the salt shaker but the flavour boosters people add without thinking. One Royco cube contains about 1,200 milligrams of sodium, more than half your daily limit. Soy sauce, ketchup, and pre-made marinades are similarly loaded.\n\nRead labels on packaged foods. Compare the sodium content per serving across brands and choose lower options. When eating out, ask for your food with less salt. At a nyama choma joint, request the meat grilled plain and add your own spices at the table. Processed meats like sausages and smokies should be occasional treats. If you use canned beans or vegetables, rinse them under water before cooking to wash off the brine.\n\nMaking it a family affair\n\nReducing salt benefits everyone in your household, not just you. High blood pressure runs in families because of shared genetics and shared eating habits. When you cook with less salt and more spices, you are protecting your children from developing hypertension later in life.\n\nInvolve your family in the transition. Let them taste the new spice blend. Ask your children to help squeeze lemons or chop dhania. When people participate in the change, they accept it more easily. Most families find that after two to three weeks of lower-salt cooking, the old way actually tastes too salty.\n\nYour taste buds will adjust. The first week might feel like something is missing. By the second week, you notice flavours the salt was masking. By the third week, heavily salted food tastes uncomfortably strong. Give your palate time to recalibrate, and you will discover that your cooking was always delicious. It just needed less salt and more spice.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Salt and blood pressure: understanding the connection","body":"Salt is not the enemy, but too much of it is. Your body needs a small amount of sodium to function properly, about 2,000 milligrams per day, which is roughly one teaspoon of table salt. The problem is that most Kenyans consume two to three times that amount without realising it. Excess sodium causes your body to hold onto more water, which increases the volume of blood in your vessels and pushes your blood pressure up. Over time, this extra pressure damages your arteries, heart, and kidneys.\\n\\nThe good news is that reducing your sodium intake can lower your blood pressure by 5 to 10 mmHg within a few weeks. That is a significant drop, sometimes enough to reduce or delay the need for additional medication. And the best part: reducing salt does not mean eating bland, tasteless food. Kenyan cooking has a rich tradition of spices and aromatics that can make your meals even more flavourful than before."},{"id":"s2","title":"Your new flavour toolkit","body":"The key to cooking with less salt is building flavour from other sources. Start with the aromatics that are already staples in most Kenyan kitchens. Garlic, ginger, and onions form a flavour base that makes almost anything taste rich and satisfying. Add them early in the cooking process and let them cook down slowly. When garlic and onions caramelise in a little oil, they develop a deep sweetness that reduces the need for salt.\\n\\nDhania (coriander) and tomatoes are your best friends. A generous handful of chopped dhania stirred into a stew at the end of cooking adds brightness and freshness that salt cannot replicate. Diced tomatoes or tomato paste add umami, a savoury depth that tricks your tongue into thinking there is more salt than there actually is. A squeeze of lemon or lime juice just before serving lifts the entire dish.\\n\\nFor your spice shelf, stock up on black pepper, cumin, turmeric, paprika, and chilli flakes. You can buy all of these at any local market for under KES 200 total. Try this homemade blend: mix equal parts garlic powder, onion powder, black pepper, and paprika. Keep it in a jar in your kitchen and use it wherever you would normally reach for salt."},{"id":"s3","title":"Meal-by-meal salt reduction","body":"Breakfast is the easiest meal to fix. Switch to plain bread or chapati made at home with minimal salt rather than processed sliced bread, which contains more sodium than you might expect. If you eat eggs, season them with black pepper and turmeric instead of salt.\\n\\nFor lunch and dinner, where most of the salt typically hides, try these swaps. When cooking sukuma wiki, use half your usual salt and add a squeeze of lemon juice after cooking. The acidity brightens the greens and your tongue perceives it as more flavourful. When making beef or chicken stew, build your flavour base with onions, garlic, ginger, and tomatoes cooked down well, then add cumin and black pepper. You will find you need very little salt.\\n\\nGitheri is a naturally low-sodium meal because the beans and maize provide their own subtle flavour. Season it with onions, tomatoes, and dhania rather than salt and Royco. Pilau gets its signature taste from the spice mix, not from salt. Focus on cinnamon, cardamom, cumin, and cloves, and reduce the salt by half. Most people cannot tell the difference."},{"id":"s4","title":"The hidden sodium traps","body":"Some of the biggest sodium sources in Kenyan kitchens are not the salt shaker but the flavour boosters people add without thinking. One Royco cube contains about 1,200 milligrams of sodium, more than half your daily limit. Soy sauce, ketchup, and pre-made marinades are similarly loaded.\\n\\nRead labels on packaged foods. Compare the sodium content per serving across brands and choose lower options. When eating out, ask for your food with less salt. At a nyama choma joint, request the meat grilled plain and add your own spices at the table. Processed meats like sausages and smokies should be occasional treats. If you use canned beans or vegetables, rinse them under water before cooking to wash off the brine."},{"id":"s5","title":"Making it a family affair","body":"Reducing salt benefits everyone in your household, not just you. High blood pressure runs in families because of shared genetics and shared eating habits. When you cook with less salt and more spices, you are protecting your children from developing hypertension later in life.\\n\\nInvolve your family in the transition. Let them taste the new spice blend. Ask your children to help squeeze lemons or chop dhania. When people participate in the change, they accept it more easily. Most families find that after two to three weeks of lower-salt cooking, the old way actually tastes too salty.\\n\\nYour taste buds will adjust. The first week might feel like something is missing. By the second week, you notice flavours the salt was masking. By the third week, heavily salted food tastes uncomfortably strong. Give your palate time to recalibrate, and you will discover that your cooking was always delicious. It just needed less salt and more spice."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'fresh-vegetables'),
  (E'the-hidden-salt-in-everyday-kenyan-foods', E'The hidden salt in everyday Kenyan foods', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'DIETARY', E'The biggest threat to your blood pressure might not be the salt shaker on your table. It is the sodium hiding inside the everyday ingredients you cook with and the packaged foods you buy without thinking twice.', E'The biggest threat to your blood pressure might not be the salt shaker on your table. It is the sodium hiding inside the everyday ingredients you cook with and the packaged foods you buy without thinking twice. Once you learn where sodium hides, you can cut your intake dramatically without making your food taste boring.\n\nThe Royco cube problem\n\nRoyco cubes are in almost every Kenyan kitchen. They make stew taste rich and savoury with minimal effort. But a single Royco cube contains roughly 1,200 milligrams of sodium. Your entire daily limit as someone managing hypertension is about 2,300 milligrams, and many doctors recommend staying below 1,500 milligrams. That means one cube already uses up half to nearly all of your daily allowance, and you have not even added any table salt yet. If you use two cubes in a pot of beef stew and then salt your ugali water, you could easily hit 3,000 milligrams in one meal alone.\n\nOther hidden sodium sources\n\nSoy sauce is another major offender. A single tablespoon contains around 900 milligrams of sodium. Tomato ketchup has about 150 milligrams per tablespoon, which adds up fast if you squeeze it generously over chips or eggs. Processed meats like sausages, bacon, and smokies are soaked in salt during manufacturing. A single smokie can contain 400 to 500 milligrams of sodium. Even bread is a hidden source. Two slices of white bread from your local bakery contain roughly 300 milligrams. Pickled vegetables, canned beans, and instant noodles like Indomie are also high-sodium foods that many people eat without realising the impact.\n\nMake your own spice blend\n\nThe good news is that flavour does not require sodium. You can make a simple spice blend at home that costs very little and transforms your cooking. Mix equal parts garlic powder, onion powder, black pepper, and paprika. Add a pinch of cumin and dried thyme if you like. Store it in a clean, dry jar or a small container in your kitchen. This blend costs about KES 200 to prepare and lasts two to three weeks. Use it when making stew, soup, or any dish where you would normally reach for Royco or salt. You will notice that your food starts tasting more complex and interesting because you are actually tasting the tomatoes, the onions, the meat, and the spices rather than just salt.\n\nCooking strategies that reduce sodium\n\nWhen making your regular beef or chicken stew, try this approach. Start by frying onions in a small amount of oil until they are deeply browned. This caramelisation creates a rich, sweet flavour that reduces the need for salt. Add fresh garlic and ginger early in the cooking process so their flavours infuse the oil. Use fresh tomatoes instead of tomato paste when possible, as paste is more concentrated in sodium. Squeeze half a lemon into the stew just before serving. The acidity brightens all the other flavours and tricks your tongue into perceiving more saltiness than is actually there. For sukuma wiki, sauté it with onions, tomatoes, and your homemade spice blend. You will be surprised how little you miss the Royco.\n\nSmart shopping at the market\n\nWhen you go to the market or supermarket, start reading labels. Look for the sodium content per serving and aim for products with less than 300 milligrams per serving. Choose fresh vegetables, fresh meat, and dried beans over canned or processed versions. Dried beans that you soak and cook yourself have almost no sodium compared to canned beans. Buy fresh tomatoes instead of canned tomato sauce. If you must use canned products, rinse them under water before cooking to wash away some of the sodium. At the butcher, choose fresh cuts of meat over pre-marinated or smoked options.\n\nEating out with confidence\n\nWhen eating at a restaurant or hotel, ask for your food to be prepared with less salt. Most cooks are happy to adjust. Avoid dishes described as smoked, cured, or marinated, as these are typically high in sodium. Choose grilled nyama choma and ask them to go light on the salt rub. Request lemon wedges and fresh dhania on the side instead of ketchup or sauces. For chapati, know that it typically contains salt in the dough, so limit yourself to one piece and fill up on vegetables and protein instead.\n\nYour family benefits too\n\nReducing sodium is not just for the person with hypertension. High sodium intake increases blood pressure risk for everyone, including your children. By changing how you cook at home, you are protecting your entire family. The adjustment takes about two to three weeks. After that, foods you used to enjoy will start tasting too salty, and your new way of cooking will simply feel normal.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The biggest threat to your blood pressure might not be the salt shaker on your table. It is the sodium hiding inside the everyday ingredients you cook with and the packaged foods you buy without thinking twice. Once you learn where sodium hides, you can cut your intake dramatically without making your food taste boring."},{"id":"s2","title":"The Royco cube problem","body":"Royco cubes are in almost every Kenyan kitchen. They make stew taste rich and savoury with minimal effort. But a single Royco cube contains roughly 1,200 milligrams of sodium. Your entire daily limit as someone managing hypertension is about 2,300 milligrams, and many doctors recommend staying below 1,500 milligrams. That means one cube already uses up half to nearly all of your daily allowance, and you have not even added any table salt yet. If you use two cubes in a pot of beef stew and then salt your ugali water, you could easily hit 3,000 milligrams in one meal alone."},{"id":"s3","title":"Other hidden sodium sources","body":"Soy sauce is another major offender. A single tablespoon contains around 900 milligrams of sodium. Tomato ketchup has about 150 milligrams per tablespoon, which adds up fast if you squeeze it generously over chips or eggs. Processed meats like sausages, bacon, and smokies are soaked in salt during manufacturing. A single smokie can contain 400 to 500 milligrams of sodium. Even bread is a hidden source. Two slices of white bread from your local bakery contain roughly 300 milligrams. Pickled vegetables, canned beans, and instant noodles like Indomie are also high-sodium foods that many people eat without realising the impact."},{"id":"s4","title":"Make your own spice blend","body":"The good news is that flavour does not require sodium. You can make a simple spice blend at home that costs very little and transforms your cooking. Mix equal parts garlic powder, onion powder, black pepper, and paprika. Add a pinch of cumin and dried thyme if you like. Store it in a clean, dry jar or a small container in your kitchen. This blend costs about KES 200 to prepare and lasts two to three weeks. Use it when making stew, soup, or any dish where you would normally reach for Royco or salt. You will notice that your food starts tasting more complex and interesting because you are actually tasting the tomatoes, the onions, the meat, and the spices rather than just salt."},{"id":"s5","title":"Cooking strategies that reduce sodium","body":"When making your regular beef or chicken stew, try this approach. Start by frying onions in a small amount of oil until they are deeply browned. This caramelisation creates a rich, sweet flavour that reduces the need for salt. Add fresh garlic and ginger early in the cooking process so their flavours infuse the oil. Use fresh tomatoes instead of tomato paste when possible, as paste is more concentrated in sodium. Squeeze half a lemon into the stew just before serving. The acidity brightens all the other flavours and tricks your tongue into perceiving more saltiness than is actually there. For sukuma wiki, sauté it with onions, tomatoes, and your homemade spice blend. You will be surprised how little you miss the Royco."},{"id":"s6","title":"Smart shopping at the market","body":"When you go to the market or supermarket, start reading labels. Look for the sodium content per serving and aim for products with less than 300 milligrams per serving. Choose fresh vegetables, fresh meat, and dried beans over canned or processed versions. Dried beans that you soak and cook yourself have almost no sodium compared to canned beans. Buy fresh tomatoes instead of canned tomato sauce. If you must use canned products, rinse them under water before cooking to wash away some of the sodium. At the butcher, choose fresh cuts of meat over pre-marinated or smoked options."},{"id":"s7","title":"Eating out with confidence","body":"When eating at a restaurant or hotel, ask for your food to be prepared with less salt. Most cooks are happy to adjust. Avoid dishes described as smoked, cured, or marinated, as these are typically high in sodium. Choose grilled nyama choma and ask them to go light on the salt rub. Request lemon wedges and fresh dhania on the side instead of ketchup or sauces. For chapati, know that it typically contains salt in the dough, so limit yourself to one piece and fill up on vegetables and protein instead."},{"id":"s8","title":"Your family benefits too","body":"Reducing sodium is not just for the person with hypertension. High sodium intake increases blood pressure risk for everyone, including your children. By changing how you cook at home, you are protecting your entire family. The adjustment takes about two to three weeks. After that, foods you used to enjoy will start tasting too salty, and your new way of cooking will simply feel normal."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'fresh-vegetables'),
  (E'safe-exercise-when-you-have-high-blood-pressure', E'Safe exercise when you have high blood pressure', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Exercise is one of the most powerful tools you have for lowering your blood pressure, and it does not require a gym membership, special equipment, or even leaving your compound. Regular moderate physical activity can lower your blood pressure by 5 to 8 mmHg over time.', E'Exercise is one of the most powerful tools you have for lowering your blood pressure, and it does not require a gym membership, special equipment, or even leaving your compound. Regular moderate physical activity can lower your blood pressure by 5 to 8 mmHg over time. That is roughly the same effect as one blood pressure medication. The key is knowing which types of exercise are safe and how to build a routine that works for your life.\n\nWhy exercise helps your blood pressure\n\nWhen you move your body regularly, your heart becomes more efficient at pumping blood. A stronger heart pushes blood with less effort, which means less force on your artery walls and lower blood pressure. Exercise also helps your blood vessels stay flexible and responsive. Over time, consistent physical activity reduces the stiffness in your arteries that contributes to high readings. It also helps you manage stress, improve sleep, and maintain a healthy weight, all of which directly affect your blood pressure.\n\nThe best types of exercise for hypertension\n\nAerobic exercise is your best friend. This means activities that get your heart rate up and keep it there for a sustained period. Walking is the simplest and most accessible option. You do not need a track or a park. Walk around your neighbourhood, walk to the market instead of taking a boda boda, or walk laps around your compound. Other good options include cycling, swimming if you have access to a pool, dancing, and even vigorous housework like mopping or gardening. The goal is 30 minutes of moderate activity on most days of the week, at least five days.\n\nA simple weekly walking plan\n\nIf you are just starting out, do not try to do 30 minutes on day one. Begin with what you can manage and build from there. Week one and two, walk for 10 minutes after your evening meal, five days a week. Walk at a comfortable pace where you can still hold a conversation. Week three and four, increase to 15 minutes per walk and pick up the pace slightly. You should feel warm and a little breathless but still able to talk. Week five and six, aim for 20 minutes. By week seven and eight, you should be comfortable with 25 to 30 minutes. If you have a phone, use the timer or step counter to track your progress. Many Kenyans find that walking with a neighbour or friend makes the time pass quickly and keeps them accountable.\n\nAdding strength exercises safely\n\nOnce you are comfortable with walking, you can add simple bodyweight exercises to further support your blood pressure. Try this routine twice a week, with at least one rest day between sessions. Start with 10 wall push-ups. Stand an arm''s length from a wall, place your palms flat, and push your body toward the wall then back. Next, do 10 chair squats. Stand in front of a sturdy chair, lower yourself until you just touch the seat, then stand back up. Follow with 10 standing calf raises. Stand behind the chair, hold the back for balance, and rise onto your toes, then lower. Finally, do 10 marching steps in place, lifting your knees to hip height. Rest for 30 seconds between each exercise. The whole routine takes about 10 minutes.\n\nExercises to avoid\n\nNot all exercise is safe when you have high blood pressure. Avoid heavy weightlifting or any exercise where you strain hard and hold your breath. This is called the Valsalva manoeuvre, and it can cause a sudden dangerous spike in blood pressure. Avoid sprinting or sudden intense bursts of activity if you are not conditioned for them. Be cautious with exercises that put your head below your heart for extended periods, like certain yoga poses, as these can increase pressure in your head and neck.\n\nWarning signs to watch for\n\nExercise should feel like work but not like distress. Stop immediately and rest if you experience any of the following: chest pain or tightness, severe headache, dizziness or feeling faint, irregular heartbeat, or unusual shortness of breath that does not improve when you slow down. If any of these symptoms persist after resting for 10 minutes, seek medical attention. It is a good idea to check your blood pressure before exercising if you have a home monitor. If your reading is above 180/110, skip the workout and consult your doctor.\n\nMaking it part of your life\n\nThe best exercise is the one you actually do. If you hate walking, try dancing to your favourite music in your sitting room. If mornings are too rushed, walk after dinner. If weekends are your only free time, a longer walk on Saturday and Sunday still helps. The key is consistency over intensity. A 15-minute walk every day is far more beneficial than one hour-long session once a week. Your blood vessels respond to regular, repeated signals that it is time to relax and open up. Give them that signal as often as you can.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Exercise is one of the most powerful tools you have for lowering your blood pressure, and it does not require a gym membership, special equipment, or even leaving your compound. Regular moderate physical activity can lower your blood pressure by 5 to 8 mmHg over time. That is roughly the same effect as one blood pressure medication. The key is knowing which types of exercise are safe and how to build a routine that works for your life."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why exercise helps your blood pressure","body":"When you move your body regularly, your heart becomes more efficient at pumping blood. A stronger heart pushes blood with less effort, which means less force on your artery walls and lower blood pressure. Exercise also helps your blood vessels stay flexible and responsive. Over time, consistent physical activity reduces the stiffness in your arteries that contributes to high readings. It also helps you manage stress, improve sleep, and maintain a healthy weight, all of which directly affect your blood pressure."},{"id":"s3","title":"The best types of exercise for hypertension","body":"Aerobic exercise is your best friend. This means activities that get your heart rate up and keep it there for a sustained period. Walking is the simplest and most accessible option. You do not need a track or a park. Walk around your neighbourhood, walk to the market instead of taking a boda boda, or walk laps around your compound. Other good options include cycling, swimming if you have access to a pool, dancing, and even vigorous housework like mopping or gardening. The goal is 30 minutes of moderate activity on most days of the week, at least five days."},{"id":"s4","title":"A simple weekly walking plan","body":"If you are just starting out, do not try to do 30 minutes on day one. Begin with what you can manage and build from there. Week one and two, walk for 10 minutes after your evening meal, five days a week. Walk at a comfortable pace where you can still hold a conversation. Week three and four, increase to 15 minutes per walk and pick up the pace slightly. You should feel warm and a little breathless but still able to talk. Week five and six, aim for 20 minutes. By week seven and eight, you should be comfortable with 25 to 30 minutes. If you have a phone, use the timer or step counter to track your progress. Many Kenyans find that walking with a neighbour or friend makes the time pass quickly and keeps them accountable."},{"id":"s5","title":"Adding strength exercises safely","body":"Once you are comfortable with walking, you can add simple bodyweight exercises to further support your blood pressure. Try this routine twice a week, with at least one rest day between sessions. Start with 10 wall push-ups. Stand an arm''s length from a wall, place your palms flat, and push your body toward the wall then back. Next, do 10 chair squats. Stand in front of a sturdy chair, lower yourself until you just touch the seat, then stand back up. Follow with 10 standing calf raises. Stand behind the chair, hold the back for balance, and rise onto your toes, then lower. Finally, do 10 marching steps in place, lifting your knees to hip height. Rest for 30 seconds between each exercise. The whole routine takes about 10 minutes."},{"id":"s6","title":"Exercises to avoid","body":"Not all exercise is safe when you have high blood pressure. Avoid heavy weightlifting or any exercise where you strain hard and hold your breath. This is called the Valsalva manoeuvre, and it can cause a sudden dangerous spike in blood pressure. Avoid sprinting or sudden intense bursts of activity if you are not conditioned for them. Be cautious with exercises that put your head below your heart for extended periods, like certain yoga poses, as these can increase pressure in your head and neck."},{"id":"s7","title":"Warning signs to watch for","body":"Exercise should feel like work but not like distress. Stop immediately and rest if you experience any of the following: chest pain or tightness, severe headache, dizziness or feeling faint, irregular heartbeat, or unusual shortness of breath that does not improve when you slow down. If any of these symptoms persist after resting for 10 minutes, seek medical attention. It is a good idea to check your blood pressure before exercising if you have a home monitor. If your reading is above 180/110, skip the workout and consult your doctor."},{"id":"s8","title":"Making it part of your life","body":"The best exercise is the one you actually do. If you hate walking, try dancing to your favourite music in your sitting room. If mornings are too rushed, walk after dinner. If weekends are your only free time, a longer walk on Saturday and Sunday still helps. The key is consistency over intensity. A 15-minute walk every day is far more beneficial than one hour-long session once a week. Your blood vessels respond to regular, repeated signals that it is time to relax and open up. Give them that signal as often as you can."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'stretching'),
  (E'myth-you-can-stop-blood-pressure-medication-when-you-feel-fine', E'Myth: You can stop blood pressure medication when you feel fine', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'Many people believe that once their blood pressure feels normal or once they feel healthy, they can stop taking their medication. You might hear someone say they stopped their pills because they feel fine, they have no headaches, and they have energy.', E'The myth\n\nMany people believe that once their blood pressure feels normal or once they feel healthy, they can stop taking their medication. You might hear someone say they stopped their pills because they feel fine, they have no headaches, and they have energy. Some people stop because the medication is expensive and they would rather spend the money elsewhere. Others stop because a friend or relative told them that long-term medication is bad for the liver or kidneys. This is one of the most dangerous myths about hypertension, and it puts lives at risk every day across Kenya.\n\nThe truth about the silent killer\n\nHigh blood pressure is called the silent killer for a very specific reason. Unlike a broken bone or a fever, high blood pressure almost never causes symptoms you can feel. Most people with blood pressure of 160/100 feel perfectly normal. They go about their day, work, eat, sleep, and feel fine. But inside their body, that elevated pressure is slowly damaging blood vessel walls, straining the heart muscle, and wearing down organs like the kidneys and brain. By the time symptoms appear, the damage is often severe and sometimes irreversible. A stroke, a heart attack, kidney failure, or vision loss can be the first sign that blood pressure was too high for too long.\n\nWhy feeling fine is actually the medication working\n\nIf you are taking blood pressure medication and you feel fine, that is not proof that you no longer need the medication. It is proof that the medication is doing its job. The tablets are actively keeping your blood pressure within a safer range. When you stop taking them, your blood pressure does not stay at the good level. It rises again, sometimes within days, sometimes within hours. For some people, stopping medication causes a rebound effect where blood pressure spikes even higher than it was before treatment started. This sudden spike is extremely dangerous and is a known trigger for strokes and heart attacks.\n\nThe real risks of stopping medication\n\nA study published in the British Medical Journal found that people who stopped their blood pressure medication without medical supervision had a significantly higher rate of heart attacks and strokes compared to those who continued treatment. In Kenya, doctors at Kenyatta National Hospital and other major facilities regularly see patients admitted with stroke or heart failure who had stopped their medication weeks or months earlier because they felt well. These emergencies are often preventable. A hospital admission for stroke in Kenya can cost KES 200,000 to KES 500,000 or more, far exceeding the cost of years of daily medication.\n\nThe cost concern is real but manageable\n\nMedication cost is a legitimate concern. Blood pressure tablets in Kenya can range from KES 200 to KES 2,000 per month depending on the type and brand. If cost is a barrier, talk to your doctor about switching to a generic version of your medication. Generic drugs contain the same active ingredients and are equally effective but cost significantly less. Many county hospitals and health centres provide blood pressure medication at subsidised rates or even free of charge. If you are registered under SHA, formerly NHIF, some of your medication costs may be covered. Ask your doctor or pharmacist about the most affordable option that works for your condition. Jireh can also help you plan for these recurring costs through your Care Saver account.\n\nWhat to do if you want to stop\n\nIf you are experiencing side effects from your medication, such as dizziness, fatigue, frequent urination, or swollen ankles, do not just stop taking the pills. Instead, book an appointment with your doctor and explain exactly what you are feeling. There are many different classes of blood pressure medication, including ACE inhibitors, ARBs, calcium channel blockers, and diuretics. If one type causes side effects, your doctor can often switch you to another that works just as well without the discomfort. Sometimes adjusting the dose is all that is needed.\n\nThe bottom line\n\nHypertension is a lifelong condition for most people. That does not mean your life is limited. It means you have a daily routine that includes taking a small pill, just like millions of Kenyans do. Think of it like charging your phone. You do it every day without questioning it because you know the phone will die without power. Your medication keeps your cardiovascular system running safely. Take it every day, keep your clinic appointments, and let your doctor make any changes to your treatment. Never stop on your own.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The myth","body":"Many people believe that once their blood pressure feels normal or once they feel healthy, they can stop taking their medication. You might hear someone say they stopped their pills because they feel fine, they have no headaches, and they have energy. Some people stop because the medication is expensive and they would rather spend the money elsewhere. Others stop because a friend or relative told them that long-term medication is bad for the liver or kidneys. This is one of the most dangerous myths about hypertension, and it puts lives at risk every day across Kenya."},{"id":"s2","title":"The truth about the silent killer","body":"High blood pressure is called the silent killer for a very specific reason. Unlike a broken bone or a fever, high blood pressure almost never causes symptoms you can feel. Most people with blood pressure of 160/100 feel perfectly normal. They go about their day, work, eat, sleep, and feel fine. But inside their body, that elevated pressure is slowly damaging blood vessel walls, straining the heart muscle, and wearing down organs like the kidneys and brain. By the time symptoms appear, the damage is often severe and sometimes irreversible. A stroke, a heart attack, kidney failure, or vision loss can be the first sign that blood pressure was too high for too long."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why feeling fine is actually the medication working","body":"If you are taking blood pressure medication and you feel fine, that is not proof that you no longer need the medication. It is proof that the medication is doing its job. The tablets are actively keeping your blood pressure within a safer range. When you stop taking them, your blood pressure does not stay at the good level. It rises again, sometimes within days, sometimes within hours. For some people, stopping medication causes a rebound effect where blood pressure spikes even higher than it was before treatment started. This sudden spike is extremely dangerous and is a known trigger for strokes and heart attacks."},{"id":"s4","title":"The real risks of stopping medication","body":"A study published in the British Medical Journal found that people who stopped their blood pressure medication without medical supervision had a significantly higher rate of heart attacks and strokes compared to those who continued treatment. In Kenya, doctors at Kenyatta National Hospital and other major facilities regularly see patients admitted with stroke or heart failure who had stopped their medication weeks or months earlier because they felt well. These emergencies are often preventable. A hospital admission for stroke in Kenya can cost KES 200,000 to KES 500,000 or more, far exceeding the cost of years of daily medication."},{"id":"s5","title":"The cost concern is real but manageable","body":"Medication cost is a legitimate concern. Blood pressure tablets in Kenya can range from KES 200 to KES 2,000 per month depending on the type and brand. If cost is a barrier, talk to your doctor about switching to a generic version of your medication. Generic drugs contain the same active ingredients and are equally effective but cost significantly less. Many county hospitals and health centres provide blood pressure medication at subsidised rates or even free of charge. If you are registered under SHA, formerly NHIF, some of your medication costs may be covered. Ask your doctor or pharmacist about the most affordable option that works for your condition. Jireh can also help you plan for these recurring costs through your Care Saver account."},{"id":"s6","title":"What to do if you want to stop","body":"If you are experiencing side effects from your medication, such as dizziness, fatigue, frequent urination, or swollen ankles, do not just stop taking the pills. Instead, book an appointment with your doctor and explain exactly what you are feeling. There are many different classes of blood pressure medication, including ACE inhibitors, ARBs, calcium channel blockers, and diuretics. If one type causes side effects, your doctor can often switch you to another that works just as well without the discomfort. Sometimes adjusting the dose is all that is needed."},{"id":"s7","title":"The bottom line","body":"Hypertension is a lifelong condition for most people. That does not mean your life is limited. It means you have a daily routine that includes taking a small pill, just like millions of Kenyans do. Think of it like charging your phone. You do it every day without questioning it because you know the phone will die without power. Your medication keeps your cardiovascular system running safely. Take it every day, keep your clinic appointments, and let your doctor make any changes to your treatment. Never stop on your own."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'fact-check'),
  (E'stress-and-blood-pressure-the-connection-nobody-warns-you-about', E'Stress and blood pressure: the connection nobody warns you about', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'Nobody sits you down after a blood pressure diagnosis and tells you that stress is about to become your most important thing to manage.', E'Nobody sits you down after a blood pressure diagnosis and tells you that stress is about to become your most important thing to manage. Your doctor might mention it briefly, maybe a passing comment about relaxing more, but they rarely explain the direct, physical connection between what you feel emotionally and what happens inside your arteries. Understanding this link can change how you approach your health.\n\nHow stress physically raises your blood pressure\n\nWhen you experience stress, whether it is a difficult conversation with a family member, financial pressure, a problem at work, or even being stuck in Nairobi traffic, your brain triggers a flood of hormones called adrenaline and cortisol. These hormones prepare your body to fight or run. Your heart beats faster, your blood vessels tighten, and your blood pressure jumps, sometimes by 20 to 30 points in a matter of minutes. This is your body''s survival system, and in short bursts it is normal and harmless. The problem is when stress becomes constant.\n\nThe Kenyan reality of chronic stress\n\nLet us be honest about what daily life looks like for many Kenyans. You might be worried about rent, school fees, or a business loan payment that is due. Perhaps you are caring for an elderly parent while also raising young children. Maybe your job is insecure, or you are dealing with family conflict over money or land. You might be managing your own health condition while also being the primary caregiver for someone else. These are not small, passing worries. They are ongoing pressures that keep your stress hormones elevated day after day, week after week. When cortisol stays high continuously, your blood vessels remain constricted and your blood pressure stays elevated even when you are sitting quietly at home. This is why some people get a high reading at the clinic even though they took their medication.\n\nStress and your daily readings\n\nIf you check your blood pressure at home, you may have noticed that your readings vary throughout the day. Morning readings might be decent, but by evening, after dealing with the day''s problems, the numbers climb. This is not your medication failing. It is stress pushing your blood pressure up on top of whatever your baseline is. Some people also experience what doctors call white coat hypertension, where their blood pressure is higher at the clinic because the visit itself causes anxiety. If you notice this pattern, mention it to your doctor. They may ask you to do home monitoring to get a more accurate picture.\n\nA breathing technique that works in minutes\n\nThis is not vague advice to just relax. This is a specific technique backed by research that you can do anywhere, anytime. It is called slow diaphragmatic breathing, and it activates your vagus nerve, the nerve that tells your body the danger has passed and it is safe to calm down. Here is how to do it. Sit comfortably or lie down if you can. Close your eyes. Breathe in slowly through your nose for four counts. Feel your belly expand, not your chest. Hold for one count. Breathe out slowly through your mouth for six counts, as if you are blowing through a straw. Repeat this cycle five times. The entire exercise takes about two minutes. Studies show that this technique can lower blood pressure by 5 to 10 mmHg in a single session. Try doing it twice a day, once in the morning before the day begins and once in the evening before bed. Over time, your body learns to activate the relaxation response more easily.\n\nOther practical stress management strategies\n\nBeyond breathing exercises, there are several things you can do to lower your stress levels. Physical activity is one of the best stress relievers available. A 15-minute walk after a stressful event can burn off the excess adrenaline and help your blood vessels relax. Talking to someone you trust, whether a friend, a spouse, a pastor, or a counsellor, helps you process difficult emotions instead of holding them inside. Many Kenyan hospitals and health centres now have mental health services. Ask at your next clinic visit whether counselling is available. Some facilities offer it at subsidised rates or free through SHA coverage.\n\nWhen to seek professional help\n\nIf you find that you are unable to sleep most nights, feel constantly overwhelmed, have lost interest in things you used to enjoy, or feel hopeless about your situation, these may be signs of depression or an anxiety disorder. Both conditions are common among people living with chronic illness, and both directly worsen blood pressure. There is no shame in seeking help. Talk to your doctor at your next visit, or ask for a referral to a counsellor. Mental health care is healthcare, and addressing it is just as important as taking your blood pressure tablet every morning.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Nobody sits you down after a blood pressure diagnosis and tells you that stress is about to become your most important thing to manage. Your doctor might mention it briefly, maybe a passing comment about relaxing more, but they rarely explain the direct, physical connection between what you feel emotionally and what happens inside your arteries. Understanding this link can change how you approach your health."},{"id":"s2","title":"How stress physically raises your blood pressure","body":"When you experience stress, whether it is a difficult conversation with a family member, financial pressure, a problem at work, or even being stuck in Nairobi traffic, your brain triggers a flood of hormones called adrenaline and cortisol. These hormones prepare your body to fight or run. Your heart beats faster, your blood vessels tighten, and your blood pressure jumps, sometimes by 20 to 30 points in a matter of minutes. This is your body''s survival system, and in short bursts it is normal and harmless. The problem is when stress becomes constant."},{"id":"s3","title":"The Kenyan reality of chronic stress","body":"Let us be honest about what daily life looks like for many Kenyans. You might be worried about rent, school fees, or a business loan payment that is due. Perhaps you are caring for an elderly parent while also raising young children. Maybe your job is insecure, or you are dealing with family conflict over money or land. You might be managing your own health condition while also being the primary caregiver for someone else. These are not small, passing worries. They are ongoing pressures that keep your stress hormones elevated day after day, week after week. When cortisol stays high continuously, your blood vessels remain constricted and your blood pressure stays elevated even when you are sitting quietly at home. This is why some people get a high reading at the clinic even though they took their medication."},{"id":"s4","title":"Stress and your daily readings","body":"If you check your blood pressure at home, you may have noticed that your readings vary throughout the day. Morning readings might be decent, but by evening, after dealing with the day''s problems, the numbers climb. This is not your medication failing. It is stress pushing your blood pressure up on top of whatever your baseline is. Some people also experience what doctors call white coat hypertension, where their blood pressure is higher at the clinic because the visit itself causes anxiety. If you notice this pattern, mention it to your doctor. They may ask you to do home monitoring to get a more accurate picture."},{"id":"s5","title":"A breathing technique that works in minutes","body":"This is not vague advice to just relax. This is a specific technique backed by research that you can do anywhere, anytime. It is called slow diaphragmatic breathing, and it activates your vagus nerve, the nerve that tells your body the danger has passed and it is safe to calm down. Here is how to do it. Sit comfortably or lie down if you can. Close your eyes. Breathe in slowly through your nose for four counts. Feel your belly expand, not your chest. Hold for one count. Breathe out slowly through your mouth for six counts, as if you are blowing through a straw. Repeat this cycle five times. The entire exercise takes about two minutes. Studies show that this technique can lower blood pressure by 5 to 10 mmHg in a single session. Try doing it twice a day, once in the morning before the day begins and once in the evening before bed. Over time, your body learns to activate the relaxation response more easily."},{"id":"s6","title":"Other practical stress management strategies","body":"Beyond breathing exercises, there are several things you can do to lower your stress levels. Physical activity is one of the best stress relievers available. A 15-minute walk after a stressful event can burn off the excess adrenaline and help your blood vessels relax. Talking to someone you trust, whether a friend, a spouse, a pastor, or a counsellor, helps you process difficult emotions instead of holding them inside. Many Kenyan hospitals and health centres now have mental health services. Ask at your next clinic visit whether counselling is available. Some facilities offer it at subsidised rates or free through SHA coverage."},{"id":"s7","title":"When to seek professional help","body":"If you find that you are unable to sleep most nights, feel constantly overwhelmed, have lost interest in things you used to enjoy, or feel hopeless about your situation, these may be signs of depression or an anxiety disorder. Both conditions are common among people living with chronic illness, and both directly worsen blood pressure. There is no shame in seeking help. Talk to your doctor at your next visit, or ask for a referral to a counsellor. Mental health care is healthcare, and addressing it is just as important as taking your blood pressure tablet every morning."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'peaceful-moment'),
  (E'your-blood-pressure-reading-is-not-your-worth', E'Your blood pressure reading is not your worth', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'When you see a high number on the blood pressure monitor, it is natural to feel a wave of frustration or defeat. You took your medication.', E'When you see a high number on the blood pressure monitor, it is natural to feel a wave of frustration or defeat. You took your medication. You skipped the salt. You walked after dinner. And still, the number is higher than you wanted. In that moment, it is easy to feel like nothing you do matters, like your body is working against you. But a blood pressure reading is not a report card, and it is not a measure of your worth or effort.\n\nA reading is information, not a verdict\n\nBlood pressure fluctuates throughout the day, every day, in every person on earth. It rises when you wake up, when you drink tea, when you climb stairs, when you are nervous, and even when you need to use the bathroom. It drops when you rest, when you sleep, and after gentle exercise. A single reading captures one moment in time. It does not capture the full picture of how well you are managing your condition. Your doctor looks at trends over weeks and months, not at isolated numbers. One high reading does not erase the good work you have been doing.\n\nThings that affect your reading that you cannot control\n\nGenetics play a significant role in hypertension. If your parents or grandparents had high blood pressure, your body may naturally run at higher levels regardless of how carefully you eat and exercise. This is not your fault. Age is another factor. Blood vessels become less flexible as we get older, which makes blood pressure harder to control over time. Hormonal changes, especially for women during menopause, can cause blood pressure to rise. Even the weather matters. Hot, humid days common across much of Kenya can affect hydration and blood vessel behaviour. Understanding these factors helps you set realistic expectations and avoid blaming yourself for things beyond your control.\n\nWhat you can control matters more\n\nThe daily choices you make are where your power lies. Did you take your medication this morning? That is a victory. Did you choose water or a glass of fresh passion juice instead of soda at lunch? That counts. Did you walk for even 10 minutes today? That is progress. Did you use your homemade spice blend instead of reaching for the Royco cube? Your arteries noticed, even if the monitor does not show it today. These choices, repeated day after day, are what move the long-term trend in the right direction. They protect your heart, your kidneys, and your brain even on days when the number on the screen is disappointing.\n\nThe emotional weight of a chronic condition\n\nLiving with hypertension means living with a condition that never fully goes away. There is no finish line where you are declared cured and can stop paying attention. That reality can feel heavy, especially on difficult days. You might feel tired of watching what you eat while everyone around you seems to eat whatever they want. You might resent the cost of medication every month, money that could go toward school fees or household needs. You might feel anxious before every clinic visit, wondering what the numbers will say. All of these feelings are valid and shared by millions of Kenyans managing the same condition.\n\nBuilding a new relationship with the monitor\n\nInstead of dreading your blood pressure check, try to approach it with curiosity rather than judgement. Think of the monitor as a tool that gives you useful information, like a fuel gauge in a car. When the gauge shows low fuel, you do not feel guilty. You just know it is time to refuel. When your blood pressure reading is high, it is information that helps you and your doctor make decisions. Maybe the medication dose needs adjusting. Maybe you had a particularly stressful week. Maybe you need to check if you are drinking enough water. The reading points you toward action, not toward self-criticism.\n\nPractical daily adaptations\n\nAcceptance does not mean giving up. It means adjusting your daily life so that managing hypertension fits naturally into your routine rather than feeling like a burden. Keep your medication next to your toothbrush so you never forget it. Carry a water bottle with you so staying hydrated becomes automatic. Learn two or three quick meals that are low in sodium and that your family also enjoys, so cooking does not feel like a chore. A simple githeri with vegetables, seasoned with garlic and dhania instead of salt, is a nutritious, affordable meal that the whole family can share. Keep your home blood pressure monitor in an easy-to-reach spot so checking becomes as routine as checking your phone.\n\nYou are more than your diagnosis\n\nHypertension is something you manage. It is not who you are. You are still a parent, a professional, a friend, a community member. You still have goals, dreams, and people who depend on you. On the days when the numbers are good, let yourself feel proud. On the days when they are not, remind yourself that one reading does not define you. Your consistency over time is what protects your health, and showing up every day, even imperfectly, is enough.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you see a high number on the blood pressure monitor, it is natural to feel a wave of frustration or defeat. You took your medication. You skipped the salt. You walked after dinner. And still, the number is higher than you wanted. In that moment, it is easy to feel like nothing you do matters, like your body is working against you. But a blood pressure reading is not a report card, and it is not a measure of your worth or effort."},{"id":"s2","title":"A reading is information, not a verdict","body":"Blood pressure fluctuates throughout the day, every day, in every person on earth. It rises when you wake up, when you drink tea, when you climb stairs, when you are nervous, and even when you need to use the bathroom. It drops when you rest, when you sleep, and after gentle exercise. A single reading captures one moment in time. It does not capture the full picture of how well you are managing your condition. Your doctor looks at trends over weeks and months, not at isolated numbers. One high reading does not erase the good work you have been doing."},{"id":"s3","title":"Things that affect your reading that you cannot control","body":"Genetics play a significant role in hypertension. If your parents or grandparents had high blood pressure, your body may naturally run at higher levels regardless of how carefully you eat and exercise. This is not your fault. Age is another factor. Blood vessels become less flexible as we get older, which makes blood pressure harder to control over time. Hormonal changes, especially for women during menopause, can cause blood pressure to rise. Even the weather matters. Hot, humid days common across much of Kenya can affect hydration and blood vessel behaviour. Understanding these factors helps you set realistic expectations and avoid blaming yourself for things beyond your control."},{"id":"s4","title":"What you can control matters more","body":"The daily choices you make are where your power lies. Did you take your medication this morning? That is a victory. Did you choose water or a glass of fresh passion juice instead of soda at lunch? That counts. Did you walk for even 10 minutes today? That is progress. Did you use your homemade spice blend instead of reaching for the Royco cube? Your arteries noticed, even if the monitor does not show it today. These choices, repeated day after day, are what move the long-term trend in the right direction. They protect your heart, your kidneys, and your brain even on days when the number on the screen is disappointing."},{"id":"s5","title":"The emotional weight of a chronic condition","body":"Living with hypertension means living with a condition that never fully goes away. There is no finish line where you are declared cured and can stop paying attention. That reality can feel heavy, especially on difficult days. You might feel tired of watching what you eat while everyone around you seems to eat whatever they want. You might resent the cost of medication every month, money that could go toward school fees or household needs. You might feel anxious before every clinic visit, wondering what the numbers will say. All of these feelings are valid and shared by millions of Kenyans managing the same condition."},{"id":"s6","title":"Building a new relationship with the monitor","body":"Instead of dreading your blood pressure check, try to approach it with curiosity rather than judgement. Think of the monitor as a tool that gives you useful information, like a fuel gauge in a car. When the gauge shows low fuel, you do not feel guilty. You just know it is time to refuel. When your blood pressure reading is high, it is information that helps you and your doctor make decisions. Maybe the medication dose needs adjusting. Maybe you had a particularly stressful week. Maybe you need to check if you are drinking enough water. The reading points you toward action, not toward self-criticism."},{"id":"s7","title":"Practical daily adaptations","body":"Acceptance does not mean giving up. It means adjusting your daily life so that managing hypertension fits naturally into your routine rather than feeling like a burden. Keep your medication next to your toothbrush so you never forget it. Carry a water bottle with you so staying hydrated becomes automatic. Learn two or three quick meals that are low in sodium and that your family also enjoys, so cooking does not feel like a chore. A simple githeri with vegetables, seasoned with garlic and dhania instead of salt, is a nutritious, affordable meal that the whole family can share. Keep your home blood pressure monitor in an easy-to-reach spot so checking becomes as routine as checking your phone."},{"id":"s8","title":"You are more than your diagnosis","body":"Hypertension is something you manage. It is not who you are. You are still a parent, a professional, a friend, a community member. You still have goals, dreams, and people who depend on you. On the days when the numbers are good, let yourself feel proud. On the days when they are not, remind yourself that one reading does not define you. Your consistency over time is what protects your health, and showing up every day, even imperfectly, is enough."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'new-beginning'),
  (E'how-to-get-an-accurate-blood-pressure-reading-at-home', E'How to get an accurate blood pressure reading at home', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'Your blood pressure does not stay the same all day. It rises when you are stressed, after eating, or when you rush to the clinic.', E'Why Home Readings Matter\n\nYour blood pressure does not stay the same all day. It rises when you are stressed, after eating, or when you rush to the clinic. That is why a single reading at the hospital does not always tell the full story. Home monitoring gives you and your doctor a much clearer picture of how your blood pressure behaves in your real, everyday life. Many people in Kenya are now buying digital blood pressure monitors from pharmacies like Goodlife, Haltons, or even from online shops on Jumia. A reliable upper-arm monitor costs between KES 3,500 and KES 7,000 and is one of the best investments you can make for your health.\n\nPreparing for an Accurate Reading\n\nBefore you take your blood pressure, sit quietly for at least five minutes. This means no talking, no scrolling through your phone, and no watching something exciting on television. Go to the toilet first if you need to, because a full bladder can raise your reading by up to 10 points. Avoid taking your reading within 30 minutes of drinking tea, coffee, or any caffeinated drink. If you have just walked home from the market or climbed stairs, wait at least 10 minutes before measuring. These small steps make a big difference in getting a number you can trust.\n\nThe Right Sitting Position\n\nSit in a chair with your back supported. Your feet should be flat on the floor, not crossed. Rest your arm on a table so that the cuff is at the same level as your heart. If the table is too low, place a pillow or folded leso under your arm to raise it. Roll up your sleeve so the cuff sits directly on your skin, about two centimetres above your elbow crease. The cuff should be snug but not tight. You should be able to slide one finger underneath it. If you are using a wrist monitor, hold your wrist at heart level during the reading.\n\nTaking the Reading\n\nPress the start button and stay still. Do not talk, cough, or move your arm while the cuff is inflating. When the reading appears, write it down immediately. Wait one to two minutes, then take a second reading. Your final number is the average of the two. For example, if your first reading is 138/88 and your second is 134/86, your average is 136/87. If the two readings are more than 10 points apart, take a third reading and average the two closest numbers.\n\nUnderstanding Your Numbers\n\nBlood pressure is written as two numbers. The top number, called systolic, measures the pressure when your heart beats. The bottom number, called diastolic, measures the pressure when your heart rests between beats. For most people, the target is below 140/90. Some doctors set a tighter target of 130/80, especially if you also have diabetes or kidney disease. If your reading is above 180/120, this is a hypertensive crisis. Do not panic, but sit quietly and retake it after five minutes. If it remains that high, go to the nearest hospital immediately or call your doctor.\n\nWhen and How Often to Measure\n\nThe most useful readings are taken in the morning, before you take your medication, and in the evening before dinner. Try to measure at roughly the same time each day. When you are first diagnosed or when your doctor changes your medication, measure twice a day for at least two weeks. Once your blood pressure is stable and well controlled, your doctor may say you only need to check a few times a week. Always measure for a full week before a clinic visit so you have a good set of numbers to share.\n\nKeeping a Simple Log\n\nBuy a small exercise book, the kind that costs KES 30 at any stationery shop, and dedicate it to your blood pressure. Write down the date, the time, whether it was before or after medication, and both numbers. If something unusual happened that day, note it too. For example, you might write "Had a stressful day at work" or "Ate nyama choma with lots of salt at a friend''s party." Over time, you and your doctor will see patterns. Maybe your readings are always higher on Monday mornings, or maybe they spike after eating certain foods. The Jireh app also lets you log readings digitally, which makes it easy to share with your care team.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n\nDo not take your blood pressure right after drinking chai or coffee. Do not measure over clothing or a thick sweater. Do not use a cuff that is too small for your arm, as this gives falsely high readings. If your upper arm is larger than 32 centimetres around, you need a large cuff. Also, do not compare your home readings to those taken at the clinic. Clinic readings are often higher because of the stress of being in a medical setting. This is called white coat hypertension and it is very common. Your home readings are usually the more accurate ones.\n\nWhen to Call Your Doctor\n\nCall your doctor or visit your nearest health facility if your home readings are consistently above 160/100, if you get a reading above 180/120, if you feel dizzy or have a severe headache along with a high reading, or if your readings suddenly change significantly from your usual pattern. Do not adjust your medication on your own based on home readings. Always discuss changes with your doctor first. Your blood pressure monitor is a tool to help you and your doctor work together, not a replacement for professional medical advice.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Why Home Readings Matter","body":"Your blood pressure does not stay the same all day. It rises when you are stressed, after eating, or when you rush to the clinic. That is why a single reading at the hospital does not always tell the full story. Home monitoring gives you and your doctor a much clearer picture of how your blood pressure behaves in your real, everyday life. Many people in Kenya are now buying digital blood pressure monitors from pharmacies like Goodlife, Haltons, or even from online shops on Jumia. A reliable upper-arm monitor costs between KES 3,500 and KES 7,000 and is one of the best investments you can make for your health."},{"id":"s2","title":"Preparing for an Accurate Reading","body":"Before you take your blood pressure, sit quietly for at least five minutes. This means no talking, no scrolling through your phone, and no watching something exciting on television. Go to the toilet first if you need to, because a full bladder can raise your reading by up to 10 points. Avoid taking your reading within 30 minutes of drinking tea, coffee, or any caffeinated drink. If you have just walked home from the market or climbed stairs, wait at least 10 minutes before measuring. These small steps make a big difference in getting a number you can trust."},{"id":"s3","title":"The Right Sitting Position","body":"Sit in a chair with your back supported. Your feet should be flat on the floor, not crossed. Rest your arm on a table so that the cuff is at the same level as your heart. If the table is too low, place a pillow or folded leso under your arm to raise it. Roll up your sleeve so the cuff sits directly on your skin, about two centimetres above your elbow crease. The cuff should be snug but not tight. You should be able to slide one finger underneath it. If you are using a wrist monitor, hold your wrist at heart level during the reading."},{"id":"s4","title":"Taking the Reading","body":"Press the start button and stay still. Do not talk, cough, or move your arm while the cuff is inflating. When the reading appears, write it down immediately. Wait one to two minutes, then take a second reading. Your final number is the average of the two. For example, if your first reading is 138/88 and your second is 134/86, your average is 136/87. If the two readings are more than 10 points apart, take a third reading and average the two closest numbers."},{"id":"s5","title":"Understanding Your Numbers","body":"Blood pressure is written as two numbers. The top number, called systolic, measures the pressure when your heart beats. The bottom number, called diastolic, measures the pressure when your heart rests between beats. For most people, the target is below 140/90. Some doctors set a tighter target of 130/80, especially if you also have diabetes or kidney disease. If your reading is above 180/120, this is a hypertensive crisis. Do not panic, but sit quietly and retake it after five minutes. If it remains that high, go to the nearest hospital immediately or call your doctor."},{"id":"s6","title":"When and How Often to Measure","body":"The most useful readings are taken in the morning, before you take your medication, and in the evening before dinner. Try to measure at roughly the same time each day. When you are first diagnosed or when your doctor changes your medication, measure twice a day for at least two weeks. Once your blood pressure is stable and well controlled, your doctor may say you only need to check a few times a week. Always measure for a full week before a clinic visit so you have a good set of numbers to share."},{"id":"s7","title":"Keeping a Simple Log","body":"Buy a small exercise book, the kind that costs KES 30 at any stationery shop, and dedicate it to your blood pressure. Write down the date, the time, whether it was before or after medication, and both numbers. If something unusual happened that day, note it too. For example, you might write \\"Had a stressful day at work\\" or \\"Ate nyama choma with lots of salt at a friend''s party.\\" Over time, you and your doctor will see patterns. Maybe your readings are always higher on Monday mornings, or maybe they spike after eating certain foods. The Jireh app also lets you log readings digitally, which makes it easy to share with your care team."},{"id":"s8","title":"Common Mistakes to Avoid","body":"Do not take your blood pressure right after drinking chai or coffee. Do not measure over clothing or a thick sweater. Do not use a cuff that is too small for your arm, as this gives falsely high readings. If your upper arm is larger than 32 centimetres around, you need a large cuff. Also, do not compare your home readings to those taken at the clinic. Clinic readings are often higher because of the stress of being in a medical setting. This is called white coat hypertension and it is very common. Your home readings are usually the more accurate ones."},{"id":"s9","title":"When to Call Your Doctor","body":"Call your doctor or visit your nearest health facility if your home readings are consistently above 160/100, if you get a reading above 180/120, if you feel dizzy or have a severe headache along with a high reading, or if your readings suddenly change significantly from your usual pattern. Do not adjust your medication on your own based on home readings. Always discuss changes with your doctor first. Your blood pressure monitor is a tool to help you and your doctor work together, not a replacement for professional medical advice."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'medication-tracking'),
  (E'three-months-of-readings-below-140-90', E'Three months of readings below 140/90', E'HYPERTENSION', '{"HYPERTENSION"}', E'MILESTONE', E'If your blood pressure has stayed below 140/90 for three consecutive months, take a moment to appreciate what you have achieved. This is not a small thing.', E'What Three Months of Control Really Means\n\nIf your blood pressure has stayed below 140/90 for three consecutive months, take a moment to appreciate what you have achieved. This is not a small thing. In Kenya, where hypertension affects nearly one in four adults, many people struggle for years to reach this level of control. Some never achieve it because they stop their medication when they feel fine, or because the daily demands of life push health to the back of the queue. You did not let that happen. You showed up for yourself every single day, and your body is thanking you in ways you cannot see but are very real.\n\nWhat Is Happening Inside Your Body\n\nWhen your blood pressure stays high, it is like running a water pump at maximum pressure all day, every day. The pipes wear out faster. In your body, those pipes are your blood vessels, and they supply everything: your brain, your heart, your kidneys, your eyes. Three months of controlled pressure means the walls of your blood vessels have had time to relax and begin healing. The tiny blood vessels in your kidneys are filtering blood more efficiently, which means less protein leaking into your urine and better kidney function. Your heart muscle, which may have been working overtime to push blood through stiff vessels, is now pumping more easily. Your risk of stroke has already started to drop measurably.\n\nThe Numbers Behind the Achievement\n\nResearch shows that every 10-point reduction in systolic blood pressure reduces your risk of stroke by about 27 percent and your risk of heart disease by about 17 percent. Over three months of sustained control, these benefits compound. Your blood vessels become more elastic, your heart becomes more efficient, and the organs that depend on good blood flow, including your brain, work better. Many patients report feeling more alert, sleeping better, and having fewer headaches once their blood pressure is consistently controlled. These are not coincidences. They are the direct results of what you are doing.\n\nThe Daily Habits That Got You Here\n\nThis achievement did not happen by accident. It came from a collection of daily choices that added up over 90 days. Maybe you switched from cooking with a lot of salt to using lemon juice, garlic, and dhania to flavour your food. Maybe you started walking for 30 minutes every evening after work, taking a route through your neighbourhood or around the local school grounds. Maybe you set a daily alarm on your phone to remind you to take your medication at the same time every morning. Perhaps you started eating more sukuma wiki, spinach, and other leafy vegetables, which are rich in potassium and help lower blood pressure naturally. Whatever your combination of habits, they are working.\n\nThe Role of Consistency\n\nThe most important word in blood pressure management is consistency. It is not about being perfect. There were probably days when you ate more salt than you planned, or when you skipped your evening walk because it was raining, or when you were late taking your medication. That is normal. The point is that on most days, you made the right choice. Consistency does not mean perfection. It means that your good days far outnumber your bad days, and that when you slip, you get back on track quickly. This is the habit pattern that produces three months of good readings.\n\nCelebrating Without Sabotaging\n\nYou deserve to celebrate this milestone, but let us be smart about it. A celebration does not have to involve nyama choma with extra salt and several beers. Instead, consider treating yourself to something that supports your health journey. Buy yourself a new pair of walking shoes. Treat yourself to a fresh fruit smoothie from your favourite juice shop. Go for a massage at a local spa. Invite a friend for a walk at Karura Forest or Uhuru Gardens. Celebrations that align with your health goals reinforce the habits that got you here.\n\nLooking Ahead\n\nBring your blood pressure log to your next doctor''s appointment. The trend of controlled readings helps your doctor make better decisions about your care. At six months of sustained control, your risk reductions become even more significant. At one year, you are in a fundamentally different health position than someone with uncontrolled hypertension. The habits you have built are becoming automatic, which means they require less willpower. If you are using the Jireh app, log this milestone. And if you are part of a Jireh Circle, share your achievement. Your story might inspire someone else who is still struggling to get their numbers under control.\n\nA Word of Caution\n\nNever stop or reduce your medication because your readings have been good. Your readings are good because of the medication. Think of it like an umbrella in the rain: the fact that you are dry does not mean the rain has stopped. Continue taking your medication exactly as prescribed. Continue monitoring at home. Continue making the food and movement choices that brought you to this point. This milestone is not the finish line. It is proof that you are on the right path.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"What Three Months of Control Really Means","body":"If your blood pressure has stayed below 140/90 for three consecutive months, take a moment to appreciate what you have achieved. This is not a small thing. In Kenya, where hypertension affects nearly one in four adults, many people struggle for years to reach this level of control. Some never achieve it because they stop their medication when they feel fine, or because the daily demands of life push health to the back of the queue. You did not let that happen. You showed up for yourself every single day, and your body is thanking you in ways you cannot see but are very real."},{"id":"s2","title":"What Is Happening Inside Your Body","body":"When your blood pressure stays high, it is like running a water pump at maximum pressure all day, every day. The pipes wear out faster. In your body, those pipes are your blood vessels, and they supply everything: your brain, your heart, your kidneys, your eyes. Three months of controlled pressure means the walls of your blood vessels have had time to relax and begin healing. The tiny blood vessels in your kidneys are filtering blood more efficiently, which means less protein leaking into your urine and better kidney function. Your heart muscle, which may have been working overtime to push blood through stiff vessels, is now pumping more easily. Your risk of stroke has already started to drop measurably."},{"id":"s3","title":"The Numbers Behind the Achievement","body":"Research shows that every 10-point reduction in systolic blood pressure reduces your risk of stroke by about 27 percent and your risk of heart disease by about 17 percent. Over three months of sustained control, these benefits compound. Your blood vessels become more elastic, your heart becomes more efficient, and the organs that depend on good blood flow, including your brain, work better. Many patients report feeling more alert, sleeping better, and having fewer headaches once their blood pressure is consistently controlled. These are not coincidences. They are the direct results of what you are doing."},{"id":"s4","title":"The Daily Habits That Got You Here","body":"This achievement did not happen by accident. It came from a collection of daily choices that added up over 90 days. Maybe you switched from cooking with a lot of salt to using lemon juice, garlic, and dhania to flavour your food. Maybe you started walking for 30 minutes every evening after work, taking a route through your neighbourhood or around the local school grounds. Maybe you set a daily alarm on your phone to remind you to take your medication at the same time every morning. Perhaps you started eating more sukuma wiki, spinach, and other leafy vegetables, which are rich in potassium and help lower blood pressure naturally. Whatever your combination of habits, they are working."},{"id":"s5","title":"The Role of Consistency","body":"The most important word in blood pressure management is consistency. It is not about being perfect. There were probably days when you ate more salt than you planned, or when you skipped your evening walk because it was raining, or when you were late taking your medication. That is normal. The point is that on most days, you made the right choice. Consistency does not mean perfection. It means that your good days far outnumber your bad days, and that when you slip, you get back on track quickly. This is the habit pattern that produces three months of good readings."},{"id":"s6","title":"Celebrating Without Sabotaging","body":"You deserve to celebrate this milestone, but let us be smart about it. A celebration does not have to involve nyama choma with extra salt and several beers. Instead, consider treating yourself to something that supports your health journey. Buy yourself a new pair of walking shoes. Treat yourself to a fresh fruit smoothie from your favourite juice shop. Go for a massage at a local spa. Invite a friend for a walk at Karura Forest or Uhuru Gardens. Celebrations that align with your health goals reinforce the habits that got you here."},{"id":"s7","title":"Looking Ahead","body":"Bring your blood pressure log to your next doctor''s appointment. The trend of controlled readings helps your doctor make better decisions about your care. At six months of sustained control, your risk reductions become even more significant. At one year, you are in a fundamentally different health position than someone with uncontrolled hypertension. The habits you have built are becoming automatic, which means they require less willpower. If you are using the Jireh app, log this milestone. And if you are part of a Jireh Circle, share your achievement. Your story might inspire someone else who is still struggling to get their numbers under control."},{"id":"s8","title":"A Word of Caution","body":"Never stop or reduce your medication because your readings have been good. Your readings are good because of the medication. Think of it like an umbrella in the rain: the fact that you are dry does not mean the rain has stopped. Continue taking your medication exactly as prescribed. Continue monitoring at home. Continue making the food and movement choices that brought you to this point. This milestone is not the finish line. It is proof that you are on the right path."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'celebration'),
  (E'foods-that-help-keep-your-airways-calm', E'Foods that help keep your airways calm', E'ASTHMA', '{"ASTHMA"}', E'DIETARY', E'You might not think of food as medicine for your lungs, but what you eat has a direct effect on the level of inflammation inside your airways. Asthma is fundamentally an inflammatory condition.', E'The Connection Between Food and Your Airways\n\nYou might not think of food as medicine for your lungs, but what you eat has a direct effect on the level of inflammation inside your airways. Asthma is fundamentally an inflammatory condition. When the lining of your airways is inflamed, it swells, produces extra mucus, and becomes more sensitive to triggers. Certain foods can calm this inflammation, while others can make it worse. By making thoughtful choices at the market and in your kitchen, you can give your airways a better chance of staying open and comfortable throughout the day.\n\nFish and Omega-3 Fatty Acids\n\nOily fish is one of the best foods for reducing airway inflammation. In Kenya, omena (the small silver fish from Lake Victoria) is affordable and widely available. A cup of omena costs between KES 100 and KES 200 at most markets, making it one of the cheapest sources of omega-3 fatty acids you can find. Tilapia and Nile perch are also good options. Try to eat fish at least two to three times a week. Omena can be fried lightly with onions and tomatoes and served alongside ugali for a satisfying dinner. Tilapia can be grilled, steamed with lemon and herbs, or added to a light stew. When frying fish, use a small amount of sunflower or olive oil rather than deep-frying, as excessive oil can increase inflammation.\n\nFruits Rich in Vitamin C\n\nVitamin C is a powerful antioxidant that helps protect lung tissue from damage. Kenya is blessed with an abundance of vitamin C-rich fruits. Oranges, guavas, passion fruit, mangoes, pineapples, and pawpaw are all excellent choices. Try to eat at least two servings of fruit every day. A serving is about the size of your fist. Add sliced mango or banana to your morning porridge. Carry a passion fruit or two in your bag as a snack. Blend guava, pineapple, and a little honey into a fresh juice. Guavas are especially valuable because they contain more vitamin C than oranges and are often cheaper. During mango season, take full advantage of the abundance and eat mangoes daily.\n\nLeafy Greens and Vegetables\n\nSukuma wiki, spinach, managu, saga, and terere are all rich in magnesium, which helps relax the muscles around your airways. Many Kenyan households already eat sukuma wiki regularly, which is wonderful. To get the most benefit, try not to overcook it. Lightly sauteing sukuma wiki with garlic, onions, and a small amount of oil preserves more of its nutrients than boiling it for a long time. Eat at least one large serving of leafy greens every day. You can add spinach to your morning eggs, mix managu into a lentil stew, or serve a generous helping of sukuma wiki alongside your lunch.\n\nGinger and Turmeric\n\nBoth ginger and turmeric have natural anti-inflammatory properties. Ginger tea, made by boiling fresh sliced ginger in water with a squeeze of lemon and a little honey, is a soothing drink that can help open your airways slightly. Turmeric can be added to soups, stews, and even chai. A pinch of turmeric in your morning tea or porridge is an easy habit to build. Some people find that a warm ginger-turmeric drink before bed helps them breathe more comfortably at night. While these are not replacements for your inhaler, they complement your medical treatment.\n\nFoods to Be Careful With\n\nSome foods can worsen asthma symptoms. Very cold drinks and ice cream can trigger bronchospasm in sensitive people, especially if you drink or eat them quickly. Processed foods with sulphite preservatives, found in some wines, dried fruits, and packaged juices, can trigger symptoms in some asthmatics. If you notice that certain foods seem to make you wheeze or feel tight, note them in your trigger diary. Common culprits include very spicy foods, foods with strong artificial colours or flavours, and dairy products for some people. Pay attention to your body''s responses and adjust accordingly.\n\nPractical Meal Ideas for the Week\n\nHere is a simple plan to get you started. Monday breakfast: porridge with sliced banana and a drizzle of honey. Monday lunch: ugali with sukuma wiki and omena. Monday dinner: grilled tilapia with steamed vegetables and a small portion of rice. Tuesday: swap the omena for a bean stew with chapati for lunch, and add a mango after dinner. Wednesday: try githeri with spinach and a boiled egg for lunch. For snacks throughout the week, keep oranges, guavas, or groundnuts nearby. On weekends, treat yourself to a fruit salad with pawpaw, pineapple, passion fruit, and watermelon. The goal is not a strict diet but a pattern of eating more fish, more fruits, and more vegetables week after week.\n\nShopping on a Budget\n\nYou do not need expensive supplements or imported superfoods. Everything your lungs need is available at your local market. Buy omena in bulk and store it in the freezer. Buy fruits that are in season because they are cheaper and fresher. Sukuma wiki is one of the most affordable vegetables in Kenya and is available everywhere. Grow your own herbs if you have a small garden or even a few pots on your balcony. Garlic, dhania, ginger, and spring onions are easy to grow and add both flavour and health benefits to your meals. When shopping, go early in the morning for the freshest produce and the best prices.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The Connection Between Food and Your Airways","body":"You might not think of food as medicine for your lungs, but what you eat has a direct effect on the level of inflammation inside your airways. Asthma is fundamentally an inflammatory condition. When the lining of your airways is inflamed, it swells, produces extra mucus, and becomes more sensitive to triggers. Certain foods can calm this inflammation, while others can make it worse. By making thoughtful choices at the market and in your kitchen, you can give your airways a better chance of staying open and comfortable throughout the day."},{"id":"s2","title":"Fish and Omega-3 Fatty Acids","body":"Oily fish is one of the best foods for reducing airway inflammation. In Kenya, omena (the small silver fish from Lake Victoria) is affordable and widely available. A cup of omena costs between KES 100 and KES 200 at most markets, making it one of the cheapest sources of omega-3 fatty acids you can find. Tilapia and Nile perch are also good options. Try to eat fish at least two to three times a week. Omena can be fried lightly with onions and tomatoes and served alongside ugali for a satisfying dinner. Tilapia can be grilled, steamed with lemon and herbs, or added to a light stew. When frying fish, use a small amount of sunflower or olive oil rather than deep-frying, as excessive oil can increase inflammation."},{"id":"s3","title":"Fruits Rich in Vitamin C","body":"Vitamin C is a powerful antioxidant that helps protect lung tissue from damage. Kenya is blessed with an abundance of vitamin C-rich fruits. Oranges, guavas, passion fruit, mangoes, pineapples, and pawpaw are all excellent choices. Try to eat at least two servings of fruit every day. A serving is about the size of your fist. Add sliced mango or banana to your morning porridge. Carry a passion fruit or two in your bag as a snack. Blend guava, pineapple, and a little honey into a fresh juice. Guavas are especially valuable because they contain more vitamin C than oranges and are often cheaper. During mango season, take full advantage of the abundance and eat mangoes daily."},{"id":"s4","title":"Leafy Greens and Vegetables","body":"Sukuma wiki, spinach, managu, saga, and terere are all rich in magnesium, which helps relax the muscles around your airways. Many Kenyan households already eat sukuma wiki regularly, which is wonderful. T