tew with chapati for lunch, and add a mango after dinner. Wednesday: try githeri with spinach and a boiled egg for lunch. For snacks throughout the week, keep oranges, guavas, or groundnuts nearby. On weekends, treat yourself to a fruit salad with pawpaw, pineapple, passion fruit, and watermelon. The goal is not a strict diet but a pattern of eating more fish, more fruits, and more vegetables week after week."},{"id":"s8","title":"Shopping on a Budget","body":"You do not need expensive supplements or imported superfoods. Everything your lungs need is available at your local market. Buy omena in bulk and store it in the freezer. Buy fruits that are in season because they are cheaper and fresher. Sukuma wiki is one of the most affordable vegetables in Kenya and is available everywhere. Grow your own herbs if you have a small garden or even a few pots on your balcony. Garlic, dhania, ginger, and spring onions are easy to grow and add both flavour and health benefits to your meals. When shopping, go early in the morning for the freshest produce and the best prices."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'market-shopping'),
  (E'the-5-minute-breathing-trick-for-asthma-control', E'The 5-minute breathing trick for asthma control', E'ASTHMA', '{"ASTHMA"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Buteyko breathing is a technique developed to help people who overbreathe, which is surprisingly common among people with asthma. When you overbreathe, or breathe faster and deeper than your body needs, you lose too much carbon dioxide.', E'What Is Buteyko Breathing\n\nButeyko breathing is a technique developed to help people who overbreathe, which is surprisingly common among people with asthma. When you overbreathe, or breathe faster and deeper than your body needs, you lose too much carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide is not just a waste gas. It actually helps keep your airways open and relaxed. When levels drop too low, your airways can tighten, triggering that familiar feeling of chest tightness or the urge to cough. Buteyko breathing retrains your breathing pattern to be slower, gentler, and more efficient. Over time, this can significantly reduce the frequency and severity of your asthma symptoms.\n\nBefore You Begin\n\nFind a quiet spot where you will not be disturbed for about five to ten minutes. This could be your bedroom, a quiet corner of your sitting room, or even a peaceful spot in your garden or compound. Sit in a comfortable chair with your back supported. You can also sit cross-legged on a mat if that is more comfortable for you. Make sure your clothing is not tight around your chest or waist. Turn off the television and put your phone on silent. The more relaxed you are when you start, the more effective the practice will be.\n\nStep-by-Step Instructions\n\nStep one: Close your mouth gently. All breathing during this exercise should be through your nose. If your nose is blocked, try sitting in a steamy bathroom for a few minutes first, or gently press and release the sides of your nose to open it up.\n\nStep two: Breathe in gently through your nose for about three seconds. The breath should be light and quiet. You should not hear yourself breathing. Imagine you are trying to breathe so softly that a feather held under your nose would barely move.\n\nStep three: Breathe out gently through your nose for about four seconds. Again, keep it soft and quiet. Your exhale should feel like a gentle sigh, not a forced push.\n\nStep four: After your exhale, pause. Hold your breath gently for three to five seconds. Do not pinch your nose or strain. Just wait calmly. You should feel a mild desire to breathe, like a gentle nudge, but nothing uncomfortable.\n\nStep five: Resume gentle nasal breathing. Breathe in softly, breathe out softly, pause again for three to five seconds. Continue this cycle for five minutes.\n\nIf at any point you feel short of breath or anxious, reduce the pause to two seconds or skip it entirely for a few breaths. This is not a competition. The goal is gentle control, not endurance.\n\nWhen to Practice\n\nThe ideal routine is twice daily: once in the morning shortly after waking, and once in the evening before bed. Morning practice sets a calm breathing pattern for the day. Evening practice helps you wind down and can improve sleep quality, which many asthmatics find challenging. You can also use a shortened version, just one to two minutes, before any activity that typically triggers your symptoms. For example, if you know that walking to the bus stop in the cold morning air usually makes you wheeze, do two minutes of Buteyko breathing before you leave the house.\n\nWhat You Might Notice in the First Week\n\nDuring the first few days, you might notice that your nose feels clearer and you breathe through it more naturally. Some people feel slightly lightheaded during the first two or three sessions, which is normal as your body adjusts to a different carbon dioxide level. This passes quickly. By the end of the first week, many people report that they feel calmer, their chest feels less tight in the mornings, and they reach for their reliever inhaler less often.\n\nWhat to Expect Over Time\n\nResearch studies have shown that people who practice Buteyko breathing regularly for several weeks experience a reduction in reliever inhaler use by up to 50 percent. This does not mean you should stop your preventer inhaler. Buteyko breathing works alongside your medication, not instead of it. Think of it as adding another tool to your asthma management kit. Over one to three months of consistent practice, you may notice fewer nighttime symptoms, easier breathing during mild exercise, and a greater overall sense of control over your condition.\n\nCombining Buteyko with Daily Life in Kenya\n\nMany of the triggers that Kenyan asthmatics face, such as dust on unpaved roads, cooking smoke, cold morning air in highland areas, and air pollution in Nairobi, cause airways to tighten. Buteyko breathing before exposure to these triggers can help your airways stay calmer. If you cook with charcoal or firewood, practice Buteyko breathing before you start cooking and make sure the cooking area is well ventilated. If you commute through dusty areas, do a quick one-minute session before you step outside. These small preparations add up to meaningful symptom reduction.\n\nKeeping Track of Your Progress\n\nKeep a simple record of your practice. In a small notebook or in your phone, note the date, whether you practiced morning, evening, or both, and how your breathing felt that day on a scale of one to five. Also note how many times you used your reliever inhaler. After two weeks, look at the trends. Most people see a clear improvement. Share this information with your doctor at your next appointment. It will help them understand your overall asthma control and may influence their treatment decisions.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"What Is Buteyko Breathing","body":"Buteyko breathing is a technique developed to help people who overbreathe, which is surprisingly common among people with asthma. When you overbreathe, or breathe faster and deeper than your body needs, you lose too much carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide is not just a waste gas. It actually helps keep your airways open and relaxed. When levels drop too low, your airways can tighten, triggering that familiar feeling of chest tightness or the urge to cough. Buteyko breathing retrains your breathing pattern to be slower, gentler, and more efficient. Over time, this can significantly reduce the frequency and severity of your asthma symptoms."},{"id":"s2","title":"Before You Begin","body":"Find a quiet spot where you will not be disturbed for about five to ten minutes. This could be your bedroom, a quiet corner of your sitting room, or even a peaceful spot in your garden or compound. Sit in a comfortable chair with your back supported. You can also sit cross-legged on a mat if that is more comfortable for you. Make sure your clothing is not tight around your chest or waist. Turn off the television and put your phone on silent. The more relaxed you are when you start, the more effective the practice will be."},{"id":"s3","title":"Step-by-Step Instructions","body":"Step one: Close your mouth gently. All breathing during this exercise should be through your nose. If your nose is blocked, try sitting in a steamy bathroom for a few minutes first, or gently press and release the sides of your nose to open it up.\\n\\nStep two: Breathe in gently through your nose for about three seconds. The breath should be light and quiet. You should not hear yourself breathing. Imagine you are trying to breathe so softly that a feather held under your nose would barely move.\\n\\nStep three: Breathe out gently through your nose for about four seconds. Again, keep it soft and quiet. Your exhale should feel like a gentle sigh, not a forced push.\\n\\nStep four: After your exhale, pause. Hold your breath gently for three to five seconds. Do not pinch your nose or strain. Just wait calmly. You should feel a mild desire to breathe, like a gentle nudge, but nothing uncomfortable.\\n\\nStep five: Resume gentle nasal breathing. Breathe in softly, breathe out softly, pause again for three to five seconds. Continue this cycle for five minutes.\\n\\nIf at any point you feel short of breath or anxious, reduce the pause to two seconds or skip it entirely for a few breaths. This is not a competition. The goal is gentle control, not endurance."},{"id":"s4","title":"When to Practice","body":"The ideal routine is twice daily: once in the morning shortly after waking, and once in the evening before bed. Morning practice sets a calm breathing pattern for the day. Evening practice helps you wind down and can improve sleep quality, which many asthmatics find challenging. You can also use a shortened version, just one to two minutes, before any activity that typically triggers your symptoms. For example, if you know that walking to the bus stop in the cold morning air usually makes you wheeze, do two minutes of Buteyko breathing before you leave the house."},{"id":"s5","title":"What You Might Notice in the First Week","body":"During the first few days, you might notice that your nose feels clearer and you breathe through it more naturally. Some people feel slightly lightheaded during the first two or three sessions, which is normal as your body adjusts to a different carbon dioxide level. This passes quickly. By the end of the first week, many people report that they feel calmer, their chest feels less tight in the mornings, and they reach for their reliever inhaler less often."},{"id":"s6","title":"What to Expect Over Time","body":"Research studies have shown that people who practice Buteyko breathing regularly for several weeks experience a reduction in reliever inhaler use by up to 50 percent. This does not mean you should stop your preventer inhaler. Buteyko breathing works alongside your medication, not instead of it. Think of it as adding another tool to your asthma management kit. Over one to three months of consistent practice, you may notice fewer nighttime symptoms, easier breathing during mild exercise, and a greater overall sense of control over your condition."},{"id":"s7","title":"Combining Buteyko with Daily Life in Kenya","body":"Many of the triggers that Kenyan asthmatics face, such as dust on unpaved roads, cooking smoke, cold morning air in highland areas, and air pollution in Nairobi, cause airways to tighten. Buteyko breathing before exposure to these triggers can help your airways stay calmer. If you cook with charcoal or firewood, practice Buteyko breathing before you start cooking and make sure the cooking area is well ventilated. If you commute through dusty areas, do a quick one-minute session before you step outside. These small preparations add up to meaningful symptom reduction."},{"id":"s8","title":"Keeping Track of Your Progress","body":"Keep a simple record of your practice. In a small notebook or in your phone, note the date, whether you practiced morning, evening, or both, and how your breathing felt that day on a scale of one to five. Also note how many times you used your reliever inhaler. After two weeks, look at the trends. Most people see a clear improvement. Share this information with your doctor at your next appointment. It will help them understand your overall asthma control and may influence their treatment decisions."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'yoga-pose'),
  (E'how-to-exercise-safely-with-asthma', E'How to exercise safely with asthma', E'ASTHMA', '{"ASTHMA"}', E'EXERCISE', E'If you have asthma, you may have been told at some point to avoid exercise. Perhaps a parent or teacher kept you on the sidelines during physical education at school.', E'Exercise Is Not the Enemy\n\nIf you have asthma, you may have been told at some point to avoid exercise. Perhaps a parent or teacher kept you on the sidelines during physical education at school. Perhaps you experienced a frightening episode while running and decided it was safer to stay still. But the truth is that regular exercise actually improves asthma control over time. It strengthens your respiratory muscles, improves your cardiovascular fitness, and helps maintain a healthy weight, all of which make breathing easier. The key is not avoiding exercise but learning how to do it safely.\n\nPrepare Before You Move\n\nAlways carry your reliever inhaler, the blue one, with you when you exercise. Take two puffs about 15 minutes before you begin your activity. This opens your airways in advance and creates a protective window of about two to four hours. Make sure your preventer inhaler is up to date. If you are not taking your preventer regularly, your airways are more inflamed than they should be, and exercise is more likely to trigger symptoms. Think of your preventer as the foundation and your reliever as the safety net.\n\nWarming Up Properly\n\nNever jump straight into intense activity. Start with a 10-minute warm-up of slow, gentle movement. Walk at a comfortable pace, swing your arms gently, and roll your shoulders. The purpose of warming up is to gradually increase your heart rate and breathing rate, giving your airways time to adjust. A sudden burst of hard breathing on cold or dry air is one of the most common exercise triggers for asthma. Warming up reduces this shock to your system.\n\nBest Types of Exercise for Asthma\n\nSwimming is widely considered the best exercise for people with asthma because the air above the pool is warm and humid, which is gentle on airways. If you have access to a pool at a gym, hotel, or community centre, take advantage of it. Walking is excellent and free. Aim for 30 minutes most days. You can walk around your estate, through a nearby park, or even around a school field. Cycling at a moderate pace is another good option. Team sports like football and basketball can work too, but they involve sudden sprints that may be more triggering, so make sure your asthma is well controlled before you play competitively.\n\nExercising in Kenya''s Climate\n\nKenya''s diverse climate affects how you should exercise. In Nairobi and the highlands, early mornings can be cold and misty. Cold air is a known trigger for exercise-induced asthma. If this affects you, exercise later in the morning once the sun has warmed the air, or exercise indoors. Wrap a light scarf loosely around your nose and mouth when walking in cold air to warm and humidify the air before it reaches your lungs. In warmer areas like Mombasa and Kisumu, heat and humidity can be an advantage for asthmatics, but avoid exercising during the hottest part of the day to prevent heat exhaustion. During the dry season, dust levels are higher. Choose routes that are paved or grassy rather than walking alongside busy, dusty roads. If the air quality is poor, as it sometimes is in Nairobi''s industrial areas, exercise indoors.\n\nRecognising Warning Signs During Exercise\n\nLearn to tell the difference between normal exercise breathlessness and asthma symptoms. It is completely normal to breathe harder when you are active. But if you notice wheezing, a tight feeling across your chest, coughing that does not stop, or difficulty getting a full breath, these are asthma warning signs. When this happens, do not stop abruptly. Slow down gradually to a gentle walk. Take two puffs of your reliever inhaler through a spacer if you have one. Sit down if needed and wait until your breathing returns to normal. If your symptoms do not improve within 10 to 15 minutes, seek medical help.\n\nA Sample Weekly Exercise Plan\n\nMonday: 30-minute brisk walk in the evening. Tuesday: 20-minute swimming session or 20 minutes of home stretching exercises. Wednesday: rest day or gentle 15-minute walk. Thursday: 30-minute brisk walk. Friday: 20 minutes of cycling or a home workout with bodyweight exercises like squats, lunges, and wall push-ups. Saturday: a longer walk of 40 to 45 minutes, perhaps exploring a new route. Sunday: rest or gentle yoga stretching. Adjust this plan based on how you feel. On days when your asthma is acting up, reduce the intensity or duration rather than skipping exercise entirely.\n\nCooling Down\n\nAlways end your exercise with a cool-down period of five to ten minutes. Gradually reduce your pace from brisk walking to slow walking, then to standing with gentle stretches. Stretch your calves, thighs, shoulders, and chest. This gradual transition prevents the sudden change in breathing rate that can trigger post-exercise symptoms. Many people find that skipping the cool-down is when problems start, so make it a non-negotiable part of your routine.\n\nBuilding Confidence Over Time\n\nIf you have avoided exercise for years because of your asthma, start very gently. Even five minutes of walking is a victory. Add one or two minutes each week. As your fitness improves, your asthma tolerance during exercise will improve too. Keep a log of your exercise and symptoms. You will likely see that as the weeks go by, you can do more with fewer symptoms. This progress is powerful motivation to keep going.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Exercise Is Not the Enemy","body":"If you have asthma, you may have been told at some point to avoid exercise. Perhaps a parent or teacher kept you on the sidelines during physical education at school. Perhaps you experienced a frightening episode while running and decided it was safer to stay still. But the truth is that regular exercise actually improves asthma control over time. It strengthens your respiratory muscles, improves your cardiovascular fitness, and helps maintain a healthy weight, all of which make breathing easier. The key is not avoiding exercise but learning how to do it safely."},{"id":"s2","title":"Prepare Before You Move","body":"Always carry your reliever inhaler, the blue one, with you when you exercise. Take two puffs about 15 minutes before you begin your activity. This opens your airways in advance and creates a protective window of about two to four hours. Make sure your preventer inhaler is up to date. If you are not taking your preventer regularly, your airways are more inflamed than they should be, and exercise is more likely to trigger symptoms. Think of your preventer as the foundation and your reliever as the safety net."},{"id":"s3","title":"Warming Up Properly","body":"Never jump straight into intense activity. Start with a 10-minute warm-up of slow, gentle movement. Walk at a comfortable pace, swing your arms gently, and roll your shoulders. The purpose of warming up is to gradually increase your heart rate and breathing rate, giving your airways time to adjust. A sudden burst of hard breathing on cold or dry air is one of the most common exercise triggers for asthma. Warming up reduces this shock to your system."},{"id":"s4","title":"Best Types of Exercise for Asthma","body":"Swimming is widely considered the best exercise for people with asthma because the air above the pool is warm and humid, which is gentle on airways. If you have access to a pool at a gym, hotel, or community centre, take advantage of it. Walking is excellent and free. Aim for 30 minutes most days. You can walk around your estate, through a nearby park, or even around a school field. Cycling at a moderate pace is another good option. Team sports like football and basketball can work too, but they involve sudden sprints that may be more triggering, so make sure your asthma is well controlled before you play competitively."},{"id":"s5","title":"Exercising in Kenya''s Climate","body":"Kenya''s diverse climate affects how you should exercise. In Nairobi and the highlands, early mornings can be cold and misty. Cold air is a known trigger for exercise-induced asthma. If this affects you, exercise later in the morning once the sun has warmed the air, or exercise indoors. Wrap a light scarf loosely around your nose and mouth when walking in cold air to warm and humidify the air before it reaches your lungs. In warmer areas like Mombasa and Kisumu, heat and humidity can be an advantage for asthmatics, but avoid exercising during the hottest part of the day to prevent heat exhaustion. During the dry season, dust levels are higher. Choose routes that are paved or grassy rather than walking alongside busy, dusty roads. If the air quality is poor, as it sometimes is in Nairobi''s industrial areas, exercise indoors."},{"id":"s6","title":"Recognising Warning Signs During Exercise","body":"Learn to tell the difference between normal exercise breathlessness and asthma symptoms. It is completely normal to breathe harder when you are active. But if you notice wheezing, a tight feeling across your chest, coughing that does not stop, or difficulty getting a full breath, these are asthma warning signs. When this happens, do not stop abruptly. Slow down gradually to a gentle walk. Take two puffs of your reliever inhaler through a spacer if you have one. Sit down if needed and wait until your breathing returns to normal. If your symptoms do not improve within 10 to 15 minutes, seek medical help."},{"id":"s7","title":"A Sample Weekly Exercise Plan","body":"Monday: 30-minute brisk walk in the evening. Tuesday: 20-minute swimming session or 20 minutes of home stretching exercises. Wednesday: rest day or gentle 15-minute walk. Thursday: 30-minute brisk walk. Friday: 20 minutes of cycling or a home workout with bodyweight exercises like squats, lunges, and wall push-ups. Saturday: a longer walk of 40 to 45 minutes, perhaps exploring a new route. Sunday: rest or gentle yoga stretching. Adjust this plan based on how you feel. On days when your asthma is acting up, reduce the intensity or duration rather than skipping exercise entirely."},{"id":"s8","title":"Cooling Down","body":"Always end your exercise with a cool-down period of five to ten minutes. Gradually reduce your pace from brisk walking to slow walking, then to standing with gentle stretches. Stretch your calves, thighs, shoulders, and chest. This gradual transition prevents the sudden change in breathing rate that can trigger post-exercise symptoms. Many people find that skipping the cool-down is when problems start, so make it a non-negotiable part of your routine."},{"id":"s9","title":"Building Confidence Over Time","body":"If you have avoided exercise for years because of your asthma, start very gently. Even five minutes of walking is a victory. Add one or two minutes each week. As your fitness improves, your asthma tolerance during exercise will improve too. Keep a log of your exercise and symptoms. You will likely see that as the weeks go by, you can do more with fewer symptoms. This progress is powerful motivation to keep going."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'morning-walk'),
  (E'myth-inhalers-are-addictive-and-make-asthma-worse', E'Myth: Inhalers are addictive and make asthma worse', E'ASTHMA', '{"ASTHMA"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'One of the most common things people say about inhalers in Kenya is that they are addictive. You may have heard this from a well-meaning relative, a neighbour, or even seen it discussed in social media groups.', E'The Myth\n\nOne of the most common things people say about inhalers in Kenya is that they are addictive. You may have heard this from a well-meaning relative, a neighbour, or even seen it discussed in social media groups. The claim goes something like this: once you start using an inhaler, your body becomes dependent on it and you can never stop. Some people go further and say that inhalers actually make your asthma worse over time. Because of this belief, many Kenyans with asthma avoid using their inhalers, use them irregularly, or stop them altogether. This is one of the most dangerous myths in respiratory health.\n\nThe Truth About Preventer Inhalers\n\nPreventer inhalers, which are usually brown, orange, or purple, contain a low dose of inhaled corticosteroids. These steroids are very different from the bodybuilding steroids that people worry about. Inhaled corticosteroids work directly inside your airways. They reduce the swelling and inflammation that makes your airways narrow and sensitive. When you use your preventer inhaler every day, your airways gradually become calmer, less swollen, and less likely to react to triggers like dust, cold air, or smoke. The medication does not create dependency. What it creates is a controlled, less inflamed airway. If you stop taking it, the inflammation returns, not because of addiction, but because the underlying condition is still there. Asthma is a chronic condition. The inhaler manages it, just like blood pressure medication manages hypertension.\n\nThe Truth About Reliever Inhalers\n\nReliever inhalers, usually blue, contain a medication called salbutamol. This works by quickly relaxing the muscles around your airways, opening them up within minutes. It is used for emergencies, when you feel tight, wheezy, or short of breath. Salbutamol is not addictive. It does not create cravings or withdrawal symptoms. If you feel like you need your reliever inhaler frequently, that is not addiction. It is a sign that your asthma is not well controlled and you need to talk to your doctor about adjusting your preventer medication.\n\nWhy This Myth Is So Dangerous\n\nWhen people believe inhalers are addictive, they stop using their preventer medication. Within days or weeks, the inflammation inside their airways comes back. Their symptoms worsen. They have more attacks. They end up in emergency rooms at Kenyatta National Hospital, Aga Khan, or their local county hospital, needing nebulisers, injections, and sometimes even admission. Emergency treatment for a severe asthma attack can cost between KES 5,000 and KES 50,000 depending on the hospital and the severity. A preventer inhaler that prevents these emergencies costs between KES 500 and KES 2,000 per month. The financial argument alone is compelling, but the real cost of the myth is measured in lives. People die from asthma attacks in Kenya every year, and many of those deaths were preventable if the patient had been using their preventer inhaler consistently.\n\nWhat About the Steroids\n\nThe word steroid frightens many people. They think of the side effects they have heard about: weight gain, moon face, weakened bones. These side effects are associated with oral steroids taken in high doses for long periods. Inhaled steroids are delivered in tiny doses directly to your airways. Very little of the medication enters your bloodstream. At the standard doses used for asthma, the risk of significant systemic side effects is very low. Some people experience mild hoarseness or oral thrush, which can be prevented by rinsing your mouth with water after each use. The small risk of these mild side effects is far outweighed by the benefit of controlled asthma.\n\nTalking to Family Members Who Believe the Myth\n\nIf your family members or friends are pressuring you to stop your inhaler because they believe it is addictive, approach the conversation with patience. Explain that you have discussed it with your doctor and that the medication is safe and necessary. Invite them to come with you to your next appointment so the doctor can explain directly. Sometimes hearing it from a medical professional makes the difference. You can also point out the practical evidence: when you use your inhaler regularly, you have fewer attacks and feel better. When you stop, you feel worse. That is not addiction. That is effective treatment for a chronic condition.\n\nThe Bottom Line\n\nYour inhaler is not your enemy. It is your ally. Using it as prescribed is the single most important thing you can do to keep your asthma under control. If you need your reliever (blue) inhaler more than twice a week, or if you wake up at night with breathing difficulties more than twice a month, your asthma is not well controlled. Visit your doctor and discuss whether your preventer medication needs to be adjusted. Well-controlled asthma means you should be able to live a full, active life with minimal interruptions from symptoms.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The Myth","body":"One of the most common things people say about inhalers in Kenya is that they are addictive. You may have heard this from a well-meaning relative, a neighbour, or even seen it discussed in social media groups. The claim goes something like this: once you start using an inhaler, your body becomes dependent on it and you can never stop. Some people go further and say that inhalers actually make your asthma worse over time. Because of this belief, many Kenyans with asthma avoid using their inhalers, use them irregularly, or stop them altogether. This is one of the most dangerous myths in respiratory health."},{"id":"s2","t