t between KES 5,000 and KES 50,000 depending on the hospital and the severity. A preventer inhaler that prevents these emergencies costs between KES 500 and KES 2,000 per month. The financial argument alone is compelling, but the real cost of the myth is measured in lives. People die from asthma attacks in Kenya every year, and many of those deaths were preventable if the patient had been using their preventer inhaler consistently."},{"id":"s5","title":"What About the Steroids","body":"The word steroid frightens many people. They think of the side effects they have heard about: weight gain, moon face, weakened bones. These side effects are associated with oral steroids taken in high doses for long periods. Inhaled steroids are delivered in tiny doses directly to your airways. Very little of the medication enters your bloodstream. At the standard doses used for asthma, the risk of significant systemic side effects is very low. Some people experience mild hoarseness or oral thrush, which can be prevented by rinsing your mouth with water after each use. The small risk of these mild side effects is far outweighed by the benefit of controlled asthma."},{"id":"s6","title":"Talking to Family Members Who Believe the Myth","body":"If your family members or friends are pressuring you to stop your inhaler because they believe it is addictive, approach the conversation with patience. Explain that you have discussed it with your doctor and that the medication is safe and necessary. Invite them to come with you to your next appointment so the doctor can explain directly. Sometimes hearing it from a medical professional makes the difference. You can also point out the practical evidence: when you use your inhaler regularly, you have fewer attacks and feel better. When you stop, you feel worse. That is not addiction. That is effective treatment for a chronic condition."},{"id":"s7","title":"The Bottom Line","body":"Your inhaler is not your enemy. It is your ally. Using it as prescribed is the single most important thing you can do to keep your asthma under control. If you need your reliever (blue) inhaler more than twice a week, or if you wake up at night with breathing difficulties more than twice a month, your asthma is not well controlled. Visit your doctor and discuss whether your preventer medication needs to be adjusted. Well-controlled asthma means you should be able to live a full, active life with minimal interruptions from symptoms."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'fact-check'),
  (E'the-anxiety-that-comes-with-breathing-difficulties', E'The anxiety that comes with breathing difficulties', E'ASTHMA', '{"ASTHMA"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'There are few things more terrifying than feeling like you cannot breathe. For people living with asthma in Kenya, this fear is not abstract or theoretical.', E'When Breathing Becomes Frightening\n\nThere are few things more terrifying than feeling like you cannot breathe. For people living with asthma in Kenya, this fear is not abstract or theoretical. It is a memory of a real moment, maybe in the middle of the night when the house was quiet and your chest suddenly tightened. Maybe it happened while you were walking along a dusty road near a construction site. Maybe it happened at work, in front of colleagues, and you felt both the physical panic of breathlessness and the social embarrassment of gasping for air in public. These experiences leave a mark. Even when your asthma is well controlled, the memory of those moments can create an undercurrent of anxiety that follows you throughout your day.\n\nThe Anxiety-Asthma Cycle\n\nAnxiety and asthma feed each other in a vicious cycle. When you feel anxious, your body responds by tensing muscles, including the muscles around your chest. Your breathing becomes fast and shallow. Your heart rate increases. These physical changes mimic the early signs of an asthma attack, which makes you more anxious, which makes your breathing worse. In some cases, pure anxiety can trigger what feels like a full asthma episode, even when your airways are not inflamed. In other cases, real asthma symptoms are amplified by the panic that accompanies them. Understanding this cycle is the first step to breaking it.\n\nThe 4-7-8 Breathing Technique\n\nWhen you feel mild tightness or the beginnings of anxiety about your breathing, try the 4-7-8 technique before reaching for your inhaler. Here is how it works:\n\nSit down if possible. If you are standing, lean against a wall or hold onto something stable.\n\nBreathe in slowly through your nose for a count of four. Count slowly: one, two, three, four.\n\nHold your breath gently for a count of seven. You are not straining. You are pausing.\n\nBreathe out slowly through pursed lips, like you are blowing through a straw, for a count of eight. Let the air flow out slowly and steadily.\n\nRepeat this cycle three to four times.\n\nThis technique works because the long exhale activates your vagus nerve, which tells your body to calm down. It slows your heart rate, relaxes your chest muscles, and interrupts the panic cycle. If after doing this you still feel short of breath, then use your reliever inhaler. But many people find that the 4-7-8 technique alone resolves anxiety-related tightness.\n\nReal Scenarios Kenyan Patients Face\n\nConsider Wanjiku, a teacher in Nyeri. She had a severe asthma attack in class two years ago. The students panicked, a colleague called an ambulance, and she spent two days at the hospital. She recovered fully, but now every time she feels the slightest tightness in her chest, she is flooded with the memory of that day. She has started avoiding the dusty staff room, declined an invitation to a school trip to a game park, and finds herself checking her bag multiple times to make sure her inhaler is there. Her asthma is well controlled now, but her anxiety about asthma is not.\n\nOr consider James, a boda boda rider in Kisumu. He cannot afford to stop working, but riding through traffic and dust regularly makes his chest tight. He worries about having an attack while riding and causing an accident. The worry itself makes him breathe faster, which makes his chest feel tighter, which makes him worry more.\n\nThese are not unusual stories. They are common, and they are valid.\n\nCoping Strategies That Work\n\nFirst, name the fear. Simply saying to yourself, "I am feeling anxious about my breathing right now," can reduce its power. Anxiety thrives in the shadows. When you acknowledge it, it becomes something you are experiencing, not something that defines you.\n\nSecond, have a plan. Knowing exactly what to do during an episode reduces panic. Your asthma action plan, written with your doctor, should specify what to do when symptoms are mild, moderate, or severe. Keep a copy on your phone and on your wall at home.\n\nThird, stay active. Regular exercise, done safely with your inhaler protocol, actually reduces anxiety over time. Walking, swimming, or gentle yoga can all help.\n\nFourth, talk to someone. This could be a friend, a family member, a support group, or a professional counsellor. Many Kenyan hospitals and clinics now have mental health services. Through the Jireh app, you may also be connected to support resources in your area.\n\nWhen to Seek Professional Help\n\nIf anxiety about your asthma is causing you to avoid normal activities, if you are having trouble sleeping because of worry, if you find yourself in a constant state of vigilance about your breathing, or if you are experiencing panic attacks, it is time to talk to a professional. Your doctor can refer you to a counsellor or psychologist. Cognitive behavioural therapy, which helps you recognise and change unhelpful thought patterns, is particularly effective for health-related anxiety. Some people also benefit from short-term medication to help manage anxiety while they develop coping skills. There is no shame in getting help. Managing the emotional side of asthma is just as important as managing the physical side.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"When Breathing Becomes Frightening","body":"There are few things more terrifying than feeling like you cannot breathe. For people living with asthma in Kenya, this fear is not abstract or theoretical. It is a memory of a real moment, maybe in the middle of the night when the house was quiet and your chest suddenly tightened. Maybe it happened while you were walking along a dusty road near a construction site. Maybe it happened at work, in front of colleagues, and you felt both the physical panic of breathlessness and the social embarrassment of gasping for air in public. These experiences leave a mark. Even when your asthma is well controlled, the memory of those moments can create an undercurrent of anxiety that follows you throughout your day."},{"id":"s2","title":"The Anxiety-Asthma Cycle","body":"Anxiety and asthma feed each other in a vicious cycle. When you feel anxious, your body responds by tensing muscles, including the muscles around your chest. Your breathing becomes fast and shallow. Your heart rate increases. These physical changes mimic the early signs of an asthma attack, which makes you more anxious, which makes your breathing worse. In some cases, pure anxiety can trigger what feels like a full asthma episode, even when your airways are not inflamed. In other cases, real asthma symptoms are amplified by the panic that accompanies them. Understanding this cycle is the first step to breaking it."},{"id":"s3","title":"The 4-7-8 Breathing Technique","body":"When you feel mild tightness or the beginnings of anxiety about your breathing, try the 4-7-8 technique before reaching for your inhaler. Here is how it works:\\n\\nSit down if possible. If you are standing, lean against a wall or hold onto something stable.\\n\\nBreathe in slowly through your nose for a count of four. Count slowly: one, two, three, four.\\n\\nHold your breath gently for a count of seven. You are not straining. You are pausing.\\n\\nBreathe out slowly through pursed lips, like you are blowing through a straw, for a count of eight. Let the air flow out slowly and steadily.\\n\\nRepeat this cycle three to four times.\\n\\nThis technique works because the long exhale activates your vagus nerve, which tells your body to calm down. It slows your heart rate, relaxes your chest muscles, and interrupts the panic cycle. If after doing this you still feel short of breath, then use your reliever inhaler. But many people find that the 4-7-8 technique alone resolves anxiety-related tightness."},{"id":"s4","title":"Real Scenarios Kenyan Patients Face","body":"Consider Wanjiku, a teacher in Nyeri. She had a severe asthma attack in class two years ago. The students panicked, a colleague called an ambulance, and she spent two days at the hospital. She recovered fully, but now every time she feels the slightest tightness in her chest, she is flooded with the memory of that day. She has started avoiding the dusty staff room, declined an invitation to a school trip to a game park, and finds herself checking her bag multiple times to make sure her inhaler is there. Her asthma is well controlled now, but her anxiety about asthma is not.\\n\\nOr consider James, a boda boda rider in Kisumu. He cannot afford to stop working, but riding through traffic and dust regularly makes his chest tight. He worries about having an attack while riding and causing an accident. The worry itself makes him breathe faster, which makes his chest feel tighter, which makes him worry more.\\n\\nThese are not unusual stories. They are common, and they are valid."},{"id":"s5","title":"Coping Strategies That Work","body":"First, name the fear. Simply saying to yourself, \\"I am feeling anxious about my breathing right now,\\" can reduce its power. Anxiety thrives in the shadows. When you acknowledge it, it becomes something you are experiencing, not something that defines you.\\n\\nSecond, have a plan. Knowing exactly what to do during an episode reduces panic. Your asthma action plan, written with your doctor, should specify what to do when symptoms are mild, moderate, or severe. Keep a copy on your phone and on your wall at home.\\n\\nThird, stay active. Regular exercise, done safely with your inhaler protocol, actually reduces anxiety over time. Walking, swimming, or gentle yoga can all help.\\n\\nFourth, talk to someone. This could be a friend, a family member, a support group, or a professional counsellor. Many Kenyan hospitals and clinics now have mental health services. Through the Jireh app, you may also be connected to support resources in your area."},{"id":"s6","title":"When to Seek Professional Help","body":"If anxiety about your asthma is causing you to avoid normal activities, if you are having trouble sleeping because of worry, if you find yourself in a constant state of vigilance about your breathing, or if you are experiencing panic attacks, it is time to talk to a professional. Your doctor can refer you to a counsellor or psychologist. Cognitive behavioural therapy, which helps you recognise and change unhelpful thought patterns, is particularly effective for health-related anxiety. Some people also benefit from short-term medication to help manage anxiety while they develop coping skills. There is no shame in getting help. Managing the emotional side of asthma is just as important as managing the physical side."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'peaceful-moment'),
  (E'tracking-your-triggers-in-a-simple-diary', E'Tracking your triggers in a simple diary', E'ASTHMA', '{"ASTHMA"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'Every person with asthma has a unique set of triggers, the things in their environment that cause their airways to tighten, swell, or produce excess mucus.', E'Why Tracking Triggers Matters\n\nEvery person with asthma has a unique set of triggers, the things in their environment that cause their airways to tighten, swell, or produce excess mucus. What triggers your neighbour''s asthma may not affect you at all, and something that does not bother most people might be a major problem for your airways. The only way to identify your personal triggers with certainty is to track them systematically. A trigger diary is a simple, powerful tool that transforms vague feelings of "my asthma has been bad lately" into specific, actionable knowledge like "my asthma gets worse when I cook with charcoal in an enclosed space."\n\nWhat You Need to Get Started\n\nYou do not need anything fancy. A small exercise book, the kind that costs KES 30 to KES 50 at any bookshop or supermarket, works perfectly. Alternatively, use the notes app on your phone. The key is that it should be something you always have with you. If you choose a notebook, keep it with your inhaler so you are reminded to write in it every time you use your medication. Label the first page with your name, your doctor''s name and phone number, and the date you started tracking.\n\nWhat to Record Each Day\n\nEvery day, whether you have symptoms or not, write down a few things. First, the date and the general weather: was it cold, hot, dusty, rainy, or humid? Second, how your breathing felt overall that day on a simple scale: good, okay, or bad. Third, any symptoms you experienced: wheezing, coughing, chest tightness, shortness of breath, waking up at night. Fourth, any time you used your reliever inhaler and how many puffs. Fifth, and this is the most important part, note what was happening around the time your symptoms started. Where were you? What were you doing? What were you near?\n\nCommon Triggers to Watch For in Kenya\n\nDust is one of the most common asthma triggers in Kenya. Unpaved roads, construction sites, and dry weather all increase dust exposure. If you live near a major road or a building site, your trigger diary will likely show a pattern of symptoms on windy, dry days.\n\nCooking smoke is another major trigger. Many households in Kenya still cook with charcoal (jiko), firewood, or kerosene. If your symptoms are worse in the evening, around cooking time, smoke is a likely trigger. Note whether you were cooking indoors or outdoors, what fuel you used, and how well ventilated the space was.\n\nCold air, especially in highland areas like Nairobi, Nyeri, Eldoret, and Kericho, triggers asthma in many people. Morning symptoms may be related to cold air exposure when you leave your house or walk to work.\n\nStrong smells from perfumes, cleaning products like bleach and Jik, insecticide sprays like Doom, fresh paint, or petrol fumes can trigger sensitive airways.\n\nMould grows in damp areas of the home, especially during the rainy seasons. Check your bathroom, kitchen corners, and around window frames. If your symptoms worsen during the long rains (March to May) or short rains (October to December), mould may be a factor.\n\nPets, particularly cats, can trigger asthma through their dander (tiny skin flakes). If your symptoms are worse when you visit a friend who has pets, note that in your diary.\n\nEmotional stress and strong emotions, including laughter, can trigger asthma in some people. If you notice symptoms during stressful periods at work or at home, record the connection.\n\nExercise-induced symptoms are also common. Note what type of exercise you were doing, how intense it was, and whether you used your reliever inhaler beforehand.\n\nHow Patterns Emerge\n\nAfter two weeks of consistent tracking, sit down with your diary and look for patterns. Highlight or circle the entries where you had symptoms. Then look at the context around each highlighted entry. You might discover that every time you swept the house without wetting the floor first, you wheezed. Or that your symptoms are consistently worse on days when you walk to work in the cold morning air. Or that they flare up whenever you visit your mother''s house where she keeps cats. These patterns are gold. They give you specific, practical information you can act on.\n\nTurning Patterns into Action\n\nOnce you know your triggers, you can develop strategies to avoid or reduce exposure. If dust is a trigger, dampen the floor before sweeping, or switch to a damp mop. Wear a cloth mask when walking on dusty roads. If cooking smoke is the problem, cook outdoors when possible, improve ventilation by opening windows, or switch to gas or electric cooking if you can afford to. The government''s clean cooking initiatives and LPG subsidies have made gas more accessible in many areas. If cold air triggers your symptoms, wear a light scarf over your nose and mouth during cold mornings. If cleaning products are a problem, switch to fragrance-free alternatives or have someone else do the cleaning while you stay in another room.\n\nSharing Your Diary with Your Doctor\n\nYour trigger diary is one of the most valuable things you can bring to a doctor''s appointment. It gives your doctor real-world data about your asthma that they cannot get from a single clinic visit. With this information, they can adjust your treatment more precisely. For example, if your diary shows that you need your reliever inhaler most often in the morning, your doctor might adjust the timing or dosage of your preventer medication. If it shows that your symptoms are mainly triggered by allergens, they might recommend allergy testing or an antihistamine.\n\nEncouragement for the Journey\n\nTracking might feel tedious at first, like extra homework. But think of it this way: your trigger diary is your personalised asthma manual. No textbook or website can tell you exactly what triggers your asthma, because it is unique to you, your home, your neighbourhood, and your daily routine. You are the only one who can gather this information. The Jireh app also lets you log health data digitally for your care team to review. Whatever method you choose, commit to recording daily for at least two to four weeks. Once you have that data, you hold the key to better control, fewer attacks, and a more confident life.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Why Tracking Triggers Matters","body":"Every person with asthma has a unique set of triggers, the things in their environment that cause their airways to tighten, swell, or produce excess mucus. What triggers your neighbour''s asthma may not affect you at all, and something that does not bother most people might be a major problem for your airways. The only way to identify your personal triggers with certainty is to track them systematically. A trigger diary is a simple, powerful tool that transforms vague feelings of \\"my asthma has been bad lately\\" into specific, actionable knowledge like \\"my asthma gets worse when I cook with charcoal in an enclosed space.\\""},{"id":"s2","title":"What You Need to Get Started","body":"You do not need anything fancy. A small exercise book, the kind that costs KES 30 to KES 50 at any bookshop or supermarket, works perfectly. Alternatively, use the notes app on your phone. The key is that it should be something you always have with you. If you choose a notebook, keep it with your inhaler so you are reminded to write in it every time you use your medication. Label the first page with your name, your doctor''s name and phone number, and the date you started tracking."},{"id":"s3","title":"What to Record Each Day","body":"Every day, whether you have symptoms or not, write down a few things. First, the date and the general weather: was it cold, hot, dusty, rainy, or humid? Second, how your