ext Goal","body":"Now that you have achieved one month, set your sights on three months of no reliever use. At three months of well-controlled asthma, the evidence shows even more significant improvements in lung function and quality of life. Some doctors may review your treatment at the three-month mark and, if appropriate, consider stepping down the dose of your preventer inhaler. This is not something you should do on your own, but it is a conversation worth having with your doctor. It means the possibility of maintaining control with less medication, which is a wonderful position to be in."},{"id":"s8","title":"Celebrate and Share","body":"Tell someone about this achievement. Tell your doctor at your next visit. Tell a friend. If you are part of a Jireh Circle, share it with your group. Your success is not just personal. It is proof to everyone around you that asthma can be well managed. In a country where many people still believe that inhalers are addictive or that asthma cannot be controlled, your experience is a powerful counter-narrative. You are living proof that the right medication, taken consistently, combined with trigger awareness and practical adjustments, leads to a life where asthma takes a back seat. Keep going. The best is yet to come."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'celebration'),
  (E'eating-well-during-cancer-treatment-when-nothing-tastes-right', E'Eating well during cancer treatment when nothing tastes right', E'CANCER', '{"CANCER"}', E'DIETARY', E'One of the most common and frustrating side effects of cancer treatment is changes in how food tastes. Chemotherapy and radiation can damage the taste buds on your tongue and alter the way your brain processes flavour signals.', E'When Food Stops Tasting Like Food\n\nOne of the most common and frustrating side effects of cancer treatment is changes in how food tastes. Chemotherapy and radiation can damage the taste buds on your tongue and alter the way your brain processes flavour signals. Food that you once loved may suddenly taste metallic, bitter, overly sweet, or like nothing at all. Meat might taste like cardboard. Water might taste strange. Your favourite cup of chai might seem off. This is not in your imagination and it is not your fault. It happens to a large number of cancer patients, and while it is temporary for most people, it can last for weeks or months during and after treatment. The challenge is getting enough nutrition into your body during a time when your body needs it most.\n\nWhy Nutrition Matters During Treatment\n\nYour body is fighting cancer and dealing with the effects of treatment at the same time. Chemotherapy targets fast-growing cells, which includes cancer cells but also healthy cells in your gut lining, blood, and immune system. To rebuild these healthy cells, your body needs protein, calories, vitamins, and minerals. Poor nutrition during treatment can lead to weakness, slower healing, more frequent infections, and longer hospital stays. You do not need a perfect diet. You need to get something nutritious in, even on your worst days.\n\nSmall Meals Throughout the Day\n\nWhen your appetite is poor and food tastes wrong, three large meals can feel impossible. Instead, try eating five to six small portions spread throughout the day. Think of it as snacking with purpose. A few spoonfuls of porridge at 7 a.m. A boiled egg at 10 a.m. A small bowl of light soup at noon. A banana and some groundnuts at 3 p.m. A small plate of githeri at 6 p.m. A cup of warm milk before bed. None of these portions is large, but together they add up to meaningful nutrition. The goal is to eat something every two to three hours, even if it is just a few bites.\n\nCold Foods Are Often Easier\n\nHot foods tend to have stronger smells, and when your taste is distorted, strong smells can make nausea worse. Many patients find that cold or room-temperature foods are much easier to tolerate. Cold githeri, which many Kenyan families prepare in large batches, is an excellent option because it is protein-rich from the beans and energy-rich from the maize. Yoghurt is gentle on the stomach and provides protein and probiotics. Fresh fruit, served cold from the fridge, can be refreshing and easier to eat than cooked food. Cold slices of watermelon, pawpaw, or pineapple can also help with the metallic taste that many patients experience.\n\nProtein When Meat Tastes Strange\n\nProtein is essential during cancer treatment because it helps repair tissues and maintain muscle mass. But meat is often the first food to taste wrong during chemotherapy. If chicken, beef, or fish taste metallic or unpleasant, switch to other protein sources. Eggs are versatile and mild. A boiled egg, scrambled eggs, or an omelette with tomatoes and onions can provide good protein without a strong flavour. Beans, lentils, and chickpeas are staples in Kenyan cooking and are excellent protein sources. Ndengu (green grams) cooked with a little coconut milk is gentle and nutritious. Groundnuts and groundnut paste (peanut butter) provide both protein and healthy fats. Soy products like soy milk can also be good alternatives.\n\nManaging Nausea with Local Remedies\n\nNausea is one of the most common side effects of chemotherapy. Ginger is a natural and widely available anti-nausea remedy. Make ginger tea by boiling fresh ginger slices in water for five to ten minutes, then add a squeeze of lemon and a little honey. Sip it slowly, especially in the morning or about 30 minutes before a meal. Sucking on a small piece of lemon can also help reduce nausea and cut through the metallic taste. Some patients find that peppermint tea helps settle their stomach. Avoid cooking or being near the kitchen when food is being prepared, as cooking smells can trigger nausea. Ask a family member or friend to prepare your meals and let the food cool before you eat.\n\nKenyan Meals That Work Well During Treatment\n\nUji (porridge) made from millet, sorghum, or a mix of flours is gentle, warm, and easy to digest. You can enrich it with milk, groundnut paste, or a spoonful of honey for extra calories and protein. Light chicken broth or bone soup provides hydration, minerals, and some protein without being heavy. Mashed sweet potatoes or arrowroot are starchy comfort foods that are easy to swallow and provide energy. Mashed bananas mixed with a little yoghurt make a simple snack. Avocado, mashed or sliced, provides healthy fats and is bland enough that it rarely triggers taste problems. Plain chapati, soft and fresh, can be eaten with a light lentil soup. Avoid very spicy foods, heavily fried foods like deep-fried mandazi, and foods with strong smells.\n\nStaying Hydrated\n\nDehydration is a serious risk during cancer treatment, especially if you are experiencing vomiting or diarrhoea. Aim to drink at least eight glasses of fluid a day. If plain water tastes odd, add a slice of lemon, cucumber, or a few mint leaves. Coconut water provides natural electrolytes. Light soups count toward your fluid intake. If you cannot keep any food or water down for more than 24 hours or you are losing weight rapidly, contact your doctor immediately. Hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital, Aga Khan, and Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital have nutrition departments that can create personalised meal plans for cancer patients.\n\nGive Yourself Grace\n\nThere is no perfect way to eat during cancer treatment. Some days you will manage a full plate of food. Other days, a few bites of banana and a cup of tea will be all you can handle. Both are okay. The goal is not perfection. The goal is persistence. Keep trying different foods, different temperatures, different times of day. Your taste will eventually return, often within a few weeks to months after treatment ends. In the meantime, focus on getting any nutrition in, and be kind to yourself on the hard days.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"When Food Stops Tasting Like Food","body":"One of the most common and frustrating side effects of cancer treatment is changes in how food tastes. Chemotherapy and radiation can damage the taste buds on your tongue and alter the way your brain processes flavour signals. Food that you once loved may suddenly taste metallic, bitter, overly sweet, or like nothing at all. Meat might taste like cardboard. Water might taste strange. Your favourite cup of chai might seem off. This is not in your imagination and it is not your fault. It happens to a large number of cancer patients, and while it is temporary for most people, it can last for weeks or months during and after treatment. The challenge is getting enough nutrition into your body during a time when your body needs it most."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why Nutrition Matters During Treatment","body":"Your body is fighting cancer and dealing with the effects of treatment at the same time. Chemotherapy targets fast-growing cells, which includes cancer cells but also healthy cells in your gut lining, blood, and immune system. To rebuild these healthy cells, your body needs protein, calories, vitamins, and minerals. Poor nutrition during treatment can lead to weakness, slower healing, more frequent infections, and longer hospital stays. You do not need a perfect diet. You need to get something nutritious in, even on your worst days."},{"id":"s3","ti