\\" Being specific makes it easier for people to help."},{"id":"s5","title":"Talking to Children","body":"If you have children, how you tell them depends on their age. Very young children, under five, need only simple, reassuring information: \\"Mummy/Daddy is sick and is getting medicine from the doctor. Sometimes I will be tired, but the doctors are helping me.\\" Children between five and twelve understand more and may have questions. Be honest in an age-appropriate way: \\"I have a sickness called cancer. It is not the same as a cold or malaria. The doctors have a plan to help me, and I am going to follow it.\\" Teenagers can handle more detail and should be given it, as they will sense if you are holding back and may imagine worse than reality.\\n\\nAll children need to hear three things: this is not their fault, they are still loved, and the family will get through this together. If you notice a child becoming withdrawn, acting out, or their school performance dropping, consider asking a school counsellor or family friend to talk with them."},{"id":"s6","title":"When Family Members React Poorly","body":"Not everyone will respond the way you hope. Some family members may react with denial, insisting that you cannot have cancer or suggesting that the doctors are wrong. Others may immediately start recommending alternative treatments, herbal remedies, or faith healing, not from malice but from their own fear and desire to fix the situation. Some may pull away, not because they do not care but because they do not know how to handle it.\\n\\nBe patient with these reactions. They are not about you. They are about each person processing their own fear and grief. Give people time. Most will come around once the initial shock passes. For those who suggest abandoning medical treatment for unproven alternatives, be firm but gentle: \\"I appreciate your concern, and I am open to supportive remedies alongside my medical treatment, but I am going to follow my doctor''s plan