ecognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'family-support'),
  (E'finding-purpose-in-the-midst-of-treatment', E'Finding purpose in the midst of treatment', E'CANCER', '{"CANCER"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'Before a cancer diagnosis, most of us live as though time is unlimited. We postpone the trip to visit our parents in shags.', E'When Time Takes on a New Meaning\n\nBefore a cancer diagnosis, most of us live as though time is unlimited. We postpone the trip to visit our parents in shags. We tell ourselves we will spend more time with the children next month. We put off the conversation, the rest, the gratitude. A cancer diagnosis shatters that illusion. Suddenly, time is no longer something you have in abundance. It is something you measure, guard, and spend with intention. This shift can be frightening at first, but many people who have walked this path describe it as one of the most profound and ultimately positive transformations of their lives. Cancer does not just change your body. It changes your relationship with everything.\n\nThe First Weeks After Diagnosis\n\nThe initial period after diagnosis is often the most turbulent. Your mind races between practicalities, who will take the children to school during treatment, how will we pay for this, and existential questions, what if the treatment does not work, what will my family do without me. It is common to feel like you are watching your life from outside your body, going through the motions while your mind struggles to catch up. This is shock, and it is a normal psychological response to overwhelming news. During this time, be gentle with yourself. You do not need to have a plan for everything immediately. You do not need to be brave or positive or strong. You just need to get through one day at a time.\n\nThe Shift from Why Me to What Now\n\nAt some point, and it happens at different times for different people, a subtle shift occurs. The question changes from "Why did this happen to me?" to "What am I going to do about it?" This is not a moment of acceptance so much as a turning of the page. It does not mean the grief and anger disappear. They may surface again and again throughout treatment. But alongside them, a quieter voice emerges: the voice of agency, of purpose, of determination to make the most of whatever time you have, whether that is decades or something less.\n\nRediscovering What Truly Matters\n\nCancer has a way of stripping away the unnecessary and revealing the essential. The promotion you were chasing may suddenly seem less important than an afternoon with your children in the garden. The social obligations you dreaded may fall away, leaving space for the people and activities that genuinely nourish you. Many cancer patients in Kenya describe rediscovering simple pleasures they had forgotten: the taste of fresh mango from the roadside, the sound of birds in the morning, the warmth of sitting with a friend over a cup of chai without checking the clock. This is not sentimentality. It is a recalibration of values that many people spend their whole lives searching for. Cancer, in its terrible way, can deliver it.\n\nGiving Yourself Permission to Rest\n\nIn Kenyan culture, productivity is deeply valued. We admire the hustle, the early mornings, the multiple jobs, the relentless drive. Resting can feel like failure. But during cancer treatment, rest is not optional. It is medicine. Your body is engaged in one of the hardest fights of its life. Every nap, every quiet afternoon, every early bedtime is an investment in your recovery. Give yourself permission to rest without guilt. If you find it hard, try reframing it: you are not doing nothing. You are healing. You are giving your body the energy it needs to fight. That is one of the most productive things you can possibly do.\n\nLetting Others Help\n\nMany people, especially those who have spent their lives taking care of others, find it incredibly difficult to accept help. If you are the anchor caregiver in your family, the one who holds everything together, receiving care instead of giving it can feel deeply uncomfortable. But this is the time to let others step in. When your sister offers to cook, say yes. When your neighbour offers to drive you to the hospital, say yes. When your Jireh Circle offers financial support, accept it with gratitude. Accepting help is not weakness. It is wisdom. It preserves your energy for healing and allows the people who love you to express their care in a tangible way. Most people want to help. Giving them the opportunity is a gift to them as well as to yourself.\n\nMaintaining Routine and Normalcy\n\nWhile some things in your life will inevitably change during treatment, maintaining elements of your normal routine can be deeply grounding. If you always had morning tea on your balcony, continue doing so. If you enjoyed a particular radio programme or podcast, keep listening. If going to church, mosque, or temple brought you peace, attend when you feel well enough or participate remotely if your place of worship offers that option. These threads of normalcy are anchors in a sea of change. They remind you that while cancer is part of your life now, it has not replaced your entire life.\n\nFinding Purpose During Treatment\n\nPurpose does not have to be grand. During treatment, purpose might be as simple as being present for your child''s homework. It might be calling a friend who is also going through a difficult time. It might be keeping a journal of your experiences, which many patients find therapeutic. It might be learning something new, a language, a skill, a hobby that you always wanted to try. Some patients find purpose in advocacy, sharing their story to encourage others to get screened or to seek treatment without delay. Whatever form it takes, purpose gives structure to your days and a reason to keep moving forward.\n\nThe Role of Faith and Spirituality\n\nFor many Kenyans, faith is a central pillar of coping with illness. Whether you find comfort in prayer, meditation, scripture, or the community of a faith group, lean into what gives you peace. Spiritual support has been shown to improve quality of life and emotional well-being during cancer treatment. At the same time, faith should complement medical treatment, not replace it. The most effective approach is one that honours both your spiritual needs and your medical needs. Many hospitals in Kenya have chaplaincy services that can provide spiritual support alongside your medical care.\n\nHope as a Practical Tool\n\nHope is not naive optimism or denial of reality. Hope is the belief that your efforts matter, that treatment can work, and that good days are still possible. Research shows that patients who maintain hope, alongside realistic understanding of their condition, tend to cope better with treatment, report higher quality of life, and may even have better medical outcomes. Hope motivates you to attend appointments, take your medication, eat well, and keep moving. It is not a luxury. It is a practical tool for survival.\n\nA Message for the Darkest Days\n\nThere will be days when hope feels impossible. When the nausea is relentless, when the fatigue pins you to the bed, when the walls of the hospital room feel like they are closing in. On those days, you do not need to be hopeful. You just need to be here. Survival on the hardest days is not about optimism. It is about endurance. Endure this hour. Then the next. The dark days pass. They always do. And on the other side of them, there will be a morning when you wake up feeling a little stronger, when the sun through your window feels warm on your face, and when the tea tastes right again. Hold on for that morning. It is coming.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"When Time Takes on a New Meaning","body":"Before a cancer diagnosis, most of us live as though time is unlimited. We postpone the trip to visit our parents in shags. We tell ourselves we will spend more time with the children next month. We put off the conversation, the rest, the gratitude. A cancer diagnosis shatters that illusion. Suddenly, time is no longer something you have in abundance. It is something you measure, guard, and spend with intention. This shift can be frightening at first, but many people who have walked this path describe it as one of the most profound and ultimately positive transformations of their lives. Cancer does not just change your body. It changes your relationship with everything."},{"id":"s2","title":"The First Weeks After Diagnosis","body":"The initial period after diagnosis is often the most turbulent. Your mind races between practicalities, who will take the children to school during treatment, how will we pay for this, and existential questions, what if the treatment does not work, what will my family do without me. It is common to feel like you are watching your life from outside your body, going through the motions while your mind struggles to catch up. This is shock, and it is a normal psychological response to overwhelming news. During this time, be gentle with yourself. You do not need to have a plan for everything immediately. You do not need to be brave or positive or strong. You just need to get through one day at a time."},{"id":"s3","title":"The Shift from Why Me to What Now","body":"At some point, and it happens at different times for different people, a subtle shift occurs. The question changes from \\"Why did this happen to me?\\" to \\"What am I going to do about it?\\" This is not a moment of acceptance so much as a turning of the page. It does not mean the grief and anger disappear. They may surface again and again throughout treatment. But alongside them, a quieter voice emerges: the voice of agency, of purpose, of determination to make the most of whatever time you have, whether that is decades or something less."},{"id":"s4","title":"Rediscovering What Truly Matters","body":"Cancer has a way of stripping away the unnecessary and revealing the essential. The promotion you were chasing may suddenly seem less important than an afternoon with your children in the garden. The social obligations you dreaded may fall away, leaving space for the people and activities that genuinely nourish you. Many cancer patients in Kenya describe rediscovering simple pleasures they had forgotten: the taste of fresh mango from the roadside, the sound of birds in the morning, the warmth of sitting with a friend over a cup of chai without checking the clock. This is not sentimentality. It is a recalibration of values that many people spend their whole lives searching for. Cancer, in its terrible way, can deliver it."},{"id":"s5","title":"Giving Yourself Permission to Rest","body":"In Kenyan culture, productivity is deeply valued. We admire the hustle, the early mornings, the multiple jobs, the relentless drive. Resting can feel like failure. But during cancer treatment, rest is not optional. It is medicine. Your body is engaged in one of the hardest fights of its life. Every nap, every quiet afternoon, every early bedtime is an investment in your recovery. Give yourself permission to rest without guilt. If you find it hard, try reframing it: you are not doing nothing. You are healing. You are giving your body the energy it needs to fight. That is one of the most productive things you can possibly do."},{"id":"s6","title":"Letting Others Help","body":"Many people, especially those who have spent their lives taking care of others, find it incredibly difficult to accept help. If you are the anchor caregiver in your family, the one who holds everything together, receiving care instead of giving it can feel deeply uncomfortable. But this is the time to let others step in. When your sister offers to cook, say yes. When your neighbour offers to drive you to the hospital, say yes. When your Jireh Circle offers financial support, accept it with gratitude. Accepting help is not weakness. It is wisdom. It preserves your energy for healing and allows the people who love you to express their care in a tangible way. Most people want to help. Giving them the opportunity is a gift to them as well as to yourself."},{"id":"s7","title":"Maintaining Routine and Normalcy","body":"While some things in your life will inevitably change during treatment, maintaining elements of your normal routine can be deeply grounding. If you always had morning tea on your balcony, continue doing so. If you enjoyed a particular radio programme or podcast, keep listening. If going to church, mosque, or temple brought you peace, attend when you feel well enough or participate remotely if your place of worship offers that option. These threads of normalcy are anchors in a sea of change. They remind you that while cancer is part of your life now, it has not replaced your entire life."},{"id":"s8","title":"Finding Purpose During Treatment","body":"Purpose does not have to be grand. During treatment, purpose might be as simple as being present for your child''s homework. It might be calling a friend who is also going through a difficult time. It might be keeping a journal of your experiences, which many patients find therapeutic. It might be learning something new, a language, a skill, a hobby that you always wanted to try. Some patients find purpose in advocacy, sharing their story to encourage others to get screened or to seek treatment without delay. Whatever form it takes, purpose gives structure to your days and a reason to keep moving forward."},{"id":"s9","title":"The Role of Faith and Spirituality","body":"For many Kenyans, faith is a central pillar of coping with illness. Whether you find comfort in prayer, meditation, scripture, or the community of a faith group, lean into what gives you peace. Spiritual support has been shown to improve quality of life and emotional well-being during cancer treatment. At the same time, faith should complement medical treatment, not replace it. The most effective approach is one that honours both your spiritual needs and your medical needs. Many hospitals in Kenya have chaplaincy services that can provide spiritual support alongside your medical care."},{"id":"s10","title":"Hope as a Practical Tool","body":"Hope is not naive optimism or denial of reality. Hope is the belief that your efforts matter, that treatment can work, and that good days are still possible. Research shows that patients who maintain hope, alongside realistic understanding of their condition, tend to cope better with treatment, report higher quality of life, and may even have better medical outcomes. Hope motivates you to attend appointments, take your medication, eat well, and keep moving. It is not a luxury. It is a practical tool for survival."},{"id":"s11","title":"A Message for the Darkest Days","body":"There will be days when hope feels impossible. When the nausea is relentless, when the fatigue pins you to the bed, when the walls of the hospital room feel like they are closing in. On those days, you do not need to be hopeful. You just need to be here. Survival on the hardest days is not about optimism. It is about endurance. Endure this hour. Then the next. The dark days pass. They always do. And on the other side of them, there will be a morning when you wake up feeling a little stronger, when the sun through your window feels warm on your face, and when the tea tastes right again. Hold on for that morning. It is coming."}]'::jsonb, 8, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'self-care'),
  (E'keeping-a-treatment-side-effects-diary', E'Keeping a treatment side-effects diary', E'CANCER', '{"CANCER"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'A treatment side-effects diary is one of the most powerful tools you can keep during your cancer journey. It costs nothing — just a notebook from any bookshop or stationery kiosk for KES 50-100 — but the information it captures can genuinely change the quality of your care.', E'A treatment side-effects diary is one of the most powerful tools you can keep during your cancer journey. It costs nothing — just a notebook from any bookshop or stationery kiosk for KES 50-100 — but the information it captures can genuinely change the quality of your care. Your oncologist sees you for a limited time during each appointment, and they rely heavily on what you tell them. A written record is far more accurate than trying to remember how you felt three weeks ago.\n\nWhat to record every day\n\nEach morning and evening, write down a few simple things. Rate your energy level from 1 to 10, where 1 means you could barely get out of bed and 10 means you felt almost normal. Note whether you experienced nausea — and if so, was it mild queasiness or did you actually vomit? Record your appetite: did you eat a full meal, just a few bites of ugali and sukuma wiki, or nothing at all? Write down any pain you felt, where it was located, and how severe it was on a scale of 1 to 10. Finally, note how many hours you slept and whether the sleep was restful or interrupted.\n\nTracking what helps and what does not\n\nThis is where your diary becomes truly valuable. Did sipping on warm ginger tea help settle your stomach after chemotherapy? Did a short 10-minute walk around the compound improve your energy in the afternoon? Did eating small frequent meals — a boiled egg in the morning, a few spoonfuls of porridge at 10am, a light lunch of steamed vegetables and rice — work better than trying to eat three large meals? Write it all down. Over a few weeks, you will start to see patterns. Maybe day two and three after chemo are always the hardest, but by day five your appetite returns. Knowing this helps you plan — you can prepare simpler meals for the hard days and stock up on easy-to-digest foods like bananas, plain rice, or clear soup.\n\nWhat your oncologist does with this information\n\nWhen you bring your diary to your appointment at the hospital, your oncologist can make much better decisions. If your nausea is severe on specific days, they might adjust your anti-nausea medication — perhaps switching from one drug to another, or adding a second medication to take on the worst days. If your pain is not well controlled, they can adjust your pain management plan. If your sleep is consistently poor, they might investigate whether anxiety, pain, or medication side effects are the cause. Some treatments can be adjusted in dosage or timing based on how your body responds, and your diary provides the evidence they need.\n\nPractical tips for keeping your diary\n\nKeep your notebook by your bed so you remember to write in it morning and evening. If you prefer your phone, use the notes app — whatever is easiest for you. Some patients use a simple table format: date in one column, energy in the next, then nausea, appetite, pain, and sleep. Others prefer to write a few sentences about how the day went. Both approaches work. The important thing is consistency. Even on good days, write something — "Felt strong today, walked to the shop and back, ate well" is useful information.\n\nBeyond the physical — tracking your emotional state\n\nConsider adding a line about your mood each day. Cancer treatment affects your emotions just as much as your body. You might notice that your mood drops on certain days of the treatment cycle, or that visits from friends and family lift your spirits. This information matters. If you are experiencing persistent sadness, hopelessness, or anxiety, your care team can connect you with counselling services. Many Kenyan hospitals, including Kenyatta National Hospital and Aga Khan, have psychosocial support units for cancer patients. Your NHIF or SHA cover may include some counselling visits — ask your care navigator about what is available to you.\n\nSharing with your caregivers\n\nYour diary is also a gift to the person caring for you at home. When a family member can look at your diary and see that your appetite is usually better in the morning, they can plan to prepare your favourite light breakfast — maybe a soft chapati with weak tea — at the time when you are most likely to eat. When they see that afternoons are your low-energy period, they know not to schedule visitors during that time. Your diary turns invisible suffering into something visible and actionable, both for your medical team and for the people who love you.\n\nRemember, there is no wrong way to keep this diary. The best diary is the one you actually use. Start today, even if your next appointment is weeks away. Every entry is a small act of taking control of your health during a time when so much feels out of your control.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"A treatment side-effects diary is one of the most powerful tools you can keep during your cancer journey. It costs nothing — just a notebook from any bookshop or stationery kiosk for KES 50-100 — but the information it captures can genuinely change the quality of your care. Your oncologist sees you for a limited time during each appointment, and they rely heavily on what you tell them. A written record is far more accurate than trying to remember how you felt three weeks ago."},{"id":"s2","title":"What to record every day","body":"Each morning and evening, write down a few simple things. Rate your energy level from 1 to 10, where 1 means you could barely get out of bed and 10 means you felt almost normal. Note whether you experienced nausea — and if so, was it mild queasiness or did you actually vomit? Record your appetite: did you eat a full meal, just a few bites of ugali and sukuma wiki, or nothing at all? Write down any pain you felt, where it was located, and how severe it was on a scale of 1 to 10. Finally, note how many hours you slept and whether the sleep was restful or interrupted."},{"id":"s3","title":"Tracking what helps and what does not","body":"This is where your diary becomes truly valuable. Did sipping on warm ginger tea help settle your stomach after chemotherapy? Did a short 10-minute walk around the compound improve your energy in the afternoon? Did eating small frequent meals — a boiled egg in the morning, a few spoonfuls of porridge at 10am, a light lunch of steamed vegetables and rice — work better than trying to eat three large meals? Write it all down. Over a few weeks, you will start to see patterns. Maybe day two and three after chemo are always the hardest, but by day five your appetite returns. Knowing this helps you plan — you can prepare simpler meals for the hard days and stock up on easy-to-digest foods like bananas, plain rice, or clear soup."},{"id":"s4","title":"What your oncologist does with this information","body":"When you bring your diary to your appointment at the hospital, your oncologist can make much better decisions. If your nausea is severe on specific days, they might adjust your anti-nausea medication — perhaps switching from one drug to another, or adding a second medication to take on the worst days. If your pain is not well controlled, they can adjust your pain management plan. If your sleep is consistently poor, they might investigate whether anxiety, pain, or medication side effects are the cause. Some treatments can be adjusted in dosage or timing based on how your body responds, and your diary provides the evidence they need."},{"id":"s5","title":"Practical tips for keeping your diary","body":"Keep your notebook by your bed so you remember to write in it morning and evening. If you prefer your phone, use the notes app — whatever is easiest for you. Some