Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'health-diary'),
  (E'you-completed-a-full-treatment-cycle', E'You completed a full treatment cycle', E'CANCER', '{"CANCER"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Completing a full treatment cycle is one of the most significant milestones in your cancer journey. Whether you have just finished your last round of chemotherapy, completed your course of radiation therapy, or recovered from surgery, this moment deserves to be recognised.', E'Completing a full treatment cycle is one of the most significant milestones in your cancer journey. Whether you have just finished your last round of chemotherapy, completed your course of radiation therapy, or recovered from surgery, this moment deserves to be recognised. You did something incredibly difficult, and you made it through.\n\nWhat you endured to get here\n\nThink about everything that went into reaching this point. The early morning trips to the hospital, sometimes travelling across Nairobi in heavy traffic or coming from upcountry to access treatment. The hours spent in waiting rooms, the IV drips, the scans, the blood tests. The days when nausea made it impossible to look at food, when even the smell of ugali cooking made your stomach turn. The nights when worry kept you awake, wondering about results, about costs, about your family. The financial strain — cancer treatment in Kenya is expensive, even with NHIF or SHA coverage, and many families dip into savings, sell assets, or rely on harambees to manage the bills. You carried all of this, and you still showed up for every appointment.\n\nYour body''s resilience\n\nYour body went through something extraordinary. Chemotherapy targets fast-growing cells throughout your body, radiation focuses intense energy on specific areas, and surgery requires physical healing. Your body fought through all of it. The fatigue, the hair changes, the mouth sores, the compromised immunity — these were signs that treatment was doing its work, and your body was enduring and recovering at the same time. Human bodies are remarkably resilient, and yours proved that.\n\nAcknowledging the emotional weight\n\nCancer treatment is not just a physical experience. The emotional toll is enormous and often invisible to those around you. You may have experienced fear, anger, sadness, frustration, and moments of hopelessness. You may have put on a brave face for your children, your spouse, or your parents, carrying the weight privately. Some days, getting out of bed and going to the hospital was the bravest thing you could have done. It is okay to feel all of these things, and it is okay to acknowledge that this was one of the hardest seasons of your life.\n\nThe people who walked with you\n\nThis milestone also belongs to the people who supported you — the family member who accompanied you to every appointment, the friend who brought you soup, the colleague who contributed to your medical fund, the care team at the hospital who knew your name and your story. Cancer is not faced alone in Kenya. Communities rally around their own, from church prayer groups to chama contributions. If people helped you get through this, let them share in this celebration too.\n\nWhat comes next\n\nCompleting treatment does not mean everything immediately goes back to normal. Your body needs time to recover. Energy levels take weeks or months to rebuild. Your appetite and taste may still be adjusting. Emotionally, you might feel a strange mix of relief and anxiety — relief that treatment is over, and anxiety because the regular monitoring schedule changes. This is completely normal. Many cancer survivors describe a period of adjustment after treatment ends, where life feels different in ways they did not expect.\n\nFollow-up matters\n\nYour oncologist will schedule follow-up appointments — typically every few months at first, then gradually less often. These check-ups monitor your recovery and watch for any signs that need attention. Keep attending them faithfully. Continue with any maintenance medications prescribed. If you experience new symptoms between appointments, do not wait — call your care team. Early attention to concerns is always better than waiting.\n\nCelebrate this moment\n\nIn many hospitals around the world, patients ring a bell when they finish treatment. Whether or not your hospital has this tradition, find your own way to mark this day. Share a meal with family. Take a photo. Write yourself a letter about what you have learned. You have every reason to feel proud. This is not the end of your journey — follow-up care and healthy living continue — but it is the end of the hardest chapter. You earned this moment. You are still here, and that matters more than anything.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Completing a full treatment cycle is one of the most significant milestones in your cancer journey. Whether you have just finished your last round of chemotherapy, completed your course of radiation therapy, or recovered from surgery, this moment deserves to be recognised. You did something incredibly difficult, and you made it through."},{"id":"s2","title":"What you endured to get here","body":"Think about everything that went into reaching this point. The early morning trips to the hospital, sometimes travelling across Nairobi in heavy traffic or coming from upcountry to access treatment. The hours spent in waiting rooms, the IV drips, the scans, the blood tests. The days when nausea made it impossible to look at food, when even the smell of ugali cooking made your stomach turn. The nights when worry kept you awake, wondering about results, about costs, about your family. The financial strain — cancer treatment in Kenya is expensive, even with NHIF or SHA coverage, and many families dip into savings, sell assets, or rely on harambees to manage the bills. You carried all of this, and you still showed up for every appointment."},{"id":"s3","title":"Your body''s resilience","body":"Your body went through something extraordinary. Chemotherapy targets fast-growing cells throughout your body, radiation focuses intense energy on specific areas, and surgery requires physical healing. Your body fought through all of it. The fatigue, the hair changes, the mouth sores, the compromised immunity — these were signs that treatment was doing its work, and your body was enduring and recovering at the same time. Human bodies are remarkably resilient, and yours proved that."},{"id":"s4","title":"Acknowledging the emotional weight","body":"Cancer treatment is not just a physical experience. The emotional toll is enormous and often invisible to those around you. You may have experienced fear, anger, sadness, frustration, and moments of hopelessness. You may have put on a brave face for your children, your spouse, or your parents, carrying the weight privately. Some days, getting out of bed and going to the hospital was the bravest thing you could have done. It is okay to feel all of these things, and it is okay to acknowledge that this was one of the hardest seasons of your life."},{"id":"s5","title":"The people who walked with you","body":"This milestone also belongs to the people who supported you — the family member who accompanied you to every appointment, the friend who brought you soup, the colleague who contributed to your medical fund, the care team at the hospital who knew your name and your story. Cancer is not faced alone in Kenya. Communities rally around their own, from church prayer groups to chama contributions. If people helped you get through this, let them share in this celebration too."},{"id":"s6","title":"What comes next","body":"Completing treatment does not mean everything immediately goes back to normal. Your body needs time to recover. Energy levels take weeks or months to rebuild. Your appetite and taste may still be adjusting. Emotionally, you might feel a strange mix of relief and anxiety — relief that treatment is over, and anxiety because the regular monitoring schedule changes. This is completely normal. Many cancer survivors describe a period of adjustment after treatment ends, where life feels different in ways they did not expect."},{"id":"s7","title":"Follow-up matters","body":"Your oncologist will schedule follow-up appointments — typically every few months at first, then gradually less often. These check-ups monitor your recovery and watch for any signs that need attention. Keep attending them faithfully. Continue with any maintenance medications prescribed. If you experience new symptoms between appointments, do not wait — call your care team. Early attention to concerns is always better than waiting."},{"id":"s8","title":"Celebrate this moment","body":"In many hospitals around the world, patients ring a bell when they finish treatment. Whether or not your hospital has this tradition, find your own way to mark this day. Share a meal with family. Take a photo. Write yourself a letter about what you have learned. You have every reason to feel proud. This is not the end of your journey — follow-up care and healthy living continue — but it is the end of the hardest chapter. You earned this moment. You are still here, and that matters more than anything."}]'::jsonb, 4, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'wellness-journey'),
  (E'why-potassium-matters-when-your-kidneys-are-struggling', E'Why potassium matters when your kidneys are struggling', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'DIETARY', E'When your kidneys are healthy, they act as highly efficient filters, removing excess potassium from your blood and sending it out through your urine. But when kidney disease damages these filters, potassium starts building up in your bloodstream.', E'When your kidneys are healthy, they act as highly efficient filters, removing excess potassium from your blood and sending it out through your urine. But when kidney disease damages these filters, potassium starts building up in your bloodstream. This is dangerous because potassium directly affects your heart rhythm. Too much potassium — a condition called hyperkalaemia — can cause your heart to beat irregularly, and in severe cases, it can be life-threatening. Understanding which foods are high in potassium and learning to manage your intake is one of the most important dietary changes you can make.\n\nHigh-potassium foods common in Kenya\n\nMany of the foods Kenyans eat daily are naturally high in potassium. Bananas are the most well-known example — a single banana contains about 400mg of potassium, which is significant when your kidneys cannot clear the excess. Ripe mangoes, oranges, passion fruit, and avocados are also high. Among vegetables, spinach, sukuma wiki, and other dark leafy greens contain substantial potassium. Potatoes — including the sweet potatoes many families rely on — are high-potassium foods. Tomatoes, both fresh and in cooking sauces, add up quickly. Githeri, which combines beans and maize, delivers a potassium load from the beans. Even pilau rice, if cooked with tomato paste and potatoes, can be surprisingly high.\n\nLower-potassium alternatives you can enjoy\n\nThe good news is that you do not have to stop eating everything you love. You just need to make strategic swaps. Instead of bananas, reach for apples, which are much lower in potassium and available at most markets for KES 10-20 each. Cabbage is a fantastic low-potassium vegetable — make a crispy cabbage salad with lemon juice, or stir-fry it with onions and a little oil. Carrots, green beans, and cucumber are all lower-potassium options. For your starch, white rice is lower in potassium than potatoes. Plain ugali made from maize flour is moderate — better than potato-based dishes. Watermelon is a refreshing low-potassium fruit choice, and pineapple is another good option.\n\nThe leaching technique for potatoes and root vegetables\n\nIf you really miss potatoes and sweet potatoes, there is a preparation technique that can help. Peel the potatoes and cut them into small, thin pieces. Soak them in a large amount of warm water for at least two to four hours, changing the water halfway through. This process, called leaching, draws some of the potassium out of the potato and into the water. Drain the potatoes, rinse them, and then boil them in fresh water. This will not remove all the potassium, but it can reduce it by 30-50 percent, making an occasional serving safer. The same technique works for sweet potatoes, yams, and cassava.\n\nPortion control matters more than total elimination\n\nYour doctor or dietitian will likely not tell you to eliminate all high-potassium foods forever. Instead, the focus is on portion control and frequency. Having a small piece of banana once a week is very different from eating two bananas every day. A few spoonfuls of sukuma wiki alongside your ugali is different from a large plate of greens. Learn what a safe portion looks like for your level of kidney function. Your nephrologist at hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital or Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital can refer you to a renal dietitian who understands local foods and can create a personalised meal plan.\n\nReading your blood test results\n\nAt your regular clinic visits, your doctor will order blood tests that include your potassium level. Normal potassium is between 3.5 and 5.0 mmol/L. If yours is consistently above 5.0, your diet needs more attention. Above 5.5 is concerning, and above 6.0 can be dangerous and may require emergency treatment. Ask your doctor to explain your potassium level at each visit. Write it down in a notebook or your phone so you can track the trend over time. If your potassium is rising, your doctor may adjust your diet recommendations or change certain medications that can raise potassium levels.\n\nShopping and cooking tips\n\nWhen shopping at the market or supermarket, focus on building your meals around lower-potassium ingredients. Buy cabbage, carrots, onions, green peppers, and green beans. Keep apples and watermelon as your go-to fruits. When cooking for the whole family, you can prepare the same base meal — say, rice and a vegetable stew — and simply serve yourself a larger portion of the low-potassium vegetables while others add the high-potassium options. Avoid using too many tomatoes in your cooking; instead, flavour with onions, garlic, ginger, and spices like cumin and coriander. These add wonderful taste without adding potassium.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When your kidneys are healthy, they act as highly efficient filters, removing excess potassium from your blood and sending it out through your urine. But when kidney disease damages these filters, potassium starts building up in your bloodstream. This is dangerous because potassium directly affects your heart rhythm. Too much potassium — a condition called hyperkalaemia — can cause your heart to beat irregularly, and in severe cases, it can be life-threatening. Understanding which foods are high in potassium and learning to manage your intake is one of the most important dietary changes you can make."},{"id":"s2","title":"High-potassium foods common in Kenya","body":"Many of the foods Kenyans eat daily are naturally high in potassium. Bananas are the most well-known example — a single banana contains about 400mg of potassium, which is significant when your kidneys cannot clear the excess. Ripe mangoes, oranges, passion fruit, and avocados are also high. Among vegetables, spinach, sukuma wiki, and other dark leafy greens contain substantial potassium. Potatoes — including the sweet potatoes many families rely on — are high-potassium foods. Tomatoes, both fresh and in cooking sauces, add up quickly. Githeri, which combines beans and maize, delivers a potassium load from the beans. Even pilau rice, if cooked with tomato paste and potatoes, can be surprisingly high."},{"id":"s3","title":"Lower-potassium alternatives you can enjoy","body":"The good news is that you do not have to stop eating everything you love. You just need to make strategic swaps. Instead of bananas, reach for apples, which are much lower in potassium and available at most markets for KES 10-20 each. Cabbage is a fantastic low-potassium vegetable — make a crispy cabbage salad with lemon juice, or stir-fry it with onions and a little oil. Carrots, green beans, and cucumber are all lower-potassium options. For your starch, white rice is lower in potassium than potatoes. Plain ugali made from maize flour is moderate — better than potato-based dishes. Watermelon is a refreshing low-potassium fruit choice, and pineapple is another good option."},{"id":"s4","title":"The leaching technique for potatoes and root vegetables","body":"If you really miss potatoes and sweet potatoes, there is a preparation technique that can help. Peel the potatoes and cut them into small, thin pieces. Soak them in a large amount of warm water for at least two to four hours, changing the water halfway through. This process, called leaching, draws some of the potassium out of the potato and into the water. Drain the potatoes, rinse them, and then boil them in fresh water. This will not remove all the potassium, but it can reduce it by 30-50 percent, making an occasional serving safer. The same technique works for sweet potatoes, yams, and cassava."},{"id":"s5","title":"Portion control matters more than total elimination","body":"Your doctor or dietitian will likely not tell you to eliminate all high-potassium foods forever. Instead, the focus is on portion control and frequency. Having a small piece of banana once a week is very different from eating two bananas every day. A few spoonfuls of sukuma wiki alongside your ugali is different from a large plate of greens. Learn what a safe portion looks like for your level of kidney function. Your nephrologist at hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital or Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital can refer you to a renal dietitian who understands local foods and can create a personalised meal plan."},{"id":"s6","title":"Reading your blood test results","body":"At your regular clinic visits, your doctor will order blood tests that include your potassium level. Normal potassium is between 3.5 and 5.0 mmol/L. If yours is consistently above 5.0, your diet needs more attention. Above 5.5 is concerning, and above 6.0 can be dangerous and may require emergency treatment. Ask your doctor to explain your potassium level at each visit. Write it down in a notebook or your phone so you can track the trend over time. If your potassium is rising, your doctor may adjust your diet recommendations or change certain medications that can raise potassium levels."},{"id":"s7","title":"Shopping and cooking tips","body":"When shopping at the market or supermarket, focus on building your meals around lower-potassium ingredients. Buy cabbage, carrots, onions, green peppers, and green beans. Keep apples and watermelon as your go-to fruits. When cooking for the whole family, you can prepare the same base meal — say, rice and a vegetable stew — and simply serve yourself a larger portion of the low-potassium vegetables while others add the high-potassium options. Avoid using too many tomatoes in your cooking; instead, flavour with onions, garlic, ginger, and spices like cumin and coriander. These add wonderful taste without adding potassium."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'market-shopping'),
  (E'protein-how-much-is-too-much-for-your-kidneys', E'Protein: how much is too much for your kidneys', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'DIETARY', E'Protein is essential for life — it builds and repairs your muscles, supports your immune system, and keeps you strong. But when your kidneys are damaged, they struggle to filter out the waste products that protein digestion creates, particularly a substance called urea.', E'Protein is essential for life — it builds and repairs your muscles, supports your immune system, and keeps you strong. But when your kidneys are damaged, they struggle to filter out the waste products that protein digestion creates, particularly a substance called urea. When urea builds up in your blood, you feel tired, nauseated, and unwell. This does not mean you should stop eating protein entirely. That would cause malnutrition, muscle wasting, and a weaker immune system. The goal is finding the right amount — enough to keep you nourished, but not so much that it overwhelms your kidneys.\n\nHow much protein is right for you\n\nThe exact amount depends on your stage of kidney disease, whether you are on dialysis, and your body size. As a general guide, most people with chronic kidney disease who are not yet on dialysis are advised to eat about 0.6 to 0.8 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day. For a person weighing 70kg, that works out to roughly 42 to 56 grams of protein daily. To put that in practical terms, a palm-sized piece of chicken breast (about the size of a deck of cards) contains roughly 25-30 grams of protein. So you can see how quickly it adds up if you eat large portions. If you are on dialysis, your protein needs actually increase because dialysis removes some protein, so your doctor may advise you to eat more.\n\nCommon Kenyan protein sources and portion guidance\n\nLet us talk about the protein sources most Kenyans eat regularly. A whole fried tilapia at a hotel or restaurant typically provides far more protein than you need in a single sitting — consider sharing it or taking half home. Nyama choma is a beloved social food, but a typical serving at a nyama choma joint can easily contain 50-60 grams of protein, which is essentially your entire day''s allowance in one meal. Instead, have a smaller portion — about the size of your palm, not your whole plate — and fill up on ugali and a vegetable side instead.\n\nBeans and lentils (ndengu) are important protein sources, especially for families on a budget. A cup of cooked beans has about 15 grams of protein. Githeri — the combination of beans and maize — is nutritious, but watch your portion of the beans. Aim for about half a cup of beans in your serving rather than a heaping plateful. Eggs are an excellent choice for kidney patients because one egg provides about 6-7 grams of high-quality protein in a small, easy-to-control portion. Two eggs at breakfast gives you a good protein start without excess.\n\nSpreading protein across your meals\n\nOne of the most practical strategies is distributing your protein evenly throughout the day rather than loading it all into one meal. Many Kenyans eat a light breakfast (perhaps just tea and bread), a moderate lunch, and then a heavy dinner with a large serving of meat or beans. For kidney health, try restructuring this pattern. Have an egg or two with your morning tea and a slice of bread. At lunch, include a small portion of beans or lentils with your ugali and vegetables. At dinner, have a palm-sized piece of fish or chicken with plenty of vegetables. This even distribution puts less strain on your kidneys at any single time.\n\nProtein quality matters\n\nNot all protein is equal when it comes to kidney health. Animal proteins — meat, fish, eggs, milk — are called high-quality or complete proteins because they contain all the essential amino acids your body needs. Plant proteins from beans, lentils, and nuts are also valuable but produce slightly different waste products. Your renal dietitian may advise a specific mix. What is most important is that the protein you eat counts — avoid wasting your protein allowance on processed foods like sausages, smokies, or cheap mincemeat that contain fillers and high levels of sodium and phosphorus, both of which are also problematic for kidney patients.\n\nPractical meal ideas\n\nBreakfast: Two boiled eggs with one slice of white bread and tea (about 14g protein). Lunch: Half a cup of lentil stew (ndengu) with ugali and steamed cabbage (about 8-10g protein). Dinner: A palm-sized piece of grilled fish with rice and a carrot-and-green-bean side dish (about 20g protein). Snack: An apple or a small portion of watermelon. This gives you roughly 42-44 grams of protein spread evenly through the day, well within a typical kidney-friendly range.\n\nWorking with your care team\n\nAsk your nephrologist or renal dietitian for a personalised protein target based on your specific kidney function and body weight. This number may change over time as your condition changes or if you start dialysis. Many county and national referral hospitals have dietitians who can help. If you are managing on a tight budget, be honest about what foods you can afford — a good dietitian will work with locally available, affordable options. Eggs, small portions of omena (silver fish), and lentils are among the most affordable protein sources in Kenya and work well within a kidney-friendly diet.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Protein is essential for life — it builds and repairs your muscles, supports your immune system, and keeps you strong. But when your kidneys are damaged, they struggle to filter out the waste products that protein digestion creates, particularly a substance called urea. When urea builds up in your blood, you feel tired, nauseated, and unwell. This does not mean you should stop eating protein entirely. That would cause malnutrition, muscle wasting, and a weaker immune system. The goal is finding the right amount — enough to keep you nourished, but not so much that it overwhelms your kidneys."},{"id":"s2","title":"How much protein is right for you","body":"The exact amount depends on your stage of kidney disease, whether you are on dialysis, and your body size. As a general guide, most people with chronic kidney disease who are not yet on dialysis are advised to eat about 0.6 to 0.8 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day. For a person weighing 70kg, that works out to roughly 42 to 56 grams of protein daily. To put that in practical terms, a palm-sized piece of chicken breast (about the size of a deck of cards) contains roughly 25-30 grams of protein. So you can see how quickly it adds up if you eat large portions. If you are on dialysis, your protein needs actually increase because dialysis removes some protein, so your doctor may advise you to eat more."},{"id":"s3","title":"Common Kenyan protein sources and portion guidance","body":"Let us talk about the protein sources most Kenyans eat regularly. A whole fried tilapia at a hotel or restaurant typically provides far more protein than you need in a single sitting — consider sharing it or taking half home. Nyama choma is a beloved social food, but a typical serving at a nyama choma joint can easily contain 50-60 grams of protein, which is essentially your entire day''s allowance in one meal. Instead, have a smaller portion — about the size of your palm, not your whole plate — and fill up on ugali and a vegetable side instead.\\n\\nBeans and lentils (ndengu) are important protein sources, especially for families on a budget. A cup of cooked beans has about 15 grams of protein. Githeri — the combination of beans and maize — is nutritious, but watch your portion of the beans. Aim for about half a cup of beans in your serving rather than a heaping plateful. Eggs are an excellent choice for kidney patients because one egg provides about 6-7 grams of high-quality protein in a small, easy-to-control portion. Two eggs at breakfast gives you a good protein start without excess."},{"id":"s4","title":"Spreading protein across your meals","body":"One of the most practical strategies is distributing your protein evenly throughout the day rather than loading it all into one meal. Many Kenyans eat a light breakfast (perhaps just tea and bread), a moderate lunch, and then a heavy dinner with a large serving of meat or beans. For kidney health, try restructuring this pattern. Have an egg or two with your morning tea and a slice of bread. At lunch, include a small portion of beans or lentils with your ugali and vegetables. At dinner, have a palm-sized piece of fish or chicken with plenty of vegetables. This even distribution puts less strain on your kidneys at any single time."},{"id":"s5","title":"Protein quality matters","body":"Not all protein is equal when it comes to kidney health. Animal proteins — meat, fish, eggs, milk — are called high-quality or complete proteins because they contain all the essential amino acids your body needs. Plant proteins from beans, lentils, and nuts are also valuable but produce slightly different waste products. Your renal dietitian may advise a specific mix. What is most important is that the protein you eat counts — avoid wasting your protein allowance on processed foods like sausages, smokies, or cheap mincemeat that contain fillers and high levels of sodium and phosphorus, both of which are also problematic for kidney patients."},{"id":"s6","title":"Practical meal ideas","body":"Breakfast: Two boiled eggs with one slice of white bread and tea (about 14g protein). Lunch: Half a cup of lentil stew (ndengu) with ugali and steamed cabbage (about 8-10g protein). Dinner: A palm-sized piece of grilled fish with rice and a carrot-and-green-bean side dish (about 20g protein). Snack: An apple or a small portion of watermelon. This gives you roughly 42-44 grams of protein spread evenly through the day, well within a typical kidney-friendly range."},{"id":"s7","title":"Working with your care team","body":"Ask your nephrologist or renal dietitian for a personalised protein target based on your specific kidney function and body weight. This number may change over time as your condition changes or if you start dialysis. Many county and national referral hospitals have dietitians who can help. If you are managing on a tight budget, be honest about what foods you can afford — a good dietitian will work with locally available, affordable options. Eggs, small portions of omena (silver fish), and lentils are among the most affordable protein sources in Kenya and work well within a kidney-friendly diet."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'balanced-plate'),
  (E'light-exercise-that-supports-kidney-function', E'Light exercise that supports kidney function', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'EXERCISE', E'When you have kidney disease, it might seem like rest is the best medicine. After all, fatigue is one of the most common and frustrating symptoms, and some days just getting through your daily routine feels like enough.', E'When you have kidney disease, it might seem like rest is the best medicine. After all, fatigue is one of the most common and frustrating symptoms, and some days just getting through your daily routine feels like enough. But research consistently shows that regular, moderate exercise actually improves kidney health, reduces the progression of the disease, and helps manage the two conditions that most often cause kidney damage in the first place — high blood pressure and diabetes.\n\nHow exercise helps your kidneys\n\nYour kidneys depend on healthy blood flow to function. When you exercise, your cardiovascular system becomes more efficient, blood pressure drops, and blood flows more smoothly to your kidneys. Exercise also improves your body''s sensitivity to insulin, which means better blood sugar control if you have diabetes. Since uncontrolled diabetes and hypertension are the top two causes of kidney disease in Kenya, managing them through exercise directly protects your remaining kidney function. Beyond the kidneys, exercise combats the bone weakness, muscle loss, and cardiovascular problems that kidney disease accelerates.\n\nStarting a walking routine\n\nWalking is the simplest, safest, and most accessible exercise for kidney patients. You do not need a gym membership or special equipment — just a pair of comfortable shoes. Start with 10-minute walks, three times a week. Walk on flat ground at a pace where you can still talk but feel slightly warmer than at rest. If you live in Nairobi, the morning hours before 7am or evening after 5pm are cooler and more comfortable. If you are in a smaller town, find a safe, flat route — perhaps around a school field, along a quiet road, or within your estate. Each week, try to add 2-3 minutes to your walk until you reach 30 minutes. Five walks of 30 minutes per week is an excellent target.\n\nOther suitable exercises\n\nCycling — whether on a stationary bike or a regular bicycle on flat roads — is excellent for kidney patients because it is low-impact and easy on the joints. Swimming is another wonderful option if you have access to a pool, as the water supports your body weight and reduces strain. At home, you can do simple exercises without any equipment: stand up from a chair and sit back down 10 times, rest for a minute, then repeat. This builds leg strength. Try standing on one foot for 30 seconds on each side to improve balance. Do gentle arm circles — 10 forward, 10 backward — to maintain shoulder mobility.\n\nExercise if you are on dialysis\n\nIf you are on haemodialysis, you spend several hours connected to a machine multiple times per week. This does not mean exercise stops. Many dialysis patients benefit from seated exercises during treatment. Ask your care team at your dialysis centre about a seated pedal exerciser — this is a small device you can use to pedal your legs while sitting in the dialysis chair. Some centres in Nairobi and other major towns have these available. Hand grip exercises using a soft ball can maintain arm strength. Between dialysis sessions, walking remains your best friend. Even 15 minutes of walking on non-dialysis days helps.\n\nSafety precautions\n\nThere are important safety rules for exercising with kidney disease. Avoid heavy weight lifting or any exercise that involves straining hard and holding your breath — this sharply raises blood pressure, which is dangerous. If you have a dialysis fistula in your arm, do not lift heavy objects with that arm or compress it during exercise. Stop exercising and rest if you feel dizzy, unusually short of breath, or experience chest pain. If you have swelling in your legs and feet, gentle walking is still okay, but avoid exercises where you are standing still for long periods. Stay hydrated according to your doctor''s fluid recommendations — this is important because kidney patients sometimes have fluid restrictions, so drink what your doctor allows, not more.\n\nBuilding exercise into your daily life\n\nYou do not have to set aside a special exercise hour if that feels overwhelming. Look for ways to move more naturally. Walk to the nearby shop instead of sending someone. Take the stairs instead of the lift if you only need to go up one or two floors. Stand up and stretch every hour if you work at a desk. Walk around the compound while talking on the phone. Do some gentle gardening — bending, digging, and carrying light watering cans all count as physical activity. Even household chores like sweeping and mopping provide gentle exercise.\n\nTracking your progress\n\nKeep a simple log of your exercise — what you did, how long, and how you felt afterward. Many patients find that within two to four weeks of regular walking, their energy levels improve noticeably. You might sleep better, feel less stressed, and find that daily tasks feel easier. Share your exercise log with your nephrologist or care team at your next appointment. They will be encouraged by your efforts, and they can adjust recommendations based on how your body is responding. Every step you take is an investment in your kidney health.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you have kidney disease, it might seem like rest is the best medicine. After all, fatigue is one of the most common and frustrating symptoms, and some days just getting through your daily routine feels like enough. But research consistently shows that regular, moderate exercise actually improves kidney health, reduces the progression of the disease, and helps manage the two conditions that most often cause kidney damage in the first place — high blood pressure and diabetes."},{"id":"s2","title":"How exercise helps your kidneys","body":"Your kidneys depend on healthy blood flow to function. When you exercise, your cardiovascular system becomes more efficient, blood pressure drops, and blood flows more smoothly to your kidneys. Exercise also improves your body''s sensitivity to insulin, which means better blood sugar control if you have diabetes. Since uncontrolled diabetes and hypertension are the top two causes of kidney disease in Kenya, managing them through exercise directly protects your remaining kidney function. Beyond the kidneys, exercise combats the bone weakness, muscle loss, and cardiovascular problems that kidney disease accelerates."},{"id":"s3","title":"Starting a walking routine","body":"Walking is the simplest, safest, and most accessible exercise for kidney patients. You do not need a gym membership or special equipment — just a pair of comfortable shoes. Start with 10-minute walks, three times a week. Walk on flat ground at a pace where you can still talk but feel slightly warmer than at rest. If you live in Nairobi, the morning hours before 7am or evening after 5pm are cooler and more comfortable. If you are in a smaller town, find a safe, flat route — perhaps around a school field, along a quiet road, or within your estate. Each week, try to add 2-3 minutes to your walk until you reach 30 minutes. Five walks of 30 minutes per week is an excellent target."},{"id":"s4","title":"Other suitable exercises","body":"Cycling — whether on a stationary bike or a regular bicycle on flat roads — is excellent for kidney patients because it is low-impact and easy on the joints. Swimming is another wonderful option if you have access to a pool, as the water supports your body weight and reduces strain. At home, you can do simple exercises without any equipment: stand up from a chair and sit back down 10 times, rest for a minute, then repeat. This builds leg strength. Try standing on one foot for 30 seconds on each side to improve balance. Do gentle arm circles — 10 forward, 10 backward — to maintain shoulder mobility."},{"id":"s5","title":"Exercise if you are on dialysis","body":"If you are on haemodialysis, you spend several hours connected to a machine multiple times per week. This does not mean exercise stops. Many dialysis patients benefit from seated exercises during treatment. Ask your care team at your dialysis centre about a seated pedal exerciser — this is a small device you can use to pedal your legs while sitting in the dialysis chair. Some centres in Nairobi and other major towns have these available. Hand grip exercises using a soft ball can maintain arm strength. Between dialysis sessions, walking remains your best friend. Even 15 minutes of walking on non-dialysis days helps."},{"id":"s6","title":"Safety precautions","body":"There are important safety rules for exercising with kidney disease. Avoid heavy weight lifting or any exercise that involves straining hard and holding your breath — this sharply raises blood pressure, which is dangerous. If you have a dialysis fistula in your arm, do not lift heavy objects with that arm or compress it during exercise. Stop exercising and rest if you feel dizzy, unusually short of breath, or experience chest pain. If you have swelling in your legs and feet, gentle walking is still okay, but avoid exercises where you are standing still for long periods. Stay hydrated according to your doctor''s fluid recommendations — this is important because kidney patients sometimes have fluid restrictions, so drink what your doctor allows, not more."},{"id":"s7","title":"Building exercise into your daily life","body":"You do not have to set aside a special exercise hour if that feels overwhelming. Look for ways to move more naturally. Walk to the nearby shop instead of sending someone. Take the stairs instead of the lift if you only need to go up one or two floors. Stand up and stretch every hour if you work at a desk. Walk around the compound while talking on the phone. Do some gentle gardening — bending, digging, and carrying light watering cans all count as physical activity. Even household chores like sweeping and mopping provide gentle exercise."},{"id":"s8","title":"Tracking your progress","body":"Keep a simple log of your exercise — what you did, how long, and how you felt afterward. Many patients find that within two to four weeks of regular walking, their energy levels improve noticeably. You might sleep better, feel less stressed, and find that daily tasks feel easier. Share your exercise log with your nephrologist or care team at your next appointment. They will be encouraged by your efforts, and they can adjust recommendations based on how your body is responding. Every step you take is an investment in your kidney health."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'stretching'),
  (E'myth-drinking-lots-of-water-will-fix-damaged-kidneys', E'Myth: Drinking lots of water will fix damaged kidneys', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The myth that drinking large amounts of water can heal or repair damaged kidneys is one of the most widespread and dangerous pieces of health misinformation in Kenya. You may have heard it from a well-meaning relative, read it in a WhatsApp group, or seen it promoted by traditional medicine sellers.', E'The myth that drinking large amounts of water can heal or repair damaged kidneys is one of the most widespread and dangerous pieces of health misinformation in Kenya. You may have heard it from a well-meaning relative, read it in a WhatsApp group, or seen it promoted by traditional medicine sellers. The logic seems to make sense on the surface — kidneys filter water, so more water should help them work better, right? Unfortunately, this is not how damaged kidneys work, and following this advice can land you in the emergency room.\n\nWhy this myth exists\n\nFor healthy people, drinking adequate water is indeed important for kidney function. Proper hydration helps healthy kidneys flush out waste products and can help prevent kidney stones. This is true. The problem is that people take this general advice — which applies to healthy kidneys — and apply it to damaged kidneys. It is like telling someone with a broken leg to walk more because walking is good for healthy legs. The advice does not transfer when the organ is not functioning properly.\n\nWhat actually happens when damaged kidneys get too much fluid\n\nHealthy kidneys can process about 800ml to 1 litre of water per hour. Damaged kidneys may only manage a fraction of that. When you drink more fluid than your kidneys can process, the excess stays in your body. This fluid builds up in your tissues, causing swelling in your ankles, legs, hands, and face — a condition called oedema. More dangerously, fluid can accumulate in your lungs, making it hard to breathe. This is called pulmonary oedema, and it is a medical emergency. Excess fluid also increases your blood volume, raising your blood pressure and putting additional strain on your heart. For someone already dealing with kidney disease, heart complications are a serious and common risk.\n\nThe reality of fluid management in kidney disease\n\nYour fluid intake when you have kidney disease must be carefully calculated by your doctor, based on several factors: your current kidney function (measured by your GFR or glomerular filtration rate), how much urine you produce in 24 hours, whether you are on dialysis, whether you have heart failure, and your current level of swelling. Some patients with advanced kidney disease are restricted to as little as 750ml to 1 litre of total fluid per day. This includes not just water, but also tea, soup, porridge, juices, and even the moisture content in fruits and cooked foods. It is a strict limit, and exceeding it has real consequences.\n\nWhy people on dialysis must be especially careful\n\nIf you are on haemodialysis, you likely dialyse two or three times per week. Between sessions, your kidneys are doing little or no filtering. Every drop of fluid you drink between sessions stays in your body until the next dialysis treatment removes it. If you drink too much, you arrive at your next session carrying several kilograms of extra fluid. The dialysis machine then has to remove this fluid quickly, which can cause your blood pressure to drop dangerously, leading to cramps, dizziness, nausea, and in severe cases, cardiovascular stress. Dialysis patients who manage their fluid intake between sessions have smoother, safer treatments and feel better overall.\n\nTips for managing thirst\n\nIf your doctor has restricted your fluids, thirst can be a real challenge. Here are some practical strategies Kenyan patients have found helpful. Suck on small ice cubes instead of drinking water — a few ice cubes give you the sensation of drinking without adding much volume. Chew sugar-free gum to keep your mouth moist. Use a smaller cup for your tea so you naturally drink less. Rinse your mouth with cold water and spit it out rather than swallowing. Avoid very salty foods like processed meats, salted omena, or heavily seasoned crisps, as salt dramatically increases thirst. When you do drink, sip slowly rather than gulping — you feel more satisfied with less.\n\nWhat to tell people who give you this advice\n\nWhen someone tells you to drink more water to heal your kidneys, you can politely explain that your doctor has given you a specific fluid allowance based on your kidney function, and that exceeding it could cause you to end up in hospital. You do not need to argue or explain the medical details. A simple "my doctor has tested my kidneys and told me exactly how much to drink" is enough. If they persist, you can suggest they ask a nephrologist themselves.\n\nThe bottom line\n\nThere is no amount of water that can reverse kidney damage. Once kidney tissue is scarred or destroyed, it does not regenerate. The treatments that actually slow kidney disease progression are controlling blood pressure (often with medications like ACE inhibitors or ARBs), managing blood sugar if you have diabetes, taking prescribed medications consistently, following dietary guidelines from your renal dietitian, and attending all your clinic appointments. These are the things that make a real difference — not drinking extra water. Always follow your nephrologist''s specific fluid recommendations. Your kidneys will thank you.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The myth that drinking large amounts of water can heal or repair damaged kidneys is one of the most widespread and dangerous pieces of health misinformation in Kenya. You may have heard it from a well-meaning relative, read it in a WhatsApp group, or seen it promoted by traditional medicine sellers. The logic seems to make sense on the surface — kidneys filter water, so more water should help them work better, right? Unfortunately, this is not how damaged kidneys work, and following this advice can land you in the emergency room."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why this myth exists","body":"For healthy people, drinking adequate water is indeed important for kidney function. Proper hydration helps healthy kidneys flush out waste products and can help prevent kidney stones. This is true. The problem is that people take this general advice — which applies to healthy kidneys — and apply it to damaged kidneys. It is like telling someone with a broken leg to walk more because walking is good for healthy legs. The advice does not transfer when the organ is not functioning properly."},{"id":"s3","title":"What actually happens when damaged kidneys get too much fluid","body":"Healthy kidneys can process about 800ml to 1 litre of water per hour. Damaged kidneys may only manage a fraction of that. When you drink more fluid than your kidneys can process, the excess stays in your body. This fluid builds up in your tissues, causing swelling in your ankles, legs, hands, and face — a condition called oedema. More dangerously, fluid can accumulate in your lungs, making it hard to breathe. This is called pulmonary oedema, and it is a medical emergency. Excess fluid also increases your blood volume, raising your blood pressure and putting additional strain on your heart. For someone already dealing with kidney disease, heart complications are a serious and common risk."},{"id":"s4","title":"The reality of fluid management in kidney disease","body":"Your fluid intake when you have kidney disease must be carefully calculated by your doctor, based on several factors: your current kidney function (measured by your GFR or glomerular filtration rate), how much urine you produce in 24 hours, whether you are on dialysis, whether you have heart failure, and your current level of swelling. Some patients with advanced kidney disease are restricted to as little as 750ml to 1 litre of total fluid per day. This includes not just water, but also tea, soup, porridge, juices, and even the moisture content in fruits and cooked foods. It is a strict limit, and exceeding it has real consequences."},{"id":"s5","title":"Why people on dialysis must be especially careful","body":"If you are on haemodialysis, you likely dialyse two or three times per week. Between sessions, your kidneys are doing little or no filtering. Every drop of fluid you drink between sessions stays in your body until the next dialysis treatment removes it. If you drink too much, you arrive at your next session carrying several kilograms of extra fluid. The dialysis machine then has to remove this fluid quickly, which can cause your blood pressure to drop dangerously, leading to cramps, dizziness, nausea, and in severe cases, cardiovascular stress. Dialysis patients who manage their fluid intake between sessions have smoother, safer treatments and feel better overall."},{"id":"s6","title":"Tips for managing thirst","body":"If your doctor has restricted your fluids, thirst can be a real challenge. Here are some practical strategies Kenyan patients have found helpful. Suck on small ice cubes instead of drinking water — a few ice cubes give you the sensation of drinking without adding much volume. Chew sugar-free gum to keep your mouth moist. Use a smaller cup for your tea so you naturally drink less. Rinse your mouth with cold water and spit it out rather than swallowing. Avoid very salty foods like processed meats, salted omena, or heavily seasoned crisps, as salt dramatically increases thirst. When you do drink, sip slowly rather than gulping — you feel more satisfied with less."},{"id":"s7","title":"What to tell people who give you this advice","body":"When someone tells you to drink more water to heal your kidneys, you can politely explain that your doctor has given you a specific fluid allowance based on your kidney function, and that exceeding it could cause you to end up in hospital. You do not need to argue or explain the medical details. A simple \\"my doctor has tested my kidneys and told me exactly how much to drink\\" is enough. If they persist, you can suggest they ask a nephrologist themselves."},{"id":"s8","title":"The bottom line","body":"There is no amount of water that can reverse kidney damage. Once kidney tissue is scarred or destroyed, it does not regenerate. The treatments that actually slow kidney disease progression are controlling blood pressure (often with medications like ACE inhibitors or ARBs), managing blood sugar if you have diabetes, taking prescribed medications consistently, following dietary guidelines from your renal dietitian, and attending all your clinic appointments. These are the things that make a real difference — not drinking extra water. Always follow your nephrologist''s specific fluid recommendations. Your kidneys will thank you."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'health-facts'),
  (E'living-with-dietary-restrictions-without-feeling-deprived', E'Living with dietary restrictions without feeling deprived', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'When you are first told that you have kidney disease and need to follow a restricted diet, it can feel like the world of food — one of life''s simplest pleasures — has suddenly closed its doors to you.', E'When you are first told that you have kidney disease and need to follow a restricted diet, it can feel like the world of food — one of life''s simplest pleasures — has suddenly closed its doors to you. In Kenya, food is deeply tied to culture, family, and identity. Sharing a meal of ugali, sukuma wiki, and nyama choma is not just about nutrition — it is about belonging. Being told you cannot eat certain foods can feel like being excluded from your own life.\n\nThe grief is real\n\nLet us be honest about what you may be feeling. You might be mourning the loss of your favourite ripe banana in the morning, the chapati your mother makes, the hearty githeri that reminds you of home, the pilau at family celebrations, or the avocado you used to enjoy with every meal. These are not trivial losses. Food is tied to memories, comfort, and joy. When a doctor tells you to limit bananas, reduce beans, watch your tomatoes, and restrict your fluids, it can feel like a punishment for being sick. You did not ask for kidney disease, and now you are losing things that brought you happiness. It is okay to grieve this. Denying your feelings or being told to just get over it does not help.\n\nThe social challenge\n\nKenyan social life revolves around food. When you visit someone, they offer you tea and something to eat. At family gatherings, there is pilau, biryani, or a nyama choma feast. At church functions, there is food. At funerals, there is food. Declining food or eating differently from everyone else can draw attention and questions. You might feel embarrassed explaining your diet to relatives. Some people may not understand — "just eat a little, it will not hurt" — without realising that for you, it genuinely could. Learning to navigate these social situations takes time and practice.\n\nShifting your mindset from restriction to discovery\n\nOne of the most powerful things you can do is gradually shift your focus from what you cannot eat to what you can. This is not about pretending you do not miss certain foods — it is about finding genuine enjoyment in the foods that are safe for you. Cabbage, for example, is a low-potassium vegetable that can be absolutely delicious when prepared well. Try shredding it finely and making a crispy coleslaw with lemon juice, a touch of salt (within your sodium limit), and a sprinkle of black pepper. Roast carrots with garlic and a drizzle of oil until they caramelise and turn sweet. Make a fish stew with onions, ginger, garlic, and just a small amount of tomato — the spices carry the flavour. Bake chicken with cumin, coriander, and a squeeze of lemon. These meals are not lesser versions of the foods you used to eat — they are genuinely delicious in their own right.\n\nInvolving your family\n\nYour dietary restrictions do not have to isolate you at mealtimes. Talk to whoever cooks in your household — whether that is you, your spouse, your parent, or a house helper — and explain which foods you need to limit and why. Many kidney-friendly meals are perfectly enjoyable for the whole family. A dinner of rice, grilled fish, and steamed vegetables works for everyone. The family can add higher-potassium sides like avocado or a banana for themselves while you have an apple or some watermelon. When your family understands your diet, they become your support system rather than a source of temptation.\n\nWorking with a dietitian\n\nA renal dietitian can be transformative. They understand both the medical requirements of your kidney condition and the practical realities of eating in Kenya. A good dietitian will not give you a generic list of foods to avoid — they will sit with you, ask what you normally eat, understand your budget and preferences, and create a meal plan that works for your specific life. Many county hospitals and national referral hospitals have dietitians. The consultation fee is often modest — sometimes included in your clinic visit under NHIF or SHA. Ask your nephrologist for a referral if you have not seen one yet.\n\nFinding community\n\nYou are not alone in this experience. There are kidney disease support groups in Kenya, both in person and on social media. Connecting with other patients who understand the dietary challenges can be incredibly helpful. They share recipes, tips for eating out, and strategies for handling social situations. Knowing that someone else has navigated the same frustrations and found a way to enjoy food again can give you hope during the harder days.\n\nPermission to have difficult days\n\nSome days you will handle your diet with grace and creativity. Other days you will feel angry, sad, or just plain tired of thinking about potassium and protein and sodium. Both kinds of days are normal. If you slip up and eat something you should not have, do not punish yourself — just return to your plan at the next meal. If the emotional weight feels too heavy, talk to someone. Your doctor, a counsellor, a pastor, a trusted friend, or a support group. Living with dietary restrictions is a daily practice, not a test you can fail. Be patient and kind with yourself.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you are first told that you have kidney disease and need to follow a restricted diet, it can feel like the world of food — one of life''s simplest pleasures — has suddenly closed its doors to you. In Kenya, food is deeply tied to culture, family, and identity. Sharing a meal of ugali, sukuma wiki, and nyama choma is not just about nutrition — it is about belonging. Being told you cannot eat certain foods can feel like being excluded from your own life."},{"id"