a drizzle of oil until they caramelise and turn sweet. Make a fish stew with onions, ginger, garlic, and just a small amount of tomato — the spices carry the flavour. Bake chicken with cumin, coriander, and a squeeze of lemon. These meals are not lesser versions of the foods you used to eat — they are genuinely delicious in their own right."},{"id":"s5","title":"Involving your family","body":"Your dietary restrictions do not have to isolate you at mealtimes. Talk to whoever cooks in your household — whether that is you, your spouse, your parent, or a house helper — and explain which foods you need to limit and why. Many kidney-friendly meals are perfectly enjoyable for the whole family. A dinner of rice, grilled fish, and steamed vegetables works for everyone. The family can add higher-potassium sides like avocado or a banana for themselves while you have an apple or some watermelon. When your family understands your diet, they become your support system rather than a source of temptation."},{"id":"s6","title":"Working with a dietitian","body":"A renal dietitian can be transformative. They understand both the medical requirements of your kidney condition and the practical realities of eating in Kenya. A good dietitian will not give you a generic list of foods to avoid — they will sit with you, ask what you normally eat, understand your budget and preferences, and create a meal plan that works for your specific life. Many county hospitals and national referral hospitals have dietitians. The consultation fee is often modest — sometimes included in your clinic visit under NHIF or SHA. Ask your nephrologist for a referral if you have not seen one yet."},{"id":"s7","title":"Finding community","body":"You are not alone in this experience. There are kidney disease support groups in Kenya, both in person and on social media. Connecting with other patients who understand the dietary challenges can be incredibly helpful. They share recipes, tips for eating out, and strategies for handling social situations. Knowing that someone else has navigated the same frustrations and found a way to enjoy food again can give you hope during the harder days."},{"id":"s8","title":"Permission to have difficult days","body":"Some days you will handle your diet with grace and creativity. Other days you will feel angry, sad, or just plain tired of thinking about potassium and protein and sodium. Both kinds of days are normal. If you slip up and eat something you should not have, do not punish yourself — just return to your plan at the next meal. If the emotional weight feels too heavy, talk to someone. Your doctor, a counsellor, a pastor, a trusted friend, or a support group. Living with dietary restrictions is a daily practice, not a test you can fail. Be patient and kind with yourself."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'family-support'),
  (E'signs-your-kidneys-need-attention-right-now', E'Signs your kidneys need attention right now', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'Kidney disease can be a quiet condition. Unlike a broken bone or a wound you can see, kidney damage often progresses without dramatic symptoms until it reaches an advanced stage.', E'Kidney disease can be a quiet condition. Unlike a broken bone or a wound you can see, kidney damage often progresses without dramatic symptoms until it reaches an advanced stage. That is why learning to notice and respond to subtle warning signs between your clinic visits is so important. Your body gives signals when something is changing, and catching those signals early can make the difference between a medication adjustment and a hospital admission.\n\nSwelling — the most visible sign\n\nOne of the earliest and most noticeable warning signs is swelling, medically called oedema. Pay attention to your ankles and feet — if your shoes feel tighter than usual or you can see the imprint of your socks on your skin, that is swelling. Check your face in the morning — puffiness around your eyes, especially if it was not there before, can indicate fluid retention. Try pressing your finger into the skin on your shin for about five seconds, then release. If the dent stays for several seconds (called pitting oedema), your body is holding extra fluid that your kidneys should be removing. Swelling means your kidneys may not be filtering well enough, or you may have taken in too much fluid or salt.\n\nChanges in your urine\n\nYour urine tells an important story about your kidney function. Normal urine is a pale to medium yellow colour. Watch for these changes: foamy or bubbly urine that looks like beaten egg whites can indicate protein leaking into your urine, a sign of kidney damage. Dark brown or cola-coloured urine could mean blood in the urine, which needs immediate investigation. Very pale urine in large quantities when you have not been drinking much could mean your kidneys are not concentrating urine properly. Decreased urine output — especially if you are producing noticeably less than usual — is a serious warning sign. On the other hand, getting up multiple times at night to urinate (more than once or twice) can be an early sign of kidney problems.\n\nFatigue that rest does not fix\n\nKidney disease causes a specific kind of fatigue that is different from ordinary tiredness. When your kidneys are struggling, waste products build up in your blood, making you feel constantly drained. You might sleep for eight hours and wake up feeling like you barely slept. Tasks that used to be easy — walking to the shops, cooking a meal, carrying water — suddenly feel exhausting. This fatigue often worsens gradually, so you may not even notice how much your energy has declined until someone points it out. If rest, good sleep, and adequate nutrition are not restoring your energy, your kidney function may have changed.\n\nItching\n\nPersistent, unexplained itching — especially if it is generalised rather than in one spot — is a common symptom of declining kidney function. When your kidneys cannot adequately remove waste products and excess minerals (particularly phosphorus) from your blood, these substances build up and irritate your skin from the inside. The itching can be intense and disruptive, especially at night. It is different from a rash caused by an allergy or a skin condition — there may be no visible rash at all, just relentless itching. If you are scratching constantly and there is no obvious external cause, tell your doctor.\n\nMetallic taste and nausea\n\nA persistent metallic or ammonia-like taste in your mouth, especially in the morning, can indicate that waste products (urea) are building up in your blood — a condition called uraemia. You might also notice that food tastes different, or that you have lost your appetite entirely. Nausea and occasional vomiting, particularly in the morning, are associated signs. If you find that even the thought of eating ugali or your favourite foods makes you feel queasy, and this persists for more than a day or two, it is worth reporting to your doctor.\n\nWhat to do when you notice these signs\n\nFirst, write down exactly what you have observed. Be specific: "My ankles have been swollen for three days," or "I have been waking up four times to urinate every night this week," or "My urine has been foamy for the past two weeks." Note when the symptom started and whether it is getting better, worse, or staying the same. Second, do not wait for your next scheduled appointment. Call your doctor''s office or clinic directly. Hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital, Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, and many county hospitals have renal clinics and phone numbers you can call for advice. Third, if you are experiencing severe symptoms — extreme swelling, difficulty breathing, inability to urinate, persistent vomiting, or confusion — go to the emergency department immediately. These could indicate acute kidney failure or dangerous fluid overload.\n\nBuilding a monitoring habit\n\nMake self-monitoring part of your daily routine. Each morning, check your face for puffiness. When you put on your shoes, notice if they feel tight. When you use the bathroom, glance at your urine. Rate your energy level. These quick checks take less than a minute but can alert you to problems days or weeks before they become emergencies. Keep a small notebook or use the notes app on your phone. When you visit your doctor, this record is invaluable — it gives them a window into your health between appointments that no single blood test can provide.\n\nYour role in your own care\n\nYou are with your body 24 hours a day, seven days a week. Your doctor sees you for perhaps 15-30 minutes every few months. You are the first line of defence in detecting changes. Trust your observations. If something feels different or wrong, it probably is. Speaking up early is never an overreaction — it is responsible self-care.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Kidney disease can be a quiet condition. Unlike a broken bone or a wound you can see, kidney damage often progresses without dramatic symptoms until it reaches an advanced stage. That is why learning to notice and respond to subtle warning signs between your clinic visits is so important. Your body gives signals when something is changing, and catching those signals early can make the difference between a medication adjustment and a hospital admission."},{"id":"s2","title":"Swelling — the most visible sign","body":"One of the earliest and most noticeable warning signs is swelling, medically called oedema. Pay attention to your ankles and feet — if your shoes feel tighter than usual or you can see the imprint of your socks on your skin, that is swelling. Check your face in the morning — puffiness around your eyes, especially if it was not there before, can indicate fluid retention. Try pressing your finger into the skin on your shin for about five seconds, then release. If the dent stays for several seconds (called pitting oedema), your body is holding extra fluid that your kidneys should be removing. Swelling means your kidneys may not be filtering well enough, or you may have taken in too much fluid or salt."},{"id":"s3","title":"Changes in your urine","body":"Your urine tells an important story about your kidney function. Normal urine is a pale to medium yellow colour. Watch for these changes: foamy or bubbly urine that looks like beaten egg whites can indicate protein leaking into your urine, a sign of kidney damage. Dark brown or cola-coloured urine could mean blood in the urine, which needs immediate investigation. Very pale urine in large quantities when you have not been drinking much could mean your kidneys are not concentrating urine properly. Decreased urine output — especially if you are producing noticeably less than usual — is a serious warning sign. On the other hand, getting up multiple times at night to urinate (more than once or twice) can be an early sign of kidney problems."},{"id":"s4","title":"Fatigue that rest does not fix","body":"Kidney disease causes a specific kind of fatigue that is different from ordinary tiredness. When your kidneys are struggling, waste products build up in your blood, making you feel constantly drained. You might sleep for eight hours and wake up feeling like you barely slept. Tasks that used to be easy — walking to the shops, cooking a meal, carrying water — suddenly feel exhausting. This fatigue often worsens gradually, so you may not even notice how much your energy has declined until someone points it out. If rest, good sleep, and adequate nutrition are not restoring your energy, your kidney function may have changed."},{"id":"s5","title":"Itching","body":"Persistent, unexplained itching — especially if it is generalised rather than in one spot — is a common symptom of declining kidney function. When your kidneys cannot adequately remove waste products and excess minerals (particularly phosphorus) from your blood, these substances build up and irritate your skin from the inside. The itching can be intense and disruptive, especially at night. It is different from a rash caused by an allergy or a skin condition — there may be no visible rash at all, just relentless itching. If you are scratching constantly and there is no obvious external cause, tell your doctor."},{"id":"s6","title":"Metallic taste and nausea","body":"A persistent metallic or ammonia-like taste in your mouth, especially in the morning, can indicate that waste products (urea) are building up in your blood — a condition called uraemia. You might also notice that food tastes different, or that you have lost your appetite entirely. Nausea and occasional vomiting, particularly in the morning, are associated signs. If you find that even the thought of eating ugali or your favourite foods makes you feel queasy, and this persists for more than a day or two, it is worth reporting to your doctor."},{"id":"s7","title":"What to do when you notice these signs","body":"First, write down exactly what you have observed. Be specific: \\"My ankles have been swollen for three days,\\" or \\"I have been waking up four times to urinate every night this week,\\" or \\"My urine has been foamy for the past two weeks.\\" Note when the symptom started and whether it is getting better, worse, or staying the same. Second, do not wait for your next scheduled appointment. Call your doctor''s office or clinic directly. Hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital, Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, and many county hospitals have renal clinics and phone numbers you can call for advice. Third, if you are experiencing severe symptoms — extreme swelling, difficulty breathing, inability to urinate, persistent vomiting, or confusion — go to the emergency department immediately. These could indicate acute kidney failure or dangerous fluid overload."},{"id":"s8","title":"Building a monitoring habit","body":"Make self-monitoring part of your daily routine. Each morning, check your face for puffiness. When you put on your shoes, notice if they feel tight. When you use the bathroom, glance at your urine. Rate your energy level. These quick checks take less than a minute but can alert you to problems days or weeks before they become emergencies. Keep a small notebook or use the notes app on your phone. When you visit your doctor, this record is invaluable — it gives them a window into your health between appointments that no single blood test can provide."},{"id":"s9","title":"Your role in your own care","body":"You are with your body 24 hours a day, seven days a week. Your doctor sees you for perhaps 15-30 minutes every few months. You are the first line of defence in detecting changes. Trust your observations. If something feels different or wrong, it probably is. Speaking up early is never an overreaction — it is responsible self-care."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'glucose-monitor'),
  (E'kidney-disease-is-a-marathon-not-a-sprint', E'Kidney disease is a marathon, not a sprint', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'Being told you have chronic kidney disease can feel like the ground has shifted beneath you. Perhaps your doctor showed you blood test results with elevated creatinine and a low GFR, and explained that your kidneys are not working as well as they should.', E'Being told you have chronic kidney disease can feel like the ground has shifted beneath you. Perhaps your doctor showed you blood test results with elevated creatinine and a low GFR, and explained that your kidneys are not working as well as they should. Perhaps you have been living with this diagnosis for months or years, and some days it still feels overwhelming. However long you have known, learning to live well with kidney disease is a marathon — a long, steady journey that rewards patience and consistency over dramatic gestures.\n\nThe shock of diagnosis\n\nMany people diagnosed with kidney disease in Kenya are caught off guard. Kidney disease is often called a silent condition because it can progress significantly before you notice any symptoms. You may have gone to the doctor for something else entirely — high blood pressure, diabetes management, or a routine check-up — and discovered that your kidneys were already damaged. The news can feel unreal. You may have thought kidney disease was something that happened to other people, older people, or people who were much sicker than you. Processing this diagnosis takes time, and there is no right speed for acceptance.\n\nWhat acceptance does not mean\n\nAcceptance does not mean giving up. It does not mean resigning yourself to a grim future. It does not mean pretending you are fine when you are not. Acceptance means acknowledging reality — "I have kidney disease, and this is part of my life now" — and then choosing to engage with that reality rather than avoiding it. It means taking your medications even when you feel fine. It means attending your clinic appointments even when you would rather not. It means adjusting your diet even when it is inconvenient. Acceptance is active, not passive.\n\nThe power of daily consistency\n\nKidney disease progression can often be slowed dramatically by consistent daily actions. Taking your blood pressure medication every morning at the same time. Checking your blood sugar if you have diabetes and adjusting as needed. Eating kidney-friendly meals — perhaps rice with a small portion of grilled fish, cabbage sauteed with onions and garlic, and an apple for dessert instead of a banana. Drinking the amount of fluid your doctor has recommended, not more. Going for a daily walk. These actions may feel small and unremarkable, but compounded over weeks, months, and years, they make an enormous difference. Many Kenyans live for decades with chronic kidney disease by consistently managing these daily basics.\n\nPerfection is not the goal\n\nYou are going to have bad days. You will eat something you should not have at a family gathering. You will miss a medication dose because life got busy. You will skip your walk because of the rain or because you were simply too tired. These slip-ups do not erase the good work you have been doing. What matters is the overall pattern. If you take your medication six days out of seven, that is still excellent. If you follow your diet most of the time but have pilau at a wedding, that single meal will not destroy your kidneys. The danger is not in occasional slip-ups — it is in giving up entirely because you feel you have already failed.\n\nAdjusting daily life practically\n\nLiving with kidney disease means making practical adjustments that become second nature over time. You might keep a small pill organiser so you never forget your morning medications. You learn to scan a restaurant menu for kidney-friendly options — grilled fish, plain rice, vegetables — rather than feeling restricted. You carry a water bottle marked with your daily allowance so you can track your fluid intake visually. You learn to politely decline a second cup of tea at someone''s home without making it awkward. You keep a food journal for the first few months until your new eating patterns feel natural. These adjustments are not burdens — they are skills you are developing.\n\nThe emotional journey\n\nAcceptance is not a destination you arrive at once. It is a process that cycles. Some weeks you will feel at peace with your diagnosis, managing well, and even proud of how you are handling things. Other weeks, you might feel angry about the unfairness of it all, or sad about the lifestyle changes, or anxious about the future. Both experiences are normal. Talking to someone — a counsellor, a fellow kidney patient, a trusted friend, or a faith leader — can help enormously. Many hospitals in Kenya, including county referral hospitals, have social workers or patient support coordinators who can connect you with counselling services. NHIF and SHA may cover some mental health visits — ask at your next appointment.\n\nConnecting with others\n\nThere are kidney disease support groups in Kenya where patients share their experiences, tips, and encouragement. Whether it is a group at your hospital, a WhatsApp group for kidney patients, or a formal organisation, connecting with people who truly understand your daily reality can be a source of strength. Hearing from someone who has lived with kidney disease for ten or fifteen years, who still works, still enjoys family life, and still finds joy in food and daily living, can reshape your perspective in a way that no medical lecture can.\n\nLooking at the long view\n\nChronic kidney disease is measured in stages from 1 to 5, and many people are diagnosed at stages 2 or 3, where there is significant room to slow progression. Even at later stages, dialysis and transplant options exist. The medical management of kidney disease continues to improve. New medications become available. Dialysis technology gets better. Transplant programmes grow. You are living with this condition at a time when more can be done for kidney patients than ever before. Your job is to show up every day, make the best choices you can, and trust that consistency compounds. That is how you run this marathon.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Being told you have chronic kidney disease can feel like the ground has shifted beneath you. Perhaps your doctor showed you blood test results with elevated creatinine and a low GFR, and explained that your kidneys are not working as well as they should. Perhaps you have been living with this diagnosis for months or years, and some days it still feels overwhelming. However long you have known, learning to live well with kidney disease is a marathon — a long, steady journey that rewards patience and consistency over dramatic gestures."},{"id":"s2","title":"The shock of diagnosis","body":"Many people diagnosed with kidney disease in Kenya are caught off guard. Kidney disease is often called a silent condition because it can progress significantly before you notice any symptoms. You may have gone to the doctor for something else entirely — high blood pressure, diabetes management, or a routine check-up — and discovered that your kidneys were already damaged. The news can feel unreal. You may have thought kidney disease was something that happened to other people, older people, or people who were much sicker than you. Processing this diagnosis takes time, and there is no right speed for acceptance."},{"id":"s3","title":"What acceptance does not mean","body":"Acceptance does not mean giving up. It does not mean resigning yourself to a grim future. It does not mean pretending you are fine when you are not. Acceptance means acknowledging reality — \\"I have kidney disease, and this is part of my life now\\" — and then choosing to engage with that reality rather than avoiding it. It means taking your medications even when you feel fine. It means attending your clinic appointments even when you would rather not. It means adjusting your diet even when it is inconvenient. Acceptance is active, not passive."},{"id":"s4","title":"The power of daily consistency","body":"Kidney disease progression can often be slowed dramatically by consistent daily actions. Taking your blood pressure medication every morning at the same time. Checking your blood sugar if you have diabetes and adjusting as needed. Eating kidney-friendly meals — perhaps rice with a small portion of grilled fish, cabbage sauteed with onions and garlic, and an apple for dessert instead of a banana. Drinking the amount of fluid your doctor has recommended, not more. Going for a daily walk. These actions may feel small and unremarkable, but compounded over weeks, months, and years, they make an enormous difference. Many Kenyans live for decades with chronic kidney disease by consistently managing these daily basics."},{"id":"s5","title":"Perfection is not the goal","body":"You are going to have bad days. You will eat something you should not have at a family gathering. You will miss a medication dose because life got busy. You will skip your walk because of the rain or because you were simply too tired. These slip-ups do not erase the good work you have been doing. What matters is the overall pattern. If you take your medication six days out of seven, that is still excellent. If you follow your diet most of the time but have pilau at a wedding, that single meal will not destroy your kidneys. The danger is not in occasional slip-ups — it is in giving up entirely because you feel you have already failed."},{"id":"s6","title":"Adjusting daily life practically","body":"Living with kidney disease means making practical adjustments that become second nature over time. You might keep a small pill organiser so you never forget your morning medications. You learn to scan a restaurant menu for kidney-friendly options — grilled fish, plain rice, vegetables — rather than feeling restricted. You carry a water bottle marked with your daily allowance so you can track your fluid intake visually. You learn to politely decline a second cup of tea at someone''s home without making it awkward. You keep a food journal for the first few months until your new eating patterns feel natural. These adjustments are not burdens — they are skills you are developing."},{"id":"s7","title":"The emotional journey","body":"Acceptance is not a destination you arrive at once. It is a process that cycles. Some weeks you will feel at peace with your diagnosis, managing well, and even proud of how you are handling things. Other weeks, you might feel angry about the unfairness of it all, or sad about the lifestyle changes, or anxious about the future. Both experiences are normal. Talking to someone — a counsellor, a fellow kidney patient, a trusted friend, or a faith leader — can help enormously. Many hospitals in Kenya, including county referral hospitals, have social workers or patient support coordinators who can connect you with counselling services. NHIF and SHA may cover some mental health visits — ask at your next appointment."},{"id":"s8","title":"Connecting with others","body":"There are kidney disease support groups in Kenya where patients share their experiences, tips, and encouragement. Whether it is a group at your hospital, a WhatsApp group for kidney patients, or a formal organisation, connecting with people who truly understand your daily reality can be a source of strength. Hearing from someone who has lived with kidney disease for ten or fifteen years, who still works, still enjoys family life, and still finds joy in food and daily living, can reshape your perspective in a way that no medical lecture can."},{"id":"s9","title":"Looking at the long view","body":"Chronic kidney disease is measured in stages from 1 to 5, and many people are diagnosed at stages 2 or 3, where there is significant room to slow progression. Even at later stages, dialysis and transplant options exist. The medical management of kidney disease continues to improve. New medications become available. Dialysis technology gets better. Transplant programmes grow. You are living with this condition at a time when more can be done for kidney patients than ever before. Your job is to show up every day, make the best choices you can, and trust that consistency compounds. That is how you run this marathon."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'journaling'),
  (E'stable-creatinine-levels-for-six-months', E'Stable creatinine levels for six months', E'KIDNEY_DISEASE', '{"KIDNEY_DISEASE"}', E'MILESTONE', E'If your latest blood tests show that your creatinine level has remained stable over the past six months, pause for a moment and take that in. In the world of kidney disease, stable creatinine is genuinely wonderful news.', E'If your latest blood tests show that your creatinine level has remained stable over the past six months, pause for a moment and take that in. In the world of kidney disease, stable creatinine is genuinely wonderful news. It means your kidney function is holding steady. Your kidneys are not getting worse. And that stability is not an accident — it is the direct result of the choices you make every single day.\n\nUnderstanding what creatinine tells you\n\nCreatinine is a waste product created by normal muscle activity. Healthy kidneys filter creatinine out of your blood and into your urine. When kidney function declines, creatinine levels in the blood rise because the kidneys cannot clear it as efficiently. Your doctor uses your creatinine level, along with your estimated GFR (glomerular filtration rate), to track how well your kidneys are working over time. A rising creatinine means your kidneys are struggling more. A stable creatinine means they are holding steady. And that is exactly what your care team wants to see.\n\nWhat stable means in real terms\n\nFor many kidney patients in Kenya and around the world, the natural progression of kidney disease is a gradual decline in function. Without management, creatinine tends to creep upward over months and years. The fact that yours has stayed level for six months means you are successfully fighting that natural progression. You are slowing down or pausing the disease. In kidney care, this is a significant achievement. It means the strategies you and your doctor have put in place are working.\n\nThe daily actions behind this result\n\nThis stability did not happen by itself. Think about what you have been doing. You have been taking your blood pressure medication consistently — perhaps an ACE inhibitor or ARB that specifically protects your kidneys. You have been monitoring your blood pressure, maybe using a home monitor you purchased for KES 3,000-5,000 at a pharmacy. If you have diabetes, you have been managing your blood sugar with medication, diet, and monitoring. You have been following your dietary guidelines — watching your potassium intake, moderating your protein portions, reducing salt. You have been attending your clinic appointments, getting your blood tests done on schedule, and listening to your nephrologist''s advice. Each of these actions, taken day after day, contributed to this result.\n\nThe financial investment you have made\n\nLet us also acknowledge the financial commitment. Kidney care in Kenya is not cheap. Regular blood tests at NHIF or SHA-accredited facilities still involve transport costs and sometimes co-payments. Medications add up — even with insurance coverage, some patients pay KES 2,000-5,000 per month on medications. The dietary changes may mean buying different foods or spending more time preparing meals. You have invested real money and real time in your health, and this stable creatinine is the return on that investment. Every shilling spent on managing your condition is a shilling you are not spending on emergency hospital admissions or dialysis sessions.\n\nWhat this means for your future\n\nStable kidney function means more time — more time before more aggressive interventions like dialysis might become necessary, more time with your family, more time doing the things you enjoy. Every month of stable function is a month where your kidneys are supporting your body, you are avoiding the burden and cost of dialysis, and you are living life more fully. For context, dialysis in Kenya can cost KES 5,000-10,000 per session, with most patients needing two to three sessions per week. Keeping your kidneys stable is not just a health achievement — it is an enormous financial one as well.\n\nSharing the credit\n\nThis milestone also belongs to the people who have supported you. Perhaps your spouse has learned to cook kidney-friendly meals, adjusting recipes for the whole family. Perhaps a family member accompanies you to your appointments. Perhaps a friend reminds you to take your medication. Perhaps your doctor has been thoughtful and thorough in managing your care, adjusting medications as needed and listening to your concerns. Kidney disease management is a team effort, and your stable result reflects everyone''s contribution.\n\nKeeping the momentum going\n\nThe temptation when things are stable is to relax — to skip a medication day here, indulge in high-potassium foods there, or postpone a clinic visit. Please resist this temptation. Stability is maintained by the very actions that created it. Continue taking your medication at the same time every day. Keep following your dietary plan. Attend your next appointment and your next blood test. Walk regularly. Manage your blood pressure and blood sugar. The same consistency that gave you six months of stability can give you years.\n\nCelebrating properly\n\nYou deserve to celebrate this result. Tell your family that your kidneys are stable and that you are proud of the work you have all done together. Allow yourself to feel good about this accomplishment. In a condition that can feel defined by limitations and restrictions, stable creatinine is a clear, measurable victory. It is proof that what you are doing matters. Your nephrologist is pleased, your care team is encouraged, and you should be too. Well done — now keep going. The next six months are waiting, and you already know how to do this.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If your latest blood tests show that your creatinine level has remained stable over the past six months, pause for a moment and take that in. In the world of kidney disease, stable creatinine is genuinely wonderful news. It means your kidney function is holding steady. Your kidneys are not getting worse. And that stability is not an accident — it is the direct result of the choices you make every single day."},{"id":"s2","title":"Understanding what creatinine tells you","body":"Creatinine is a waste product created by normal muscle activity. Healthy kidneys filter creatinine out of your blood and into your urine. When kidney function declines, creatinine levels in the blood rise because the kidneys cannot clear it as efficiently. Your doctor uses your creatinine level, along with your estimated GFR (glomerular filtration rate), to track how well your kidneys are working over time. A rising creatinine means your kidneys are struggling more. A stable creatinine means they are holding steady. And that is exactly what your care team wants to see."},{"id":"s3","title":"What stable means in real terms","body":"For many kidney patients in Kenya and around the world, the natural progression of kidney disease is a gradual decline in function. Without management, creatinine tends to creep upward over months and years. The fact that yours has stayed level for six months means you are successfully fighting that natural progression. You are slowing down or pausing the disease. In kidney care, this is a significant achievement. It means the strategies you and your doctor have put in place are working."},{"id":"s4","title":"The daily actions behind this result","body":"This stability did not happen by itself. Think about what you have been doing. You have been taking your blood pressure medication consistently — perhaps an ACE inhibitor or ARB that specifically protects your kidneys. You have been monitoring your blood pressure, maybe using a home monitor you purchased for KES 3,000-5,000 at a pharmacy. If you have diabetes, you have been managing your blood sugar with medication, diet, and monitoring. You have been following your dietary guidelines — watching your potassium intake, moderating your protein portions, reducing salt. You have been attending your clinic appointments, getting your blood tests done on schedule, and listening to your nephrologist''s advice. Each of these actions, taken day after day, contributed to this result."},{"id":"s5","title":"The financial investment you have made","body":"Let us also acknowledge the financial commitment. Kidney care in Kenya is not cheap. Regular blood tests at NHIF or SHA-accredited facilities still involve transport costs and sometimes co-payments. Medications add up — even with insurance coverage, some patients pay KES 2,000-5,000 per month on medications. The dietary changes may mean buying different foods or spending more time preparing meals. You have invested real money and real time in your health, and this stable creatinine is the return on that investment. Every shilling spent on managing your condition is a shilling you are not spending on emergency hospital admissions or dialysis sessions."},{"id":"s6","title":"What this means for your future","body":"Stable kidney function means more time — more time before more aggressive interventions like dialysis might become necessary, more time with your family, more time doing the things you enjoy. Every month of stable function is a month where your kidneys are supporting your body, you are avoiding the burden and cost of dialysis, and you are living life more fully. For context, dialysis in Kenya can cost KES 5,000-10,000 per session, with most patients needing two to three sessions per week. Keeping your kidneys stable is not just a health achievement — it is an enormous financial one as well."},{"id":"s7","title":"Sharing the credit","body":"This milestone also belongs to the people who have supported you. Perhaps your spouse has learned to cook kidney-friendly meals, adjusting recipes for the whole family. Perhaps a family member accompanies you to your appointments. Perhaps a friend reminds you to take your medication. Perhaps your doctor has been thoughtful and thorough in managing your care, adjusting medications as needed and listening to your concerns. Kidney disease management is a team effort, and your stable result reflects everyone''s contribution."},{"id":"s8","title":"Keeping the momentum going","body":"The temptation when things are stable is to relax — to skip a medication day here, indulge in high-potassium foods there, or postpone a clinic visit. Please resist this temptation. Stability is maintained by the very actions that created it. Continue taking your medication at the same time every day. Keep following your dietary plan. Attend your next appointment and your next blood test. Walk regularly. Manage your blood pressure and blood sugar. The same consistency that gave you six months of stability can give you years."},{"id":"s9","title":"Celebrating properly","body":"You deserve to celebrate this result. Tell your family that your kidneys are stable and that you are proud of the work you have all done together. Allow yourself to feel good about this accomplishment. In a condition that can feel defined by limitations and restrictions, stable creatinine is a clear, measurable victory. It is proof that what you are doing matters. Your nephrologist is pleased, your care team is encouraged, and you should be too. Well done — now keep going. The next six months are waiting, and you already know how to do this."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'achievement'),
  (E'cooking-oils-that-protect-your-heart', E'Cooking oils that protect your heart', E'HEART_DISEASE', '{"HEART_DISEASE"}', E'DIETARY', E'The cooking oil you use every day has a direct and significant impact on your heart health. This is not about dramatic changes or expensive specialty products — it is about understanding which oils protect your arteries and which ones contribute to plaque buildup, and then making simple swaps in.', E'The cooking oil you use every day has a direct and significant impact on your heart health. This is not about dramatic changes or expensive specialty products — it is about understanding which oils protect your arteries and which ones contribute to plaque buildup, and then making simple swaps in your kitchen that add up over time.\n\nUnderstanding fats and your heart\n\nYour body needs some fat to function — fat provides energy, helps absorb vitamins, and protects your organs. But not all fats are equal. Unsaturated fats (found in sunflower oil, olive oil, and canola oil) help maintain healthy cholesterol levels and protect your blood vessels. Saturated fats (found in coconut oil, butter, ghee, and solid animal fat) raise the LDL cholesterol that clogs your arteries. Trans fats (found in some margarines and deep-fried street foods) are the worst — they simultaneously raise bad cholesterol and lower good cholesterol. For someone with heart disease, choosing the right cooking oil is one of the simplest and most impactful changes you can make.\n\nBest cooking oils for heart health in Kenya\n\nSunflower oil is widely available in Kenya at every supermarket and corner kiosk. A litre costs between KES 200-350 depending on the brand. It is a good all-purpose oil for daily cooking — sauteing vegetables, making stews, frying eggs. Olive oil is excellent for heart health, particularly extra virgin olive oil, which is rich in protective antioxidants. It is more expensive — KES 500-1,500 per litre — but you use very little because a small amount goes a long way. Use olive oil for salad dressings, drizzling over cooked vegetables, or light sauteing. Canola oil is another heart-healthy option available at most supermarkets.\n\nOils to limit or avoid\n\nCoconut oil has become trendy globally, with claims about health benefits. However, coconut oil is about 82 percent saturated fat — higher than butter. If you have heart disease, regular coconut oil use is working against you. Solid cooking fat and lard are also high in saturated fats. Ghee, while delicious and culturally important in some Kenyan communities, is pure butterfat and should be used sparingly. If you love the taste of ghee, use just a tiny amount for flavour and do the bulk of your cooking with sunflower or olive oil.\n\nHow much oil to use\n\nThe amount of oil matters as much as the type. Even healthy oils are calorie-dense — one tablespoon of any oil contains about 120 calories. For daily cooking, aim for about one tablespoon of oil per serving. This might sound like very little, but there are techniques that make it work. Use a non-stick pan, which requires less oil. Add a splash of water or stock to the pan when sauteing vegetables — this creates steam that helps cook the food while the oil provides flavour. Measure your oil with a tablespoon rather than pouring freely from the bottle. You will be surprised how little you actually need when you use a good pan and the right technique.\n\nMaking heart-healthy chapati\n\nChapati is a staple in many Kenyan households, but traditional preparation uses generous amounts of oil. Each chapati can absorb 1-2 tablespoons of oil, and most people eat two or three at a sitting. That is potentially 6 tablespoons of oil in one meal — an enormous amount. Here is a healthier approach: when rolling out your chapati, brush a very thin layer of oil on the surface using a silicone brush or the back of a spoon, rather than pouring it on. Use a well-heated non-stick pan so the chapati does not stick. The result is a chapati that is still soft and layered but uses about a quarter of the oil. Your family may not even notice the difference.\n\nRethinking deep frying\n\nDeep frying is common in Kenyan cooking — chips, mandazi, fried fish, bhajia. But deep frying dramatically increases your fat intake. When food is submerged in hot oil, it absorbs a significant amount. A serving of deep-fried chips can contain three to five times more fat than the same potatoes would if they were baked or roasted. For your heart, this matters enormously. Try these alternatives: bake your chips in the oven with a light spray of oil. Make mandazi in an air fryer or bake them — they will not be quite the same, but they are still enjoyable. Grill or bake fish instead of frying it. When you do fry occasionally, use sunflower oil, keep the temperature correct (oil that is not hot enough causes food to absorb more), and drain the food thoroughly on a paper towel.\n\nEating out and street food\n\nWhen you eat at a hotel or restaurant, you have less control over the oil used. Most restaurants and street food vendors use whatever oil is cheapest, and they reuse it multiple times, which creates harmful compounds. When eating out, choose grilled or roasted options over fried ones. Ask for your fish grilled rather than fried. Choose rice over chips as your starch. If you are at a nyama choma place, the meat is already grilled, which is a better choice — just go easy on the kachumbari if it is dressed in oil, and skip the fried sides. These choices do not eliminate the enjoyment of eating out — they just redirect it toward heart-friendlier options.\n\nSmall changes, big protection\n\nYou do not need to overhaul your entire kitchen overnight. Start with one change: switch your daily cooking oil to sunflower if you are currently using coconut oil or solid fat. Next month, try brushing your chapati instead of pouring oil. The month after, try baking your chips once. Each small change reduces the amount of harmful fat reaching your arteries, and over time, these changes add up to significant heart protection.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The cooking oil you use every day has a direct and significant impact on your heart health. This is not about dramatic changes or expensive specialty products — it is about understanding which oils protect your arteries and which ones contribute to plaque buildup, and then making simple swaps in your kitchen that add up over time."},{"id":"s2","title":"Understanding fats and your heart","body":"Your body needs some fat to function — fat provides energy, helps absorb vitamins, and protects your organs. But not all fats are equal. Unsaturated fats (found in sunflower oil, olive oil, and canola oil) help maintain healthy cholesterol levels and protect your blood vessels. Saturated fats (found in coconut oil, butter, ghee, and solid animal fat) raise the LDL cholesterol that clogs your arteries. Trans fats (found in some margarines and deep-fried street foods) are the worst — they simultaneously raise bad cholesterol and lower good cholesterol. For someone with heart disease, choosing the right cooking oil is one of the simplest and most impactful changes you can make."},{"id":"s3","title":"Best cooking oils for heart health in Kenya","body":"Sunflower oil is widely available in Kenya at every supermarket and corner kiosk. A litre costs between KES 200-350 depending on the brand. It is a good all-purpose oil for daily cooking — sauteing vegetables, making stews, frying eggs. Olive oil is excellent for heart health, particularly extra virgin olive oil, which is rich in protective antioxidants. It is more expensive — KES 500-1,500 per litre — but you use very little because a small amount goes a long way. Use olive oil for salad dressings, drizzling over cooked vegetables, or light sauteing. Canola oil is another heart-healthy option available at most supermarkets."},{"id":"s4","title":"Oils to limit or avoid","body":"Coconut oil has becom