p frying is common in Kenyan cooking — chips, mandazi, fried fish, bhajia. But deep frying dramatically increases your fat intake. When food is submerged in hot oil, it absorbs a significant amount. A serving of deep-fried chips can contain three to five times more fat than the same potatoes would if they were baked or roasted. For your heart, this matters enormously. Try these alternatives: bake your chips in the oven with a light spray of oil. Make mandazi in an air fryer or bake them — they will not be quite the same, but they are still enjoyable. Grill or bake fish instead of frying it. When you do fry occasionally, use sunflower oil, keep the temperature correct (oil that is not hot enough causes food to absorb more), and drain the food thoroughly on a paper towel."},{"id":"s8","title":"Eating out and street food","body":"When you eat at a hotel or restaurant, you have less control over the oil used. Most restaurants and street food vendors use whatever oil is cheapest, and they reuse it multiple times, which creates harmful compounds. When eating out, choose grilled or roasted options over fried ones. Ask for your fish grilled rather than fried. Choose rice over chips as your starch. If you are at a nyama choma place, the meat is already grilled, which is a better choice — just go easy on the kachumbari if it is dressed in oil, and skip the fried sides. These choices do not eliminate the enjoyment of eating out — they just redirect it toward heart-friendlier options."},{"id":"s9","title":"Small changes, big protection","body":"You do not need to overhaul your entire kitchen overnight. Start with one change: switch your daily cooking oil to sunflower if you are currently using coconut oil or solid fat. Next month, try brushing your chapati instead of pouring oil. The month after, try baking your chips once. Each small change reduces the amount of harmful fat reaching your arteries, and over time, these changes add up to significant heart protection."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'fresh-vegetables'),
  (E'how-to-start-moving-again-after-a-heart-event', E'How to start moving again after a heart event', E'HEART_DISEASE', '{"HEART_DISEASE"}', E'EXERCISE', E'After a heart attack, heart surgery, or any cardiac event, the thought of exercising can feel terrifying. Your heart just told you something was wrong, and now you are supposed to make it work harder?', E'After a heart attack, heart surgery, or any cardiac event, the thought of exercising can feel terrifying. Your heart just told you something was wrong, and now you are supposed to make it work harder? It sounds counterintuitive, and the fear is completely understandable. But here is the truth that cardiologists in Kenya and worldwide agree on: your heart is a muscle, and like every muscle in your body, it gets stronger with regular, appropriate use. Carefully reintroducing exercise after a heart event is one of the best things you can do for your long-term survival and quality of life.\n\nWhy exercise matters after a heart event\n\nWhen you had your heart event, part of your heart muscle may have been damaged. The remaining healthy muscle has to work harder to compensate. Regular exercise helps this remaining muscle become more efficient — it pumps more blood with each beat, your resting heart rate decreases, and your blood pressure improves. Exercise also improves your blood vessels'' ability to relax and expand, reducing the resistance your heart pumps against. Beyond the heart, exercise improves cholesterol levels, helps control blood sugar, reduces stress hormones, and boosts your mood. Studies show that heart patients who exercise regularly have significantly fewer future cardiac events than those who remain sedentary.\n\nThe first weeks — starting safely\n\nBefore beginning any exercise after a heart event, you must have clearance from your cardiologist. This is not optional. Your doctor will assess your heart function, check for any remaining blockages, review your medications, and give you specific guidance on what is safe. Once cleared, start with the most basic exercise: walking. Begin with 5-minute walks on flat ground, three times a day. Walk at a comfortable pace — you should be able to talk normally while walking. If you are in Nairobi, find a flat stretch in your estate or a park. If you live upcountry, a level road or school field works well. Do this for one to two weeks. If you feel comfortable and have no symptoms, increase to 10-minute walks, three times a day.\n\nGradual progression over weeks and months\n\nThe key word is gradual. There is no rush. Over the following weeks, slowly combine your shorter walks into longer ones. By week three or four, you might be walking for 15 minutes twice a day. By week six to eight, aim for one continuous 20-30 minute walk per day. Pay attention to how you feel. Some days will be better than others. If you did not sleep well, or it is unusually hot, or you are feeling stressed, it is okay to do a shorter walk. Consistency matters more than any single day''s performance. Over two to three months, most patients can work up to 30 minutes of brisk walking five times a week, which meets the recommended exercise guidelines for heart patients.\n\nKnowing when to stop\n\nYou must learn and respect the warning signs that mean you should stop exercising immediately. Stop and rest if you experience: chest pain, pressure, or tightness; unusual shortness of breath that is disproportionate to what you are doing; dizziness or lightheadedness; an irregular or racing heartbeat; pain in your jaw, arm, or back; nausea; or cold sweating. If these symptoms pass within a few minutes of rest, report them to your cardiologist at your next visit. If they do not pass, or if they are severe, call for emergency help immediately. Keep your doctor''s emergency number and the nearest hospital''s number saved in your phone.\n\nCardiac rehabilitation programmes\n\nCardiac rehabilitation — often called cardiac rehab — is a supervised exercise and education programme designed specifically for people recovering from heart events. In Kenya, several hospitals offer cardiac rehab, including Kenyatta National Hospital, Aga Khan University Hospital, Nairobi Hospital, and some county referral hospitals. A typical programme runs for 8-12 weeks and includes supervised exercise sessions two to three times per week, education about heart-healthy living, dietary counselling, and emotional support. Having a physiotherapist and nurse monitoring you while you exercise provides both safety and confidence. Ask your cardiologist whether cardiac rehab is available at your hospital and whether your NHIF or SHA cover includes it.\n\nExercise beyond walking\n\nOnce your cardiologist clears you for more varied exercise — usually after several weeks of successful walking — you can gradually introduce other activities. Cycling on flat ground, either on a real bicycle or a stationary bike, is excellent for heart patients. Swimming is gentle on the joints and provides a good cardiovascular workout. Light strength training with small weights or resistance bands, focusing on major muscle groups, can help rebuild the muscle mass you may have lost during recovery. Avoid heavy lifting, competitive sports, or any activity where you might need to strain hard and hold your breath. Your cardiologist or cardiac rehab team can guide you on what is appropriate for your specific situation.\n\nExercise with medication\n\nMany heart patients take beta-blockers, which slow your heart rate. This means your heart rate during exercise may not increase as much as you expect, and you should not chase a high heart rate. Instead, use the "talk test" — you should be able to carry on a conversation while exercising. If you are too breathless to talk, you are working too hard. Also, some blood pressure medications can cause dizziness when you change position quickly, so get up slowly from sitting or lying down before you start your walk. Take your medications as prescribed and exercise at a time of day when you feel the medication has settled — most patients find mid-morning or late afternoon works best.\n\nThe mental barrier\n\nThe biggest obstacle for many heart patients is not physical — it is psychological. The fear that exercise will trigger another heart attack is powerful and persistent. Remind yourself: controlled, moderate exercise reduces your risk. Sitting still and avoiding all activity actually increases your risk. Each walk you complete without a problem builds your confidence. Many patients report that within a few weeks of regular walking, they feel better than they have in years — more energetic, sleeping better, and less anxious. That feeling is your heart getting stronger.\n\nMaking it part of your life\n\nThe goal is not a short burst of exercise followed by returning to a sedentary life. The goal is making regular physical activity a permanent part of your routine. Walk every morning before the day gets busy. Find a walking partner — a spouse, a neighbour, a friend — who will hold you accountable. Track your walks in a notebook or on your phone. Celebrate when you hit milestones — your first 20-minute walk, your first full week without missing a day. This is not just exercise. This is your heart''s ongoing rehabilitation, one step at a time.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"After a heart attack, heart surgery, or any cardiac event, the thought of exercising can feel terrifying. Your heart just told you something was wrong, and now you are supposed to make it work harder? It sounds counterintuitive, and the fear is completely understandable. But here is the truth that cardiologists in Kenya and worldwide agree on: your heart is a muscle, and like every muscle in your body, it gets stronger with regular, appropriate use. Carefully reintroducing exercise after a heart event is one of the best things you can do for your long-term survival and quality of life."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why exercise matters after a heart event","body":"When you had your heart event, part of your heart muscle may have been damaged. The remaining healthy muscle has to work harder to compensate. Regular exercise helps this remaining muscle become more efficient — it pumps more blood with each beat, your resting heart rate decreases, and your blood pressure improves. Exercise also improves your blood vessels'' ability to relax and expand, reducing the resistance your heart pumps against. Beyond the heart, exercise improves cholesterol levels, helps control blood sugar, reduces stress hormones, and boosts your mood. Studies show that heart patients who exercise regularly have significantly fewer future cardiac events than those who remain sedentary."},{"id":"s3","title":"The first weeks — starting safely","body":"Before beginning any exercise after a heart event, you must have clearance from your cardiologist. This is not optional. Your doctor will assess your heart function, check for any remaining blockages, review your medications, and give you specific guidance on what is safe. Once cleared, start with the most basic exercise: walking. Begin with 5-minute walks on flat ground, three times a day. Walk at a comfortable pace — you should be able to talk normally while walking. If you are in Nairobi, find a flat stretch in your estate or a park. If you live upcountry, a level road or school field works well. Do this for one to two weeks. If you feel comfortable and have no symptoms, increase to 10-minute walks, three times a day."},{"id":"s4","title":"Gradual progression over weeks and months","body":"The key word is gradual. There is no rush. Over the following weeks, slowly combine your shorter walks into longer ones. By week three or four, you might be walking for 15 minutes twice a day. By week six to eight, aim for one continuous 20-30 minute walk per day. Pay attention to how you feel. Some days will be better than others. If you did not sleep well, or it is unusually hot, or you are feeling stressed, it is okay to do a shorter walk. Consistency matters more than any single day''s performance. Over two to three months, most patients can work up to 30 minutes of brisk walking five times a week, which meets the recommended exercise guidelines for heart patients."},{"id":"s5","title":"Knowing when to stop","body":"You must learn and respect the warning signs that mean you should stop exercising immediately. Stop and rest if you experience: chest pain, pressure, or tightness; unusual shortness of breath that is disproportionate to what you are doing; dizziness or lightheadedness; an irregular or racing heartbeat; pain in your jaw, arm, or back; nausea; or cold sweating. If these symptoms pass within a few minutes of rest, report them to your cardiologist at your next visit. If they do not pass, or if they are severe, call for emergency help immediately. Keep your doctor''s emergency number and the nearest hospital''s number saved in your phone."},{"id":"s6","title":"Cardiac rehabilitation programmes","body":"Cardiac rehabilitation — often called cardiac rehab — is a supervised exercise and education programme designed specifically for people recovering from heart events. In Kenya, several hospitals offer cardiac rehab, including Kenyatta National Hospital, Aga Khan University Hospital, Nairobi Hospital, and some county referral hospitals. A typical programme runs for 8-12 weeks and includes supervised exercise sessions two to three times per week, education about heart-healthy living, dietary counselling, and emotional support. Having a physiotherapist and nurse monitoring you while you exercise provides both safety and confidence. Ask your cardiologist whether cardiac rehab is available at your hospital and whether your NHIF or SHA cover includes it."},{"id":"s7","title":"Exercise beyond walking","body":"Once your cardiologist clears you for more varied exercise — usually after several weeks of successful walking — you can gradually introduce other activities. Cycling on flat ground, either on a real bicycle or a stationary bike, is excellent for heart patients. Swimming is gentle on the joints and provides a good cardiovascular workout. Light strength training with small weights or resistance bands, focusing on major muscle groups, can help rebuild the muscle mass you may have lost during recovery. Avoid heavy lifting, competitive sports, or any activity where you might need to strain hard and hold your breath. Your cardiologist or cardiac rehab team can guide you on what is appropriate for your specific situation."},{"id":"s8","title":"Exercise with medication","body":"Many heart patients take beta-blockers, which slow your heart rate. This means your heart rate during exercise may not increase as much as you expect, and you should not chase a high heart rate. Instead, use the \\"talk test\\" — you should be able to carry on a conversation while exercising. If you are too breathless to talk, you are working too hard. Also, some blood pressure medications can cause dizziness when you change position quickly, so get up slowly from sitting or lying down before you start your walk. Take your medications as prescribed and exercise at a time of day when you feel the medication has settled — most patients find mid-morning or late afternoon works best."},{"id":"s9","title":"The mental barrier","body":"The biggest obstacle for many heart patients is not physical — it is psychological. The fear that exercise will trigger another heart attack is powerful and persistent. Remind yourself: controlled, moderate exercise reduces your risk. Sitting still and avoiding all activity actually increases your risk. Each walk you complete without a problem builds your confidence. Many patients report that within a few weeks of regular walking, they feel better than they have in years — more energetic, sleeping better, and less anxious. That feeling is your heart getting stronger."},{"id":"s10","title":"Making it part of your life","body":"The goal is not a short burst of exercise followed by returning to a sedentary life. The goal is making regular physical activity a permanent part of your routine. Walk every morning before the day gets busy. Find a walking partner — a spouse, a neighbour, a friend — who will hold you accountable. Track your walks in a notebook or on your phone. Celebrate when you hit milestones — your first 20-minute walk, your first full week without missing a day. This is not just exercise. This is your heart''s ongoing rehabilitation, one step at a time."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'stretching'),
  (E'myth-heart-disease-only-affects-older-men', E'Myth: Heart disease only affects older men', E'HEART_DISEASE', '{"HEART_DISEASE"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The belief that heart disease is an old man''s problem is one of the most dangerous health myths in Kenya today. It leads younger people to ignore warning signs, women to dismiss symptoms, and families to skip screenings that could save lives.', E'The belief that heart disease is an old man''s problem is one of the most dangerous health myths in Kenya today. It leads younger people to ignore warning signs, women to dismiss symptoms, and families to skip screenings that could save lives. The reality is starkly different from this myth, and understanding the truth could genuinely protect you or someone you love.\n\nThe myth and where it comes from\n\nThe stereotype is powerful: a grey-haired man in his sixties clutching his chest and collapsing. This is how heart attacks are shown in films, on television, and in health posters. Over decades, this image has become so embedded in public consciousness that many people genuinely believe heart disease is exclusive to older men. In Kenya, where NCDs have historically received less attention than infectious diseases like malaria and HIV, awareness of heart disease risk across all demographics is still catching up. Many people under 50 believe they are immune, and many women believe heart disease is not something they need to worry about.\n\nThe truth: heart disease affects all ages\n\nHeart disease is increasingly striking Kenyans in their 30s and 40s. The reasons are clear: hypertension, diabetes, obesity, sedentary lifestyles, stress, and smoking are all rising among younger Kenyans. The 2015 Kenya STEPwise Survey found that nearly one in four Kenyan adults had raised blood pressure, and many did not know it. Uncontrolled hypertension over even a few years begins damaging blood vessels and the heart. A 35-year-old who has had undiagnosed high blood pressure since their mid-twenties may already have significant cardiovascular damage. Young Kenyans working stressful jobs, eating fast food regularly, and getting little exercise are building heart disease risk factors that do not wait for old age to cause problems.\n\nWomen and heart disease\n\nGlobally, heart disease is the number one killer of women — not breast cancer, not maternal conditions, but heart disease. In Kenya, this fact is poorly understood. Women are less likely to be screened for heart disease, less likely to recognise their own symptoms, and less likely to seek emergency help when they are having a cardiac event. Part of the reason is that women''s heart attack symptoms are often different from the classic chest-clutching presentation. Women are more likely to experience: extreme fatigue that comes on suddenly, nausea or vomiting, pain in the jaw, neck, or back, shortness of breath without chest pain, dizziness, and a feeling of general unwellness. These symptoms are frequently dismissed as stress, indigestion, or exhaustion — by the women themselves, by their families, and sometimes even by healthcare workers.\n\nRisk factors that do not care about age or gender\n\nThe major risk factors for heart disease are equal-opportunity threats. High blood pressure damages your blood vessels regardless of whether you are 30 or 60, male or female. Diabetes accelerates atherosclerosis (the buildup of plaque in your arteries) at any age. Smoking damages blood vessel walls and promotes clotting. Obesity — particularly abdominal obesity, which is increasingly common among younger Kenyans — is a major risk factor. A family history of heart disease increases your risk regardless of your own age. High stress levels, which many Kenyans face due to financial pressures, job insecurity, and urban living, contribute to elevated blood pressure and unhealthy coping mechanisms like overeating, smoking, and excessive alcohol use.\n\nWhy this myth is dangerous\n\nWhen a 38-year-old woman experiences unusual fatigue and jaw pain, she is likely to attribute it to stress and push through rather than going to the hospital. When a 42-year-old man gets occasional chest tightness during his commute, he might blame it on anxiety and not mention it to a doctor. When young parents are told by their doctor to check their blood pressure regularly, they may think it is unnecessary at their age. This myth creates a false sense of security that prevents early detection and treatment. Heart disease caught early — through blood pressure management, cholesterol control, lifestyle changes, and medication — is highly treatable. Heart disease caught late, after a major heart attack, means permanent heart damage that cannot be reversed.\n\nWhat you should do\n\nRegardless of your age or gender, know your numbers. Get your blood pressure checked at least once a year — many Kenyan pharmacies offer this for free or for a small fee of KES 50-100. If you are over 35, or if you have risk factors like diabetes, obesity, smoking, or a family history of heart disease, ask your doctor for a full cardiovascular screening including cholesterol and blood sugar tests. NHIF and SHA cover preventive check-ups at accredited facilities. If you have been diagnosed with high blood pressure or diabetes, take your medications consistently — these conditions are the leading drivers of heart disease in Kenya, and controlling them is your most powerful protection.\n\nTeach the women in your life\n\nShare this information with the women in your family and social circle. Make sure your mother, wife, sisters, daughters, and friends know that heart disease affects women as much as men, and that their symptoms may look different. Encourage them to take unexplained fatigue, nausea, jaw pain, and shortness of breath seriously. A woman who knows her symptoms could save her own life by getting to the hospital in time, rather than dismissing a heart attack as a bad day.\n\nTeach the young men too\n\nYoung men in Kenya often feel invincible — they work long hours, eat heavily, drink, smoke, and skip health check-ups because they feel fine. But heart disease builds silently. The 45-year-old man who has a sudden heart attack did not develop heart disease overnight — it was building for years. Encourage the young men in your life to get screened, manage their blood pressure, eat more vegetables and less fried food, stay active, and take their health seriously before a crisis forces them to.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The belief that heart disease is an old man''s problem is one of the most dangerous health myths in Kenya today. It leads younger people to ignore warning signs, women to dismiss symptoms, and families to skip screenings that could save lives. The reality is starkly different from this myth, and understanding the truth could genuinely protect you or someone you love."},{"id":"s2","title":"The myth and where it comes from","body":"The stereotype is powerful: a grey-haired man in his sixties clutching his chest and collapsing. This is how heart attacks are shown in films, on television, and in health posters. Over decades, this image has become so embedded in public consciousness that many people genuinely believe heart disease is exclusive to older men. In Kenya, where NCDs have historically received less attention than infectious diseases like malaria and HIV, awareness of heart disease risk across all demographics is still catching up. Many people under 50 believe they are immune, and many women believe heart disease is not something they need to worry about."},{"id":"s3","title":"The truth: heart disease affects all ages","body":"Heart disease is increasingly striking Kenyans in their 30s and 40s. The reasons are clear: hypertension, diabetes, obesity, sedentary lifestyles, stress, and smoking are all rising among younger Kenyans. The 2015 Kenya STEPwise Survey found that nearly one in four Kenyan adults had raised blood pressure, and many did not know it. Uncontrolled hypertension over even a few years begins damaging blood vessels and the heart. A 35-year-old who has had undiagnosed high blood pressure since their mid-twenties may already have significant cardiovascular damage. Young Kenyans working stressful jobs, eating fast food regularly, and getting little exercise are building heart disease risk factors that do not wait for old age to cause problems."},{"id":"s4","title":"Women and heart disease","body":"Globally, heart disease is the number one killer of women — not breast cancer, not maternal conditions, but heart disease. In Kenya, this fact is poorly understood. Women are less likely to be screened for heart disease, less likely to recognise their own symptoms, and less likely to seek emergency help when they are having a cardiac event. Part of the reason is that women''s heart attack symptoms are often different from the classic chest-clutching presentation. Women are more likely to experience: extreme fatigue that comes on suddenly, nausea or vomiting, pain in the jaw, neck, or back, shortness of breath without chest pain, dizziness, and a feeling of general unwellness. These symptoms are frequently dismissed as stress, indigestion, or exhaustion — by the women themselves, by their families, and sometimes even by healthcare workers."},{"id":"s5","title":"Risk factors that do not care about age or gender","body":"The major risk factors for heart disease are equal-opportunity threats. High blood pressure damages your blood vessels regardless of whether you are 30 or 60, male or female. Diabetes accelerates atherosclerosis (the buildup of plaque in your arteries) at any age. Smoking damages blood vessel walls and promotes clotting. Obesity — particularly abdominal obesity, which is increasingly common among younger Kenyans — is a major risk factor. A family history of heart disease increases your risk regardless of your own age. High stress levels, which many Kenyans face due to financial pressures, job insecurity, and urban living, contribute to elevated blood pressure and unhealthy coping mechanisms like overeating, smoking, and excessive alcohol use."},{"id":"s6","title":"Why this myth is dangerous","body":"When a 38-year-old woman experiences unusual fatigue and jaw pain, she is likely to attribute it to stress and push through rather than going to the hospital. When a 42-year-old man gets occasional chest tightness during his commute, he might blame it on anxiety and not mention it to a doctor. When young parents are told by their doctor to check their blood pressure regularly, they may think it is unnecessary at their age. This myth creates a false sense of security that prevents early detection and treatment. Heart disease caught early — through blood pressure management, cholesterol control, lifestyle changes, and medication — is highly treatable. Heart disease caught late, after a major heart attack, means permanent heart damage that cannot be reversed."},{"id":"s7","title":"What you should do","body":"Regardless of your age or gender, know your numbers. Get your blood pressure checked at least once a year — many Kenyan pharmacies offer this for free or for a small fee of KES 50-100. If you are over 35, or if you have risk factors like diabetes, obesity, smoking, or a family history of heart disease, ask your doctor for a full cardiovascular screening including cholesterol and blood sugar tests. NHIF and SHA cover preventive check-ups at accredited facilities. If you have been diagnosed with high blood pressure or diabetes, take your medications consistently — these conditions are the leading drivers of heart disease in Kenya, and controlling them is your most powerful protection."},{"id":"s8","title":"Teach the women in your life","body":"Share this information with the women in your family and social circle. Make sure your mother, wife, sisters, daughters, and friends know that heart disease affects women as much as men, and that their symptoms may