  (E'fear-of-another-heart-event-is-normal-here-is-how-to-cope', E'Fear of another heart event is normal — here is how to cope', E'HEART_DISEASE', '{"HEART_DISEASE"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'If you have survived a heart attack, been diagnosed with heart disease, or undergone a heart procedure, you may be living with a constant companion you did not expect: fear. The fear of another event.', E'If you have survived a heart attack, been diagnosed with heart disease, or undergone a heart procedure, you may be living with a constant companion you did not expect: fear. The fear of another event. The fear that your heart could betray you again at any moment. Every chest twinge, every flutter, every moment of breathlessness sends a spike of panic through your body. This is one of the most common and least discussed aspects of living with heart disease, and if you are experiencing it, you are absolutely not alone.\n\nWhy this fear is so intense\n\nA heart event is unlike most medical experiences. It threatens your life directly and suddenly. One moment you were fine, the next you were in an ambulance or an emergency room. That kind of experience creates a deep imprint on your brain''s fear response. Your nervous system learned, in a very visceral way, that your heart can be dangerous. Now it is on high alert, constantly scanning for signs of trouble. Every unusual sensation in your chest — which healthy people experience and ignore every day — gets flagged as a potential emergency. This hyper-vigilance is your brain trying to protect you. It is not a sign of weakness. It is a normal response to a traumatic experience.\n\nHow fear harms your heart\n\nHere is the cruel irony: chronic fear and anxiety are actually bad for your heart. When you are anxious, your body releases stress hormones like adrenaline and cortisol. These hormones raise your heart rate, increase your blood pressure, and make your blood more likely to clot. Over time, chronic anxiety contributes to the very cardiovascular problems you are afraid of. Living in constant fear of a heart event can actually increase your risk of having one. Understanding this is not meant to add to your worry — it is meant to motivate you to address the anxiety as part of your heart care, not separate from it.\n\nRecognising the patterns\n\nMany heart patients describe similar patterns. You might avoid physical activity entirely because you are afraid it will strain your heart — even though your cardiologist has told you exercise is safe and beneficial. You might check your pulse obsessively, multiple times an hour. You might lie awake at night, hand on your chest, monitoring every heartbeat. You might avoid going places where you feel far from a hospital. You might have stopped driving, stopped travelling, or stopped socialising because the anxiety is too overwhelming. Some patients develop panic attacks that mimic heart attack symptoms — chest tightness, sweating, racing heart, shortness of breath — which creates a terrifying cycle where anxiety symptoms trigger more anxiety.\n\nImmediate coping strategies\n\nWhen fear strikes, these techniques can help bring your nervous system back to calm. First, the hand-on-chest breathing technique: place one hand on your chest and one on your belly. Breathe in slowly through your nose for 4 counts, feeling your belly push out (not your chest). Hold for 2 counts. Breathe out slowly through your mouth for 6 counts. Repeat this five times. This activates your vagus nerve, which slows your heart rate and reduces the fight-or-flight response. Second, grounding: name five things you can see, four you can touch, three you can hear, two you can smell, and one you can taste. This pulls your mind out of the spiral and back into the present moment.\n\nThe reassurance inventory\n\nWhen anxiety tells you that another heart event is imminent, challenge it with facts. Create a mental inventory of the protective actions you are taking: "I take my medication every day. I had my check-up last month and my cardiologist was satisfied. My blood pressure is controlled. I am eating better. I am walking regularly. I am doing everything I can." You cannot guarantee that nothing will ever happen, but you can remind yourself that you are not helpless. You are actively managing your health, and that meaningfully reduces your risk.\n\nTalking to your doctor\n\nMany heart patients are reluctant to mention anxiety to their cardiologist because they think it is not a cardiac issue. But any good cardiologist in Kenya will tell you that mental health is directly relevant to heart health. If anxiety is affecting your daily life — if it is stopping you from exercising, sleeping, working, or enjoying your family — tell your doctor. There are effective treatments. Some patients benefit from anti-anxiety medication, which can be prescribed alongside your cardiac medications. Others benefit from counselling, particularly cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT), which specifically helps you retrain your brain''s fear responses. Your NHIF or SHA cover may include some mental health visits at accredited facilities.\n\nThe role of your support network\n\nShare your feelings with someone you trust — your spouse, a sibling, a close friend. Keeping the fear locked inside makes it grow. When you say it out loud — "I am scared it will happen again" — it loses some of its power. You might find that the people around you have been worried about you too, and they are relieved to finally talk about it. In Kenya, discussing mental health is becoming more accepted, particularly in urban areas. Support groups for cardiac patients exist at some hospitals. Ask your cardiologist or the hospital''s patient services department about what is available.\n\nFaith and community\n\nFor many Kenyans, faith is a significant source of comfort during health challenges. If you are a person of faith, leaning into your spiritual community — prayer groups, church fellowships, mosque gatherings — can provide emotional support and a sense of peace. Many pastors, priests, and imams are increasingly aware of mental health needs and can be a bridge to professional counselling if needed. Spiritual comfort and medical treatment are not competing approaches — they work together.\n\nGiving it time\n\nThe intensity of the fear typically decreases with time, especially when you are consistently taking care of your health and seeing stable results from your check-ups. Each month that passes without an event builds your confidence. Each walk you complete, each normal blood pressure reading, each reassuring appointment chips away at the fear. It may never disappear completely — and that is okay. A small amount of awareness keeps you attentive to genuine warning signs. The goal is not to eliminate all concern, but to keep it from controlling your life. You survived your heart event. You are here, you are caring for yourself, and you are stronger than the fear.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you have survived a heart attack, been diagnosed with heart disease, or undergone a heart procedure, you may be living with a constant companion you did not expect: fear. The fear of another event. The fear that your heart could betray you again at any moment. Every chest twinge, every flutter, every moment of breathlessness sends a spike of panic through your body. This is one of the most common and least discussed aspects of living with heart disease, and if you are experiencing it, you are absolutely not alone."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why this fear is so intense","body":"A heart event is unlike most medical experiences. It threatens your life directly and suddenly. One moment you were fine, the next you were in an ambulance or an emergency room. That kind of experience creates a deep imprint on your brain''s fear response. Your nervous system learned, in a very visceral way, that your heart can be dangerous. Now it is on high alert, constantly scanning for signs of trouble. Every unusual sensation in your chest — which healthy people experience and ignore every day — gets flagged as a potential emergency. This hyper-vigilance is your brain trying to protect you. It is not a sign of weakness. It is a normal response to a traumatic experience."},{"id":"s3","title":"How fear harms your heart","body":"Here is the cruel irony: chronic fear and anxiety are actually bad for your heart. When you are anxious, your body releases stress hormones like adrenaline and cortisol. These hormones raise your heart rate, increase your blood pressure, and make your blood more likely to clot. Over time, chronic anxiety contributes to the very cardiovascular problems you are afraid of. Living in constant fear of a heart event can actually increase your risk of having one. Understanding this is not meant to add to your worry — it is meant to motivate you to address the anxiety as part of your heart care, not separate from it."},{"id":"s4","title":"Recognising the patterns","body":"Many heart patients describe similar patterns. You might avoid physical activity entirely because you are afraid it will strain your heart — even though your cardiologist has told you exercise is safe and beneficial. You might check your pulse obsessively, multiple times an hour. You might lie awake at night, hand on your chest, monitoring every heartbeat. You might avoid going places where you feel far from a hospital. You might have stopped driving, stopped travelling, or stopped socialising because the anxiety is too overwhelming. Some patients develop panic attacks that mimic heart attack symptoms — chest tightness, sweating, racing heart, shortness of breath — which creates a terrifying cycle where anxiety symptoms trigger more anxiety."},{"id":"s5","title":"Immediate coping strategies","body":"When fear strikes, these techniques can help bring your nervous system back to calm. First, the hand-on-chest breathing technique: place one hand on your chest and one on your belly. Breathe in slowly through your nose for 4 counts, feeling your belly push out (not your chest). Hold for 2 counts. Breathe out slowly through your mouth for 6 counts. Repeat this five times. This activates your vagus nerve, which slows your heart rate and reduces the fight-or-flight response. Second, grounding: name five things you can see, four you can touch, three you can hear, two you can smell, and one you can taste. This pulls your mind out of the spiral and back into the present moment."},{"id":"s6","title":"The reassurance inventory","body":"When anxiety tells you that another heart event is imminent, challenge it with facts. Create a mental inventory of the protective actions you are taking: \\"I take my medication every day. I had my check-up last month and my cardiologist was satisfied. My blood pressure is controlled. I am eating better. I am walking regularly. I am doing everything I can.\\" You cannot guarantee that nothing will ever happen, but you can remind yourself that you are not helpless. You are actively managing your health, and that meaningfully reduces your risk."},{"id":"s7","title":"Talking to your doctor","body":"Many heart patients are reluctant to mention anxiety to their cardiologist because they think it is not a cardiac issue. But any good cardiologist in Kenya will tell you that mental health is directly relevant to heart health. If anxiety is affecting your daily life — if it is stopping you from exercising, sleeping, working, or enjoying your family — tell your doctor. There are effective treatments. Some patients benefit from anti-anxiety medication, which can be prescribed alongside your cardiac medications. Others benefit from counselling, particularly cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT), which specifically helps you retrain your brain''s fear responses. Your NHIF or SHA cover may include some mental health visits at accredited facilities."},{"id":"s8","title":"The role of your support network","body":"Share your feelings with someone you trust — your spouse, a sibling, a close friend. Keeping the fear locked inside makes it grow. When you say it out loud — \\"I am scared it will happen again\\" — it loses some of its power. You might find that the people around you have been worried about you too, and they are relieved to finally talk about it. In Kenya, discussing mental health is becoming more accepted, particularly in urban areas. Support groups for cardiac patients exist at some hospitals. Ask your cardiologist or the hospital''s patient services department about what is available."},{"id":"s9","title":"Faith and community","body":"For many Kenyans, faith is a significant source of comfort during health challenges. If you are a person of faith, leaning into your spiritual community — prayer groups, church fellowships, mosque gatherings — can provide emotional support and a sense of peace. Many pastors, priests, and imams are increasingly aware of mental health needs and can be a bridge to professional counselling if needed. Spiritual comfort and medical treatment are not competing approaches — they work together."},{"id":"s10","title":"Giving it time","body":"The intensity of the fear typically decreases with time, especially when you are consistently taking care of your health and seeing stable results from your check-ups. Each month that passes without an event builds your confidence. Each walk you complete, each normal blood pressure reading, each reassuring appointment chips away at the fear. It may never disappear completely — and that is okay. A small amount of awareness keeps you attentive to genuine warning signs. The goal is not to eliminate all concern, but to keep it from controlling your life. You survived your heart event. You are here, you are caring for yourself, and you are stronger than the fear."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'meditation'),
  (E'warning-signs-that-need-immediate-attention', E'Warning signs that need immediate attention', E'HEART_DISEASE', '{"HEART_DISEASE"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'When it comes to heart disease, knowing the warning signs that demand immediate action is not optional knowledge — it is essential, potentially life-saving information that you, your family, and the people around you must understand.', E'When it comes to heart disease, knowing the warning signs that demand immediate action is not optional knowledge — it is essential, potentially life-saving information that you, your family, and the people around you must understand. The phrase cardiologists use is "time is muscle" — every minute that passes during a heart attack without treatment, more heart muscle dies. Quick recognition and response can mean the difference between full recovery and permanent heart damage, or even between life and death.\n\nThe classic warning signs\n\nThe most widely recognised sign of a heart attack is chest pain or pressure. But it is important to understand what cardiac chest pain typically feels like. Patients often describe it not as a sharp, stabbing pain, but as a heavy pressure, squeezing, or tightness — as if someone is sitting on your chest or an elephant is pressing down. This feeling may come and go, or it may be constant. It typically lasts more than a few minutes. If you are experiencing chest pressure that does not go away when you change position, rest, or take antacids, take it seriously. This is not indigestion. This is your heart telling you something is wrong.\n\nPain that travels\n\nHeart-related pain often radiates — it spreads from the chest to other areas. The most common pattern is pain moving down the left arm, but it can also spread to the right arm, the jaw, the neck, the back (especially between the shoulder blades), or the upper stomach. Some patients feel the pain primarily in these other locations rather than in the chest itself. If you are experiencing unexplained pain in your jaw or arm that is different from your normal aches, especially if it comes with any other symptoms on this list, treat it as a possible cardiac event.\n\nSudden shortness of breath\n\nIf you suddenly cannot catch your breath — especially if it comes on at rest or during an activity that normally does not wind you — this is a red flag. Some heart attack patients describe this as their primary symptom, with little or no chest pain. Shortness of breath can also indicate heart failure, where your heart is not pumping efficiently and fluid builds up in your lungs. If you wake up in the middle of the night gasping for air, or if you need to prop yourself up on multiple pillows to breathe comfortably while sleeping, tell your doctor urgently. If shortness of breath comes on suddenly and severely, call for emergency help.\n\nCold sweat and nausea\n\nBreaking out in a cold, clammy sweat for no obvious reason — not because you are hot or exercising — is a common heart attack symptom. It often comes with nausea or a feeling that you are about to vomit. Many patients, particularly women, report this as one of their first symptoms. If you suddenly feel cold and sweaty while sitting still, and especially if it is accompanied by any chest discomfort or shortness of breath, do not dismiss it.\n\nDizziness and feeling faint\n\nSudden lightheadedness, dizziness, or feeling like you might faint can indicate that your heart is not pumping enough blood to your brain. This can happen during a heart attack or when your heart rhythm becomes dangerously abnormal. If you feel faint and it does not pass quickly, or if you actually lose consciousness even briefly, this needs emergency attention.\n\nWhat to do — act fast\n\nIf you or someone near you experiences these symptoms, here is what to do immediately. First, stop all activity and sit or lie down. Second, call for emergency help. In Kenya, the emergency numbers are 999, 112, or the specific emergency line for your area. If you are in Nairobi, many hospitals have their own ambulance services — Aga Khan (0711 092 000), Nairobi Hospital (020 284 5000), and St John Ambulance (1199). Save these numbers in your phone today, not when the emergency happens. Third, if you have been prescribed nitroglycerin, take it as directed. Fourth, if you are not allergic to aspirin and have it available, chew (do not swallow whole) one regular aspirin. Chewing allows it to enter your bloodstream faster, where it helps prevent the blood clot causing the heart attack from growing.\n\nDo not wait to see if it passes\n\nThis is perhaps the most critical point. Many Kenyans — especially those in rural areas or those who worry about hospital costs — wait to see if symptoms will pass on their own. They lie down, drink water, take a painkiller, and hope for the best. During a heart attack, every minute of delay means more heart muscle is dying. The treatments that save heart muscle and lives — clot-dissolving drugs and emergency procedures to open blocked arteries — are most effective within the first one to two hours. After six hours, the window for saving heart muscle narrows dramatically. Do not let cost concerns delay you — NHIF and SHA cover emergency cardiac care at accredited facilities, and saving your life is always worth it.\n\nPreparing your family\n\nDo not keep this information to yourself. Sit down with your spouse, your children, your parents, and anyone who lives with you or sees you daily. Teach them the warning signs. Make sure they know where the emergency numbers are saved in your phone and in theirs. Discuss a plan: who calls for help, who accompanies you to the hospital, who watches the children. Have this conversation when everyone is calm and healthy, not during an emergency. If your family members can recognise a heart attack and act quickly, they could save your life if you are too unwell to act for yourself.\n\nDaily monitoring between emergencies\n\nBeyond knowing the emergency signs, regular self-monitoring helps you stay ahead of problems. Check your blood pressure at home using a digital monitor — reliable ones cost KES 3,000-5,000 at pharmacies like Goodlife, Haltons, or Portal Pharmacy. Check it at the same time each day, ideally in the morning before medication and in the evening. Write down the numbers. Your target is typically below 130/80, but your cardiologist will give you your personal target. Weigh yourself daily in the morning — a sudden gain of 1-2 kg over a day or two can indicate fluid retention, a sign that your heart is struggling. Report any consistent upward trend in weight or blood pressure to your doctor.\n\nThe bottom line\n\nHeart disease is manageable, and most days will be normal days. But being prepared for the abnormal day — knowing what to look for, knowing what to do, and having a plan in place — could save your life or the life of someone you love. Review these signs regularly. Quiz your family members. Keep your emergency numbers accessible. Your preparation today is an investment in your survival tomorrow.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When it comes to heart disease, knowing the warning signs that demand immediate action is not optional knowledge — it is essential, potentially life-saving information that you, your family, and the people around you must understand. The phrase cardiologists use is \\"time is muscle\\" — every minute that passes during a heart attack without treatment, more heart muscle dies. Quick recognition and response can mean the difference between full recovery and permanent heart damage, or even between life and death."},{"id":"s2","title":"The classic warning signs","body":"The most widely recognised sign of a heart attack is chest pain or pressure. But it is important to understand what cardiac chest pain typically feels like. Patients often describe it not as a sharp, stabbing pain, but as a heavy pressure, squeezing, or tightness — as if someone is sitting on your chest or an elephant is pressing down. This feeling may come and go, or it may be constant. It typically lasts more than a few minutes. If you are experiencing chest pressure that does not go away when you change position, rest, or take antacids, take it seriously. This is not indigestion. This is your heart telling you something is wrong."},{"id":"s3","title":"Pain that travels","body":"Heart-related pain often radiates — it spreads from the chest to other areas. The most common pattern is pain moving down the left arm, but it can also spread to the right arm, the jaw, the neck, the back (especially between the shoulder blades), or the upper stomach. Some patients feel the pain primarily in these other locations rather than in the chest itself. If you are experiencing unexplained pain in your jaw or arm that is different from your normal aches, especially if it comes with any other symptoms on this list, treat it as a possible cardiac event."},{"id":"s4","title":"Sudden shortness of breath","body":"If you suddenly cannot catch your breath — especially if it comes on at rest or during an activity that normally does not wind you — this is a red flag. Some heart attack patients describe this as their primary symptom, with little or no chest pain. Shortness of breath can also indicate heart failure, where your heart is not pumping efficiently and fluid builds up in your lungs. If you wake up in the middle of the night gasping for air, or if you need to prop yourself up on multiple pillows to breathe comfortably while sleeping, tell your doctor urgently. If shortness of breath comes on suddenly and severely, call for emergency help."},{"id":"s5","title":"Cold sweat and nausea","body":"Breaking out in a cold, clammy sweat for no obvious reason — not because you are hot or exercising — is a common heart attack symptom. It often comes with nausea or a feeling that you are about to vomit. Many patients, particularly women, report this as one of their first symptoms. If you suddenly feel cold and sweaty while sitting still, and especially if it is accompanied by any chest discomfort or shortness of breath, do not dismiss it."},{"id":"s6","title":"Dizziness and feeling faint","body":"Sudden lightheadedness, dizziness, or feeling like you might faint can indicate that your heart is not pumping enough blood to your brain. This can happen during a heart attack or when your heart rhythm becomes dangerously abnormal. If you feel faint and it does not pass quickly, or if you actually lose consciousness even briefly, this needs emergency attention."},{"id":"s7","title":"What to do — act fast","body":"If you or someone near you experiences these symptoms, here is what to do immediately. First, stop all activity and sit or lie down. Second, call for emergency help. In Kenya, the emergency numbers are 999, 112, or the specific emergency line for your area. If you are in Nairobi, many hospitals have their own ambulance services — Aga Khan (0711 092 000), Nairobi Hospital (020 284 5000), and St John Ambulance (1199). Save these numbers in your phone today, not when the emergency happens. Third, if you have been prescribed nitroglycerin, take it as directed. Fourth, if you are not allergic to aspirin and have it available, chew (do not swallow whole) one regular aspirin. Chewing allows it to enter your bloodstream faster, where it helps prevent the blood clot causing the heart attack from growing."},{"id":"s8","title":"Do not wait to see if it passes","body":"This is perhaps the most critical point. Many Kenyans — especially those in rural areas or those who worry about hospital costs — wait to see if symptoms will pass on their own. They lie down, drink water, take a painkiller, and hope for the best. During a heart attack, every minute of delay means more heart muscle is dying. The treatments that save heart muscle and lives — clot-dissolving drugs and emergency procedures to open blocked arteries — are most effective within the first one to two hours. After six hours, the window for saving heart muscle narrows dramatically. Do not let cost concerns delay you — NHIF and SHA cover emergency cardiac care at accredited facilities, and saving your life is always worth it."},{"id":"s9","title":"Preparing your family","body":"Do not keep this information to yourself. Sit down with your spouse, your children, your parents, and anyone who lives with you or sees you daily. Teach them the warning signs. Make sure they know where the emergency numbers are saved in your phone and in theirs. Discuss a plan: who calls for help, who accompanies you to the hospital, who watches the children. Have this conversation when everyone is calm and healthy, not during an emergency. If your family members can recognise a heart attack and act quickly, they could save your life if you are too unwell to act for yourself."},{"id":"s10","title":"Daily monitoring between emergencies","body":"Beyond knowing the emergency signs, regular self-monitoring helps you stay ahead of problems. Check your blood pressure at home using a digital monitor — reliable ones cost KES 3,000-5,000 at pharmacies like Goodlife, Haltons, or Portal Pharmacy. Check it at the same time each day, ideally in the morning before medication and in the evening. Write down the numbers. Your target is typically below 130/80, but your cardiologist will give you your personal target. Weigh yourself daily in the morning — a sudden gain of 1-2 kg over a day or two can indicate fluid retention, a sign that your heart is struggling. Report any consistent upward trend in weight or blood pressure to your doctor."},{"id":"s11","title":"The bottom line","body":"Heart disease is manageable, and most days will be normal days. But being prepared for the abnormal day — knowing what to look for, knowing what to do, and having a plan in place — could save your life or the life of someone you love. Review these signs regularly. Quiz your family members. Keep your emergency numbers accessible. Your preparation today is an investment in your survival tomorrow."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'journaling'),
  (E'your-heart-condition-does-not-have-to-shrink-your-world', E'Your heart condition does not have to shrink your world', E'HEART_DISEASE', '{"HEART_DISEASE"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'A heart disease diagnosis can make you want to wrap yourself in cotton wool. You stop climbing stairs, cancel plans with friends, say no to family gatherings, and slowly your world shrinks to the size of your living room.', E'A heart disease diagnosis can make you want to wrap yourself in cotton wool. You stop climbing stairs, cancel plans with friends, say no to family gatherings, and slowly your world shrinks to the size of your living room. This is a natural first reaction, but it is not a strategy that will serve you well in the long run.\n\nWhy withdrawal makes things worse\n\nWhen you stop moving, your heart actually loses fitness faster. The heart is a muscle, and like any muscle, it needs regular, gentle use to stay strong. Sitting all day also raises your risk of blood clots, worsens blood pressure, and increases depression and anxiety. Research from Kenyatta National Hospital''s cardiology department confirms that patients who remain moderately active after a heart diagnosis have significantly better outcomes than those who become sedentary. Isolation compounds the problem because depression is a known independent risk factor for heart attacks and strokes.\n\nWhat you can actually do with heart disease\n\nMost people with well-managed heart disease can do far more than they think. Your cardiologist can help you understand your specific limits, but here is what is generally possible. You can walk daily, even in your neighbourhood or at Uhuru Park. You can climb stairs at a comfortable pace. You can travel, including flying, as long as your condition is stable and your doctor clears you. You can attend social gatherings, go to church, visit friends, and enjoy family events. You can work a full job, especially if it does not involve extreme physical labour. You can enjoy intimacy with your partner, though you should discuss timing with your medication schedule.\n\nBuilding your activity back up safely\n\nStart with short walks of 10 to 15 minutes around your estate or compound. Walk at a pace where you can still hold a conversation comfortably. Gradually increase by 5 minutes each week until you reach 30 minutes most days. If you live in Nairobi, early morning walks before the heat builds are ideal. In Mombasa or other coastal areas, the evening breeze makes late afternoon walks pleasant. Avoid exercising right after a heavy meal like a big plate of pilau or nyama choma. Wait at least an hour after eating.\n\nYour medication is your safety net\n\nThe medications your doctor prescribes, whether statins, ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, or blood thinners, are what protect your heart during daily activities. Take them consistently, at the same time each day. If cost is a concern, ask your doctor about generic options available at government pharmacies, which can cost as little as KES 200 to KES 500 per month compared to KES 2,000 or more at private pharmacies. NHIF and the new SHA cover many cardiac medications, so make sure your contributions are up to date and ask your hospital''s billing department what is covered.\n\nProtecting your heart through lifestyle, not avoidance\n\nReal protection comes from the combination of medication, a heart-friendly diet, moderate exercise, stress management, and regular check-ups. It does not come from avoiding life. Cut back on salt in your cooking. Use less Royco and instead season with garlic, ginger, coriander, and lemon. Choose grilled fish or chicken over deep-fried options. When eating ugali with sukuma wiki, keep portions moderate and add a variety of vegetables. Replace full-fat milk in your chai with low-fat options.\n\nMaintaining your social and emotional life\n\nTell your close friends and family about your condition so they understand if you need to rest during outings or avoid certain foods at gatherings. You do not need to broadcast it to everyone, but having a support circle makes an enormous difference. Join a cardiac support group if one is available at your hospital. Many county referral hospitals now run these groups, and meeting other people who understand what you are going through reduces the feeling of being alone in this.\n\nWhen to be cautious versus when to push through\n\nLearn your warning signs. Chest tightness, unusual breathlessness, dizziness, or pain spreading to your arm or jaw means stop what you are doing and rest. If symptoms persist for more than a few minutes, call for help or get to the nearest hospital. But ordinary tiredness after a walk or mild muscle soreness the next day is normal and not dangerous. Over time, you will learn to distinguish between your heart sending a warning and your body simply adjusting to being active again.\n\nYour heart condition is something you manage, not something that manages you. Every person at your clinic who is living well with heart disease made a choice at some point to stop shrinking their world and start carefully expanding it again. You can make that same choice today.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"A heart disease diagnosis can make you want to wrap yourself in cotton wool. You stop climbing stairs, cancel plans with friends, say no to family gatherings, and slowly your world shrinks to the size of your living room. This is a natural first reaction, but it is not a strategy that will serve you well in the long run."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why withdrawal makes things worse","body":"When you stop moving, your heart actually loses fitness faster. The heart is a muscle, and like any muscle, it needs regular, gentle use to stay strong. Sitting all day also raises your risk of blood clots, worsens blood pressure, and increases depression and anxiety. Research from Kenyatta National Hospital''s cardiology department confirms that patients who remain moderately active after a heart diagnosis have significantly better outcomes than those who become sedentary. Isolation compounds the problem because depression is a known independent risk factor for heart attacks and strokes."},{"id":"s3","title":"What you can actually do with heart disease","body":"Most people with well-managed heart disease can do far more than they think. Your cardiologist can help you understand your specific limits, but here is what is generally possible. You can walk daily, even in your neighbourhood or at Uhuru Park. You can climb stairs at a comfortable pace. You can travel, including flying, as long as your condition is stable and your doctor clears you. You can attend social gatherings, go to church, visit friends, and enjoy family events. You can work a full job, especially if it does not involve extreme physical labour. You can enjoy intimacy with your partner, though you should discuss timing with your medication schedule."},{"id":"s4","title":"Building your activity back up safely","body":"Start with short walks of 10 to 15 minutes around your estate or compound. Walk at a pace where you can still hold a conversation comfortably. Gradually increase by 5 minutes each week until you reach 30 minutes most days. If you live in Nairobi, early morning walks before the heat builds are ideal. In Mombasa or other coastal areas, the evening breeze makes late afternoon walks pleasant. Avoid exercising right after a heavy meal like a big plate of pilau or nyama choma. Wait at least an hour after eating."},{"id":"s5","title":"Your medication is your safety net","body":"The medications your doctor prescribes, whether statins, ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, or blood thinners, are what protect your heart during daily activities. Take them consistently, at the same time each day. If cost is a concern, ask your doctor about generic options available at government pharmacies, which can cost as little as KES 200 to KES 500 per month compared to KES 2,000 or more at private pharmacies. NHIF and the new SHA cover many cardiac medications, so make sure your contributions are up to date and ask your hospital''s billing department what is covered."},{"id":"s6","title":"Protecting your heart through lifestyle, not avoidance","body":"Real protection comes from the combination of medication, a heart-friendly diet, moderate exercise, stress management, and regular check-ups. It does not come from avoiding life. Cut back on salt in your cooking. Use less Royco and instead season with garlic, ginger, coriander, and lemon. Choose grilled fish or chicken over deep-fried options. When eating ugali with sukuma wiki, keep portions moderate and add a variety of vegetables. Replace full-fat milk in your chai with low-fat options."},{"id":"s7","title":"Maintaining your social and emotional life","body":"Tell your close friends and family about your condition so they understand if you need to rest during outings or avoid certain foods at gatherings. You do not need to broadcast it to everyone, but having a support circle makes an enormous difference. Join a cardiac support group if one is available at your hospital. Many county referral hospitals now run these groups, and meeting other people who understand what you are going through reduces the feeling of being alone in this."},{"id":"s8","title":"When to be cautious versus when to push through","body":"Learn your warning signs. Chest tightness, unusual breathlessness, dizziness, or pain spreading to your arm or jaw means stop what you are doing and rest. If symptoms persist for more than a few minutes, call for help or get to the nearest hospital. But ordinary tiredness after a walk or mild muscle soreness the next day is normal and not dangerous. Over time, you will learn to distinguish between your heart sending a warning and your body simply adjusting to being active again.\\n\\nYour heart condition is something you manage, not something that manages you. Every person at your clinic who is living well with heart disease made a choice at some point to stop shrinking their world and start carefully expanding it again. You can make that same choice today."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'self-care'),
  (E'your-cholesterol-is-improving', E'Your cholesterol is improving', E'HEART_DISEASE', '{"HEART_DISEASE"}', E'MILESTONE', E'If your LDL cholesterol has come down since your last blood test, take a moment to appreciate what that number means. You are actively reducing the buildup of plaque inside your arteries.', E'If your LDL cholesterol has come down since your last blood test, take a moment to appreciate what that number means. You are actively reducing the buildup of plaque inside your arteries. Every point your LDL drops is a small victory for your heart and blood vessels, and it came from real effort on your part.\n\nWhat LDL cholesterol actually does\n\nLDL stands for low-density lipoprotein, and it is often called bad cholesterol because when there is too much of it in your blood, it deposits fatty material on the walls of your arteries. Over time, this buildup narrows your arteries, making it harder for blood to flow to your heart and brain. This is what leads to heart attacks and strokes. When your LDL drops, less fatty material is being deposited, and your body can actually start to repair some of the existing damage. Think of it like a road that was getting clogged with debris. Reducing the debris means traffic flows better.\n\nThe numbers that matter\n\nFor most people with heart disease, your doctor wants your LDL below 2.6 mmol/L, and for high-risk patients, below 1.8 mmol/L. Ask your doctor what your target is and write it down. When you go for your next lipid panel test, which typically costs between KES 800 and KES 1,500 at private labs and is often free or subsidized at county hospitals, you will know exactly what number to look for. Total cholesterol, HDL (the good cholesterol), and triglycerides also matter, but LDL is the main one to track.\n\nHow your statin medication helped\n\nIf you are taking a statin like atorvastatin or rosuvastatin, this medication is doing heavy lifting in lowering your LDL. Statins work by blocking an enzyme in your liver that produces cholesterol. They are among the most studied medications in the world and have been proven to reduce heart attack and stroke risk significantly. Generic statins are available at most Kenyan pharmacies for between KES 300 and KES 800 per month. If you have been consistent with your daily dose, that consistency is a big part of why your numbers improved. Do not stop taking your statin because your numbers look better. The improvement is because of the medication. Stopping it will cause your cholesterol to rise again within weeks.\n\nThe dietary changes that made a difference\n\nYour food choices also played a role in this improvement. If you have been making any of these changes, they contributed to your better numbers. Switching from solid cooking fats like lard or hard margarine to liquid oils like sunflower or olive oil. Eating more beans, lentils, and githeri instead of relying heavily on red meat. Adding more vegetables to your plate, whether it is sukuma wiki, managu, terere, cabbage, or carrots. Reducing how often you eat deep-fried foods like mandazi, bhajia, and chips. Choosing to grill or boil meat instead of frying it. Cutting back on full-cream milk and processed snacks.\n\nEvery meal where you made a healthier choice contributed to this result. It may not have felt significant at the time, but your blood test proves that small daily choices add up.\n\nThe exercise component\n\nPhysical activity raises your HDL (good cholesterol) and helps your body process fats more efficiently. If you have been walking regularly, even 20 to 30 minutes most days around your estate, to the market, or at a nearby park, that movement helped shift your cholesterol profile in the right direction. You do not need intense workouts. Consistent moderate movement is what matters most.\n\nWhat to do next to keep improving\n\nContinue taking your statin exactly as prescribed. Set a daily alarm on your phone if it helps you remember. Keep making heart-friendly food choices. When you go to a hotel or restaurant, choose grilled options over fried ones. At home, experiment with herbs and spices like garlic, turmeric, ginger, and black pepper to make healthy food taste great. When shopping at the market, load up on leafy greens, tomatoes, onions, and legumes. These are affordable and widely available across Kenya, and they are some of the best foods for your heart.\n\nSchedule your next lipid panel test in three to six months, or whenever your doctor recommends. Track your numbers over time so you can see the trend. A single good reading is encouraging, but a consistent downward trend in LDL over multiple tests means your arteries are getting real, sustained protection.\n\nShare this milestone with your doctor\n\nAt your next clinic visit, mention that you noticed the improvement. Your doctor tracks these numbers to guide your treatment, and knowing that you are paying attention too makes the partnership stronger. If your numbers are improving well, your doctor might adjust your medication or set a new target. This is good collaborative care.\n\nYour arteries are thanking you for the effort you have put in. Every healthy meal, every walk, every pill taken on time brought you to this point. Keep going.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If your LDL cholesterol has come down since your last blood test, take a moment to appreciate what that number means. You are actively reducing the buildup of plaque inside your arteries. Every point your LDL drops is a small victory for your heart and blood vessels, and it came from real effort on your part."},{"id":"s2","title":"What LDL cholesterol actually does","body":"LDL stands for low-density lipoprotein, and it is often called bad cholesterol because when there is too much of it in your blood, it deposits fatty material on the walls of your arteries. Over time, this buildup narrows your arteries, making it harder for blood to flow to your heart and brain. This is what leads to heart attacks and strokes. When your LDL drops, less fatty material is being deposited, and your body can actually start to repair some of the existing damage. Think of it like a road that was getting clogged with debris. Reducing the debris means traffic flows better."},{"id":"s3","title":"The numbers that matter","body":"For most people with heart disease, your doctor wants your LDL below 2.6 mmol/L, and for high-risk patients, below 1.8 mmol/L. Ask your doctor what your target is and write it down. When you go for your next lipid panel test, which typically costs between KES 800 and KES 1,500 at private labs and is often free or subsidized at county hospitals, you will know exactly what number to look for. Total cholesterol, HDL (the good cholesterol), and triglycerides also matter, but LDL is the main one to track."},{"id":"s4","title":"How your statin medication helped","body":"If you are taking a statin like atorvastatin or rosuvastatin, this medication is doing heavy lifting in lowering your LDL. Statins work by blocking an enzyme in your liver that produces cholesterol. They are among the most studied medications in the world and have been proven to reduce heart attack and stroke risk significantly. Generic statins are available at most Kenyan pharmacies for between KES 300 and KES 800 per month. If you have been consistent with your daily dose, that consistency is a big part of why your numbers improved. Do not stop taking your statin because your numbers look better. The improvement is because of the medication. Stopping it will cause your cholesterol to rise again within weeks."},{"id":"s5","title":"The dietary changes that made a difference","body":"Your food choices also played a role in this improvement. If you have been making any of these changes, they contributed to your better numbers. Switching from solid cooking fats like lard or hard margarine to liquid oils like sunflower or olive oil. Eating more beans, lentils, and githeri instead of relying heavily on red meat. Adding more vegetables to your plate, whether it is sukuma wiki, managu, terere, cabbage, or carrots. Reducing how often you eat deep-fried foods like mandazi, bhajia, and chips. Choosing to grill or boil meat instead of frying it. Cutting back on full-cream milk and processed snacks.\\n\\nEvery meal where you made a healthier choice contributed to this result. It may not have felt significant at the time, but your blood test proves that small daily choices add up."},{"id":"s6","title":"The exercise component","body":"Physical activity raises your HDL (good cholesterol) and helps your body process fats more efficiently. If you have been walking regularly, even 20 to 30 minutes most days around your estate, to the market, or at a nearby park, that movement helped shift your cholesterol profile in the right direction. You do not need intense workouts. Consistent moderate movement is what matters most."},{"id":"s7","title":"What to do next to keep improving","body":"Continue taking your statin exactly as prescribed. Set a daily alarm on your phone if it helps you remember. Keep making heart-friendly food choices. When you go to a hotel or restaurant, choose grilled options over fried ones. At home, experiment with herbs and spices like garlic, turmeric, ginger, and black pepper to make healthy food taste great. When shopping at the market, load up on leafy greens, tomatoes, onions, and legumes. These are affordable and widely available across Kenya, and they are some of the best foods for your heart.\\n\\nSchedule your next lipid panel test in three to six months, or whenever your doctor recommends. Track your numbers over time so you can see the trend. A single good reading is encouraging, but a consistent downward trend in LDL over multiple tests means your arteries are getting real, sustained protection."},{"id":"s8","title":"Share this milestone with your doctor","body":"At your next clinic visit, mention that you noticed the improvement. Your doctor tracks these numbers to guide your treatment, and knowing that you are paying attention too makes the partnership stronger. If your numbers are improving well, your doctor might adjust your medication or set a new target. This is good collaborative care.\\n\\nYour arteries are thanking you for the effort you have put in. Every healthy meal, every walk, every pill taken on time brought you to this point. Keep going."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'progress-chart'),
  (E'staying-hydrated-to-prevent-sickle-cell-crises', E'Staying hydrated to prevent sickle cell crises', E'SICKLE_CELL', '{"SICKLE_CELL"}', E'DIETARY', E'Dehydration is one of the biggest and most preventable triggers for a sickle cell pain crisis. Understanding why water matters so much for your specific condition, and building habits that keep you hydrated throughout the day, can dramatically reduce how often you end up in pain or in hospital.', E'Dehydration is one of the biggest and most preventable triggers for a sickle cell pain crisis. Understanding why water matters so much for your specific condition, and building habits that keep you hydrated throughout the day, can dramatically reduce how often you end up in pain or in hospital.\n\nWhy dehydration triggers a crisis\n\nWhen you do not drink enough fluids, your blood becomes thicker and more concentrated. For someone with sickle cell disease, this is dangerous because sickle-shaped red blood cells are already stiff and sticky. In thick, dehydrated blood, these abnormal cells clump together much more easily and block small blood vessels. When blood flow is blocked, the tissue beyond the blockage does not get oxygen, and that is what causes the intense pain of a vaso-occlusive crisis. By keeping your blood well-hydrated, the sickle cells flow more freely and are less likely to cause blockages.\n\nHow much fluid you need daily\n\nAim for at least 8 to 10 glasses of water every day, which is roughly 2 to 2.5 litres. During hot weather, especially in places like Mombasa, Kisumu, or the Kenyan coast, or if you are physically active, you need even more. A good rule is to add an extra 2 glasses for every hour of activity or time spent in heat above 30 degrees. Do not wait until you feel thirsty to drink. By the time thirst kicks in, you are already mildly dehydrated. Instead, drink on a schedule.\n\nPractical hydration strategies for your day\n\nCarry a reusable water bottle everywhere you go. A 1-litre bottle is ideal because you can track how many times you refill it. Keep one at your desk if you work in an office, one in your bag when you travel, and one by your bed at night. Drink a full glass of water first thing in the morning before you eat anything. Have another glass with each meal. Sip water throughout the day, not just in large amounts at once.\n\nFluids that count toward your intake\n\nWater is the best choice, but other fluids count too. Warm herbal teas like chamomile, lemon and ginger, or hibiscus are excellent options. Uji, the fermented porridge many Kenyans enjoy for breakfast, has a high water content and is a good hydration option. Soups and broths, including the broth from cooking githeri or stews, all count. Coconut water, especially fresh from the coast, is an excellent natural hydrator.\n\nWhat to avoid\n\nAlcohol is a strong dehydrator and should be avoided or strictly limited. Beer, spirits, and wine all cause your body to lose more water than you take in, directly increasing your crisis risk. A cup or two of chai per day is generally fine as long as you are drinking plenty of water alongside it. Very sugary sodas are not ideal because high sugar content can slow down fluid absorption.\n\nMonitoring your hydration\n\nThe simplest way to check if you are drinking enough is to look at your urine colour. Pale yellow or nearly clear means you are well hydrated. Dark yellow or amber means you need to drink more right now. If you notice dark urine, do not wait. Drink two glasses of water immediately and continue sipping throughout the next hour.\n\nHydration during illness\n\nWhen you are sick with a fever, vomiting, or diarrhoea, your dehydration risk skyrockets. These are also times when you are most vulnerable to a sickle cell crisis. During illness, increase your fluid intake significantly. Oral rehydration salts, available at any pharmacy in Kenya for about KES 20 to KES 50 per sachet, are extremely effective at replacing lost fluids and electrolytes. Mix them with clean water according to the packet instructions. If you cannot keep fluids down due to vomiting, go to a hospital or clinic immediately because you may need intravenous fluids.\n\nShopping and preparation tips\n\nWhen you go to the market or supermarket, buy fruits with high water content. Watermelon, oranges, pineapple, mangoes, and cucumbers are all widely available in Kenya and help boost your hydration naturally. Keep cut fruit in your fridge as a ready snack. When cooking at home, include soups and stews in your meal rotation. A simple vegetable soup with carrots, potatoes, tomatoes, and leafy greens provides both nutrition and hydration. Even the broth from cooking githeri can be consumed as a warm drink.\n\nMaking it a family habit\n\nIf your family understands why hydration is so critical for you, they can help remind you to drink and can keep water accessible around the house. Children in the family can be encouraged to drink more water too, which builds good habits for everyone. Put a water jug on the dining table during meals so everyone serves themselves regularly.\n\nHydration is one of the most powerful and affordable tools you have for managing sickle cell disease. It costs almost nothing, it has no side effects, and it can prevent the terrible pain and hospital visits that come with a crisis. Make water your constant companion.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Dehydration is one of the biggest and most preventable triggers for a sickle cell pain crisis. Understanding why water matters so much for your specific condition, and building habits that keep you hydrated throughout the day, can dramatically reduce how often you end up in pain or in hospital."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why dehydration triggers a crisis","body":"When you do not drink enough fluids, your blood becomes thicker and more concentrated. For someone with sickle cell disease, this is dangerous because sickle-shaped red blood cells are already stiff and sticky. In th