Beer, spirits, and wine all cause your body to lose more water than you take in, directly increasing your crisis risk. A cup or two of chai per day is generally fine as long as you are drinking plenty of water alongside it. Very sugary sodas are not ideal because high sugar content can slow down fluid absorption."},{"id":"s7","title":"Monitoring your hydration","body":"The simplest way to check if you are drinking enough is to look at your urine colour. Pale yellow or nearly clear means you are well hydrated. Dark yellow or amber means you need to drink more right now. If you notice dark urine, do not wait. Drink two glasses of water immediately and continue sipping throughout the next hour."},{"id":"s8","title":"Hydration during illness","body":"When you are sick with a fever, vomiting, or diarrhoea, your dehydration risk skyrockets. These are also times when you are most vulnerable to a sickle cell crisis. During illness, increase your fluid intake significantly. Oral rehydration salts, available at any pharmacy in Kenya for about KES 20 to KES 50 per sachet, are extremely effective at replacing lost fluids and electrolytes. Mix them with clean water according to the packet instructions. If you cannot keep fluids down due to vomiting, go to a hospital or clinic immediately because you may need intravenous fluids."},{"id":"s9","title":"Shopping and preparation tips","body":"When you go to the market or supermarket, buy fruits with high water content. Watermelon, oranges, pineapple, mangoes, and cucumbers are all widely available in Kenya and help boost your hydration naturally. Keep cut fruit in your fridge as a ready snack. When cooking at home, include soups and stews in your meal rotation. A simple vegetable soup with carrots, potatoes, tomatoes, and leafy greens provides both nutrition and hydration. Even the broth from cooking githeri can be consumed as a warm drink."},{"id":"s10","title":"Making it a family habit","body":"If your family understands why hydration is so critical for you, they can help remind you to drink and can keep water accessible around the house. Children in the family can be encouraged to drink more water too, which builds good habits for everyone. Put a water jug on the dining table during meals so everyone serves themselves regularly.\\n\\nHydration is one of the most powerful and affordable tools you have for managing sickle cell disease. It costs almost nothing, it has no side effects, and it can prevent the terrible pain and hospital visits that come with a crisis. Make water your constant companion."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'market-shopping'),
  (E'exercise-without-triggering-a-crisis', E'Exercise without triggering a crisis', E'SICKLE_CELL', '{"SICKLE_CELL"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Physical activity is important for everyone, including people living with sickle cell disease. Exercise strengthens your heart, improves circulation, lifts your mood, and helps maintain a healthy weight.', E'Physical activity is important for everyone, including people living with sickle cell disease. Exercise strengthens your heart, improves circulation, lifts your mood, and helps maintain a healthy weight. But with sickle cell, you need to be strategic about how you move. Overdoing it can trigger a painful crisis. The goal is to find the sweet spot where you get the benefits of exercise without pushing your body into danger.\n\nThe talk test for safe intensity\n\nThe simplest way to know if you are exercising at the right intensity is the talk test. While you are moving, you should be able to carry on a conversation without gasping for breath. If you can talk comfortably, you are in the moderate zone, which is safe for sickle cell. If you are so breathless that you can only manage a word or two between breaths, you have gone too hard and need to slow down immediately.\n\nBest exercises for sickle cell\n\nWalking is the most accessible and safest exercise. You do not need special equipment or a gym membership. Walk around your estate, along the road to the market, or in a nearby park. In Nairobi, places like Karura Forest, Uhuru Gardens, and City Park offer pleasant walking paths. Aim for 20 to 30 minutes at a comfortable pace, five days a week. Gentle cycling, whether on a stationary bike or a regular bicycle on flat terrain, is another excellent option. It is low-impact and you can easily control the intensity. Yoga and stretching exercises are wonderful for sickle cell because they improve flexibility and promote relaxation without raising your heart rate dangerously. Light resistance exercises using your own body weight, like wall push-ups, seated leg lifts, and gentle squats, help maintain muscle strength.\n\nThe warm-up is not optional\n\nAlways warm up for at least 10 minutes before your main exercise. Start with gentle walking in place, arm circles, and slow marching. This gradually increases blood flow and helps prevent the sudden oxygen demand that can trigger sickling. After your main activity, cool down for 5 to 10 minutes with slow walking and gentle stretches. Never stop vigorous movement suddenly.\n\nWhat to avoid\n\nHigh-intensity activities that push your heart rate very high are risky. Avoid sprinting, competitive running races, intense aerobics classes, and heavy weightlifting. Contact sports like rugby and boxing carry the added risk of injury, which can trigger a crisis through pain and stress. Be very cautious about exercising in extreme heat. Nairobi''s highland climate is generally manageable, but if you live in or visit hotter areas like Mombasa, Kisumu, or Turkana, exercise during the coolest parts of the day, early morning or late evening. Swimming is generally good exercise, but avoid cold water. Cold temperatures cause blood vessels to constrict, which increases sickling risk. If you swim, choose a heated pool or swim during warm weather in water that feels comfortable, not chilly.\n\nHydration before, during, and after\n\nDrink at least two glasses of water 30 minutes before you start exercising. Take small sips every 10 to 15 minutes during your activity. After you finish, drink another two to three glasses over the next hour. Dehydration during exercise is a double risk because both the fluid loss and the increased oxygen demand can trigger a crisis. Keep a water bottle with you at all times during exercise. If you are exercising outdoors in warm weather, consider adding a pinch of salt or using oral rehydration salts in your water to replace electrolytes lost through sweat.\n\nListening to your body\n\nStop exercising immediately if you experience any of these warning signs: pain in your chest, back, arms, legs, or abdomen. Extreme fatigue that feels different from normal exercise tiredness. Shortness of breath that does not improve when you slow down. Dizziness or feeling faint. A rapid or irregular heartbeat. Fever or feeling unusually cold. These could be early signs of a crisis, and continuing to exercise will make things worse. Rest, drink water, keep warm, and if symptoms persist for more than 15 to 20 minutes, seek medical attention.\n\nBuilding a routine that works for you\n\nStart slowly, especially if you have not been active for a while. Begin with just 10 minutes of walking per day for the first week. Add 5 minutes each week until you reach 30 minutes. Exercise at the same time each day to build a habit. Many people find that morning exercise works best because temperatures are cooler and your energy is typically higher. Keep a simple exercise diary noting what you did, how long, and how you felt afterward. This helps you identify patterns and know what your body tolerates well.\n\nExercise should leave you feeling energised and positive, not drained and in pain. With sickle cell, the approach is always gentle consistency rather than occasional intensity. Your body will thank you for moving regularly, and over time, you will notice improved stamina, better mood, and fewer days spent feeling sluggish.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Physical activity is important for everyone, including people living with sickle cell disease. Exercise strengthens your heart, improves circulation, lifts your mood, and helps maintain a healthy weight. But with sickle cell, you need to be strategic about how you move. Overdoing it can trigger a painful crisis. The goal is to find the sweet spot where you get the benefits of exercise without pushing your body into danger."},{"id":"s2","title":"The talk test for safe intensity","body":"The simplest way to know if you are exercising at the right intensity is the talk test. While you are moving, you should be able to carry on a conversation without gasping for breath. If you can talk comfortably, you are in the moderate zone, which is safe for sickle cell. If you are so breathless that you can only manage a word or two between breaths, you have gone too hard and need to slow down immediately."},{"id":"s3","title":"Best exercises for sickle cell","body":"Walking is the most accessible and safest exercise. You do not need special equipment or a gym membership. Walk around your estate, along the road to the market, or in a nearby park. In Nairobi, places like Karura Forest, Uhuru Gardens, and City Park offer pleasant walking paths. Aim for 20 to 30 minutes at a comfortable pace, five days a week. Gentle cycling, whether on a stationary bike or a regular bicycle on flat terrain, is another excellent option. It is low-impact and you can easily control the intensity. Yoga and stretching exercises are wonderful for sickle cell because they improve flexibility and promote relaxation without raising your heart rate dangerously. Light resistance exercises using your own body weight, like wall push-ups, seated leg lifts, and gentle squats, help maintain muscle strength."},{"id":"s4","title":"The warm-up is not optional","body":"Always warm up for at least 10 minutes before your main exercise. Start with gentle walking in place, arm circles, and slow marching. This gradually increases blood flow and helps prevent the sudden oxygen demand that can trigger sickling. After your main activity, cool down for 5 to 10 minutes with slow walking and gentle stretches. Never stop vigorous movement suddenly."},{"id":"s5","title":"What to avoid","body":"High-intensity activities that push your heart rate very high are risky. Avoid sprinting, competitive running races, intense aerobics classes, and heavy weightlifting. Contact sports like rugby and boxing carry the added risk of injury, which can trigger a crisis through pain and stress. Be very cautious about exercising in extreme heat. Nairobi''s highland climate is generally manageable, but if you live in or visit hotter areas like Mombasa, Kisumu, or Turkana, exercise during the coolest parts of the day, early morning or late evening. Swimming is generally good exercise, but avoid cold water. Cold temperatures cause blood vessels to constrict, which increases sickling risk. If you swim, choose a heated pool or swim during warm weather in water that feels comfortable, not chilly."},{"id":"s6","title":"Hydration before, during, and after","body":"Drink at least two glasses of water 30 minutes before you start exercising. Take small sips every 10 to 15 minutes during your activity. After you finish, drink another two to three glasses over the next hour. Dehydration during exercise is a double risk because both the fluid loss and the increased oxygen demand can trigger a crisis. Keep a water bottle with you at all times during exercise. If you are exercising outdoors in warm weather, consider adding a pinch of salt or using oral rehydration salts in your water to replace electrolytes lost through sweat."},{"id":"s7","title":"Listening to your body","body":"Stop exercising immediately if you experience any of these warning signs: pain in your chest, back, arms, legs, or abdomen. Extreme fatigue that feels different from normal exercise tiredness. Shortness of breath that does not improve when you slow down. Dizziness or feeling faint. A rapid or irregular heartbeat. Fever or feeling unusually cold. These could be early signs of a crisis, and continuing to exercise will make things worse. Rest, drink water, keep warm, and if symptoms persist for more than 15 to 20 minutes, seek medical attention."},{"id":"s8","title":"Building a routine that works for you","body":"Start slowly, especially if you have not been active for a while. Begin with just 10 minutes of walking per day for the first week. Add 5 minutes each week until you reach 30 minutes. Exercise at the same time each day to build a habit. Many people find that morning exercise works best because temperatures are cooler and your energy is typically higher. Keep a simple exercise diary noting what you did, how long, and how you felt afterward. This helps you identify patterns and know what your body tolerates well.\\n\\nExercise should leave you feeling energised and positive, not drained and in pain. With sickle cell, the approach is always gentle consistency rather than occasional intensity. Your body will thank you for moving regularly, and over time, you will notice improved stamina, better mood, and fewer days spent feeling sluggish."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'yoga-pose'),
  (E'myth-sickle-cell-disease-only-affects-children', E'Myth: Sickle cell disease only affects children', E'SICKLE_CELL', '{"SICKLE_CELL"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The myth that sickle cell disease only affects children is one of the most harmful misconceptions about this condition. It leads to adult patients being overlooked, underfunded, and under-supported by healthcare systems that focus almost exclusively on paediatric care.', E'The myth that sickle cell disease only affects children is one of the most harmful misconceptions about this condition. It leads to adult patients being overlooked, underfunded, and under-supported by healthcare systems that focus almost exclusively on paediatric care.\n\nWhere this myth comes from\n\nHistorically, sickle cell disease was so severe that many children did not survive to adulthood, particularly in regions with limited access to healthcare. In the past, the median life expectancy for sickle cell patients in sub-Saharan Africa was tragically low. This created a perception that sickle cell was a childhood disease because few adults with the condition were visible in communities. But medical advances over the past few decades have changed this dramatically.\n\nThe truth about life expectancy today\n\nThanks to improved care, including newborn screening, prophylactic antibiotics, hydroxyurea therapy, better pain management, and blood transfusion protocols, many people with sickle cell disease now live into their 40s, 50s, and beyond. In Kenya, where awareness and treatment have improved significantly through efforts at hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital, Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, and various county referral hospitals, more adults than ever are living with sickle cell disease. The Kenya Sickle Cell Alliance and various patient advocacy groups have been instrumental in pushing for better adult care.\n\nAdult complications are real and serious\n\nWhile surviving to adulthood is a triumph, it comes with its own set of medical challenges requiring ongoing care. Chronic pain is one of the most common adult complications. Years of repeated crises can lead to avascular necrosis, where bone tissue dies due to poor blood supply, particularly in the hips and shoulders. Organ damage accumulates over time. The kidneys, lungs, liver, and eyes can all be affected by years of sickling episodes. Chronic kidney disease is common and may require dialysis. Pulmonary hypertension develops in some adults and increases the risk of heart failure. Stroke risk remains elevated throughout life, not just in childhood. Leg ulcers and gallstones from chronic red blood cell breakdown are frustrating complications that affect quality of life.\n\nWhy this myth is harmful\n\nWhen healthcare systems treat sickle cell as a childhood condition, several harmful things happen. Adult patients struggle to find doctors who specialize in their care. Paediatric haematologists manage children beautifully, but when those children turn 18 and transition to adult care, they often fall into a gap where no specialist takes over. Funding and research focus disproportionately on children, leaving adult treatment protocols less developed. Employers and schools may not understand that a grown adult can be seriously affected by sickle cell, leading to discrimination and lack of accommodation. Adults with sickle cell may internalize the myth themselves and stop seeking regular care, thinking they should have outgrown their symptoms.\n\nWhat adult sickle cell care looks like\n\nIf you are an adult living with sickle cell disease in Kenya, here is what comprehensive care should include. Regular check-ups with a haematologist, ideally every three to six months. Annual screening for organ damage including kidney function tests, which cost between KES 500 and KES 1,500 at most Kenyan labs, liver function tests, eye exams, and echocardiograms. Hydroxyurea therapy if prescribed. This medication reduces the frequency of crises and is increasingly available in Kenya. A daily dose can cost between KES 1,000 and KES 3,000 per month depending on the pharmacy and dosage. Ask about coverage through NHIF or the new SHA scheme. Folic acid supplementation to support red blood cell production. Up-to-date vaccinations, including pneumococcal and influenza vaccines, because the spleen often does not function properly in sickle cell patients. A pain management plan developed with your doctor that you can implement at home for mild to moderate crises. Mental health support, because the chronic nature of the disease takes an emotional toll that deserves professional attention.\n\nWhat you can do to advocate for yourself\n\nKnow your condition thoroughly. Keep copies of your medical records, including your genotype, baseline haemoglobin level, and history of complications. When you visit a new doctor or end up in an emergency room where staff may not know your history, this information can be life-saving. Connect with adult sickle cell support groups in Kenya. These communities share practical advice, recommend doctors, and provide the emotional support that comes from being understood by people who share your experience.\n\nSickle cell disease does not end at childhood. If you are an adult living with it, you deserve the same quality of medical care, the same research attention, and the same community support that children with the condition receive. Do not let an outdated myth convince you otherwise.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The myth that sickle cell disease only affects children is one of the most harmful misconceptions about this condition. It leads to adult patients being overlooked, underfunded, and under-supported by healthcare systems that focus almost exclusively on paediatric care."},{"id":"s2","title":"Where this myth comes from","body":"Historically, sickle cell disease was so severe that many children did not survive to adulthood, particularly in regions with limited access to healthcare. In the past, the median life expectancy for sickle cell patients in sub-Saharan Africa was tragically low. This created a perception that sickle cell was a childhood disease because few adults with the condition were visible in communities. But medical advances over the past few decades have changed this dramatically."},{"id":"s3","title":"The truth about life expectancy today","body":"Thanks to improved care, including newborn screening, prophylactic antibiotics, hydroxyurea therapy, better pain management, and blood transfusion protocols, many people with sickle cell disease now live into their 40s, 50s, and beyond. In Kenya, where awareness and treatment have improved significantly through efforts at hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital, Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, and various county referral hospitals, more adults than ever are living with sickle cell disease. The Kenya Sickle Cell Alliance and various patient advocacy groups have been instrumental in pushing for better adult care."},{"id":"s4","title":"Adult complications are real and serious","body":"While surviving to adulthood is a triumph, it comes with its own set of medical challenges requiring ongoing care. Chronic pain is one of the most common adult complications. Years of repeated crises can lead to avascular necrosis, where bone tissue dies due to poor blood supply, particularly in the hips and shoulders. Organ damage accumulates over time. The kidneys, lungs, liver, and eyes can all be affected by years of sickling episodes. Chronic kidney disease is common and may require dialysis. Pulmonary hypertension develops in some adults and increases the risk of heart failure. Stroke risk remains elevated throughout life, not just in childhood. Leg ulcers and gallstones from chronic red blood cell breakdown are frustrating complications that affect quality of life."},{"id":"s5","title":"Why this myth is harmful","body":"When healthcare systems treat sickle cell as a childhood condition, several harmful things happen. Adult patients struggle to find doctors who specialize in their care. Paediatric haematologists manage children beautifully, but when those children turn 18 and transition to adult care, they often fall into a gap where no specialist takes over. Funding and research focus disproportionately on children, leaving adult treatment protocols less developed. Employers and schools may not understand that a grown adult can be seriously affected by sickle cell, leading to discrimination and lack of accommodation. Adults with sickle cell may internalize the myth themselves and stop seeking regular care, thinking they should have outgrown their symptoms."},{"id":"s6","title":"What adult sickle cell care looks like","body":"If you are an adult living with sickle cell disease in Kenya, here is what comprehensive care should include. Regular check-ups with a haematologist, ideally every three to six months. Annual screening for organ damage including kidney function tests, which cost between KES 500 and KES 1,500 at most Kenyan labs, liver function tests, eye exams, and echocardiograms. Hydroxyurea therapy if prescribed. This medication reduces the frequency of crises and is increasingly available in Kenya. A daily dose can cost between KES 1,000 and KES 3,000 per month depending on the pharmacy and dosage. Ask about coverage through NHIF or the new SHA scheme. Folic acid supplementation to support red blood cell production. Up-to-date vaccinations, including pneumococcal and influenza vaccines, because the spleen often does not function properly in sickle cell patients. A pain management plan developed with your doctor that you can implement at home for mild to moderate crises. Mental health support, because the chronic nature of the disease takes an emotional toll that deserves professional attention."},{"id":"s7","title":"What you can do to advocate for yourself","body":"Know your condition thoroughly. Keep copies of your medical records, including your genotype, baseline haemoglobin level, and history of complications. When you visit a new doctor or end up in an emergency room where staff may not know your history, this information can be life-saving. Connect with adult sickle cell support groups in Kenya. These communities share practical advice, recommend doctors, and provide the emotional support that comes from being understood by people who share your experience.\\n\\nSickle cell disease does not end at childhood. If you are an adult living with it, you deserve the same quality of medical care, the same research attention, and the same community support that children with the condition receive. Do not let an outdated myth convince you otherwise."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'medical-truth'),
  (E'dealing-with-unpredictable-pain-episodes', E'Dealing with unpredictable pain episodes', E'SICKLE_CELL', '{"SICKLE_CELL"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'Living with sickle cell disease means living with uncertainty. You can wake up feeling fine and be in excruciating pain by afternoon.', E'Living with sickle cell disease means living with uncertainty. You can wake up feeling fine and be in excruciating pain by afternoon. You can plan a trip, a work project, or a family celebration, only to have a crisis force you to cancel everything. This unpredictability takes an enormous emotional toll that is often invisible to the people around you.\n\nThe anxiety of not knowing when pain will strike\n\nMany people with sickle cell describe a constant low-level anxiety, like waiting for a storm that may or may not come. You might check the weather obsessively because cold or extreme heat can trigger a crisis. You might avoid making commitments because you have been embarrassed too many times by last-minute cancellations. Some people stop planning anything enjoyable altogether because the disappointment of missing out feels worse than never having plans at all. This is a completely understandable response to unpredictable pain, but over time, it shrinks your life in ways that make the emotional burden even heavier.\n\nHow isolation compounds the problem\n\nWhen you withdraw from social activities, friendships, and community involvement, you lose the very support systems that help you cope. Friends may stop inviting you because they think you are not interested. Family members may not understand why you seem withdrawn or moody. At work or school, colleagues may view your absences as unreliability rather than a medical reality. In Kenyan communities where social connection is central to daily life, from church gatherings to neighbourhood events to family functions, this isolation can feel especially painful. You see others living full, active lives while you sit at home waiting for pain to pass.\n\nBuilding a support network that understands\n\nThe most powerful thing you can do for your emotional wellbeing is to build a circle of people who truly understand your condition. Start with honest conversations with your closest family members. Explain what a crisis feels like, how unpredictable it is, and what you need from them during an episode. Many family members want to help but do not know how. Tell them specifically: I need someone to bring me warm water. I need help getting to the hospital if the pain does not respond to medication at home. I need you to understand that when I cancel plans, it is not because I do not want to be there.\n\nLook for sickle cell support groups in your area. In Nairobi, organizations like the Kenya Sickle Cell Alliance connect patients and families. County hospitals sometimes run support groups facilitated by social workers. Online communities on Facebook and WhatsApp also provide spaces where people with sickle cell share experiences and encouragement.\n\nCreating your pain management plan\n\nOne of the best ways to reduce the fear of crises is to have a clear plan for when they happen. Keep a crisis kit at home that includes your prescribed pain medication, paracetamol, a hot water bottle, a warm blanket, a large water bottle, oral rehydration salts costing about KES 20 to KES 50 per sachet at any pharmacy, and a list of emergency contacts. At the first sign of pain, take your prescribed pain relief immediately, apply heat to the painful area, drink plenty of warm fluids, and rest in a comfortable position. If the pain does not improve within two hours or gets worse, go to the hospital. Knowing you have a plan ready reduces the fear of the unknown.\n\nCommunicating with your employer or school\n\nIf you work or study, a brief explanation that you have a chronic blood condition that sometimes causes acute pain can help them understand your occasional absences. Under Kenyan employment law, you are entitled to sick leave, and a doctor''s note from your haematologist can protect your position.\n\nProfessional mental health support\n\nIf anxiety, depression, or emotional exhaustion are significantly affecting your quality of life, consider speaking to a mental health professional. Counselling is not a sign of weakness. It is a practical tool for managing the psychological impact of a chronic, painful condition. Many county hospitals have clinical psychologists, and sessions may be covered or subsidized through NHIF or SHA. Private counsellors in Kenya typically charge between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 per session. Online counselling platforms have also made mental health support more accessible, especially if you live in an area without nearby services.\n\nYou are not being dramatic\n\nSickle cell pain is among the most severe pain conditions known in medicine. The emotional weight of living with that kind of pain, unpredictably and repeatedly, is enormous. If you feel overwhelmed, anxious, or depressed, you are having a normal reaction to an extraordinarily difficult situation. You deserve support, understanding, and compassion, both from others and from yourself.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Living with sickle cell disease means living with uncertainty. You can wake up feeling fine and be in excruciating pain by afternoon. You can plan a trip, a work project, or a family celebration, only to have a crisis force you to cancel everything. This unpredictability takes an enormous emotional toll that is often invisible to the people around you."},{"id":"s2","title":"The anxiety of not knowing when pain will strike","body":"Many people with sickle cell describe a constant low-level anxiety, like waiting for a storm that may or may not come. You might check the weather obsessively because cold or extreme heat can trigger a crisis. You might avoid making commitments because you have been embarrassed too many times by last-minute cancellations. Some people stop planning anything enjoyable altogether because the disappointment of missing out feels worse than never having plans at all. This is a completely understandable response to unpredictable pain, but over time, it shrinks your life in ways that make the emotional burden even heavier."},{"id":"s3","title":"How isolation compounds the problem","body":"When you withdraw from social activities, friendships, and community involvement, you lose the very support systems that help you cope. Friends may stop inviting you because they think you are not interested. Family members may not understand why you seem withdrawn or moody. At work or school, colleagues may view your absences as unreliability rather than a medical reality. In Kenyan communities where social connection is central to daily life, from church gatherings to neighbourhood events to family functions, this isolation can feel especially painful. You see others living full, active lives while you sit at home waiting for pain to pass."},{"id":"s4","title":"Building a support network that understands","body":"The most powerful thing you can do for your emotional wellbeing is to build a circle of people who truly understand your condition. Start with honest conversations with your closest family members. Explain what a crisis feels like, how unpredictable it is, and what you need from them during an episode. Many family members want to help but do not know how. Tell them specifically: I need someone to bring me warm water. I need help getting to the hospital if the pain does not respond to medication at home. I need you to understand that when I cancel plans, it is not because I do not want to be there.\\n\\nLook for sickle cell support groups in your area. In Nairobi, organizations like the Kenya Sickle Cell Alliance connect patients and families. County hospitals sometimes run support groups facilitated by social workers. Online communities on Facebook and WhatsApp also provide spaces where people with sickle cell share experiences and encouragement."},{"id":"s5","title":"Creating your pain management plan","body":"One of the best ways to reduce the fear of crises is to have a clear plan for when they happen. Keep a crisis kit at home that includes your prescribed pain medication, paracetamol, a hot water bottle, a warm blanket, a large water bottle, oral rehydration salts costing about KES 20 to KES 50 per sachet at any pharmacy, and a list of emergency contacts. At the first sign of pain, take your prescribed pain relief immediately, apply heat to the painful area, drink plenty of warm fluids, and rest in a comfortable position. If the pain does not improve within two hours or gets worse, go to the hospital. Knowing you have a plan ready reduces the fear of the unknown."},{"id":"s6","title":"Communicating with your employer or school","body":"If you work or study, a brief explanation that you have a chronic blood condition that sometimes causes acute pain can help them understand your occasional absences. Under Kenyan employment law, you are entitled to sick leave, and a doctor''s note from your haematologist can protect your position."},{"id":"s7","title":"Professional mental health support","body":"If anxiety, depression, or emotional exhaustion are significantly affecting your quality of life, consider speaking to a mental health professional. Counselling is not a sign of weakness. It is a practical tool for managing the psychological impact of a chronic, painful condition. Many county hospitals have clinical psychologists, and sessions may be covered or subsidized through NHIF or SHA. Private counsellors in Kenya typically charge between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 per session. Online counselling platforms have also made mental health support more accessible, especially if you live in an area without nearby services."},{"id":"s8","title":"You are not being dramatic","body":"Sickle cell pain is among the most severe pain conditions known in medicine. The emotional weight of living with that kind of pain, unpredictably and repeatedly, is enormous. If you feel overwhelmed, anxious, or depressed, you are having a normal reaction to an extraordinarily difficult situation. You deserve support, understanding, and compassion, both from others and from yourself."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'community-care'),
  (E'recognising-early-warning-signs-of-a-crisis', E'Recognising early warning signs of a crisis', E'SICKLE_CELL', '{"SICKLE_CELL"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'A sickle cell crisis often does not arrive without warning. If you learn to recognise the subtle early signs, you can frequently prevent a mild episode from becoming a full-blown crisis that lands you in hospital.', E'A sickle cell crisis often does not arrive without warning. If you learn to recognise the subtle early signs, you can frequently prevent a mild episode from becoming a full-blown crisis that lands you in hospital. Self-monitoring is one of the most powerful skills you can develop as a person living with sickle cell disease.\n\nThe early warning signs to watch for\n\nYour body often sends signals hours or even a day before severe pain sets in. The key is learning to distinguish these signals from ordinary tiredness or minor aches. Mild aching in your bones or joints that feels different from the soreness you get after physical activity. This aching may start in your lower back, hips, knees, or arms and gradually build. Fatigue that feels deeper and heavier than normal tiredness. If you slept well but still feel drained and foggy, pay attention. A slight fever, usually between 37.5 and 38 degrees Celsius. Even a small temperature rise can indicate that sickling is beginning. Pale or yellowish tint to the whites of your eyes or your palms, suggesting increased red blood cell breakdown. A feeling of general unease or discomfort that you cannot quite explain. Many patients describe a gut feeling that something is off before any specific pain begins.\n\nWhat to do when you notice early signs\n\nThe moment you recognise any of these warning signs, act immediately. Do not wait to see if it gets worse. The earlier you intervene, the better your chance of preventing a full crisis. Start drinking water aggressively. Aim for a glass every 30 minutes for the next two hours. This helps thin your blood and reduce sickling. Rest and avoid any strenuous activity. Your body is telling you it is under stress, so listen. Keep yourself warm. Cold temperatures constrict blood vessels and worsen sickling. Put on an extra layer of clothing, use a blanket, or use a hot water bottle on areas that are beginning to ache. Take your prescribed pain relief early. If your doctor has given you medications to use at the start of a crisis, take them now, not later. Paracetamol and ibuprofen, available at Kenyan pharmacies for KES 50 to KES 200, can be effective when taken early. If your doctor has prescribed stronger medications for crisis management, follow their instructions.\n\nKeeping a crisis diary\n\nOne of the most useful self-monitoring tools is a simple diary where you record each crisis or near-crisis. Write down the date symptoms started, what you were doing before, the weather conditions, what you ate and drank in the 24 hours before, and what medications you took. Over time, this diary reveals patterns. You might discover that your crises tend to happen during cold weather, after poor hydration, or during high-stress weeks at work. Once you know your personal triggers, you can take extra precautions during high-risk times.\n\nMonitoring your temperature\n\nA fever in someone with sickle cell disease is always a medical concern. Keep a digital thermometer at home, available at most Kenyan pharmacies for KES 300 to KES 1,000. A reading above 38 degrees Celsius requires medical attention because fever can indicate infection, and people with sickle cell are more vulnerable to serious infections due to reduced spleen function. Go to a hospital or call your doctor, especially if the fever is accompanied by chills, cough, or pain.\n\nKnowing your baseline numbers\n\nAsk your haematologist for your baseline haemoglobin level, the number when you are feeling well. For most people with sickle cell SS disease, this is typically between 6 and 9 g/dL. If your haemoglobin drops more than 2 points below your baseline, you may need a blood transfusion. Keep a card in your wallet or a note on your phone with your baseline haemoglobin, your genotype, and any known complications.\n\nYour crisis kit\n\nKeep a crisis kit packed and ready at home at all times. It should contain paracetamol and any prescribed pain medications, a hot water bottle available at most pharmacies for KES 500 to KES 1,500, oral rehydration salts, a warm blanket, a thermometer, a large water bottle, a list of emergency contacts including your haematologist''s phone number, and enough M-Pesa balance to call for help or arrange transport to hospital.\n\nWhen to go to the hospital\n\nGo to the hospital immediately if your pain does not improve after two hours of home management. If you develop a fever above 38 degrees Celsius. If you experience sudden severe chest pain or difficulty breathing, which could indicate acute chest syndrome, a life-threatening complication. If you notice sudden weakness on one side of your body or difficulty speaking, which could indicate stroke. If your eyes become very yellow very quickly. If you feel your spleen suddenly enlarging, felt as a mass in the upper left abdomen.\n\nSelf-monitoring puts you in the driver''s seat of your sickle cell management. You know your body better than anyone. Trust what it tells you, act early, and you will spend more of your days living well and fewer in hospital beds.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"A sickle cell crisis often does not arrive without warning. If you learn to recognise the subtle early signs, you can frequently prevent a mild episode from becoming a full-blown crisis that lands you in hospital. Self-monitoring is one of the most powerful skills you can develop as a person living with sickle cell disease."},{"id":"s2","title":"The early warning signs to watch for","body":"Your body often sends signals hours or even a day before severe pain sets in. The key is learning to distinguish these signals from ordinary tiredness or minor aches. Mild aching in your bones or joints that feels different from the soreness you get after physical activity. This aching may start in your lower back, hips, knees, or arms and gradually build. Fatigue that feels deeper and heavier than normal tiredness. If you slept well but still feel drained and foggy, pay attention. A slight fever, usually between 37.5 and 38 degrees Celsius. Even a small temperature rise can indicate that sickling is beginning. Pale or yellowish tint to the whites of your eyes or your palms, suggesting increased red blood cell breakdown. A feeling of general unease or discomfort that you cannot quite explain. Many patients describe a gut feeling that something is off before any specific pain begins."},{"id":"s3","title":"What to do when you notice early signs","body":"The moment you recognise any of these warning signs, act immediately. Do not wait to see if it gets worse. The earlier you intervene, the better your chance of preventing a full crisis. Start drinking water aggressively. Aim for a glass every 30 minutes for the next two hours. This helps thin your blood and reduce sickling. Rest and avoid any strenuous activity. Your body is telling you it is under stress, so listen. Keep yourself warm. Cold temperatures constrict blood vessels and worsen sickling. Put on an extra layer of clothing, use a blanket, or use a hot water bottle on areas that are beginning to ache. Take your prescribed pain relief early. If your doctor has given you medications to use at the start of a crisis, take them now, not later. Paracetamol and ibuprofen, available at Kenyan pharmacies for KES 50 to KES 200, can be effective when taken early. If your doctor has prescribed stronger medications for crisis management, follow their instructions."},{"id":"s4","title":"Keeping a crisis diary","body":"One of the most useful self-monitoring tools is a simple diary where you record each crisis or near-crisis. Write down the date symptoms started, what you were doing before, the weather conditions, what you ate and drank in the 24 hours before, and what medications you took. Over time, this diary reveals patterns. You might discover that your crises tend to happen during cold weather, after poor hydration, or during high-stress weeks at work. Once you know your personal triggers, you can take extra precautions during high-risk times."},{"id":"s5","title":"Monitoring your temperature","body":"A fever in someone with sickle cell disease is always a medical concern. Keep a digital thermometer at home, available at most Kenyan pharmacies for KES 300 to KES 1,000. A reading above 38 degrees Celsius requires medical attention because fever can indicate infection, and people with sickle cell are more vulnerable to serious infections due to reduced spleen function. Go to a hospital or call your doctor, especially if the fever is accompanied by chills, cough, or pain."},{"id":"s6","title":"Knowing your baseline numbers","body":"Ask your haematologist for your baseline haemoglobin level, the number when you are feeling well. For most people with sickle cell SS disease, this is typically between 6 and 9 g/dL. If your haemoglobin drops more than 2 points below your baseline, you may need a blood transfusion. Keep a card in your wallet or a note on your phone with your baseline haemoglobin, your genotype, and any known complications."},{"id":"s7","title":"Your crisis kit","body":"Keep a crisis kit packed and ready at home at all times. It should contain paracetamol and any prescribed pain medications, a hot water bottle available at most pharmacies for KES 500 to KES 1,500, oral rehydration salts, a warm blanket, a thermometer, a large water bottle, a list of emergency contacts including your haematologist''s phone number, and enough M-Pesa balance to call for help or arrange transport to hospital."},{"id":"s8","title":"When to go to the hospital","body":"Go to the hospital immediately if your pain does not improve after two hours of home management. If you develop a fever above 38 degrees Celsius. If you experience sudden severe chest pain or difficulty breathing, which could indicate acute chest syndrome, a life-threatening complication. If you notice sudden weakness on one side of your body or difficulty speaking, which could indicate stroke. If your eyes become very yellow very quickly. If you feel your spleen suddenly enlarging, felt as a mass in the upper left abdomen.\\n\\nSelf-monitoring puts you in the driver''s seat of your sickle cell management. You know your body better than anyone. Trust what it tells you, act early, and you will spend more of your days living well and fewer in hospital beds."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'medication-tracking'),
  (E'you-are-more-than-your-sickle-cell-status', E'You are more than your sickle cell status', E'SICKLE_CELL', '{"SICKLE_CELL"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'Living with sickle cell disease since birth means it has shaped your entire experience in ways that other people may never fully understand. You have known hospital corridors since before you could walk.', E'Living with sickle cell disease since birth means it has shaped your entire experience in ways that other people may never fully understand. You have known hospital corridors since before you could walk. You have missed school events, family celebrations, and ordinary childhood moments because of pain that no one around you could see. As an adult, sickle cell continues to influence your career, relationships, and daily decisions. Accepting this reality, truly accepting it, is not about giving up. It is about finding freedom within it.\n\nYou are not defined by your haemoglobin\n\nSickle cell disease is a condition in your blood, but it is not who you are. You are a person with dreams, talents, humour, intelligence, and relationships that have nothing to do with your red blood cells. Maybe you are a brilliant student, a creative artist, a loving parent, a dedicated worker, or a loyal friend. These identities are just as real and just as important as your medical identity. In Kenyan culture, where community and family roles are central, it can feel like your condition becomes your defining label, the one who is always sick, the one who cannot be relied upon, the one who needs special treatment. Rejecting that label is not denial. It is a choice to lead with your strengths rather than your struggles.\n\nThe grief of living with a chronic condition\n\nAcceptance does not mean you cannot grieve for what sickle cell has taken from you. It is okay to feel angry about missed opportunities, frustrated by limitations, and sad about the uncertainty of your future. Many people with sickle cell describe a sense of loss for the life they might have had without this condition. Grieving these losses is healthy and necessary. What is not healthy is getting stuck in that grief permanently. Allow yourself to feel it, talk about it with someone you trust, and then gently redirect your energy toward the life you can build within your reality.\n\nLiving fully on good days\n\nOne of the most important shifts in thinking is learning to make the most of your good days rather than spending them worrying about when the next bad day will come. When you feel well, go out. See your friends. Take that walk. Enjoy a meal of ugali and fish stew at your favourite spot. Visit family. Work on your projects. Do the things that bring you joy and fulfilment. These good days are not borrowed time. They are your life. Living them fully is not reckless. It is wise.\n\nBeing gentle on hard days\n\nEqually important is learning to be compassionate with yourself on difficult days. When a crisis strikes and you are in bed with a hot water bottle, unable to move without pain, do not add guilt to your suffering. You are not lazy. You are not a burden. You are managing one of the most painful conditions known to medicine. Rest is not defeat. It is treatment. Let people help you. Accept the warm water, the blankets, the company or solitude you need. If you have children or dependents, accept help from family or friends without feeling like you have failed in your responsibilities.\n\nPractical acceptance in daily life\n\nAcceptance shows up in practical ways. It means always carrying water because hydration matters for you specifically. It means owning a good hot water bottle and keeping pain medication accessible. It means having honest conversations with your employer about your condition. It means telling a new romantic partner about sickle cell before the relationship gets serious, because the right person will understand. It means attending your clinic appointments faithfully, even when you feel fine.\n\nThe financial reality\n\nSickle cell disease is expensive to manage in Kenya. Between medications, regular blood tests costing KES 500 to KES 2,000 per visit, specialist appointments, and emergency hospital visits that can run into tens of thousands of shillings, the financial burden is real. Set aside an emergency fund, even KES 500 to KES 1,000 per month, specifically for healthcare costs. Explore what NHIF or the new SHA covers. Use platforms like Jireh to save specifically for healthcare expenses so that when a crisis comes, you are not choosing between treatment and rent.\n\nThe strength you have built\n\nThe strength you have developed through managing sickle cell disease is extraordinary. You have endured pain that would hospitalise most people. You have shown up for responsibilities while managing a condition that never takes a day off. This strength is real, even on the days when you feel anything but strong. Seek out communities of others living with sickle cell in Kenya, in Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu, and across the country. Support groups offer a space where you do not need to explain yourself.\n\nYou are more than your sickle cell status. On your good days, live boldly. On your hard days, rest without guilt. And on every day, know that you are managing something incredibly difficult with more grace than you probably give yourself credit for.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Living with sickle cell disease since birth means it has shaped your entire experience in ways that other people may never fully understand. You have known hospital corridors since before you could walk. You have missed school events, family celebrations, and ordinary childhood moments because of pain that no one around you could see. As an adult, sickle cell continues to influence your career, relationships, and daily decisions. Accepting this reality, truly accepting it, is not about giving up. It is about finding freedom within it."},{"id":"s2","title":"You are not defined by your haemoglobin","body":"Sickle cell disease is a condition in your blood, but it is not who you are. You are a person with dreams, talents, humour, intelligence, and relationships that have nothing to do with your red blood cells. Maybe you are a brilliant student, a creative artist, a loving parent, a dedicated worker, or a loyal friend. These identities are just as real and just as important as your medical identity. In Kenyan culture, where community and family roles are central, it can feel like your condition becomes your defining label, the one who is always sick, the one who cannot be relied upon, the one who needs special treatment. Rejecting that label is not denial. It is a choice to lead with your strengths rather than your struggles."},{"id":"s3","title":"The grief of living with a chronic condition","body":"Acceptance does not mean you cannot grieve for what sickle cell has taken from you. It is okay to feel angry about missed opportunities, frustrated by limitations, and sad about the uncertainty of your future. Many people with sickle cell describe a sense of loss for the life they might have had without this condition. Grieving these losses is healthy and necessary. What is not healthy is getting stuck in that grief permanently. Allow yourself to feel it, talk about it with someone you trust, and then gently redirect your energy toward the life you can build within your reality."},{"id":"s4","title":"Living fully on good days","body":"One of the most important shifts in thinking is learning to make the most of your good days rather than spending them worrying about when the next bad day will come. When you feel well, go out. See your friends. Take that walk. Enjoy a meal of ugali and fish stew at your favourite spot. Visit family. Work on your projects. Do the things that bring you joy and fulfilment. These good days are not borrowed time. They are your life. Living them fully is not reckless. It is wise."},{"id":"s5","title":"Being gentle on hard days","body":"Equally important is learning to be compassionate with yourself on difficult days. When a crisis strikes and you are in bed with a hot water bottle, unable to move without pain, do not add guilt to your suffering. You are not lazy. You are not a burden. You are managing one of the most painful conditions known to medicine. Rest is not defeat. It is treatment. Let people help you. Accept the warm water, the blankets, the company or solitude you need. If you have children or dependents, accept help from family or friends without feeling like you have failed in your responsibilities."},{"id":"s6","title":"Practical acceptance in daily life","body":"Acceptance shows up in practical ways. It means always carrying water because hydration matters for you specifically. It means owning a good hot water bottle and keeping pain medication accessible. It means having honest conversations with your employer about your condition. It means telling a new romantic partner about sickle cell before the relationship gets serious, because the right person will understand. It means attending your clinic appointments faithfully, even when you feel fine."},{"id":"s7","title":"The financial reality","body":"Sickle cell disease is expensive to manage in Kenya. Between medications, regular blood tests costing KES 500 to KES 2,000 per visit, specialist appointments, and emergency hospital visits that can run into tens of thousands of shillings, the financial burden is real. Set aside an emergency fund, even KES 500 to KES 1,000 per month, specifically for healthcare costs. Explore what NHIF or the new SHA covers. Use platforms like Jireh to save specifically for healthcare expenses so that when a crisis comes, you are not choosing between treatment and rent."},{"id":"s8","title":"The strength you have built","body":"The strength you have developed through managing sickle cell disease is extraordinary. You have endured pain that would hospitalise most people. You have shown up for responsibilities while managing a condition that never takes a day off. This strength is real, even on the days when you feel anything but strong. Seek out communities of others living with sickle cell in Kenya, in Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu, and across the country. Support groups offer a space where you do not need to explain yourself.\\n\\nYou are more than your sickle cell status. On your good days, live boldly. On your hard days, rest without guilt. And on every day, know that you are managing something incredibly difficult with more grace than you probably give yourself credit for."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'new-beginning'),
  (E'six-months-without-a-hospital-admission', E'Six months without a hospital admission', E'SICKLE_CELL', '{"SICKLE_CELL"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Going six months without a hospital admission for a sickle cell crisis is a major achievement that deserves to be recognised and celebrated. If you are reading this because you have reached this milestone, take a moment to feel genuinely proud of what you have accomplished.', E'Going six months without a hospital admission for a sickle cell crisis is a major achievement that deserves to be recognised and celebrated. If you are reading this because you have reached this milestone, take a moment to feel genuinely proud of what you have accomplished. This did not happen by accident.\n\nWhat this milestone actually means\n\nSix months without a hospital admission means six months of careful, consistent self-management. It means your hydration habits are working. You have been drinking enough water every day, even when you were busy, even when the weather was cool and you did not feel thirsty. It means your medication routine is solid. Whether you take hydroxyurea, folic acid, or other prescribed medications, you have been consistent. It means you have been avoiding or managing your triggers effectively, whether those are cold temperatures, dehydration, overexertion, stress, or illness. It means when you noticed early warning signs of a crisis, you acted quickly enough to prevent it from escalating to the point of needing hospital care.\n\nThe impact on your quality of life\n\nEvery day you spend at home or at work instead of in a hospital bed is a day you get back. Think about what you have been able to do in these six months that previous hospital admissions might have interrupted. Maybe you attended every day of school or work for months straight. Maybe you were present for a family celebration that you would have missed from a hospital bed. Maybe you travelled to visit relatives, went on a trip, or simply enjoyed an uninterrupted stretch of normal life. These experiences are the real measure of success, not just the absence of hospital visits, but the presence of living.\n\nThe financial savings are significant\n\nHospital admissions for sickle cell crises in Kenya are expensive. A typical admission of three to five days at a county hospital can cost between KES 15,000 and KES 40,000 even with NHIF or SHA coverage, and at private hospitals, costs can easily exceed KES 80,000 to KES 150,000. This includes bed charges, medications, blood transfusions if needed, laboratory tests, and doctor consultations. By avoiding even one hospital admission in six months, you have potentially saved tens of thousands of shillings that can go toward your education, your family, your business, or your savings for future healthcare needs. The preventive medications and regular check-ups that kept you out of hospital almost certainly cost far less than a single admission would have.\n\nWhat got you here\n\nReflect on the specific habits that contributed to this milestone. Your hydration routine, drinking 8 to 10 glasses of water daily and carrying a water bottle everywhere, kept your blood flowing smoothly. Your medication adherence, taking prescribed medications at the same time every day, maintained steady protection. Hydroxyurea, if prescribed, increases fetal haemoglobin production, which prevents sickling. Your trigger awareness, dressing warmly during cold Nairobi mornings, staying hydrated during hot Mombasa afternoons, managing stress, these conscious choices add up. And your early intervention skills, acting quickly at the first hints of a crisis with water, pain relief, rest, and heat, prevented hospital-level events.\n\nShare this with your haematologist\n\nAt your next clinic appointment, tell your doctor about this milestone. Your haematologist tracks hospitalisation frequency as a key indicator of disease management. A six-month crisis-free period may confirm that your current regimen is working well, or prompt your doctor to adjust your monitoring schedule.\n\nKeeping the momentum going\n\nDo not become complacent with hydration just because things are going well. Continue attending your regular haematology appointments, typically every three to six months. Keep your crisis kit stocked and ready at home. Continue managing stress through prayer, exercise, time with friends, or professional counselling. Stay up to date with vaccinations, especially pneumococcal and influenza vaccines, because infections can trigger severe crises.\n\nYour achievement is not luck. It is the result of daily discipline and good medical care. Celebrate it. And if a crisis does come eventually, because sickle cell is unpredictable, it does not erase these six months. Every good day counts, and you have had six months of them.\n\nYou are living proof that careful management of sickle cell disease works. Your consistency is paying off, and your body is benefiting from every smart decision you make.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Going six months without a hospital admission for a sickle cell crisis is a major achievement that deserves to be recognised and celebrated. If you are reading this because you have reached this milestone, take a moment to feel genuinely proud of what you have accomplished. This did not happen by accident."},{"id":"s2","title":"What this milestone actually means","body":"Six months without a hospital admission means six months of careful, consistent self-management. It means your hydration habits are working. You have been drinking enough water every day, even when you were busy, even when the weather was cool and you did not feel thirsty. It means your medication routine is solid. Whether you take hydroxyurea, folic acid, or other prescribed medications, you have been consistent. It means you have been avoiding or managing your triggers effectively, whether those are cold temperatures, dehydration, overexertion, stress, or illness. It means when you noticed early warning signs of a crisis, you acted quickly enough to prevent it from escalating to the point of needing hospital care."},{"id":"s3","title":"The impact on your quality of life","body":"Every day you spend at home or at work instead of in a hospital bed is a day you get back. Think about what you have been able to do in these six months that previous hospital admissions might have interrupted. Maybe you attended every day of school or work for months straight. Maybe you were present for a family celebration that you would have missed from a hospital bed. Maybe you travelled to visit relatives, went on a trip, or simply enjoyed an uninterrupted stretch of normal life. These experiences are the real measure of success, not just the absence of hospital visits, but the presence of living."},{"id":"s4","title":"The financial savings are significant","body":"Hospital admissions for sickle cell crises in Kenya are expensive. A typical admission of three to five days at a county hospital can cost between KES 15,000 and KES 40,000 even with NHIF or SHA coverage, and at private hospitals, costs can easily exceed KES 80,000 to KES 150,000. This includes bed charges, medications, blood transfusions if needed, laboratory tests, and doctor consultations. By avoiding even one hospital admission in six months, you have potentially saved tens of thousands of shillings that can go toward your education, your family, your business, or your savings for future healthcare needs. The preventive medications and regular check-ups that kept you out of hospital almost certainly cost far less than a single admission would have."},{"id":"s5","title":"What got you here","body":"Reflect on the specific habits that contributed to this milestone. Your hydration routine, drinking 8 to 10 glasses of water daily and carrying a water bottle everywhere, kept your blood flowing smoothly. Your medication adherence, taking prescribed medications at the same time every day, maintained steady protection. Hydroxyurea, if prescribed, increases fetal haemoglobin production, which prevents sickling. Your trigger awareness, dressing warmly during cold Nairobi mornings, staying hydrated during hot Mombasa afternoons, managing stress, these conscious choices add up. And your early intervention skills, acting quickly at the first hints of a crisis with water, pain relief, rest, and heat, prevented hospital-level events."},{"id":"s6","title":"Share this with your haematologist","body":"At your next clinic appointment, tell your doctor about this milestone. Your haematologist tracks hospitalisation frequency as a key indicator of disease management. A six-month crisis-free period may confirm that your current regimen is working well, or prompt your doctor to adjust your monitoring schedule."},{"id":"s7","title":"Keeping the momentum going","body":"Do not become complacent with hydration just because things are going well. Continue attending your regular haematology appointments, typically every three to six months. Keep your crisis kit stocked and ready at home. Continue managing stress through prayer, exercise, time with friends, or professional counselling. Stay up to date with vaccinations, especially pneumococcal and influenza vaccines, because infections can trigger severe crises.\\n\\nYour achievement is not luck. It is the result of daily discipline and good medical care. Celebrate it. And if a crisis does come eventually, because sickle cell is unpredictable, it does not erase these six months. Every good day counts, and you have had six months of them.\\n\\nYou are living proof that careful management of sickle cell disease works. Your consistency is paying off, and your body is benefiting from every smart decision you make."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'achievement'),
  (E'eating-well-to-support-your-immune-system-on-arvs', E'Eating well to support your immune system on ARVs', E'HIV_AIDS', '{"HIV_AIDS"}', E'DIETARY', E'When you are living with HIV and taking antiretroviral medication, good nutrition is not just generally healthy advice. It is a specific medical strategy that helps your body build the immune cells it needs to stay strong.', E'When you are living with HIV and taking antiretroviral medication, good nutrition is not just generally healthy advice. It is a specific medical strategy that helps your body build the immune cells it needs to stay strong. Your ARVs fight the virus, but your food provides the raw materials your body uses to rebuild its defences.\n\nWhy nutrition matters more when you are on ARVs\n\nHIV attacks CD4 cells, which are the immune cells that protect you from infections. Your ARVs stop the virus from multiplying, giving your immune system a chance to recover. But building new immune cells requires protein, vitamins, minerals, and energy. If your body does not have these building blocks, recovery is slower even when your ARVs are working perfectly. Studies conducted in Kenya and across East Africa consistently show that people living with HIV who eat well-balanced diets have better CD4 recovery, fewer opportunistic infections, and better overall treatment outcomes.\n\nProtein at every meal\n\nProtein is the most important nutrient for immune cell production. Aim to include a protein source at every meal. Eggs are one of the most affordable and complete protein sources available. A boiled or scrambled egg with your morning tea and bread provides an excellent start. Beans and lentils, including kidney beans, green grams (ndengu), and black-eyed peas, are affordable protein staples that you can find at any market in Kenya. A cup of cooked beans costs as little as KES 30 to KES 50 to prepare at home. Omena (silver cyprinid fish) is packed with protein and calcium. A serving of fried or stewed omena with ugali and sukuma wiki is an affordable, nutrient-dense meal. Chicken, beef, goat, or fish in small portions provide high-quality protein. You do not need large quantities. A piece the size of your palm at your main meal is sufficient. Groundnuts (peanuts) and other nuts make excellent protein-rich snacks between meals.\n\nPractical Kenyan meal plans that support immunity\n\nBreakfast options that support your treatment include uji (porridge) made with millet or sorghum flour mixed with milk and groundnut flour for extra protein. Alternatively, a boiled egg with whole wheat bread and a cup of chai made with milk. For lunch, a plate of githeri (maize and beans) with a side of sukuma wiki and a few slices of avocado provides protein, fibre, vitamins, and healthy fats. Or try a serving of brown rice with lentil stew and a cabbage and tomato salad. For dinner, ugali served with a small piece of grilled tilapia or chicken and a generous serving of managu or terere (amaranth greens) cooked with tomatoes and onions. Alternatively, chapati with a bean and vegetable stew.\n\nColourful vegetables for immune-boosting vitamins\n\nDifferent coloured vegetables provide different vitamins and minerals, all of which support immune function. Orange and yellow vegetables like carrots, butternut, and sweet potatoes are rich in vitamin A, which is essential for immune cell function. Dark leafy greens including spinach, kale, sukuma wiki, and managu provide iron, folate, and vitamin C. Red vegetables and fruits like tomatoes, watermelon, and red bell peppers contain lycopene and vitamin C. Try to eat vegetables from at least two different colour groups every day. When shopping at the market, variety is affordable. Buy what is in season for the best prices.\n\nTiming your food with your ARVs\n\nSome ARV medications work better when taken with food, while others should be taken on an empty stomach. Ask your doctor or pharmacist specifically about your regimen. Common guidelines include efavirenz (EFV), which is best taken at bedtime and can be taken with or without food but avoid high-fat meals close to the dose as they increase side effects. Dolutegravir (DTG), which is commonly used in Kenya''s first-line regimen and can be taken with or without food. If your ARVs cause nausea, eating a small amount before taking them can help. Try dry toast, a few crackers, or a banana 30 minutes before your dose.\n\nManaging ARV side effects through diet\n\nNausea is one of the most common early side effects when starting ARVs or switching regimens. Eat small, frequent meals rather than three large ones. Ginger tea, made by boiling fresh ginger in water with a squeeze of lemon, is an excellent local remedy. Avoid spicy or very greasy foods until the nausea passes. Diarrhoea can occur with some regimens. Stay hydrated with oral rehydration salts, available at any Kenyan pharmacy for KES 20 to KES 50. Eat bananas, rice, and toast, which help firm up stools. Avoid very oily or heavily spiced foods during episodes. Appetite loss is common, especially in the first few weeks of treatment. Even when you do not feel hungry, try to eat something small every three to four hours. A handful of groundnuts, a banana, a small serving of uji, or a boiled egg is better than skipping meals entirely.\n\nFood safety matters even more\n\nBecause HIV affects your immune system, you are more vulnerable to food-borne illnesses. Wash all fruits and vegetables thoroughly before eating. Cook meat and eggs fully, no rare or partially cooked options. Drink clean, safe water. If you are unsure about your water source, boil it for at least one minute before drinking or cooking with it. Avoid eating food that has been sitting out at room temperature for more than two hours. When buying food from street vendors or hotels, choose freshly prepared, hot food over items that have been sitting in display cases.\n\nAffordable nutrition on a budget\n\nEating well does not have to be expensive. Dried beans, lentils, and ndengu are among the cheapest protein sources available, costing KES 100 to KES 200 per kilogram at most markets. Sukuma wiki and other leafy greens are affordable year-round. Seasonal fruits provide vitamins at lower cost than supplements. Eggs are a budget-friendly protein source at roughly KES 15 to KES 20 each. When money is tight, prioritise protein and vegetables over processed snacks and sodas. A plate of githeri with sukuma wiki costs less than a soda and chips, and it does far more for your immune system.\n\nA well-nourished body responds better to treatment. Every healthy meal you eat is an investment in your immune recovery and your future.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you are living with HIV and taking antiretroviral medication, good nutrition is not just generally healthy advice. It is a specific medical strategy that helps your body build the immune cells it needs to stay strong. Your ARVs fight the virus, but your food provides the raw materials your body uses to rebuild its defences."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why nutrition matters more when you are on ARVs","body":"HIV attacks CD4 cells, which are the immune cells that protect you from infections. Your ARVs stop the virus from multiplying, giving your immune system a chance to recover. But building new immune cells requires protein, vitamins, minerals, and energy. If your body does not have these building blocks, recovery is slower even when your ARVs are working perfectly. Studies conducted in Kenya and across East Africa consistently show that people living with HIV who eat well-balanced diets have better CD4 recovery, fewer opportunistic infections, and better overall treatment outcomes."},{"id":"s3","title":"Protein at every meal","body":"Protein is the most important nutrient for immune cell production. Aim to include a protein source at every meal. Eggs are one of the most affordable and complete protein sources available. A boiled or scrambled egg with your morning tea and bread provides an excellent start. Beans and lentils, including kidney beans, green grams (ndengu), and black-eyed peas, are affordable protein staples that you can find at any market in Kenya. A cup of cooked beans costs as little as KES 30 to KES 50 to prepare at home. Omena (silver cyprinid fish) is packed with protein and calcium. A serving of fried or stewed omena with ugali and sukuma wiki is an affordable, nutrient-dense meal. Chicken, beef, goat, or fish in small portions provide high-quality protein. You do not need large quantities. A piece the size of your palm at your main meal is sufficient. Groundnuts (peanuts) and other nuts make excellent protein-rich snacks between meals."},{"id":"s4","title":"Practical Kenyan meal plans that support immunity","body":"Breakfast options that support your treatment include uji (porridge) made with millet or sorghum flour mixed with milk and groundnut flour for extra protein. Alternatively, a boiled egg with whole wheat bread and a cup of chai made with milk. For lunch, a plate of githeri (maize and beans) with a side of sukuma wiki and a few slices of avocado provides protein, fibre, vitamins, and healthy fats. Or try a serving of brown rice with lentil stew and a cabbage and tomato salad. For dinner, ugali served with a small piece of grilled tilapia or chicken and a generous serving of managu or terere (amaranth greens) cooked with tomatoes and onions. Alternatively, chapati with a bean and vegetable stew."},{"id":"s5","title":"Colourful vegetables for immune-boosting vitamins","body":"Different coloured vegetables provide different vitamins and minerals, all of which support immune function. Orange and yellow vegetables like carrots, butternut, and sweet potatoes are rich in vitamin A, which is essential for immune cell function. Dark leafy greens including spinach, kale, sukuma wiki, and managu provide iron, folate, and vitamin C. Red vegetables and fruits like tomatoes, watermelon, and red bell peppers