 Cook meat and eggs fully, no rare or partially cooked options. Drink clean, safe water. If you are unsure about your water source, boil it for at least one minute before drinking or cooking with it. Avoid eating food that has been sitting out at room temperature for more than two hours. When buying food from street vendors or hotels, choose freshly prepared, hot food over items that have been sitting in display cases."},{"id":"s9","title":"Affordable nutrition on a budget","body":"Eating well does not have to be expensive. Dried beans, lentils, and ndengu are among the cheapest protein sources available, costing KES 100 to KES 200 per kilogram at most markets. Sukuma wiki and other leafy greens are affordable year-round. Seasonal fruits provide vitamins at lower cost than supplements. Eggs are a budget-friendly protein source at roughly KES 15 to KES 20 each. When money is tight, prioritise protein and vegetables over processed snacks and sodas. A plate of githeri with sukuma wiki costs less than a soda and chips, and it does far more for your immune system.\\n\\nA well-nourished body responds better to treatment. Every healthy meal you eat is an investment in your immune recovery and your future."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'healthy-cooking'),
  (E'how-exercise-boosts-your-cd4-count', E'How exercise boosts your CD4 count', E'HIV_AIDS', '{"HIV_AIDS"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Regular moderate exercise is one of the most powerful things you can do to support your immune system alongside your ARV medication.', E'Regular moderate exercise is one of the most powerful things you can do to support your immune system alongside your ARV medication. Research consistently shows that physical activity can improve CD4 cell counts, reduce viral load, combat fatigue and depression, and enhance overall quality of life for people living with HIV.\n\nHow exercise supports your immune system\n\nWhen you exercise at a moderate intensity, your body increases circulation, which helps immune cells move more efficiently through your bloodstream and detect threats faster. Regular exercise has been shown to increase the number and activity of CD4 cells, which are the very immune cells that HIV targets. Studies in East African populations have found that people living with HIV who exercise regularly have better CD4 recovery rates after starting ARVs compared to those who are sedentary. Exercise also reduces chronic inflammation, which is elevated in people with HIV even when viral load is controlled, and chronic inflammation contributes to the increased risk of heart disease, diabetes, and other conditions that people living with HIV face.\n\nWhat counts as moderate exercise\n\nModerate exercise means activity that raises your heart rate and makes you breathe faster, but still allows you to carry on a conversation. You should feel like you are working, but not struggling. On a scale of 1 to 10, where 1 is sitting on your sofa and 10 is sprinting as fast as you can, moderate exercise is about a 5 or 6. Aim for at least 30 minutes of this level of activity, five days a week, which is 150 minutes total per week.\n\nExercise options that do not require a gym\n\nYou absolutely do not need a gym membership or special equipment to get effective exercise. Walking briskly is the most accessible form of exercise. Walk to the market instead of taking a matatu for short distances. Walk around your estate or neighbourhood in the morning or evening when temperatures are comfortable. In Nairobi, Karura Forest charges a small entry fee of KES 100 for residents and offers beautiful, well-maintained trails. Uhuru Park, City Park, and the Arboretum are also excellent free or affordable walking options. Dancing in your living room to your favourite music is genuinely effective exercise. Put on gospel, gengetone, or whatever gets you moving, and dance for 30 minutes. You will raise your heart rate, improve your mood, and have fun doing it. Household chores done vigorously count as exercise. Mopping floors with energy, hand-washing clothes, sweeping the compound, digging in a garden, and carrying water all burn calories and strengthen muscles. Walking up and down stairs, if you live in an apartment building, is excellent cardiovascular exercise. Start with a few flights and build up gradually.\n\nA sample weekly exercise routine\n\nMonday: 30-minute brisk walk around your neighbourhood. Start slowly for the first 5 minutes to warm up, walk briskly for 20 minutes, then slow down for 5 minutes to cool down. Tuesday: Home strength exercises. Do 10 wall push-ups, rest 30 seconds, repeat 3 times. Do 10 squats holding the back of a chair for balance, rest 30 seconds, repeat 3 times. Do 10 seated leg raises on each leg. Finish with 5 minutes of gentle stretching. Wednesday: 30-minute brisk walk, same pattern as Monday. Try a different route to keep it interesting. Thursday: Dance workout. Put on music you enjoy and dance for 30 minutes. Mix up your movements, use your arms, move your hips, and step side to side. Friday: 30-minute brisk walk or gentle cycling if you have access to a bicycle. Saturday: Active household tasks for at least 30 minutes, cleaning, gardening, washing clothes by hand, or walking to the market and back. Sunday: Rest or gentle stretching and yoga. Your body needs recovery time.\n\nExercise and ARV side effects\n\nSome ARV side effects can be directly helped by exercise. Fatigue is one of the most common complaints among people on ARVs, and while it seems counterintuitive, regular exercise actually reduces fatigue over time by improving your body''s energy production systems. Depression and anxiety, which affect many people living with HIV, respond well to exercise. Physical activity releases endorphins, which are natural mood elevators. Even a 20-minute walk can noticeably improve your mood. Lipodystrophy, the changes in body fat distribution that some older ARV regimens cause, can be managed with a combination of cardiovascular exercise and strength training. Sleep problems improve with regular exercise, though avoid vigorous activity within two hours of bedtime.\n\nImportant precautions\n\nDrink plenty of water before, during, and after exercise. Dehydration weakens your immune system. If you are just starting to exercise after a period of inactivity, begin slowly. Ten minutes of walking per day for the first week is perfectly fine. Build up gradually. Do not exercise if you have a fever, are feeling acutely unwell, or have an active opportunistic infection. Wait until you feel better and your doctor clears you. If you experience chest pain, severe shortness of breath, dizziness, or fainting during exercise, stop immediately and seek medical attention. Take your ARVs at your usual time regardless of your exercise schedule. If your ARVs cause nausea, wait at least an hour after taking them before exercising.\n\nMaking it sustainable\n\nThe best exercise is the one you will actually do consistently. If you hate running, do not force yourself to run. Walk, dance, swim, cycle, or do yoga instead. Find a walking partner or exercise buddy because accountability helps. Join a community exercise group if one exists in your area. Many churches and community centres organise morning exercise sessions. Track your progress in a simple notebook or on your phone. Write down what you did and how you felt afterward. Over time, you will see improvements in your stamina, mood, and energy levels.\n\nConsistency matters more than intensity. Thirty minutes of walking five days a week will do more for your CD4 count and overall health than one intense gym session followed by two weeks of inactivity. Start where you are, use what you have, and keep moving.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Regular moderate exercise is one of the most powerful things you can do to support your immune system alongside your ARV medication. Research consistently shows that physical activity can improve CD4 cell counts, reduce viral load, combat fatigue and depression, and enhance overall quality of life for people living with HIV."},{"id":"s2","title":"How exercise supports your immune system","body":"When you exercise at a moderate intensity, your body increases circulation, which helps immune cells move more efficiently through your bloodstream and detect threats faster. Regular exercise has been shown to increase the number and activity of CD4 cells, which are the very immune cells that HIV targets. Studies in East African populations have found that people living with HIV who exercise regularly have better CD4 recovery rates after starting ARVs compared to those who are sedentary. Exercise also reduces chronic inflammation, which is elevated in people with HIV even when viral load is controlled, and chronic inflammation contributes to the increased risk of heart disease, diabetes, and other conditions that people living with HIV face."},{"id":"s3","title":"What counts as moderate exercise","body":"Moderate exercise means activity that raises your heart rate and makes you breathe faster, but still allows you to carry on a conversation. You should feel like you are working, but not struggling. On a scale of 1 to 10, where 1 is sitting on your sofa and 10 is spr