 workout. Put on music you enjoy and dance for 30 minutes. Mix up your movements, use your arms, move your hips, and step side to side. Friday: 30-minute brisk walk or gentle cycling if you have access to a bicycle. Saturday: Active household tasks for at least 30 minutes, cleaning, gardening, washing clothes by hand, or walking to the market and back. Sunday: Rest or gentle stretching and yoga. Your body needs recovery time."},{"id":"s6","title":"Exercise and ARV side effects","body":"Some ARV side effects can be directly helped by exercise. Fatigue is one of the most common complaints among people on ARVs, and while it seems counterintuitive, regular exercise actually reduces fatigue over time by improving your body''s energy production systems. Depression and anxiety, which affect many people living with HIV, respond well to exercise. Physical activity releases endorphins, which are natural mood elevators. Even a 20-minute walk can noticeably improve your mood. Lipodystrophy, the changes in body fat distribution that some older ARV regimens cause, can be managed with a combination of cardiovascular exercise and strength training. Sleep problems improve with regular exercise, though avoid vigorous activity within two hours of bedtime."},{"id":"s7","title":"Important precautions","body":"Drink plenty of water before, during, and after exercise. Dehydration weakens your immune system. If you are just starting to exercise after a period of inactivity, begin slowly. Ten minutes of walking per day for the first week is perfectly fine. Build up gradually. Do not exercise if you have a fever, are feeling acutely unwell, or have an active opportunistic infection. Wait until you feel better and your doctor clears you. If you experience chest pain, severe shortness of breath, dizziness, or fainting during exercise, stop immediately and seek medical attention. Take your ARVs at your usual time regardless of your exercise schedule. If your ARVs cause nausea, wait at least an hour after taking them before exercising."},{"id":"s8","title":"Making it sustainable","body":"The best exercise is the one you will actually do consistently. If you hate running, do not force yourself to run. Walk, dance, swim, cycle, or do yoga instead. Find a walking partner or exercise buddy because accountability helps. Join a community exercise group if one exists in your area. Many churches and community centres organise morning exercise sessions. Track your progress in a simple notebook or on your phone. Write down what you did and how you felt afterward. Over time, you will see improvements in your stamina, mood, and energy levels.\\n\\nConsistency matters more than intensity. Thirty minutes of walking five days a week will do more for your CD4 count and overall health than one intense gym session followed by two weeks of inactivity. Start where you are, use what you have, and keep moving."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'morning-walk'),
  (E'myth-undetectable-viral-load-means-you-are-cured', E'Myth: Undetectable viral load means you are cured', E'HIV_AIDS', '{"HIV_AIDS"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'An undetectable viral load is one of the greatest achievements in HIV treatment. It means your antiretroviral medication is working so effectively that standard laboratory tests cannot find the virus in your blood.', E'An undetectable viral load is one of the greatest achievements in HIV treatment. It means your antiretroviral medication is working so effectively that standard laboratory tests cannot find the virus in your blood. This is wonderful, life-changing news. But it is critically important to understand that undetectable does not mean cured.\n\nWhat undetectable actually means\n\nWhen your viral load test comes back as undetectable, it means the amount of HIV in your blood is below the level that the test can measure, typically below 50 copies per millilitre. This is the goal of ARV treatment and a sign that your medication is working exactly as it should. Your immune system can now recover and strengthen because the virus is no longer actively destroying CD4 cells at a rapid rate. In Kenya, viral load testing is available at most comprehensive care clinics and is typically done every six to twelve months as part of routine HIV monitoring. The test is covered under NHIF and the new SHA for enrolled members.\n\nWhy you are not cured\n\nHIV is an exceptionally clever virus. Even when ARVs have suppressed it to undetectable levels in your blood, the virus does not disappear completely. It hides in what scientists call viral reservoirs. These are cells, primarily in your lymph nodes, gut, brain, and bone marrow, where HIV has inserted its genetic material into the cell''s own DNA. The virus lies dormant in these reservoir cells, invisible to both your immune system and your ARVs. Your medication cannot reach these hidden copies of the virus. This is why, despite decades of research and billions of dollars in funding, a true cure for HIV remains elusive. The reservoir is the barrier.\n\nWhat happens if you stop your ARVs\n\nIf you stop taking your ARV medication, the virus will rebound from these reservoirs. The hidden copies of HIV will reactivate, begin multiplying, and within days to weeks, your viral load will become detectable again. Within a few weeks to months, it can rise to the levels it was at before you started treatment. This rebound can happen surprisingly quickly, sometimes within two to four weeks of stopping medication. Importantly, rebound is not just theoretical. It has been observed consistently in clinical studies where treatment was interrupted, including studies conducted in East Africa. This is why lifelong adherence to ARVs is essential, not optional.\n\nThe U=U revolution\n\nNow for the truly empowering part. Undetectable does mean untransmittable, commonly expressed as U=U. This is not wishful thinking. It is backed by some of the largest and most rigorous studies ever conducted in HIV research, including the PARTNER and PARTNER2 studies, which followed thousands of couples where one partner had HIV and the other did not. Among couples where the HIV-positive partner maintained an undetectable viral load, there were zero transmissions through sex, not even one, across tens of thousands of sexual encounters without condoms. This means that when your viral load is undetectable, you cannot pass HIV to your sexual partner through sex. This finding has been endorsed by the World Health Organization, UNAIDS, and Kenya''s own Ministry of Health.\n\nWhat U=U means for your life\n\nU=U is profoundly liberating. It means you can be in a romantic relationship without the constant fear of transmitting HIV to your partner. It means you can have children with significantly reduced transmission risk, and with proper medical care including prevention of mother-to-child transmission protocols available throughout Kenya, the risk to a baby can be reduced to below 1 percent. It means the stigma that paints people living with HIV as dangerous to others is scientifically unfounded when treatment is effective.\n\nThe conditions for U=U to hold\n\nU=U only works when your viral load is consistently undetectable. This requires taking your ARVs every single day, at the right time, as prescribed. Missing doses can allow the virus to rebound temporarily, even if you do not feel any different. Regular viral load testing, every six to twelve months or as your doctor recommends, confirms that you remain undetectable. If a viral load test shows a detectable result, it does not mean U=U is broken. It means something needs attention, perhaps a medication adjustment, an adherence review, or testing for drug resistance. Talk to your doctor immediately.\n\nWhy some people stop taking their ARVs\n\nUnderstanding the reasons people stop ARVs helps prevent it. Some people feel so healthy on treatment that they believe they no longer need medication. Others face stigma and want to hide their HIV status by not having pills visible. Some experience side effects that make daily medication burdensome. Others hear from friends, family, or traditional healers that they have been cured. In Kenya, some patients travel to rural areas for extended periods and run out of medication without access to a refill facility. Whatever the reason, stopping ARVs is always risky and can lead to drug resistance, making your current regimen ineffective when you restart.\n\nPractical tips for maintaining your undetectable status\n\nTake your ARVs at the same time every day. Set an alarm on your phone if needed. Keep a small emergency supply of three to five days of medication in your bag in case you are away from home unexpectedly. When travelling within Kenya, locate the nearest comprehensive care clinic at your destination in case you need a refill. These services are available in every county. If you experience side effects, talk to your doctor about switching to a different regimen rather than stopping treatment entirely. Modern ARV regimens, including those used in Kenya''s national program, have far fewer side effects than older ones. Never share your ARVs with others or take someone else''s medication. Each regimen is prescribed based on individual factors.\n\nYour undetectable status is a testament to your discipline and your body''s response to treatment. It means your ARVs are working beautifully. Protect that achievement by taking your medication consistently, every single day, for the rest of your life. Not because you are sick, but because your medication is what is keeping you well.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"An undetectable viral load is one of the greatest achievements in HIV treatment. It means your antiretroviral medication is working so effectively that standard laboratory tests cannot find the virus in your blood. This is wonderful, life-changing news. But it is critically important to understand that undetectable does not mean cured."},{"id":"s2","title":"What undetectable actually means","body":"When your viral load test comes back as undetectable, it means the amount of HIV in your blood is below the level that the test can measure, typically below 50 copies per millilitre. This is the goal of ARV treatment and a sign that your medication is working exactly as it should. Your immune system can now recover and strengthen because the virus is no longer actively destroying CD4 cells at a rapid rate. In Kenya, viral load testing is available at most comprehensive care clinics and is typically done every six to twelve months as part of routine HIV monitoring. The test is covered under NHIF and the new SHA for enrolled members."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why you are not cured","body":"HIV is an exceptionally clever virus. Even when ARVs have suppressed it to undetectable levels in your blood, the virus does not disappear completely. It hides in what scientists call viral reservoirs. These are cells, primarily in your lymph nodes, gut, brain, and bone marrow, where HIV has inserted its genetic material into the cell''s own DNA. The virus lies dormant in these reservoir cells, invisible to both your immune system and your ARVs. Your medication cannot reach these hidden copies of the virus. This is why, despite decades of research and billions of dollars in funding, a true cure for HIV remains elusive. The reservoir is the barrier."},{"id":"s4","title":"What happens if you stop your ARVs","body":"If you stop taking your ARV medication, the virus will rebound from these reservoirs. The hidden copies of HIV will reactivate, begin multiplying, and within days to weeks, your viral load will become detectable again. Within a few weeks to months, it can rise to the levels it was at before you started treatment. This rebound can happen surprisingly quickly, sometimes within two to four weeks of stopping medication. Importantly, rebound is not just theoretical. It has been observed consistently in clinical studies where treatment was interrupted, including studies conducted in East Africa. This is why lifelong adherence to ARVs is essential, not optional."},{"id":"s5","title":"The U=U revolution","body":"Now for the truly empowering part. Undetectable does mean untransmittable, commonly expressed as U=U. This is not wishful thinking. It is backed by some of the largest and most rigorous studies ever conducted in HIV research, including the PARTNER and PARTNER2 studies, which followed thousands of couples where one partner had HIV and the other did not. Among couples where the HIV-positive partner maintained an undetectable viral load, there were zero transmissions through sex, not even one, across tens of thousands of sexual encounters without condoms. This means that when your viral load is undetectable, you cannot pass HIV to your sexual partner through sex. This finding has been endorsed by the World Health Organization, UNAIDS, and Kenya''s own Ministry of Health."},{"id":"s6","title":"What U=U means for your life","body":"U=U is profoundly liberating. It means you can be in a romantic relationship without the constant fear of transmitting HIV to your partner. It means you can have children with significantly reduced transmission risk, and with proper medical care including prevention of mother-to-child transmission protocols available throughout Kenya, the risk to a baby can be reduced to below 1 percent. It means the stigma that paints people living with HIV as dangerous to others is scientifically unfounded when treatment is effective."},{"id":"s7","title":"The conditions for U=U to hold","body":"U=U only works when your viral load is consistently undetectable. This requires taking your ARVs every single day, at the right time, as prescribed. Missing doses can allow the virus to rebound temporarily, even if you do not feel any different. Regular viral load testing, every six to twelve months or as your doctor recommends, confirms that you remain undetectable. If a viral load test shows a detectable result, it does not mean U=U is broken. It means something needs attention, perhaps a medication adjustment, an adherence review, or testing for drug resistance. Talk to your doctor immediately."},{"id":"s8","title":"Why some people stop taking their ARVs","body":"Understanding the reasons people stop ARVs helps prevent it. Some people feel so healthy on treatment that they believe they no longer need medication. Others face stigma and want to hide their HIV status by not having pills visible. Some experience side effects that make daily medication burdensome. Others hear from friends, family, or traditional healers that they have been cured. In Kenya, some patients travel to rural areas for extended periods and run out of medication without access to a refill facility. Whatever the reason, stopping ARVs is always risky and can lead to drug resistance, making your current regimen ineffective when you restart."},{"id":"s9","title":"Practical tips for maintaining your undetectable status","body":"Take your ARVs at the same time every day. Set an alarm on your phone if needed. Keep a small emergency supply of three to five days of medication in your bag in case you are away from home unexpectedly. When travelling within Kenya, locate the nearest comprehensive care clinic at your destination in case you need a refill. These services are available in every county. If you experience side effects, talk to your doctor about switching to a different regimen rather than stopping treatment entirely. Modern ARV regimens, including those used in Kenya''s national program, have far fewer side effects than older ones. Never share your ARVs with others or take someone else''s medication. Each regimen is prescribed based on individual factors.\\n\\nYour undetectable status is a testament to your discipline and your body''s response to treatment. It means your ARVs are working beautifully. Protect that achievement by taking your medication consistently, every single day, for the rest of your life. Not because you are sick, but because your medication is what is keeping you well."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'fact-check'),
  (E'the-weight-of-secrecy-and-the-relief-of-selective-disclosure', E'The weight of secrecy and the relief of selective disclosure', E'HIV_AIDS', '{"HIV_AIDS"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'Living with HIV in Kenya often means carrying a secret that weighs on you every single day. The fear of stigma, rejection, gossip, and discrimination can be overwhelming, and for many people, the emotional burden of secrecy is heavier than the virus itself.', E'Living with HIV in Kenya often means carrying a secret that weighs on you every single day. The fear of stigma, rejection, gossip, and discrimination can be overwhelming, and for many people, the emotional burden of secrecy is heavier than the virus itself. If you are carrying this weight, you are not alone, and there are ways to lighten it without putting yourself at risk.\n\nWhy secrecy is so heavy\n\nKeeping your HIV status secret requires constant vigilance. You have to hide your medication, taking ARVs in the bathroom or waiting until everyone is asleep. You have to make excuses for clinic visits, pretending you are going somewhere else or inventing other appointments. You have to be careful about what you say, avoiding any comment that might make someone suspicious. You have to manage your fear every time you are sick, worried that a cough or weight loss will make people guess. This constant alertness is exhausting. It creates a low-level anxiety that never fully goes away. Over time, it can contribute to depression, insomnia, and a feeling of profound loneliness even when surrounded by people who love you.\n\nThe stigma reality in Kenya\n\nLet us be honest about why people keep their status secret. HIV stigma in Kenya, while decreasing, is still real. Some families reject members who disclose. Some communities gossip cruelly. Some employers discriminate despite legal protections. Some romantic partners react with anger or fear rather than compassion. These are not irrational fears. They are based on experiences that many Kenyans living with HIV have had or witnessed. Any conversation about disclosure must acknowledge this reality rather than pretending that Kenya is a stigma-free environment. It is not, though it is improving.\n\nYou do not have to tell everyone\n\nSelective disclosure is the key concept here. You do not owe your HIV status to anyone. Not your extended family, not your neighbours, not your coworkers, not casual acquaintances. Your medical information is private, and choosing who to share it with is your right. The goal is not to become public about your status. The goal is to find even one person you can be honest with, because carrying the secret entirely alone is a weight no one should bear indefinitely.\n\nChoosing your first person\n\nThink carefully about who you might tell. The ideal first person has several qualities. They are someone you trust deeply and who has shown that they can keep confidences. They are someone who is unlikely to react with fear, anger, or judgment. They are someone who has demonstrated compassion and understanding in other situations. They are someone whose relationship with you would survive difficult conversations.\n\nThis might be a close sibling, a best friend, a counsellor at your clinic, or a member of a support group. It does not have to be your spouse or partner first, especially if you are still processing your own emotions. Some people find it easiest to start with a professional, either a counsellor at their comprehensive care clinic, which is free in most Kenyan CCC facilities, or a private counsellor, typically charging between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 per session. The advantage of starting with a professional is that confidentiality is guaranteed and the counsellor is trained to help you process your emotions.\n\nThe relief of being known\n\nAlmost universally, people who have disclosed to a trusted person describe a profound sense of relief. The constant performance of being okay drops away with that one person. You can take your medication openly in their presence. You can talk about your clinic visits, your CD4 count, your fears about the future without masking. You can cry if you need to without having to explain why. This emotional release is not trivial. It directly improves your mental health, which in turn supports your physical health and treatment adherence.\n\nSupport groups as safe spaces\n\nHIV support groups offer a unique form of relief because everyone in the room shares your experience. You do not have to explain what it feels like to hear the diagnosis, the fear of disclosure, the anxiety of waiting for test results, or the frustration of daily medication. In Kenya, support groups operate at most comprehensive care clinics attached to county and sub-county hospitals. NASCOP, the National AIDS and STI Control Programme, supports peer education networks across the country. Many faith-based organisations also run confidential support groups. If attending a physical group feels too risky because you might be seen entering, online support communities exist on platforms like WhatsApp and Facebook where membership is private and anonymous.\n\nNavigating disclosure with a romantic partner\n\nDisclosing to a romantic partner is one of the most feared conversations for people living with HIV. The timing, setting, and approach all matter. Choose a private, calm moment when you are both relaxed and not in the middle of a conflict. Be direct and honest. Share basic facts: you are living with HIV, you are on treatment, your viral load is undetectable, and with adherence, you pose no transmission risk through sex (U=U). Bring written materials from your clinic if it helps. Allow your partner time to process. Their initial reaction may not reflect their ultimate response. Some partners need days or weeks to process the information. If a partner rejects you solely because of your HIV status, it is painful, but it also reveals something important about their capacity for partnership and compassion.\n\nDisclosure and children\n\nIf you have children, you may face the question of when and how to tell them. Very young children do not need detailed information, but as they grow older, age-appropriate conversations become important, especially if they notice your medication routine. Paediatric counsellors at HIV clinics can guide you on how to have these conversations at different developmental stages.\n\nProtecting yourself legally\n\nKenya''s Constitution protects the right to privacy, and the HIV and AIDS Prevention and Control Act provides specific protections against discrimination based on HIV status. If you face workplace discrimination, denial of services, or other rights violations due to your HIV status, organisations like KELIN (Kenya Legal and Ethical Issues Network on HIV and AIDS) provide free legal support. Knowing your rights can give you confidence to make disclosure decisions from a position of strength rather than fear.\n\nThe weight of secrecy is real, but you do not have to carry it alone forever. Find your one person. Join your one group. Let yourself be known by someone. The relief that comes with being accepted as you truly are can transform your relationship with your diagnosis and with yourself.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Living with HIV in Kenya often means carrying a secret that weighs on you every single day. The fear of stigma, rejection, gossip, and discrimination can be overwhelming, and for many people, the emotional burden of secrecy is heavier than the virus itself. If you are carrying this weight, you are not alone, and there are ways to lighten it without putting yourself at risk."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why secrecy is so heavy","body":"Keeping your HIV status secret requires constant vigilance. You have to hide your medication, taking ARVs in the bathroom or waiting until everyone is asleep. You have to make excuses for clinic visits, pretending you are going somewhere else or inventing other appointments. You have to be careful about what you say, avoiding any comment that might make someone suspicious. You have to manage your fear every time you are sick, worried that a cough or weight loss will make people guess. This constant alertness is exhausting. It creates a low-level anxiety that never fully goes away. Over time, it can contribute to depression, insomnia, and a feeling of profound loneliness even when surrounded by people who love you."},{"id":"s3","title":"The stigma reality in Kenya","body":"Let us be honest about why people keep their status secret. HIV stigma in Kenya, while decreasing, is still real. Some families reject members who disclose. Some communities gossip cruelly. Some employers discriminate despite legal protections. Some romantic partners react with anger or fear rather than compassion. These are not irrational fears. They are based on experiences that many Kenyans living with HIV have had or witnessed. Any conversation about disclosure must acknowledge this reality rather than pretending that Kenya is a stigma-free environment. It is not, though it is improving."},{"id":"s4","title":"You do not have to tell everyone","body":"Selective disclosure is the key concept here. You do not owe your HIV status to anyone. Not your extended family, not your neighbours, not your coworkers, not casual acquaintances. Your medical information is private, and choosing who to share it with is your right. The goal is not to become public about your status. The goal is to find even one person you can be honest with, because carrying the secret entirely alone is a weight no one should bear indefinitely."},{"id":"s5","title":"Choosing your first person","body":"Think carefully about who you might tell. The ideal first person has several qualities. They are someone you trust deeply and who has shown that they can keep confidences. They are someone who is unlikely to react with fear, anger, or judgment. They are someone who has demonstrated compassion and understanding in other situations. They are someone whose relationship with you would survive difficult conversations.\\n\\nThis might be a close sibling, a best friend, a counsellor at your clinic, or a member of a support group. It does not have to be your spouse or partner first, especially if you are still processing your own emotions. Some people find it easiest to start with a professional, either a counsellor at their comprehensive care clinic, which is free in most Kenyan CCC facilities, or a private counsellor, typically charging between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 per session. The advantage of starting with a professional is that confidentiality is guaranteed and the counsellor is trained to help you process your emotions."},{"id":"s6","title":"The relief of being known","body":"Almost universally, people who have disclosed to a trusted person describe a profound sense of relief. The constant performance of being okay drops away with that one person. You can take your medication openly in their presence. You can talk about your clinic visits, your CD4 count, your fears about the future without masking. You can cry if you need to without having to explain why. This emotional release is not trivial. It directly improves your mental health, which in turn supports your physical health and treatment adherence."},{"id":"s7","title":"Support groups as safe spaces","body":"HIV support groups offer a unique form of relief because everyone in the room shares your experience. You do not have to explain what it feels like to hear the diagnosis, the fear of disclosure, the anxiety of waiting for test results, or the frustration of daily medication. In Kenya, support groups operate at most comprehensive care clinics attached to county and sub-county hospitals. NASCOP, the National AIDS and STI Control Programme, supports peer education networks across the country. Many faith-based organisations also run confidential support groups. If attending a physical group feels too risky because you might be seen entering, online support communities exist on platforms like WhatsApp and Facebook where membership is private and anonymous."},{"id":"s8","title":"Navigating disclosure with a romantic partner","body":"Disclosing to a romantic partner is one of the most feared conversations for people living with HIV. The timing, setting, and approach all matter. Choose a private, calm moment when you are both relaxed and not in the middle of a conflict. Be direct and honest. Share basic facts: you are living with HIV, you are on treatment, your viral load is undetectable, and with adherence, you pose no transmission risk through sex (U=U). Bring written materials from your clinic if it helps. Allow your partner time to process. Their initial reaction may not reflect their ultimate response. Some partners need days or weeks to process the information. If a partner rejects you solely because of your HIV status, it is painful, but it also reveals something important about their capacity for partnership and compassion."},{"id":"s9","title":"Disclosure and children","body":"If you have children, you may face the question of when and how to tell them. Very young children do not need detailed information, but as they grow older, age-appropriate conversations become important, especially if they notice your medication routine. Paediatric counsellors at HIV clinics can guide you on how to have these conversations at different developmental stages."},{"id":"s10","title":"Protecting yourself legally","body":"Kenya''s Constitution protects the right to privacy, and the HIV and AIDS Prevention and Control Act provides specific protections against discrimination based on HIV status. If you face workplace discrimination, denial of services, or other rights violations due to your HIV status, organisations like KELIN (Kenya Legal and Ethical Issues Network on HIV and AIDS) provide free legal support. Knowing your rights can give you confidence to make disclosure decisions from a position of strength rather than fear.\\n\\nThe weight of secrecy is real, but you do not have to carry it alone forever. Find your one person. Join your one group. Let yourself be known by someone. The relief that comes with being accepted as you truly are can transform your relationship with your diagnosis and with yourself."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'peaceful-moment'),
  (E'understanding-your-viral-load-and-cd4-results', E'Understanding your viral load and CD4 results', E'HIV_AIDS', '{"HIV_AIDS"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'If you are living with HIV, two numbers tell you more about your health than almost anything else: your viral load and your CD4 count.', E'If you are living with HIV, two numbers tell you more about your health than almost anything else: your viral load and your CD4 count. Understanding what these numbers mean, how to track them, and what to do when they change puts you in control of your health rather than leaving you guessing between clinic visits.\n\nWhat viral load measures\n\nYour viral load tells you how much HIV is present in your blood. It is measured in copies of the virus per millilitre of blood. When you first start ARV treatment, your viral load may be high, sometimes in the hundreds of thousands. As your medication takes effect, this number should drop steadily over the first three to six months. The ultimate goal is an undetectable viral load, which means the amount of virus is so low that the test cannot measure it. In most Kenyan laboratories, undetectable means below 50 copies per millilitre, though some labs use a threshold of below 200 copies.\n\nWhat your viral load numbers mean\n\nUndetectable or below 50 copies per millilitre means your ARVs are working excellently. Your virus is suppressed and you cannot transmit HIV through sex (U=U). Below 200 copies per millilitre is still considered viral suppression and is a good result. It means your treatment is effective, though your doctor may want to continue monitoring to ensure the trend goes toward full undetectability. Between 200 and 1,000 copies per millilitre is a low-level viraemia. This is concerning and needs attention. Your doctor will likely repeat the test and review your adherence. It may indicate that you have been missing doses or that the virus is beginning to develop resistance. Above 1,000 copies per millilitre indicates treatment failure. This means your current regimen is not adequately suppressing the virus. Your doctor will assess whether this is due to adherence issues or drug resistance, and may need to switch your medication to a different regimen.\n\nWhat CD4 count measures\n\nYour CD4 count measures how many CD4 immune cells are in a cubic millimetre of your blood. CD4 cells are the white blood cells that coordinate your immune response. HIV specifically targets and destroys these cells. A healthy person without HIV typically has a CD4 count between 500 and 1,500 cells per cubic millimetre.\n\nWhat your CD4 numbers mean\n\nAbove 500 cells per cubic millimetre is a strong immune system. At this level, your body can fight off most infections effectively. Between 350 and 500 is adequate but not yet optimal. Your immune system is functional but still recovering. Continue your ARVs and good nutrition to help it strengthen. Between 200 and 350 means your immune system is weakened. You are more vulnerable to certain infections and your doctor may prescribe additional preventive medications such as co-trimoxazole, commonly known as Septrin, which is available at Kenyan pharmacies for about KES 100 to KES 300 per month. Below 200 means your immune system is severely compromised. This is the level at which opportunistic infections become a serious risk, and it requires urgent and careful medical management.\n\nHow often to test\n\nIn Kenya''s national HIV treatment guidelines, viral load testing is recommended at six months after starting ARVs, then at twelve months, and annually thereafter if your viral load is undetectable. If your viral load is detectable, your doctor will test more frequently, usually every three months, to assess whether adherence support or a regimen change is needed. CD4 testing is done less frequently once you are stable on treatment, usually annually. However, if you were diagnosed with a low CD4 count or are experiencing new health problems, your doctor may order it more often. These tests are available at comprehensive care clinics across Kenya and are free of charge for patients enrolled in the national HIV program. Private labs charge between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 for a viral load test and KES 800 to KES 1,500 for a CD4 count.\n\nTracking your numbers over time\n\nDo not just hear your results and forget them. Write them down every single time. Keep a small notebook or use the notes app on your phone to record the date, your viral load result, your CD4 count, and any notes your doctor made about the results. Over time, this creates a picture of your treatment journey. You want to see your viral load going down and staying down, and your CD4 count going up and staying up. If either number moves in the wrong direction, you will spot it immediately rather than relying on memory.\n\nWhat to do if your viral load starts rising\n\nA rising viral load is a signal that something needs attention, but it is not a reason to panic. The most common cause is missed doses of medication. Be honest with your doctor about your adherence. There is no judgment in this conversation, only problem-solving. If you have been taking your medication perfectly and your viral load is still rising, your doctor will test for drug resistance. This test, available at referral laboratories in Kenya, checks whether the virus in your body has developed mutations that make it resistant to your current ARVs. If resistance is found, your doctor will switch you to a different regimen that the virus cannot evade. This is why second-line and third-line regimens exist. Do not wait for your next scheduled appointment if you receive a concerning result. Contact your clinic and discuss next steps as soon as possible.\n\nAsking the right questions at your clinic visit\n\nWhen you see your doctor, come prepared with questions. What is my viral load today compared to last time? What is my CD4 count and is it improving? Am I on track for the treatment goals we discussed? Do I need any additional tests, like kidney function, liver function, or lipid panels, which can be affected by some ARV medications? Are there any changes to my regimen that we should consider? Should I still be taking co-trimoxazole prophylaxis? When is my next viral load test due?\n\nYour numbers are tools, not judgments\n\nA good viral load result is not a gold star for being a good patient, and a concerning result is not a punishment. These numbers are medical measurements that guide your treatment. They help your doctor and you make informed decisions about your care. Approach them with curiosity and pragmatism, not fear. If you understand what they mean and track them over time, you become an active partner in your healthcare rather than a passive recipient.\n\nKnowledge truly is your best tool in managing HIV. The more you understand your numbers, the more empowered you are to make decisions that keep you healthy for decades to come.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you are living with HIV, two numbers tell you more about your health than almost anything else: your viral load and your CD4 count. Understanding what these numbers mean, how to track them, and what to do when they change puts you in control of your health rather than leaving you guessing between clinic visits."},{"id":"s2","title":"What viral load measures","body":"Your viral load tells you how much HIV is present in your blood. It is measured in copies of the virus per millilitre of blood. When you first start ARV treatment, your viral load may be high, sometimes in the hundreds of thousands. As your medication takes effect, this number should drop steadily over the first three to six months. The ultimate goal is an undetectable viral load, which means the amount of virus is so low that the test cannot measure it. In most Kenyan laboratories, undetectable means below 50 copies per millilitre, though some labs use a threshold of below 200 copies."},{"id":"s3","title":"What your viral load numbers mean","body":"Undetectable or below 50 copies per millilitre means your ARVs are working excellently. Your virus is suppressed and you cannot transmit HIV through sex (U=U). Below 200 copies per millilitre is still considered viral suppression and is a good result. It means your treatment is effective, though your doctor may want to continue monitoring to ensure the trend goes toward full undetectability. Between 200 and 1,000 copies per millilitre is a low-level viraemia. This is concerning and needs attention. Your doctor will likely repeat the test and review your adherence. It may indicate that you have been missing doses or that the virus is beginning to develop resistance. Above 1,000 copies per millilitre indicates treatment failure. This means your current regimen is not adequately suppressing the virus. Your doctor will assess whether this is due to adherence issues or drug resistance, and may need to switch your medication to a different regimen."},{"id":"s4","title":"What CD4 count measures","body":"Your CD4 count measures how many CD4 immune cells are in a cubic millimetre of your blood. CD4 cells are the white blood cells that coordinate your immune response. HIV specifically targets and destroys these cells. A healthy person without HIV typically has a CD4 count between 500 and 1,500 cells per cubic millimetre."},{"id":"s5","title":"What your CD4 numbers mean","body":"Above 500 cells per cubic millimetre is a strong immune system. At this level, your body can fight off most infections effectively. Between 350 and 500 is adequate but not yet optimal. Your immune system is functional but still recovering. Continue your ARVs and good nutrition to help it strengthen. Between 200 and 350 means your immune system is weakened. You are more vulnerable to certain infections and your doctor may prescribe additional preventive medications such as co-trimoxazole, commonly known as Septrin, which is available at Kenyan pharmacies for about KES 100 to KES 300 per month. Below 200 means your immune system is severely compromised. This is the level at which opportunistic infections become a serious risk, and it requires urgent and careful medical management."},{"id":"s6","title":"How often to test","body":"In Kenya''s national HIV treatment guidelines, viral load testing is recommended at six months after starting ARVs, then at twelve months, and annually thereafter if your viral load is undetectable. If your viral load is detectable, your doctor will test more frequently, usually every three months, to assess whether adherence support or a regimen change is needed. CD4 testing is done less frequently once you are stable on treatment, usually annually. However, if you were diagnosed with a low CD4 count or are experiencing new health problems, your doctor may order it more often. These tests are available at comprehensive care clinics across Kenya and are free of charge for patients enrolled in the national HIV program. Private labs charge between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 for a viral load test and KES 800 to KES 1,500 for a CD4 count."},{"id":"s7","title":"Tracking your numbers over time","body":"Do not just hear your results and forget them. Write them down every single time. Keep a small notebook or use the notes app on your phone to record the date, your viral load result, your CD4 count, and any notes your doctor made about the results. Over time, this creates a picture of your treatment journey. You want to see your viral load going down and staying down, and your CD4 count going up and staying up. If either number moves in the wrong direction, you will spot it immediately rather than relying on memory."},{"id":"s8","title":"What to do if your viral load starts rising","body":"A rising viral load is a signal that something needs attention, but it is not a reason to panic. The most common cause is missed doses of medication. Be honest with your doctor about your adherence. There is no judgment in this conversation, only problem-solving. If you have been taking your medication perfectly and your viral load is still rising, your doctor will test for drug resistance. This test, available at referral laboratories in Kenya, checks whether the virus in your body has developed mutations that make it resistant to your current ARVs. If resistance is found, your doctor will switch you to a different regimen that the virus cannot evade. This is why second-line and third-line regimens exist. Do not wait for your next scheduled appointment if you receive a concerning result. Contact your clinic and discuss next steps as soon as possible."},{"id":"s9","title":"Asking the right questions at your clinic visit","body":"When you see your doctor, come prepared with questions. What is my viral load today compared to last time? What is my CD4 count and is it improving? Am I on track for the treatment goals we discussed? Do I need any additional tests, like kidney function, liver function, or lipid panels, which can be affected by some ARV medications? Are there any changes to my regimen that we should consider? Should I still be taking co-trimoxazole prophylaxis? When is my next viral load test due?"},{"id":"s10","title":"Your numbers are tools, not judgments","body":"A good viral load result is not a gold star for being a good patient, and a concerning result is not a punishment. These numbers are medical measurements that guide your treatment. They help your doctor and you make informed decisions about your care. Approach them with curiosity and pragmatism, not fear. If you understand what they mean and track them over time, you become an active partner in your healthcare rather than a passive recipient.\\n\\nKnowledge truly is your best tool in managing HIV. The more you understand your numbers, the more empowered you are to make decisions that keep you healthy for decades to come."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'health-diary'),
  (E'hiv-is-a-chapter-not-the-whole-book', E'HIV is a chapter, not the whole book', E'HIV_AIDS', '{"HIV_AIDS"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'An HIV diagnosis can feel like a full stop. Like everything you planned, everything you hoped for, and everything you imagined about your future suddenly ends.', E'An HIV diagnosis can feel like a full stop. Like everything you planned, everything you hoped for, and everything you imagined about your future suddenly ends. But with modern antiretroviral treatment, HIV is not a death sentence. It is a chronic, manageable condition, and people living with HIV on effective treatment can expect to live long, full, productive lives. Accepting your status is the bridge between diagnosis and truly living again.\n\nThe reality of HIV treatment today\n\nThe ARVs available today are remarkably effective. Kenya''s national HIV program provides free first-line treatment, typically a single pill taken once daily, to all citizens living with HIV. This medication suppresses the virus to undetectable levels in most people within three to six months. With an undetectable viral load, your immune system recovers, opportunistic infections become rare, and your life expectancy approaches that of someone without HIV. This is not theory. This is the lived experience of millions of people in Kenya and worldwide who are thriving on treatment.\n\nWhat acceptance actually means\n\nAcceptance does not mean being happy about having HIV. It does not mean you cannot be angry, sad, or frustrated about your diagnosis. It does not mean pretending everything is fine when some days it is not. Acceptance means acknowledging reality as it is and choosing to work with it rather than against it. It means taking your medication daily because you have decided your life is worth protecting. It means going to your clinic appointments because you value your health. It means allowing yourself to plan for the future because you have one.\n\nThe things HIV does not take away\n\nYou can work. HIV does not affect your ability to do your job. Whether you are a teacher, a farmer, a businessperson, a doctor, an engineer, or anything else, your professional capabilities are unchanged by your status. Kenyan law protects you from workplace discrimination based on HIV status. You can love. You can be in a fulfilling romantic relationship. With an undetectable viral load, you cannot transmit HIV to your partner through sex (U=U). You can build a partnership based on honesty, trust, and mutual care. You can raise children. Prevention of mother-to-child transmission programs, available at maternal health facilities across Kenya, reduce the risk of transmission to a baby to below 1 percent. Many people living with HIV are wonderful, healthy parents raising healthy children. You can travel. There are very few countries that restrict entry based on HIV status, and Kenya is not one of them. You can visit family, take holidays, and explore the world. Just carry enough medication for your trip plus a few extra days in case of delays. You can pursue every dream you had before your diagnosis. Whether it is starting a business, going back to school, building a house, or running a marathon, HIV does not set the boundaries of your ambitions.\n\nThe hardest part is often not the virus\n\nFor many people in Kenya, the greatest challenge of living with HIV is not the virus or the medication. It is the stigma. The fear of what people will say. The worry about being treated differently. The concern that a potential partner will walk away. The anxiety of neighbours whispering. Stigma is a social disease, and while it has decreased significantly in Kenya over the past two decades, it has not disappeared. Accepting your status includes accepting that you will need to navigate stigma strategically, choosing carefully who you tell and when, and building resilience against the ignorance of others.\n\nThe moment of acceptance\n\nMany people living with HIV describe a turning point, a moment when something shifted inside them. For some, it came when they saw their first undetectable viral load result and realized the medication was working. For others, it came when they met someone else living openly and joyfully with HIV. For some, it came through prayer or spiritual reflection. For others, it came through counselling or therapy. There is no single path to acceptance, and there is no timeline. Some people arrive there within months of diagnosis. Others take years. Both are valid.\n\nPractical steps toward acceptance\n\nTake your medication every day, even on days when you feel angry about needing it. Adherence is not just a medical act. It is an act of self-care and self-worth. Learn about your condition. Understanding how HIV works, what your numbers mean, and how treatment protects you reduces fear and increases your sense of control. Talk to someone. A counsellor at your comprehensive care clinic, a support group, a trusted friend, or a religious leader you trust. Breaking the silence, even with one person, begins to lighten the emotional weight. Take care of your body. Eat well, adding protein to every meal through eggs, beans, lentils, or omena. Stay active with walks, dancing, or whatever movement you enjoy. Drink plenty of water. These daily acts of caring for your physical health reinforce the message to yourself that your life matters. Set goals for the future. When you plan for next year, five years from now, and beyond, you are telling yourself that you believe in your future. And you should believe in it, because treatment makes a full future possible.\n\nFinding strength in your journey\n\nAs you come to accept your status, you may discover something unexpected. Many people describe finding a strength and a depth of compassion they did not know they had before their diagnosis. Living with HIV, navigating the healthcare system, managing medication, dealing with stigma, and choosing to keep going despite everything requires extraordinary resilience. That resilience is yours. It does not go away on the hard days. It is the foundation you stand on.\n\nConnecting with community\n\nKenya has one of the most robust HIV support infrastructures in Africa. Comprehensive care clinics in every county provide free treatment and counselling. Organizations like NEPHAK (National Empowerment Network of People Living with HIV/AIDS in Kenya), KELIN, and countless community-based organisations provide support, advocacy, and community. Faith-based organisations across denominations offer support ministries. You are not alone in this, and you do not have to figure it out by yourself.\n\nHIV is a chapter in your life, not the whole book. The chapters that come after, the relationships you build, the goals you achieve, the family you nurture, the community you contribute to, these are the chapters that define you. Your diagnosis opened a difficult chapter, but you hold the pen for everything that comes next.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"An HIV diagnosis can feel like a full stop. Like everything you planned, everything you hoped for, and everything you imagined about your future suddenly ends. But with modern antiretroviral treatment, HIV is not a death sentence. It is a chronic, manageable condition, and people living with HIV on effective treatment can expect to live long, full, productive lives. Accepting your status is the bridge between diagnosis and truly living again."},{"id":"s2","title":"The reality of HIV treatment today","body":"The ARVs available today are remarkably effective. Kenya''s national HIV program provides free first-line treatment, typically a single pill taken once daily, to all citizens living with HIV. This medication suppresses the virus to undetectable levels in most people within three to six months. With an undetectable viral load, your immune system recovers, opportunistic infections become rare, and your life expectancy approaches that of someone without HIV. This is not