on, HIV does not set the boundaries of your ambitions."},{"id":"s5","title":"The hardest part is often not the virus","body":"For many people in Kenya, the greatest challenge of living with HIV is not the virus or the medication. It is the stigma. The fear of what people will say. The worry about being treated differently. The concern that a potential partner will walk away. The anxiety of neighbours whispering. Stigma is a social disease, and while it has decreased significantly in Kenya over the past two decades, it has not disappeared. Accepting your status includes accepting that you will need to navigate stigma strategically, choosing carefully who you tell and when, and building resilience against the ignorance of others."},{"id":"s6","title":"The moment of acceptance","body":"Many people living with HIV describe a turning point, a moment when something shifted inside them. For some, it came when they saw their first undetectable viral load result and realized the medication was working. For others, it came when they met someone else living openly and joyfully with HIV. For some, it came through prayer or spiritual reflection. For others, it came through counselling or therapy. There is no single path to acceptance, and there is no timeline. Some people arrive there within months of diagnosis. Others take years. Both are valid."},{"id":"s7","title":"Practical steps toward acceptance","body":"Take your medication every day, even on days when you feel angry about needing it. Adherence is not just a medical act. It is an act of self-care and self-worth. Learn about your condition. Understanding how HIV works, what your numbers mean, and how treatment protects you reduces fear and increases your sense of control. Talk to someone. A counsellor at your comprehensive care clinic, a support group, a trusted friend, or a religious leader you trust. Breaking the silence, even with one person, begins to lighten the emotional weight. Take care of your body. Eat well, adding protein to every meal through eggs, beans, lentils, or omena. Stay active with walks, dancing, or whatever movement you enjoy. Drink plenty of water. These daily acts of caring for your physical health reinforce the message to yourself that your life matters. Set goals for the future. When you plan for next year, five years from now, and beyond, you are telling yourself that you believe in your future. And you should believe in it, because treatment makes a full future possible."},{"id":"s8","title":"Finding strength in your journey","body":"As you come to accept your status, you may discover something unexpected. Many people describe finding a strength and a depth of compassion they did not know they had before their diagnosis. Living with HIV, navigating the healthcare system, managing medication, dealing with stigma, and choosing to keep going despite everything requires extraordinary resilience. That resilience is yours. It does not go away on the hard days. It is the foundation you stand on."},{"id":"s9","title":"Connecting with community","body":"Kenya has one of the most robust HIV support infrastructures in Africa. Comprehensive care clinics in every county provide free treatment and counselling. Organizations like NEPHAK (National Empowerment Network of People Living with HIV/AIDS in Kenya), KELIN, and countless community-based organisations provide support, advocacy, and community. Faith-based organisations across denominations offer support ministries. You are not alone in this, and you do not have to figure it out by yourself.\\n\\nHIV is a chapter in your life, not the whole book. The chapters that come after, the relationships you build, the goals you achieve, the family you nurture, the community you contribute to, these are the chapters that define you. Your diagnosis opened a difficult chapter, but you hold the pen for everything that comes next."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'new-beginning'),
  (E'one-year-of-undetectable-viral-load', E'One year of undetectable viral load', E'HIV_AIDS', '{"HIV_AIDS"}', E'MILESTONE', E'What an extraordinary achievement. You have maintained an undetectable viral load for a full year. In this lesson, you will recognise and celebrate the progress you have made.', E'What an extraordinary achievement. You have maintained an undetectable viral load for a full year. That means 365 days of taking your ARVs consistently, showing up for yourself every single day, and proving that you are stronger than this virus. Take a moment to let that sink in — you did this.\n\nWhat undetectable really means\n\nWhen your viral load is undetectable, the amount of HIV in your blood is so low that standard laboratory tests cannot measure it. This does not mean the virus is gone — it means your medication is keeping it fully suppressed. Your immune system is protected, your CD4 count is stable or climbing, and your body can fight off infections the way it is supposed to. You are living well with HIV, not just surviving it.\n\nU=U — you cannot pass it on\n\nOne of the most powerful facts in modern medicine is Undetectable equals Untransmittable, known as U=U. When your viral load has been undetectable for at least six months, you cannot transmit HIV to your sexual partners. This is backed by massive global studies involving thousands of couples. If anyone ever questions this, your clinic can provide resources that explain the science. U=U means freedom — freedom from fear, freedom from shame, and freedom to enjoy intimate relationships without guilt.\n\nThe discipline behind this milestone\n\nLet us be honest about what it took to get here. Taking ARVs every day at the same time is not easy. Maybe you set an alarm on your phone. Maybe you paired your pills with your morning cup of tea or your evening meal of ugali and sukuma wiki. Maybe there were days you felt fine and wondered whether you really needed the medication — but you took it anyway. Maybe you travelled upcountry for a funeral or a wedding and had to pack your pills carefully, worrying about what people might think. You pushed through all of that. That is discipline, and it deserves recognition.\n\nYour health gains over the past year\n\nOver the past twelve months, your body has been healing and strengthening in ways you might not even notice. Your immune system has been rebuilding. Your risk of opportunistic infections like tuberculosis and pneumonia has dropped significantly. Your kidneys, liver, and heart are all benefiting from a suppressed viral load. Studies show that people who maintain an undetectable viral load have a life expectancy that is close to that of someone who does not have HIV at all. You are not just managing a condition — you are thriving.\n\nWhat your care team wants you to know\n\nYour doctor, your clinical officer, your pharmacist at the comprehensive care centre — they all celebrate these milestones with you. They have seen the work you put in. When you go for your next appointment at your county hospital or at a facility like Kenyatta National Hospital, Mbagathi, or your local health centre, share this achievement with them. They may adjust your appointment schedule from monthly to every three or six months as a reward for your consistency. Under SHA, your follow-up visits and ARVs are covered, so cost should never be a barrier to staying on track.\n\nProtecting this achievement going forward\n\nThe key to keeping your viral load undetectable is simple but not always easy — keep taking your medication. Do not stop because you feel healthy. Do not switch to herbal remedies without talking to your doctor. Do not share your pills or skip doses when money is tight, because ARVs are free at government facilities across Kenya. If you experience side effects, talk to your doctor about switching to a different regimen rather than stopping altogether. Keep your clinic appointments, get your blood work done on schedule, and continue eating well. A balanced plate of githeri with some avocado, a side of traditional vegetables, and a glass of water costs less than KES 150 to prepare at home and keeps your body strong.\n\nCelebrate and keep going\n\nYou have proven something important this year — that you can manage this condition with grace and determination. Treat yourself to something that makes you happy. Call a friend who has supported you. Write down how you feel today so you can look back on it when tough days come. You are not defined by HIV. You are defined by the courage, consistency, and quiet strength you bring to every single day. Here is to the next year — and every year after that.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"What an extraordinary achievement. You have maintained an undetectable viral load for a full year. That means 365 days of taking your ARVs consistently, showing up for yourself every single day, and proving that you are stronger than this virus. Take a moment to let that sink in — you did this."},{"id":"s2","title":"What undetectable really means","body":"When your viral load is undetectable, the amount of HIV in your blood is so low that standard laboratory tests cannot measure it. This does not mean the virus is gone — it means your medication is keeping it fully suppressed. Your immune system is protected, your CD4 count is stable or climbing, and your body can fight off infections the way it is supposed to. You are living well with HIV, not just surviving it."},{"id":"s3","title":"U=U — you cannot pass it on","body":"One of the most powerful facts in modern medicine is Undetectable equals Untransmittable, known as U=U. When your viral load has been undetectable for at least six months, you cannot transmit HIV to your sexual partners. This is backed by massive global studies involving thousands of couples. If anyone ever questions this, your clinic can provide resources that explain the science. U=U means freedom — freedom from fear, freedom from shame, and freedom to enjoy intimate relationships without guilt."},{"id":"s4","title":"The discipline behind this milestone","body":"Let us be honest about what it took to get here. Taking ARVs every day at the same time is not easy. Maybe you set an alarm on your phone. Maybe you paired your pills with your morning cup of tea or your evening meal of ugali and sukuma wiki. Maybe there were days you felt fine and wondered whether you really needed the medication — but you took it anyway. Maybe you travelled upcountry for a funeral or a wedding and had to pack your pills carefully, worrying about what people might think. You pushed through all of that. That is discipline, and it deserves recognition."},{"id":"s5","title":"Your health gains over the past year","body":"Over the past twelve months, your body has been healing and strengthening in ways you might not even notice. Your immune system has been rebuilding. Your risk of opportunistic infections like tuberculosis and pneumonia has dropped significantly. Your kidneys, liver, and heart are all benefiting from a suppressed viral load. Studies show that people who maintain an undetectable viral load have a life expectancy that is close to that of someone who does not have HIV at all. You are not just managing a condition — you are thriving."},{"id":"s6","title":"What your care team wants you to know","body":"Your doctor, your clinical officer, your pharmacist at the comprehensive care centre — they all celebrate these milestones with you. They have seen the work you put in. When you go for your next appointment at your county hospital or at a facility like Kenyatta National Hospital, Mbagathi, or your local health centre, share this achievement with them. They may adjust your appointment schedule from monthly to every three or six months as a reward for your consistency. Under SHA, your follow-up visits and ARVs are covered, so cost should never be a barrier to staying on track."},{"id":"s7","title":"Protecting this achievement going forward","body":"The key to keeping your viral load undetectable is simple but not always easy — keep taking your medication. Do not stop because you feel healthy. Do not switch to herbal remedies without talking to your doctor. Do not share your pills or skip doses when money is tight, because ARVs are free at government facilities across Kenya. If you experience side effects, talk to your doctor about switching to a different regimen rather than stopping altogether. Keep your clinic appointments, get your blood work done on schedule, and continue eating well. A balanced plate of githeri with some avocado, a side of traditional vegetables, and a glass of water costs less than KES 150 to prepare at home and keeps your body strong."},{"id":"s8","title":"Celebrate and keep going","body":"You have proven something important this year — that you can manage this condition with grace and determination. Treat yourself to something that makes you happy. Call a friend who has supported you. Write down how you feel today so you can look back on it when tough days come. You are not defined by HIV. You are defined by the courage, consistency, and quiet strength you bring to every single day. Here is to the next year — and every year after that."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'celebration'),
  (E'how-missed-meals-can-trigger-seizures', E'How missed meals can trigger seizures', E'EPILEPSY', '{"EPILEPSY"}', E'DIETARY', E'If you live with epilepsy, one of the simplest things you can do to reduce your seizure risk is to eat regular, balanced meals. Low blood sugar — what doctors call hypoglycaemia — is a well-known seizure trigger, and it happens most often when you skip meals or go too long without eating.', E'If you live with epilepsy, one of the simplest things you can do to reduce your seizure risk is to eat regular, balanced meals. Low blood sugar — what doctors call hypoglycaemia — is a well-known seizure trigger, and it happens most often when you skip meals or go too long without eating. The good news is that this trigger is completely within your control.\n\nWhy blood sugar matters for seizures\n\nYour brain uses more energy than any other organ in your body. It runs almost entirely on glucose, which comes from the food you eat. When you skip a meal, your blood sugar drops, and your brain does not get the fuel it needs. For someone with epilepsy, this energy shortage can lower your seizure threshold — the point at which your brain''s electrical activity becomes unstable enough to trigger a seizure. Eating at regular times keeps your blood sugar steady and your brain well-fuelled throughout the day.\n\nA steady meal schedule\n\nAim to eat three main meals and two small snacks every day, at roughly the same times. A good rhythm might look like this: breakfast by 7:30am, a mid-morning snack around 10:00am, lunch by 1:00pm, an afternoon snack around 4:00pm, and dinner by 7:00pm. Your body thrives on routine, and so does your brain. Try not to let more than four hours pass without eating something.\n\nBreakfast ideas that protect you\n\nStart your day with foods that release energy slowly. A bowl of uji made with millet or sorghum flour is excellent — it costs around KES 20 to prepare at home and provides slow-burning carbohydrates. Add a spoonful of groundnuts or a boiled egg for protein. If you prefer something more filling, two slices of brown bread with avocado and a cup of tea works well. Avoid starting your day with just sweet tea and a mandazi — the sugar spike will be followed by a crash that can leave you vulnerable.\n\nLunch and dinner staples\n\nFor your main meals, build your plate around complex carbohydrates. Brown ugali made from whole grain maize flour is a better choice than white ugali because it releases energy more slowly. Pair it with sukuma wiki or managu and a protein like beans, lentils, or a small piece of fish. Githeri — the traditional mix of maize and beans — is one of the best meals you can eat because it combines slow-release carbohydrates with protein and fibre. A plate of githeri with some vegetables from the market costs as little as KES 80 to prepare and will keep your blood sugar stable for hours. Sweet potatoes, arrow roots, and brown rice are also excellent choices.\n\nSmart snacking between meals\n\nKeep simple snacks within reach so you are never caught without food. Roasted groundnuts are affordable — a small packet costs about KES 20-30 from any kiosk — and they provide protein and healthy fats that stabilise blood sugar. A banana or an orange is another good option. Avoid crisps, sweets, and fizzy drinks as snacks because they cause rapid blood sugar spikes and crashes.\n\nWhat to buy at the market\n\nWhen you go to your local market or to a supermarket like Naivas or Quickmart, stock up on whole grain maize flour, sweet potatoes, bananas, groundnuts, eggs, sukuma wiki, beans, and lentils. These are all affordable staples that support steady blood sugar. Buy in bulk when you can — a 2kg bag of dried beans costs around KES 200-280 and will last you more than a week. Having these foods at home means you are less likely to skip meals because there is nothing to eat.\n\nEating out and at events\n\nWhen you eat at a hotel or restaurant, choose meals built around whole foods. Ask for ugali and fish stew, or rice and beans, rather than chips and sausage. At family gatherings or events where food service is unpredictable, eat something before you go so you are not dependent on when food is served. If you know there will be a long wait — like at a church event or a wedding — carry a small snack in your bag.\n\nFasting and medication\n\nIf religious fasting is part of your life, whether during Ramadan or other periods, talk to your doctor before you begin. They can help you adjust the timing of your anti-seizure medication so you are still protected. Never skip your medication because you skipped a meal — your pills work independently of food, and missing doses is one of the biggest risk factors for breakthrough seizures. Take your medication with a glass of water even if you have not eaten.\n\nThe bottom line\n\nEating regularly is one of the most affordable, practical, and effective ways to reduce your seizure risk. You do not need expensive or special foods — you need the everyday Kenyan staples you already know, eaten at consistent times. Plan your meals, stock your kitchen, carry a snack, and never let hunger catch you off guard. Your brain will thank you for it.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you live with epilepsy, one of the simplest things you can do to reduce your seizure risk is to eat regular, balanced meals. Low blood sugar — what doctors call hypoglycaemia — is a well-known seizure trigger, and it happens most often when you skip meals or go too long without eating. The good news is that this trigger is completely within your control."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why blood sugar matters for seizures","body":"Your brain uses more energy than any other organ in your body. It runs almost entirely on glucose, which comes from the food you eat. When you skip a meal, your blood sugar drops, and your brain does not get the fuel it needs. For someone with epilepsy, this energy shortage can lower your seizure threshold — the point at which your brain''s electrical activity becomes unstable enough to trigger a seizure. Eating at regular times keeps your blood sugar steady and your brain well-fuelled throughout the day."},{"id":"s3","title":"A steady meal schedule","body":"Aim to eat three main meals and two small snacks every day, at roughly the same times. A good rhythm might look like this: breakfast by 7:30am, a mid-morning snack around 10:00am, lunch by 1:00pm, an afternoon snack around 4:00pm, and dinner by 7:00pm. Your body thrives on routine, and so does your brain. Try not to let more than four hours pass without eating something."},{"id":"s4","title":"Breakfast ideas that protect you","body":"Start your day with foods that release energy slowly. A bowl of uji made with millet or sorghum flour is excellent — it costs around KES 20 to prepare at home and provides slow-burning carbohydrates. Add a spoonful of groundnuts or a boiled egg for protein. If you prefer something more filling, two slices of brown bread with avocado and a cup of tea works well. Avoid starting your day with just sweet tea and a mandazi — the sugar spike will be followed by a crash that can leave you vulnerable."},{"id":"s5","title":"Lunch and dinner staples","body":"For your main meals, build your plate around complex carbohydrates. Brown ugali made from whole grain maize flour is a better choice than white ugali because it releases energy more slowly. Pair it with sukuma wiki or managu and a protein like beans, lentils, or a small piece of fish. Githeri — the traditional mix of maize and beans — is one of the best meals you can eat because it combines slow-release carbohydrates with protein and fibre. A plate of githeri with some vegetables from the market costs as little as KES 80 to prepare and will keep your blood sugar stable for hours. Sweet potatoes, arrow roots, and brown rice are also excellent choices."},{"id":"s6","title":"Smart snacking between meals","body":"Keep simple snacks within reach so you are never caught without food. Roasted groundnuts are affordable — a small packet costs about KES 20-30 from any kiosk — and they provide protein and healthy fats that stabilise blood sugar. A banana or an orange is another good option. Avoid crisps, sweets, and fizzy drinks as snacks because they cause rapid blood sugar spikes and crashes."},{"id":"s7","title":"What to buy at the market","body":"When you go to your local market or to a supermarket like Naivas or Quickmart, stock up on whole grain maize flour, sweet potatoes, bananas, groundnuts, eggs, sukuma wiki, beans, and lentils. These are all affordable staples that support steady blood sugar. Buy in bulk when you can — a 2kg bag of dried beans costs around KES 200-280 and will last you more than a week. Having these foods at home means you are less likely to skip meals because there is nothing to eat."},{"id":"s8","title":"Eating out and at events","body":"When you eat at a hotel or restaurant, choose meals built around whole foods. Ask for ugali and fish stew, or rice and beans, rather than chips and sausage. At family gatherings or events where food service is unpredictable, eat something before you go so you are not dependent on when food is served. If you know there will be a long wait — like at a church event or a wedding — carry a small snack in your bag."},{"id":"s9","title":"Fasting and medication","body":"If religious fasting is part of your life, whether during Ramadan or other periods, talk to your doctor before you begin. They can help you adjust the timing of your anti-seizure medication so you are still protected. Never skip your medication because you skipped a meal — your pills work independently of food, and missing doses is one of the biggest risk factors for breakthrough seizures. Take your medication with a glass of water even if you have not eaten."},{"id":"s10","title":"The bottom line","body":"Eating regularly is one of the most affordable, practical, and effective ways to reduce your seizure risk. You do not need expensive or special foods — you need the everyday Kenyan staples you already know, eaten at consistent times. Plan your meals, stock your kitchen, carry a snack, and never let hunger catch you off guard. Your brain will thank you for it."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'healthy-cooking'),
  (E'safe-exercise-with-epilepsy-what-you-can-and-cannot-do', E'Safe exercise with epilepsy: what you can and cannot do', E'EPILEPSY', '{"EPILEPSY"}', E'EXERCISE', E'If you have epilepsy, you might worry that exercise could trigger a seizure. Many people with the condition avoid physical activity out of fear, or because well-meaning family members tell them to rest.', E'If you have epilepsy, you might worry that exercise could trigger a seizure. Many people with the condition avoid physical activity out of fear, or because well-meaning family members tell them to rest. But research shows the opposite — regular exercise actually reduces seizure frequency in many people by lowering stress, improving sleep quality, and boosting overall brain health. The key is choosing the right activities and taking simple safety precautions.\n\nExercise is good for your brain\n\nPhysical activity increases blood flow to the brain, releases natural mood-boosting chemicals, and helps regulate the electrical activity that causes seizures. Studies from epilepsy centres around the world have found that people who exercise regularly have fewer seizures than those who are sedentary. Exercise also helps with the anxiety and depression that often accompany epilepsy. You deserve to move your body and feel strong.\n\nSafe activities you can enjoy\n\nMost forms of exercise are perfectly safe when your epilepsy is well controlled. Walking is the easiest place to start — a brisk 30-minute walk around your estate or at a local park like Uhuru Gardens, Karura Forest, or along the Nairobi River costs nothing and requires no equipment. Jogging, dancing, cycling on flat roads, and playing team sports like football or volleyball are all excellent choices. Yoga and stretching improve flexibility and reduce stress, which can help with seizure control. Many community groups and churches offer free exercise sessions that you can join.\n\nA simple weekly routine\n\nHere is a beginner-friendly plan you can follow. On Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, go for a 30-minute brisk walk or jog. Start with 10 minutes if you are not used to exercise and add 5 minutes each week until you reach 30. On Tuesday and Thursday, do a simple strength routine at home: 10 squats, 10 wall push-ups, 10 lunges on each leg, and a 20-second plank. Rest for 30 seconds between each exercise and repeat the circuit twice. On Saturday, do something social — play football with friends, join a community aerobics class, or go for a nature walk. Sunday is your rest day. This routine requires no gym membership and no equipment.\n\nActivities to avoid or modify\n\nSome activities carry higher risk if you were to have a seizure during them. Never swim alone — always have someone with you who knows about your condition and can pull you to safety. Avoid deep water entirely if your seizures are not well controlled. Rock climbing, bungee jumping, and activities at dangerous heights are not advisable. If you ride a bicycle, stick to flat, quiet roads away from heavy traffic, and always wear a helmet. Avoid scuba diving, as a seizure underwater can be fatal. These are not rules to make you feel limited — they are practical steps to keep you safe while staying active.\n\nExercise with a buddy\n\nAlways try to exercise with someone who knows you have epilepsy and understands basic seizure first aid. Teach your exercise partner what to do: turn you on your side, cushion your head, do not put anything in your mouth, time the seizure, and call for help if it lasts more than five minutes. This is a simple conversation that takes two minutes and could save your life. Many people find that having an exercise buddy also keeps them motivated and consistent.\n\nStay hydrated and listen to your body\n\nDehydration and exhaustion can both lower your seizure threshold. Drink water before, during, and after exercise — carry a water bottle with you. In Kenya''s warm climate, this is especially important. Aim for at least two litres of water throughout the day, more if you are exercising. If you start feeling unusually tired, dizzy, or strange during a workout, stop immediately and rest. Pushing through warning signs is not worth the risk. Exercise should make you feel energised, not drained.\n\nWhen to talk to your doctor\n\nBefore starting a new exercise programme, especially if you have been inactive for a long time, have a conversation with your neurologist or clinical officer at your nearest county hospital or epilepsy clinic. Facilities like Kenyatta National Hospital, Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, and many county-level hospitals have neurology services. Your doctor can advise you on any specific precautions based on your seizure type and frequency. If you notice that seizures seem to happen after exercise, keep a record of what you did, how long you exercised, and how you felt — this information helps your doctor fine-tune your care plan.\n\nThe cost of not exercising\n\nAvoiding exercise because of epilepsy often leads to weight gain, poor cardiovascular health, low mood, and social isolation — all of which can make your condition harder to manage. Movement is medicine. Start small, stay consistent, and build up gradually. You have every right to an active, fulfilling life.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you have epilepsy, you might worry that exercise could trigger a seizure. Many people with the condition avoid physical activity out of fear, or because well-meaning family members tell them to rest. But research shows the opposite — regular exercise actually reduces seizure frequency in many people by lowering stress, improving sleep quality, and boosting overall brain health. The key is choosing the right activities and taking simple safety precautions."},{"id":"s2","title":"Exercise is good for your brain","body":"Physical activity increases blood flow to the brain, releases natural mood-boosting chemicals, and helps regulate the electrical activity that causes seizures. Studies from epilepsy centres around the world have found that people who exercise regularly have fewer seizures than those who are sedentary. Exercise also helps with the anxiety and depression that often accompany epilepsy. You deserve to move your body and feel strong."},{"id":"s3","title":"Safe activities you can enjoy","body":"Most forms of exercise are perfectly safe when your epilepsy is well controlled. Walking is the easiest place to start — a brisk 30-minute walk around your estate or at a local park like Uhuru Gardens, Karura Forest, or along the Nairobi River costs nothing and requires no equipment. Jogging, dancing, cycling on flat roads, and playing team sports like football or volleyball are all excellent choices. Yoga and stretching improve flexibility and reduce stress, which can help with seizure control. Many community groups and churches offer free exercise sessions that you can join."},{"id":"s4","title":"A simple weekly routine","body":"Here is a beginner-friendly plan you can follow. On Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, go for a 30-minute brisk walk or jog. Start with 10 minutes if you are not used to exercise and add 5 minutes each week until you reach 30. On Tuesday and Thursday, do a simple strength routine at home: 10 squats, 10 wall push-ups, 10 lunges on each leg, and a 20-second plank. Rest for 30 seconds between each exercise and repeat the circuit twice. On Saturday, do something social — play football with friends, join a community aerobics class, or go for a nature walk. Sunday is your rest day. This routine requires no gym membership and no equipment."},{"id":"s5","title":"Activities to avoid or modify","body":"Some activities carry higher risk if you were to have a seizure during them. Never swim alone — always have someone with you who knows about your condition and can pull you to safety. Avoid deep water entirely if your seizures are not well controlled. Rock climbing, bungee jumping, and activities at dangerous heights are not advisable. If you ride a bicycle, stick to flat, quiet roads away from heavy traffic, and always wear a helmet. Avoid scuba diving, as a seizure underwater can be fatal. These are not rules to make you feel limited — they are practical steps to keep you safe while staying active."},{"id":"s6","title":"Exercise with a buddy","body":"Always try to exercise with someone who knows you have epilepsy and understands basic seizure first aid. Teach your exercise partner what to do: turn you on your side, cushion your head, do not put anything in your mouth, time the seizure, and call for help if it lasts more than five minutes. This is a simple conversation that takes two minutes and could save your life. Many people find that having an exercise buddy also keeps them motivated and consistent."},{"id":"s7","title":"Stay hydrated and listen to your body","body":"Dehydration and exhaustion can both lower your seizure threshold. Drink water before, during, and after exercise — carry a water bottle with you. In Kenya''s warm climate, this is especially important. Aim for at least two litres of water throughout the day, more if you are exercising. If you start feeling unusually tired, dizzy, or strange during a workout, stop immediately and rest. Pushing through warning signs is not worth the risk. Exercise should make you feel energised, not drained."},{"id":"s8","title":"When to talk to your doctor","body":"Before starting a new exercise programme, especially if you have been inactive for a long time, have a conversation with your neurologist or clinical officer at your nearest county hospital or epilepsy clinic. Facilities like Kenyatta National Hospital, Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, and many county-level hospitals have neurology services. Your doctor can advise you on any specific precautions based on your seizure type and frequency. If you notice that seizures seem to happen after exercise, keep a record of what you did, how long you exercised, and how you felt — this information helps your doctor fine-tune your care plan."},{"id":"s9","title":"The cost of not exercising","body":"Avoiding exercise because of epilepsy often leads to weight gain, poor cardiovascular health, low mood, and social isolation — all of which can make your condition harder to manage. Movement is medicine. Start small, stay consistent, and build up gradually. You have every right to an active, fulfilling life."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'light-exercise'),
  (E'myth-you-should-put-something-in-a-seizing-persons-mouth', E'Myth: You should put something in a seizing person''s mouth', E'EPILEPSY', '{"EPILEPSY"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'One of the most common and dangerous myths about epilepsy is that you should force something into the mouth of a person who is having a seizure — a spoon, a stick, a wallet, a piece of cloth, or even your fingers.', E'The myth\n\nOne of the most common and dangerous myths about epilepsy is that you should force something into the mouth of a person who is having a seizure — a spoon, a stick, a wallet, a piece of cloth, or even your fingers. Many people in Kenya and around the world believe this is necessary to prevent the person from swallowing their tongue. You may have seen it done, or been told to do it by a well-meaning elder or neighbour. But this advice is completely wrong, and following it can cause serious harm.\n\nThe truth — you cannot swallow your tongue\n\nIt is physically impossible to swallow your own tongue. The tongue is firmly attached to the floor of the mouth by a band of tissue called the frenulum. During a seizure, the tongue may move or the jaw may clench, but the tongue cannot fall back and block the airway in the way people imagine. This myth has persisted for generations, but it has no basis in anatomy or medicine. Every neurologist and epilepsy specialist will tell you the same thing: the tongue stays in place.\n\nWhat actually happens when you put something in the mouth\n\nForcing an object into a seizing person''s mouth is dangerous for both of you. The person''s jaw muscles contract powerfully during a seizure — strong enough to bite through a wooden spoon or break their own teeth on a metal object. Pieces of a broken object can fall into the throat and cause choking, which is far more dangerous than the seizure itself. If you put your fingers in their mouth, you could lose a fingertip. Foreign objects in the mouth can also cause cuts, broken jaw bones, and aspiration — where fragments or blood enter the lungs and cause pneumonia. Every year in Kenya, people are injured because of this myth.\n\nWhat you should actually do\n\nIf someone near you has a seizure — whether at home, at the market, at church, at work, or on a matatu — here is exactly what to do. First, stay calm. Most seizures look frightening but they end on their own within one to three minutes. Gently guide the person to the ground if they are standing, to prevent a fall. Turn them onto their side — this is called the recovery position, and it allows saliva or any fluid to drain from the mouth rather than going into the lungs. Place something soft under their head, like a folded jacket, a leso, or even your hand. Look around and move away any sharp objects, furniture edges, or anything that could cause injury.\n\nWhat not to do during a seizure\n\nDo not hold the person down or try to restrain their movements. Their muscles are contracting involuntarily and fighting against them can cause joint injuries or fractures. Do not pour water on them — this is another common myth in some communities and it does nothing to stop the seizure. Do not try to give them water or food to drink or eat during the seizure, as they could choke. Do not slap them or shout at them to wake up. Simply stay beside them, keep them safe, and wait.\n\nTiming matters — when to call for help\n\nUse your phone to time the seizure from the moment it starts. Most seizures last between one and three minutes and resolve without any medical intervention. However, call for emergency help or get the person to the nearest hospital immediately if the seizure lasts longer than five minutes, if a second seizure begins shortly after the first, if the person does not regain consciousness within ten minutes after the seizure stops, if the person is pregnant, if they were injured during the seizure, or if this is their first-ever seizure. In Kenya, you can call 999 or 112 for emergency services, or go directly to the casualty department of your nearest hospital.\n\nAfter the seizure ends\n\nWhen the seizure stops, the person will likely be confused, drowsy, or disoriented. This is called the postictal state, and it is completely normal. Speak to them gently and let them know what happened. Stay with them until they are fully alert. Offer them water when they are awake enough to drink safely. Do not leave them alone, especially near roads or other hazards. If they are a stranger, check whether they have a medical bracelet or a card in their wallet with emergency contact information.\n\nWhy this myth is harmful\n\nEvery time someone puts an object in a seizing person''s mouth, there is a risk of broken teeth, choking, aspiration, or jaw injury. These injuries can require expensive emergency dental or surgical treatment — potentially costing thousands of shillings that would not have been needed if the right first aid had been applied. More importantly, the myth creates a culture of panic around seizures that increases stigma. When people believe seizures are so dangerous that extreme measures are needed, they begin to fear people with epilepsy rather than understanding them.\n\nSpread the truth\n\nIf you learn one thing from this article, let it be this: turn them on their side, protect their head, and wait. That is all you need to do. Share this information with your family, your neighbours, your colleagues, and your community. Print it out and put it on a noticeboard at your church or school. The more people who know the correct first aid for seizures, the safer everyone with epilepsy will be.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The myth","body":"One of the most common and dangerous myths about epilepsy is that you should force something into the mouth of a person who is having a seizure — a spoon, a stick, a wallet, a piece of cloth, or even your fingers. Many people in Kenya and around the world believe this is necessary to prevent the person from swallowing their tongue. You may have seen it done, or been told to do it by a well-meaning elder or neighbour. But this advice is completely wrong, and following it can cause serious harm."},{"id":"s2","title":"The truth — you cannot swallow your tongue","body":"It is physically impossible to swallow your own tongue. The tongue is firmly attached to the floor of the mouth by a band of tissue called the frenulum. During a seizure, the tongue may move or the jaw may clench, but the tongue cannot fall back and block the airway in the way people imagine. This myth has persisted for generations, but it has no basis in anatomy or medicine. Every neurologist and epilepsy specialist will tell you the same thing: the tongue stays in place."},{"id":"s3","title":"What actually happens when you put something in the mouth","body":"Forcing an object into a seizing person''s mouth is dangerous for both of you. The person''s jaw muscles contract powerfully during a seizure — strong enough to bite through a wooden spoon or break their own teeth on a metal object. Pieces of a broken object can fall into the throat and cause choking, which is far more dangerous than the seizure itself. If you put your fingers in their mouth, you could lose a fingertip. Foreign objects in the mouth can also cause cuts, broken jaw bones, and aspiration — where fragments or blood enter the lungs and cause pneumonia. Every year in Kenya, people are injured because of this myth."},{"id":"s4","title":"What you should actually do","body":"If someone near you has a seizure — whether at home, at the market, at church, at work, or on a matatu — here is exactly what to do. First, stay calm. Most seizures look frightening but they end on their own within one to three minutes. Gently guide the person to the ground if they are standing, to prevent a fall. Turn them onto their side — this is called the recovery position, and it allows saliva or any fluid to drain from the mouth rather than going into the lungs. Place something soft under their head, like a folded jacket, a leso, or even your hand. Look around and move away any sharp objects, furniture edges, or anything that could cause injury."},{"id":"s5","title":"What not to do during a seizure","body":"Do not hold the person down or try to restrain their movements. Their muscles are contracting involuntarily and fighting against them can cause joint injuries or fractures. Do not pour water on them — this is another common myth in some communities and it does nothing to stop the seizure. Do not try to give them water or food to drink or eat during the seizure, as they could choke. Do not slap them or shout at them to wake up. Simply stay beside them, keep them safe, and wait."},{"id":"s6","title":"Timing matters — when to call for help","body":"Use your phone to time the seizure from the moment it starts. Most seizures last between one and three minutes and resolve without any medical intervention. However, call for emergency help or get the person to the nearest hospital immediately if the seizure lasts longer than five minutes, if a second seizure begins shortly after the first, if the person does not regain consciousness within ten minutes after the seizure stops, if the person is pregnant, if they were injured during the seizure, or if this is their first-ever seizure. In Kenya, you can call 999 or 112 for emergency services, or go directly to the casualty department of your nearest hospital."},{"id":"s7","title":"After the seizure ends","body":"When the seizure stops, the person will likely be confused, drowsy, or disoriented. This is called the postictal state, and it is completely normal. Speak to them gently and let them know what happened. Stay with them until they are fully alert. Offer them water when they are awake enough to drink safely. Do not leave them alone, especially near roads or other hazards. If they are a stranger, check whether they have a medical bracelet or a card in their wallet with emergency contact information."},{"id":"s8","title":"Why this myth is harmful","body":"Every time someone puts an object in a seizing person''s mouth, there is a risk of broken teeth, choking, aspiration, or jaw injury. These injuries can require expensive emergency dental or surgical treatment — potentially costing thousands of shillings that would not have been needed if the right first aid had been applied. More importantly, the myth creates a culture of panic around seizures that increases stigma. When people believe seizures are so dangerous that extreme measures are needed, they begin to fear people with epilepsy rather than understanding them."},{"id":"s9","title":"Spread the truth","body":"If you learn one thing from this article, let it be this: turn them on their side, protect their head, and wait. That is all you need to do. Share this information with your family, your neighbours, your colleagues, and your community. Print it out and put it on a noticeboard at your church or school. The more people who know the correct first aid for seizures, the safer everyone with epilepsy will be."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'health-facts'),
  (E'the-loneliness-of-living-with-epilepsy-stigma', E'The loneliness of living with epilepsy stigma', E'EPILEPSY', '{"EPILEPSY"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'Living with epilepsy in Kenya can be deeply lonely. Not because of the seizures themselves, but because of how other people respond to them.', E'Living with epilepsy in Kenya can be deeply lonely. Not because of the seizures themselves, but because of how other people respond to them. In many communities across the country — from Nairobi estates to rural villages in Western Kenya and the Coast — epilepsy is still surrounded by fear, misunderstanding, and stigma that can make you feel like you are carrying an invisible burden that nobody wants to acknowledge.\n\nWhat stigma looks like in everyday life\n\nStigma is not always dramatic. Sometimes it is quiet and subtle. It is the friend who stops inviting you out because they are afraid you will have a seizure in public. It is the employer who finds a reason not to hire you after learning about your condition. It is the whisper at a family gathering — someone saying your epilepsy is a curse or the result of witchcraft. It is the parent who warns their children to stay away from you. It is the person on the matatu who moves to another seat when they see you taking your medication. These moments accumulate, and over time they can erode your confidence, your sense of belonging, and your mental health.\n\nThe weight of being misunderstood\n\nIn some Kenyan communities, epilepsy is still called by names that associate it with spiritual possession or punishment. Some people believe it is contagious — that you can catch epilepsy by touching someone who is having a seizure. Others believe it is caused by a curse that can only be lifted by a traditional healer. These beliefs, while deeply held, are completely wrong. Epilepsy is a medical condition caused by unusual electrical activity in the brain. It is not a curse, it is not contagious, and it is not a punishment for anything you or your family did. It can happen to anyone, regardless of age, gender, or background, and it is treatable with medication that is available at government hospitals and NHIF/SHA-registered facilities across Kenya.\n\nThe emotional toll\n\nLiving with this kind of stigma takes a real toll on your mental health. Many people with epilepsy in Kenya report feeling anxious, depressed, or hopeless — not because of the seizures, but because of the social isolation that surrounds them. You might start avoiding social situations to prevent the embarrassment of having a seizure in public. You might hide your medication so people do not ask questions. You might pretend to be fine when you are struggling, because you have learned that showing vulnerability leads to judgment rather than support. This kind of emotional suppression is exhausting, and it can make your epilepsy worse — stress and poor mental health are known seizure triggers.\n\nYou are not alone\n\nIt can feel like nobody understands what you are going through, but there are thousands of Kenyans living with epilepsy right now who share your experience. The Kenya Association for the Welfare of People with Epilepsy and other support organisations connect people with the condition across the country. Online communities and WhatsApp groups allow you to share your story, ask questions, and find encouragement from people who truly understand. Some county hospitals and private facilities like Aga Khan, Nairobi Hospital, and Gertrude''s Children''s Hospital run epilepsy clinics where support groups meet regularly. Ask your neurologist or clinical officer if there is a group near you.\n\nCoping strategies that help\n\nFirst, educate yourself about your condition. Understanding epilepsy takes away much of the fear and shame. When you know the science behind seizures, the myths lose their power over you. Second, choose carefully who you tell — you do not owe everyone your medical history, but having a few trusted people who know about your condition and can support you is important. Third, practice self-compassion. On days when stigma feels heavy, remind yourself that the problem is not you — it is ignorance. You are living with a medical condition and managing it with courage. Fourth, stay active and engaged. Do not let stigma shrink your world. Go to work, go to church, visit your friends, play with your children. The more you participate in life, the harder it is for isolation to take hold.\n\nWhen to seek professional support\n\nIf you are feeling persistently sad, anxious, or hopeless — if you have lost interest in things you used to enjoy, if you are withdrawing from people, or if you are having thoughts of harming yourself — please talk to a mental health professional. Many county hospitals now have mental health units, and organisations like Befrienders Kenya offer free and confidential support. Counselling is not a sign of weakness — it is a practical tool for managing the emotional side of a chronic condition. Some facilities charge as little as KES 200-500 for a counselling session, and SHA may cover some of these costs.\n\nYou are worthy\n\nEpilepsy does not define you. It is something you live with, not something you are. You are still a parent, a friend, a colleague, a neighbour — a person with dreams, gifts, and value. The stigma you face says everything about the ignorance of others and nothing about your worth. Keep showing up. Keep taking your medication. Keep living your life. And know that with every day you do so, you are proving that epilepsy cannot hold you back.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Living with epilepsy in Kenya can be deeply lonely. Not because of the seizures themselves, but because of how other people respond to them. In many communities across the country — from Nairobi estates to rural villages in Western Kenya and the Coast — epilepsy is still surrounded by fear, misunderstanding, and stigma that can make you feel like you are carrying an invisible burden that nobody wants to acknowledge."},{"id":"s2","title":"What stigma looks like in everyday life","body":"Stigma is not always dramatic. Sometimes it is quiet and subtle. It is the friend who stops inviting you out because they are afraid you will have a seizure in public. It is the employer who finds a reason not to hire you after learning about your condition. It is the whisper at a family gathering — someone saying your epilepsy is a curse or the result of witchcraft. It is the parent who warns their children to stay away from you. It is the person on the matatu who moves to another seat when they see you taking your medication. These moments accumulate, and over time they can erode your confidence, your sense of belonging, and your mental health."},{"id":"s3","title":"The weight of being misunderstood","body":"In some Kenyan communities, epilepsy is still called by names that associate it with spiritual possession or punishment. Some people believe it is contagious — that you can catch epilepsy by touching someone who is having a seizure. Others believe it is caused by a curse that can only be lifted by a traditional healer. These beliefs, while deeply held, are completely wrong. Epilepsy is a medical condition caused by unusual electrical activity in the brain. It is not a curse, it is not contagious, and it is not a punishment for anything you or your family did. It can happen to anyone, regardless of age, gender, or background, and it is treatable with medication that is available at government hospitals and NHIF/SHA-registered facilities across Kenya."},{"id":"s4","title":"The emotional toll","body":"Living with this kind of stigma takes a real toll on your mental health. Many people with epilepsy in Kenya report feeling anxious, depressed, or hopeless — not because of the seizures, but because of the social isolation that surrounds them. You might start avoiding social situations to prevent the embarrassment of having a seizure in public. You might hide your medication so people do not ask questions. You might pretend to be fine when you are struggling, because you have learned that showing vulnerability leads to judgment rather than support. This kind of emotional suppression is exhausting, and it can make your epilepsy worse — stress and poor mental health are known seizure triggers."},{"id":"s5","title":"You are not alone","body":"It can feel like nobody understands what you are going through, but there are thousands of Kenyans living with epilepsy right now who share your experience. The Kenya Association for the Welfare of People with Epilepsy and other support organisations connect people with the condition across the country. Online communities and WhatsApp groups allow you to share your story, ask questions, and find encouragement from people who truly understand. Some county hospitals and private facilities like Aga Khan, Nairobi Hospital, and Gertrude''s Children''s Hospital run epilepsy clinics where support groups meet regularly. Ask your neurologist or clinical officer if there is a group near you."},{"id":"s6","title":"Coping strategies that help","body":"First, educate yourself about your condition. Understanding epilepsy takes away much of the fear and shame. When you know the science behind seizures, the myths lose their power over you. Second, choose carefully who you tell — you do not owe everyone your medical history, but having a few trusted people who know about your condition and can support you is important. Third, practice self-compassion. On days when stigma feels heavy, remind yourself that the problem is not you — it is ignorance. You are living with a medical condition and managing it with courage. Fourth, stay active and engaged. Do not let stigma shrink your world. Go to work, go to church, visit your friends, play with your children. The more you participate in life, the harder it is for isolation to take hold."},{"id":"s7","title":"When to seek professional support","body":"If you are feeling persistently sad, anxious, or hopeless — if you have lost interest in things you used to enjoy, if you are withdrawing from people, or if you are having thoughts of harming yourself — please talk to a mental health professional. Many county hospitals now have mental health units, and organisations like Befrienders Kenya offer free and confidential support. Counselling is not a sign of weakness — it is a practical tool for managing the emotional side of a chronic condition. Some facilities charge as little as KES 200-500 for a counselling session, and SHA may cover some of these costs."},{"id":"s8","title":"You are worthy","body":"Epilepsy does not define you. It is something you live with, not something you are. You are still a parent, a friend, a colleague, a neighbour — a person with dreams, gifts, and value. The stigma you face says everything about the ignorance of others and nothing about your worth. Keep showing up. Keep taking your medication. Keep living your life. And know that with every day you do so, you are proving that epilepsy cannot hold you back."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'family-support'),
  (E'keeping-a-seizure-diary-that-helps-your-doctor-help-you', E'Keeping a seizure diary that helps your doctor help you', E'EPILEPSY', '{"EPILEPSY"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'A seizure diary is one of the most powerful tools you can bring to your doctor''s appointment. It turns scattered memories into clear patterns that help your neurologist make better decisions about your medication and dosing.', E'A seizure diary is one of the most powerful tools you can bring to your doctor''s appointment. It turns scattered memories into clear patterns that help your neurologist make better decisions about your medication and dosing. You do not need a fancy notebook or an expensive app. A simple exercise book costing KES 50 from any bookshop will do. What matters is that you write things down consistently, every single time.\n\nWhat to record after every seizure\n\nThe moment you recover from a seizure, or as soon as someone who witnessed it can help you, write down these details. The date and exact time it started. Where you were and what you were doing — were you at home cooking ugali, walking to the market, sitting in a matatu, or at work? How long the seizure lasted — ask whoever was with you to time it on their phone. What type of seizure it was — did your whole body shake, or was it just one arm or one side of your face? Did you lose consciousness completely, or were you aware but unable to move? How you felt afterwards — confused, tired, sore muscles, headache, or back to normal quickly. Every detail matters to your doctor.\n\nTracking what happened before the seizure\n\nThe hours leading up to a seizure often hold important clues. Write down what you ate and drank. Did you have your usual breakfast of tea and chapati, or did you skip meals? Did you drink alcohol the night before? Record how much sleep you got — did you stay up late or get a full seven to eight hours? Note your stress level honestly. Were you worried about school fees, a family argument, or pressure at work? For women, track your menstrual cycle as well. Many women with epilepsy find that seizures cluster around their period due to hormonal changes. This pattern can be managed with specific medication adjustments if your doctor knows about it.\n\nRecording auras and warning signs\n\nMany people experience warning signs called auras before a seizure. These might include a strange smell nobody else notices, a sudden feeling of déjà vu, tingling in your hands or face, a rising feeling in your stomach, or unusual anxiety from nowhere. Not everyone gets auras, and that is normal. But if you do experience them, recording their exact nature helps your neurologist pinpoint where the seizure activity starts in your brain.\n\nTracking medication and side effects\n\nYour diary should note your medication schedule — the name, dose, and time you take each tablet. If you missed a dose or took it late, record that honestly. There is no judgement, only information. Also note side effects like drowsiness, dizziness, weight changes, or difficulty concentrating. Your neurologist needs to balance seizure control with your quality of life.\n\nHow patterns emerge over time\n\nAfter four to six weeks, sit down and look for patterns. You might discover seizures cluster on Monday mornings after a weekend of poor sleep. Or that they happen in the afternoon when you skip lunch. Perhaps they increase during stressful weeks at work. These patterns are gold for your neurologist, who can adjust medication timing accordingly — a higher evening dose if seizures happen in the morning, or extra medication around your period if that is the pattern.\n\nWhen to call your doctor immediately\n\nSome diary entries should trigger an immediate call. Contact your neurologist or go to the nearest hospital if you have more than two seizures in twenty-four hours, if a seizure lasts longer than five minutes, if you do not regain consciousness between seizures, or if you injure yourself during an episode. At Kenyatta National Hospital, the neurology department can be reached during working hours, and casualty operates around the clock. Keep your hospital''s emergency number saved in your phone.\n\nMaking your diary work at appointments\n\nBring your diary to every visit. Doctors at public hospitals like KNH or Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital often have limited time per patient, so coming prepared helps you make the most of each consultation. If your SHA or NHIF cover includes neurology visits, your diary ensures each one is productive. This diary is a conversation between you and your doctor that happens between appointments, capturing details memory alone cannot preserve.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"A seizure diary is one of the most powerful tools you can bring to your doctor''s appointment. It turns scattered memories into clear patterns that help your neurologist make better decisions about your medication and dosing. You do not need a fancy notebook or an expensive app. A simple exercise book costing KES 50 from any bookshop will do. What matters is that you write things down consistently, every single time."},{"id":"s2","title":"What to record after every seizure","body":"The moment you recover from a seizure, or as soon as someone who witnessed it can help you, write down these details. The date and exact time it started. Where you were and what you were doing — were you at home cooking ugali, walking to the market, sitting in a matatu, or at work? How long the seizure lasted — ask whoever was with you to time it on their phone. What type of seizure it was — did your whole body shake, or was it just one arm or one side of your face? Did you lose consciousness completely, or were you aware but unable to move? How you felt afterwards — confused, tired, sore muscles, headache, or back to normal quickly. Every detail matters to your doctor."},{"id":"s3","title":"Tracking what happened before the seizure","body":"The hours leading up to a seizure often hold important clues. Write down what you ate and drank. Did you have your usual breakfast of tea and chapati, or did you skip meals? Did you drink alcohol the night before? Record how much sleep you got — did you stay up late or get a full seven to eight hours? Note your stress level honestly. Were you worried about school fees, a family argument, or pressure at work? For women, track your menstrual cycle as well. Many women with epilepsy find that seizures cluster around their period due to hormonal changes. This pattern can be managed with specific medication adjustments if your doctor knows about it."},{"id":"s4","title":"Recording auras and warning signs","body":"Many people experience warning signs called auras before a seizure. These might include a strange smell nobody else notices, a sudden feeling of déjà vu, tingling in your hands or face, a rising feeling in your stomach, or unusual anxiety from nowhere. Not everyone gets auras, and that is normal. But if you do experience them, recording their exact nature helps your neurologist pinpoint where the seizure activity starts in your brain."},{"id":"s5","title":"Tracking medication and side effects","body":"Your diary should note your medication schedule — the name, dose, and time you take each tablet. If you missed a dose or took it late, record that honestly. There is no judgement, only information. Also note side effects like drowsiness, dizziness, weight changes, or difficulty concentrating. Your neurologist needs to balance seizure control with your quality of life."},{"id":"s6","title":"How patterns emerge over time","body":"After four to six weeks, sit down and look for patterns. You might discover seizures cluster on Monday mornings after a weekend of poor sleep. Or that they happen in the afternoon when you skip lunch. Perhaps they increase during stressful weeks at work. These patterns are gold for your neurologist, who can adjust medication timing accordingly — a higher evening dose if seizures happen in the morning, or extra medication around your period if that is the pattern."},{"id":"s7","title":"When to call your doctor immediately","body":"Some diary entries should trigger an immediate call. Contact your neurologist or go to the nearest hospital if you have more than two seizures in twenty-four hours, if a seizure lasts longer than five minutes, if you do not regain consciousness between seizures, or if you injure yourself during an episode. At Kenyatta National Hospital, the neurology department can be reached during working hours, and casualty operates around the clock. Keep your hospital''s emergency number saved in your phone."},{"id":"s8","title":"Making your diary work at appointments","body":"Bring your diary to every visit. Doctors at public hospitals like KNH or Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital often have limited time per patient, so coming prepared helps you make the most of each consultation. If your SHA or NHIF cover includes neurology visits, your diary ensures each one is productive. This diary is a conversation between you and your doctor that happens between appointments, capturing details memory alone cannot preserve."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'glucose-monitor'),
  (E'epilepsy-takes-but-also-gives-perspective', E'Epilepsy takes but also gives perspective', E'EPILEPSY', '{"EPILEPSY"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'Living with epilepsy in Kenya comes with challenges that go beyond the seizures themselves. The daily medication routines, the worry about when the next episode might happen, the explanations to colleagues and neighbours, the quiet adjustments nobody else notices.', E'Living with epilepsy in Kenya comes with challenges that go beyond the seizures themselves. The daily medication routines, the worry about when the next episode might happen, the explanations to colleagues and neighbours, the quiet adjustments nobody else notices. Acceptance does not mean you are happy about having epilepsy. It means you have decided that epilepsy is one part of your life, not the whole of it, and you are going to live fully despite it.\n\nWhat epilepsy takes from you\n\nLet us be honest about what this condition costs. It may have taken some independence — perhaps you cannot drive, or your family insists someone always accompanies you. It may have cost you job opportunities when employers made assumptions about your capability. It might have changed your social life, making you cautious about late nights, crowded events, or travelling far from home. Some people with epilepsy in Kenya have faced broken relationships or been excluded from family gatherings because of fear and misunderstanding. These losses are real and you have every right to grieve them.\n\nWhat epilepsy gives back\n\nHere is what many people discover over time. The condition teaches you things that healthy people often never learn. You understand the value of routine — your consistent sleep schedule, your regular meals of githeri or ugali with sukuma wiki at the same times each day, your disciplined medication adherence. These habits that epilepsy demands actually make you healthier in ways that extend far beyond seizure control. You learn to read your body with extraordinary sensitivity. You notice when you are overtired or overstressed, and you take action before your body forces you to stop.\n\nBuilding daily routines that support you\n\nPractical acceptance means building a life that works with your condition rather than against it. Set a consistent wake-up time and bedtime, even on weekends. Take your medication at the same times every day — tie it to something you already do, like your morning tea or evening meal. Keep a water bottle with you because dehydration can lower your seizure threshold. Plan meals so you never go long stretches without eating. A mid-morning snack of groundnuts and a banana, or an afternoon chapati with tea, keeps blood sugar steady. These routines become second nature, and the stability they create gives you freedom to focus on everything else.\n\nNavigating work and social life\n\nYou get to decide who knows about your epilepsy and when. You are not obligated to disclose to everyone, but it is wise to tell people you spend significant time with — a close colleague, your regular boda boda rider, or your closest friends. Give them simple instructions: lay me on my side, cushion my head, do not put anything in my mouth, time the seizure, and call for help if it lasts more than five minutes. At work, the Persons with Disabilities Act in Kenya protects you from discrimination based on your health condition.\n\nHandling the spiritual dimension\n\nIn many Kenyan communities, epilepsy is still attributed to spiritual causes — curses, demonic possession, or witchcraft. Family members may want you to see a traditional healer instead of taking medication. Faith and medicine are not enemies. Your doctor treats the electrical activity in your brain. Your faith community supports your spirit. Both matter. What you must not do is stop medication without your neurologist''s guidance.\n\nFinding your community\n\nAcceptance becomes easier when you are not alone. Support groups and WhatsApp communities connect you with people who truly understand. When you meet someone who knows what it feels like to wake up on the floor confused or to have a seizure in public, the loneliness lifts in a way that no reassurance from well-meaning friends can match.\n\nAcceptance is not a single moment\n\nThere will be setbacks — a breakthrough seizure after months of stability, a new medication side effect, or a hurtful comment from someone who does not understand. On those days, be gentle with yourself. Tomorrow you will pick up your routines again. You will take your medication, eat your meals, get your sleep, and keep building the life you want. Epilepsy is part of your story, but you are the one holding the pen.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Living with epilepsy in Kenya comes with challenges that go beyond the seizures themselves. The daily medication routines, the worry about when the next episode might happen, the explanations to colleagues and neighbours, the quiet adjustments nobody else notices. Acceptance does not mean you are happy about having epilepsy. It means you have decided that epilepsy is one part of your life, not the whole of it, and you are going to live fully despite it."},{"id":"s2","title":"What epilepsy takes from you","body":"Let us be honest about what this condition costs. It may have taken some independence — perhaps you cannot drive, or your family insists someone always accompanies you. It may have cost you job opportunities when employers made assumptions about your capability. It might have changed your social life, making you cautious about late nights, crowded events, or travelling far from home. Some people with epilepsy in Kenya have faced broken relationships or been excluded from family gatherings because of fear and misunderstanding. These losses are real and you have every right to grieve them."},{"id":"s3","title":"What epilepsy gives back","body":"Here is what many people discover over time. The condition teaches you things that healthy people often never learn. You understand the value of routine — your consistent sleep schedule, your regular meals of githeri or ugali with sukuma wiki at the same times each day, your disciplined medication adherence. These habits that epilepsy demands actually make you healthier in ways that extend far beyond seizure control. You learn to read your body with extraordinary sensitivity. You notice when you are overtired or overstressed, and you take action before your body forces you to stop."},{"id":"s4","title":"Building daily routines that support you","body":"Practical acceptance means building a life that works with your condition rather than against it. Set a consistent wake-up time and bedtime, even on weekends. Take your medication at the same times every day — tie it to something you already do, like your morning tea or evening meal. Keep a water bottle with you because dehydration can lower your seizure threshold. Plan meals so you never go long stretches without eating. A mid-morning snack of groundnuts and a banana, or an afternoon chapati with tea, keeps blood sugar steady. These routines become second nature, and the stability they create gives you freedom to focus on everything else."},{"id":"s5","title":"Navigating work and social life","body":"You get to decide who knows about your epilepsy and when. You are not obligated to disclose to everyone, but it is wise to tell people you spend significant time with — a close colleague, your regular boda boda rider, or your closest friends. Give them simple instructions: lay me on my side, cushion my head, do not put anything in my mouth, time the seizure, and call for help if it lasts more than five minutes. At work, the Persons with Disabilities Act in Kenya protects you from discrimination based on your health condition."},{"id":"s6","title":"Handling the spiritual dimension","body":"In many Kenyan communities, epilepsy is still attributed to spiritual causes — curses, demonic possession, or witchcraft. Family members may want you to see a traditional healer instead of taking medication. Faith and medicine are not enemies. Your doctor treats the electrical activity in your brain. Your faith community supports your spirit. Both matter. What you must not do is stop medication without your neurologist''s guidance."},{"id":"s7","title":"Finding your community","body":"Acceptance becomes easier when you are not alone. Support groups and WhatsApp communities connect you with people who truly understand. When you meet someone who knows what it feels like to wake up on the floor confused or to have a seizure in public, the loneliness lifts in a way that no reassurance from well-meaning friends can match."},{"id":"s8","title":"Acceptance is not a single moment","body":"There will be setbacks — a breakthrough seizure after months of stability, a new medication side effect, or a hurtful comment from someone who does not understand. On those days, be gentle with yourself. Tomorrow you will pick up your routines again. You will take your medication, eat your meals, get your sleep, and keep building the life you want. Epilepsy is part of your story, but you are the one holding the pen."}]'::jsonb, 4, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'journaling'),
  (E'three-months-seizure-free', E'Three months seizure-free', E'EPILEPSY', '{"EPILEPSY"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Three consecutive months without a seizure. Ninety days of waking up each morning and going to bed each night without an episode.', E'Three consecutive months without a seizure. Ninety days of waking up each morning and going to bed each night without an episode. This is a significant medical achievement that reflects your daily discipline, your commitment to your treatment plan, and your courage in managing a condition that demands constant vigilance. Take a moment to recognise what you have accomplished.\n\nWhat three months seizure-free actually means\n\nWhen your neurologist sees three months of seizure freedom, it tells them several important things. Your current medication is likely at the right dose. The anticonvulsant you are taking — whether carbamazepine, sodium valproate, lamotrigine, or another drug — is stabilising the electrical activity in your brain. Your lifestyle management is working too. The regular sleep schedule, the meals you have not skipped, the alcohol you have limited, and the stress management strategies you have used all contribute to this stability. Three months suggests a genuine pattern rather than a lucky streak.\n\nThe medication behind your success\n\nThis milestone exists because you took your medication faithfully every single day for ninety days. You did not skip doses when you felt fine and wondered if you really needed the tablets. You did not stop because of side effects without talking to your doctor first. You found a routine — perhaps taking your morning dose with breakfast tea and your evening dose with dinner. You made sure you had enough tablets and did not run out over weekends or holidays. If you buy your medication from a Kenyan pharmacy, you know the discipline this takes. Anticonvulsants can cost between KES 500 and KES 2,000 per month depending on brand and dosage. If your SHA or NHIF cover includes your medication, you have navigated the system to ensure uninterrupted access. That is no small feat.\n\nWhat this means for your daily life\n\nThree seizure-free months may already be changing your experience. You might be sleeping more peacefully, without the anxiety of a nocturnal seizure. Your confidence may be growing — perhaps you accepted a social invitation you would have declined six months ago, or started a new project at work. The constant low-level fear that follows many people with epilepsy may be loosening its grip. Your family members who worry about you are breathing easier too.\n\nDriving and independence in Kenya\n\nThe general medical guidance is that you should be seizure-free for six to twelve months before driving. Three months is an important step toward that goal. Discuss the timeline with your neurologist. Even before reaching the driving threshold, three seizure-free months may mean your family is more comfortable with you travelling independently by matatu or boda boda, restoring a sense of freedom that seizures may have taken away.\n\nCelebrating wisely\n\nYou deserve to celebrate in a way that supports your continued success. Treat yourself to something you enjoy — a meal at your favourite restaurant, a new book, a day trip, or a special meal at home. Cook a celebratory pilau or nyama choma with your family. Share the news with your support system. Your neurologist and the nurses who supported you through medication adjustments will be genuinely happy to hear about your progress. Just be mindful that celebration does not mean abandoning the routines that got you here — keep your sleep schedule, take your medication on time, and avoid excessive alcohol.\n\nWhat comes next\n\nThree months is a milestone, not a finish line. Your next goal is six months, then a year. With each passing month of seizure freedom, the likelihood of continued freedom increases. At your next appointment — whether at KNH, a county referral hospital, or a private neurologist — bring your seizure diary showing three clear months and discuss what comes next. Do not make any medication changes on your own based on feeling well. The stability you experience exists because of your medication, not in spite of it.\n\nYou are proving something important\n\nEvery seizure-free day is proof that epilepsy can be managed. You are living evidence that the right medication, taken consistently, combined with healthy lifestyle choices, works. Your success can encourage others who are newly diagnosed and frightened. It is worth it. You are worth it. Keep going.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Three consecutive months without a seizure. Ninety days of waking up each morning and going to bed each night without an episode. This is a significant medical achievement that reflects your daily discipline, your commitment to your treatment plan, and your courage in managing a condition that demands constant vigilance. Take a moment to recognise what you have accomplished."},{"id":"s2","title":"What three months seizure-free actually means","body":"When your neurologist sees three months of seizure freedom, it tells them several important things. Your current medication is likely at the right dose. The anticonvulsant you are taking — whether carbamazepine, sodium valproate, lamotrigine, or another drug — is stabilising the electrical activity in your brain. Your lifestyle management is working too. The regular sleep schedule, the meals you have not skipped, the alcohol you have limited, and the stress management strategies you have used all contribute to this stability. Three months suggests a genuine pattern rather than a lucky streak."},{"id":"s3","title":"The medication behind your success","body":"This milestone exists because you took your medication faithfully every single day for ninety days. You did not skip doses when you felt fine and wondered if you really needed the tablets. You did not stop because of side effects without talking to your doctor first. You found a routine — perhaps taking your morning dose with breakfast tea and your evening dose with dinner. You made sure you had enough tablets and did not run out over weekends or holidays. If you buy your medication from a Kenyan pharmacy, you know the discipline this takes. Anticonvulsants can cost between KES 500 and KES 2,000 per month depending on brand and dosage. If your SHA or NHIF cover includes your medication, you have navigated the system to ensure uninterrupted access. That is no small feat."},{"id":"s4","title":"What this means for your daily life","body":"Three seizure-free months may already be changing your experience. You might be sleeping more peacefully, without the anxiety of a nocturnal seizure. Your confidence may be growing — perhaps you accepted a social invitation you would have declined six months ago, or started a new project at work. The constant low-level fear that follows many people with epilepsy may be loosening its grip. Your family members who worry about you are breathing easier too."},{"id":"s5","title":"Driving and independence in Kenya","body":"The general medical guidance is that you should be seizure-free for six to twelve months before driving. Three months is an important step toward that goal. Discuss the timeline with your neurologist. Even before reaching the driving threshold, three seizure-free months may mean your family is more comfortable with you travelling independently by matatu or boda boda, restoring a sense of freedom that seizures may have taken away."},{"id":"s6","title":"Celebrating wisely","body":"You deserve to celebrate in a way that supports your continued success. Treat yourself to something you enjoy — a meal at your favourite restaurant, a new book, a day trip, or a special meal at home. Cook a celebratory pilau or nyama choma with your family. Share the news with your support system. Your neurologist and the nurses who supported you through medication adjustments will be genuinely happy to hear about your progress. Just be mindful that celebration does not mean abandoning the routines that got you here — keep your sleep schedule, take your medication on time, and avoid excessive alcohol."},{"id":"s7","title":"What comes next","body":"Three months is a milestone, not a finish line. Your next goal is six months, then a year. With each passing month of seizure freedom, the likelihood of continued freedom increases. At your next appointment — whether at KNH, a county referral hospital, or a private neurologist — bring your seizure diary showing three clear months and discuss what comes next. Do not make any medication changes on your own based on feeling well. The stability you experience exists because of your medication, not in spite of it."},{"id":"s8","title":"You are proving something important","body":"Every seizure-free day is proof that epilepsy can be managed. You are living evidence that the right medication, taken consistently, combined with healthy lifestyle choices, works. Your success can encourage others who are newly diagnosed and frightened. It is worth it. You are worth it. Keep going."}]'::jsonb, 4, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'wellness-journey'),
  (E'why-eating-less-but-more-often-helps-you-breathe-easier', E'Why eating less but more often helps you breathe easier', E'COPD', '{"COPD"}', E'DIETARY', E'When you have COPD, something as simple as eating a large meal can leave you struggling to breathe. A full stomach physically pushes upward against your diaphragm, the main muscle you use for breathing, compressing your already-challenged lungs and making each breath shallower.', E'When you have COPD, something as simple as eating a large meal can leave you struggling to breathe. A full stomach physically pushes upward against your diaphragm, the main muscle you use for breathing, compressing your already-challenged lungs and making each breath shallower. The good news is that a simple change — smaller amounts more frequently — can make a real difference in how comfortably you breathe throughout the day.\n\nWhy smaller meals help your breathing\n\nYour diaphragm sits just below your lungs, separating your chest from your abdomen. When your stomach is full after a big plate of ugali and beef stew, it pushes the diaphragm upward, leaving less room for your lungs to expand. For healthy lungs, this causes mild discomfort. But with COPD, that extra pressure triggers significant breathlessness. Eating five or six smaller meals instead of three large ones keeps your stomach from getting so full that it compromises your breathing. Think of it as giving your lungs room to work.\n\nPlanning your six small meals\n\nHere is what a day of lung-friendly meals might look like using foods from any Kenyan market. Early breakfast around 7am: a cup of tea with milk and one slice of whole wheat bread with half an avocado. Mid-morning around 10am: a small bowl of uji made with millet flour, or groundnuts with a banana. Lunch around 12:30pm: a small portion of ugali — about the size of your fist — with sukuma wiki cooked with tomatoes and a piece of fish or two boiled eggs. Mid-afternoon around 3pm: a cup of yoghurt with diced mango or watermelon. Dinner around 6:30pm: a small serving of githeri with stew, or a light chapati with lentil soup. Before bed: a small glass of warm milk if hungry. No single meal should leave you feeling stuffed.\n\nFoods that give you the most nutrition per bite\n\nWith smaller portions, every bite needs to count. Eggs are excellent — affordable at about KES 15 to 20 each and full of protein. Avocados are rich in healthy fats and calories. Groundnuts give you protein and energy in a small handful. Well-cooked beans and lentils — ndengu, kidney beans — provide both protein and iron. Full-fat yoghurt from any supermarket or duka gives you protein and calcium. Omena are packed with protein and calcium. Sweet potatoes and arrowroot provide steady energy. Cook your sukuma wiki or managu with cooking oil to increase calories and help your body absorb fat-soluble vitamins.\n\nFoods to be cautious with\n\nSome foods produce extra gas that creates bloating and puts more pressure on your diaphragm. Raw cabbage is a common culprit — cook it thoroughly rather than eating it raw. Other gas producers include raw onions, broccoli, and beans that have not been soaked long enough. Soak your beans overnight and cook until completely soft. Avoid carbonated drinks entirely — sodas, sparkling water, and beer introduce gas directly into your stomach. Drink still water, plain tea, or fresh juice without fizz.\n\nShopping smart at the market\n\nPlan purchases around small, frequent meals. Buy smaller quantities more often rather than large amounts that tempt you to cook big meals. Ask your vegetable vendor for sukuma wiki, spinach, and tomatoes to last two to three days. Buy eggs by the half-tray. Many market vendors in Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu, and other towns are happy to sell small quantities.\n\nEating out and at family gatherings\n\nKenyan social life revolves around food, and large portions signal hospitality. When visiting family or eating at a restaurant, explain you are eating smaller amounts for your health. Ask for a smaller serving of pilau or nyama choma. At a hotel, request a half portion or share a plate. At gatherings, serve yourself a small amount and return for a second helping later rather than loading up your plate.\n\nThe cost advantage\n\nEating smaller meals more frequently does not cost more. It can actually cost less because you buy only what you need and waste less food. A day of small meals built around ugali, sukuma wiki, eggs, groundnuts, bananas, and yoghurt might cost between KES 200 and KES 400, comparable to three large meals. Your lungs will thank you for every meal that ends with comfortable breathing instead of gasping.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you have COPD, something as simple as eating a large meal can leave you struggling to breathe. A full stomach physically pushes upward against your diaphragm, the main muscle you use for breathing, compressing your already-challenged lungs and making each breath shallower. The good news is that a simple change — smaller amounts more frequently — can make a real difference in how comfortably you breathe throughout the day."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why smaller meals help your breathing","body":"Your diaphragm sits just below your lungs, separating your chest from your abdomen. When your stomach is full after a big plate of ugali and beef stew, it pushes the diaphragm upward, leaving less room for your lungs to expand. For healthy lungs, this causes mild discomfort. But with COPD, that extra pressure triggers significant breathlessness. Eating five or six smaller meals instead of three large ones keeps your stomach from getting so full that it compromises your breathing. Think of it as giving your lungs room to work."},{"id":"s3","title":"Planning your six small meals","body":"Here is what a day of lung-friendly meals might look like using foods from any Kenyan market. Early breakfast around 7am: a cup of tea with milk and one slice of whole wheat bread with half an avocado. Mid-morning around 10am: a small bowl of uji made with millet flour, or groundnuts with a banana. Lunch around 12:30pm: a small portion of ugali — about the size of your fist — with sukuma wiki cooked with tomatoes and a piece of fish or two boiled eggs. Mid-afternoon around 3pm: a cup of yoghurt with diced mango or watermelon. Dinner around 6:30pm: a small serving of githeri with stew, or a light chapati with lentil soup. Before bed: a small glass of warm milk if hungry. No single meal should leave you feeling stuffed."},{"id":"s4","title":"Foods that give you the most nutrition per bite","body":"With smaller portions, every bite needs to count. Eggs are excellent — affordable at about KES 15 to 20 each and full of protein. Avocados are rich in healthy fats and calories. Groundnuts give you protein and energy in a small handful. Well-cooked beans and lentils — ndengu, kidney beans — provide both protein and iron. Full-fat yoghurt from any supermarket or duka gives you protein and calcium. Omena are packed with protein and calcium. Sweet potatoes and arrowroot provide steady energy. Cook your sukuma wiki or managu with cooking oil to increase calories and help your body absorb fat-soluble vitamins."},{"id":"s5","title":"Foods to be cautious with","body":"Some foods produce extra gas that creates bloating and puts more pressure on your diaphragm. Raw cabbage is a common culprit — cook it thoroughly rather than eating it raw. Other gas producers include raw onions, broccoli, and beans that have not been soaked long enough. Soak your beans overnight and cook until completely soft. Avoid carbonated drinks entirely — sodas, sparkling water, and beer introduce gas directly into your stomach. Drink still water, plain tea, or fresh juice without fizz."},{"id":"s6","title":"Shopping smart at the market","body":"Plan purchases around small, frequent meals. Buy smaller quantities more often rather than large amounts that tempt you to cook big meals. Ask your vegetable vendor for sukuma wiki, spinach, and tomatoes to last two to three days. Buy eggs by the half-tray. Many market vendors in Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu, and other towns are happy to sell small quantities."},{"id":"s7","title":"Eating out and at family gatherings","body":"Kenyan social life revolves around food, and large portions signal hospitality. When visiting family or eating at a restaurant, explain you are eating smaller amounts for your health. Ask for a smaller serving of pilau or nyama choma. At a hotel, request a half portion or share a plate. At gatherings, serve yourself a small amount and return for a second helping later rather than loading up your plate."},{"id":"s8","title":"The cost advantage","body":"Eating smaller meals more frequently does not cost more. It can actually cost less because you buy only what you need and waste less food. A day of small meals built around ugali, sukuma wiki, eggs, groundnuts, bananas, and yoghurt might cost between KES 200 and KES 400, comparable to three large meals. Your lungs will thank you for every meal that ends with comfortable breathing instead of gasping."}]'::jsonb, 4, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'balanced-plate'),
  (E'pursed-lip-breathing-your-best-tool-for-breathlessness', E'Pursed-lip breathing: your best tool for breathlessness', E'COPD', '{"COPD"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Pursed-lip breathing is a simple technique that can transform how you manage breathlessness from COPD. It costs nothing, requires no equipment, and can be done anywhere — at home, in a matatu, at the market, during a church service.', E'Pursed-lip breathing is a simple technique that can transform how you manage breathlessness from COPD. It costs nothing, requires no equipment, and can be done anywhere — at home, in a matatu, at the market, during a church service. Doctors and respiratory therapists worldwide teach this as a first-line tool because it genuinely helps your lungs work more efficiently.\n\nWhy pursed-lip breathing works\n\nWith COPD, your airways are narrowed and damaged, making it hard to push stale air out when you exhale. This trapped air takes up space that fresh, oxygen-rich air needs. You feel like you cannot get a full breath because your lungs are overfilled with old air. Pursed-lip breathing creates a slight back-pressure when you exhale through nearly-closed lips. This props your airways open longer, allowing more trapped air to escape. Your next inhale then brings in more fresh oxygen. It is like opening a second door in a crowded room — suddenly there is space to move.\n\nThe basic technique step by step\n\nFind a comfortable position — sitting, standing, or lying propped up on pillows. Relax your shoulders and neck. Do not hunch forward.\n\nStep one: Close your mouth and breathe in slowly through your nose for a count of two. Count in your head — one, two. A normal, gentle inhale is perfect. Breathing through your nose warms and filters the air before it reaches your lungs.\n\nStep two: Purse your lips as if about to whistle, or as if gently blowing on a cup of hot tea to cool it. Your lips should be almost closed, with just a small opening.\n\nStep three: Breathe out slowly through your pursed lips for a count of four. The exhale should take twice as long as the inhale. Do not force the air out. Let it flow gently, as if blowing through a thin straw. The slight resistance from your lips is exactly what keeps your airways open.\n\nStep four: Repeat. Breathe in through your nose for two counts, out through pursed lips for four counts. Continue for four to six cycles, about one to two minutes.\n\nYour daily practice schedule\n\nDo not wait until you are breathless to use this technique. Practice regularly so it becomes automatic. Aim for four to five sessions throughout the day. First thing in the morning while sitting on the edge of your bed, do six to eight breath cycles. Mid-morning during a tea break, do another set. After lunch, when digestion can make breathing feel tighter, practice again. Late afternoon and before bed, do your final sessions. Each session takes only two to three minutes. Within a week, the technique will feel natural.\n\nUsing pursed-lip breathing during activities\n\nThis is where the technique becomes truly powerful. When climbing stairs, breathe in while standing still on one step, then breathe out through pursed lips as you step up. When walking, breathe in for two steps and out for four steps. When bending to pick something up, breathe in before you bend and out as you straighten up. When carrying shopping from the market, stop every few metres if needed and do three or four pursed-lip breaths before continuing. There is no shame in stopping to breathe — you are managing your condition intelligently.\n\nAdvanced variations\n\nOnce comfortable, try extending your exhale — two counts in, six out, or even two in, eight out. The longer the exhale, the more stale air you empty. Go at your own pace and never push to dizziness. You can also add diaphragmatic breathing — place one hand on your chest and one on your belly, ensuring your belly rises when you inhale. This means you are using your diaphragm properly.\n\nSafety and when to seek help\n\nPursed-lip breathing is safe for virtually everyone with COPD. If you feel dizzy during practice, you are breathing too forcefully — slow down. If the technique no longer controls breathlessness during rest, this could signal a flare-up. Contact your doctor or visit the chest clinic at your nearest hospital. Keep your reliever inhaler with you always — pursed-lip breathing complements your medication, it does not replace it.\n\nA technique that gives you confidence\n\nMany COPD patients in Kenya say the fear of breathlessness is worse than the breathlessness itself. You avoid activities not because you cannot do them, but because you fear not being able to recover. Pursed-lip breathing gives you a reliable tool. When you know you can calm your breathing in any situation, you start saying yes again — a walk with a friend, a visit to family, a trip to the market. That confidence is priceless. Practice today.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Pursed-lip breathing is a simple technique that can transform how you manage breathlessness from COPD. It costs nothing, requires no equipment, and can be done anywhere — at home, in a matatu, at the market, during a church service. Doctors and respiratory therapists worldwide teach this as a first-line tool because it genuinely helps your lungs work more efficiently."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why pursed-lip breathing works","body":"With COPD, your airways are narrowed and damaged, making it hard to push stale air out when you exhale. This trapped air takes up space that fresh, oxygen-rich air needs. You feel like you cannot get a full breath because your lungs are overfilled with old air. Pursed-lip breathing creates a slight back-pressure when you exhale through nearly-closed lips. This props your airways open longer, allowing more trapped air to escape. Your next inhale then brings in more fresh oxygen. It is like opening a second door in a crowded room — suddenly there is space to move."},{"id":"s3","title":"The basic technique step by step","body":"Find a comfortable position — sitting, standing, or lying propped up on pillows. Relax your shoulders and neck. Do not hunch forward.\\n\\nStep one: Close your mouth and breathe in slowly through your nose for a count of two. Count in your head — one, two. A normal, gentle inhale is perfect. Breathing through your nose warms and filters the air before it reaches your lungs.\\n\\nStep two: Purse your lips as if about to whistle, or as if gently blowing on a cup of hot tea to cool it. Your lips should be almost closed, with just a small opening.\\n\\nStep three: Breathe out slowly through your pursed lips for a count of four. The exhale should take twice as long as the inhale. Do not force the air out. Let it flow gently, as if blowing through a thin straw. The slight resistance from your lips is exactly what keeps your airways open.\\n\\nStep four: Repeat. Breathe in through your nose for two counts, out through pursed lips for four counts. Continue for four to six cycles, about one to two minutes."},{"id":"s4","title":"Your daily practice schedule","body":"Do not wait until you are breathless to use this technique. Practice regularly so it becomes automatic. Aim for four to five sessions throughout the day. First thing in the morning while sitting on the edge of your bed, do six to eight breath cycles. Mid-morning during a tea break, do another set. After lunch, when digestion can make breathing feel tighter, practice again. Late afternoon and before bed, do your final sessions. Each session takes only two to three minutes. Within a week, the technique will feel natural."},{"id":"s5","title":"Using pursed-lip breathing during activities","body":"This is where the technique becomes truly powerful. When climbing stairs, breathe in while standing still on one step, then breathe out through pursed lips as you step up. When walking, breathe in for two steps and out for four steps. When bending to pick something up, breathe in before you bend and out as you straighten up. When carrying shopping from the market, stop every few metres if needed and do three or four pursed-lip breaths before continuing. There is no shame in stopping to breathe — you are managing your condition intelligently."},{"id":"s6","title":"Advanced variations","body":"Once comfortable, try extending your exhale — two counts in, six out, or even two in, eight out. The longer the exhale, the more stale air you empty. Go at your own pace and never push to dizziness. You can also add diaphragmatic breathing — place one hand on your chest and one on your belly, ensuring your belly rises when you inhale. This means you are using your diaphragm properly."},{"id":"s7","title":"Safety and when to seek help","body":"Pursed-lip breathing is safe for virtually everyone with COPD. If you feel dizzy during practice, you are breathing too forcefully — slow down. If the technique no longer controls breathlessness during rest, this could signal a flare-up. Contact your doctor or visit the chest clinic at your nearest hospital. Keep your reliever inhaler with you always — pursed-lip breathing complements your medication, it does not replace it."},{"id":"s8","title":"A technique that gives you confidence","body":"Many COPD patients in Kenya say the fear of breathlessness is worse than the breathlessness itself. You avoid activities not because you cannot do them, but because you fear not being able to recover. Pursed-lip breathing gives you a reliable tool. When you know you can calm your breathing in any situation, you start saying yes again — a walk with a friend, a visit to family, a trip to the market. That confidence is priceless. Practice today."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'light-exercise'),
  (E'myth-if-the-damage-is-done-there-is-no-point-in-quitting-smoking', E'Myth: If the damage is done, there is no point in quitting smoking', E'COPD', '{"COPD"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'You may have heard someone say, or even thought yourself, that once your lungs are damaged from smoking there is no reason to quit.', E'The myth that many people believe\n\nYou may have heard someone say, or even thought yourself, that once your lungs are damaged from smoking there is no reason to quit. The thinking goes like this: the harm is already done, the lungs are already scarred, so why put yourself through the pain of quitting when it will not make a difference? This belief is incredibly common among smokers in Kenya and around the world, and it sounds logical on the surface. But it is completely, dangerously wrong.\n\nWhat the science actually shows\n\nResearch involving tens of thousands of COPD patients has proven that quitting smoking at any stage of the disease slows down the rate at which your lungs lose function. It is true that your lungs will not fully heal. The scarring and damage that has already occurred is permanent. But here is the critical part: when you continue smoking, your lung function drops steeply, like a matatu speeding downhill with no brakes. When you quit, that decline slows dramatically, more like a gentle slope. Within just twelve months of quitting, your risk of heart disease is already cut in half. Within two to five years, your risk of stroke drops to nearly the same level as someone who never smoked. Your body begins repairing what it can almost immediately. Within 48 hours of your last cigarette, your nerve endings start regrowing. Within three months, your circulation improves and your lung capacity increases. These are real, measurable changes that doctors can see in your test results.\n\nYour remaining lung tissue is precious\n\nThink of your lungs like a shamba. If part of the farm has been damaged by erosion, you would not set fire to the rest of it and say there is no point in protecting it. You would fence off the healthy portion, water it, care for it, and make the most of what you have. That is exactly what quitting smoking does for your lungs. Every cigarette you do not smoke is protecting the healthy tissue that remains. Every smoke-free day gives your airways a chance to reduce inflammation and produce less mucus, making it easier to breathe.\n\nWhy this myth is so harmful\n\nThis myth gives people permission to keep doing the one thing that is accelerating their illness. It turns a treatable, manageable condition into a fast track toward oxygen dependency, hospital admissions, and early death. In Kenyan hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital and Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, doctors see COPD patients every week who delayed quitting because they believed it would not help. Many of them wish they had stopped years earlier. The truth is that it is never too late. Whether you have had COPD for one year or ten years, quitting smoking today will improve your quality of life.\n\nGetting help to quit in Kenya\n\nQuitting is hard. Nicotine is genuinely addictive, and willpower alone is often not enough. Talk to your doctor about nicotine replacement therapy. Nicotine patches and gum are available at most Kenyan pharmacies including Goodlife Pharmacy and Haltons, typically costing between KES 800 and KES 2,500 per course. Some county hospitals offer free or subsidised smoking cessation support through their outpatient clinics. If your first attempt does not work, try again. Most successful quitters tried several times before they succeeded. You can also ask your doctor whether your SHA benefits cover any cessation support.\n\nThe people around you matter too\n\nSecondhand smoke worsens your COPD and harms the lungs of everyone in your household, especially children and elderly family members. Quitting protects them as well as yourself. Ask family members and friends not to smoke around you. If your social circle revolves around smoking, consider that this is one area where you may need to set firm boundaries for your own survival. You are not being difficult. You are choosing to breathe.\n\nThe bottom line\n\nThe damage already done is real. But the damage you prevent by quitting today is also real, and it is enormous. Do not let a myth rob you of years of better breathing, more energy, and more time with the people you love. Every single cigarette you do not smoke is a gift to your future self.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The myth that many people believe","body":"You may have heard someone say, or even thought yourself, that once your lungs are damaged from smoking there is no reason to quit. The thinking goes like this: the harm is already done, the lungs are already scarred, so why put yourself through the pain of quitting when it will not make a difference? This belief is incredibly common among smokers in Kenya and around the world, and it sounds logical on the surface. But it is completely, dangerously wrong."},{"id":"s2","title":"What the science actually shows","body":"Research involving tens of thousands of COPD patients has proven that quitting smoking at any stage of the disease slows down the rate at which your lungs lose function. It is true that your lungs will not fully heal. The scarring and damage that has already occurred is permanent. But here is the critical part: when you continue smoking, your lung function drops steeply, like a matatu speeding downhill with no brakes. When you quit, that decline slows dramatically, more like a gentle slope. Within just twelve months of quitting, your risk of heart disease is already cut in half. Within two to five years, your risk of stroke drops to nearly the same level as someone who never smoked. Your body begins repairing what it can almost immediately. Within 48 hours of your last cigarette, your nerve endings start regrowing. Within three months, your circulation improves and your lung capacity increases. These are real, measurable changes that doctors can see in your test results."},{"id":"s3","title":"Your remaining lung tissue is precious","body":"Think of your lungs like a shamba. If part of the farm has been damaged by erosion, you would not set fire to the rest of it and say there is no point in protecting it. You would fence off the healthy portion, water it, care for it, and make the most of what you have. That is exactly what quitting smoking does for your lungs. Every cigarette you do not smoke is protecting the healthy tissue that remains. Every smoke-free day gives your airways a chance to reduce inflammation and produce less mucus, making it easier to breathe."},{"id":"s4","title":"Why this myth is so harmful","body":"This myth gives people permission to keep doing the one thing that is accelerating their illness. It turns a treatable, manageable condition into a fast track toward oxygen dependency, hospital admissions, and early death. In Kenyan hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital and Moi Teaching and Referral Hospital, doctors see COPD patients every week who delayed quitting because they believed it would not help. Many of them wish they had stopped years earlier. The truth is that it is never too late. Whether you have had COPD for one year or ten years, quitting smoking today will improve your quality of life."},{"id":"s5","title":"Getting help to quit in Kenya","body":"Quitting is hard. Nicotine is genuinely addictive, and willpower alone is often not enough. Talk to your doctor about nicotine replacement therapy. Nicotine patches and gum are available at most Kenyan pharmacies including Goodlife Pharmacy and Haltons, typically costing between KES 800 and KES 2,500 per course. Some county hospitals offer free or subsidised smoking cessation support through their outpatient clinics. If your first attempt does not work, try again. Most successful quitters tried several times before they succeeded. You can also ask your doctor whether your SHA benefits cover any cessation support."},{"id":"s6","title":"The people around you matter too","body":"Secondhand smoke worsens your COPD and harms the lungs of everyone in your household, especially children and elderly family members. Quitting protects them as well as yourself. Ask family members and friends not to smoke around you. If your social circle revolves around smoking, consider that this is one area where you may need to set firm boundaries for your own survival. You are not being difficult. You are choosing to breathe."},{"id":"s7","title":"The bottom line","body":"The damage already done is real. But the damage you prevent by quitting today is also real, and it is enormous. Do not let a myth rob you of years of better breathing, more energy, and more time with the people you love. Every single cigarette you do not smoke is a gift to your future self."}]'::jsonb, 4, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'doctor-consultation'),
  (E'the-invisible-struggle-when-breathlessness-limits-your-world', E'The invisible struggle: when breathlessness limits your world', E'COPD', '{"COPD"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'It does not happen overnight. At first, you stop walking to the market because the hill on the way back leaves you gasping.', E'How COPD quietly shrinks your world\n\nIt does not happen overnight. At first, you stop walking to the market because the hill on the way back leaves you gasping. So you send someone else. Then you skip your friend''s birthday gathering because you know you will be the one sitting in the corner struggling to catch your breath while everyone else is dancing and laughing. Then church becomes too much because the walk from the parking area to your seat feels endless. Slowly, without anyone noticing, your world becomes your house, your chair, and your television. This is one of the most painful parts of living with COPD, and it is something that many Kenyans experience in silence.\n\nThe emotional weight of breathlessness\n\nBreathlessness is frightening. When you cannot get enough air, your body goes into panic mode. Your heart races, your muscles tense, and anxiety floods your mind. Over time, this fear of breathlessness can become as limiting as the breathlessness itself. You start avoiding anything that might trigger it, even things that used to bring you joy. Maybe you used to love cooking a big Sunday lunch for your family, making pilau or chapati and nyama choma. Now standing over a hot jiko or stove makes you feel like you cannot breathe, so you stop. Maybe you used to enjoy walking through the neighbourhood greeting your neighbours. Now you stay inside because you do not want people to see you struggling. This withdrawal is completely understandable. But it is also dangerous because isolation feeds depression, and depression makes COPD harder to manage.\n\nYou are not lazy and you are not weak\n\nOne of the most hurtful things about COPD is that people who do not understand it may think you are simply unfit or lazy. They cannot see your damaged lungs. They cannot feel the effort it takes you to walk across a room. When a neighbour says you should just exercise more, or a family member sighs because you need to rest again, it cuts deep. Know that your struggle is real, it is medical, and it is not your fault. COPD is a serious chronic illness that affects millions of people worldwide, and you deserve compassion, not judgement.\n\nPractical ways to stay connected\n\nBreathlessness does not have to mean isolation. It means you need to do things differently, not that you cannot do them at all. Plan outings for the cooler parts of the day, early morning or late afternoon, when Nairobi''s air quality is better and the heat will not make your breathing worse. Walk slowly and rest whenever you need to. There is no shame in pausing to catch your breath. Use pursed-lip breathing when you feel short of breath. Ask a family member or friend to accompany you so you feel safe. If walking to the market or church is too far, see if someone can drop you nearby so you only have a short distance to cover. At home, invite people to visit you instead of always going to them. A cup of chai and good conversation in your own sitting room is just as meaningful as meeting at a restaurant.\n\nWhen sadness becomes something more\n\nIt is normal to feel sad, frustrated, or angry about what COPD has taken from you. But if you find that sadness has settled in and will not leave, if you have lost interest in things you used to enjoy, if you are sleeping too much or too little, if you feel hopeless or worthless, these may be signs of depression. Depression is a medical condition, just like COPD, and it responds to treatment. Talk to your doctor about how you are feeling. Many county hospitals and facilities like Mathare National Teaching and Referral Hospital offer mental health services. Counselling is available through some SHA-covered outpatient visits. You can also call the Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 if you need someone to talk to urgently.\n\nYour mental health matters as much as your lungs\n\nYour inhalers keep your airways open. But staying connected to the people and activities you love keeps your spirit alive. Both are essential. Do not let COPD convince you that you have nothing to offer or nowhere to go. You are still you. You still have stories to tell, wisdom to share, and love to give. The world may feel smaller some days, but it does not have to disappear. Reach out. Let people in. And on the days when breathing is hardest, be gentle with yourself. Tomorrow may be easier.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"How COPD quietly shrinks your world","body":"It does not happen overnight. At first, you stop walking to the market because the hill on the way back leaves you gasping. So you send someone else. Then you skip your friend''s birthday gathering because you know you will be the one sitting in the corner struggling to catch your breath while everyone else is dancing and laughing. Then church becomes too much because the walk from the parking area to your seat feels endless. Slowly, without anyone noticing, your world becomes your house, your chair, and your television. This is one of the most painful parts of living with COPD, and it is something that many Kenyans experience in silence."},{"id":"s2","title":"The emotional weight of breathlessness","body":"Breathlessness is frightening. When you cannot get enough air, your body goes into panic mode. Your heart races, your muscles tense, and anxiety floods your mind. Over time, this fear of breathlessness can become as limiting as the breathlessness itself. You start avoiding anything that might trigger it, even things that used to bring you joy. Maybe you used to love cooking a big Sunday lunch for your family, making pilau or chapati and nyama choma. Now standing over a hot jiko or stove makes you feel like you cannot breathe, so you stop. Maybe you used to enjoy