lost interest in things you used to enjoy, if you are sleeping too much or too little, if you feel hopeless or worthless, these may be signs of depression. Depression is a medical condition, just like COPD, and it responds to treatment. Talk to your doctor about how you are feeling. Many county hospitals and facilities like Mathare National Teaching and Referral Hospital offer mental health services. Counselling is available through some SHA-covered outpatient visits. You can also call the Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 if you need someone to talk to urgently."},{"id":"s6","title":"Your mental health matters as much as your lungs","body":"Your inhalers keep your airways open. But staying connected to the people and activities you love keeps your spirit alive. Both are essential. Do not let COPD convince you that you have nothing to offer or nowhere to go. You are still you. You still have stories to tell, wisdom to share, and love to give. The world may feel smaller some days, but it does not have to disappear. Reach out. Let people in. And on the days when breathing is hardest, be gentle with yourself. Tomorrow may be easier."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'meditation'),
  (E'recognising-a-copd-flare-up-before-it-becomes-an-emergency', E'Recognising a COPD flare-up before it becomes an emergency', E'COPD', '{"COPD"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'COPD flare-ups, also called exacerbations, are episodes where your breathing suddenly gets much worse. They are the main reason people with COPD end up in emergency rooms at hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital or Aga Khan.', E'Why tracking your symptoms matters\n\nCOPD flare-ups, also called exacerbations, are episodes where your breathing suddenly gets much worse. They are the main reason people with COPD end up in emergency rooms at hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital or Aga Khan. But here is the important thing: most flare-ups do not come out of nowhere. They build up over hours or even days, giving you warning signs that something is going wrong. If you learn to spot these early signs, you can take action at home and often avoid a trip to the hospital altogether. This can save you thousands of shillings in emergency care costs and, more importantly, protect your lungs from further damage.\n\nThe five early warning signs\n\nThere are five key changes to watch for every single day. First, increased breathlessness. If activities that are normally manageable, like walking to the kitchen, making a cup of chai, or getting dressed, suddenly leave you more winded than usual, pay attention. Second, more coughing than your normal baseline. Everyone with COPD has some level of daily cough, but if yours is noticeably worse, that is a signal. Third, changes in your sputum. If the mucus you cough up becomes thicker, stickier, or changes colour from clear or white to yellow, green, or brown, this often indicates an infection. Fourth, increased use of your reliever inhaler. If you are reaching for your blue Ventolin or salbutamol inhaler more than three or four times a day when your normal is once or twice, your airways are struggling. Fifth, disrupted sleep. If you are waking up at night because of coughing, wheezing, or the feeling that you cannot get enough air, your COPD is not under control.\n\nHow to track these signs daily\n\nKeep a simple daily log. You do not need anything fancy. A small notebook by your bed works perfectly. Every morning and every evening, rate your breathing on a scale of one to five, where one is your best day and five is your worst. Note how many times you used your reliever inhaler. Describe your sputum colour and thickness. Record whether you slept through the night or woke up. After a week or two, you will know your personal baseline. This baseline is your reference point. Any change from it deserves attention.\n\nWhat the numbers and patterns mean\n\nIf your breathing score stays at one or two most days, your COPD is well controlled. If it creeps up to three, be watchful and consider whether something in your environment has changed. Dusty conditions, smoke from cooking on a jiko or charcoal stove, cold weather, or being around someone with a cold can all trigger worsening symptoms. If your score hits four or five, or if you notice two or more of the five warning signs at the same time, it is time to act.\n\nYour rescue action plan\n\nEvery person with COPD should have a written action plan agreed with their doctor. This plan tells you exactly what to do when symptoms worsen. Typically it includes increasing the dose or frequency of your reliever inhaler, starting a short course of oral steroids like prednisolone if your doctor has prescribed them for this purpose, and beginning antibiotics if your sputum has changed colour, suggesting an infection. Your doctor may have given you a rescue pack to keep at home for exactly these situations. If you do not have a rescue plan yet, ask your doctor to create one at your next visit. Having these medications ready at home can cost between KES 500 and KES 2,000 from pharmacies like Goodlife or Portal Pharmacy, and they can save you a KES 15,000 to KES 50,000 emergency room bill.\n\nWhen to call your doctor\n\nIf you start your rescue plan and do not feel improvement within 24 to 48 hours, call your doctor. If at any point you experience severe breathlessness that does not improve with your inhaler, chest pain, confusion, or bluish colour around your lips or fingernails, do not wait. Go to the nearest emergency department immediately or call an ambulance. These are signs of a serious flare-up that needs hospital treatment.\n\nPulse oximeters can help at home\n\nA small device called a pulse oximeter clips onto your finger and measures the oxygen level in your blood. They are available at Kenyan pharmacies for around KES 1,500 to KES 4,000. A normal reading is 95 to 100 percent. If your reading drops below 92 percent, or falls more than 3 to 4 points below your usual level, contact your doctor. This is an objective measurement that adds real value to your daily monitoring. Ask your doctor what your personal target range should be.\n\nCatching it early changes everything\n\nEvery flare-up causes some permanent damage to your lungs. The faster you respond, the less damage occurs. Patients who recognise and act on early warning signs spend fewer days in hospital, recover faster, and maintain better lung function over time. Your daily log is one of the most powerful tools you have. It costs nothing, takes two minutes, and it could keep you out of hospital.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Why tracking your symptoms matters","body":"COPD flare-ups, also called exacerbations, are episodes where your breathing suddenly gets much worse. They are the main reason people with COPD end up in emergency rooms at hospitals like Kenyatta National Hospital or Aga Khan. But here is the important thing: most flare-ups do not come out of nowhere. They build up over hours or even days, giving you warning signs that something is going wrong. If you learn to spot these early signs, you can take action at home and often avoid a trip to the hospital altogether. This can save you thousands of shillings in emergency care costs and, more importantly, protect your lungs from further damage."},{"id":"s2","title":"The five early warning signs","body":"There are five key changes to watch for every single day. First, increased breathlessness. If activities that are normally manageable, like walking to the kitchen, making a cup of chai, or getting dressed, suddenly leave you more winded than usual, pay attention. Second, more coughing than your normal baseline. Everyone with COPD has some level of daily cough, but if yours is noticeably worse, that is a signal. Third, changes in your sputum. If the mucus you cough up becomes thicker, stickier, or changes colour from clear or white to yellow, green, or brown, this often indicates an infection. Fourth, increased use of your reliever inhaler. If you are reaching for your blue Ventolin or salbutamol inhaler more than three or four times a day when your normal is once or twice, your airways are struggling. Fifth, disrupted sleep. If you are waking up at night because of coughing, wheezing, or the feeling that you cannot get enough air, your COPD is not under control."},{"id":"s3","title":"How to track these signs daily","body":"Keep a simple daily log. You do not need anything fancy. A small notebook by your bed works perfectly. Every morning and every evening, rate your breathing on a scale of one to five, where one is your best day and five is your worst. Note how many times you used your reliever inhaler. Describe your sputum colour and thickness. Record whether you slept through the night or woke up. After a week or two, you will know your personal baseline. This baseline is your reference point. Any change from it deserves attention."},{"id":"s4","title":"What the numbers and patterns mean","body":"If your breathing score stays at one or two most days, your COPD is well controlled. If it creeps up to three, be watchful and consider whether something in your environment has changed. Dusty conditions,