is an objective measurement that adds real value to your daily monitoring. Ask your doctor what your personal target range should be."},{"id":"s8","title":"Catching it early changes everything","body":"Every flare-up causes some permanent damage to your lungs. The faster you respond, the less damage occurs. Patients who recognise and act on early warning signs spend fewer days in hospital, recover faster, and maintain better lung function over time. Your daily log is one of the most powerful tools you have. It costs nothing, takes two minutes, and it could keep you out of hospital."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'blood-pressure-check'),
  (E'accepting-help-does-not-mean-giving-up', E'Accepting help does not mean giving up', E'COPD', '{"COPD"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'There comes a day when you realise you cannot do something you have always done. Maybe it is carrying your shopping bags from the matatu stage to your house.', E'The moment things change\n\nThere comes a day when you realise you cannot do something you have always done. Maybe it is carrying your shopping bags from the matatu stage to your house. Maybe it is climbing the stairs to your flat without stopping three times to catch your breath. Maybe it is playing with your grandchildren in the compound. The moment you realise your body has changed is a grief that no one really prepares you for. For many Kenyans living with COPD, this moment comes with shame. In a culture where strength and self-reliance are valued deeply, admitting that you need help can feel like admitting defeat.\n\nHelp is not the same as helplessness\n\nLet us be honest about what accepting help actually means. It does not mean you are giving up. It does not mean you are a burden. It means you are making a smart, practical decision about how to use your limited energy for the things that matter most. Think of it this way. If you spend all your energy struggling to carry heavy bags from the market, you arrive home exhausted and spend the rest of the day on the sofa unable to do anything else. But if you let someone help with the bags, you arrive home with enough energy to cook a meal, sit with your family, or take a gentle walk in the evening. Accepting help with one thing gives you the freedom to do others.\n\nPractical adaptations for daily life\n\nSmall changes in how you do things can make a huge difference in how you feel. When cooking, sit on a stool instead of standing. Prepare ingredients in advance so you are not rushing. Use a gas cooker instead of a charcoal jiko when possible, as the smoke from charcoal irritates your airways. When shopping at the market for your sukuma wiki, tomatoes, and onions, bring a lightweight bag with wheels or ask the vendor to pack items in smaller, lighter bags. When dressing, sit down on the bed and take your time. Put on your shoes using a long-handled shoehorn so you do not have to bend over and compress your chest.\n\nRethinking your home\n\nIf you live in a house with stairs, consider moving your bedroom downstairs or to the ground floor if possible. Keep the things you use most often, your medications, phone, water, and a snack, within easy reach of your favourite chair so you do not have to get up repeatedly. If you cook with firewood or charcoal, try to improve ventilation in your kitchen by opening windows or cooking outdoors when weather permits. Smoke from cooking fires is one of the leading causes of COPD in Kenya, and continued exposure makes existing COPD worse. If you can gradually transition to cleaner fuel, even a small LPG cylinder costing around KES 2,000 to refill, your lungs will thank you.\n\nTalking to your family\n\nYour family wants to help but may not know how. They may hover too much, doing things for you that you can still do yourself, which feels patronising. Or they may not realise how much you are struggling because you have been hiding it. Have an honest conversation. Tell them specifically what you find difficult and what you can still manage. For example, you might say that you can still cook but need someone to carry the heavy pots, or that you can walk to church but need to rest twice on the way. Giving people clear, specific ways to help is much better than either suffering in silence or letting them take over completely.\n\nUsing mobility aids without shame\n\nA walking stick, a wheelchair for long distances, or even just a sturdy arm to lean on are tools, not symbols of weakness. A farmer uses a jembe to dig because it is more efficient than using bare hands. That does not make the farmer weak. It makes the farmer wise. If a walking stick helps you get to church, use it. If a wheelchair lets you attend your grandchild''s school event, use it. If holding someone''s arm lets you walk through the market at Gikomba or Toi, hold that arm. The goal is to keep living your life, not to prove that you can do everything the hard way.\n\nEnergy conservation is a real strategy\n\nDoctors and physiotherapists call it energy conservation, and it is a recognised medical strategy for people with COPD. The idea is simple: plan your day so that heavy tasks are spread out with rest periods in between. Do your most demanding activity in the morning when your energy is highest. Sit down for tasks you would normally stand for. Avoid rushing. Take your time with everything. This is not laziness. This is medical management of a chronic condition.\n\nAcceptance is a form of courage\n\nAccepting that your life has changed takes more courage than pretending it has not. It takes courage to ask for help. It takes courage to slow down in a world that values speed. It takes courage to say I cannot do this alone. But that courage opens doors that stubbornness keeps shut. When you accept help, you stay mobile, social, and engaged with the world. When you refuse it out of pride, you risk exhaustion, falls, hospital visits, and deepening isolation. The strongest thing you can do is adapt, and let the people who love you share the load.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"The moment things change","body":"There comes a day when you realise you cannot do something you have always done. Maybe it is carrying your shopping bags from the matatu stage to your house. Maybe it is climbing the stairs to your flat without stopping three times to catch your breath. Maybe it is playing with your grandchildren in the compound. The moment you realise your body has changed is a grief that no one really prepares you for. For many Kenyans living with COPD, this moment comes with shame. In a culture where strength and self-reliance are valued deeply, admitting that you need help can feel like admitting defeat."},{"id":"s2","title":"Help is not the same as helplessness","body":"Let us be honest about what accepting help actually means. It does not mean you are giving up. It does not mean you are a burden. It means you are making a smart, practical decision about how to use your limited energy for the things that matter most. Think of it this way. If you spend all your energy struggling to carry heavy bags from the market, you arrive home exhausted and spend the rest of the day on the sofa unable to do anything else. But if you let someone help with the bags, you arrive home with enough energy to cook a meal, sit with your family, or take a gentle walk in the evening. Accepting help with one thing gives you the freedom to do others."},{"id":"s3","title":"Practical adaptations for daily life","body":"Small changes in how you do things can make a huge difference in how you feel. When cooking, sit on a stool instead of standing. Prepare ingredients in advance so you are not rushing. Use a gas cooker instead of a charcoal jiko when possible, as the smoke from charcoal irritates your airways. When shopping at the market for your sukuma wiki, tomatoes, and onions, bring a lightweight bag with wheels or ask the vendor to pack items in smaller, lighter bags. When dressing, sit down on the bed and take your time. Put on your shoes using a long-handled shoehorn so you do not have to bend over and compress your chest."},{"id":"s4","title":"Rethinking your home","body":"If you live in a house with stairs, consider moving your bedroom downstairs or to the ground floor if possible. Keep the things you use most often, your medications, phone, water, and a snack, within easy reach of your favourite chair so you do not have to get up repeatedly. If you cook with firewood or charcoal, try to improve ventilation in your kitchen by opening windows or cooking outdoors when weather permits. Smoke from cooking fires is one of the leading causes of COPD in Kenya, and continued exposure makes existing COPD worse. If you can gradually transition to cleaner fuel, even a small LPG cylinder costing around KES 2,000 to refill, your lungs will thank you."},{"id":"s5","title":"Talking to your family","body":"Your family wants to help but may not know how. They may hover too much, doing things for you that you can still do yourself, which feels patronising. Or they may not realise how much you are struggling because you have been hiding it. Have an honest conversation. Tell them specifically what you find difficult and what you can still manage. For example, you might say that you can still cook but need someone to carry the heavy pots, or that you can walk to church but need to rest twice on the way. Giving people clear, specific ways to help is much better than either suffering in silence or letting them take over completely."},{"id":"s6","title":"Using mobility aids without shame","body":"A walking stick, a wheelchair for long distances, or even just a sturdy arm to lean on are tools, not symbols of weakness. A farmer uses a jembe to dig because it is more efficient than using bare hands. That does not make the farmer weak. It makes the farmer wise. If a walking stick helps you get to church, use it. If a wheelchair lets you attend your grandchild''s school event, use it. If holding someone''s arm lets you walk through the market at Gikomba or Toi, hold that arm. The goal is to keep living your life, not to prove that you can do everything the hard way."},{"id":"s7","title":"Energy conservation is a real strategy","body":"Doctors and physiotherapists call it energy conservation, and it is a recognised medical strategy for people with COPD. The idea is simple: plan your day so that heavy tasks are spread out with rest periods in between. Do your most demanding activity in the morning when your energy is highest. Sit down for tasks you would normally stand for. Avoid rushing. Take your time with everything. This is not laziness. This is medical management of a chronic condition."},{"id":"s8","title":"Acceptance is a form of courage","body":"Accepting that your life has changed takes more courage than pretending it has not. It takes courage to ask for help. It takes courage to slow down in a world that values speed. It takes courage to say I cannot do this alone. But that courage opens doors that stubbornness keeps shut. When you accept help, you stay mobile, social, and engaged with the world. When you refuse it out of pride, you risk exhaustion, falls, hospital visits, and deepening isolation. The strongest thing you can do is adapt, and let the people who love you share the load."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'self-care'),
  (E'one-month-smoke-free', E'One month smoke-free', E'COPD', '{"COPD"}', E'MILESTONE', E'If you have gone thirty days without smoking, take a moment to recognise what you have achieved. One month smoke-free is one of the most important milestones in managing COPD, and it deserves to be celebrated.', E'A full month without a cigarette\n\nIf you have gone thirty days without smoking, take a moment to recognise what you have achieved. One month smoke-free is one of the most important milestones in managing COPD, and it deserves to be celebrated. Quitting smoking is the single most effective thing you can do to slow down the progression of your lung disease. No inhaler, no medication, no surgery can match the benefit of stopping the one habit that is actively destroying your remaining lung tissue. And you have done it for an entire month.\n\nWhat has already changed in your body\n\nThe improvements started within hours of your last cigarette, even if you did not notice them. Within twenty minutes, your heart rate began to drop back toward normal. Within twelve hours, the carbon monoxide level in your blood returned to normal, meaning your red blood cells could carry oxygen properly again. Within two to three weeks, your circulation improved and your lung function began to increase. Now, at the one-month mark, the cilia in your airways, the tiny hair-like structures that sweep mucus and debris out of your lungs, are starting to recover. You may actually be coughing more than you did while smoking, and that is a good sign. It means your lungs are cleaning themselves out. Your sense of taste and smell are sharper too. That cup of Kenyan chai tastes richer than it has in years. The aroma of freshly cooked pilau or chapati hits you before you even enter the kitchen.\n\nThe financial savings are real\n\nIf you were smoking a pack a day at roughly KES 250 to KES 350 per pack, you have saved somewhere between KES 7,500 and KES 10,500 in just one month. Over a year, that is KES 90,000 to KES 126,000. That money could cover several months of COPD medication, a pulse oximeter for home monitoring, or even the refill costs for an LPG cooking cylinder that protects your lungs from charcoal smoke. Think about what you could do with that money. A nice meal for your family. New shoes for your grandchild. A contribution to your Jireh Care Saver that builds up funds for future medical needs. The financial benefit of quitting is enormous and it grows every single day.\n\nThe hardest part is behind you\n\nMost people who quit smoking report that the first two to four weeks are the most difficult. The nicotine cravings are strongest during this period, the habit triggers are freshest, and the temptation is everywhere. If you made it through one month, you have already survived the hardest stretch. That does not mean cravings will disappear completely, but they will come less often and feel less intense. The moments when you desperately want a cigarette will become shorter and further apart. Many former smokers say that after three months, they rarely think about smoking at all.\n\nWhat to do when temptation strikes\n\nEven after a month, certain situations may trigger a strong urge to smoke. Social gatherings where others are smoking, stressful moments at work or home, or the habit of having a cigarette with your morning tea. When this happens, use the four-D technique: Delay for ten minutes, because most cravings pass within that time. Drink a glass of water slowly. Deep breathe using the pursed-lip technique your doctor taught you. Distract yourself by calling a friend, taking a short walk, or chewing on a piece of sugarcane or a handful of groundnuts. If certain places or people are strong triggers, it is okay to avoid them for now. Protecting your progress is more important than any social obligation.\n\nIf you slipped, do not give up\n\nIf at some point during this month you had a cigarette or two, do not let that erase your progress. A slip is not the same as a relapse. Many successful quitters had one or two moments of weakness before they quit for good. What matters is that you got back on track. Do not punish yourself or use it as an excuse to start smoking again. Just reset, learn what triggered the slip, and plan how to handle that trigger next time. You can also talk to your doctor about nicotine replacement therapy. Patches and gum are available at most Kenyan pharmacies including Goodlife Pharmacy, Haltons, and Portal Pharmacy, usually costing between KES 800 and KES 2,500 for a course.\n\nLooking ahead to the next milestone\n\nAt three months smoke-free, your lung function will have improved even further, and the risk of a heart attack starts dropping significantly. At six months, your airways will produce much less excess mucus, and many people notice they can walk further and climb stairs more easily. At one year, your risk of coronary heart disease is half that of a current smoker. These milestones are ahead of you and within reach. You have already proven you can do this. Keep going.\n\nYou are giving yourself the gift of time\n\nEvery smoke-free day is a day where your lungs are healing instead of being harmed. Every smoke-free day is a day where you can breathe a little easier, walk a little further, and enjoy life a little more. You are not just quitting a habit. You are choosing to live longer and live better. That is something to be genuinely proud of. One month down. A whole new chapter ahead.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"A full month without a cigarette","body":"If you have gone thirty days without smoking, take a moment to recognise what you have achieved. One month smoke-free is one of the most important milestones in managing COPD, and it deserves to be celebrated. Quitting smoking is the single most effective thing you can do to slow down the progression of your lung disease. No inhaler, no medication, no surgery can match the benefit of stopping the one habit that is actively destroying your remaining lung tissue. And you have done it for an entire month."},{"id":"s2","title":"What has already changed in your body","body":"The improvements started within hours of your last cigarette, even if you did not notice them. Within twenty minutes, your heart rate began to drop back toward normal. Within twelve hours, the carbon monoxide level in your blood returned to normal, meaning your red blood cells could carry oxygen properly again. Within two to three weeks, your circulation improved and your lung function began to increase. Now, at the one-month mark, the cilia in your airways, the tiny hair-like structures that sweep mucus and debris out of your lungs, are starting to recover. You may actually be coughing more than you did while smoking, and that is a good sign. It means your lungs are cleaning themselves out. Your sense of taste and smell are sharper too. That cup of Kenyan chai tastes richer than it has in years. The aroma of freshly cooked pilau or chapati hits you before you even enter the kitchen."},{"id":"s3","title":"The financial savings are real","body":"If you were smoking a pack a day at roughly KES 250 to KES 350 per pack, you have saved somewhere between KES 7,500 and KES 10,500 in just one month. Over a year, that is KES 90,000 to KES 126,000. That money could cover several months of COPD medication, a pulse oximeter for home monitoring, or even the refill costs for an LPG cooking cylinder that protects your lungs from charcoal smoke. Think about what you could do with that money. A nice meal for your family. New shoes for your grandchild. A contribution to your Jireh Care Saver that builds up funds for future medical needs. The financial benefit of quitting is enormous and it grows every single day."},{"id":"s4","title":"The hardest part is behind you","body":"Most people who quit smoking report that the first two to four weeks are the most difficult. The nicotine cravings are strongest during this period, the habit triggers are freshest, and the temptation is everywhere. If you made it through one month, you have already survived the hardest stretch. That does not mean cravings will disappear completely, but they will come less often and feel less intense. The moments when you desperately want a cigarette will become shorter and further apart. Many former smokers say that after three months, they rarely think about smoking at all."},{"id":"s5","title":"What to do when temptation strikes","body":"Even after a month, certain situations may trigger a strong urge to smoke. Social gatherings where others are smoking, stressful moments at work or home, or the habit of having a cigarette with your morning tea. When this happens, use the four-D technique: Delay for ten minutes, because most cravings pass within that time. Drink a glass of water slowly. Deep breathe using the pursed-lip technique your doctor taught you. Distract yourself by calling a friend, taking a short walk, or chewing on a piece of sugarcane or a handful of groundnuts. If certain places or people are strong triggers, it is okay to avoid them for now. Protecting your progress is more important than any social obligation."},{"id":"s6","title":"If you slipped, do not give up","body":"If at some point during this month you had a cigarette or two, do not let that erase your progress. A slip is not the same as a relapse. Many successful quitters had one or two moments of weakness before they quit for good. What matters is that you got back on track. Do not punish yourself or use it as an excuse to start smoking again. Just reset, learn what triggered the slip, and plan how to handle that trigger next time. You can also talk to your doctor about nicotine replacement therapy. Patches and gum are available at most Kenyan pharmacies including Goodlife Pharmacy, Haltons, and Portal Pharmacy, usually costing between KES 800 and KES 2,500 for a course."},{"id":"s7","title":"Looking ahead to the next milestone","body":"At three months smoke-free, your lung function will have improved even further, and the risk of a heart attack starts dropping significantly. At six months, your airways will produce much less excess mucus, and many people notice they can walk further and climb stairs more easily. At one year, your risk of coronary heart disease is half that of a current smoker. These milestones are ahead of you and within reach. You have already proven you can do this. Keep going."},{"id":"s8","title":"You are giving yourself the gift of time","body":"Every smoke-free day is a day where your lungs are healing instead of being harmed. Every smoke-free day is a day where you can breathe a little easier, walk a little further, and enjoy life a little more. You are not just quitting a habit. You are choosing to live longer and live better. That is something to be genuinely proud of. One month down. A whole new chapter ahead."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'progress-chart'),
  (E'anti-inflammatory-foods-that-ease-joint-stiffness', E'Anti-inflammatory foods that ease joint stiffness', E'ARTHRITIS', '{"ARTHRITIS"}', E'DIETARY', E'Chronic inflammation is the engine behind arthritis pain, swelling, and stiffness. Every time your joints flare up, inflammatory chemicals are circulating through your body, attacking the lining of your joints.', E'Chronic inflammation is the engine behind arthritis pain, swelling, and stiffness. Every time your joints flare up, inflammatory chemicals are circulating through your body, attacking the lining of your joints. The good news is that what you put on your plate can either fuel that fire or help put it out. You do not need expensive supplements or imported superfoods. Right here in Kenya, we have some of the best anti-inflammatory ingredients in the world, and you are probably already cooking with some of them.\n\nOily fish is your joints'' best friend\n\nOmega-3 fatty acids are among the most powerful natural anti-inflammatory compounds known to science. You do not need to buy expensive salmon. Omena, sardines, and tilapia from Lake Victoria are rich in these same omega-3s. Aim to eat fish at least three times a week. A simple plate of omena fried with onions, tomatoes, and a squeeze of lemon alongside some ugali is a meal that genuinely fights inflammation. When shopping at the market, look for fresh omena or the dried variety, which is affordable at around KES 150-250 per tin depending on your area. Sardines in tomato sauce from the supermarket also work well and cost about KES 100-150 per tin.\n\nTurmeric and ginger belong in your daily cooking\n\nTurmeric contains a compound called curcumin that has been studied extensively for its ability to reduce joint inflammation. Ginger contains similar compounds called gingerols. Both have been used in Kenyan and East African cooking for generations, especially along the coast. Add a teaspoon of turmeric powder to your stews, soups, and lentil dishes. Grate fresh ginger into your morning chai. When making pilau, the turmeric and ginger you already use are doing more than adding flavour — they are actively calming your joints. For an extra boost, try golden milk before bed: warm a cup of milk with half a teaspoon of turmeric, a pinch of black pepper (which helps your body absorb the curcumin), and a little honey.\n\nLoad up on dark green vegetables\n\nSukuma wiki is not just affordable and filling — it is packed with vitamins C and K, calcium, and antioxidants that protect your joints. Spinach, managu, and terere are equally beneficial. These vegetables contain compounds that help neutralise the free radicals that contribute to joint damage. Try to include a generous serving of greens with at least two meals a day. When preparing sukuma wiki, avoid overcooking it. Light sauteing with onions, tomatoes, and a little oil preserves more of the nutrients. A plate of githeri with plenty of sukuma wiki on the side gives you both anti-inflammatory greens and the protein your muscles need to support your joints.\n\nFoods that make inflammation worse\n\nJust as some foods calm inflammation, others pour fuel on the fire. Deep-fried foods like chips, fried chicken, and mandazi made with lots of oil contain compounds that trigger inflammatory responses. This does not mean you can never enjoy these foods, but making them occasional treats rather than daily staples will help your joints. Excessive red meat, especially processed meats like sausages and bacon, also increases inflammation. Sugary drinks, including sodas and heavily sweetened tea, cause blood sugar spikes that worsen inflammatory responses throughout your body. White bread and refined flour products have similar effects.\n\nYour weekly anti-inflammatory shopping list\n\nNext time you visit the market, focus on these items: omena or sardines, fresh ginger, turmeric powder, sukuma wiki or spinach, tomatoes, onions, garlic, lemons, bananas, avocados, groundnuts, and beans or lentils. These are all affordable, widely available across Kenya, and genuinely helpful for your joints. Avocados are particularly good because they contain healthy fats that reduce inflammation and help your body absorb the nutrients from your vegetables. Groundnuts provide protein and healthy fats without the inflammatory effects of processed snacks.\n\nPutting it all together\n\nA sample day might look like this: breakfast of uji with groundnuts and a banana; lunch of githeri with sukuma wiki and a side of omena; afternoon chai with fresh ginger; dinner of fish stew with turmeric, served with brown ugali and steamed cabbage. This is not a special diet — it is simply a Kenyan diet that emphasises the ingredients your joints need. Small, consistent changes in what you eat can reduce your reliance on pain medication over time. Talk to your doctor about how dietary changes are working alongside your treatment plan. Many patients at Kenyan hospitals report noticeable improvements in joint stiffness within three to four weeks of making these changes.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Chronic inflammation is the engine behind arthritis pain, swelling, and stiffness. Every time your joints flare up, inflammatory chemicals are circulating through your body, attacking the lining of your joints. The good news is that what you put on your plate can either fuel that fire or help put it out. You do not need expensive supplements or imported superfoods. Right here in Kenya, we have some of the best anti-inflammatory ingredients in the world, and you are probably already cooking with some of them."},{"id":"s2","title":"Oily fish is your joints'' best friend","body":"Omega-3 fatty acids are among the most powerful natural anti-inflammatory compounds known to science. You do not need to buy expensive salmon. Omena, sardines, and tilapia from Lake Victoria are rich in these same omega-3s. Aim to eat fish at least three times a week. A simple plate of omena fried with onions, tomatoes, and a squeeze of lemon alongside some ugali is a meal that genuinely fights inflammation. When shopping at the market, look for fresh omena or the dried variety, which is affordable at around KES 150-250 per tin depending on your area. Sardines in tomato sauce from the supermarket also work well and cost about KES 100-150 per tin."},{"id":"s3","title":"Turmeric and ginger belong in your daily cooking","body":"Turmeric contains a compound called curcumin that has been studied extensively for its ability to reduce joint inflammation. Ginger contains similar compounds called gingerols. Both have been used in Kenyan and East African cooking for generations, especially along the coast. Add a teaspoon of turmeric powder to your stews, soups, and lentil dishes. Grate fresh ginger into your morning chai. When making pilau, the turmeric and ginger you already use are doing more than adding flavour — they are actively calming your joints. For an extra boost, try golden milk before bed: warm a cup of milk with half a teaspoon of turmeric, a pinch of black pepper (which helps your body absorb the curcumin), and a little honey."},{"id":"s4","title":"Load up on dark green vegetables","body":"Sukuma wiki is not just affordable and filling — it is packed with vitamins C and K, calcium, and antioxidants that protect your joints. Spinach, managu, and terere are equally beneficial. These vegetables contain compounds that help neutralise the free radicals that contribute to joint damage. Try to include a generous serving of greens with at least two meals a day. When preparing sukuma wiki, avoid overcooking it. Light sauteing with onions, tomatoes, and a little oil preserves more of the nutrients. A plate of githeri with plenty of sukuma wiki on the side gives you both anti-inflammatory greens and the protein your muscles need to support your joints."},{"id":"s5","title":"Foods that make inflammation worse","body":"Just as some foods calm inflammation, others pour fuel on the fire. Deep-fried foods like chips, fried chicken, and mandazi made with lots of oil contain compounds that trigger inflammatory responses. This does not mean you can never enjoy these foods, but making them occasional treats rather than daily staples will help your joints. Excessive red meat, especially processed meats like sausages and bacon, also increases inflammation. Sugary drinks, including sodas and heavily sweetened tea, cause blood sugar spikes that worsen inflammatory responses throughout your body. White bread and refined flour products have similar effects."},{"id":"s6","title":"Your weekly anti-inflammatory shopping list","body":"Next time you visit the market, focus on these items: omena or sardines, fresh ginger, turmeric powder, sukuma wiki or spinach, tomatoes, onions, garlic, lemons, bananas, avocados, groundnuts, and beans or lentils. These are all affordable, widely available across Kenya, and genuinely helpful for your joints. Avocados are particularly good because they contain healthy fats that reduce inflammation and help your body absorb the nutrients from your vegetables. Groundnuts provide protein and healthy fats without the inflammatory effects of processed snacks."},{"id":"s7","title":"Putting it all together","body":"A sample day might look like this: breakfast of uji with groundnuts and a banana; lunch of githeri with sukuma wiki and a side of omena; afternoon chai with fresh ginger; dinner of fish stew with turmeric, served with brown ugali and steamed cabbage. This is not a special diet — it is simply a Kenyan diet that emphasises the ingredients your joints need. Small, consistent changes in what you eat can reduce your reliance on pain medication over time. Talk to your doctor about how dietary changes are working alongside your treatment plan. Many patients at Kenyan hospitals report noticeable improvements in joint stiffness within three to four weeks of making these changes."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'fresh-vegetables'),
  (E'morning-stretches-that-loosen-stiff-joints', E'Morning stretches that loosen stiff joints', E'ARTHRITIS', '{"ARTHRITIS"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Morning stiffness is one of the most frustrating parts of living with arthritis. You wake up and your body feels like it has rusted overnight.', E'Morning stiffness is one of the most frustrating parts of living with arthritis. You wake up and your body feels like it has rusted overnight. Your fingers do not want to bend, your knees protest when you swing your legs off the bed, and even walking to the bathroom feels like a major effort. This happens because during sleep, your joints are still for hours, and the inflammatory fluid that causes swelling settles around them. The good news is that a simple stretching routine, done before you even leave your bed, can dramatically reduce this stiffness and set you up for a better day.\n\nBefore you get out of bed\n\nStart while you are still lying down. This is important because your joints are at their stiffest right now, and standing up immediately puts weight on them before they are ready. Begin with your hands. Make gentle fists, then spread your fingers wide. Repeat this 10 times. It should feel tight but not painful. Next, rotate your wrists slowly in circles, five times in each direction. These hand and wrist movements are especially important if you struggle with opening jars, holding a cup of chai, or gripping utensils.\n\nNow move to your knees. While lying on your back, slowly pull one knee toward your chest, hold it for five seconds, then lower it back down. Repeat with the other knee. Do this five times on each side. This stretches the muscles around your hip and knee joints and encourages blood flow to the areas that need it most. Finish the bed routine by pointing your toes away from you, then pulling them back toward your shins. Rotate each ankle in circles, five times each way. The entire bed routine takes about three to four minutes.\n\nThe warm-up walk\n\nOnce you are standing, walk slowly around your house for two to three minutes. Do not rush. Let your body find its rhythm. Many people in Kenya start their mornings early, and the temptation is to jump straight into chores or preparing breakfast for the family. Resist that urge for just a few minutes. A slow walk around the sitting room or along the corridor gives your joints time to warm up. If you have stairs at home, avoid them for the first 10 minutes of your morning.\n\nStanding stretches\n\nOnce you have walked for a few minutes, try these standing stretches. Hold onto a chair or countertop for balance. Rise up onto your toes, hold for three seconds, then lower back down. Repeat 10 times. This strengthens your calf muscles, which support your ankle and knee joints. Next, stand on one leg for 10 seconds, then switch. This improves balance, which is important because arthritis can make you unsteady. If 10 seconds is too long, start with five. Finally, gently swing each leg forward and back like a pendulum, five times per leg. Keep the movements smooth and controlled.\n\nThe power of a warm shower\n\nAfter your stretches, take a warm shower if you can. Heat relaxes the muscles around your joints and increases blood flow, which carries away some of the inflammatory chemicals causing your stiffness. Let the warm water run over your most affected joints for a few extra minutes. If you do not have a shower, a basin of warm water and a cloth applied to stiff joints has a similar effect. Some patients find that keeping a hot water bottle near their bed to use on their knees or hands before starting stretches makes the entire routine easier.\n\nWhen to push through and when to stop\n\nThere is an important difference between the discomfort of a stiff joint warming up and the sharp pain of a joint being damaged. Stretching should feel tight and mildly uncomfortable, like stretching a rubber band. It should not cause sharp, stabbing, or burning pain. If a particular movement causes that kind of pain, skip it and mention it to your doctor or physiotherapist at your next appointment. On days when your arthritis is flaring badly, do a shorter, gentler version of the routine. Even five finger flexes and some ankle rotations are better than nothing.\n\nMaking it a habit\n\nConsistency matters more than perfection. Do this routine every single morning, even on days when you feel relatively good. Over time, your body will adapt and morning stiffness will become shorter and less severe. Many arthritis patients in Kenya who have adopted a morning stretch routine report that their stiffness reduces from over an hour to 15 or 20 minutes within a few weeks. Set your alarm three minutes earlier than usual. That is all it takes. Your joints will thank you for the rest of the day.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Morning stiffness is one of the most frustrating parts of living with arthritis. You wake up and your body feels like it has rusted overnight. Your fingers do not want to bend, your knees protest when you swing your legs off the bed, and even walking to the bathroom feels like a major effort. This happens because during sleep, your joints are still for hours, and the inflammatory fluid that causes swelling settles around them. The good news is that a simple stretching routine, done before you even leave your bed, can dramatically reduce this stiffness and set you up for a better day."},{"id":"s2","title":"Before you get out of bed","body":"Start while you are still lying down. This is important because your joints are at their stiffest right now, and standing up immediately puts weight on them before they are ready. Begin with your hands. Make gentle fists, then spread your fingers wide. Repeat this 10 times. It should feel tight but not painful. Next, rotate your wrists slowly in circles, five times in each direction. These hand and wrist movements are especially important if you struggle with opening jars, holding a cup of chai, or gripping utensils.\\n\\nNow move to your knees. While lying on your back, slowly pull one knee toward your chest, hold it for five seconds, then lower it back down. Repeat with the other knee. Do this five times on each side. This stretches the muscles around your hip and knee joints and encourages blood flow to the areas that need it most. Finish the bed routine by pointing your toes away from you, then pulling them back toward your shins. Rotate each ankle in circles, five times each way. The entire bed routine takes about three to four minutes."},{"id":"s3","title":"The warm-up walk","body":"Once you are standing, walk slowly around your house for two to three minutes. Do not rush. Let your body find its rhythm. Many people in Kenya start their mornings early, and the temptation is to jump straight into chores or preparing breakfast for the family. Resist that urge for just a few minutes. A slow walk around the sitting room or along the corridor gives your joints time to warm up. If you have stairs at home, avoid them for the first 10 minutes of your morning."},{"id":"s4","title":"Standing stretches","body":"Once you have walked for a few minutes, try these standing stretches. Hold onto a chair or countertop for balance. Rise up onto your toes, hold for three seconds, then lower back down. Repeat 10 times. This strengthens your calf muscles, which support your ankle and knee joints. Next, stand on one leg for 10 seconds, then switch. This improves balance, which is important because arthritis can make you unsteady. If 10 seconds is too long, start with five. Finally, gently swing each leg forward and back like a pendulum, five times per leg. Keep the movements smooth and controlled."},{"id":"s5","title":"The power of a warm shower","body":"After your stretches, take a warm shower if you can. Heat relaxes the muscles around your joints and increases blood flow, which carries away some of the inflammatory chemicals causing your stiffness. Let the warm water run over your most affected joints for a few extra minutes. If you do not have a shower, a basin of warm water and a cloth applied to stiff joints has a similar effect. Some patients find that keeping a hot water bottle near their bed to use on their knees or hands before starting stretches makes the entire routine easier."},{"id":"s6","title":"When to push through and when to stop","body":"There is an important difference between the discomfort of a stiff joint warming up and the sharp pain of a joint being damaged. Stretching should feel tight and mildly uncomfortable, like stretching a rubber band. It should not cause sharp, stabbing, or burning pain. If a particular movement causes that kind of pain, skip it and mention it to your doctor or physiotherapist at your next appointment. On days when your arthritis is flaring badly, do a shorter, gentler version of the routine. Even five finger flexes and some ankle rotations are better than nothing."},{"id":"s7","title":"Making it a habit","body":"Consistency matters more than perfection. Do this routine every single morning, even on days when you feel relatively good. Over time, your body will adapt and morning stiffness will become shorter and less severe. Many arthritis patients in Kenya who have adopted a morning stretch routine report that their stiffness reduces from over an hour to 15 or 20 minutes within a few weeks. Set your alarm three minutes earlier than usual. That is all it takes. Your joints will thank you for the rest of the day."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'stretching'),
  (E'myth-exercise-makes-arthritis-worse', E'Myth: Exercise makes arthritis worse', E'ARTHRITIS', '{"ARTHRITIS"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The myth that exercise makes arthritis worse is one of the most common and most harmful beliefs among people living with joint conditions. It seems logical on the surface: your joints hurt, so surely moving them more will cause more damage.', E'The myth that exercise makes arthritis worse is one of the most common and most harmful beliefs among people living with joint conditions. It seems logical on the surface: your joints hurt, so surely moving them more will cause more damage. Many well-meaning family members and friends reinforce this by saying things like "Rest more," "Stop walking so much," or "You should not be exercising with your knees like that." But medical science tells us the exact opposite. Gentle, regular exercise is one of the single best things you can do for arthritis, and avoiding movement is one of the worst.\n\nWhat actually happens when you stop moving\n\nWhen you avoid exercise because of joint pain, a destructive cycle begins. The muscles around your joints weaken because they are not being used. Weaker muscles provide less support for the joint, which means the joint itself bears more stress. The cartilage in your joints depends on movement to receive nutrients, because it does not have its own blood supply. Instead, it absorbs nutrients from the joint fluid, and this only happens when the joint is moved and compressed, like a sponge being squeezed and released. Without movement, the cartilage slowly deteriorates. The joint becomes stiffer, more painful, and less functional. This leads to even less movement, and the cycle continues downward.\n\nWhat the research shows\n\nStudies involving thousands of arthritis patients around the world, including research conducted in East Africa, consistently show that regular exercise reduces pain, improves joint function, and slows the progression of the disease. Exercise strengthens the muscles that act as shock absorbers for your joints. It increases the production of synovial fluid, which lubricates the joint and reduces friction. It also releases natural pain-relieving chemicals called endorphins. Patients who exercise regularly report less pain, better sleep, improved mood, and greater ability to perform daily tasks like climbing stairs, carrying shopping from the market, or playing with their grandchildren.\n\nThe right kind of exercise\n\nThe key is choosing the right type of exercise. Low-impact activities are best. Walking is the simplest and most accessible option in Kenya. You do not need a gym membership or special equipment. A 20 to 30 minute walk on flat ground, done at a comfortable pace, provides excellent benefits for your joints. Swimming is another outstanding option because the water supports your body weight, taking pressure off your joints while allowing you to move freely. If you have access to a pool at a local sports club or county facility, even swimming once or twice a week can make a significant difference. Cycling, whether on a stationary bike or a regular bicycle on flat roads, is also gentle on the joints while building leg strength.\n\nStretching and flexibility exercises, like the morning routine described in other articles in this series, keep your joints mobile and reduce stiffness. Strengthening exercises using light resistance, such as lifting small water bottles or using resistance bands, build the muscles that protect your joints. Yoga and tai chi, which are becoming more available in Kenyan cities, combine stretching, strengthening, and balance work in one practice.\n\nWhat to avoid\n\nHigh-impact activities that put sudden, heavy stress on your joints should be approached with caution. Running on hard surfaces like tarmac, jumping, or playing high-impact sports like football can aggravate already damaged joints. Heavy weightlifting that puts excessive strain on affected joints is also not recommended. But this is not the same as saying you should avoid all exercise. It means choosing activities that work with your body rather than against it.\n\nHow to start safely\n\nIf you have been inactive for a while because of pain, start very slowly. Begin with just five to ten minutes of gentle walking or stretching. Increase gradually, adding a few minutes each week. Some discomfort during exercise is normal and expected. The difference between normal exercise discomfort and a warning sign is this: if the pain goes away within a couple of hours after you stop, you are fine. If it persists into the next day or causes swelling, you did too much. Scale back slightly and try again. Talk to your doctor or physiotherapist before starting a new exercise programme, especially if your arthritis is severe. They can help you design a routine that is safe for your specific joints.\n\nThe bottom line\n\nExercise does not make arthritis worse. Inactivity makes arthritis worse. Movement is medicine for your joints. It may feel counterintuitive at first, but every step you take, every stretch you do, and every muscle you strengthen is an investment in keeping your joints functional for years to come. Many Kenyan patients who overcome the fear of exercise and commit to a gentle routine are surprised by how much better they feel within just a few weeks.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The myth that exercise makes arthritis worse is one of the most common and most harmful beliefs among people living with joint conditions. It seems logical on the surface: your joints hurt, so surely moving them more will cause more damage. Many well-meaning family members and friends reinforce this by saying things like \\"Rest more,\\" \\"Stop walking so much,\\" or \\"You should not be exercising with your knees like that.\\" But medical science tells us the exact opposite. Gentle, regular exercise is one of the single best things you can do for arthritis, and avoiding movement is one of the worst."},{"id":"s2","title":"What actually happens when you stop moving","body":"When you avoid exercise because of joint pain, a destructive cycle begins. The muscles around your joints weaken because they are not being used. Weaker muscles provide less support for the joint, which means the joint itself bears more stress. The cartilage in your joints depends on movement to receive nutrients, because it does not have its own blood supply. Instead, it absorbs nutrients from the joint fluid, and this only happens when the joint is moved and compressed, like a sponge being squeezed and released. Without movement, the cartilage slowly deteriorates. The joint becomes stiffer, more painful, and less functional. This leads to even less movement, and the cycle continues downward."},{"id":"s3","title":"What the research shows","body":"Studies involving thousands of arthritis patients around the world, including research conducted in East Africa, consistently show that regular exercise reduces pain, improves joint function, and slows the progression of the disease. Exercise strengthens the muscles that act as shock absorbers for your joints. It increases the production of synovial fluid, which lubricates the joint and reduces friction. It also releases natural pain-relieving chemicals called endorphins. Patients who exercise regularly report less pain, better sleep, improved mood, and greater ability to perform daily tasks like climbing stairs, carrying shopping from the market, or playing with their grandchildren."},{"id":"s4","title":"The right kind of exercise","body":"The key is choosing the right type of exercise. Low-impact activities are best. Walking is the simplest and most accessible option in Kenya. You do not need a gym membership or special equipment. A 20 to 30 minute walk on flat ground, done at a comfortable pace, provides excellent benefits for your joints. Swimming is another outstanding option because the water supports your body weight, taking pressure off your joints while allowing you to move freely. If you have access to a pool at a local sports club or county facility, even swimming once or twice a week can make a significant difference. Cycling, whether on a stationary bike or a regular bicycle on flat roads, is also gentle on the joints while building leg strength.\\n\\nStretching and flexibility exercises, like the morning routine described in other articles in this series, keep your joints mobile and reduce stiffness. Strengthening exercises using light resistance, such as lifting small water bottles or using resistance bands, build the muscles that protect your joints. Yoga and tai chi, which are becoming more available in Kenyan cities, combine stretching, strengthening, and balance work in one practice."},{"id":"s5","title":"What to avoid","body":"High-impact activities that put sudden, heavy stress on your joints should be approached with caution. Running on hard surfaces like tarmac, jumping, or playing high-impact sports like football can aggravate already damaged joints. Heavy weightlifting that puts excessive strain on affected joints is also not recommended. But this is not the same as saying you should avoid all exercise. It means choosing activities that work with your body rather than against it."},{"id":"s6","title":"How to start safely","body":"If you have been inactive for a while because of pain, start very slowly. Begin with just five to ten minutes of gentle walking or stretching. Increase gradually, adding a few minutes each week. Some discomfort during exercise is normal and expected. The difference between normal exercise discomfort and a warning sign is this: if the pain goes away within a couple of hours after you stop, you are fine. If it persists into the next day or causes swelling, you did too much. Scale back slightly and try again. Talk to your doctor or physiotherapist before starting a new exercise programme, especially if your arthritis is severe. They can help you design a routine that is safe for your specific joints."},{"id":"s7","title":"The bottom line","body":"Exercise does not make arthritis worse. Inactivity makes arthritis worse. Movement is medicine for your joints. It may feel counterintuitive at first, but every step you take, every stretch you do, and every muscle you strengthen is an investment in keeping your joints functional for years to come. Many Kenyan patients who overcome the fear of exercise and commit to a gentle routine are surprised by how much better they feel within just a few weeks."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'medical-truth'),
  (E'when-pain-makes-you-irritable-with-the-people-you-love', E'When pain makes you irritable with the people you love', E'ARTHRITIS', '{"ARTHRITIS"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'Living with chronic pain changes you in ways that other people cannot see. On the outside, you might look the same. In this lesson, you will recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition.', E'Living with chronic pain changes you in ways that other people cannot see. On the outside, you might look the same. But inside, the constant grinding of pain, the disrupted sleep, the worry about whether today will be a good day or a bad day — all of it wears down your patience, your energy, and your emotional reserves. If you have found yourself snapping at your spouse, being short with your children, feeling irritable when someone asks you a simple question, or withdrawing into silence because you just cannot deal with one more thing, know this: that is the pain talking, not the real you.\n\nWhy pain makes you irritable\n\nPain is not just a physical sensation. It activates the same stress pathways in your brain as fear and anxiety. When you are in pain, your body is in a constant state of low-level fight-or-flight, producing stress hormones like cortisol and adrenaline. These hormones make you hyper-reactive to small annoyances. The TV volume that would normally not bother you becomes unbearable. A child asking the same question twice feels like an assault on your nerves. Your partner''s suggestion to try a new remedy feels like they are dismissing what you are going through. None of these reactions are rational, but they are real, and they are not your fault.\n\nSleep disruption makes everything worse. Many arthritis patients in Kenya describe nights of tossing and turning, unable to find a comfortable position, waking up at 3am with throbbing knees or aching hands. Sleep deprivation alone makes people irritable, and when you combine it with chronic pain, the effect is doubled. You are running on an empty tank, and then being asked to perform as if everything is normal.\n\nThe power of naming what is happening\n\nOne of the most effective things you can do is simply name what is happening, both to yourself and to the people around you. Telling your family "I am in a lot of pain today and I may seem short-tempered. It is not about you" is incredibly powerful. It does three things. First, it prevents your loved ones from taking your mood personally. Second, it gives them the chance to offer support rather than reacting defensively. Third, it reminds you that the irritability is a symptom, not who you are.\n\nIn many Kenyan families, there is an expectation to bear pain silently, to be strong, to not complain. This cultural pressure can make it harder to open up about what you are going through. But silence creates distance. Your family sees you being withdrawn or angry and does not understand why. They may start walking on eggshells around you or, worse, pulling away emotionally. A brief, honest statement breaks that cycle.\n\nPractical strategies for difficult days\n\nOn days when pain is high and patience is low, have a plan. First, identify what helps you. For some people, it is a quiet room with no demands for 30 minutes. For others, it is a cup of hot chai made by someone who cares. Some people feel better with gentle movement, while others need to sit still. Know your own pattern and communicate it. You might say to your spouse, "Today is a tough day. What would really help me is if you could handle the children''s homework while I rest for an hour."\n\nSecond, give yourself permission to reduce your commitments on bad days. You do not have to attend every family gathering, cook every meal, or complete every chore. Asking for help is not weakness. In fact, it protects your relationships by preventing the blowups that happen when you push beyond what your body and mind can handle.\n\nThird, find at least one person you can be completely honest with about your pain. This might be your spouse, a sibling, a close friend, or a fellow arthritis patient you have met at the clinic. Having someone who understands, who does not judge, and who lets you vent without trying to fix everything is invaluable.\n\nWhen irritability becomes something more\n\nIf you find that you are constantly angry, unable to enjoy things you used to love, feeling hopeless about the future, or having thoughts of harming yourself, this may have moved beyond normal pain-related irritability into depression. Chronic pain and depression frequently occur together, and there is no shame in that. Talk to your doctor about it at your next appointment. Mental health support is available at many county hospitals in Kenya, and organisations like the Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 offer free, confidential support. Treating the depression can actually reduce your perception of pain, creating a positive cycle instead of a negative one.\n\nYour relationships matter\n\nThe people around you are part of your support system. Protecting those relationships by being honest about your pain, asking for what you need, and forgiving yourself on difficult days is not separate from managing your arthritis. It is a core part of it. You are not a burden. You are someone dealing with a difficult condition, and the people who love you want to help. Let them.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Living with chronic pain changes you in ways that other people cannot see. On the outside, you might look the same. But inside, the constant grinding of pain, the disrupted sleep, the worry about whether today will be a good day or a bad day — all of it wears down your patience, your energy, and your emotional reserves. If you have found yourself snapping at your spouse, being short with your children, feeling irritable when someone asks you a simple question, or withdrawing into silence because you just cannot deal with one more thing, know this: that is the pain talking, not the real you."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why pain makes you irritable","body":"Pain is not just a physical sensation. It activates the same stress pathways in your brain as fear and anxiety. When you are in pain, your body is in a constant state of low-level fight-or-flight, producing stress hormones like cortisol and adrenaline. These hormones make you hyper-reactive to small annoyances. The TV volume that would normally not bother you becomes unbearable. A child asking the same question twice feels like an assault on your nerves. Your partner''s suggestion to try a new remedy feels like they are dismissing what you are going through. None of these reactions are rational, but they are real, and they are not your fault.\\n\\nSleep disruption makes everything worse. Many arthritis patients in Kenya describe nights of tossing and turning, unable to find a comfortable position, waking up at 3am with throbbing knees or aching hands. Sleep deprivation alone makes people irritable, and when you combine it with chronic pain, the effect is doubled. You are running on an empty tank, and then being asked to perform as if everything is normal."},{"id":"s3","title":"The power of naming what is happening","body":"One of the most effective things you can do is simply name what is happening, both to yourself and to the people around you. Telling your family \\"I am in a lot of pain today and I may seem short-tempered. It is not about you\\" is incredibly powerful. It does three things. First, it prevents your loved ones from taking your mood personally. Second, it gives them the chance to offer support rather than reacting defensively. Third, it reminds you that the irritability is a symptom, not who you are.\\n\\nIn many Kenyan families, there is an expectation to bear pain silently, to be strong, to not complain. This cultural pressure can make it harder to open up about what you are going through. But silence creates distance. Your family sees you being withdrawn or angry and does not understand why. They may start walking on eggshells around you or, worse, pulling away emotionally. A brief, honest statement breaks that cycle."},{"id":"s4","title":"Practical strategies for difficult days","body":"On days when pain is high and patience is low, have a plan. First, identify what helps you. For some people, it is a quiet room with no demands for 30 minutes. For others, it is a cup of hot chai made by someone who cares. Some people feel better with gentle movement, while others need to sit still. Know your own pattern and communicate it. You might say to your spouse, \\"Today is a tough day. What would really help me is if you could handle the children''s homework while I rest for an hour.\\"\\n\\nSecond, give yourself permission to reduce your commitments on bad days. You do not have to attend every family gathering, cook every meal, or complete every chore. Asking for help is not weakness. In fact, it protects your relationships by preventing the blowups that happen when you push beyond what your body and mind can handle.\\n\\nThird, find at least one person you can be completely honest with about your pain. This might be your spouse, a sibling, a close friend, or a fellow arthritis patient you have met at the clinic. Having someone who understands, who does not judge, and who lets you vent without trying to fix everything is invaluable."},{"id":"s5","title":"When irritability becomes something more","body":"If you find that you are constantly angry, unable to enjoy things you used to love, feeling hopeless about the future, or having thoughts of harming yourself, this may have moved beyond normal pain-related irritability into depression. Chronic pain and depression frequently occur together, and there is no shame in that. Talk to your doctor about it at your next appointment. Mental health support is available at many county hospitals in Kenya, and organisations like the Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 offer free, confidential support. Treating the depression can actually reduce your perception of pain, creating a positive cycle instead of a negative one."},{"id":"s6","title":"Your relationships matter","body":"The people around you are part of your support system. Protecting those relationships by being honest about your pain, asking for what you need, and forgiving yourself on difficult days is not separate from managing your arthritis. It is a core part of it. You are not a burden. You are someone dealing with a difficult condition, and the people who love you want to help. Let them."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'community-care'),
  (E'tracking-your-pain-patterns-to-get-better-treatment', E'Tracking your pain patterns to get better treatment', E'ARTHRITIS', '{"ARTHRITIS"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'When you walk into your doctor''s appointment and they ask "How has your pain been?", most people give a vague answer like "It has been bad" or "Some days are okay." This is understandable, but it does not give your doctor enough information to fine-tune your treatment.', E'When you walk into your doctor''s appointment and they ask "How has your pain been?", most people give a vague answer like "It has been bad" or "Some days are okay." This is understandable, but it does not give your doctor enough information to fine-tune your treatment. Arthritis pain is rarely random. It follows patterns related to time of day, weather, activity, diet, stress, and sleep. By tracking these patterns, you transform yourself from a passive patient into an active partner in your own care, and the quality of treatment you receive improves dramatically.\n\nWhat to track each day\n\nGet a small notebook or use the notes app on your phone. Each evening, before bed, take two minutes to record the following. First, rate your overall pain from 0 to 10, where 0 is no pain and 10 is the worst pain you can imagine. Second, note which joints were most affected today. Was it your knees? Your hands? Your lower back? Third, write down what you did physically today: did you walk a lot, sit for long periods, do heavy lifting, or exercise? Fourth, note what you ate, especially if you had foods that might affect inflammation like deep-fried chips, nyama choma, alcohol, or lots of sugar. Fifth, record how well you slept last night, from 1 being terrible to 5 being great. Sixth, note the weather. Was it cold? Rainy? Hot and humid? Finally, record any medication changes or whether you missed a dose.\n\nThis might sound like a lot, but once you develop a shorthand, it takes less than two minutes. For example, a daily entry might look like: "Pain 6/10. Knees worst. Walked to market. Had chapati and beans. Sleep 3/5. Cold and rainy. Took all meds."\n\nWhat patterns to look for\n\nAfter two weeks of consistent tracking, sit down and review your entries. Look for connections between your pain levels and the other factors. Many arthritis patients in Kenya discover surprising patterns. Some find that their pain is significantly worse on cold mornings, which is common during the June to August season in highland areas like Nairobi, Nyeri, or Eldoret. Others discover that their joints flare up the day after eating certain foods or after drinking alcohol. Some notice that days with gentle exercise actually have lower pain scores than rest days, which surprises them.\n\nPay special attention to sleep quality. There is a strong, well-documented connection between poor sleep and increased arthritis pain. If you consistently see that nights rated 1 or 2 for sleep are followed by days with pain scores of 7 or higher, improving your sleep should become a priority in your treatment plan.\n\nHow to present this data to your doctor\n\nWhen you visit your doctor at the hospital or clinic, bring your notebook or phone with your tracking data. You might say something like, "Doctor, I have been tracking my pain for three weeks. My average pain is about 5 out of 10, but it goes up to 7 or 8 on cold days and after poor sleep. My knees are worst in the morning, but my hands are worse in the evening. I noticed that on days when I walk for 20 minutes, my pain is actually lower." This kind of information is incredibly valuable. It helps your doctor decide whether to adjust your medication, recommend physiotherapy, suggest sleep interventions, or investigate whether a specific food is triggering your flares.\n\nUsing tracking to evaluate treatment changes\n\nWhen your doctor makes a change to your treatment, whether it is a new medication, a different dose, or a recommended exercise, your tracking diary becomes essential for evaluating whether the change is working. Without tracking, you are relying on memory and general impressions, which are unreliable. With tracking, you can say with confidence, "Since starting the new medication two weeks ago, my average pain score has dropped from 6.5 to 4.2, and my morning stiffness is resolving 20 minutes faster." This is the kind of data that helps your doctor make precise adjustments rather than guessing.\n\nFree tools you can use\n\nA simple exercise book costing KES 30 to 50 from any bookshop or stationery kiosk works perfectly. If you prefer your phone, the standard notes app is sufficient. There are also free pain tracking apps available on Android, which is the most common smartphone platform in Kenya. Some patients prefer to use a WhatsApp message to themselves as a daily diary, which has the advantage of being timestamped automatically.\n\nThe empowerment of understanding your pain\n\nTracking your pain does something beyond improving medical care. It gives you a sense of control. When pain feels random and unpredictable, it is more frightening and more demoralising. When you can see that cold weather, poor sleep, and skipping exercise are your main triggers, you have something concrete to work with. You can plan for cold days by warming up your joints before getting out of bed, prioritise sleep hygiene, and commit to gentle daily exercise. Knowledge turns you from a victim of your pain into a manager of it.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you walk into your doctor''s appointment and they ask \\"How has your pain been?\\", most people give a vague answer like \\"It has been bad\\" or \\"Some days are okay.\\" This is understandable, but it does not give your doctor enough information to fine-tune your treatment. Arthritis pain is rarely random. It follows patterns related to time of day, weather, activity, diet, stress, and sleep. By tracking these patterns, you transform yourself from a passive patient into an active partner in your own care, and the quality of treatment you receive improves dramatically."},{"id":"s2","title":"What to track each day","body":"Get a small notebook or use the notes app on your phone. Each evening, before bed, take two minutes to record the following. First, rate your overall pain from 0 to 10, where 0 is no pain and 10 is the worst pain you can imagine. Second, note which joints were most affected today. Was it your knees? Your hands? Your lower back? Third, write down what you did physically today: did you walk a lot, sit for long periods, do heavy lifting, or exercise? Fourth, note what you ate, especially if you had foods that might affect inflammation like deep-fried chips, nyama choma, alcohol, or lots of sugar. Fifth, record how well you slept last night, from 1 being terrible to 5 being great. Sixth, note the weather. Was it cold? Rainy? Hot and humid? Finally, record any medication changes or whether you missed a dose.\\n\\nThis might sound like a lot, but once you develop a shorthand, it takes less than two minutes. For example, a daily entry might look like: \\"Pain 6/10. Knees worst. Walked to market. Had chapati and beans. Sleep 3/5. Cold and rainy. Took all meds.\\""},{"id":"s3","title":"What patterns to look for","body":"After two weeks of consistent tracking, sit down and review your entries. Look for connections between your pain levels and the other factors. Many arthritis patients in Kenya discover surprising patterns. Some find that their pain is significantly worse on cold mornings, which is common during the June to August season in highland areas like Nairobi, Nyeri, or Eldoret. Others discover that their joints flare up the day after eating certain foods or after drinking alcohol. Some notice that days with gentle exercise actually have lower pain scores than rest days, which surprises them.\\n\\nPay special attention to sleep quality. There is a strong, well-documented connection between poor sleep and increased arthritis pain. If you consistently see that nights rated 1 or 2 for sleep are followed by days with pain scores of 7 or higher, improving your sleep should become a priority in your treatment plan."},{"id":"s4","title":"How to present this data to your doctor","body":"When you visit your doctor at the hospital or clinic, bring your notebook or phone with your tracking data. You might say something like, \\"Doctor, I have been tracking my pain for three weeks. My average pain is about 5 out of 10, but it goes up to 7 or 8 on cold days and after poor sleep. My knees are worst in the morning, but my hands are worse in the evening. I noticed that on days when I walk for 20 minutes, my pain is actually lower.\\" This kind of information is incredibly valuable. It helps your doctor decide whether to adjust your medication, recommend physiotherapy, suggest sleep interventions, or investigate whether a specific food is triggering your flares."},{"id":"s5","title":"Using tracking to evaluate treatment changes","body":"When your doctor makes a change to your treatment, whether it is a new medication, a different dose, or a recommended exercise, your tracking diary becomes essential for evaluating whether the change is working. Without tracking, you are relying on memory and general impressions, which are unreliable. With tracking, you can say with confidence, \\"Since starting the new medication two weeks ago, my average pain score has dropped from 6.5 to 4.2, and my morning stiffness is resolving 20 minutes faster.\\" This is the kind of data that helps your doctor make precise adjustments rather than guessing."},{"id":"s6","title":"Free tools you can use","body":"A simple exercise book costing KES 30 to 50 from any bookshop or stationery kiosk works perfectly. If you prefer your phone, the standard notes app is sufficient. There are also free pain tracking apps available on Android, which is the most common smartphone platform in Kenya. Some patients prefer to use a WhatsApp message to themselves as a daily diary, which has the advantage of being timestamped automatically."},{"id":"s7","title":"The empowerment of understanding your pain","body":"Tracking your pain does something beyond improving medical care. It gives you a sense of control. When pain feels random and unpredictable, it is more frightening and more demoralising. When you can see that cold weather, poor sleep, and skipping exercise are your main triggers, you have something concrete to work with. You can plan for cold days by warming up your joints before getting out of bed, prioritise sleep hygiene, and commit to gentle daily exercise. Knowledge turns you from a victim of your pain into a manager of it."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'health-diary'),
  (E'adapting-your-home-for-easier-daily-living', E'Adapting your home for easier daily living', E'ARTHRITIS', '{"ARTHRITIS"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'When you are first diagnosed with arthritis, or when it progresses to the point where daily tasks become difficult, there is a natural temptation to fight against it and insist on doing everything exactly the way you always have.', E'When you are first diagnosed with arthritis, or when it progresses to the point where daily tasks become difficult, there is a natural temptation to fight against it and insist on doing everything exactly the way you always have. Opening jars with brute force despite the pain. Refusing to use a walking stick. Pushing through agony to cook a full meal standing at the counter. This determination is understandable, and it speaks to your strength. But there is a smarter approach: adapting your environment so that daily living requires less joint strain, preserving your independence for the long term rather than wearing out your joints in the short term.\n\nThis is not giving up\n\nLet us address the elephant in the room. In many Kenyan families and communities, using assistive devices or making home modifications can feel like admitting defeat. You might worry that your children will see you as old or incapable, or that neighbours will talk. But consider this: wearing glasses is not giving up on your eyes. Using a jiko instead of rubbing sticks together is not giving up on making fire. Adaptations are intelligent responses to a situation. They free up your energy for the things that matter, like spending time with your grandchildren, going to church, or running your business, instead of wasting that energy fighting with a jar lid.\n\nChanges in the kitchen\n\nThe kitchen is where many arthritis patients struggle most, especially in Kenyan homes where meals are prepared from scratch daily. Here are practical changes that make a real difference. First, sit on a stool while cooking. Standing for 30 or 40 minutes while preparing ugali, chopping sukuma wiki, and stirring stew puts enormous strain on your hip and knee joints. A sturdy stool at counter height lets you do the same work with far less pain. Second, use lightweight pots and pans. A heavy sufuria full of water for boiling githeri can weigh several kilograms. Switch to lighter aluminium options or fill pots on the stove rather than carrying them full. Third, buy pre-cut vegetables when available, or ask a family member to help with the chopping. Fourth, use a jar opener or rubber grip to open stubborn lids. These cost between KES 200 and 500 at kitchen supply shops in town.\n\nChanges around the house\n\nDoor handles are a surprisingly big issue for people with hand and wrist arthritis. Round doorknobs require a tight grip and twisting motion that can be extremely painful. Lever-style handles, which you press down rather than twist, are much easier. Replacing doorknobs costs around KES 300 to 800 per handle at most hardware shops in Kenya, and it is a change you will appreciate every single day. If you cannot replace them right now, wrapping a thick rubber band around the knob gives you more grip.\n\nA raised toilet seat is one of the most impactful modifications for people with hip or knee arthritis. Getting up from a low toilet puts tremendous force through your knee joints. Raised toilet seat attachments are available at medical supply shops and some pharmacies in Nairobi and other major towns for around KES 3,000 to 8,000. They fit over your existing toilet and make sitting down and standing up significantly easier.\n\nLong-handled reachers, which are like extended grabbers, allow you to pick things up from the floor, reach items on high shelves, or pull clothes from a washing line without bending or stretching painfully. These typically cost between KES 1,500 and 3,000 at medical equipment suppliers.\n\nChanges in your daily routine\n\nBeyond physical modifications, adjusting how you organise your day can reduce joint strain. Move frequently used items to waist-height shelves and counters so you do not have to reach up or bend down repeatedly. Do heavy tasks like laundry or cleaning in the morning when your pain medication is at peak effectiveness and your energy is highest. Break big tasks into smaller chunks with rest periods in between, rather than trying to clean the entire house in one go. Use a backpack or trolley bag instead of carrying heavy shopping bags in your hands.\n\nGetting professional help\n\nOccupational therapists specialise in helping people adapt their homes and routines to manage physical limitations. In Kenya, occupational therapy services are available at Kenyatta National Hospital, many county referral hospitals, and some private facilities. An occupational therapist can visit your home or discuss your layout with you and suggest specific, affordable modifications tailored to your situation. If you are covered under SHA, ask your doctor for a referral. Even a single session can provide ideas that make daily life dramatically easier.\n\nThe goal is independence, not dependence\n\nEvery adaptation you make is an investment in your independence. The person who uses a jar opener and a raised toilet seat can live independently for years longer than the person who refuses all help and damages their joints further. The stool in the kitchen means you can still cook the meals your family loves. The lever handles mean you can open every door in your house without pain. These are not signs of weakness. They are the decisions of someone who is managing their condition wisely, and that is something to be proud of.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you are first diagnosed with arthritis, or when it progresses to the point where daily tasks become difficult, there is a natural temptation to fight against it and insist on doing everything exactly the way you always have. Opening jars with brute force despite the pain. Refusing to use a walking stick. Pushing through agony to cook a full meal standing at the counter. This determination is understandable, and it speaks to your strength. But there is a smarter approach: adapting your environment so that daily living requires less joint strain, preserving your independence for the long term rather than wearing out your joints in the short term."},{"id":"s2","title":"This is not giving up","body":"Let us address the elephant in the room. In many Kenyan families and communities, using assistive devices or making home modifications can feel like admitting defeat. You might worry that your children will see you as old or incapable, or that neighbours will talk. But consider this: wearing glasses is not giving up on your eyes. Using a jiko instead of rubbing sticks together is not giving up on making fire. Adaptations are intelligent responses to a situation. They free up your energy for the things that matter, like spending time with your grandchildren, going to church, or running your business, instead of wasting that energy fighting with a jar lid."},{"id":"s3","title":"Changes in the kitchen","body":"The kitchen is where many arthritis patients struggle most, especially in Kenyan homes where meals are prepared from scratch daily. Here are practical changes that make a real difference. First, sit on a stool while cooking. Standing for 30 or 40 minutes while preparing ugali, chopping sukuma wiki, and stirring stew puts enormous strain on your hip and knee joints. A sturdy stool at counter height lets you do the same work with far less pain. Second, use lightweight pots and pans. A heavy sufuria full of water for boiling githeri can weigh several kilograms. Switch to lighter aluminium options or fill pots on the stove rather than carrying them full. Third, buy pre-cut vegetables when available, or ask a family member to help with the chopping. Fourth, use a jar opener or rubber grip to open stubborn lids. These cost between KES 200 and 500 at kitchen supply shops in town."},{"id":"s4","title":"Changes around the house","body":"Door handles are a surprisingly big issue for people with hand and wrist arthritis. Round doorknobs require a tight grip and twisting motion that can be extremely painful. Lever-style handles, which you press down rather than twist, are much easier. Replacing doorknobs costs around KES 300 to 800 per handle at most hardware shops in Kenya, and it is a change you will appreciate every single day. If you cannot replace them right now, wrapping a thick rubber band around the knob gives you more grip.\\n\\nA raised toilet seat is one of the most impactful modifications for people with hip or knee arthritis. Getting up from a low toilet puts tremendous force through your knee joints. Raised toilet seat attachments are available at medical supply shops and some pharmacies in Nairobi and other major towns for around KES 3,000 to 8,000. They fit over your existing toilet and make sitting down and standing up significantly easier.\\n\\nLong-handled reachers, which are like extended grabbers, allow you to pick things up from the floor, reach items on high shelves, or pull clothes from a washing line without bending or stretching painfully. These typically cost between KES 1,500 and 3,000 at medical equipment suppliers."},{"id":"s5","title":"Changes in your daily routine","body":"Beyond physical modifications, adjusting how you organise your day can reduce joint strain. Move frequently used items to waist-height shelves and counters so you do not have to reach up or bend down repeatedly. Do heavy tasks like laundry or cleaning in the morning when your pain medication is at peak effectiveness and your energy is highest. Break big tasks into smaller chunks with rest periods in between, rather than trying to clean the entire house in one go. Use a backpack or trolley bag instead of carrying heavy shopping bags in your hands."},{"id":"s6","title":"Getting professional help","body":"Occupational therapists specialise in helping people adapt their homes and routines to manage physical limitations. In Kenya, occupational therapy services are available at Kenyatta National Hospital, many county referral hospitals, and some private facilities. An occupational therapist can visit your home or discuss your layout with you and suggest specific, affordable modifications tailored to your situation. If you are covered under SHA, ask your doctor for a referral. Even a single session can provide ideas that make daily life dramatically easier."},{"id":"s7","title":"The goal is independence, not dependence","body":"Every adaptation you make is an investment in your independence. The person who uses a jar opener and a raised toilet seat can live independently for years longer than the person who refuses all help and damages their joints further. The stool in the kitchen means you can still cook the meals your family loves. The lever handles mean you can open every door in your house without pain. These are not signs of weakness. They are the decisions of someone who is managing their condition wisely, and that is something to be proud of."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'new-beginning'),
  (E'you-walked-further-this-week-than-last-month', E'You walked further this week than last month', E'ARTHRITIS', '{"ARTHRITIS"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Take a moment to recognise what you have achieved. If you can walk further this week than you could last month, your body is telling you something important: what you are doing is working.', E'Take a moment to recognise what you have achieved. If you can walk further this week than you could last month, your body is telling you something important: what you are doing is working. Your joints are responding. Your muscles are getting stronger. The effort you have been putting into your stretches, your walks, your dietary changes, and your medication routine is paying off in the most practical way possible — you can move more, and that means you can live more.\n\nWhy walking distance matters\n\nFor people with arthritis, walking distance is one of the best measures of joint health and overall function. It reflects multiple factors working together: the strength of the muscles supporting your joints, the flexibility of the joints themselves, your pain levels, your cardiovascular fitness, and your confidence in your body. When your walking distance improves, it means all of these things are trending in the right direction. Doctors and physiotherapists around the world use walking tests as a standard measure of arthritis management. You are doing the same thing, just in your daily life instead of a clinic.\n\nEven small gains are real gains\n\nYou might think that walking an extra 100 or 200 metres does not sound like much. But consider what it means in practical terms. That extra distance might be the difference between being able to walk to the local duka and having to send someone. It might mean you can make it to church without needing to rest halfway. It might mean you can walk around the market to compare prices instead of buying from the first stall because you cannot go further. In Kenyan daily life, where many people walk as their primary means of getting around, every extra metre of comfortable walking range directly translates to independence and quality of life.\n\nHow to track your progress\n\nYour phone''s built-in step counter is a simple and free tool for monitoring your walking. Most smartphones, including the budget Android phones common in Kenya, have a health app or pedometer built in. If yours does not, there are free apps you can download. Check your step count at the end of each day and note it down. Over weeks and months, you will see trends. You do not need to hit 10,000 steps a day — that number is not based on medical evidence for arthritis patients. What matters is your personal trend. If you were averaging 2,000 steps a day last month and now you are averaging 2,500, that is a 25 percent improvement. That is enormous.\n\nAnother simple method is to use landmarks. Pick a route you walk regularly, like from your home to a particular shop, church, or bus stage. Time yourself or note how you feel when you arrive. Over time, you might find that a walk that used to leave you sore and exhausted now feels manageable. That is progress.\n\nWhat made this improvement possible\n\nThink about what has changed in recent weeks. Have you been more consistent with your morning stretches? Have you been taking your medication on time? Have you been eating more anti-inflammatory foods like omena, sukuma wiki, and ginger? Have you been resting when your body needs it instead of pushing through severe pain? Whatever combination of changes led to this improvement, make a mental note of it. This is your personal formula. When you have bad days, and you will, because arthritis is a condition that fluctuates, remembering what works helps you get back on track faster.\n\nCelebrate this, then keep going\n\nIn Kenyan culture, we celebrate milestones. A child''s first steps, a student''s graduation, a business opening. Your improved walking distance deserves recognition too. Tell your family. Tell your doctor at your next visit. Share it with other patients you know. Celebration reinforces positive behaviour. When you acknowledge your progress, your brain associates the effort with reward, making it easier to stay motivated.\n\nBut celebration is not the same as complacency. Your joints will continue to benefit from the routines you have built. Keep walking. Keep stretching. Keep eating well. Keep tracking your medication and your pain patterns. The goal is not to run a marathon. The goal is to maintain and gradually improve your functional ability so that arthritis does not dictate what you can and cannot do.\n\nThe road ahead\n\nYour joints may never be completely pain-free, and that is an honest reality of living with arthritis. But pain-free is not the only goal that matters. Functional is what matters. Functional means you can walk to the places you need to go. It means you can cook for your family, play with your grandchildren, attend social gatherings, and maintain your independence. You are moving toward that, and the evidence is in your own feet. The distance you covered this week is proof that your body, with the right support, is capable of more than arthritis wants you to believe. Keep walking.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Take a moment to recognise what you have achieved. If you can walk further this week than you could last month, your body is telling you something important: what you are doing is working. Your joints are responding. Your muscles are getting stronger. The effort you have been putting into your stretches, your walks, your dietary changes, and your medication routine is paying off in the most practical way possible — you can move more, and that means you can live more."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why walking distance matters","body":"For people with arthritis, walking distance is one of the best measures of joint health and overall function. It reflects multiple factors working together: the strength of the muscles supporting your joints, the flexibility of the joints themselves, your pain levels, your cardiovascular fitness, and your confidence in your body. When your walking distance improves, it means all of these things are trending in the right direction. Doctors and physiotherapists around the world use walking tests as a standard measure of arthritis management. You are doing the same thing, just in your daily life instead of a clinic."},{"id":"s3","title":"Even small gains are real gains","body":"You might think that walking an extra 100 or 200 metres does not sound like much. But consider what it means in practical terms. That extra distance might be the difference between being able to walk to the local duka and having to send someone. It might mean you can make it to church without needing to rest halfway. It might mean you can walk around the market to compare prices instead of buying from the first stall because you cannot go further. In Kenyan daily life, where many people walk as their primary means of getting around, every extra metre of comfortable walking range directly translates to independence and quality of life."},{"id":"s4","title":"How to track your progress","body":"Your phone''s built-in step counter is a simple and free tool for monitoring your walking. Most smartphones, including the budget Android phones common in Kenya, have a health app or pedometer built in. If yours does not, there are free apps you can download. Check your step count at the end of each day and note it down. Over weeks and months, you will see trends. You do not need to hit 10,000 steps a day — that number is not based on medical evidence for arthritis patients. What matters is your personal trend. If you were averaging 2,000 steps a day last month and now you are averaging 2,500, that is a 25 percent improvement. That is enormous.\\n\\nAnother simple method is to use landmarks. Pick a route you walk regularly, like from your home to a particular shop, church, or bus stage. Time yourself or note how you feel when you arrive. Over time, you might find that a walk that used to leave you sore and exhausted now feels manageable. That is progress."},{"id":"s5","title":"What made this improvement possible","body":"Think about what has changed in recent weeks. Have you been more consistent with your morning stretches? Have you been taking your medication on time? Have you been eating more anti-inflammatory foods like omena, sukuma wiki, and ginger? Have you been resting when your body needs it instead of pushing through severe pain? Whatever combination of changes led to this improvement, make a mental note of it. This is your personal formula. When you have bad days, and you will, because arthritis is a condition that fluctuates, remembering what works helps you get back on track faster."},{"id":"s6","title":"Celebrate this, then keep going","body":"In Kenyan culture, we celebrate milestones. A child''s first steps, a student''s graduation, a business opening. Your improved walking distance deserves recognition too. Tell your family. Tell your doctor at your next visit. Share it with other patients you know. Celebration reinforces positive behaviour. When you acknowledge your progress, your brain associates the effort with reward, making it easier to stay motivated.\\n\\nBut celebration is not the same as complacency. Your joints will continue to benefit from the routines you have built. Keep walking. Keep stretching. Keep eating well. Keep tracking your medication and your pain patterns. The goal is not to run a marathon. The goal is to maintain and gradually improve your functional ability so that arthritis does not dictate what you can and cannot do."},{"id":"s7","title":"The road ahead","body":"Your joints may never be completely pain-free, and that is an honest reality of living with arthritis. But pain-free is not the only goal that matters. Functional is what matters. Functional means you can walk to the places you need to go. It means you can cook for your family, play with your grandchildren, attend social gatherings, and maintain your independence. You are moving toward that, and the evidence is in your own feet. The distance you covered this week is proof that your body, with the right support, is capable of more than arthritis wants you to believe. Keep walking."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'achievement'),
  (E'the-gut-brain-connection-how-food-affects-your-mood', E'The gut-brain connection: how food affects your mood', E'MENTAL_HEALTH', '{"MENTAL_HEALTH"}', E'DIETARY', E'Most people think of food as fuel for the body — energy for your muscles, nutrition for your bones, vitamins for your immune system.', E'Most people think of food as fuel for the body — energy for your muscles, nutrition for your bones, vitamins for your immune system. But your brain is the most energy-hungry organ you have, consuming about 20 percent of everything you eat, and what you feed it directly affects how you think and feel. There is a powerful connection between your gut and your brain, and understanding it can give you a practical, affordable tool for managing your mental health alongside any other treatment you are receiving.\n\nYour gut is a mood factory\n\nYour digestive system produces about 90 percent of your body''s serotonin, the chemical that regulates mood, sleep, and feelings of wellbeing. The bacteria living in your gut, trillions of tiny organisms collectively called the gut microbiome, play a direct role in producing serotonin and other mood-regulating chemicals. When your gut bacteria are healthy and diverse, they produce more of these beneficial compounds. When your gut is unhealthy, due to a poor diet, stress, or illness, the production drops, and your mood suffers. This is not alternative medicine or guesswork. It is established science that has been confirmed by research at institutions around the world.\n\nFermented foods support your gut bacteria\n\nIn Kenya, we have a rich tradition of fermented foods that is often undervalued. Mursik, the fermented milk traditionally prepared by the Kalenjin community, is a natural probiotic that supports gut health. Yoghurt, whether homemade or bought from the shop, contains similar beneficial bacteria. Fermented uji, which is common in many Kenyan households, is another excellent source. These foods introduce beneficial bacteria directly into your gut, helping to restore and maintain a healthy microbiome. Try to include at least one serving of fermented food in your daily diet. A cup of yoghurt with your breakfast, a glass of mursik with lunch, or fermented porridge in the morning are all simple additions that can make a real difference over time.\n\nThe breakfast that builds a better mood\n\nWhat you eat first thing in the morning sets the tone for your brain chemistry for the rest of the day. A breakfast of uji made with millet or sorghum flour, topped with groundnuts and sliced banana, is one of the best mood-supporting meals you can eat. Here is why. The whole grains provide slow-release energy that keeps your blood sugar stable, avoiding the crashes that worsen anxiety and low mood. The groundnuts provide protein and healthy fats that your brain needs for cell repair and neurotransmitter production. The banana contains tryptophan, an amino acid that your brain converts directly into serotonin. Together, these affordable, widely available ingredients give your brain the raw materials it needs to produce the chemicals that help you feel calm, focused, and stable.\n\nContrast this with a common breakfast of white bread with margarine and heavily sweetened tea. The refined carbohydrates cause a rapid blood sugar spike followed by a crash. These crashes trigger cortisol, the stress hormone, which increases anxiety and irritability. Over time, a diet high in sugar and refined carbohydrates is associated with higher rates of depression.\n\nWhat to eat throughout the day\n\nFor lunch, a plate of githeri with plenty of beans provides protein and complex carbohydrates that sustain your energy and mood. Add sukuma wiki or spinach for folate, a B vitamin important for serotonin production. If you can add omena or sardines, the omega-3 fatty acids have been shown to reduce symptoms of depression in multiple studies.\n\nFor dinner, a stew made with lentils, tomatoes, onions, and turmeric served with brown ugali or sweet potatoes provides a balanced mix of nutrients your brain needs. Turmeric contains curcumin, which has been studied for its antidepressant properties.\n\nFoods that undermine your mental health\n\nExcessive sugar is one of the biggest dietary enemies of mental health, including sodas, heavily sweetened chai, and packaged juices. Alcohol is a depressant that disrupts sleep and worsens anxiety the day after drinking. Heavily processed foods provide little nutritional value and can negatively affect gut bacteria.\n\nYou do not need to eliminate these foods completely. Occasional mandazi with chai or a celebratory drink are part of life. The goal is to ensure the foundation of your diet is built on whole grains, vegetables, legumes, fermented foods, and fish.\n\nA practical weekly plan\n\nBreakfasts: alternate between uji with groundnuts and banana, and eggs with whole wheat bread and lightly sweetened tea. Lunches: githeri, beans and rice, or lentil stew with sukuma wiki. Dinners: fish stew, chicken with vegetables, or bean curry with ugali. Include one fermented food daily. Most of these ingredients are available at any market in Kenya and affordable for most households. What you eat is one more tool in your mental health toolkit, and it is one you use three times a day.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Most people think of food as fuel for the body — energy for your muscles, nutrition for your bones, vitamins for your immune system. But your brain is the most energy-hungry organ you have, consuming about 20 percent of everything you eat, and what you feed it directly affects how you think and feel. There is a powerful connection between your gut and your brain, and understanding it can give you a practical, affordable tool for managing your mental health alongside any other treatment you are receiving."},{"id":"s2","title":"Your gut is a mood factory","body":"Your digestive system produces about 90 percent of your body''s serotonin, the chemical that regulates mood, sleep, and feelings of wellbeing. The bacteria living in your gut, trillions of tiny organisms collectively called the gut microbiome, play a direct role in producing serotonin and other mood-regulating chemicals. When your gut bacteria are healthy and diverse, they produce more of these beneficial compounds. When your gut is unhealthy, due to a poor diet, stress, or illness, the production drops, and your mood suffers. This is not alternative medicine or guesswork. It is established science that has been confirmed by research at institutions around the world."},{"id":"s3","title":"Fermented foods support your gut bacteria","body":"In Kenya, we have a rich tradition of fermented foods that is often undervalued. Mursik, the fermented milk traditionally prepared by the Kalenjin community, is a natural probiotic that supports gut health. Yoghurt, whether homemade or bought from the shop, contains similar beneficial bacteria. Fermented uji, which is common in many Kenyan households, is another excellent source. These foods introduce beneficial bacteria directly into your gut, helping to restore and maintain a healthy microbiome. Try to include at least one serving of fermented food in your daily diet. A cup of yoghurt with your breakfast, a glass of mursik with lunch, or fermented porridge in the morning are all simple additions that can make a real difference over time."},{"id":"s4","title":"The breakfast that builds a better mood","body":"What you eat first thing in the morning sets the tone for your brain chemistry for the rest of the day. A breakfast of uji made with millet or sorghum flour, topped with groundnuts and sliced banana, is one of the best mood-supporting meals you can eat. Here is why. The whole grains provide slow-release energy that keeps your blood sugar stable, avoiding the crashes that worsen anxiety and low mood. The groundnuts provide protein and healthy fats that your brain needs for cell repair and neurotransmitter production. The banana contains tryptophan, an amino acid that your brain converts directly into serotonin. Together, these affordable, widely available ingredients give your brain the raw materials it needs to produce the chemicals that help you feel calm, focused, and stable.\\n\\nContrast this with a common breakfast of white bread with margarine and heavily sweetened tea. The refined carbohydrates cause a rapid blood sugar spike followed by a crash. These crashes trigger cortisol, the stress hormone, which increases anxiety and irritability. Over time, a diet high in sugar and refined carbohydrates is associated with higher rates of depression."},{"id":"s5","title":"What to eat throughout the day","body":"For lunch, a plate of githeri with plenty of beans provides protein and complex carbohydrates that sustain your energy and mood. Add sukuma wiki or spinach for folate, a B vitamin important for serotonin production. If you can add omena or sardines, the omega-3 fatty acids have been shown to reduce symptoms of depression in multiple studies.\\n\\nFor dinner, a stew made with lentils, tomatoes, onions, and turmeric served with brown ugali or sweet potatoes provides a balanced mix of nutrients your brain needs. Turmeric contains curcumin, which has been studied for its antidepressant properties."},{"id":"s6","title":"Foods that undermine your mental health","body":"Excessive sugar is one of the biggest dietary enemies of mental health, including sodas, heavily sweetened chai, and packaged juices. Alcohol is a depressant that disrupts sleep and worsens anxiety the day after drinking. Heavily processed foods provide little nutritional value and can negatively affect gut bacteria.\\n\\nYou do not need to eliminate these foods completely. Occasional mandazi with chai or a celebratory drink are part of life. The goal is to ensure the foundation of your diet is built on whole grains, vegetables, legumes, fermented foods, and fish."},{"id":"s7","title":"A practical weekly plan","body":"Breakfasts: alternate between uji with groundnuts and banana, and eggs with whole wheat bread and lightly sweetened tea. Lunches: githeri, beans and rice, or lentil stew with sukuma wiki. Dinners: fish stew, chicken with vegetables, or bean curry with ugali. Include one fermented food daily. Most of these ingredients are available at any market in Kenya and affordable for most households. What you eat is one more tool in your mental health toolkit, and it is one you use three times a day."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'market-shopping'),
  (E'the-10-minute-walk-that-works-like-an-antidepressant', E'The 10-minute walk that works like an antidepressant', E'MENTAL_HEALTH', '{"MENTAL_HEALTH"}', E'EXERCISE', E'On the days when depression is at its heaviest, when getting out of bed feels like a monumental task, when the world seems grey and pointless, the last thing you want to hear is "just go for a walk." It sounds dismissive.', E'On the days when depression is at its heaviest, when getting out of bed feels like a monumental task, when the world seems grey and pointless, the last thing you want to hear is "just go for a walk." It sounds dismissive. It sounds too simple to work for something that feels so overwhelming. But here is what the research consistently shows: just 10 minutes of brisk walking produces a measurable improvement in mood that can last for up to two hours. Not 10 minutes of running. Not an hour at the gym. Just a walk, at a pace that is slightly faster than your normal stroll, for the time it takes to boil chai.\n\nWhat happens in your brain when you walk\n\nThe moment you start moving, your body begins releasing a cocktail of chemicals that directly combat the biology of depression. Endorphins, your body''s natural painkillers, reduce physical tension and create a mild sense of wellbeing. Serotonin production increases, which is the same chemical that antidepressant medications target. Your brain also releases something called Brain-Derived Neurotrophic Factor, or BDNF, which protects and repairs brain cells that are damaged by chronic stress and depression. These are not vague, feel-good claims. They are measurable chemical changes that scientists can observe in brain scans and blood tests.\n\nAdditionally, walking outside exposes you to natural light, which regulates your circadian rhythm, the internal clock that controls sleep and wakefulness. Many people with depression have disrupted circadian rhythms, and even brief exposure to daylight helps reset them. In Kenya, where we are blessed with abundant sunshine for most of the year, stepping outside for a walk gives you this benefit naturally and freely.\n\nThe trick for the hardest days\n\nThe biggest challenge is not the walk itself. It is getting started. Depression steals motivation. It makes everything feel impossible and pointless. The voice in your head says, "What is the point? A walk will not fix anything." Here is a strategy that many people find helpful: do not commit to a walk. Commit to putting on your shoes. That is it. Just put on your shoes. Once your shoes are on, commit to stepping outside your door. Just the doorstep. Once you are at the doorstep, commit to walking to the end of your road. Just to the corner and back. Most people find that once they have started moving, they go further than they planned. But even if you only make it to the end of the road and back, you have still moved, and your brain still benefits.\n\nAnother approach is to combine walking with a small errand. Walk to the nearby duka to buy milk. Walk to the kiosk for airtime. Walk to the neighbour''s house to return something you borrowed. Giving the walk a purpose beyond exercise makes it feel less like a chore and more like a normal part of your day.\n\nWhat if 10 minutes feels impossible\n\nThen try five minutes. Or three. Or one. Seriously. Walk to your gate and back. Stand outside for 60 seconds and feel the sun on your face. Any movement is better than no movement. On the very worst days, just sitting outside on your doorstep for a few minutes, where there is natural light and fresh air, is a step in the right direction. Depression tells you that if you cannot do something perfectly or fully, there is no point doing it at all. That is the depression lying to you. Every small action counts.\n\nBuilding a walking routine\n\nIf you can manage 10 minutes today, that is your starting point. Try to do it at the same time each day, because routine is powerful for mental health. Morning walks are ideal because they combine exercise with exposure to morning light, which is most effective at resetting your circadian rhythm. But any time of day works. Walk in the evening if that suits your schedule. Walk during your lunch break. Walk whenever you can.\n\nOver time, try to extend your walks gradually. From 10 minutes to 15, then to 20, then to 30. At 30 minutes of brisk walking per day, the antidepressant effect of exercise becomes comparable to medication for mild to moderate depression. This does not mean you should stop your medication without consulting your doctor. It means exercise and medication together can be more effective than either one alone.\n\nMake it social when you can\n\nWalking with another person adds a social dimension that amplifies the mental health benefits. If you have a friend, neighbour, or family member who is willing to walk with you, the combination of exercise and social connection is more powerful than either alone. You do not have to talk about your feelings. You do not have to explain what you are going through. Just walking together, maybe chatting about everyday things, is enough. In many Kenyan neighbourhoods, there are people walking in the early morning or evening. Joining that flow, even anonymously, can reduce the sense of isolation that depression creates.\n\nA prescription you write yourself\n\nThink of your daily walk as medication that you prescribe for yourself. It costs nothing. It has no negative side effects. It is available 365 days a year. And unlike a pill, it gets you out of your house, into the sunlight, and moving through the world. On the days when you manage it, acknowledge what you did. You fought against the weight of depression and you moved. That is not a small thing. That is courage.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"On the days when depression is at its heaviest, when getting out of bed feels like a monumental task, when the world seems grey and pointless, the last thing you want to hear is \\"just go for a walk.\\" It sounds dismissive. It sounds too simple to work for something that feels so overwhelming. But here is what the research consistently shows: just 10 minutes of brisk walking produces a measurable improvement in mood that can last for up to two hours. Not 10 minutes of running. Not an hour at the gym. Just a walk, at a pace that is slightly faster than your normal stroll, for the time it takes to boil chai."},{"id":"s2","title":"What happens in your brain when you walk","body":"The moment you start moving, your body begins releasing a cocktail of chemicals that directly combat the biology of depression. Endorphins, your body''s natural painkillers, reduce physical tension and create a mild sense of wellbeing. Serotonin production increases, which is the same chemical that antidepressant medications target. Your brain also releases something called Brain-Derived Neurotrophic Factor, or BDNF, which protects and repairs brain cells that are damaged by chronic stress and depression. These are not vague, feel-good claims. They are measurable chemical changes that scientists can observe in brain scans and blood tests.\\n\\nAdditionally, walking outside exposes you to natural light, which regulates your circadian rhythm, the internal clock that controls sleep and wakefulness. Many people with depression have disrupted circadian rhythms, and even brief exposure to daylight helps reset them. In Kenya, where we are blessed with abundant sunshine for most of the year, stepping outside for a walk gives you this benefit naturally and freely."},{"id":"s3","title":"The trick for the hardest days","body":"The biggest challenge is not the walk itself. It is getting started. Depression steals motivation. It makes everything feel impossible and pointless. The voice in your head says, \\"What is the point? A walk will not fix anything.\\" Here is a strategy that many people find helpful: do not commit to a walk. Commit to putting on your shoes. That is it. Just put on your shoes. Once your shoes are on, commit to stepping outside your door. Just the doorstep. Once you are at the doorstep, commit to walking to the end of your road. Just to the corner and back. Most people find that once they have started moving, they go further than they planned. But even if you only make it to the end of the road and back, you have still moved, and your brain still benefits.\\n\\nAnother approach is to combine walking with a small errand. Walk to the nearby duka to buy milk. Walk to the kiosk for airtime. Walk to the neighbour''s house to return something you borrowed. Giving the walk a purpose beyond exercise makes it feel less like a chore and more like a normal part of your day."},{"id":"s4","title":"What if 10 minutes feels impossible","body":"Then try five minutes. Or three. Or one. Seriously. Walk to your gate and back. Stand outside for 60 seconds and feel the sun on your face. Any movement is better than no movement. On the very worst days, just sitting outside on your doorstep for a few minutes, where there is natural light and fresh air, is a step in the right direction. Depression tells you that if you cannot do something perfectly or fully, there is no point doing it at all. That is the depression lying to you. Every small action counts."},{"id":"s5","title":"Building a walking routine","body":"If you can manage 10 minutes today, that is your starting point. Try to do it at the same time each day, because routine is powerful for mental health. Morning walks are ideal because they combine exercise with exposure to morning light, which is most effective at resetting your circadian rhythm. But any time of day works. Walk in the evening if that suits your schedule. Walk during your lunch break. Walk whenever you can.\\n\\nOver time, try to extend your walks gradually. From 10 minutes to 15, then to 20, then to 30. At 30 minutes of brisk walking per day, the antidepressant effect of exercise becomes comparable to medication for mild to moderate depression. This does not mean you should stop your medication without consulting your doctor. It means exercise and medication together can be more effective than either one alone."},{"id":"s6","title":"Make it social when you can","body":"Walking with another person adds a social dimension that amplifies the mental health benefits. If you have a friend, neighbour, or family member who is willing to walk with you, the combination of exercise and social connection is more powerful than either alone. You do not have to talk about your feelings. You do not have to explain what you are going through. Just walking together, maybe chatting about everyday things, is enough. In many Kenyan neighbourhoods, there are people walking in the early morning or evening. Joining that flow, even anonymously, can reduce the sense of isolation that depression creates."},{"id":"s7","title":"A prescription you write yourself","body":"Think of your daily walk as medication that you prescribe for yourself. It costs nothing. It has no negative side effects. It is available 365 days a year. And unlike a pill, it gets you out of your house, into the sunlight, and moving through the world. On the days when you manage it, acknowledge what you did. You fought against the weight of depression and you moved. That is not a small thing. That is courage."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'yoga-pose'),
  (E'myth-depression-is-just-laziness-or-a-lack-of-faith', E'Myth: Depression is just laziness or a lack of faith', E'MENTAL_HEALTH', '{"MENTAL_HEALTH"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'In many Kenyan communities, depression is not recognised as a medical condition. Instead, it is dismissed as laziness, weakness, or a spiritual problem.', E'In many Kenyan communities, depression is not recognised as a medical condition. Instead, it is dismissed as laziness, weakness, or a spiritual problem. You might have heard phrases like "That one is just lazy," "They need to pray harder," "People in the village did not have depression because they were busy working," or "Depression is a disease for wazungu." These statements are not only wrong, they are actively harmful. They prevent people who are suffering from seeking the treatment that could help them, and they add shame to an already painful experience.\n\nWhat depression actually is\n\nDepression is a medical condition that involves measurable changes in brain chemistry and function. Your brain communicates using chemical messengers called neurotransmitters, including serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine. In depression, the production, release, or reception of these chemicals is disrupted. This is not something a person can control through willpower any more than they can control their blood sugar through willpower. The comparison to malaria is useful: telling someone with depression to "snap out of it" is exactly like telling someone with malaria to just stop having a fever. The underlying biology must be addressed.\n\nBrain imaging studies show that people with depression have measurable differences in the activity of specific brain regions. The prefrontal cortex, which is involved in decision-making, motivation, and concentration, shows reduced activity. The amygdala, which processes emotions including fear, becomes overactive. These are not character traits. They are physiological states that respond to treatment.\n\nWhy the laziness myth is so damaging\n\nWhen depression is labelled as laziness, several harmful things happen. First, the person suffering feels ashamed. They already feel worthless and hopeless, and being told it is their own fault deepens the despair. Many people in Kenya have described hiding their depression for years because they feared being judged by their family and community. Second, they do not seek treatment. If you believe your problem is laziness or weak faith, you try to solve it with more effort or more prayer. When that does not work, because it cannot, you feel even more hopeless. The depression worsens. Third, well-meaning family members and friends provide the wrong kind of support. Instead of helping the person access medical care, they lecture them, push them to work harder, or take them for spiritual interventions that delay actual treatment.\n\nDepression does not discriminate\n\nDepression affects people across every category you can think of. Rich and poor. Religious and secular. Urban and rural. Young and old. Educated and uneducated. Hard-working and relaxed. Some of the most accomplished, busy, and faithful people suffer from depression. Pastors, teachers, doctors, business owners, athletes, and community leaders have all publicly shared their experiences with depression. It is not about how strong your character is or how deep your faith goes. It is about brain chemistry, genetics, life experiences, and circumstances.\n\nIn Kenya, the World Health Organization estimates that depression affects about 4.4 percent of the population, over two million people. Many more cases go undiagnosed because of stigma.\n\nFaith and treatment are not enemies\n\nFor many Kenyans, faith is a central part of life and plays a genuinely positive role in coping. No one is asking you to abandon your faith to treat depression. But faith and medical treatment work best together. If you had diabetes, you would take your insulin and pray. Depression deserves the same approach. Take the medication if prescribed. Attend the therapy sessions. And pray as well, if that is part of your life.\n\nTreatment works\n\nDepression is treatable. Therapy, particularly Cognitive Behavioural Therapy available at many hospitals in Kenya, helps people change the thought patterns that feed depression. Medication like fluoxetine and sertraline, available at Kenyan pharmacies and covered by many SHA plans, can restore normal brain chemistry. Most people who receive appropriate treatment show significant improvement.\n\nWhat you can do\n\nIf someone you know seems withdrawn, persistently sad, or hopeless, gently encourage them to talk to a doctor or counsellor. The Kenya Red Cross offers a free helpline at 1199. Befrienders Kenya can be reached at 0722 178 177. Your encouragement could help someone take the first step toward recovery.\n\nSeeking help is not weakness. It is wisdom.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"In many Kenyan communities, depression is not recognised as a medical condition. Instead, it is dismissed as laziness, weakness, or a spiritual problem. You might have heard phrases like \\"That one is just lazy,\\" \\"They need to pray harder,\\" \\"People in the village did not have depression because they were busy working,\\" or \\"Depression is a disease for wazungu.\\" These statements are not only wrong, they are actively harmful. They prevent people who are suffering from seeking the treatment that could help them, and they add shame to an already painful experience."},{"id":"s2","title":"What depression actually is","body":"Depression is a medical condition that involves measurable changes in brain chemistry and function. Your brain communicates using chemical messengers called neurotransmitters, including serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine. In depression, the production, release, or reception of these chemicals is disrupted. This is not something a person can control through willpower any more than they can control their blood sugar through willpower. The comparison to malaria is useful: telling someone with depression to \\"snap out of it\\" is exactly like telling someone with malaria to just stop having a fever. The underlying biology must be addressed.\\n\\nBrain imaging studies show that people with depression have measurable differences in the activity of specific brain regions. The prefrontal cortex, which is involved in decision-making, motivation, and concentration, shows reduced activity. The amygdala, which processes emotions including fear, becomes overactive. These are not character traits. They are physiological states that respond to treatment."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why the laziness myth is so damaging","body":"When depression is labelled as laziness, several harmful things happen. First, the person suffering feels ashamed. They already feel worthless and hopeless, and being told it is their own fault deepens the despair. Many people in Kenya have described hiding their depression for years because they feared being judged by their family and community. Second, they do not seek treatment. If you believe your problem is laziness or weak faith, you try to solve it with more effort or more prayer. When that does not work, because it cannot, you feel even more hopeless. The depression worsens. Third, well-meaning family members and friends provide the wrong kind of support. Instead of helping the person access medical care, they lecture them, push them to work harder, or take them for spiritual interventions that delay actual treatment."},{"id":"s4","title":"Depression does not discriminate","body":"Depression affects people across every category you can think of. Rich and poor. Religious and secular. Urban and rural. Young and old. Educated and uneducated. Hard-working and relaxed. Some of the most accomplished, busy, and faithful people suffer from depression. Pastors, teachers, doctors, business owners, athletes, and community leaders have all publicly shared their experiences with depression. It is not about how strong your character is or how deep your faith goes. It is about brain chemistry, genetics, life experiences, and circumstances.\\n\\nIn Kenya, the World Health Organization estimates that depression affects about 4.4 percent of the population, over two million people. Many more cases go undiagnosed because of stigma."},{"id":"s5","title":"Faith and treatment are not enemies","body":"For many Kenyans, faith is a central part of life and plays a genuinely positive role in coping. No one is asking you to abandon your faith to treat depression. But faith and medical treatment work best together. If you had diabetes, you would take your insulin and pray. Depression deserves the same approach. Take the medication if prescribed. Attend the therapy sessions. And pray as well, if that is part of your life."},{"id":"s6","title":"Treatment works","body":"Depression is treatable. Therapy, particularly Cognitive Behavioural Therapy available at many hospitals in Kenya, helps people change the thought patterns that feed depression. Medication like fluoxetine and sertraline, available at Kenyan pharmacies and covered by many SHA plans, can restore normal brain chemistry. Most people who receive appropriate treatment show significant improvement."},{"id":"s7","title":"What you can do","body":"If someone you know seems withdrawn, persistently sad, or hopeless, gently encourage them to talk to a doctor or counsellor. The Kenya Red Cross offers a free helpline at 1199. Befrienders Kenya can be reached at 0722 178 177. Your encouragement could help someone take the first step toward recovery.\\n\\nSeeking help is not weakness. It is wisdom."}]'::jsonb, 4, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'doctor-consultation'),
  (E'it-is-okay-to-not-be-okay-right-now', E'It is okay to not be okay right now', E'MENTAL_HEALTH', '{"MENTAL_HEALTH"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'There is enormous pressure in our culture to be positive, to be strong, to keep going no matter what. When someone asks "How are you?" the expected answer is "I am fine, thank God." But what happens when you are not fine?', E'There is enormous pressure in our culture to be positive, to be strong, to keep going no matter what. When someone asks "How are you?" the expected answer is "I am fine, thank God." But what happens when you are not fine? What happens when the weight of anxiety, depression, grief, or emotional exhaustion makes it hard to function? When you are struggling with your mental health, the demand to perform wellness can become one more burden you cannot carry.\n\nThe pressure to perform\n\nIn many Kenyan families and communities, admitting that you are struggling emotionally is seen as a sign of weakness or ingratitude. You might hear things like, "Other people have it worse," "You have a roof and food, what is there to be sad about?" or "Just focus on the positive." These statements usually come from people who care about you and are trying to help, but they can make you feel that your pain is not valid, that you are wrong for feeling what you feel. This adds a layer of shame on top of the original suffering, creating a situation where you are not only struggling but also hiding that struggle, which takes even more energy.\n\nYou do not owe anyone a performance of happiness. Your feelings are not a choice, and they are not a reflection of your character. You can be grateful for what you have and still be in pain. You can love your family and still feel overwhelmed. You can have faith and still feel lost. These things are not contradictions. They are the reality of being human.\n\nSome days are just hard\n\nThere will be days when you cannot find the motivation to get out of bed. Days when tears come without warning. Days when the thought of facing people, making decisions, or even eating feels impossible. These days are part of the landscape of mental health challenges, and they do not mean you are getting worse or that your treatment is failing. Recovery from mental health conditions is not a straight line going upward. It is a path with good days and bad days, progress and setbacks. What matters is not whether you have bad days, but what you do with them.\n\nOn the hardest days, lower your expectations to the bare minimum. Feed yourself something, even if it is just a cup of uji or a banana. Drink water. If you can, step outside for a few minutes. Do not judge yourself for what you cannot accomplish. The laundry, the errands, the phone calls — they can wait. Your only job today is to get through today.\n\nReaching out when you are not okay\n\nThe most important thing you can do on a difficult day is to reach out to one person. Not to perform wellness. Not to pretend you are fine. Just to say, "I am not doing well today." You might be surprised by how powerful that simple sentence is. It breaks the isolation that depression and anxiety feed on. It gives someone who cares about you the chance to help. And it reminds you that you are not alone, even when your brain is telling you that you are.\n\nYou do not have to explain everything. You do not have to justify your feelings or provide a reason. "I am struggling today" or "I need someone to talk to" is enough. Choose the person who is most likely to listen without judging. It might be a close friend. It might be a family member. It might be a colleague who has been open about their own struggles. It might be a professional.\n\nProfessional support in Kenya\n\nIf you are in crisis or need to talk to someone immediately, the Kenya Red Cross mental health hotline is available at 1199. Befrienders Kenya offers support at 0722 178 177. These services are free, confidential, and staffed by trained counsellors who understand what you are going through. You do not need to be suicidal to call. Feeling overwhelmed, hopeless, or unable to cope is reason enough.\n\nFor ongoing support, counselling services are available at county hospitals, private practices, and through organisations like MHFA Kenya and the Kenya Psychological Association. If cost is a concern, many county hospitals offer mental health services at subsidised rates through SHA. Some churches and community organisations also offer free or low-cost counselling. Ask at your local health facility about what is available in your area.\n\nWhat to say to yourself\n\nWhen the voice in your head tells you that you are weak, that you should be able to handle this, that you are a burden to others, recognise that voice for what it is: a symptom. Depression lies. Anxiety lies. They distort your thinking to make everything seem worse and more hopeless than it actually is. When those thoughts come, try this: imagine a friend came to you and said exactly what you are thinking about yourself. Would you agree with them? Would you tell your friend they are weak and a burden? Of course not. You would be compassionate. You would tell them it is okay, that they are going through a hard time, and that it will not always feel this way. Offer yourself the same compassion.\n\nIt will not always feel like this\n\nThis is perhaps the most important truth when you are in the middle of a bad day or a bad week: it will not always feel like this. Your brain is telling you that this is permanent, that this is who you are now. That is not true. Moods shift. Treatment takes effect. Bad days end. The fact that you are reading this, that you are still engaged with your health, that you are still here, means that some part of you is still fighting. Hold onto that. It is enough for now.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"There is enormous pressure in our culture to be positive, to be strong, to keep going no matter what. When someone asks \\"How are you?\\" the expected answer is \\"I am fine, thank God.\\" But what happens when you are not fine? What happens when the weight of anxiety, depression, grief, or emotional exhaustion makes it hard to function? When you are struggling with your mental health, the demand to perform wellness can become one more burden you cannot carry."},{"id":"s2","title":"The pressure to perform","body":"In many Kenyan families and communities, admitting that you are struggling emotionally is seen as a sign of weakness or ingratitude. You might hear things like, \\"Other people have it worse,\\" \\"You have a roof and food, what is there to be sad about?\\" or \\"Just focus on the positive.\\" These statements usually come from people who care about you and are trying to help, but they can make you feel that your pain is not valid, that you are wrong for feeling what you feel. This adds a layer of shame on top of the original suffering, creating a situation where you are not only struggling but also hiding that struggle, which takes even more energy.\\n\\nYou do not owe anyone a performance of happiness. Your feelings are not a choice, and they are not a reflection of your character. You can be grateful for what you have and still be in pain. You can love your family and still feel overwhelmed. You can have faith and still feel lost. These things are not contradictions. They are the reality of being human."},{"id":"s3","title":"Some days are just hard","body":"There will be days when you cannot find the motivation to get out of bed. Days when tears come without warning. Days when the thought of facing people, making decisions, or even eating feels impossible. These days are part of the landscape of mental health challenges, and they do not mean you are getting worse or that your treatment is failing. Recovery from mental health conditions is not a straight line going upward. It is a path with good days and bad days, progress and setbacks. What matters is not whether you have bad days, but what you do with them.\\n\\nOn the hardest days, lower your expectations to the bare minimum. Feed yourself something, even if it is just a cup of uji or a banana. Drink water. If you can, step outside for a few minutes. Do not judge yourself for what you cannot accomplish. The laundry, the errands, the phone calls — they can wait. Your only job today is to get through today."},{"id":"s4","title":"Reaching out when you are not okay","body":"The most important thing you can do on a difficult day is to reach out to one person. Not to perform wellness. Not to pretend you are fine. Just to say, \\"I am not doing well today.\\" You might be surprised by how powerful that simple sentence is. It breaks the isolation that depression and anxiety feed on. It gives someone who cares about you the chance to help. And it reminds you that you are not alone, even when your brain is telling you that you are.\\n\\nYou do not have to explain everything. You do not have to justify your feelings or provide a reason. \\"I am struggling today\\" or \\"I need someone to talk to\\" is enough. Choose the person who is most likely to listen without judging. It might be a close friend. It might be a family member. It might be a colleague who has been open about their own struggles. It might be a professional."},{"id":"s5","title":"Professional support in Kenya","body":"If you are in crisis or need to talk to someone immediately, the Kenya Red Cross mental health hotline is available at 1199. Befrienders Kenya offers support at 0722 178 177. These services are free, confidential, and staffed by trained counsellors who understand what you are going through. You do not need to be suicidal to call. Feeling overwhelmed, hopeless, or unable to cope is reason enough.\\n\\nFor ongoing support, counselling services are available at county hospitals, private practices, and through organisations like MHFA Kenya and the Kenya Psychological Association. If cost is a concern, many county hospitals offer mental health services at subsidised rates through SHA. Some churches and community organisations also offer free or low-cost counselling. Ask at your local health facility about what is available in your area."},{"id":"s6","title":"What to say to yourself","body":"When the voice in your head tells you that you are weak, that you should be able to handle this, that you are a burden to others, recognise that voice for what it is: a symptom. Depression lies. Anxiety lies. They distort your thinking to make everything seem worse and more hopeless than it actually is. When those thoughts come, try this: imagine a friend came to you and said exactly what you are thinking about yourself. Would you agree with them? Would you tell your friend they are weak and a burden? Of course not. You would be compassionate. You would tell them it is okay, that they are going through a hard time, and that it will not always feel this way. Offer yourself the same compassion."},{"id":"s7","title":"It will not always feel like this","body":"This is perhaps the most important truth when you are in the middle of a bad day or a bad week: it will not always feel like this. Your brain is telling you that this is permanent, that this is who you are now. That is not true. Moods shift. Treatment takes effect. Bad days end. The fact that you are reading this, that you are still engaged with your health, that you are still here, means that some part of you is still fighting. Hold onto that. It is enough for now."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'peaceful-moment'),
  (E'a-simple-daily-mood-check-that-reveals-patterns', E'A simple daily mood check that reveals patterns', E'MENTAL_HEALTH', '{"MENTAL_HEALTH"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'When you are living with a mental health condition, it can feel like your moods are random and unpredictable. One day you feel okay, the next you cannot get out of bed, and you have no idea why.', E'When you are living with a mental health condition, it can feel like your moods are random and unpredictable. One day you feel okay, the next you cannot get out of bed, and you have no idea why. This unpredictability is one of the most exhausting aspects of conditions like depression and anxiety. But here is something that many people discover when they start tracking: their moods are not as random as they seem. There are patterns, triggers, and connections that only become visible when you write them down consistently.\n\nHow to do a daily mood check\n\nThe method is simple and takes less than two minutes each evening. Before you go to sleep, rate your mood on a scale of 1 to 10. A score of 1 means you felt very low, hopeless, or unable to function. A score of 10 means you felt genuinely good, engaged with life, and emotionally stable. Most days will fall somewhere in between, and that is completely normal. Next to your score, jot down one or two things that you think affected your mood that day. These might include how well you slept the night before, whether you exercised, whether you took your medication, a specific event that happened, whether you spent time with other people or were alone all day, what you ate, or whether you consumed alcohol.\n\nKeep it brief. An entry might look like: "Mood: 4. Slept badly. Skipped walk. Stayed home alone." Or: "Mood: 7. Good sleep. Walked in the morning. Had lunch with Mary." You do not need to write paragraphs. Short, consistent entries are more useful than detailed journals that you abandon after a week.\n\nWhat tools to use\n\nA simple exercise book costing KES 30 to 50 from any bookshop or stationery kiosk is all you need. Keep it on your bedside table with a pen so it is right there when you are ready for sleep. If you prefer your phone, use the notes app or send yourself a WhatsApp message each evening. The WhatsApp approach has the advantage of being automatically timestamped and easy to scroll through later. There are also free mood tracking apps available for Android phones, such as Daylio or MoodFlow, which make it easy to log your mood with just a couple of taps.\n\nWhat patterns to look for\n\nAfter two weeks of consistent tracking, sit down and review your entries. Look for connections. Common patterns that people in Kenya discover include sleep being the strongest predictor of mood. If you consistently see that bad sleep is followed by a low mood score the next day, then improving your sleep becomes a top priority. Strategies might include reducing screen time before bed, avoiding caffeine after 2pm, or talking to your doctor about sleep medication if insomnia is severe.\n\nExercise is another common pattern. Many people find that days when they walk or do any physical activity consistently have higher mood scores than days when they stay sedentary. Social connection often appears as a factor too. Days spent entirely alone, especially for people who live alone, tend to have lower scores. Even brief social interactions, like chatting with a neighbour or calling a friend, can make a measurable difference.\n\nMedication adherence is critical to track. If you are taking antidepressants or anti-anxiety medication, skipping doses can cause mood dips that appear one to three days later. Your tracking diary can reveal this connection clearly. It also helps if you share this with your doctor, who may adjust your treatment based on the pattern.\n\nAlcohol often shows up as a mood disruptor. While a drink may feel relaxing in the evening, many people notice that their mood score drops the following day. This is not just a hangover effect. Alcohol disrupts the brain chemicals that regulate mood, and this disruption can last 48 hours or more.\n\nSharing your data with your doctor or counsellor\n\nWhen you visit your doctor or counsellor, bring your tracking records. Instead of saying "I have been feeling bad lately," you can say, "My average mood over the past two weeks was 4.5 out of 10. It drops to 3 or below when I sleep poorly and skip exercise. It is highest, around 6 or 7, on days when I walk in the morning and spend time with friends." This kind of specific information allows your healthcare provider to make targeted recommendations rather than generic advice. They might focus on sleep interventions, encourage you to build more social activities into your week, or adjust your medication timing.\n\nSeeing progress over time\n\nOne of the most motivating aspects of mood tracking is being able to see progress that you cannot feel in the moment. Depression has a way of making you forget that you have had good days. When everything feels dark, it is hard to believe it was ever different. But your tracking diary does not forget. When you look back at entries from a month ago and see that your average mood was 3, and now it is 5, that is concrete evidence that your treatment and self-care are working. On bad days, flip back through your diary and remind yourself: you have been this low before, and you came back up. The evidence is in your own handwriting.\n\nThis is not about being perfect. You will miss days. Your entries will sometimes be messy or incomplete. That is fine. Even tracking five days out of seven gives you useful data. The goal is not perfection. The goal is turning the vague fog of depression into something you can see, understand, and work with. And that is a powerful shift.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When you are living with a mental health condition, it can feel like your moods are random and unpredictable. One day you feel okay, the next you cannot get out of bed, and you have no idea why. This unpredictability is one of the most exhausting aspects of conditions like depression and anxiety. But here is something that many people discover when they start tracking: their moods are not as random as they seem. There are patterns, triggers, and connections that only become visible when you write them down consistently."},{"id":"s2","title":"How to do a daily mood check","body":"The method is simple and takes less than two minutes each evening. Before you go to sleep, rate your mood on a scale of 1 to 10. A score of 1 means you felt very low, hopeless, or unable to function. A score of 10 means you felt genuinely good, engaged with life, and emotionally stable. Most days will fall somewhere in between, and that is completely normal. Next to your score, jot down one or two things that you think affected your mood that day. These might include how well you slept the night before, whether you exercised, whether you took your medication, a specific event that happened, whether you spent time with other people or were alone all day, what you ate, or whether you consumed alcohol.\\n\\nKeep it brief. An entry might look like: \\"Mood: 4. Slept badly. Skipped walk. Stayed home alone.\\" Or: \\"Mood: 7. Good sleep. Walked in the morning. Had lunch with Mary.\\" You do not need to write paragraphs. Short, consistent entries are more useful than detailed journals that you abandon after a week."},{"id":"s3","title":"What tools to use","body":"A simple exercise book costing KES 30 to 50 from any bookshop or stationery kiosk is all you need. Keep it on your bedside table with a pen so it is right there when you are ready for sleep. If you prefer your phone, use the notes app or send yourself a WhatsApp message each evening. The WhatsApp approach has the advantage of being automatically timestamped and easy to scroll through later. There are also free mood tracking apps available for Android phones, such as Daylio or MoodFlow, which make it easy to log your mood with just a couple of taps."},{"id":"s4","title":"What patterns to look for","body":"After two weeks of consistent tracking, sit down and review your entries. Look for connections. Common patterns that people in Kenya discover include sleep being the strongest predictor of mood. If you consistently see that bad sleep is followed by a low mood score the next day, then improving your sleep becomes a top priority. Strategies might include reducing screen time before bed, avoiding caffeine after 2pm, or talking to your doctor about sleep medication if insomnia is severe.\\n\\nExercise is another common pattern. Many people find that days when they walk or do any physical activity consistently have higher mood scores than days when they stay sedentary. Social connection often appears as a factor too. Days spent entirely alone, especially for people who live alone, tend to have lower scores. Even brief social interactions, like chatting with a neighbour or calling a friend, can make a measurable difference.\\n\\nMedication adherence is critical to track. If you are taking antidepressants or anti-anxiety medication, skipping doses can cause mood dips that appear one to three days later. Your tracking diary can reveal this connection clearly. It also helps if you share this with your doctor, who may adjust your treatment based on the pattern.\\n\\nAlcohol often shows up as a mood disruptor. While a drink may feel relaxing in the evening, many people notice that their mood score drops the following day. This is not just a hangover effect. Alcohol disrupts the brain chemicals that regulate mood, and this disruption can last 48 hours or more."},{"id":"s5","title":"Sharing your data with your doctor or counsellor","body":"When you visit your doctor or counsellor, bring your tracking records. Instead of saying \\"I have been feeling bad lately,\\" you can say, \\"My average mood over the past two weeks was 4.5 out of 10. It drops to 3 or below when I sleep poorly and skip exercise. It is highest, around 6 or 7, on days when I walk in the morning and spend time with friends.\\" This kind of specific information allows your healthcare provider to make targeted recommendations rather than generic advice. They might focus on sleep interventions, encourage you to build more social activities into your week, or adjust your medication timing."},{"id":"s6","title":"Seeing progress over time","body":"One of the most motivating aspects of mood tracking is being able to see progress that you cannot feel in the moment. Depression has a way of making you forget that you have had good days. When everything feels dark, it is hard to believe it was ever different. But your tracking diary does not forget. When you look back at entries from a month ago and see that your average mood was 3, and now it is 5, that is concrete evidence that your treatment and self-care are working. On bad days, flip back through your diary and remind yourself: you have been this low before, and you came back up. The evidence is in your own handwriting.\\n\\nThis is not about being perfect. You will miss days. Your entries will sometimes be messy or incomplete. That is fine. Even tracking five days out of seven gives you useful data. The goal is not perfection. The goal is turning the vague fog of depression into something you can see, understand, and work with. And that is a powerful shift."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'health-diary'),
  (E'accepting-that-mental-health-is-health', E'Accepting that mental health is health', E'MENTAL_HEALTH', '{"MENTAL_HEALTH"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'If you had malaria, you would go to the hospital, get tested, take your medication, and rest until you recovered. Nobody would tell you to just pray harder or stop being lazy.', E'If you had malaria, you would go to the hospital, get tested, take your medication, and rest until you recovered. Nobody would tell you to just pray harder or stop being lazy. Nobody would suggest that malaria is a character flaw or that you brought it on yourself. Yet when it comes to depression, anxiety, or other mental health conditions, many people in Kenya are met with exactly this kind of response. The first and perhaps most important step in managing your mental health is accepting a simple truth: mental health is health. It is not a separate category. It is not less real. It is not less deserving of proper medical attention.\n\nWhy mental health conditions are medical conditions\n\nYour brain is an organ, just like your heart, your lungs, or your kidneys. It can get sick, just like any other organ. Depression involves measurable changes in the levels of neurotransmitters, the chemicals your brain cells use to communicate with each other. Anxiety involves an overactive amygdala, the part of your brain that processes fear and threat. Post-traumatic stress disorder involves structural changes in brain areas responsible for memory and emotional regulation. These are not abstract concepts. They are physical realities that can be observed on brain scans and measured in laboratory tests.\n\nThe stigma around mental health often comes from the mistaken belief that these conditions are about willpower or character. They are not. You did not choose to have depression any more than you chose to have diabetes. Accepting this is not about making excuses. It is about being honest, and honesty is the foundation of effective treatment.\n\nThe cost of denial\n\nMany people in Kenya spend months or years trying to manage their mental health through sheer willpower or denial alone. When these approaches do not work, because they cannot fully address the underlying biology, the person feels even more like a failure. The condition worsens. Relationships suffer. Physical health deteriorates, because untreated mental health conditions increase the risk of heart disease and other chronic illnesses.\n\nThe tragic irony is that treatment is readily available and effective. In Kenya, counselling services are offered at county hospitals, many covered by SHA. Antidepressant medications like fluoxetine are available at Kenyan pharmacies for as little as KES 200 to 500 per month. The barrier is not usually access or cost. It is stigma.\n\nAcceptance is not defeat\n\nSaying "I have depression" or "I have anxiety" is not an admission of weakness. It is an act of honesty and courage. It means you are seeing your situation clearly and opening the door to getting help. Compare it to other health conditions that require ongoing management. People with high blood pressure take their medication every day and monitor their numbers. People with diabetes check their blood sugar and adjust their diet. People with asthma carry an inhaler. None of these people are considered weak or broken. They are managing a medical condition responsibly. That is exactly what you are doing when you accept and treat a mental health condition.\n\nAcceptance also means letting go of the timeline you had in mind. You might have expected to feel better by now, or wished that a few weeks of medication would fix everything. Mental health recovery takes time, and it is not linear. There will be good weeks and bad weeks. Accepting this as normal, rather than as evidence of failure, reduces the frustration and self-blame that can slow recovery.\n\nWhat acceptance looks like in daily life\n\nAcceptance is practical, not just philosophical. It means taking your medication as prescribed, even on days when you feel fine, because mental health medication works best when taken consistently. It means keeping your counselling appointments, even when you do not feel like talking. It means telling at least one trusted person in your life what you are going through, because isolation feeds mental illness while connection fights it. It means adjusting your expectations on bad days, giving yourself permission to do less without guilt. It means tracking your mood and identifying your triggers, as discussed in other articles, so that you can manage your condition actively rather than passively.\n\nIt also means being willing to educate the people around you. When a family member suggests that you just need to pray more, you can gently say, "Prayer helps me, and so does my medication and therapy. I need all of it." When a colleague makes an insensitive comment about mental health, you can choose, if you feel safe, to share what you know about the medical reality of these conditions. Every conversation like this chips away at the stigma, making it a little easier for the next person to seek help.\n\nYou deserve proper care\n\nYou deserve the same quality of care for your mental health that you would expect for any physical illness. You deserve a doctor who listens, medication that is affordable and accessible, therapy that helps you understand your patterns, and a support network that does not judge you. You deserve compassion from others, and you deserve compassion from yourself. Mental health is health. Your brain is worth caring for. And accepting this truth is the first step toward a life where your condition is managed, not hidden.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you had malaria, you would go to the hospital, get tested, take your medication, and rest until you recovered. Nobody would tell you to just pray harder or stop being lazy. Nobody would suggest that malaria is a character flaw or that you brought it on yourself. Yet when it comes to depression, anxiety, or other mental health conditions, many people in Kenya are met with exactly this kind of response. The first and perhaps most important step in managing your mental health is accepting a simple truth: mental health is health. It is not a separate category. It is not less real. It is not less deserving of proper medical attention."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why mental health conditions are medical conditions","body":"Your brain is an organ, just like your heart, your lungs, or your kidneys. It can get sick, just like any other organ. Depression involves measurable changes in the levels of neurotransmitters, the chemicals your brain cells use to communicate with each other. Anxiety involves an overactive amygdala, the part of your brain that processes fear and threat. Post-traumatic stress disorder involves structural changes in brain areas responsible for memory and emotional regulation. These are not abstract concepts. They are physical realities that can be observed on brain scans and measured in laboratory tests.\\n\\nThe stigma around mental health often comes from the mistaken belief that these conditions are about willpower or character. They are not. You did not choose to have depression any more than you chose to have diabetes. Accepting this is not about making excuses. It is about being honest, and honesty is the foundation of effective treatment."},{"id":"s3","title":"The cost of denial","body":"Many people in Kenya spend months or years trying to manage their mental health through sheer willpower or denial alone. When these approaches do not work, because they cannot fully address the underlying biology, the person feels even more like a failure. The condition worsens. Relationships suffer. Physical health deteriorates, because untreated mental health conditions increase the risk of heart disease and other chronic illnesses.\\n\\nThe tragic irony is that treatment is readily available and effective. In Kenya, counselling services are offered at county hospitals, many covered by SHA. Antidepressant medications like fluoxetine are available at Kenyan pharmacies for as little as KES 200 to 500 per month. The barrier is not usually access or cost. It is stigma."},{"id":"s4","title":"Acceptance is not defeat","body":"Saying \\"I have depression\\" or \\"I have anxiety\\" is not an admission of weakness. It is an act of honesty and courage. It means you are seeing your situation clearly and opening the door to getting help. Compare it to other health conditions that require ongoing management. People with high blood pressure take their medication every day and monitor their numbers. People with diabetes check their blood sugar and adjust their diet. People with asthma carry an inhaler. None of these people are considered weak or broken. They are managing a medical condition responsibly. That is exactly what you are doing when you accept and treat a mental health condition.\\n\\nAcceptance also means letting go of the timeline you had in mind. You might have expected to feel better by now, or wished that a few weeks of medication would fix everything. Mental health recovery takes time, and it is not linear. There will be good weeks and bad weeks. Accepting this as normal, rather than as evidence of failure, reduces the frustration and self-blame that can slow recovery."},{"id":"s5","title":"What acceptance looks like in daily life","body":"Acceptance is practical, not just philosophical. It means taking your medication as prescribed, even on days when you feel fine, because mental health medication works best when taken consistently. It means keeping your counselling appointments, even when you do not feel like talking. It means telling at least one trusted person in your life what you are going through, because isolation feeds mental illness while connection fights it. It means adjusting your expectations on bad days, giving yourself permission to do less without guilt. It means tracking your mood and identifying your triggers, as discussed in other articles, so that you can manage your condition actively rather than passively.\\n\\nIt also means being willing to educate the people around you. When a family member suggests that you just need to pray more, you can gently say, \\"Prayer helps me, and so does my medication and therapy. I need all of it.\\" When a colleague makes an insensitive comment about mental health, you can choose, if you feel safe, to share what you know about the medical reality of these conditions. Every conversation like this chips away at the stigma, making it a little easier for the next person to seek help."},{"id":"s6","title":"You deserve proper care","body":"You deserve the same quality of care for your mental health that you would expect for any physical illness. You deserve a doctor who listens, medication that is affordable and accessible, therapy that helps you understand your patterns, and a support network that does not judge you. You deserve compassion from others, and you deserve compassion from yourself. Mental health is health. Your brain is worth caring for. And accepting this truth is the first step toward a life where your condition is managed, not hidden."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'sunrise'),
  (E'you-asked-for-help-that-was-the-hardest-step', E'You asked for help — that was the hardest step', E'MENTAL_HEALTH', '{"MENTAL_HEALTH"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Whether you called a helpline, booked a counselling session, spoke honestly to a friend about how you have been feeling, or told your doctor that you are struggling, you did something that millions of people put off for months, years, or even decades.', E'Whether you called a helpline, booked a counselling session, spoke honestly to a friend about how you have been feeling, or told your doctor that you are struggling, you did something that millions of people put off for months, years, or even decades. You asked for help. And that single act may be the most important thing you have done for your mental health. Everything that follows, every therapy session, every medication adjustment, every coping strategy you learn, builds on the foundation of this one brave decision.\n\nWhy asking for help is the hardest step\n\nIn Kenya, powerful cultural forces work against this moment. The belief that mental health is a private matter. The fear of being labelled "mad" or "weak." The expectation, especially for men, to handle everything stoically. The misconception that willpower or faith alone should be enough. Every time you considered reaching out and held back, these forces were at work. The fact that you pushed through them is a testament to your courage.\n\nAsking for help requires overcoming three things that depression actively fights against: acknowledging something is wrong, believing things could be better, and trusting that another person will not judge you. That you managed all three is remarkable.\n\nWhat this step means for your recovery\n\nResearch consistently shows that the single biggest factor in mental health recovery is engagement with treatment. People who seek help, whether through professional counselling, medication, support groups, or even confiding in a trusted friend, have significantly better outcomes than those who try to manage alone. By asking for help, you have moved yourself from the category of people who suffer in silence to the category of people who are actively working toward recovery. That shift changes everything.\n\nYour first conversation, whether it was with a counsellor, a doctor, a friend, or a helpline operator, opened a channel of communication that can now be maintained and expanded. You have broken the seal. The second conversation will be easier. The third easier still. Over time, talking about your mental health becomes a normal part of managing your condition, not a crisis event.\n\nWhat happens next\n\nThe road ahead is not a straight line. Mental health recovery involves good days and difficult days, breakthroughs and setbacks. There will be therapy sessions where you feel like you made real progress and others where you feel stuck. If medication is part of your treatment, it may take a few weeks to reach full effectiveness, and the first medication tried is not always the right fit. Your doctor may need to adjust your dose or switch to a different medication. This is normal medical practice, not a sign that treatment is failing.\n\nIf you have started counselling, your therapist will help you understand your thinking patterns and develop practical coping strategies. In Kenya, Cognitive Behavioural Therapy is widely available at county hospitals, private practices, and through the Kenya Psychological Association.\n\nIf you have been prescribed medication, take it as directed, even on days when you feel fine. Do not stop or adjust without consulting your doctor. Side effects are common in the first week or two but are usually temporary. Talk to your doctor about them rather than stopping on your own.\n\nBuilding your support network\n\nNow that you have opened the door, consider who else might be part of your support system. You do not need to tell everyone, but having two or three people who know what you are going through can make a significant difference. These might be a close friend, a family member, a fellow church member, or a colleague. Support groups, whether in person or online, connect you with people who understand your experience firsthand. Ask your counsellor or doctor about groups available in your area.\n\nThe Kenya Red Cross mental health hotline at 1199 and Befrienders Kenya at 0722 178 177 remain available whenever you need them. These are not just for crisis moments. You can call on a bad day, when you need to talk, or when you need reassurance that what you are feeling is normal.\n\nRecognising your courage\n\nIn Kenyan culture, we celebrate courage in many forms. The student who perseveres through school despite hardship. The entrepreneur who starts a business against the odds. The parent who sacrifices for their children. Asking for help with your mental health belongs in this same category of courageous acts. It required you to be vulnerable, honest, and brave at a time when your condition was telling you to hide, deny, and give up.\n\nSo pause for a moment and acknowledge what you did. You are no longer walking this road alone. You have a team forming around you: professionals, loved ones, and your own growing understanding of your condition. The road may not be straight, but the direction is forward. And you took the step that made that possible.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Whether you called a helpline, booked a counselling session, spoke honestly to a friend about how you have been feeling, or told your doctor that you are struggling, you did something that millions of people put off for months, years, or even decades. You asked for help. And that single act may be the most important thing you have done for your mental health. Everything that follows, every therapy session, every medication adjustment, every coping strategy you learn, builds on the foundation of this one brave decision."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why asking for help is the hardest step","body":"In Kenya, powerful cultural forces work against this moment. The belief that mental health is a private matter. The fear of being labelled \\"mad\\" or \\"weak.\\" The expectation, especially for men, to handle everything stoically. The misconception that willpower or faith alone should be enough. Every time you considered reaching out and held back, these forces were at work. The fact that you pushed through them is a testament to your courage.\\n\\nAsking for help requires overcoming three things that depression actively fights against: acknowledging something is wrong, believing things could be better, and trusting that another person will not judge you. That you managed all three is remarkable."},{"id":"s3","title":"What this step means for your recovery","body":"Research consistently shows that the single biggest factor in mental health recovery is engagement with treatment. People who seek help, whether through professional counselling, medication, support groups, or even confiding in a trusted friend, have significantly better outcomes than those who try to manage alone. By asking for help, you have moved yourself from the category of people who suffer in silence to the category of people who are actively working toward recovery. That shift changes everything.\\n\\nYour first conversation, whether it was with a counsellor, a doctor, a friend, or a helpline operator, opened a channel of communication that can now be maintained and expanded. You have broken the seal. The second conversation will be easier. The third easier still. Over time, talking about your mental health becomes a normal part of managing your condition, not a crisis event."},{"id":"s4","title":"What happens next","body":"The road ahead is not a straight line. Mental health recovery involves good days and difficult days, breakthroughs and setbacks. There will be therapy sessions where you feel like you made real progress and others where you feel stuck. If medication is part of your treatment, it may take a few weeks to reach full effectiveness, and the first medication tried is not always the right fit. Your doctor may need to adjust your dose or switch to a different medication. This is normal medical practice, not a sign that treatment is failing.\\n\\nIf you have started counselling, your therapist will help you understand your thinking patterns and develop practical coping strategies. In Kenya, Cognitive Behavioural Therapy is widely available at county hospitals, private practices, and through the Kenya Psychological Association.\\n\\nIf you have been prescribed medication, take it as directed, even on days when you feel fine. Do not stop or adjust without consulting your doctor. Side effects are common in the first week or two but are usually temporary. Talk to your doctor about them rather than stopping on your own."},{"id":"s5","title":"Building your support network","body":"Now that you have opened the door, consider who else might be part of your support system. You do not need to tell everyone, but having two or three people who know what you are going through can make a significant difference. These might be a close friend, a family member, a fellow church member, or a colleague. Support groups, whether in person or online, connect you with people who understand your experience firsthand. Ask your counsellor or doctor about groups available in your area.\\n\\nThe Kenya Red Cross mental health hotline at 1199 and Befrienders Kenya at 0722 178 177 remain available whenever you need them. These are not just for crisis moments. You can call on a bad day, when you need to talk, or when you need reassurance that what you are feeling is normal."},{"id":"s6","title":"Recognising your courage","body":"In Kenyan culture, we celebrate courage in many forms. The student who perseveres through school despite hardship. The entrepreneur who starts a business against the odds. The parent who sacrifices for their children. Asking for help with your mental health belongs in this same category of courageous acts. It required you to be vulnerable, honest, and brave at a time when your condition was telling you to hide, deny, and give up.\\n\\nSo pause for a moment and acknowledge what you did. You are no longer walking this road alone. You have a team forming around you: professionals, loved ones, and your own growing understanding of your condition. The road may not be straight, but the direction is forward. And you took the step that made that possible."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'celebration'),
  (E'foods-that-interfere-with-your-thyroid-medication', E'Foods that interfere with your thyroid medication', E'THYROID', '{"THYROID"}', E'DIETARY', E'If you have been diagnosed with hypothyroidism and prescribed levothyroxine, also known by brand names like Euthyrox or Eltroxin, this medication replaces a hormone your thyroid gland no longer produces enough of.', E'If you have been diagnosed with hypothyroidism and prescribed levothyroxine, also known by brand names like Euthyrox or Eltroxin, this medication replaces a hormone your thyroid gland no longer produces enough of. It affects virtually every system in your body, from metabolism and energy to heart rate and mental clarity. But here is something many patients in Kenya are not told clearly enough: how and when you take levothyroxine matters just as much as whether you take it.\n\nThe golden rule: empty stomach, first thing in the morning\n\nTake your levothyroxine the moment you wake up, on a completely empty stomach, with a full glass of plain water. Not chai. Not juice. Not milk. Then wait at least 30 minutes, ideally 60 minutes, before eating or drinking anything else. Levothyroxine is absorbed in your small intestine, and food changes the pH and slows absorption. Studies show that taking it with food can reduce absorption by up to 40 percent.\n\nFor many Kenyans, the morning routine involves chai almost immediately after waking. You will need to adjust this habit. Set your alarm 30 to 60 minutes earlier, take your medication, then go back to sleep or start your routine. Some patients keep their medication and water on the bedside table so they can take it the moment they open their eyes.\n\nFoods and drinks that interfere with absorption\n\nCoffee, including instant coffee common in Kenyan households, significantly reduces levothyroxine absorption if consumed within an hour of taking the medication. Wait at least 60 minutes after your medication before having your first cup.\n\nSoy products, including soy milk and soy protein foods, bind to levothyroxine and reduce absorption. While soy is not common in traditional Kenyan diets, it is increasingly present in processed foods and supplements. Time soy products away from your medication.\n\nCalcium-rich foods and supplements are a major concern. Calcium binds directly to levothyroxine, forming a compound your body cannot absorb. This includes calcium tablets, milk, yoghurt, and mursik. Take calcium supplements at least four hours apart from your levothyroxine. The same applies to iron supplements, which many Kenyan women take during pregnancy. Space them at least four hours from your thyroid medication.\n\nAntacids and acid reflux medications like omeprazole, available at Kenyan pharmacies, also interfere with absorption. Discuss timing with your pharmacist.\n\nThe cabbage and kale question\n\nYou may have heard that cabbage, kale, and sukuma wiki are bad for your thyroid. These vegetables contain compounds called goitrogens that can theoretically interfere with thyroid function. However, this only occurs when eaten raw in very large quantities. In normal Kenyan cooking, where cabbage and sukuma wiki are sauteed or boiled, the cooking breaks down most goitrogenic compounds. Continue eating your sukuma wiki and cabbage stew. Just avoid juicing large quantities of raw cabbage or kale daily.\n\nConsistency is everything\n\nThe single most important principle for thyroid medication is consistency. Take it at the same time every day. Take it the same way every day, on an empty stomach with water. Eat breakfast at roughly the same interval after taking it. This consistency allows your doctor to accurately assess whether your dose is correct based on your blood test results. If some days you take it with food and other days on an empty stomach, your absorption varies wildly and your blood levels become unpredictable. This makes it impossible for your doctor to find the right dose.\n\nIf you miss a dose, take it as soon as you remember, as long as it is still morning and you have not eaten. If you have already eaten, skip that dose and take your normal dose the next morning. Do not double up. Missing an occasional dose is not dangerous, but missing them regularly will cause your symptoms to return.\n\nWorking with your doctor\n\nYour doctor monitors your thyroid levels through a TSH blood test, widely available at Kenyan hospitals and laboratories for KES 500 to 1,500. When starting treatment, your doctor may check TSH every six to eight weeks. Once stable, testing every six to twelve months is sufficient. If your levels are not normalising despite consistent medication, discuss your diet and timing with your doctor. It may be a food interaction rather than a dose problem.\n\nIn Kenya, a month''s supply of levothyroxine costs between KES 200 and 800, making it one of the more affordable chronic medications. The key to getting the most from every tablet is correct timing, an empty stomach, and consistent daily use. Your thyroid may not be working properly, but with the right medication habits, your body can function as if it were.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you have been diagnosed with hypothyroidism and prescribed levothyroxine, also known by brand names like Euthyrox or Eltroxin, this medication replaces a hormone your thyroid gland no longer produces enough of. It affects virtually every system in your body, from metabolism and energy to heart rate and mental clarity. But here is something many patients in Kenya are not told clearly enough: how and when you take levothyroxine matters just as much as whether you take it."},{"id":"s2","title":"The golden rule: empty stomach, first thing in the morning","body":"Take your levothyroxine the moment you wake up, on a completely empty stomach, with a full glass of plain water. Not chai. Not juice. Not milk. Then wait at least 30 minutes, ideally 60 minutes, before eating or drinking anything else. Levothyroxine is absorbed in your small intestine, and food changes the pH and slows absorption. Studies show that taking it with food can reduce absorption by up to 40 percent.\\n\\nFor many Kenyans, the morning routine involves chai almost immediately after waking. You will need to adjust this habit. Set your alarm 30 to 60 minutes earlier, take your medication, then go back to sleep or start your routine. Some patients keep their medication and water on the bedside table so they can take it the moment they open their eyes."},{"id":"s3","title":"Foods and drinks that interfere with absorption","body":"Coffee, including instant coffee common in Kenyan households, significantly reduces levothyroxine absorption if consumed within an hour of taking the medication. Wait at least 60 minutes after your medication before having your first cup.\\n\\nSoy products, including soy milk and soy protein foods, bind to levothyroxine and reduce absorption. While soy is not common in traditional Kenyan diets, it is increasingly present in processed foods and supplements. Time soy products away from your medication.\\n\\nCalcium-rich foods and supplements are a major concern. Calcium binds directly to levothyroxine, forming a compound your body cannot absorb. This includes calcium tablets, milk, yoghurt, and mursik. Take calcium supplements at least four hours apart from your levothyroxine. The same applies to iron supplements, which many Kenyan women take during pregnancy. Space them at least four hours from your thyroid medication.\\n\\nAntacids and acid reflux medications like omeprazole, available at Kenyan pharmacies, also interfere with absorption. Discuss timing with your pharmacist."},{"id":"s4","title":"The cabbage and kale question","body":"You may have heard that cabbage, kale, and sukuma wiki are bad for your thyroid. These vegetables contain compounds called goitrogens that can theoretically interfere with thyroid function. However, this only occurs when eaten raw in very large quantities. In normal Kenyan cooking, where cabbage and sukuma wiki are sauteed or boiled, the cooking breaks down most goitrogenic compounds. Continue eating your sukuma wiki and cabbage stew. Just avoid juicing large quantities of raw cabbage or kale daily."},{"id":"s5","title":"Consistency is everything","body":"The single most important principle for thyroid medication is consistency. Take it at the same time every day. Take it the same way every day, on an empty stomach with water. Eat breakfast at roughly the same interval after taking it. This consistency allows your doctor to accurately assess whether your dose is correct based on your blood test results. If some days you take it with food and other days on an empty stomach, your absorption varies wildly and your blood levels become unpredictable. This makes it impossible for your doctor to find the right dose.\\n\\nIf you miss a dose, take it as soon as you remember, as long as it is still morning and you have not eaten. If you have already eaten, skip that dose and take your normal dose the next morning. Do not double up. Missing an occasional dose is not dangerous, but missing them regularly will cause your symptoms to return."},{"id":"s6","title":"Working with your doctor","body":"Your doctor monitors your thyroid levels through a TSH blood test, widely available at Kenyan hospitals and laboratories for KES 500 to 1,500. When starting treatment, your doctor may check TSH every six to eight weeks. Once stable, testing every six to twelve months is sufficient. If your levels are not normalising despite consistent medication, discuss your diet and timing with your doctor. It may be a food interaction rather than a dose problem.\\n\\nIn Kenya, a month''s supply of levothyroxine costs between KES 200 and 800, making it one of the more affordable chronic medications. The key to getting the most from every tablet is correct timing, an empty stomach, and consistent daily use. Your thyroid may not be working properly, but with the right medication habits, your body can function as if it were."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'market-shopping'),
  (E'exercise-when-your-thyroid-makes-you-feel-exhausted', E'Exercise when your thyroid makes you feel exhausted', E'THYROID', '{"THYROID"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Living with a thyroid condition can make you feel like your body has been swapped for someone else''s. The fatigue that comes with hypothyroidism is not ordinary tiredness — it is a bone-deep exhaustion that makes even small tasks feel like climbing Mount Kenya.', E'Living with a thyroid condition can make you feel like your body has been swapped for someone else''s. The fatigue that comes with hypothyroidism is not ordinary tiredness — it is a bone-deep exhaustion that makes even small tasks feel like climbing Mount Kenya. If you have hyperthyroidism, the opposite problem hits: your heart races, your hands tremble, and you feel wired but weak at the same time. Either way, exercise might be the last thing on your mind. But gentle, consistent movement is one of the most powerful tools you have for feeling better.\n\nWhy exercise matters for thyroid patients\n\nYour thyroid hormone controls your metabolism — the engine that powers every cell in your body. When that engine is running too slow (hypothyroidism) or too fast (hyperthyroidism), exercise helps nudge things back toward balance. For hypothyroidism, movement boosts your metabolic rate, improves mood by releasing endorphins, and fights the weight gain that often accompanies an underactive thyroid. For hyperthyroidism, gentle exercise helps burn off excess nervous energy and anxiety without pushing your already-elevated heart rate into dangerous territory.\n\nStarting when you feel exhausted\n\nIf you have hypothyroidism, your medication — usually levothyroxine, which costs between KES 200 and KES 500 per month at most Kenyan pharmacies — takes four to six weeks to reach full effect. During that adjustment period, be very gentle with yourself. Start with a 10-minute walk around your estate or neighbourhood. Walk at a pace where you can still hold a conversation comfortably. If 10 minutes feels like too much, start with five. There is no shame in starting small. The goal is simply to move your body every day.\n\nWeek one and two, aim for 10-minute walks, five days a week. Choose a flat route — perhaps along the road near your home or around a nearby school field. Walk in the morning or late afternoon when the Nairobi sun is gentler, and carry a bottle of water. Week three and four, increase to 15 minutes. You should start feeling your medication taking effect around this time. Your energy may lift slightly, and the walks may feel less like a chore. By week five and six, try 20-minute walks and add some gentle arm swings as you go.\n\nA simple home routine for hypothyroidism\n\nOnce you have built a walking habit, add this gentle home routine two or three times per week. You need no equipment — just a chair and a mat or folded blanket on the floor.\n\nStart with seated marching. Sit upright in a sturdy chair and lift your knees one at a time, as if marching in place, for two minutes. This warms up your legs and gets blood flowing. Next, do wall push-ups. Stand an arm''s length from a wall, place your palms flat against it at shoulder height, and slowly bend your elbows to bring your chest toward the wall, then push back. Do 10 repetitions, rest for 30 seconds, and repeat twice. Move to standing calf raises — hold the back of your chair for balance and rise up on your toes, hold for two seconds, then lower. Do 15 repetitions twice. Finish with gentle stretching: reach your arms overhead and hold for 15 seconds, then touch your toes (or reach as far as you can) and hold for 15 seconds. The whole routine takes about 15 minutes.\n\nSpecial precautions for hyperthyroidism\n\nIf you have an overactive thyroid, your resting heart rate may already be elevated — sometimes above 100 beats per minute. Adding intense exercise on top of this puts unnecessary strain on your heart. Until your doctor confirms that your thyroid levels are controlled (usually through blood tests showing TSH, T3, and T4 in normal ranges), stick to very low-intensity activities. Walking, gentle yoga, and light stretching are your friends. Avoid running, intense aerobics, competitive sports, or heavy lifting.\n\nCheck your pulse before exercising. Place two fingers on the inside of your wrist and count beats for 15 seconds, then multiply by four. If your resting heart rate is above 100, skip the workout and rest instead. During exercise, if you feel your heart pounding, experience chest tightness, feel dizzy, or notice excessive sweating beyond what is normal for you, stop immediately and sit down.\n\nExercise and thyroid medication timing\n\nTake your levothyroxine on an empty stomach first thing in the morning with plain water — not with tea, coffee, or uji. Wait at least 30 to 60 minutes before eating breakfast. If you prefer to exercise in the morning, take your medication when you wake up, do your workout, and then have breakfast after. Some people find that exercising before their morning meal feels fine on thyroid medication, while others prefer a light breakfast first and exercise later. Find what works for you, but keep your medication timing consistent every day.\n\nWhen to see your doctor about exercise\n\nTell your doctor at Kenyatta National Hospital, your county referral hospital, or your local clinic about your exercise plans at your next appointment. They can check whether your thyroid levels are stable enough for increased activity. Seek medical attention urgently if exercise causes chest pain, irregular heartbeat, fainting, or extreme breathlessness. These symptoms deserve same-day attention.\n\nPatience is everything\n\nThyroid conditions respond well to proper medication and lifestyle changes, but improvement takes time. You may not feel the benefits of exercise for several weeks. Some days will be harder than others — the fatigue may return unexpectedly, or your energy may dip before your next dose adjustment. On those days, a five-minute walk still counts. On good days, push a little further. Over weeks and months, you will notice your stamina building, your mood lifting, and your body feeling more like your own again. Every step forward is a step toward feeling like yourself.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Living with a thyroid condition can make you feel like your body has been swapped for someone else''s. The fatigue that comes with hypothyroidism is not ordinary tiredness — it is a bone-deep exhaustion that makes even small tasks feel like climbing Mount Kenya. If you have hyperthyroidism, the opposite problem hits: your heart races, your hands tremble, and you feel wired but weak at the same time. Either way, exercise might be the last thing on your mind. But gentle, consistent movement is one of the most powerful tools you have for feeling better."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why exercise matters for thyroid patients","body":"Your thyroid hormone controls your metabolism — the engine that powers every cell in your body. When that engine is running too slow (hypothyroidism) or too fast (hyperthyroidism), exercise helps nudge things back toward balance. For hypothyroidism, movement boosts your metabolic rate, improves mood by releasing endorphins, and fights the weight gain that often accompanies an underactive thyroid. For hyperthyroidism, gentle exercise helps burn off excess nervous energy and anxiety without pushing your already-elevated heart rate into dangerous territory."},{"id":"s3","title":"Starting when you feel exhausted","body":"If you have hypothyroidism, your medication — usually levothyroxine, which costs between KES 200 and KES 500 per month at most Kenyan pharmacies — takes four to six weeks to reach full effect. During that adjustment period, be very gentle with yourself. Start with a 10-minute walk around your estate or neighbourhood. Walk at a pace where you can still hold a conversation comfortably. If 10 minutes feels like too much, start with five. There is no shame in starting small. The goal is simply to move your body every day.\\n\\nWeek one and two, aim for 10-minute walks, five days a week. Choose a flat route — perhaps along the road near your home or around a nearby school field. Walk in the morning or late afternoon when the Nairobi sun is gentler, and carry a bottle of water. Week three and four, increase to 15 minutes. You should start feeling your medication taking effect around this time. Your energy may lift slightly, and the walks may feel less like a chore. By week five and six, try 20-minute walks and add some gentle arm swings as you go."},{"id":"s4","title":"A simple home routine for hypothyroidism","body":"Once you have built a walking habit, add this gentle home routine two or three times per week. You need no equipment — just a chair and a mat or folded blanket on the floor.\\n\\nStart with seated marching. Sit upright in a sturdy chair and lift your knees one at a time, as if marching in place, for two minutes. This warms up your legs and gets blood flowing. Next, do wall push-ups. Stand an arm''s length from a wall, place your palms flat against it at shoulder height, and slowly bend your elbows to bring your chest toward the wall, then push back. Do 10 repetitions, rest for 30 seconds, and repeat twice. Move to standing calf raises — hold the back of your chair for balance and rise up on your toes, hold for two seconds, then lower. Do 15 repetitions twice. Finish with gentle stretching: reach your arms overhead and hold for 15 seconds, then touch your toes (or reach as far as you can) and hold for 15 seconds. The whole routine takes about 15 minutes."},{"id":"s5","title":"Special precautions for hyperthyroidism","body":"If you have an overactive thyroid, your resting heart rate may already be elevated — sometimes above 100 beats per minute. Adding intense exercise on top of this puts unnecessary strain on your heart. Until your doctor confirms that your thyroid levels are controlled (usually through blood tests showing TSH, T3, and T4 in normal ranges), stick to very low-intensity activities. Walking, gentle yoga, and light stretching are your friends. Avoid running, intense aerobics, competitive sports, or heavy lifting.\\n\\nCheck your pulse before exercising. Place two fingers on the inside of your wrist and count beats for 15 seconds, then multiply by four. If your resting heart rate is above 100, skip the workout and rest instead. During exercise, if you feel your heart pounding, experience chest tightness, feel dizzy, or notice excessive sweating beyond what is normal for you, stop immediately and sit down."},{"id":"s6","title":"Exercise and thyroid medication timing","body":"Take your levothyroxine on an empty stomach first thing in the morning with plain water — not with tea, coffee, or uji. Wait at least 30 to 60 minutes before eating breakfast. If you prefer to exercise in the morning, take your medication when you wake up, do your workout, and then have breakfast after. Some people find that exercising before their morning meal feels fine on thyroid medication, while others prefer a light breakfast first and exercise later. Find what works for you, but keep your medication timing consistent every day."},{"id":"s7","title":"When to see your doctor about exercise","body":"Tell your doctor at Kenyatta National Hospital, your county referral hospital, or your local clinic about your exercise plans at your next appointment. They can check whether your thyroid levels are stable enough for increased activity. Seek medical attention urgently if exercise causes chest pain, irregular heartbeat, fainting, or extreme breathlessness. These symptoms deserve same-day attention."},{"id":"s8","title":"Patience is everything","body":"Thyroid conditions respond well to proper medication and lifestyle changes, but improvement takes time. You may not feel the benefits of exercise for several weeks. Some days will be harder than others — the fatigue may return unexpectedly, or your energy may dip before your next dose adjustment. On those days, a five-minute walk still counts. On good days, push a little further. Over weeks and months, you will notice your stamina building, your mood lifting, and your body feeling more like your own again. Every step forward is a step toward feeling like yourself."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'morning-walk'),
  (E'myth-thyroid-problems-just-make-you-gain-or-lose-weight', E'Myth: Thyroid problems just make you gain or lose weight', E'THYROID', '{"THYROID"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The myth: Thyroid problems are basically just a weight issue. If your thyroid is underactive, you gain weight. In this lesson, you will separate medical facts from common misconceptions.', E'The myth: Thyroid problems are basically just a weight issue. If your thyroid is underactive, you gain weight. If it is overactive, you lose weight. Beyond that, it is not a serious condition. Some people even say things like, "Just watch your diet and you will be fine," or "Thyroid issues are not a real disease — it is just hormones."\n\nThe truth is far more complex\n\nThyroid disorders affect virtually every system in your body. Your thyroid gland — the small butterfly-shaped gland at the front of your neck — produces hormones that regulate your heart rate, body temperature, energy levels, brain function, digestion, menstrual cycle, skin health, and even your cholesterol levels. When this gland malfunctions, the effects ripple through your entire body in ways that many people, including some healthcare workers, fail to recognise.\n\nWhat hypothyroidism really does\n\nAn underactive thyroid (hypothyroidism) means your body is not producing enough thyroid hormone. Yes, weight gain is one symptom — but it is often the least distressing one. People with untreated hypothyroidism commonly experience crushing fatigue that no amount of sleep can fix. You might sleep eight or nine hours and still wake up feeling as if you ran a marathon. Brain fog makes it hard to concentrate at work or remember simple things like where you put your keys or what you needed from the duka.\n\nDepression is another major symptom. The low mood, lack of motivation, and feelings of hopelessness that accompany hypothyroidism are not "just feeling down" — they are caused by inadequate thyroid hormone reaching your brain. Many Kenyans with undiagnosed hypothyroidism are told they are lazy, unmotivated, or stressed when they actually have a treatable medical condition. Some are even prescribed antidepressants when what they truly need is levothyroxine.\n\nOther symptoms include constipation that does not respond to dietary changes, dry and flaky skin especially during the cold season, hair loss that clogs your shower drain, irregular or heavy menstrual periods, feeling cold when everyone around you is comfortable, muscle aches and joint pain, and a hoarse voice. In severe cases, untreated hypothyroidism can cause dangerously high cholesterol, heart problems, and a life-threatening condition called myxoedema coma.\n\nWhat hyperthyroidism really does\n\nAn overactive thyroid pushes everything into overdrive. Your heart races even when you are resting — sometimes reaching 120 or 130 beats per minute. You may feel anxious, jittery, and unable to sit still. Sleep becomes difficult because your mind will not switch off. Your hands may tremble, making it hard to write, type, or hold a cup of chai steady.\n\nHyperthyroidism causes diarrhoea and rapid weight loss that can leave you feeling weak and malnourished. Your eyes may become bulging and irritated, a condition called Graves'' ophthalmopathy. Relationships suffer because irritability and emotional volatility make you feel like a different person — snapping at your spouse, losing patience with your children, or becoming tearful for no clear reason. Untreated hyperthyroidism can cause a dangerous heart rhythm called atrial fibrillation, which increases your risk of stroke.\n\nWhy this myth is harmful in Kenya\n\nWhen people — or even healthcare providers — reduce thyroid disease to "just a weight issue," patients do not get the comprehensive care they need. In Kenya, thyroid function testing is available at most hospitals and many private labs. A basic TSH test costs between KES 800 and KES 2,000 depending on the facility. The full thyroid panel including TSH, free T3, and free T4 may cost KES 2,000 to KES 5,000. These are affordable tests that can change someone''s life, yet many people never get tested because their symptoms are dismissed.\n\nWomen are especially affected. Thyroid disorders are five to eight times more common in women than men, and symptoms often overlap with what society dismisses as normal female complaints — tiredness from childcare, mood swings from hormones, weight gain from age. If you are a woman in Kenya experiencing unexplained fatigue, mood changes, menstrual irregularities, or any of the symptoms described above, ask your doctor specifically for a thyroid test. Do not accept "it is just stress" or "you just need to exercise more" as final answers.\n\nThe path forward\n\nThyroid conditions are among the most treatable chronic diseases in medicine. Hypothyroidism is managed with daily levothyroxine tablets that cost between KES 200 and KES 500 per month. Hyperthyroidism can be managed with medications like carbimazole, radioactive iodine treatment, or surgery depending on the cause and severity. Most people with properly treated thyroid conditions live completely normal lives.\n\nBut treatment starts with proper diagnosis, and proper diagnosis starts with taking the full range of symptoms seriously — not just the number on the weighing scale. If you suspect a thyroid problem, request a TSH blood test from your doctor. If the results are abnormal, you deserve a referral to an endocrinologist or physician who specialises in thyroid care. Your symptoms are real, they are medical, and they deserve proper attention.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The myth: Thyroid problems are basically just a weight issue. If your thyroid is underactive, you gain weight. If it is overactive, you lose weight. Beyond that, it is not a serious condition. Some people even say things like, \\"Just watch your diet and you will be fine,\\" or \\"Thyroid issues are not a real disease — it is just hormones.\\""},{"id":"s2","title":"The truth is far more complex","body":"Thyroid disorders affect virtually every system in your body. Your thyroid gland — the small butterfly-shaped gland at the front of your neck — produces hormones that regulate your heart rate, body temperature, energy levels, brain function, digestion, menstrual cycle, skin health, and even your cholesterol levels. When this gland malfunctions, the effects ripple through your entire body in ways that many people, including some healthcare workers, fail to recognise."},{"id":"s3","title":"What hypothyroidism really does","body":"An underactive thyroid (hypothyroidism) means your body is not producing enough thyroid hormone. Yes, weight gain is one symptom — but it is often the least distressing one. People with untreated hypothyroidism commonly experience crushing fatigue that no amount of sleep can fix. You might sleep eight or nine hours and still wake up feeling as if you ran a marathon. Brain fog makes it hard to concentrate at work or remember simple things like where you put your keys or what you needed from the duka.\\n\\nDepression is another major symptom. The low mood, lack of motivation, and feelings of hopelessness that accompany hypothyroidism are not \\"just feeling down\\" — they are caused by inadequate thyroid hormone reaching your brain. Many Kenyans with undiagnosed hypothyroidism are told they are lazy, unmotivated, or stressed when they actually have a treatable medical condition. Some are even prescribed antidepressants when what they truly need is levothyroxine.\\n\\nOther symptoms include constipation that does not respond to dietary changes, dry and flaky skin especially during the cold season, hair loss that clogs your shower drain, irregular or heavy menstrual periods, feeling cold when everyone around you is comfortable, muscle aches and joint pain, and a hoarse voice. In severe cases, untreated hypothyroidism can cause dangerously high cholesterol, heart problems, and a life-threatening condition called myxoedema coma."},{"id":"s4","title":"What hyperthyroidism really does","body":"An overactive thyroid pushes everything into overdrive. Your heart races even when you are resting — sometimes reaching 120 or 130 beats per minute. You may feel anxious, jittery, and unable to sit still. Sleep becomes difficult because your mind will not switch off. Your hands may tremble, making it hard to write, type, or hold a cup of chai steady.\\n\\nHyperthyroidism causes diarrhoea and rapid weight loss that can leave you feeling weak and malnourished. Your eyes may become bulging and irritated, a condition called Graves'' ophthalmopathy. Relationships suffer because irritability and emotional volatility make you feel like a different person — snapping at your spouse, losing patience with your children, or becoming tearful for no clear reason. Untreated hyperthyroidism can cause a dangerous heart rhythm called atrial fibrillation, which increases your risk of stroke."},{"id":"s5","title":"Why this myth is harmful in Kenya","body":"When people — or even healthcare providers — reduce thyroid disease to \\"just a weight issue,\\" patients do not get the comprehensive care they need. In Kenya, thyroid function testing is available at most hospitals and many private labs. A basic TSH test costs between KES 800 and KES 2,000 depending on the facility. The full thyroid panel including TSH, free T3, and free T4 may cost KES 2,000 to KES 5,000. These are affordable tests that can change someone''s life, yet many people never get tested because their symptoms are dismissed.\\n\\nWomen are especially affected. Thyroid disorders are five to eight times more common in women than men, and symptoms often overlap with what society dismisses as normal female complaints — tiredness from childcare, mood swings from hormones, weight gain from age. If you are a woman in Kenya experiencing unexplained fatigue, mood changes, menstrual irregularities, or any of the symptoms described above, ask your doctor specifically for a thyroid test. Do not accept \\"it is just stress\\" or \\"you just need to exercise more\\" as final answers."},{"id":"s6","title":"The path forward","body":"Thyroid conditions are among the most treatable chronic diseases in medicine. Hypothyroidism is managed with daily levothyroxine tablets that cost between KES 200 and KES 500 per month. Hyperthyroidism can be managed with medications like carbimazole, radioactive iodine treatment, or surgery depending on the cause and severity. Most people with properly treated thyroid conditions live completely normal lives.\\n\\nBut treatment starts with proper diagnosis, and proper diagnosis starts with taking the full range of symptoms seriously — not just the number on the weighing scale. If you suspect a thyroid problem, request a TSH blood test from your doctor. If the results are abnormal, you deserve a referral to an endocrinologist or physician who specialises in thyroid care. Your symptoms are real, they are medical, and they deserve proper attention."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'health-facts'),
  (E'when-your-thyroid-affects-your-mind-more-than-your-body', E'When your thyroid affects your mind more than your body', E'THYROID', '{"THYROID"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'When people think of thyroid disease, they usually picture the physical symptoms — weight changes, fatigue, hair loss. But for many Kenyans living with thyroid conditions, the hardest part is what happens inside your mind.', E'When people think of thyroid disease, they usually picture the physical symptoms — weight changes, fatigue, hair loss. But for many Kenyans living with thyroid conditions, the hardest part is what happens inside your mind. Thyroid hormones directly influence brain chemistry, and when those hormone levels are off, your thoughts, emotions, and mental clarity can change dramatically.\n\nThe depression connection with hypothyroidism\n\nIf your thyroid is underactive, you may feel a heavy sadness that settles over you like a blanket. Activities you once enjoyed — visiting friends, cooking a meal for your family, attending church on Sunday, walking through the market — may feel pointless or overwhelming. You might withdraw from family and friends, not because you do not love them, but because you simply do not have the emotional energy to engage. This is not weakness or a character flaw. It is your brain being starved of the thyroid hormone it needs to function properly.\n\nMany people describe hypothyroid depression as different from ordinary sadness. It comes with brain fog — a cloudiness that makes it hard to think clearly, follow conversations, or remember things. You might walk into a room and forget why you are there. You might read a page of text three times and still not absorb it. At work, tasks that used to take an hour might take three. This cognitive sluggishness is frustrating and frightening, especially if you do not know its cause.\n\nIn Kenya, mental health still carries stigma in many communities. You may hear people say, "Just pray about it," or "Be strong — other people have bigger problems." Family members may accuse you of being lazy or dramatic. This makes the isolation worse. If you are experiencing these symptoms and have not had your thyroid checked, please ask your doctor for a TSH blood test. It costs between KES 800 and KES 2,000 at most labs, and it could be the test that finally explains what you have been going through.\n\nThe anxiety hurricane of hyperthyroidism\n\nHyperthyroidism creates the opposite emotional storm. Your body floods with excess thyroid hormone, and your nervous system goes into overdrive. You may feel a constant undercurrent of anxiety — a tightness in your chest, a racing mind, a sense that something terrible is about to happen even when everything is fine. Your heart pounds. Your hands shake. You cannot sleep, and the lack of sleep makes the anxiety worse.\n\nIrritability is another hallmark. Small things that you would normally handle with patience — a child spilling milk, a matatu being late, a colleague asking a simple question — may trigger an outsized reaction. You might find yourself snapping at your spouse, raising your voice at your children, or crying over something minor. Afterwards, you feel guilty and confused because you know your reaction was disproportionate, but in the moment, you could not control it.\n\nThis emotional volatility can damage relationships. Your partner may feel like they are walking on eggshells around you. Your children may become anxious themselves. Friends may pull away because they do not know how to help. If this sounds familiar, please know that this is the thyroid talking, not the real you. Treatment can restore your emotional equilibrium.\n\nWhen thyroid problems mimic mental illness\n\nHere is something important that not enough people know: thyroid disorders can so closely mimic depression, anxiety, bipolar disorder, or even dementia that patients receive psychiatric diagnoses and medications without ever having their thyroid checked. A study might find that a significant number of patients diagnosed with depression actually had undiagnosed hypothyroidism. While Kenya does not yet have large-scale data on this overlap, doctors at Kenyatta National Hospital and other major facilities are increasingly aware of the connection.\n\nIf you were prescribed antidepressants or anti-anxiety medication and they do not seem to be working, or if they help somewhat but you still do not feel like yourself, ask your doctor whether your thyroid has been tested. This is not about rejecting mental health treatment — it is about making sure the root cause is being addressed.\n\nPractical coping strategies while you stabilise\n\nWhile your medication adjusts your thyroid levels — which can take four to eight weeks — here are things you can do to protect your mental health.\n\nTalk to someone you trust. This might be your spouse, a sibling, a close friend, a pastor, or a counsellor. Simply saying, "I am struggling and it is related to my thyroid condition," can lift some of the weight. You do not have to explain the science — just let people know you are going through a medical challenge that affects your mood.\n\nKeep a mood journal. Each evening, rate your mood on a scale of one to ten and note any triggers. Over time, you will see patterns — perhaps your mood dips at certain times of the month, or improves on days when you exercise or sleep well. This information is valuable for your doctor when adjusting your medication dose.\n\nMaintain a basic routine. Even on days when getting out of bed feels impossible, try to do three things: take your medication, eat a proper meal, and step outside for at least 10 minutes. Routine provides structure when your emotions feel chaotic.\n\nBe honest with your doctor. Kenyan culture sometimes encourages us to be stoic with healthcare providers — to focus on physical symptoms and downplay emotional ones. But your doctor needs to know about your mood changes, sleep problems, anxiety, or depression. These symptoms are just as medically relevant as your heart rate or blood pressure. Write them down before your appointment so you do not forget.\n\nThe fog does lift\n\nOnce your thyroid medication reaches the right dose and your hormone levels stabilise, most people experience significant improvement in their mental health. Many describe it as a fog lifting — suddenly the world has colour again, thoughts come more easily, and emotions feel proportionate to events. This moment is worth waiting for and worth fighting for. You are not broken. Your thyroid is treatable, and the person you remember being is still there, waiting for the chemistry to be set right.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"When people think of thyroid disease, they usually picture the physical symptoms — weight changes, fatigue, hair loss. But for many Kenyans living with thyroid conditions, the hardest part is what happens inside your mind. Thyroid hormones directly influence brain chemistry, and when those hormone levels are off, your thoughts, emotions, and mental clarity can change dramatically."},{"id":"s2","title":"The depression connection with hypothyroidism","body":"If your thyroid is underactive, you may feel a heavy sadness that settles over you like a blanket. Activities you once enjoyed — visiting friends, cooking a meal for your family, attending church on Sunday, walking through the market — may feel pointless or overwhelming. You might withdraw from family and friends, not because you do not love them, but because you simply do not have the emotional energy to engage. This is not weakness or a character flaw. It is your brain being starved of the thyroid hormone it needs to function properly.\\n\\nMany people describe hypothyroid depression as different from ordinary sadness. It comes with brain fog — a cloudiness that makes it hard to think clearly, follow conversations, or remember things. You might walk into a room and forget why you are there. You might read a page of text three times and still not absorb it. At work, tasks that used to take an hour might take three. This cognitive sluggishness is frustrating and frightening, especially if you do not know its cause.\\n\\nIn Kenya, mental health still carries stigma in many communities. You may hear people say, \\"Just pray about it,\\" or \\"Be strong — other people have bigger problems.\\" Family members may accuse you of being lazy or dramatic. This makes the isolation worse. If you are experiencing these symptoms and have not had your thyroid checked, please ask your doctor for a TSH blood test. It costs between KES 800 and KES 2,000 at most labs, and it could be the test that finally explains what you have been going through."},{"id":"s3","title":"The anxiety hurricane of hyperthyroidism","body":"Hyperthyroidism creates the opposite emotional storm. Your body floods with excess thyroid hormone, and your nervous system goes into overdrive. You may feel a constant undercurrent of anxiety — a tightness in your chest, a racing mind, a sense that something terrible is about to happen even when everything is fine. Your heart pounds. Your hands shake. You cannot sleep, and the lack of sleep makes the anxiety worse.\\n\\nIrritability is another hallmark. Small things that you would normally handle with patience — a child spilling milk, a matatu being late, a colleague asking a simple question — may trigger an outsized reaction. You might find yourself snapping at your spouse, raising your voice at your children, or crying over something minor. Afterwards, you feel guilty and confused because you know your reaction was disproportionate, but in the moment, you could not control it.\\n\\nThis emotional volatility can damage relationships. Your partner may feel like they are walking on eggshells around you. Your children may become anxious themselves. Friends may pull away because they do not know how to help. If this sounds familiar, please know that this is the thyroid talking, not the real you. Treatment can restore your emotional equilibrium."},{"id":"s4","title":"When thyroid problems mimic mental illness","body":"Here is something important that not enough people know: thyroid disorders can so closely mimic depression, anxiety, bipolar disorder, or even dementia that patients receive psychiatric diagnoses and medications without ever having their thyroid checked. A study might find that a significant number of patients diagnosed with depression actually had undiagnosed hypothyroidism. While Kenya does not yet have large-scale data on this overlap, doctors at Kenyatta National Hospital and other major facilities are increasingly aware of the connection.\\n\\nIf you were prescribed antidepressants or anti-anxiety medication and they do not seem to be working, or if they help somewhat but you still do not feel like yourself, ask your doctor whether your thyroid has been tested. This is not about rejecting mental health treatment — it is about making sure the root cause is being addressed."},{"id":"s5","title":"Practical coping strategies while you stabilise","body":"While your medication adjusts your thyroid levels — which can take four to eight weeks — here are things you can do to protect your mental health.\\n\\nTalk to someone you trust. This might be your spouse, a sibling, a close friend, a pastor, or a counsellor. Simply saying, \\"I am struggling and it is related to my thyroid condition,\\" can lift some of the weight. You do not have to explain the science — just let people know you are going through a medical challenge that affects your mood.\\n\\nKeep a mood journal. Each evening, rate your mood on a scale of one to ten and note any triggers. Over time, you will see patterns — perhaps your mood dips at certain times of the month, or improves on days when you exercise or sleep well. This information is valuable for your doctor when adjusting your medication dose.\\n\\nMaintain a basic routine. Even on days when getting out of bed feels impossible, try to do three things: take your medication, eat a proper meal, and step outside for at least 10 minutes. Routine provides structure when your emotions feel chaotic.\\n\\nBe honest with your doctor. Kenyan culture sometimes encourages us to be stoic with healthcare providers — to focus on physical symptoms and downplay emotional ones. But your doctor needs to know about your mood changes, sleep problems, anxiety, or depression. These symptoms are just as medically relevant as your heart rate or blood pressure. Write them down before your appointment so you do not forget."},{"id":"s6","title":"The fog does lift","body":"Once your thyroid medication reaches the right dose and your hormone levels stabilise, most people experience significant improvement in their mental health. Many describe it as a fog lifting — suddenly the world has colour again, thoughts come more easily, and emotions feel proportionate to events. This moment is worth waiting for and worth fighting for. You are not broken. Your thyroid is treatable, and the person you remember being is still there, waiting for the chemistry to be set right."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'family-support'),
  (E'tracking-symptoms-between-blood-tests', E'Tracking symptoms between blood tests', E'THYROID', '{"THYROID"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'Managing a thyroid condition is not just about taking your medication and waiting for your next doctor''s appointment. What happens between appointments matters enormously.', E'Managing a thyroid condition is not just about taking your medication and waiting for your next doctor''s appointment. What happens between appointments matters enormously. Your thyroid levels can shift due to stress, dietary changes, other medications, illness, or even changes in the brand of levothyroxine you take. Tracking your symptoms daily gives you and your doctor critical information for fine-tuning your treatment.\n\nWhy tracking matters\n\nWhen you visit your doctor for a thyroid check-up, the blood test gives a snapshot of your hormone levels at that single moment. But your symptoms tell the story of how you have been feeling over weeks and months. A doctor who sees your TSH is in the normal range might assume everything is fine — but your symptom diary might reveal that you have been experiencing crushing fatigue every afternoon, or that your hair loss has worsened, or that your anxiety has returned. This information can prompt your doctor to look deeper, perhaps checking free T3 and free T4 levels, or adjusting your dose even within the "normal" range.\n\nWhat to track every day\n\nGet a small notebook — the kind you can buy for KES 50 at any bookshop or stationery stall — and dedicate it to your thyroid tracking. Each day, note these key symptoms.\n\nEnergy level: Rate it from one to ten each morning and each evening. A one means you can barely get out of bed, a ten means you feel fully energised. Over time, you will see your average creep up as your medication works, or you might notice it dropping before your next blood test confirms a dosage problem.\n\nWeight: Weigh yourself once a week, on the same day, at the same time (ideally first thing in the morning after using the bathroom, before eating). Unexplained weight gain of more than two kilograms in a month may suggest your hypothyroidism is undercontrolled. Unexplained weight loss may suggest your hyperthyroidism is flaring. Write the number down — do not rely on memory.\n\nMood: Note your general emotional state each day. Were you calm, anxious, irritable, tearful, or numb? Did your mood feel proportionate to your day, or did small things trigger big reactions? Mood changes are often the earliest sign of a thyroid level shift.\n\nHeart rate: Check your resting pulse each morning before getting out of bed. Place two fingers on the inside of your wrist, below your thumb, and count beats for 15 seconds, then multiply by four. A resting heart rate consistently above 90 to 100 could suggest excess thyroid hormone. A rate below 60, especially if you are not an athlete, might indicate insufficient thyroid hormone. Many affordable blood pressure monitors available in Kenyan pharmacies for KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 also display heart rate.\n\nSleep quality: Note how many hours you slept and whether you woke frequently. Hypothyroidism often causes excessive sleepiness despite long hours in bed. Hyperthyroidism causes insomnia and restless sleep. Also note if you needed to nap during the day.\n\nBowel habits: This is not a topic most people enjoy discussing, but it is medically important. Constipation is a hallmark of hypothyroidism, while loose stools or diarrhoea often accompany hyperthyroidism. Note whether your digestion felt normal each day.\n\nOther symptoms to note: Hair loss (more than usual in your comb or shower), dry skin, feeling unusually cold or hot, menstrual changes, trembling hands, muscle cramps, swelling in your face or legs, or a hoarse voice.\n\nHow often are blood tests needed\n\nWhen you are first diagnosed or when your medication dose changes, your doctor will order blood tests every six to eight weeks. This is because it takes that long for your body to fully adjust to a new dose of thyroid medication. Once your levels are stable, testing moves to every three to six months, and eventually to once or twice a year for long-term stable patients.\n\nIn Kenya, a basic TSH test costs approximately KES 800 to KES 2,000 depending on the facility. A full thyroid panel — TSH, free T3, and free T4 — costs between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000. If you are covered under SHA (the Social Health Authority, which replaced NHIF), check with your facility whether thyroid tests are covered under your plan. Some Level 4 and Level 5 hospitals include basic thyroid testing in their outpatient package. Private labs such as Lancet and PathCare offer reliable results, often within 24 to 48 hours.\n\nWhen your blood is drawn, make sure you have taken your levothyroxine as usual that morning, unless your doctor specifically tells you otherwise. Some doctors prefer you skip the morning dose before the test to avoid a temporary spike in hormone levels, but this varies — ask your specific doctor for their preference and follow it consistently.\n\nWarning signs that need immediate attention\n\nDo not wait for your scheduled appointment if you experience any of these symptoms. Contact your doctor or visit your nearest hospital the same day.\n\nRapid heart rate above 120 beats per minute that does not settle with rest. This could indicate a thyroid storm if you have hyperthyroidism, which is a medical emergency. Severe, sudden fatigue — the kind where you cannot get out of bed or stay awake — especially if it comes on suddenly after weeks of feeling stable. Significant swelling in your neck, face, or legs. Chest pain or difficulty breathing. Confusion, disorientation, or feeling extremely cold with a low body temperature. Any sudden, dramatic change from your baseline.\n\nSharing your tracking data with your doctor\n\nBring your symptom notebook to every appointment. Before you go in, review the past few weeks and circle any concerning patterns. You might say to your doctor, "My energy has been averaging three out of ten for the past month," or "My resting heart rate has been above 95 every morning this week." This kind of specific, tracked information is far more useful than saying, "I have not been feeling great." It helps your doctor make faster, more accurate decisions about your care.\n\nIf you prefer digital tracking, simple note-taking apps on your phone work well. Some patients use a basic spreadsheet with columns for date, energy, mood, heart rate, weight, and notes. Whatever method you choose, the key is consistency — a complete record over time is worth more than a perfect but sporadic one.\n\nYour symptoms are data, and data drives better treatment. You are your own best health monitor between those blood test appointments.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Managing a thyroid condition is not just about taking your medication and waiting for your next doctor''s appointment. What happens between appointments matters enormously. Your thyroid levels can shift due to stress, dietary changes, other medications, illness, or even changes in the brand of levothyroxine you take. Tracking your symptoms daily gives you and your doctor critical information for fine-tuning your treatment."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why tracking matters","body":"When you visit your doctor for a thyroid check-up, the blood test gives a snapshot of your hormone levels at that single moment. But your symptoms tell the story of how you have been feeling over weeks and months. A doctor who sees your TSH is in the normal range might assume everything is fine — but your symptom diary might reveal that you have been experiencing crushing fatigue every afternoon, or that your hair loss has worsened, or that your anxiety has returned. This information can prompt your doctor to look deeper, perhaps checking free T3 and free T4 levels, or adjusting your dose even within the \\"normal\\" range."},{"id":"s3","title":"What to track every day","body":"Get a small notebook — the kind you can buy for KES 50 at any bookshop or stationery stall — and dedicate it to your thyroid tracking. Each day, note these key symptoms.\\n\\nEnergy level: Rate it from one to ten each morning and each evening. A one means you can barely get out of bed, a ten means you feel fully energised. Over time, you will see your average creep up as your medication works, or you might notice it dropping before your next blood test confirms a dosage problem.\\n\\nWeight: Weigh yourself once a week, on the same day, at the same time (ideally first thing in the morning after using the bathroom, before eating). Unexplained weight gain of more than two kilograms in a month may suggest your hypothyroidism is undercontrolled. Unexplained weight loss may suggest your hyperthyroidism is flaring. Write the number down — do not rely on memory.\\n\\nMood: Note your general emotional state each day. Were you calm, anxious, irritable, tearful, or numb? Did your mood feel proportionate to your day, or did small things trigger big reactions? Mood changes are often the earliest sign of a thyroid level shift.\\n\\nHeart rate: Check your resting pulse each morning before getting out of bed. Place two fingers on the inside of your wrist, below your thumb, and count beats for 15 seconds, then multiply by four. A resting heart rate consistently above 90 to 100 could suggest excess thyroid hormone. A rate below 60, especially if you are not an athlete, might indicate insufficient thyroid hormone. Many affordable blood pressure monitors available in Kenyan pharmacies for KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 also display heart rate.\\n\\nSleep quality: Note how many hours you slept and whether you woke frequently. Hypothyroidism often causes excessive sleepiness despite long hours in bed. Hyperthyroidism causes insomnia and restless sleep. Also note if you needed to nap during the day.\\n\\nBowel habits: This is not a topic most people enjoy discussing, but it is medically important. Constipation is a hallmark of hypothyroidism, while loose stools or diarrhoea often accompany hyperthyroidism. Note whether your digestion felt normal each day.\\n\\nOther symptoms to note: Hair loss (more than usual in your comb or shower), dry skin, feeling unusually cold or hot, menstrual changes, trembling hands, muscle cramps, swelling in your face or legs, or a hoarse voice."},{"id":"s4","title":"How often are blood tests needed","body":"When you are first diagnosed or when your medication dose changes, your doctor will order blood tests every six to eight weeks. This is because it takes that long for your body to fully adjust to a new dose of thyroid medication. Once your levels are stable, testing moves to every three to six months, and eventually to once or twice a year for long-term stable patients.\\n\\nIn Kenya, a basic TSH test costs approximately KES 800 to KES 2,000 depending on the facility. A full thyroid panel — TSH, free T3, and free T4 — costs between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000. If you are covered under SHA (the Social Health Authority, which replaced NHIF), check with your facility whether thyroid tests are covered under your plan. Some Level 4 and Level 5 hospitals include basic thyroid testing in their outpatient package. Private labs such as Lancet and PathCare offer reliable results, often within 24 to 48 hours.\\n\\nWhen your blood is drawn, make sure you have taken your levothyroxine as usual that morning, unless your doctor specifically tells you otherwise. Some doctors prefer you skip the morning dose before the test to avoid a temporary spike in hormone levels, but this varies — ask your specific doctor for their preference and follow it consistently."},{"id":"s5","title":"Warning signs that need immediate attention","body":"Do not wait for your scheduled appointment if you experience any of these symptoms. Contact your doctor or visit your nearest hospital the same day.\\n\\nRapid heart rate above 120 beats per minute that does not settle with rest. This could indicate a thyroid storm if you have hyperthyroidism, which is a medical emergency. Severe, sudden fatigue — the kind where you cannot get out of bed or stay awake — especially if it comes on suddenly after weeks of feeling stable. Significant swelling in your neck, face, or legs. Chest pain or difficulty breathing. Confusion, disorientation, or feeling extremely cold with a low body temperature. Any sudden, dramatic change from your baseline."},{"id":"s6","title":"Sharing your tracking data with your doctor","body":"Bring your symptom notebook to every appointment. Before you go in, review the past few weeks and circle any concerning patterns. You might say to your doctor, \\"My energy has been averaging three out of ten for the past month,\\" or \\"My resting heart rate has been above 95 every morning this week.\\" This kind of specific, tracked information is far more useful than saying, \\"I have not been feeling great.\\" It helps your doctor make faster, more accurate decisions about your care.\\n\\nIf you prefer digital tracking, simple note-taking apps on your phone work well. Some patients use a basic spreadsheet with columns for date, energy, mood, heart rate, weight, and notes. Whatever method you choose, the key is consistency — a complete record over time is worth more than a perfect but sporadic one.\\n\\nYour symptoms are data, and data drives better treatment. You are your own best health monitor between those blood test appointments."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'glucose-monitor'),
  (E'the-relief-of-finally-getting-a-diagnosis', E'The relief of finally getting a diagnosis', E'THYROID', '{"THYROID"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'If you have recently been diagnosed with a thyroid condition, you might be experiencing a strange mix of emotions — relief that there is finally an explanation for how you have been feeling, but also frustration, anger, or sadness about what you have been through to get here.', E'If you have recently been diagnosed with a thyroid condition, you might be experiencing a strange mix of emotions — relief that there is finally an explanation for how you have been feeling, but also frustration, anger, or sadness about what you have been through to get here. Both reactions are completely valid, and you are not alone in feeling this way.\n\nThe long road to diagnosis\n\nMany thyroid patients in Kenya spend months or even years being told there is nothing wrong with them. You may have visited multiple doctors. You may have been told your fatigue is because you are working too hard, or because you are a new mother, or because you are getting older. Your weight gain may have been blamed on eating too much ugali and chapati. Your hair loss may have been attributed to stress or harsh hair products. Your depression may have been dismissed as spiritual weakness or a bad attitude.\n\nFor women, the dismissal is often worse. Thyroid symptoms overlap heavily with what society considers normal female complaints — tiredness, mood swings, weight fluctuations, irregular periods. How many times were you told, "That is just how women''s bodies work"? How many times did you doubt your own experience because a medical professional told you there was nothing to find?\n\nAnd then, finally, someone orders the right blood test. The TSH comes back abnormal. And suddenly everything makes sense.\n\nThe relief is real\n\nThat moment of diagnosis can feel like the biggest exhale of your life. You are not lazy. You are not imagining things. You are not weak, dramatic, or making excuses. There is a measurable, treatable medical condition causing your symptoms, and it has a name. For many people, the diagnosis itself is therapeutic — even before treatment begins — because it validates what you knew all along: something was genuinely wrong.\n\nSome patients describe crying when they receive their diagnosis. Not tears of sadness, but tears of relief mixed with grief for the time lost. If you find yourself emotional, let it flow. You earned those tears.\n\nThe frustration is also real\n\nAlongside the relief, you may feel angry. Angry at the doctors who dismissed you. Angry at the months or years you spent struggling unnecessarily. Angry at the relationships that suffered because you were not yourself. Angry at the work opportunities you missed because your brain fog made you seem incompetent. Angry at yourself for not pushing harder for answers sooner.\n\nThis frustration is valid but it is important not to let it consume you. The diagnosis is your turning point. What matters now is what happens next, and what happens next is very encouraging.\n\nThyroid treatment — one of medicine''s success stories\n\nHere is the genuinely good news: thyroid conditions are among the most effectively treated chronic diseases in all of medicine. If you have hypothyroidism, you will take a daily tablet called levothyroxine. It is affordable — typically KES 200 to KES 500 per month at Kenyan pharmacies including those at Kenyatta National Hospital, Aga Khan, and local chemists in your town. It replaces the hormone your thyroid is not making enough of. Over four to six weeks, your body adjusts, and symptoms begin to lift.\n\nIf you have hyperthyroidism, treatment options include medication (usually carbimazole), radioactive iodine therapy, or surgery, depending on the cause and severity. Your doctor will explain the best option for your specific situation.\n\nThe important thing to understand is this: treatment works. It is not a matter of hoping or trying. Thyroid hormone replacement is one of the most predictable, reliable treatments in medicine. When the dosage is right, most people feel dramatically better.\n\nAdjusting to life with a chronic condition\n\nEven with effective treatment, accepting that you have a lifelong condition takes time. You will need to take medication every day — first thing in the morning, on an empty stomach, with plain water. You will need regular blood tests to ensure your levels stay stable. You will need to tell future doctors about your thyroid condition, because it can interact with other health issues and medications.\n\nFor some people, the idea of lifelong medication feels heavy. In Kenya, there can be cultural resistance to the concept of taking pills every day. You may hear well-meaning relatives suggest herbal alternatives or pray that you can stop the medication. It is fine to incorporate prayer and cultural practices into your healing journey, but please do not stop your thyroid medication without your doctor''s guidance. Unlike some conditions, thyroid hormone deficiency does not resolve on its own — your body needs that daily replacement.\n\nPractical tips for your first weeks after diagnosis\n\nTake your medication at the same time every morning. Set a phone alarm if needed. Keep your pills on your bedside table so they are the first thing you reach for. Wait 30 to 60 minutes before eating breakfast or drinking tea, as food interferes with absorption.\n\nBe patient with yourself. Improvement is not overnight. Some symptoms like energy and mood may improve within two to three weeks. Others, like hair regrowth and weight normalisation, may take three to six months. Celebrate small improvements — sleeping better, having enough energy to cook dinner, laughing at something your child said.\n\nTell your close family what is happening. You do not need to announce it to everyone, but your spouse, parents, or siblings should understand that you have a medical condition that has been affecting your health and that you are now receiving treatment. Their support and patience during your adjustment period makes a real difference.\n\nConnect with others if you can. Ask your doctor or check community health groups whether there are support groups for thyroid patients in your area. Even an online group where Kenyan patients share experiences can help you feel less alone.\n\nYou have a path forward now\n\nThe hardest part — the not knowing, the being dismissed, the doubting yourself — is behind you. You have a diagnosis, you have treatment, and you have every reason to expect significant improvement. The journey is not over, but the worst of it is. Hold onto that. You advocated for your health, you got answers, and things are about to get better.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you have recently been diagnosed with a thyroid condition, you might be experiencing a strange mix of emotions — relief that there is finally an explanation for how you have been feeling, but also frustration, anger, or sadness about what you have been through to get here. Both reactions are completely valid, and you are not alone in feeling this way."},{"id":"s2","title":"The long road to diagnosis","body":"Many thyroid patients in Kenya spend months or even years being told there is nothing wrong with them. You may have visited multiple doctors. You may have been told your fatigue is because you are working too hard, or because you are a new mother, or because you are getting older. Your weight gain may have been blamed on eating too much ugali and chapati. Your hair loss may have been attributed to stress or harsh hair products. Your depression may have been dismissed as spiritual weakness or a bad attitude.\\n\\nFor women, the dismissal is often worse. Thyroid symptoms overlap heavily with what society considers normal female complaints — tiredness, mood swings, weight fluctuations, irregular periods. How many times were you told, \\"That is just how women''s bodies work\\"? How many times did you doubt your own experience because a medical professional told you there was nothing to find?\\n\\nAnd then, finally, someone orders the right blood test. The TSH comes back abnormal. And suddenly everything makes sense."},{"id":"s3","title":"The relief is real","body":"That moment of diagnosis can feel like the biggest exhale of your life. You are not lazy. You are not imagining things. You are not weak, dramatic, or making excuses. There is a measurable, treatable medical condition causing your symptoms, and it has a name. For many people, the diagnosis itself is therapeutic — even before treatment begins — because it validates what you knew all along: something was genuinely wrong.\\n\\nSome patients describe crying when they receive their diagnosis. Not tears of sadness, but tears of relief mixed with grief for the time lost. If you find yourself emotional, let it flow. You earned those tears."},{"id":"s4","title":"The frustration is also real","body":"Alongside the relief, you may feel angry. Angry at the doctors who dismissed you. Angry at the months or years you spent struggling unnecessarily. Angry at the relationships that suffered because you were not yourself. Angry at the work opportunities you missed because your brain fog made you seem incompetent. Angry at yourself for not pushing harder for answers sooner.\\n\\nThis frustration is valid but it is important not to let it consume you. The diagnosis is your turning point. What matters now is what happens next, and what happens next is very encouraging."},{"id":"s5","title":"Thyroid treatment — one of medicine''s success stories","body":"Here is the genuinely good news: thyroid conditions are among the most effectively treated chronic diseases in all of medicine. If you have hypothyroidism, you will take a daily tablet called levothyroxine. It is affordable — typically KES 200 to KES 500 per month at Kenyan pharmacies including those at Kenyatta National Hospital, Aga Khan, and local chemists in your town. It replaces the hormone your thyroid is not making enough of. Over four to six weeks, your body adjusts, and symptoms begin to lift.\\n\\nIf you have hyperthyroidism, treatment options include medication (usually carbimazole), radioactive iodine therapy, or surgery, depending on the cause and severity. Your doctor will explain the best option for your specific situation.\\n\\nThe important thing to understand is this: treatment works. It is not a matter of hoping or trying. Thyroid hormone replacement is one of the most predictable, reliable treatments in medicine. When the dosage is right, most people feel dramatically better."},{"id":"s6","title":"Adjusting to life with a chronic condition","body":"Even with effective treatment, accepting that you have a lifelong condition takes time. You will need to take medication every day — first thing in the morning, on an empty stomach, with plain water. You will need regular blood tests to ensure your levels stay stable. You will need to tell future doctors about your thyroid condition, because it can interact with other health issues and medications.\\n\\nFor some people, the idea of lifelong medication feels heavy. In Kenya, there can be cultural resistance to the concept of taking pills every day. You may hear well-meaning relatives suggest herbal alternatives or pray that you can stop the medication. It is fine to incorporate prayer and cultural practices into your healing journey, but please do not stop your thyroid medication without your doctor''s guidance. Unlike some conditions, thyroid hormone deficiency does not resolve on its own — your body needs that daily replacement."},{"id":"s7","title":"Practical tips for your first weeks after diagnosis","body":"Take your medication at the same time every morning. Set a phone alarm if needed. Keep your pills on your bedside table so they are the first thing you reach for. Wait 30 to 60 minutes before eating breakfast or drinking tea, as food interferes with absorption.\\n\\nBe patient with yourself. Improvement is not overnight. Some symptoms like energy and mood may improve within two to three weeks. Others, like hair regrowth and weight normalisation, may take three to six months. Celebrate small improvements — sleeping better, having enough energy to cook dinner, laughing at something your child said.\\n\\nTell your close family what is happening. You do not need to announce it to everyone, but your spouse, parents, or siblings should understand that you have a medical condition that has been affecting your health and that you are now receiving treatment. Their support and patience during your adjustment period makes a real difference.\\n\\nConnect with others if you can. Ask your doctor or check community health groups whether there are support groups for thyroid patients in your area. Even an online group where Kenyan patients share experiences can help you feel less alone."},{"id":"s8","title":"You have a path forward now","body":"The hardest part — the not knowing, the being dismissed, the doubting yourself — is behind you. You have a diagnosis, you have treatment, and you have every reason to expect significant improvement. The journey is not over, but the worst of it is. Hold onto that. You advocated for your health, you got answers, and things are about to get better."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'self-care'),
  (E'tsh-in-normal-range-your-medication-is-dialled-in', E'TSH in normal range — your medication is dialled in', E'THYROID', '{"THYROID"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Congratulations — your latest TSH blood test came back in the normal range. This is a significant achievement and one worth pausing to appreciate.', E'Congratulations — your latest TSH blood test came back in the normal range. This is a significant achievement and one worth pausing to appreciate. If you have been on the thyroid treatment journey for weeks or months, adjusting doses and waiting patiently through blood test after blood test, this result confirms that your persistence has paid off.\n\nWhat this result means\n\nTSH stands for thyroid-stimulating hormone. It is produced by your pituitary gland (a small gland at the base of your brain) and acts as a messenger, telling your thyroid how much hormone to make. When your thyroid is underactive, your pituitary gland sends out more TSH — essentially shouting louder to try to get the thyroid to work harder. A high TSH means your body needs more thyroid hormone than it is getting. When your TSH is in the normal range (typically 0.4 to 4.0 mIU/L, though your doctor may have a narrower target), it means your body is getting the right amount of thyroid hormone from your medication.\n\nFor hyperthyroid patients on medication like carbimazole, a normalised TSH means the overproduction has been reined in. Your thyroid is no longer flooding your body with excess hormone.\n\nEither way, this result means your treatment is working and your dose is correct. Your cells, organs, and brain are receiving the thyroid hormone they need to function properly.\n\nThe journey to get here\n\nReaching this point typically takes three to six months of careful dose adjustments. After your initial diagnosis, your doctor prescribed a starting dose of thyroid medication. Six to eight weeks later, you had a blood test. Perhaps your levels improved but were not quite right, so the dose was adjusted — maybe increased by 25 micrograms. Another six to eight weeks of waiting, another blood test. For some people, it takes two or three adjustments before the dose is dialled in perfectly.\n\nDuring this period, you may have experienced fluctuating symptoms. Some weeks you felt almost normal; other weeks the fatigue or anxiety crept back. You kept taking your medication faithfully, kept going to your appointments, kept trusting the process even when it felt slow. That consistency is what got you here, and it deserves recognition.\n\nIf you have been paying for blood tests at a private lab — TSH tests cost KES 800 to KES 2,000 per draw — the financial commitment over several months adds up. If you are on SHA, check whether your plan covers the ongoing monitoring. Either way, these tests are an investment in your health that is clearly paying dividends.\n\nWhat you should be feeling now\n\nWith your thyroid levels stabilised, you should notice improvements across multiple areas of your life. Your energy levels should be more consistent throughout the day — less of the afternoon crashes and bone-deep exhaustion. Your mood should feel more stable and more like the person you were before thyroid problems began. Brain fog should be lifting, making it easier to concentrate at work, follow conversations, and remember things.\n\nPhysical symptoms improve too. If you had hypothyroidism, your weight may begin to normalise (though medication alone does not cause dramatic weight loss — healthy eating with plenty of vegetables, moderate portions of ugali and rice, and lean proteins like fish and chicken will help). Hair loss should slow down and you may notice new growth. Constipation often resolves. Dry skin improves. If you had hyperthyroidism, your heart rate should return to normal, hand tremors should ease, and sleep should become more restful.\n\nIf you are not feeling significantly better despite normal TSH levels, tell your doctor. Some patients feel best when their TSH is in the lower half of the normal range rather than the upper half. Others may need their free T3 and free T4 levels checked, as TSH alone does not always tell the complete story.\n\nKeeping your levels stable going forward\n\nReaching normal TSH is the beginning of the maintenance phase, not the end of your treatment. Here is how to keep your levels stable.\n\nTake your medication consistently. Same time every morning, on an empty stomach, with plain water. Wait at least 30 minutes before eating breakfast or drinking tea or coffee. If you have uji or chai every morning, take your pill when your alarm goes off and have breakfast after you have dressed and prepared for the day. Consistency in timing is more important than the exact hour — pick a routine that works for you and stick with it every single day.\n\nDo not switch brands without discussing it with your doctor. Different brands of levothyroxine can have slightly different absorption rates. If your usual brand is out of stock at your pharmacy, let your doctor know so they can monitor whether your levels remain stable on the new brand. Pharmacies at major hospitals like Nairobi Hospital, MP Shah, and Aga Khan typically stock consistent brands.\n\nTell your doctor about any new medications. Calcium supplements, iron tablets, antacids, and some other drugs interfere with thyroid medication absorption. If you start any new medication, your doctor may need to adjust the timing of your levothyroxine or recheck your levels.\n\nContinue monitoring. Your doctor will likely schedule your next blood test in three to six months. Once you have been stable for a year or more, annual testing may be sufficient. Do not skip these tests — thyroid needs can change over time due to ageing, pregnancy, menopause, weight changes, or other factors.\n\nCelebrate this milestone\n\nIn a healthcare journey that often feels like an endless series of appointments and blood draws, a normal TSH result is a concrete, measurable win. Share this good news with your family. Let the people who supported you through the difficult months know that the treatment is working. You showed up, you were patient, you trusted the process, and your body responded. That is worth celebrating.\n\nYour thyroid condition is not gone — it is managed. And managed well, as your results show. Keep doing what you are doing, keep taking your medication, and keep showing up for your health. You have proven you can do this.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Congratulations — your latest TSH blood test came back in the normal range. This is a significant achievement and one worth pausing to appreciate. If you have been on the thyroid treatment journey for weeks or months, adjusting doses and waiting patiently through blood test after blood test, this result confirms that your persistence has paid off."},{"id":"s2","title":"What this result means","body":"TSH stands for thyroid-stimulating hormone. It is produced by your pituitary gland (a small gland at the base of your brain) and acts as a messenger, telling your thyroid how much hormone to make. When your thyroid is underactive, your pituitary gland sends out more TSH — essentially shouting louder to try to get the thyroid to work harder. A high TSH means your body needs more thyroid hormone than it is getting. When your TSH is in the normal range (typically 0.4 to 4.0 mIU/L, though your doctor may have a narrower target), it means your body is getting the right amount of thyroid hormone from your medication.\\n\\nFor hyperthyroid patients on medication like carbimazole, a normalised TSH means the overproduction has been reined in. Your thyroid is no longer flooding your body with excess hormone.\\n\\nEither way, this result means your treatment is working and your dose is correct. Your cells, organs, and brain are receiving the thyroid hormone they need to function properly."},{"id":"s3","title":"The journey to get here","body":"Reaching this point typically takes three to six months of careful dose adjustments. After your initial diagnosis, your doctor prescribed a starting dose of thyroid medication. Six to eight weeks later, you had a blood test. Perhaps your levels improved but were not quite right, so the dose was adjusted — maybe increased by 25 micrograms. Another six to eight weeks of waiting, another blood test. For some people, it takes two or three adjustments before the dose is dialled in perfectly.\\n\\nDuring this period, you may have experienced fluctuating symptoms. Some weeks you felt almost normal; other weeks the fatigue or anxiety crept back. You kept taking your medication faithfully, kept going to your appointments, kept trusting the process even when it felt slow. That consistency is what got you here, and it deserves recognition.\\n\\nIf you have been paying for blood tests at a private lab — TSH tests cost KES 800 to KES 2,000 per draw — the financial commitment over several months adds up. If you are on SHA, check whether your plan covers the ongoing monitoring. Either way, these tests are an investment in your health that is clearly paying dividends."},{"id":"s4","title":"What you should be feeling now","body":"With your thyroid levels stabilised, you should notice improvements across multiple areas of your life. Your energy levels should be more consistent throughout the day — less of the afternoon crashes and bone-deep exhaustion. Your mood should feel more stable and more like the person you were before thyroid problems began. Brain fog should be lifting, making it easier to concentrate at work, follow conversations, and remember things.\\n\\nPhysical symptoms improve too. If you had hypothyroidism, your weight may begin to normalise (though medication alone does not cause dramatic weight loss — healthy eating with plenty of vegetables, moderate portions of ugali and rice, and lean proteins like fish and chicken will help). Hair loss should slow down and you may notice new growth. Constipation often resolves. Dry skin improves. If you had hyperthyroidism, your heart rate should return to normal, hand tremors should ease, and sleep should become more restful.\\n\\nIf you are not feeling significantly better despite normal TSH levels, tell your doctor. Some patients feel best when their TSH is in the lower half of the normal range rather than the upper half. Others may need their free T3 and free T4 levels checked, as TSH alone does not always tell the complete story."},{"id":"s5","title":"Keeping your levels stable going forward","body":"Reaching normal TSH is the beginning of the maintenance phase, not the end of your treatment. Here is how to keep your levels stable.\\n\\nTake your medication consistently. Same time every morning, on an empty stomach, with plain water. Wait at least 30 minutes before eating breakfast or drinking tea or coffee. If you have uji or chai every morning, take your pill when your alarm goes off and have breakfast after you have dressed and prepared for the day. Consistency in timing is more important than the exact hour — pick a routine that works for you and stick with it every single day.\\n\\nDo not switch brands without discussing it with your doctor. Different brands of levothyroxine can have slightly different absorption rates. If your usual brand is out of stock at your pharmacy, let your doctor know so they can monitor whether your levels remain stable on the new brand. Pharmacies at major hospitals like Nairobi Hospital, MP Shah, and Aga Khan typically stock consistent brands.\\n\\nTell your doctor about any new medications. Calcium supplements, iron tablets, antacids, and some other drugs interfere with thyroid medication absorption. If you start any new medication, your doctor may need to adjust the timing of your levothyroxine or recheck your levels.\\n\\nContinue monitoring. Your doctor will likely schedule your next blood test in three to six months. Once you have been stable for a year or more, annual testing may be sufficient. Do not skip these tests — thyroid needs can change over time due to ageing, pregnancy, menopause, weight changes, or other factors."},{"id":"s6","title":"Celebrate this milestone","body":"In a healthcare journey that often feels like an endless series of appointments and blood draws, a normal TSH result is a concrete, measurable win. Share this good news with your family. Let the people who supported you through the difficult months know that the treatment is working. You showed up, you were patient, you trusted the process, and your body responded. That is worth celebrating.\\n\\nYour thyroid condition is not gone — it is managed. And managed well, as your results show. Keep doing what you are doing, keep taking your medication, and keep showing up for your health. You have proven you can do this."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'wellness-journey'),
  (E'eating-to-prevent-a-second-stroke', E'Eating to prevent a second stroke', E'STROKE', '{"STROKE"}', E'DIETARY', E'After surviving a stroke, your relationship with food takes on new significance. What you eat every day directly affects your blood pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar, and blood clotting — the very factors that caused your stroke and could cause another one.', E'After surviving a stroke, your relationship with food takes on new significance. What you eat every day directly affects your blood pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar, and blood clotting — the very factors that caused your stroke and could cause another one. The good news is that the Kenyan diet, with some thoughtful adjustments, can be powerfully protective. You do not need expensive imported foods or complicated recipes. You need the right choices at the market, in your kitchen, and at the table.\n\nThe salt problem — and it is bigger than you think\n\nHigh salt intake raises blood pressure, which is the single biggest risk factor for stroke. The World Health Organisation recommends less than one teaspoon (5 grams) of salt per day, but many Kenyans consume two to three times that amount without realising it. Salt hides in places you might not expect.\n\nRoyco cubes and powdered seasonings are among the biggest culprits. A single Royco cube contains about 1.4 grams of salt — nearly a third of your entire daily allowance in one tiny cube. If you use two cubes in a pot of stew, that pot already contains more salt than you should eat in a day. Instead, season your food with fresh garlic, ginger, onions, tomatoes, dhania (coriander), and a squeeze of lemon. These add tremendous flavour without the sodium.\n\nProcessed and packaged foods are another hidden source. Sausages, bacon, canned baked beans, instant noodles, and many packaged snacks contain high amounts of salt. Read labels when shopping at Naivas, Carrefour, or your local shop — look for sodium content and aim for products with less than 120 milligrams of sodium per 100 grams.\n\nWhen eating at a restaurant or hotel, ask the kitchen to prepare your food with less salt. Most cooks are willing to accommodate this request. At a nyama choma joint, ask for your meat to be seasoned lightly or with just black pepper and lemon rather than heavy salt rubs.\n\nBuilding a stroke-prevention plate\n\nA simple way to think about every meal is the half-plate rule: fill half your plate with vegetables, a quarter with a starchy food, and a quarter with protein. This works whether you are having lunch at home, eating at a work cafeteria, or ordering at a restaurant.\n\nVegetables: Sukuma wiki (kale), spinach, managu (African nightshade), terere (amaranth leaves), cabbage, carrots, tomatoes, green beans, and peas are all excellent choices. These are rich in potassium, which actually helps lower blood pressure — the opposite of salt. Try to eat at least three different vegetables each day. Buy what is in season at your local market to keep costs low. A bundle of sukuma wiki costs as little as KES 10 to KES 30 at most open-air markets.\n\nWhole grains: Choose brown ugali (made from whole maize flour) over refined white ugali when possible. Githeri — the traditional mix of maize and beans — is an excellent stroke-prevention food because it combines whole grains with plant protein and fibre. Brown rice, sweet potatoes, and arrowroots (nduma) are also good starchy options. Limit refined carbohydrates like white bread, mandazi, and chapati made with white flour, which can spike blood sugar and contribute to the conditions that cause strokes.\n\nProtein: Fish is your best friend after a stroke. Tilapia, Nile perch, omena (silver cyprinid), and sardines are all available in Kenyan markets and contain omega-3 fatty acids that reduce inflammation and help prevent blood clots. Aim to eat fish at least twice a week. When buying at the market, fresh fish should smell like the lake, not fishy. A kilogram of tilapia costs roughly KES 300 to KES 600 depending on your location.\n\nChicken (without the skin) and beans are also excellent protein sources. Limit red meat — beef and goat — to once or twice a week, and when you do eat it, choose lean cuts and trim visible fat. Avoid deep-fried meats and organs (liver, kidney) which are high in cholesterol.\n\nFats: The right kind of fat is not your enemy. Use small amounts of sunflower oil or olive oil for cooking instead of large quantities of cooking fat (lard). Avocado, which is abundant and affordable in Kenya, contains heart-healthy monounsaturated fats. Have half an avocado with your lunch a few times a week. Nuts like groundnuts (peanuts) in small handfuls are also beneficial.\n\nSpecial dietary considerations after stroke\n\nSwallowing difficulties: If your stroke affected your ability to swallow (a condition called dysphagia), eating safely requires careful attention. Foods may need to be pureed, mashed, or thickened. Thin liquids like water and juice can be dangerous because they flow quickly to the back of the throat before you can control them. Ask your doctor for a referral to a speech and language therapist who can assess your swallowing and recommend safe food textures. This is not just about comfort — swallowing problems can lead to food entering your lungs (aspiration), causing pneumonia.\n\nBlood-thinning medication: If you are taking warfarin to prevent blood clots, be aware that foods rich in vitamin K — such as sukuma wiki, spinach, and broccoli — can interact with your medication. This does not mean you should avoid these foods entirely. It means you should eat a consistent amount each week so your doctor can adjust your warfarin dose accordingly. Do not suddenly start eating large amounts of green vegetables or suddenly stop eating them.\n\nMeal planning on a budget\n\nStroke-prevention eating does not have to be expensive. Here is a sample day that costs less than KES 300.\n\nBreakfast: Uji (porridge) made with whole grain millet flour and a sliced banana. Lunch: A plate of githeri with a generous serving of steamed sukuma wiki, seasoned with tomatoes, onions, and garlic instead of salt. Supper: Grilled tilapia fillet with a portion of brown rice and a cabbage and carrot salad dressed with lemon juice.\n\nSmall, consistent changes in your daily eating add up to enormous protection over time. You do not need to overhaul your diet overnight. Start with one change this week — perhaps replacing Royco cubes with fresh spices — and build from there. Your plate is one of the most powerful medicines you have.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"After surviving a stroke, your relationship with food takes on new significance. What you eat every day directly affects your blood pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar, and blood clotting — the very factors that caused your stroke and could cause another one. The good news is that the Kenyan diet, with some thoughtful adjustments, can be powerfully protective. You do not need expensive imported foods or complicated recipes. You need the right choices at the market, in your kitchen, and at the table."},{"id":"s2","title":"The salt problem — and it is bigger than you think","body":"High salt intake raises blood pressure, which is the single biggest risk factor for stroke. The World Health Organisation recommends less than one teaspoon (5 grams) of salt per day, but many Kenyans consume two to three times that amount without realising it. Salt hides in places you might not expect.\\n\\nRoyco cubes and powdered seasonings are among the biggest culprits. A single Royco cube contains about 1.4 grams of salt — nearly a third of your entire daily allowance in one tiny cube. If you use two cubes in a pot of stew, that pot already contains more salt than you should eat in a day. Instead, season your food with fresh garlic, ginger, onions, tomatoes, dhania (coriander), and a squeeze of lemon. These add tremendous flavour without the sodium.\\n\\nProcessed and packaged foods are another hidden source. Sausages, bacon, canned baked beans, instant noodles, and many packaged snacks contain high amounts of salt. Read labels when shopping at Naivas, Carrefour, or your local shop — look for sodium content and aim for products with less than 120 milligrams of sodium per 100 grams.\\n\\nWhen eating at a restaurant or hotel, ask the kitchen to prepare your food with less salt. Most cooks are willing to accommodate this request. At a nyama choma joint, ask for your meat to be seasoned lightly or with just black pepper and lemon rather than heavy salt rubs."},{"id":"s3","title":"Building a stroke-prevention plate","body":"A simple way to think about every meal is the half-plate rule: fill half your plate with vegetables, a quarter with a starchy food, and a quarter with protein. This works whether you are having lunch at home, eating at a work cafeteria, or ordering at a restaurant.\\n\\nVegetables: Sukuma wiki (kale), spinach, managu (African nightshade), terere (amaranth leaves), cabbage, carrots, tomatoes, green beans, and peas are all excellent choices. These are rich in potassium, which actually helps lower blood pressure — the opposite of salt. Try to eat at least three different vegetables each day. Buy what is in season at your local market to keep costs low. A bundle of sukuma wiki costs as little as KES 10 to KES 30 at most open-air markets.\\n\\nWhole grains: Choose brown ugali (made from whole maize flour) over refined white ugali when possible. Githeri — the traditional mix of maize and beans — is an excellent stroke-prevention food because it combines whole grains with plant protein and fibre. Brown rice, sweet potatoes, and arrowroots (nduma) are also good starchy options. Limit refined carbohydrates like white bread, mandazi, and chapati made with white flour, which can spike blood sugar and contribute to the conditions that cause strokes.\\n\\nProtein: Fish is your best friend after a stroke. Tilapia, Nile perch, omena (silver cyprinid), and sardines are all available in Kenyan markets and contain omega-3 fatty acids that reduce inflammation and help prevent blood clots. Aim to eat fish at least twice a week. When buying at the market, fresh fish should smell like the lake, not fishy. A kilogram of tilapia costs roughly KES 300 to KES 600 depending on your location.\\n\\nChicken (without the skin) and beans are also excellent protein sources. Limit red meat — beef and goat — to once or twice a week, and when you do eat it, choose lean cuts and trim visible fat. Avoid deep-fried meats and organs (liver, kidney) which are high in cholesterol.\\n\\nFats: The right kind of fat is not your enemy. Use small amounts of sunflower oil or olive oil for cooking instead of large quantities of cooking fat (lard). Avocado, which is abundant and affordable in Kenya, contains heart-healthy monounsaturated fats. Have half an avocado with your lunch a few times a week. Nuts like groundnuts (peanuts) in small handfuls are also beneficial."},{"id":"s4","title":"Special dietary considerations after stroke","body":"Swallowing difficulties: If your stroke affected your ability to swallow (a condition called dysphagia), eating safely requires careful attention. Foods may need to be pureed, mashed, or thickened. Thin liquids like water and juice can be dangerous because they flow quickly to the back of the throat before you can control them. Ask your doctor for a referral to a speech and language therapist who can assess your swallowing and recommend safe food textures. This is not just about comfort — swallowing problems can lead to food entering your lungs (aspiration), causing pneumonia.\\n\\nBlood-thinning medication: If you are taking warfarin to prevent blood clots, be aware that foods rich in vitamin K — such as sukuma wiki, spinach, and broccoli — can interact with your medication. This does not mean you should avoid these foods entirely. It means you should eat a consistent amount each week so your doctor can adjust your warfarin dose accordingly. Do not suddenly start eating large amounts of green vegetables or suddenly stop eating them."},{"id":"s5","title":"Meal planning on a budget","body":"Stroke-prevention eating does not have to be expensive. Here is a sample day that costs less than KES 300.\\n\\nBreakfast: Uji (porridge) made with whole grain millet flour and a sliced banana. Lunch: A plate of githeri with a generous serving of steamed sukuma wiki, seasoned with tomatoes, onions, and garlic instead of salt. Supper: Grilled tilapia fillet with a portion of brown rice and a cabbage and carrot salad dressed with lemon juice.\\n\\nSmall, consistent changes in your daily eating add up to enormous protection over time. You do not need to overhaul your diet overnight. Start with one change this week — perhaps replacing Royco cubes with fresh spices — and build from there. Your plate is one of the most powerful medicines you have."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'healthy-cooking'),
  (E'recovery-exercises-you-can-do-at-home', E'Recovery exercises you can do at home', E'STROKE', '{"STROKE"}', E'EXERCISE', E'Stroke recovery does not end when you leave the hospital. In many ways, the most important phase of your rehabilitation happens at home, in the weeks and months after discharge.', E'Stroke recovery does not end when you leave the hospital. In many ways, the most important phase of your rehabilitation happens at home, in the weeks and months after discharge. The exercises your physiotherapist showed you during your hospital stay are not optional extras — they are the mechanism by which your brain rewires itself, finding new pathways around the damaged area to restore lost functions. Consistent daily practice is what makes the difference between recovering abilities and losing them permanently.\n\nHow exercise heals the brain after stroke\n\nWhen a stroke damages part of your brain, the nerve cells in that area die. But the human brain has a remarkable ability called neuroplasticity — it can reorganise itself, with healthy areas learning to take over functions that the damaged area used to control. This rewiring does not happen automatically. It is driven by repetition. Every time you attempt to move your affected hand, lift your weakened leg, or practice saying a difficult word, you are sending signals through your brain that encourage new neural connections to form. The more repetitions, the stronger those connections become.\n\nThis is why daily exercise is so critical, even when progress feels painfully slow. Each attempt matters, whether or not you can see immediate improvement.\n\nUpper body exercises you can do at home\n\nThese exercises target the arm and hand, which are commonly affected by stroke. Do them while sitting in a sturdy chair at your dining table. Perform each exercise 10 to 15 repetitions, twice a day — once in the morning and once in the evening.\n\nBall squeezes: Get a soft ball or a rolled-up pair of socks. Place it in your affected hand and try to squeeze it. If you cannot squeeze yet, use your other hand to wrap the affected fingers around the ball and hold for five seconds. Even the attempt to squeeze sends important signals to your brain. Do 10 to 15 squeezes.\n\nTable slides: Place your affected arm on a smooth table. Using your unaffected hand to help if needed, slowly slide the affected arm forward as far as it will go, then slide it back. This encourages shoulder and elbow movement. Do 10 to 15 slides.\n\nWrist rotations: Rest your affected forearm on the table with your hand hanging over the edge. Slowly try to rotate your wrist — turning your palm up, then down. If you cannot do this independently, use your other hand to gently assist the movement. Do 10 rotations in each direction.\n\nFinger extensions: Place your affected hand flat on the table. Try to spread your fingers apart, then bring them back together. If your fingers do not move yet, use your other hand to gently spread them and hold for five seconds. Repeat 10 times.\n\nLower body exercises\n\nThese exercises can be done sitting or standing, depending on your current ability. Always have a family member nearby when doing standing exercises, especially in the early weeks.\n\nSeated leg lifts: Sit upright in a chair with your feet flat on the floor. Slowly lift your affected leg, straightening the knee as much as possible. Hold for three seconds, then lower slowly. If you cannot straighten the knee fully, lift as high as you can. Do 10 to 15 repetitions.\n\nSeated marching: Sit upright and alternately lift your knees as if marching in place. Lift each knee 10 to 15 times. This strengthens hip flexors and improves coordination between your legs.\n\nStanding balance practice: Stand behind a sturdy chair, holding the back for support. Slowly shift your weight onto your affected leg, holding for five to ten seconds, then shift back. As your balance improves over weeks, try briefly lifting your unaffected foot off the ground. Always have someone nearby when you practice this.\n\nHeel raises: While standing and holding the chair back, rise onto your toes and hold for two seconds, then lower. Do 10 to 15 repetitions. This strengthens calf muscles and improves ankle stability, which is important for walking.\n\nSpeech and communication exercises\n\nIf your stroke affected your speech, practice is equally important. Read a paragraph from the newspaper (Daily Nation or The Standard) aloud each day, slowly and deliberately. If reading is too difficult, start with individual words — the names of family members, foods you eat, places you know. Sing along to familiar songs — music uses a different part of the brain than speech, and singing can sometimes help unlock words that are difficult to say in conversation. Practice the sounds that are hardest for you, repeating them 10 to 20 times.\n\nCreating a daily rehabilitation routine\n\nConsistency matters more than duration. Here is a sample daily schedule that takes about 30 to 40 minutes total.\n\nMorning session, 15 to 20 minutes: Start with five minutes of deep breathing and gentle stretching. Then do your upper body exercises — ball squeezes, table slides, wrist rotations, and finger extensions, 10 repetitions each. Finish with five minutes of speech practice if needed.\n\nEvening session, 15 to 20 minutes: Start with seated marching for two minutes to warm up. Do your lower body exercises — seated leg lifts and standing balance practice. If you are able, take a short walk around your house or compound. Finish with gentle stretching.\n\nSafety precautions\n\nNever exercise alone in the early months of recovery, especially standing exercises. Falls are a serious risk after stroke, and a fall can cause injuries that set your recovery back significantly. Have a family member or caregiver present, or at minimum, exercise near a wall or sturdy furniture you can grab.\n\nStop exercising immediately if you experience chest pain, severe dizziness, sudden weakness, or headache. These could indicate a new medical problem that needs urgent attention.\n\nStay hydrated — keep a water bottle within reach during exercises. If you take blood pressure medication, check your pressure before exercising if possible. A home blood pressure monitor costs KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 at pharmacies across Kenya and is a worthwhile investment for stroke survivors.\n\nProgress looks different for everyone\n\nSome people recover significant function within weeks. For others, meaningful improvement takes months or even years. The pace of recovery depends on the size and location of the stroke, your age, your overall health, and how consistently you practice. Do not compare your progress to anyone else''s. What matters is that you are doing the work, every day, building those new brain connections one repetition at a time.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Stroke recovery does not end when you leave the hospital. In many ways, the most important phase of your rehabilitation happens at home, in the weeks and months after discharge. The exercises your physiotherapist showed you during your hospital stay are not optional extras — they are the mechanism by which your brain rewires itself, finding new pathways around the damaged area to restore lost functions. Consistent daily practice is what makes the difference between recovering abilities and losing them permanently."},{"id":"s2","title":"How exercise heals the brain after stroke","body":"When a stroke damages part of your brain, the nerve cells in that area die. But the human brain has a remarkable ability called neuroplasticity — it can reorganise itself, with healthy areas learning to take over functions that the damaged area used to control. This rewiring does not happen automatically. It is driven by repetition. Every time you attempt to move your affected hand, lift your weakened leg, or practice saying a difficult word, you are sending signals through your brain that encourage new neural connections to form. The more repetitions, the stronger those connections become.\\n\\nThis is why daily exercise is so critical, even when progress feels painfully slow. Each attempt matters, whether or not you can see immediate improvement."},{"id":"s3","title":"Upper body exercises you can do at home","body":"These exercises target the arm and hand, which are commonly affected by stroke. Do them while sitting in a sturdy chair at your dining table. Perform each exercise 10 to 15 repetitions, twice a day — once in the morning and once in the evening.\\n\\nBall squeezes: Get a soft ball or a rolled-up pair of socks. Place it in your affected hand and try to squeeze it. If you cannot squeeze yet, use your other hand to wrap the affected fingers around the ball and hold for five seconds. Even the attempt to squeeze sends important signals to your brain. Do 10 to 15 squeezes.\\n\\nTable slides: Place your affected arm on a smooth table. Using your unaffected hand to help if needed, slowly slide the affected arm forward as far as it will go, then slide it back. This encourages shoulder and elbow movement. Do 10 to 15 slides.\\n\\nWrist rotations: Rest your affected forearm on the table with your hand hanging over the edge. Slowly try to rotate your wrist — turning your palm up, then down. If you cannot do this independently, use your other hand to gently assist the movement. Do 10 rotations in each direction.\\n\\nFinger extensions: Place your affected hand flat on the table. Try to spread your fingers apart, then bring them back together. If your fingers do not move yet, use your other hand to gently spread them and hold for five seconds. Repeat 10 times."},{"id":"s4","title":"Lower body exercises","body":"These exercises can be done sitting or standing, depending on your current ability. Always have a family member nearby when doing standing exercises, especially in the early weeks.\\n\\nSeated leg lifts: Sit upright in a chair with your feet flat on the floor. Slowly lift your affected leg, straightening the knee as much as possible. Hold for three seconds, then lower slowly. If you cannot straighten the knee fully, lift as high as you can. Do 10 to 15 repetitions.\\n\\nSeated marching: Sit upright and alternately lift your knees as if marching in place. Lift each knee 10 to 15 times. This strengthens hip flexors and improves coordination between your legs.\\n\\nStanding balance practice: Stand behind a sturdy chair, holding the back for support. Slowly shift your weight onto your affected leg, holding for five to ten seconds, then shift back. As your balance improves over weeks, try briefly lifting your unaffected foot off the ground. Always have someone nearby when you practice this.\\n\\nHeel raises: While standing and holding the chair back, rise onto your toes and hold for two seconds, then lower. Do 10 to 15 repetitions. This strengthens calf muscles and improves ankle stability, which is important for walking."},{"id":"s5","title":"Speech and communication exercises","body":"If your stroke affected your speech, practice is equally important. Read a paragraph from the newspaper (Daily Nation or The Standard) aloud each day, slowly and deliberately. If reading is too difficult, start with individual words — the names of family members, foods you eat, places you know. Sing along to familiar songs — music uses a different part of the brain than speech, and singing can sometimes help unlock words that are difficult to say in conversation. Practice the sounds that are hardest for you, repeating them 10 to 20 times."},{"id":"s6","title":"Creating a daily rehabilitation routine","body":"Consistency matters more than duration. Here is a sample daily schedule that takes about 30 to 40 minutes total.\\n\\nMorning session, 15 to 20 minutes: Start with five minutes of deep breathing and gentle stretching. Then do your upper body exercises — ball squeezes, table slides, wrist rotations, and finger extensions, 10 repetitions each. Finish with five minutes of speech practice if needed.\\n\\nEvening session, 15 to 20 minutes: Start with seated marching for two minutes to warm up. Do your lower body exercises — seated leg lifts and standing balance practice. If you are able, take a short walk around your house or compound. Finish with gentle stretching."},{"id":"s7","title":"Safety precautions","body":"Never exercise alone in the early months of recovery, especially standing exercises. Falls are a serious risk after stroke, and a fall can cause injuries that set your recovery back significantly. Have a family member or caregiver present, or at minimum, exercise near a wall or sturdy furniture you can grab.\\n\\nStop exercising immediately if you experience chest pain, severe dizziness, sudden weakness, or headache. These could indicate a new medical problem that needs urgent attention.\\n\\nStay hydrated — keep a water bottle within reach during exercises. If you take blood pressure medication, check your pressure before exercising if possible. A home blood pressure monitor costs KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 at pharmacies across Kenya and is a worthwhile investment for stroke survivors."},{"id":"s8","title":"Progress looks different for everyone","body":"Some people recover significant function within weeks. For others, meaningful improvement takes months or even years. The pace of recovery depends on the size and location of the stroke, your age, your overall health, and how consistently you practice. Do not compare your progress to anyone else''s. What matters is that you are doing the work, every day, building those new brain connections one repetition at a time."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'light-exercise'),
  (E'myth-strokes-only-happen-to-the-elderly', E'Myth: Strokes only happen to the elderly', E'STROKE', '{"STROKE"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The myth: Strokes are a disease of old age. They happen to grandparents, not to young, working-age adults. In this lesson, you will separate medical facts from common misconceptions.', E'The myth: Strokes are a disease of old age. They happen to grandparents, not to young, working-age adults. If you are in your twenties, thirties, or forties, you do not need to worry about stroke.\n\nThe truth: This myth is dangerously wrong, and it is costing young Kenyans their lives and independence\n\nStroke rates among people under 50 are rising across Kenya and sub-Saharan Africa. This is not a distant statistical trend — it is happening in families, workplaces, and communities right now. Young professionals, students, parents of young children, and even teenagers are being affected. The belief that stroke is only an old person''s disease causes young people to ignore warning signs, delay seeking emergency treatment, and neglect the risk factors they could be managing today.\n\nWhy strokes are increasing in younger Kenyans\n\nSeveral factors are driving this trend, and most of them are related to how modern Kenyan lifestyles have changed over the past two decades.\n\nHypertension (high blood pressure) is the number one risk factor for stroke at any age, and it is shockingly common among young Kenyans. Many people in their twenties and thirties have never had their blood pressure checked. They feel healthy, so they assume they are healthy. But hypertension is called the "silent killer" because it rarely causes symptoms until it triggers a catastrophic event — like a stroke. A blood pressure check takes two minutes and costs nothing at most public health facilities. If you are over 18, you should know your numbers.\n\nDiabetes is another major risk factor, and type 2 diabetes is increasingly diagnosed in younger adults due to changes in diet and physical activity levels. The shift from traditional foods like githeri, sweet potatoes, and leafy vegetables to processed foods, sugary drinks, and refined carbohydrates has created an epidemic of metabolic disease that sets the stage for stroke.\n\nObesity has increased significantly in urban Kenya. Sedentary jobs, reliance on matatus and boda bodas instead of walking, and easy access to high-calorie foods contribute to weight gain that increases stroke risk.\n\nSmoking and alcohol use are well-established stroke risk factors that disproportionately affect younger adults. Heavy drinking, including binge drinking culture, damages blood vessels and raises blood pressure. Smoking, including shisha, accelerates atherosclerosis — the hardening and narrowing of arteries — at any age.\n\nStress is an underappreciated risk factor. The financial pressures of modern Kenyan life — rent, school fees, family obligations, job insecurity — create chronic stress that raises blood pressure and contributes to unhealthy coping behaviours like overeating, drinking, or smoking.\n\nUse of hormonal contraceptives combined with smoking significantly increases stroke risk in young women. If you smoke and use birth control pills, discuss this risk with your healthcare provider.\n\nWhy the age myth is dangerous\n\nWhen a 35-year-old experiences sudden weakness on one side of their body, or their speech becomes slurred, or they develop a severe headache unlike any they have had before, the last thing they think is "stroke." They think it is stress, exhaustion, a migraine, or malaria. Their family thinks the same. Even some healthcare workers may not immediately consider stroke in a young patient, leading to delayed diagnosis.\n\nThis delay is devastating because stroke treatment is extremely time-sensitive. The most effective treatment for the most common type of stroke (ischaemic stroke, caused by a blood clot) is a clot-busting medication that must be given within four and a half hours of symptom onset. Every minute of delay, an estimated 1.9 million brain cells die. The difference between receiving treatment within one hour versus five hours can be the difference between walking out of the hospital and being permanently disabled.\n\nKnow the signs — regardless of your age\n\nThe FAST method helps you recognise stroke quickly.\n\nFace: Ask the person to smile. Does one side of their face droop? Arm: Ask them to raise both arms. Does one arm drift downward? Speech: Ask them to repeat a simple sentence. Is their speech slurred or strange? Time: If you observe any of these signs, time is critical. Get to the nearest hospital immediately.\n\nAdditional warning signs include sudden severe headache with no known cause, sudden trouble seeing in one or both eyes, sudden confusion or difficulty understanding others, sudden trouble walking, dizziness, or loss of balance.\n\nDo not drive to a far-away hospital hoping it has better facilities. Go to the nearest hospital or health centre and let them stabilise and refer if necessary. Every minute counts.\n\nProtecting yourself now, whatever your age\n\nKnow your blood pressure. Get checked at your local health facility, pharmacy, or during health campaigns. A normal reading is below 120/80 mmHg. If yours is consistently above 140/90, you need treatment. A home blood pressure monitor costs KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 and is a worthwhile investment.\n\nKnow your blood sugar. A fasting blood glucose test costs KES 200 to KES 500. If you have a family history of diabetes, get tested annually starting in your twenties.\n\nEat a diet rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains, and fish. Reduce salt, processed foods, and fried snacks. Exercise for at least 30 minutes, five days a week — even brisk walking counts.\n\nIf you smoke, stop. If you drink heavily, reduce. If you are stressed, find healthier coping mechanisms — exercise, talking to friends, prayer, or professional counselling.\n\nStroke does not check your ID before it strikes. Protecting yourself starts today, not when you turn 60.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The myth: Strokes are a disease of old age. They happen to grandparents, not to young, working-age adults. If you are in your twenties, thirties, or forties, you do not need to worry about stroke.\\n\\nThe truth: This myth is dangerously wrong, and it is costing young Kenyans their lives and independence\\n\\nStroke rates among people under 50 are rising across Kenya and sub-Saharan Africa. This is not a distant statistical trend — it is happening in families, workplaces, and communities right now. Young professionals, students, parents of young children, and even teenagers are being affected. The belief that stroke is only an old person''s disease causes young people to ignore warning signs, delay seeking emergency treatment, and neglect the risk factors they could be managing today."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why strokes are increasing in younger Kenyans","body":"Several factors are driving this trend, and most of them are related to how modern Kenyan lifestyles have changed over the past two decades.\\n\\nHypertension (high blood pressure) is the number one risk factor for stroke at any age, and it is shockingly common among young Kenyans. Many people in their twenties and thirties have never had their blood pressure checked. They feel healthy, so they assume they are healthy. But hypertension is called the \\"silent killer\\" because it rarely causes symptoms until it triggers a catastrophic event — like a stroke. A blood pressure check takes two minutes and costs nothing at most public health facilities. If you are over 18, you should know your numbers.\\n\\nDiabetes is another major risk factor, and type 2 diabetes is increasingly diagnosed in younger adults due to changes in diet and physical activity levels. The shift from traditional foods like githeri, sweet potatoes, and leafy vegetables to processed foods, sugary drinks, and refined carbohydrates has created an epidemic of metabolic disease that sets the stage for stroke.\\n\\nObesity has increased significantly in urban Kenya. Sedentary jobs, reliance on matatus and boda bodas instead of walking, and easy access to high-calorie foods contribute to weight gain that increases stroke risk.\\n\\nSmoking and alcohol use are well-established stroke risk factors that disproportionately affect younger adults. Heavy drinking, including binge drinking culture, damages blood vessels and raises blood pressure. Smoking, including shisha, accelerates atherosclerosis — the hardening and narrowing of arteries — at any age.\\n\\nStress is an underappreciated risk factor. The financial pressures of modern Kenyan life — rent, school fees, family obligations, job insecurity — create chronic stress that raises blood pressure and contributes to unhealthy coping behaviours like overeating, drinking, or smoking.\\n\\nUse of hormonal contraceptives combined with smoking significantly increases stroke risk in young women. If you smoke and use birth control pills, discuss this risk with your healthcare provider."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why the age myth is dangerous","body":"When a 35-year-old experiences sudden weakness on one side of their body, or their speech becomes slurred, or they develop a severe headache unlike any they have had before, the last thing they think is \\"stroke.\\" They think it is stress, exhaustion, a migraine, or malaria. Their family thinks the same. Even some healthcare workers may not immediately consider stroke in a young patient, leading to delayed diagnosis.\\n\\nThis delay is devastating because stroke treatment is extremely time-sensitive. The most effective treatment for the most common type of stroke (ischaemic stroke, caused by a blood clot) is a clot-busting medication that must be given within four and a half hours of symptom onset. Every minute of delay, an estimated 1.9 million brain cells die. The difference between receiving treatment within one hour versus five hours can be the difference between walking out of the hospital and being permanently disabled."},{"id":"s4","title":"Know the signs — regardless of your age","body":"The FAST method helps you recognise stroke quickly.\\n\\nFace: Ask the person to smile. Does one side of their face droop? Arm: Ask them to raise both arms. Does one arm drift downward? Speech: Ask them to repeat a simple sentence. Is their speech slurred or strange? Time: If you observe any of these signs, time is critical. Get to the nearest hospital immediately.\\n\\nAdditional warning signs include sudden severe headache with no known cause, sudden trouble seeing in one or both eyes, sudden confusion or difficulty understanding others, sudden trouble walking, dizziness, or loss of balance.\\n\\nDo not drive to a far-away hospital hoping it has better facilities. Go to the nearest hospital or health centre and let them stabilise and refer if necessary. Every minute counts."},{"id":"s5","title":"Protecting yourself now, whatever your age","body":"Know your blood pressure. Get checked at your local health facility, pharmacy, or during health campaigns. A normal reading is below 120/80 mmHg. If yours is consistently above 140/90, you need treatment. A home blood pressure monitor costs KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 and is a worthwhile investment.\\n\\nKnow your blood sugar. A fasting blood glucose test costs KES 200 to KES 500. If you have a family history of diabetes, get tested annually starting in your twenties.\\n\\nEat a diet rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains, and fish. Reduce salt, processed foods, and fried snacks. Exercise for at least 30 minutes, five days a week — even brisk walking counts.\\n\\nIf you smoke, stop. If you drink heavily, reduce. If you are stressed, find healthier coping mechanisms — exercise, talking to friends, prayer, or professional counselling.\\n\\nStroke does not check your ID before it strikes. Protecting yourself starts today, not when you turn 60."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'doctor-consultation'),
  (E'grieving-the-person-you-were-before-the-stroke', E'Grieving the person you were before the stroke', E'STROKE', '{"STROKE"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'After a stroke, there is a grief that people around you may not fully understand. It is not grief for a person who has died — it is grief for the person you used to be.', E'After a stroke, there is a grief that people around you may not fully understand. It is not grief for a person who has died — it is grief for the person you used to be. The you who could walk without thinking about it, who could button a shirt with both hands, who could speak fluently, drive to work, carry your child, cook a meal without help. That person feels like they vanished overnight, and the person who remains feels unfamiliar and diminished.\n\nThis grief is real, it is valid, and it needs to be acknowledged — not pushed aside, not covered with forced positivity, and not dismissed by well-meaning people who tell you to "just be grateful you survived."\n\nThe losses you are mourning\n\nStroke takes different things from different people, and every loss matters. Perhaps you lost mobility on one side of your body. Simple things — tying your shoes, cutting your food, opening a jar, climbing the steps to your front door — now require enormous effort or assistance. Perhaps your speech was affected, and words that used to flow easily now get stuck. You know what you want to say, but it comes out jumbled or unclear, and you see the confusion or pity in people''s eyes. Perhaps your memory or thinking was affected, and tasks at work that you used to handle effortlessly now feel overwhelming.\n\nBeyond the physical losses, there are social and economic ones. You may have lost your job or your ability to work in your previous role. In Kenya, where many families depend on one or two breadwinners, this financial impact creates enormous stress. You may have lost your independence — suddenly needing help with bathing, dressing, or going to the toilet. For adults who have been self-sufficient their entire lives, this loss of dignity can feel unbearable.\n\nRelationships change too. Your spouse or partner becomes a caregiver, which shifts the dynamic of your relationship in ways neither of you expected. Friends may visit less frequently over time, unsure of what to say or how to help. You may feel isolated in your own home.\n\nThe stages of grief after stroke\n\nThe grief you experience after a stroke often follows recognisable stages, though they do not always come in order and you may cycle through them repeatedly.\n\nShock and denial may come first. In the early days and weeks, you might think, "This cannot be permanent. I will wake up tomorrow and everything will be back to normal." This is a natural protective mechanism.\n\nAnger often follows. Why did this happen to you? Why did your body betray you? You may feel angry at your doctors, at God, at your own body, at family members who seem to carry on with life while yours has stopped. You may feel angry at the healthy people around you who take their abilities for granted.\n\nBargaining sounds like, "If I just do all my exercises perfectly, I will recover completely." While exercise is essential for recovery, bargaining often sets unrealistic expectations that lead to deeper disappointment.\n\nDepression is extremely common after stroke — studies suggest that one in three stroke survivors experiences significant depression. This is caused by both the emotional weight of your losses and by actual changes in brain chemistry from the stroke itself. Depression after stroke is a medical condition, not a character weakness, and it deserves treatment.\n\nAcceptance does not mean being happy about what happened. It means reaching a place where you can acknowledge your new reality and begin building a life within it — a life that has meaning, purpose, and moments of joy, even if it looks different from what you planned.\n\nWhat to do with this grief\n\nAllow yourself to feel it. Kenyan culture often values stoicism, especially in men. You may hear, "Be strong for your family," or "God does not give you more than you can handle." While these words come from a place of love, they can make you feel that your pain is not allowed. Your grief is allowed. Crying is allowed. Being angry is allowed. Feeling scared about the future is allowed.\n\nTalk to someone who listens without fixing. This might be a counsellor, a pastor who understands chronic illness, a fellow stroke survivor, or a family member who can simply sit with your pain without trying to make it better with motivational quotes. If professional counselling is available to you — many county hospitals now have mental health units, and private counsellors charge between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 per session — consider it seriously. Depression after stroke responds well to treatment.\n\nSet small, meaningful daily goals. Instead of focusing on everything you have lost, focus on one thing you can do today. It might be as small as brushing your teeth with your affected hand, walking to the gate and back, or saying a complete sentence clearly. These small victories are not trivial — they are the building blocks of your new life.\n\nConnect with other stroke survivors. Ask your doctor, physiotherapist, or local hospital whether there are stroke support groups in your area. Hearing from someone who has walked this path before you — who understands the frustration, the slow progress, the good days and bad days — can be profoundly comforting. If in-person groups are not available, search for online communities of Kenyan or African stroke survivors.\n\nBe patient with your family. Your loved ones are grieving too — grieving the person you were before the stroke, adjusting to new caregiving roles, and often dealing with financial stress. They may say the wrong thing, push you too hard, or seem insensitive. Most of the time, they are doing their best with a situation none of you were prepared for. When you can, tell them what you need: "I need you to sit with me quietly," or "I need you to let me try this myself, even if it takes longer."\n\nRecovery is not about going back\n\nHere is a truth that is hard to hear but important to understand: stroke recovery is not about returning to the person you were before. It is about discovering who you are becoming. Some abilities may return fully. Others may return partially. Some may not return at all. But within your new reality, there is still a full life to be lived — with purpose, with relationships, with moments that make you laugh, with contributions that matter.\n\nMany stroke survivors eventually describe reaching a place where they would not trade their experience, because it taught them what truly matters. You may not be there yet, and that is perfectly fine. Right now, your job is simply to keep going — one day, one exercise, one small victory at a time.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"After a stroke, there is a grief that people around you may not fully understand. It is not grief for a person who has died — it is grief for the person you used to be. The you who could walk without thinking about it, who could button a shirt with both hands, who could speak fluently, drive to work, carry your child, cook a meal without help. That person feels like they vanished overnight, and the person who remains feels unfamiliar and diminished.\\n\\nThis grief is real, it is valid, and it needs to be acknowledged — not pushed aside, not covered with forced positivity, and not dismissed by well-meaning people who tell you to \\"just be grateful you survived.\\""},{"id":"s2","title":"The losses you are mourning","body":"Stroke takes different things from different people, and every loss matters. Perhaps you lost mobility on one side of your body. Simple things — tying your shoes, cutting your food, opening a jar, climbing the steps to your front door — now require enormous effort or assistance. Perhaps your speech was affected, and words that used to flow easily now get stuck. You know what you want to say, but it comes out jumbled or unclear, and you see the confusion or pity in people''s eyes. Perhaps your memory or thinking was affected, and tasks at work that you used to handle effortlessly now feel overwhelming.\\n\\nBeyond the physical losses, there are social and economic ones. You may have lost your job or your ability to work in your previous role. In Kenya, where many families depend on one or two breadwinners, this financial impact creates enormous stress. You may have lost your independence — suddenly needing help with bathing, dressing, or going to the toilet. For adults who have been self-sufficient their entire lives, this loss of dignity can feel unbearable.\\n\\nRelationships change too. Your spouse or partner becomes a caregiver, which shifts the dynamic of your relationship in ways neither of you expected. Friends may visit less frequently over time, unsure of what to say or how to help. You may feel isolated in your own home."},{"id":"s3","title":"The stages of grief after stroke","body":"The grief you experience after a stroke often follows recognisable stages, though they do not always come in order and you may cycle through them repeatedly.\\n\\nShock and denial may come first. In the early days and weeks, you might think, \\"This cannot be permanent. I will wake up tomorrow and everything will be back to normal.\\" This is a natural protective mechanism.\\n\\nAnger often follows. Why did this happen to you? Why did your body betray you? You may feel angry at your doctors, at God, at your own body, at family members who seem to carry on with life while yours has stopped. You may feel angry at the healthy people around you who take their abilities for granted.\\n\\nBargaining sounds like, \\"If I just do all my exercises perfectly, I will recover completely.\\" While exercise is essential for recovery, bargaining often sets unrealistic expectations that lead to deeper disappointment.\\n\\nDepression is extremely common after stroke — studies suggest that one in three stroke survivors experiences significant depression. This is caused by both the emotional weight of your losses and by actual changes in brain chemistry from the stroke itself. Depression after stroke is a medical condition, not a character weakness, and it deserves treatment.\\n\\nAcceptance does not mean being happy about what happened. It means reaching a place where you can acknowledge your new reality and begin building a life within it — a life that has meaning, purpose, and moments of joy, even if it looks different from what you planned."},{"id":"s4","title":"What to do with this grief","body":"Allow yourself to feel it. Kenyan culture often values stoicism, especially in men. You may hear, \\"Be strong for your family,\\" or \\"God does not give you more than you can handle.\\" While these words come from a place of love, they can make you feel that your pain is not allowed. Your grief is allowed. Crying is allowed. Being angry is allowed. Feeling scared about the future is allowed.\\n\\nTalk to someone who listens without fixing. This might be a counsellor, a pastor who understands chronic illness, a fellow stroke survivor, or a family member who can simply sit with your pain without trying to make it better with motivational quotes. If professional counselling is available to you — many county hospitals now have mental health units, and private counsellors charge between KES 2,000 and KES 5,000 per session — consider it seriously. Depression after stroke responds well to treatment.\\n\\nSet small, meaningful daily goals. Instead of focusing on everything you have lost, focus on one thing you can do today. It might be as small as brushing your teeth with your affected hand, walking to the gate and back, or saying a complete sentence clearly. These small victories are not trivial — they are the building blocks of your new life.\\n\\nConnect with other stroke survivors. Ask your doctor, physiotherapist, or local hospital whether there are stroke support groups in your area. Hearing from someone who has walked this path before you — who understands the frustration, the slow progress, the good days and bad days — can be profoundly comforting. If in-person groups are not available, search for online communities of Kenyan or African stroke survivors.\\n\\nBe patient with your family. Your loved ones are grieving too — grieving the person you were before the stroke, adjusting to new caregiving roles, and often dealing with financial stress. They may say the wrong thing, push you too hard, or seem insensitive. Most of the time, they are doing their best with a situation none of you were prepared for. When you can, tell them what you need: \\"I need you to sit with me quietly,\\" or \\"I need you to let me try this myself, even if it takes longer.\\""},{"id":"s5","title":"Recovery is not about going back","body":"Here is a truth that is hard to hear but important to understand: stroke recovery is not about returning to the person you were before. It is about discovering who you are becoming. Some abilities may return fully. Others may return partially. Some may not return at all. But within your new reality, there is still a full life to be lived — with purpose, with relationships, with moments that make you laugh, with contributions that matter.\\n\\nMany stroke survivors eventually describe reaching a place where they would not trade their experience, because it taught them what truly matters. You may not be there yet, and that is perfectly fine. Right now, your job is simply to keep going — one day, one exercise, one small victory at a time."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'peaceful-moment'),
  (E'recognising-mini-strokes-tias-that-warn-of-bigger-ones', E'Recognising mini-strokes (TIAs) that warn of bigger ones', E'STROKE', '{"STROKE"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'If you have had a stroke, or if you have risk factors for stroke such as high blood pressure, diabetes, or heart disease, understanding transient ischaemic attacks — commonly called TIAs or mini-strokes — could save your life.', E'If you have had a stroke, or if you have risk factors for stroke such as high blood pressure, diabetes, or heart disease, understanding transient ischaemic attacks — commonly called TIAs or mini-strokes — could save your life. A TIA is your body''s most urgent warning that a major stroke is coming, and recognising it gives you a critical window to prevent disaster.\n\nWhat is a TIA\n\nA transient ischaemic attack happens when blood flow to part of your brain is temporarily blocked, usually by a small blood clot. The key word is "temporarily" — unlike a full stroke, the blockage clears on its own, usually within minutes to hours, and symptoms resolve completely. Because the symptoms go away, many people dismiss a TIA as nothing serious. This is a potentially fatal mistake.\n\nA TIA is not a stroke that did not happen. It is a stroke that is about to happen. Research shows that up to 20 percent of people who experience a TIA will have a full, major stroke within 90 days. The risk is highest in the first 48 hours after a TIA. This means that a TIA is a medical emergency that demands immediate hospital evaluation, even though you may feel completely fine by the time you arrive.\n\nRecognising TIA symptoms\n\nTIA symptoms are identical to stroke symptoms. The only difference is that TIA symptoms resolve on their own, while stroke symptoms persist. Here is what to watch for.\n\nFace drooping: One side of the face suddenly droops or feels numb. Ask the person to smile — if the smile is uneven, this is a warning sign.\n\nArm weakness: One arm suddenly becomes weak or numb. Ask the person to raise both arms — if one drifts downward, this is a warning sign.\n\nSpeech difficulty: Speech suddenly becomes slurred, garbled, or hard to understand. The person may also have difficulty understanding what you are saying to them.\n\nVision changes: Sudden loss of vision in one or both eyes, or sudden double vision. Some people describe a curtain coming down over one eye.\n\nSevere headache: A sudden, severe headache unlike any you have experienced before, sometimes described as "the worst headache of my life."\n\nBalance problems: Sudden dizziness, loss of balance, or difficulty walking. The person may stumble or be unable to stand steadily.\n\nConfusion: Sudden difficulty thinking clearly or understanding a situation that would normally be straightforward.\n\nThese symptoms typically last anywhere from a few minutes to an hour. In some cases, they can last up to 24 hours. Regardless of how quickly they resolve, they require emergency medical evaluation.\n\nWhy people ignore TIAs\n\nThe very nature of a TIA — symptoms that appear suddenly and then vanish — makes it easy to dismiss. Here are common reactions that lead to dangerous delays.\n\n"It was probably just fatigue or stress." When your arm goes weak for ten minutes and then returns to normal, it is tempting to chalk it up to sleeping in a wrong position or being overworked. But normal fatigue does not cause sudden one-sided weakness.\n\n"I feel fine now, so it cannot have been serious." The fact that you feel fine is actually what makes a TIA so dangerous. You feel fine, so you do not seek help, and then the major stroke hits days later.\n\n"I do not want to waste the doctor''s time." This thought has cost lives. No doctor will ever consider a possible TIA to be a waste of their time. They would far rather see you for a false alarm than treat you for a major stroke that could have been prevented.\n\n"Hospital is too expensive or too far." This is a real barrier for many Kenyans. But consider the cost comparison: emergency evaluation for a TIA might cost KES 5,000 to KES 20,000 depending on the tests and facility. Treatment and rehabilitation after a major stroke can cost hundreds of thousands of shillings, plus the ongoing cost of disability, lost income, and full-time caregiving.\n\nWhat to do if you suspect a TIA\n\nAct immediately, even if symptoms have already resolved. Use the FAST method to confirm your suspicion: Face drooping, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, Time to act.\n\nGet to the nearest hospital emergency department. Do not wait to see if the symptoms come back. Do not sleep on it and plan to see a doctor in the morning. Do not take an aspirin and assume you have handled it. Go to the hospital now.\n\nAt the hospital, doctors will typically perform several tests. A CT or MRI scan of your brain looks for evidence of stroke. Blood tests check your cholesterol, blood sugar, and clotting factors. An ECG (heart tracing) checks for irregular heart rhythms like atrial fibrillation, which is a major cause of blood clots that travel to the brain. An ultrasound of the carotid arteries in your neck checks for dangerous narrowing caused by plaque buildup.\n\nBased on these results, your doctor will start preventive treatment immediately. This often includes blood-thinning medication (aspirin or clopidogrel), blood pressure medication if yours is elevated, cholesterol-lowering medication (statins), and management of any other risk factors identified.\n\nMonitoring yourself at home\n\nIf you have already had a stroke or TIA, or if you are at high risk due to hypertension, diabetes, or heart disease, daily self-monitoring becomes essential.\n\nCheck your blood pressure every morning using a home monitor. These cost KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 at pharmacies across Kenya and are a critical investment. Record your readings in a notebook. Target is generally below 130/80 mmHg for stroke survivors, though your doctor may set a specific target for you.\n\nTake your medications exactly as prescribed. Blood thinners, blood pressure pills, and statins only work if you take them consistently. Set a daily phone alarm as a reminder. Keep a backup supply so you never run out — interrupting blood thinners abruptly can actually increase your clot risk.\n\nKnow your baseline. Pay attention to how your body normally feels so you can quickly recognise when something changes. If you normally have steady hands and suddenly notice a tremor, if your speech is normally clear and suddenly becomes garbled, if your vision is normally sharp and suddenly blurs — these changes deserve immediate attention.\n\nTeach your family the FAST signs. Your family members are often the first people to notice stroke symptoms, especially if the stroke affects your awareness or ability to communicate. Make sure your spouse, children, parents, or housemates know the signs and know to call for emergency help immediately. Post the FAST acronym on your refrigerator or near your family''s gathering area.\n\nA TIA is a gift — a terrifying gift, but a gift nonetheless. It gives you the chance to prevent a major stroke that could leave you permanently disabled or take your life. Do not waste that warning.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you have had a stroke, or if you have risk factors for stroke such as high blood pressure, diabetes, or heart disease, understanding transient ischaemic attacks — commonly called TIAs or mini-strokes — could save your life. A TIA is your body''s most urgent warning that a major stroke is coming, and recognising it gives you a critical window to prevent disaster."},{"id":"s2","title":"What is a TIA","body":"A transient ischaemic attack happens when blood flow to part of your brain is temporarily blocked, usually by a small blood clot. The key word is \\"temporarily\\" — unlike a full stroke, the blockage clears on its own, usually within minutes to hours, and symptoms resolve completely. Because the symptoms go away, many people dismiss a TIA as nothing serious. This is a potentially fatal mistake.\\n\\nA TIA is not a stroke that did not happen. It is a stroke that is about to happen. Research shows that up to 20 percent of people who experience a TIA will have a full, major stroke within 90 days. The risk is highest in the first 48 hours after a TIA. This means that a TIA is a medical emergency that demands immediate hospital evaluation, even though you may feel completely fine by the time you arrive."},{"id":"s3","title":"Recognising TIA symptoms","body":"TIA symptoms are identical to stroke symptoms. The only difference is that TIA symptoms resolve on their own, while stroke symptoms persist. Here is what to watch for.\\n\\nFace drooping: One side of the face suddenly droops or feels numb. Ask the person to smile — if the smile is uneven, this is a warning sign.\\n\\nArm weakness: One arm suddenly becomes weak or numb. Ask the person to raise both arms — if one drifts downward, this is a warning sign.\\n\\nSpeech difficulty: Speech suddenly becomes slurred, garbled, or hard to understand. The person may also have difficulty understanding what you are saying to them.\\n\\nVision changes: Sudden loss of vision in one or both eyes, or sudden double vision. Some people describe a curtain coming down over one eye.\\n\\nSevere headache: A sudden, severe headache unlike any you have experienced before, sometimes described as \\"the worst headache of my life.\\"\\n\\nBalance problems: Sudden dizziness, loss of balance, or difficulty walking. The person may stumble or be unable to stand steadily.\\n\\nConfusion: Sudden difficulty thinking clearly or understanding a situation that would normally be straightforward.\\n\\nThese symptoms typically last anywhere from a few minutes to an hour. In some cases, they can last up to 24 hours. Regardless of how quickly they resolve, they require emergency medical evaluation."},{"id":"s4","title":"Why people ignore TIAs","body":"The very nature of a TIA — symptoms that appear suddenly and then vanish — makes it easy to dismiss. Here are common reactions that lead to dangerous delays.\\n\\n\\"It was probably just fatigue or stress.\\" When your arm goes weak for ten minutes and then returns to normal, it is tempting to chalk it up to sleeping in a wrong position or being overworked. But normal fatigue does not cause sudden one-sided weakness.\\n\\n\\"I feel fine now, so it cannot have been serious.\\" The fact that you feel fine is actually what makes a TIA so dangerous. You feel fine, so you do not seek help, and then the major stroke hits days later.\\n\\n\\"I do not want to waste the doctor''s time.\\" This thought has cost lives. No doctor will ever consider a possible TIA to be a waste of their time. They would far rather see you for a false alarm than treat you for a major stroke that could have been prevented.\\n\\n\\"Hospital is too expensive or too far.\\" This is a real barrier for many Kenyans. But consider the cost comparison: emergency evaluation for a TIA might cost KES 5,000 to KES 20,000 depending on the tests and facility. Treatment and rehabilitation after a major stroke can cost hundreds of thousands of shillings, plus the ongoing cost of disability, lost income, and full-time caregiving."},{"id":"s5","title":"What to do if you suspect a TIA","body":"Act immediately, even if symptoms have already resolved. Use the FAST method to confirm your suspicion: Face drooping, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, Time to act.\\n\\nGet to the nearest hospital emergency department. Do not wait to see if the symptoms come back. Do not sleep on it and plan to see a doctor in the morning. Do not take an aspirin and assume you have handled it. Go to the hospital now.\\n\\nAt the hospital, doctors will typically perform several tests. A CT or MRI scan of your brain looks for evidence of stroke. Blood tests check your cholesterol, blood sugar, and clotting factors. An ECG (heart tracing) checks for irregular heart rhythms like atrial fibrillation, which is a major cause of blood clots that travel to the brain. An ultrasound of the carotid arteries in your neck checks for dangerous narrowing caused by plaque buildup.\\n\\nBased on these results, your doctor will start preventive treatment immediately. This often includes blood-thinning medication (aspirin or clopidogrel), blood pressure medication if yours is elevated, cholesterol-lowering medication (statins), and management of any other risk factors identified."},{"id":"s6","title":"Monitoring yourself at home","body":"If you have already had a stroke or TIA, or if you are at high risk due to hypertension, diabetes, or heart disease, daily self-monitoring becomes essential.\\n\\nCheck your blood pressure every morning using a home monitor. These cost KES 2,500 to KES 5,000 at pharmacies across Kenya and are a critical investment. Record your readings in a notebook. Target is generally below 130/80 mmHg for stroke survivors, though your doctor may set a specific target for you.\\n\\nTake your medications exactly as prescribed. Blood thinners, blood pressure pills, and statins only work if you take them consistently. Set a daily phone alarm as a reminder. Keep a backup supply so you never run out — interrupting blood thinners abruptly can actually increase your clot risk.\\n\\nKnow your baseline. Pay attention to how your body normally feels so you can quickly recognise when something changes. If you normally have steady hands and suddenly notice a tremor, if your speech is normally clear and suddenly becomes garbled, if your vision is normally sharp and suddenly blurs — these changes deserve immediate attention.\\n\\nTeach your family the FAST signs. Your family members are often the first people to notice stroke symptoms, especially if the stroke affects your awareness or ability to communicate. Make sure your spouse, children, parents, or housemates know the signs and know to call for emergency help immediately. Post the FAST acronym on your refrigerator or near your family''s gathering area.\\n\\nA TIA is a gift — a terrifying gift, but a gift nonetheless. It gives you the chance to prevent a major stroke that could leave you permanently disabled or take your life. Do not waste that warning."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'blood-pressure-check'),
  (E'progress-is-not-linear-and-that-is-okay', E'Progress is not linear — and that is okay', E'STROKE', '{"STROKE"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'If you are recovering from a stroke and feel like your progress has stalled, or like the improvements you were making have suddenly stopped, you are not alone. This experience — the dreaded plateau — is one of the most discouraging parts of stroke recovery.', E'If you are recovering from a stroke and feel like your progress has stalled, or like the improvements you were making have suddenly stopped, you are not alone. This experience — the dreaded plateau — is one of the most discouraging parts of stroke recovery. But understanding why plateaus happen and what they actually mean can transform how you navigate them.\n\nWhat a plateau looks like\n\nIn the early weeks after your stroke, progress may have been visible and encouraging. Perhaps you went from being unable to lift your arm to being able to raise it slightly. Perhaps your speech improved from garbled sounds to recognisable words. Perhaps you went from needing full assistance to walk to being able to take a few steps with a frame. Each day brought something new, and each achievement fuelled your motivation.\n\nThen, seemingly without warning, the improvements stopped. You are doing the same exercises, taking the same medication, putting in the same effort — but nothing seems to be changing. Days stretch into weeks at the same level. You start to wonder: Is this it? Have I recovered as much as I am going to? Is there any point in continuing?\n\nThese thoughts are natural, and nearly every stroke survivor has them. But the answers are: No, this is probably not it. You likely have more recovery ahead. And yes, there is every reason to continue.\n\nWhy plateaus happen\n\nYour brain heals in a different way from the rest of your body. When you cut your skin, healing is relatively linear — the wound closes a little more each day until it is healed. Brain recovery does not work this way. It happens in waves.\n\nDuring active improvement phases, your brain is forming new neural connections rapidly. Healthy brain areas are learning to take over functions that the damaged area used to control. This rewiring is visible as improvements in your movement, speech, or thinking.\n\nDuring plateau phases, your brain is consolidating those new connections — strengthening and stabilising them. Think of it like building a house: after you put up the walls quickly, you need time to install the wiring, plumbing, and finishes before you can build the next floor. The plateau is the consolidation phase. It looks like nothing is happening on the outside, but critical reinforcement is happening on the inside.\n\nResearch has shown that stroke recovery can continue for months and even years after the event. Some patients experience significant breakthroughs at six months, twelve months, or even two years after their stroke. The brain''s capacity for reorganisation is far greater than was believed even twenty years ago.\n\nGetting through the flat periods\n\nKeep doing your exercises. This is the single most important thing you can do during a plateau. The repetitions you perform during a plateau are not wasted — they are providing the stimulation your brain needs to consolidate and then advance. If you stop exercising because you do not see immediate progress, you risk losing the gains you have already made.\n\nAdjust your expectations, not your effort. During a plateau, shift your focus from achieving new milestones to perfecting existing abilities. If you can lift your arm to shoulder height, work on making that lift smoother and more controlled. If you can say certain words clearly, practice stringing them into sentences. Quality of movement matters as much as range of movement.\n\nTrack your progress in writing. When improvement is slow, it is easy to forget how far you have come. Keep a simple diary where you note what you can do each week. Compare this month to two months ago rather than comparing today to yesterday. You may be surprised to discover that progress you cannot see day to day is quite visible over longer periods.\n\nTalk to your physiotherapist about changing your exercise routine. Sometimes a plateau is a signal that your current exercises have become too easy for your brain — it has mastered them and needs new challenges to continue rewiring. Your physiotherapist can introduce new movements, increase difficulty, or try different approaches. If you were discharged from physiotherapy and are doing home exercises, ask your doctor for a follow-up referral. Many county referral hospitals and facilities like Kenyatta National Hospital have outpatient rehabilitation units.\n\nStay connected to your support system. Plateaus are lonely because the people around you may also lose hope or become less actively supportive when visible progress slows. Let your family know that plateaus are a normal, expected part of stroke recovery, not a sign that recovery has ended. If a stroke support group is available in your area, attending during a plateau can be especially helpful because other survivors understand exactly what you are going through.\n\nTake care of your mental health. Depression and discouragement during plateaus are common and understandable. If you feel persistently hopeless, lose interest in your exercises, withdraw from family, or have thoughts of giving up, please tell your doctor. Depression after stroke is treatable — counselling and, if needed, medication can help you through the difficult periods. Many county hospitals now have mental health services, and private counsellors are available in major towns for KES 2,000 to KES 5,000 per session.\n\nThe stories of late recovery\n\nStroke recovery literature is filled with stories of people who experienced significant breakthroughs long after conventional timelines would suggest recovery was complete. A person who could not speak at six months suddenly starts forming sentences at fourteen months. A hand that was clenched in a fist for a year slowly begins to open. Walking that was impossible at three months becomes possible with a stick at eighteen months.\n\nThese late recoveries are not miracles or outliers. They are the result of persistent effort meeting the brain''s ongoing capacity for change. The plateau was not the end — it was the preparation.\n\nYour recovery is on its own timeline\n\nNo two strokes are identical, and no two recoveries follow the same curve. Comparing your progress to someone else''s is neither fair nor useful. The size of your stroke, its location in your brain, your age, your overall health, the quality of your rehabilitation, and even your personality all influence the pace and pattern of your recovery.\n\nWhat you can control is your consistency. Show up for your exercises every day. Take your medication every day. Attend your appointments. Ask for help when you need it. And when the progress stalls, remind yourself: plateaus are not stop signs. They are rest stops on a longer journey than you expected. The road continues, and so do you.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you are recovering from a stroke and feel like your progress has stalled, or like the improvements you were making have suddenly stopped, you are not alone. This experience — the dreaded plateau — is one of the most discouraging parts of stroke recovery. But understanding why plateaus happen and what they actually mean can transform how you navigate them."},{"id":"s2","title":"What a plateau looks like","body":"In the early weeks after your stroke, progress may have been visible and encouraging. Perhaps you went from being unable to lift your arm to being able to raise it slightly. Perhaps your speech improved from garbled sounds to recognisable words. Perhaps you went from needing full assistance to walk to being able to take a few steps with a frame. Each day brought something new, and each achievement fuelled your motivation.\\n\\nThen, seemingly without warning, the improvements stopped. You are doing the same exercises, taking the same medication, putting in the same effort — but nothing seems to be changing. Days stretch into weeks at the same level. You start to wonder: Is this it? Have I recovered as much as I am going to? Is there any point in continuing?\\n\\nThese thoughts are natural, and nearly every stroke survivor has them. But the answers are: No, this is probably not it. You likely have more recovery ahead. And yes, there is every reason to continue."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why plateaus happen","body":"Your brain heals in a different way from the rest of your body. When you cut your skin, healing is relatively linear — the wound closes a little more each day until it is healed. Brain recovery does not work this way. It happens in waves.\\n\\nDuring active improvement phases, your brain is forming new neural connections rapidly. Healthy brain areas are learning to take over functions that the damaged area used to control. This rewiring is visible as improvements in your movement, speech, or thinking.\\n\\nDuring plateau phases, your brain is consolidating those new connections — strengthening and stabilising them. Think of it like building a house: after you put up the walls quickly, you need time to install the wiring, plumbing, and finishes before you can build the next floor. The plateau is the consolidation phase. It looks like nothing is happening on the outside, but critical reinforcement is happening on the inside.\\n\\nResearch has shown that stroke recovery can continue for months and even years after the event. Some patients experience significant breakthroughs at six months, twelve months, or even two years after their stroke. The brain''s capacity for reorganisation is far greater than was believed even twenty years ago."},{"id":"s4","title":"Getting through the flat periods","body":"Keep doing your exercises. This is the single most important thing you can do during a plateau. The repetitions you perform during a plateau are not wasted — they are providing the stimulation your brain needs to consolidate and then advance. If you stop exercising because you do not see immediate progress, you risk losing the gains you have already made.\\n\\nAdjust your expectations, not your effort. During a plateau, shift your focus from achieving new milestones to perfecting existing abilities. If you can lift your arm to shoulder height, work on making that lift smoother and more controlled. If you can say certain words clearly, practice stringing them into sentences. Quality of movement matters as much as range of movement.\\n\\nTrack your progress in writing. When improvement is slow, it is easy to forget how far you have come. Keep a simple diary where you note what you can do each week. Compare this month to two months ago rather than comparing today to yesterday. You may be surprised to discover that progress you cannot see day to day is quite visible over longer periods.\\n\\nTalk to your physiotherapist about changing your exercise routine. Sometimes a plateau is a signal that your current exercises have become too easy for your brain — it has mastered them and needs new challenges to continue rewiring. Your physiotherapist can introduce new movements, increase difficulty, or try different approaches. If you were discharged from physiotherapy and are doing home exercises, ask your doctor for a follow-up referral. Many county referral hospitals and facilities like Kenyatta National Hospital have outpatient rehabilitation units.\\n\\nStay connected to your support system. Plateaus are lonely because the people around you may also lose hope or become less actively supportive when visible progress slows. Let your family know that plateaus are a normal, expected part of stroke recovery, not a sign that recovery has ended. If a stroke support group is available in your area, attending during a plateau can be especially helpful because other survivors understand exactly what you are going through.\\n\\nTake care of your mental health. Depression and discouragement during plateaus are common and understandable. If you feel persistently hopeless, lose interest in your exercises, withdraw from family, or have thoughts of giving up, please tell your doctor. Depression after stroke is treatable — counselling and, if needed, medication can help you through the difficult periods. Many county hospitals now have mental health services, and private counsellors are available in major towns for KES 2,000 to KES 5,000 per session."},{"id":"s5","title":"The stories of late recovery","body":"Stroke recovery literature is filled with stories of people who experienced significant breakthroughs long after conventional timelines would suggest recovery was complete. A person who could not speak at six months suddenly starts forming sentences at fourteen months. A hand that was clenched in a fist for a year slowly begins to open. Walking that was impossible at three months becomes possible with a stick at eighteen months.\\n\\nThese late recoveries are not miracles or outliers. They are the result of persistent effort meeting the brain''s ongoing capacity for change. The plateau was not the end — it was the preparation."},{"id":"s6","title":"Your recovery is on its own timeline","body":"No two strokes are identical, and no two recoveries follow the same curve. Comparing your progress to someone else''s is neither fair nor useful. The size of your stroke, its location in your brain, your age, your overall health, the quality of your rehabilitation, and even your personality all influence the pace and pattern of your recovery.\\n\\nWhat you can control is your consistency. Show up for your exercises every day. Take your medication every day. Attend your appointments. Ask for help when you need it. And when the progress stalls, remind yourself: plateaus are not stop signs. They are rest stops on a longer journey than you expected. The road continues, and so do you."}]'::jsonb, 7, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'self-care'),
  (E'you-regained-a-skill-you-thought-was-lost', E'You regained a skill you thought was lost', E'STROKE', '{"STROKE"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Today you did something that, not long ago, felt impossible. Maybe you wrote your name for the first time since your stroke.', E'Today you did something that, not long ago, felt impossible. Maybe you wrote your name for the first time since your stroke. Maybe you buttoned your shirt without help. Maybe you climbed a step. Maybe you said a word clearly that had been tangled in your mouth for weeks. Whatever it is — no matter how small it might seem to someone who has never experienced stroke — this is a profound achievement, and it deserves to be celebrated.\n\nWhat this moment really means\n\nWhen you regain a skill after a stroke, you are not just performing a physical action. You are witnessing your brain rebuilding itself. The area of your brain that originally controlled that skill was damaged by the stroke. The fact that you can now perform that action means your brain found another way — it created new neural pathways, rerouted signals through undamaged areas, and rebuilt a connection that was severed. This is neuroplasticity in action, and it is one of the most remarkable things the human body can do.\n\nEvery skill you recover is evidence that your rehabilitation is working. Your daily exercises, your physiotherapy sessions, your speech practice, your determination to keep trying when progress felt invisible — all of that effort created the conditions for this breakthrough. This did not happen by accident. It happened because you showed up, day after day, and did the work.\n\nWhy celebrating matters\n\nIn Kenyan culture, we tend to downplay our own achievements, especially when we feel there is still a long way to go. You might think, "It is just writing my name — I used to write entire reports." Or, "I only climbed one step — I used to run up three flights of stairs." This kind of thinking robs you of the encouragement you need to keep going.\n\nStroke recovery is a marathon, not a sprint. And in a marathon, you celebrate every kilometre marker, not just the finish line. Each recovered skill proves that more recovery is possible. It reinforces the truth that your brain is still adapting, still healing, still capable of change. This evidence of progress is fuel for the difficult days ahead — and there will be difficult days.\n\nCelebrate this milestone actively. Tell your family what you accomplished today. Let them see your pride and let them share in your joy. If your spouse, children, parents, or siblings have been supporting you through your recovery — helping with exercises, driving you to appointments, adjusting their lives to accommodate your needs — they need this good news as much as you do. Your progress is their reward for their sacrifice.\n\nTell your physiotherapist or doctor at your next appointment. Healthcare workers who support stroke patients are often deeply moved by recovery milestones. They may also use your achievement to recalibrate your rehabilitation programme, introducing new challenges now that you have demonstrated readiness for the next level.\n\nWrite it down\n\nGet a notebook and start a recovery journal. Each time you regain a skill or achieve something new, write the date and what you accomplished. This journal serves two critical purposes.\n\nFirst, it creates a record of progress that you can look back on during difficult periods. When you hit a plateau — and you will — you can open this journal and remind yourself of how far you have come. The person who wrote that first entry could not do what you can do today. That trajectory of improvement is powerful evidence that more improvement is ahead.\n\nSecond, it helps your healthcare team understand your recovery pattern. When you see your doctor, you can share specific milestones and their dates, helping them assess whether your recovery is on track or whether your treatment plan needs adjustment.\n\nThe skills that matter most to you\n\nRecovery milestones are deeply personal. Medical professionals measure recovery in standardised scales, but the milestones that matter most to you are the ones connected to your identity and your daily life.\n\nFor a mother, it might be the first time she can hold her child again with her affected arm. For a driver, it might be the first time he can grip a steering wheel. For someone who loved cooking, it might be the first time she can chop vegetables. For a churchgoer, it might be being able to sing a hymn clearly. For a football fan, it might be walking to the field to watch a neighbourhood match.\n\nThese are not small things. They are the activities that make up a life, and recovering them is recovering your independence, your identity, and your sense of self.\n\nBuilding on this success\n\nEvery recovered skill opens the door to more recovery. The neural pathways you have built do not just serve one function — they create a foundation that other skills can build upon. Recovering hand grip can lead to recovering fine motor skills. Recovering single words can lead to recovering full sentences. Recovering the ability to stand can lead to recovering the ability to walk.\n\nWork with your physiotherapist to set the next goal now, while your motivation is high. Make it specific and achievable: "I want to button three buttons on my own," or "I want to walk to the gate and back without my frame," or "I want to say my children''s names clearly." Having a concrete next target keeps you focused and gives your exercises purpose.\n\nKeep doing the exercises that got you here. It is tempting to ease up after a breakthrough, but the repetitions that built this skill need to continue to cement it. The neural pathways you created are still new and fragile — consistent practice makes them permanent.\n\nA message for your family\n\nIf you are a family member reading this alongside a stroke survivor, here is what you can do today. Celebrate with them. Do not measure their achievement against what they could do before the stroke — measure it against what they could do last month. Acknowledge their effort, not just their result. Say, "I am proud of how hard you have been working," not just, "That is great." And understand that this milestone, however small it may appear, represents an extraordinary amount of courage, patience, and daily effort.\n\nYour support has been part of this recovery. Every time you helped with exercises, encouraged them to try again, or simply sat with them on a hard day, you contributed to this moment. This is your celebration too.\n\nYou are rebuilding, one connection at a time\n\nThe human brain contains roughly 86 billion neurons. Your stroke damaged some of them, but billions remain, ready to be recruited, retrained, and reconnected. Every recovered skill is proof that this recruitment is happening. You are not the same person you were before your stroke, and you are not the same person you were last month. You are growing, adapting, and rebuilding — one neural connection, one exercise repetition, one brave attempt at a time. Keep going.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Today you did something that, not long ago, felt impossible. Maybe you wrote your name for the first time since your stroke. Maybe you buttoned your shirt without help. Maybe you climbed a step. Maybe you said a word clearly that had been tangled in your mouth for weeks. Whatever it is — no matter how small it might seem to someone who has never experienced stroke — this is a profound achievement, and it deserves to be celebrated."},{"id":"s2","title":"What this moment really means","body":"When you regain a skill after a stroke, you are not just performing a physical action. You are witnessing your brain rebuilding itself. The area of your brain that originally controlled that skill was damaged by the stroke. The fact that you can now perform that action means your brain found another way — it created new neural pathways, rerouted signals through undamaged areas, and rebuilt a connection that was severed. This is neuroplasticity in action, and it is one of the most remarkable things the human body can do.\\n\\nEvery skill you recover is evidence that your rehabilitation is working. Your daily exercises, your physiotherapy sessions, your speech practice, your determination to keep trying when progress felt invisible — all of that effort created the conditions for this breakthrough. This did not happen by accident. It happened because you showed up, day after day, and did the work."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why celebrating matters","body":"In Kenyan culture, we tend to downplay our own achievements, especially when we feel there is still a long way to go. You might think, \\"It is just writing my name — I used to write entire reports.\\" Or, \\"I only climbed one step — I used to run up three flights of stairs.\\" This kind of thinking robs you of the encouragement you need to keep going.\\n\\nStroke recovery is a marathon, not a sprint. And in a marathon, you celebrate every kilometre marker, not just the finish line. Each recovered skill proves that more recovery is possible. It reinforces the truth that your brain is still adapting, still healing, still capable of change. This evidence of progress is fuel for the difficult days ahead — and there will be difficult days.\\n\\nCelebrate this milestone actively. Tell your family what you accomplished today. Let them see your pride and let them share in your joy. If your spouse, children, parents, or siblings have been supporting you through your recovery — helping with exercises, driving you to appointments, adjusting their lives to accommodate your needs — they need this good news as much as you do. Your progress is their reward for their sacrifice.\\n\\nTell your physiotherapist or doctor at your next appointment. Healthcare workers who support stroke patients are often deeply moved by recovery milestones. They may also use your achievement to recalibrate your rehabilitation programme, introducing new challenges now that you have demonstrated readiness for the next level."},{"id":"s4","title":"Write it down","body":"Get a notebook and start a recovery journal. Each time you regain a skill or achieve something new, write the date and what you accomplished. This journal serves two critical purposes.\\n\\nFirst, it creates a record of progress that you can look back on during difficult periods. When you hit a plateau — and you will — you can open this journal and remind yourself of how far you have come. The person who wrote that first entry could not do what you can do today. That trajectory of improvement is powerful evidence that more improvement is ahead.\\n\\nSecond, it helps your healthcare team understand your recovery pattern. When you see your doctor, you can share specific milestones and their dates, helping them assess whether your recovery is on track or whether your treatment plan needs adjustment."},{"id":"s5","title":"The skills that matter most to you","body":"Recovery milestones are deeply personal. Medical professionals measure recovery in standardised scales, but the milestones that matter most to you are the ones connected to your identity and your daily life.\\n\\nFor a mother, it might be the first time she can hold her child again with her affected arm. For a driver, it might be the first time he can grip a steering wheel. For someone who loved cooking, it might be the first time she can chop vegetables. For a churchgoer, it might be being able to sing a hymn clearly. For a football fan, it might be walking to the field to watch a neighbourhood match.\\n\\nThese are not small things. They are the activities that make up a life, and recovering them is recovering your independence, your identity, and your sense of self."},{"id":"s6","title":"Building on this success","body":"Every recovered skill opens the door to more recovery. The neural pathways you have built do not just serve one function — they create a foundation that other skills can build upon. Recovering hand grip can lead to recovering fine motor skills. Recovering single words can lead to recovering full sentences. Recovering the ability to stand can lead to recovering the ability to walk.\\n\\nWork with your physiotherapist to set the next goal now, while your motivation is high. Make it specific and achievable: \\"I want to button three buttons on my own,\\" or \\"I want to walk to the gate and back without my frame,\\" or \\"I want to say my children''s names clearly.\\" Having a concrete next target keeps you focused and gives your exercises purpose.\\n\\nKeep doing the exercises that got you here. It is tempting to ease up after a breakthrough, but the repetitions that built this skill need to continue to cement it. The neural pathways you created are still new and fragile — consistent practice makes them permanent."},{"id":"s7","title":"A message for your family","body":"If you are a family member reading this alongside a stroke survivor, here is what you can do today. Celebrate with them. Do not measure their achievement against what they could do before the stroke — measure it against what they could do last month. Acknowledge their effort, not just their result. Say, \\"I am proud of how hard you have been working,\\" not just, \\"That is great.\\" And understand that this milestone, however small it may appear, represents an extraordinary amount of courage, patience, and daily effort.\\n\\nYour support has been part of this recovery. Every time you helped with exercises, encouraged them to try again, or simply sat with them on a hard day, you contributed to this moment. This is your celebration too."},{"id":"s8","title":"You are rebuilding, one connection at a time","body":"The human brain contains roughly 86 billion neurons. Your stroke damaged some of them, but billions remain, ready to be recruited, retrained, and reconnected. Every recovered skill is proof that this recruitment is happening. You are not the same person you were before your stroke, and you are not the same person you were last month. You are growing, adapting, and rebuilding — one neural connection, one exercise repetition, one brave attempt at a time. Keep going."}]'::jsonb, 8, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'progress-chart'),
  (E'protecting-your-liver-with-everyday-food-choices', E'Protecting your liver with everyday food choices', E'LIVER_DISEASE', '{"LIVER_DISEASE"}', E'DIETARY', E'Your liver is the hardest-working organ in your body. Every single thing you eat or drink passes through it for processing — nutrients get sorted and stored, toxins get neutralised and removed, proteins get built, bile gets produced for digestion.', E'Your liver is the hardest-working organ in your body. Every single thing you eat or drink passes through it for processing — nutrients get sorted and stored, toxins get neutralised and removed, proteins get built, bile gets produced for digestion. When your liver is damaged or diseased, its ability to perform these critical functions is reduced. This means that what you eat has a direct and powerful impact on how well your liver can cope, heal, and function.\n\nThe good news is that the foods that support your liver are not expensive or exotic. They are the everyday Kenyan foods you already know — chosen carefully and prepared thoughtfully.\n\nThe non-negotiable: eliminate alcohol completely\n\nIf you have been diagnosed with any form of liver disease — whether it is fatty liver, hepatitis B or C, cirrhosis, or alcoholic liver disease — alcohol must stop entirely. Not reduce. Not switch to lighter drinks. Stop completely.\n\nAlcohol is directly toxic to liver cells. Every drink you take forces your already-damaged liver to work harder to break down the alcohol, causing further inflammation and scarring. Even small amounts that would be harmless to a healthy liver can accelerate damage in a diseased one. This includes beer, wine, spirits, traditional brews like muratina and busaa, and chang''aa. There is no safe amount of alcohol when you have liver disease.\n\nThis can be socially difficult in Kenya, where drinking is deeply embedded in social gatherings, business meetings, and celebrations. Prepare yourself with a simple response: "I am on medication that does not mix with alcohol" or "My doctor has put me on a strict plan." Most people will respect this without pressing further. If you struggle with alcohol dependence, speak honestly with your doctor — there are medications and counselling services that can help, and asking for support is a sign of strength, not weakness.\n\nFoods that support liver healing\n\nVegetables are your liver''s best friend, particularly those rich in antioxidants. Antioxidants neutralise the harmful molecules (called free radicals) that damage liver cells. Prioritise these.\n\nBeetroot: Rich in betalains, a powerful antioxidant that studies have shown supports liver detoxification. You can buy beetroot at most Kenyan markets for KES 20 to KES 50 per piece. Grate it raw into salads, boil and slice it as a side dish, or blend it into a smoothie with banana and ginger.\n\nCarrots: High in beta-carotene, which your liver converts to vitamin A for cell repair. A kilogram of carrots costs KES 40 to KES 80 at the market. Eat them raw as snacks, grate them into your sukuma wiki, or add them to stews.\n\nLeafy greens: Sukuma wiki (kale), spinach, managu, and terere are all excellent choices. They contain chlorophyll, which helps your liver filter toxins from the blood. A bundle of sukuma wiki costs as little as KES 10 to KES 30 and can form the basis of a liver-supportive meal.\n\nGarlic and onions: These contain sulphur compounds that activate liver enzymes responsible for flushing out toxins. Use them generously when cooking — they add flavour that replaces the need for processed seasonings.\n\nTomatoes: Rich in lycopene, another antioxidant that supports liver health. Use fresh tomatoes in your cooking rather than tomato paste from tins, which often contains added salt and preservatives.\n\nWhole grains over refined flour\n\nYour liver plays a central role in regulating blood sugar. Refined carbohydrates — white bread, white rice, mandazi made with white flour, white ugali — cause rapid blood sugar spikes that strain your liver. Switch to whole grain alternatives where possible.\n\nWhole grain ugali made from unrefined maize flour is available in many Kenyan supermarkets. It has a slightly darker colour and nuttier flavour than white ugali. Githeri — the traditional dish of boiled maize and beans — is an excellent choice because it combines whole grains with plant protein and fibre, all of which support liver health. Sweet potatoes and arrowroots (nduma) are also good starchy options that release energy slowly.\n\nIf you enjoy chapati, try making it with a mix of half whole wheat flour and half regular flour. The flavour difference is subtle, but the health benefit is significant.\n\nProtein — essential but in the right amounts\n\nYour liver needs protein to repair itself, but too much protein can be harmful if your liver is severely damaged, because the liver processes the waste products of protein metabolism. Work with your doctor or nutritionist to determine the right amount for your specific stage of liver disease.\n\nFor most liver disease patients who are not in advanced cirrhosis, moderate amounts of lean protein are beneficial. Good sources include skinless chicken, fish (tilapia, Nile perch, and omena are affordable options), eggs (two to three per week), and plant proteins like beans, lentils, and green grams (ndengu). A kilogram of green grams costs approximately KES 150 to KES 250 and provides excellent protein with the added benefit of fibre.\n\nLimit red meat to once or twice per week, and avoid processed meats entirely — sausages, bacon, ham, and canned meats are high in both salt and preservatives that burden your liver.\n\nFats — choose wisely\n\nFatty liver disease is one of the most common liver conditions in Kenya, and it is directly linked to the types of fats you eat. Avoid deep-fried foods — this means limiting chips (French fries), deep-fried fish, deep-fried chicken, bhajias, and fried mandazi. When you cook at home, use small amounts of sunflower oil or olive oil and prefer methods like steaming, boiling, grilling, or baking.\n\nAvocado, which is abundant and affordable in Kenya especially during harvest season (often as low as KES 10 to KES 30 per fruit), contains healthy monounsaturated fats that actually support liver function. Half an avocado with lunch several times a week is a great habit.\n\nSmall, frequent meals\n\nA damaged liver works best with smaller, more frequent meals rather than two or three large ones. Try eating five to six small meals throughout the day instead of the traditional breakfast, lunch, and dinner pattern. This maintains steady blood sugar levels and prevents your liver from being overwhelmed.\n\nA sample day might look like this. Early morning: a cup of warm water with lemon (lemon supports bile production). Breakfast at 8 AM: whole grain uji with a sliced banana. Mid-morning snack at 10:30 AM: a small handful of groundnuts and a piece of fruit. Lunch at 1 PM: githeri with steamed sukuma wiki and a few slices of avocado. Afternoon snack at 4 PM: a boiled egg with a raw carrot. Dinner at 7 PM: grilled tilapia with a small portion of brown rice and a beetroot and cabbage salad.\n\nHydration matters\n\nWater is essential for your liver''s detoxification processes. Aim for at least eight glasses (about two litres) per day. Plain water is best. You can add lemon or cucumber slices for flavour. Avoid sodas and commercial fruit juices, which are high in sugar that converts to fat in your liver. If you enjoy hot drinks, plain green tea is a good option — research suggests it has liver-protective properties.\n\nThese dietary changes are not just for you — they benefit your whole family. When you cook healthier meals at home, everyone at the table eats better. You are not just protecting your liver; you are building a culture of health in your household that can prevent liver disease in your children and grandchildren.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Your liver is the hardest-working organ in your body. Every single thing you eat or drink passes through it for processing — nutrients get sorted and stored, toxins get neutralised and removed, proteins get built, bile gets produced for digestion. When your liver is damaged or diseased, its ability to perform these critical functions is reduced. This means that what you eat has a direct and powerful impact on how well your liver can cope, heal, and function.\\n\\nThe good news is that the foods that support your liver are not expensive or exotic. They are the everyday Kenyan foods you already know — chosen carefully and prepared thoughtfully."},{"id":"s2","title":"The non-negotiable: eliminate alcohol completely","body":"If you have been diagnosed with any form of liver disease — whether it is fatty liver, hepatitis B or C, cirrhosis, or alcoholic liver disease — alcohol must stop entirely. Not reduce. Not switch to lighter drinks. Stop completely.\\n\\nAlcohol is directly toxic to liver cells. Every drink you take forces your already-damaged liver to work harder to break down the alcohol, causing further inflammation and scarring. Even small amounts that would be harmless to a healthy liver can accelerate damage in a diseased one. This includes beer, wine, spirits, traditional brews like muratina and busaa, and chang''aa. There is no safe amount of alcohol when you have liver disease.\\n\\nThis can be socially difficult in Kenya, where drinking is deeply embedded in social gatherings, business meetings, and celebrations. Prepare yourself with a simple response: \\"I am on medication that does not mix with alcohol\\" or \\"My doctor has put me on a strict plan.\\" Most people will respect this without pressing further. If you struggle with alcohol dependence, speak honestly with your doctor — there are medications and counselling services that can help, and asking for support is a sign of strength, not weakness."},{"id":"s3","title":"Foods that support liver healing","body":"Vegetables are your liver''s best friend, particularly those rich in antioxidants. Antioxidants neutralise the harmful molecules (called free radicals) that damage liver cells. Prioritise these.\\n\\nBeetroot: Rich in betalains, a powerful antioxidant that studies have shown supports liver detoxification. You can buy beetroot at most Kenyan markets for KES 20 to KES 50 per piece. Grate it raw into salads, boil and slice it as a side dish, or blend it into a smoothie with banana and ginger.\\n\\nCarrots: High in beta-carotene, which your liver converts to vitamin A for cell repair. A kilogram of carrots costs KES 40 to KES 80 at the market. Eat them raw as snacks, grate them into your sukuma wiki, or add them to stews.\\n\\nLeafy greens: Sukuma wiki (kale), spinach, managu, and terere are all excellent choices. They contain chlorophyll, which helps your liver filter toxins from the blood. A bundle of sukuma wiki costs as little as KES 10 to KES 30 and can form the basis of a liver-supportive meal.\\n\\nGarlic and onions: These contain sulphur compounds that activate liver enzymes responsible for flushing out toxins. Use them generously when cooking — they add flavour that replaces the need for processed seasonings.\\n\\nTomatoes: Rich in lycopene, another antioxidant that supports liver health. Use fresh tomatoes in your cooking rather than tomato paste from tins, which often contains added salt and preservatives."},{"id":"s4","title":"Whole grains over refined flour","body":"Your liver plays a central role in regulating blood sugar. Refined carbohydrates — white bread, white rice, mandazi made with white flour, white ugali — cause rapid blood sugar spikes that strain your liver. Switch to whole grain alternatives where possible.\\n\\nWhole grain ugali made from unrefined maize flour is available in many Kenyan supermarkets. It has a slightly darker colour and nuttier flavour than white ugali. Githeri — the traditional dish of boiled maize and beans — is an excellent choice because it combines whole grains with plant protein and fibre, all of which support liver health. Sweet potatoes and arrowroots (nduma) are also good starchy options that release energy slowly.\\n\\nIf you enjoy chapati, try making it with a mix of half whole wheat flour and half regular flour. The flavour difference is subtle, but the health benefit is significant."},{"id":"s5","title":"Protein — essential but in the right amounts","body":"Your liver needs protein to repair itself, but too much protein can be harmful if your liver is severely damaged, because the liver processes the waste products of protein metabolism. Work with your doctor or nutritionist to determine the right amount for your specific stage of liver disease.\\n\\nFor most liver disease patients who are not in advanced cirrhosis, moderate amounts of lean protein are beneficial. Good sources include skinless chicken, fish (tilapia, Nile perch, and omena are affordable options), eggs (two to three per week), and plant proteins like beans, lentils, and green grams (ndengu). A kilogram of green grams costs approximately KES 150 to KES 250 and provides excellent protein with the added benefit of fibre.\\n\\nLimit red meat to once or twice per week, and avoid processed meats entirely — sausages, bacon, ham, and canned meats are high in both salt and preservatives that burden your liver."},{"id":"s6","title":"Fats — choose wisely","body":"Fatty liver disease is one of the most common liver conditions in Kenya, and it is directly linked to the types of fats you eat. Avoid deep-fried foods — this means limiting chips (French fries), deep-fried fish, deep-fried chicken, bhajias, and fried mandazi. When you cook at home, use small amounts of sunflower oil or olive oil and prefer methods like steaming, boiling, grilling, or baking.\\n\\nAvocado, which is abundant and affordable in Kenya especially during harvest season (often as low as KES 10 to KES 30 per fruit), contains healthy monounsaturated fats that actually support liver function. Half an avocado with lunch several times a week is a great habit."},{"id":"s7","title":"Small, frequent meals","body":"A damaged liver works best with smaller, more frequent meals rather than two or three large ones. Try eating five to six small meals throughout the day instead of the traditional breakfast, lunch, and dinner pattern. This maintains steady blood sugar levels and prevents your liver from being overwhelmed.\\n\\nA sample day might look like this. Early morning: a cup of warm water with lemon (lemon supports bile production). Breakfast at 8 AM: whole grain uji with a sliced banana. Mid-morning snack at 10:30 AM: a small handful of groundnuts and a piece of fruit. Lunch at 1 PM: githeri with steamed sukuma wiki and a few slices of avocado. Afternoon snack at 4 PM: a boiled egg with a raw carrot. Dinner at 7 PM: grilled tilapia with a small portion of brown rice and a beetroot and cabbage salad."},{"id":"s8","title":"Hydration matters","body":"Water is essential for your liver''s detoxification processes. Aim for at least eight glasses (about two litres) per day. Plain water is best. You can add lemon or cucumber slices for flavour. Avoid sodas and commercial fruit juices, which are high in sugar that converts to fat in your liver. If you enjoy hot drinks, plain green tea is a good option — research suggests it has liver-protective properties.\\n\\nThese dietary changes are not just for you — they benefit your whole family. When you cook healthier meals at home, everyone at the table eats better. You are not just protecting your liver; you are building a culture of health in your household that can prevent liver disease in your children and grandchildren."}]'::jsonb, 8, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'balanced-plate'),
  (E'gentle-movement-to-combat-liver-disease-fatigue', E'Gentle movement to combat liver disease fatigue', E'LIVER_DISEASE', '{"LIVER_DISEASE"}', E'EXERCISE', E'If you are living with liver disease, you know fatigue all too well. It is not ordinary tiredness that a good night''s sleep can fix — it is a deep, persistent exhaustion that makes even simple activities feel overwhelming.', E'If you are living with liver disease, you know fatigue all too well. It is not ordinary tiredness that a good night''s sleep can fix — it is a deep, persistent exhaustion that makes even simple activities feel overwhelming. Getting out of bed takes effort. Walking to the shops feels like a marathon. The idea of exercise might seem not just difficult but absurd. Why would you push a body that is already struggling to get through the day?\n\nThe answer may surprise you: gentle, regular movement is one of the best things you can do for your liver, even when — especially when — fatigue is your biggest challenge.\n\nWhy exercise helps a damaged liver\n\nYour liver plays a central role in how your body processes energy, manages fat, and handles insulin. When your liver is damaged, these processes become less efficient. Fat accumulates in and around the liver (even in non-alcoholic fatty liver disease), insulin resistance develops, and your body becomes trapped in a cycle of inflammation, fatigue, and metabolic dysfunction.\n\nGentle exercise breaks this cycle at multiple points. It improves blood flow to the liver, helping it receive oxygen and nutrients for repair. It reduces insulin resistance, which eases one of the major burdens on a struggling liver. It helps burn stored fat, including the fat deposits in your liver that worsen inflammation. And it releases endorphins — your body''s natural mood lifters — which combat the depression and low motivation that often accompany chronic liver disease.\n\nStudies have shown that regular moderate exercise can actually reduce liver fat by 10 to 20 percent, even without weight loss. This means that even if the number on the scale does not change, your liver is getting healthier with each walk you take.\n\nStarting from zero\n\nIf you have been mostly inactive because of fatigue, the key is starting at a level so gentle that it feels almost too easy. This is not about pushing through pain or exhausting yourself — it is about teaching your body that movement is safe and beneficial.\n\nWeek one and two: Walk for 10 minutes at a gentle pace. Choose a flat route — perhaps around your compound, along your estate''s road, or to the nearest duka and back. Walk slowly enough that you can speak comfortably. If 10 minutes feels like too much, walk for five. Do this five days a week, resting on two days.\n\nWeek three and four: Increase to 12 to 13 minutes. You are adding just two to three minutes, but this gradual increase is important. It allows your body to adapt without triggering the kind of exhaustion that makes you want to quit entirely.\n\nWeek five and six: Aim for 15 minutes. By now, your body should be adjusting to the routine. You may notice that the fatigue after walking is less severe than it was in the first week, and that you recover faster.\n\nWeek seven and eight: Increase to 18 to 20 minutes. If you are feeling good, try picking up the pace slightly — not fast, just a bit brisker than your comfortable stroll.\n\nThe long-term goal is 20 to 30 minutes of gentle walking most days of the week. Getting there might take two months or it might take four — there is no rush. The pace of progression depends on the severity of your liver disease, your overall fitness, and how your body responds.\n\nA gentle home yoga and stretching routine\n\nOn days when walking feels too demanding, or as a complement to your walking routine, try this gentle stretching and yoga sequence. You need only a mat, a folded blanket, or a leso spread on the floor.\n\nSeated breathing: Sit cross-legged on the floor or in a chair. Place your hands on your knees. Close your eyes and breathe deeply through your nose — inhale for four counts, hold for two counts, exhale for six counts. Do this for two to three minutes. Deep breathing improves oxygen delivery to your liver and activates your body''s relaxation response.\n\nGentle neck rolls: Slowly roll your head in a circle — right ear toward right shoulder, chin toward chest, left ear toward left shoulder, and back. Do five circles in each direction. This releases tension that builds up from fatigue-related muscle stiffness.\n\nSeated spinal twist: Sit upright in a chair. Place your right hand on your left knee and gently twist your upper body to the left, looking over your left shoulder. Hold for 15 seconds, breathing deeply. This gentle compression and release is believed to promote blood flow to the abdominal organs, including the liver. Repeat on the other side.\n\nCat and cow stretch: Get on your hands and knees. On an inhale, drop your belly toward the floor and lift your head (cow pose). On an exhale, round your back toward the ceiling and tuck your chin (cat pose). Move slowly between these positions 10 times. This mobilises your spine and gently massages your internal organs.\n\nLegs up the wall: Lie on your back near a wall and rest your legs vertically against the wall. Stay here for three to five minutes, breathing deeply. This restorative position improves circulation and reduces swelling in the legs, which can be an issue for people with advanced liver disease.\n\nChild''s pose: Kneel on the floor, sit back on your heels, and fold forward with your arms extended in front of you and your forehead resting on the mat. Stay here for one to two minutes, breathing deeply. This is a resting pose that promotes relaxation and gently stretches the back.\n\nThe entire routine takes about 15 to 20 minutes and can be done at any time of day. Many people find it helpful before bed, as it promotes better sleep.\n\nImportant precautions for liver disease patients\n\nAvoid intense exercise if you have advanced liver disease (cirrhosis with complications). Your body''s ability to regulate energy, blood sugar, and clotting may be impaired. Signs that you are pushing too hard include extreme breathlessness, dizziness, nausea, pain in your upper right abdomen (where your liver is), or unusual bruising.\n\nIf you have ascites (fluid buildup in your abdomen), exercise choices need to be modified. Walking is generally safe, but exercises that require lying flat may be uncomfortable or affect your breathing. Sit-up-type exercises should be avoided. Consult your doctor about what is safe for your specific situation.\n\nStay hydrated during exercise. Drink water before, during, and after your walk or stretching session. However, if your doctor has placed you on fluid restriction due to ascites or other complications, follow their guidance on fluid intake.\n\nIf you take blood pressure medication or diuretics (water tablets), you may feel dizzy when standing up quickly. Rise slowly from sitting or lying positions, and hold onto something stable until you are steady.\n\nExercise timing and your medication\n\nSome liver disease medications need to be taken with food, while others should be taken on an empty stomach. If you exercise in the morning, plan your medication and breakfast around your walk. A good pattern might be: wake up, take morning medications with a small snack if required, wait 30 minutes, then go for your walk. Have a proper breakfast when you return.\n\nIf you attend a liver clinic at Kenyatta National Hospital, Nairobi Hospital, or your county referral hospital, ask your doctor about exercise at your next appointment. They can advise on any specific precautions based on your liver function tests and overall condition. If your hospital has a nutritionist or physiotherapist, request a referral — even one session can give you a personalised plan to follow at home.\n\nRegularity over intensity\n\nThe single most important principle for exercise with liver disease is consistency. A daily 15-minute walk provides far more benefit than an occasional 45-minute session followed by days of recovery. Your liver heals through sustained, gentle support — not through dramatic effort. Show up for your walk today, tomorrow, and the day after. The cumulative effect of those regular, gentle sessions is what will make the difference in your liver health, your energy levels, and your quality of life.\n\nYou are not asking your body to run a marathon. You are asking it to move, gently and regularly, in a way that supports healing. That is something you can do.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you are living with liver disease, you know fatigue all too well. It is not ordinary tiredness that a good night''s sleep can fix — it is a deep, persistent exhaustion that makes even simple activities feel overwhelming. Getting out of bed takes effort. Walking to the shops feels like a marathon. The idea of exercise might seem not just difficult but absurd. Why would you push a body that is already struggling to get through the day?\\n\\nThe answer may surprise you: gentle, regular movement is one of the best things you can do for your liver, even when — especially when — fatigue is your biggest challenge."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why exercise helps a damaged liver","body":"Your liver plays a central role in how your body processes energy, manages fat, and handles insulin. When your liver is damaged, these processes become less efficient. Fat accumulates in and around the liver (even in non-alcoholic fatty liver disease), insulin resistance develops, and your body becomes trapped in a cycle of inflammation, fatigue, and metabolic dysfunction.\\n\\nGentle exercise breaks this cycle at multiple points. It improves blood flow to the liver, helping it receive oxygen and nutrients for repair. It reduces insulin resistance, which eases one of the major burdens on a struggling liver. It helps burn stored fat, including the fat deposits in your liver that worsen inflammation. And it releases endorphins — your body''s natural mood lifters — which combat the depression and low motivation that often accompany chronic liver disease.\\n\\nStudies have shown that regular moderate exercise can actually reduce liver fat by 10 to 20 percent, even without weight loss. This means that even if the number on the scale does not change, your liver is getting healthier with each walk you take."},{"id":"s3","title":"Starting from zero","body":"If you have been mostly inactive because of fatigue, the key is starting at a level so gentle that it feels almost too easy. This is not about pushing through pain or exhausting yourself — it is about teaching your body that movement is safe and beneficial.\\n\\nWeek one and two: Walk for 10 minutes at a gentle pace. Choose a flat route — perhaps around your compound, along your estate''s road, or to the nearest duka and back. Walk slowly enough that you can speak comfortably. If 10 minutes feels like too much, walk for five. Do this five days a week, resting on two days.\\n\\nWeek three and four: Increase to 12 to 13 minutes. You are adding just two to three minutes, but this gradual increase is important. It allows your body to adapt without triggering the kind of exhaustion that makes you want to quit entirely.\\n\\nWeek five and six: Aim for 15 minutes. By now, your body should be adjusting to the routine. You may notice that the fatigue after walking is less severe than it was in the first week, and that you recover faster.\\n\\nWeek seven and eight: Increase to 18 to 20 minutes. If you are feeling good, try picking up the pace slightly — not fast, just a bit brisker than your comfortable stroll.\\n\\nThe long-term goal is 20 to 30 minutes of gentle walking most days of the week. Getting there might take two months or it might take four — there is no rush. The pace of progression depends on the severity of your liver disease, your overall fitness, and how your body responds."},{"id":"s4","title":"A gentle home yoga and stretching routine","body":"On days when walking feels too demanding, or as a complement to your walking routine, try this gentle stretching and yoga sequence. You need only a mat, a folded blanket, or a leso spread on the floor.\\n\\nSeated breathing: Sit cross-legged on the floor or in a chair. Place your hands on your knees. Close your eyes and breathe deeply through your nose — inhale for four counts, hold for two counts, exhale for six counts. Do this for two to three minutes. Deep breathing improves oxygen delivery to your liver and activates your body''s relaxation response.\\n\\nGentle neck rolls: Slowly roll your head in a circle — right ear toward right shoulder, chin toward chest, left ear toward left shoulder, and back. Do five circles in each direction. This releases tension that builds up from fatigue-related muscle stiffness.\\n\\nSeated spinal twist: Sit upright in a chair. Place your right hand on your left knee and gently twist your upper body to the left, looking over your left shoulder. Hold for 15 seconds, breathing deeply. This gentle compression and release is believed to promote blood flow to the abdominal organs, including the liver. Repeat on the other side.\\n\\nCat and cow stretch: Get on your hands and knees. On an inhale, drop your belly toward the floor and lift your head (cow pose). On an exhale, round your back toward the ceiling and tuck your chin (cat pose). Move slowly between these positions 10 times. This mobilises your spine and gently massages your internal organs.\\n\\nLegs up the wall: Lie on your back near a wall and rest your legs vertically against the wall. Stay here for three to five minutes, breathing deeply. This restorative position improves circulation and reduces swelling in the legs, which can be an issue for people with advanced liver disease.\\n\\nChild''s pose: Kneel on the floor, sit back on your heels, and fold forward with your arms extended in front of you and your forehead resting on the mat. Stay here for one to two minutes, breathing deeply. This is a resting pose that promotes relaxation and gently stretches the back.\\n\\nThe entire routine takes about 15 to 20 minutes and can be done at any time of day. Many people find it helpful before bed, as it promotes better sleep."},{"id":"s5","title":"Important precautions for liver disease patients","body":"Avoid intense exercise if you have advanced liver disease (cirrhosis with complications). Your body''s ability to regulate energy, blood sugar, and clotting may be impaired. Signs that you are pushing too hard include extreme breathlessness, dizziness, nausea, pain in your upper right abdomen (where your liver is), or unusual bruising.\\n\\nIf you have ascites (fluid buildup in your abdomen), exercise choices need to be modified. Walking is generally safe, but exercises that require lying flat may be uncomfortable or affect your breathing. Sit-up-type exercises should be avoided. Consult your doctor about what is safe for your specific situation.\\n\\nStay hydrated during exercise. Drink water before, during, and after your walk or stretching session. However, if your doctor has placed you on fluid restriction due to ascites or other complications, follow their guidance on fluid intake.\\n\\nIf you take blood pressure medication or diuretics (water tablets), you may feel dizzy when standing up quickly. Rise slowly from sitting or lying positions, and hold onto something stable until you are steady."},{"id":"s6","title":"Exercise timing and your medication","body":"Some liver disease medications need to be taken with food, while others should be taken on an empty stomach. If you exercise in the morning, plan your medication and breakfast around your walk. A good pattern might be: wake up, take morning medications with a small snack if required, wait 30 minutes, then go for your walk. Have a proper breakfast when you return.\\n\\nIf you attend a liver clinic at Kenyatta National Hospital, Nairobi Hospital, or your county referral hospital, ask your doctor about exercise at your next appointment. They can advise on any specific precautions based on your liver function tests and overall condition. If your hospital has a nutritionist or physiotherapist, request a referral — even one session can give you a personalised plan to follow at home."},{"id":"s7","title":"Regularity over intensity","body":"The single most important principle for exercise with liver disease is consistency. A daily 15-minute walk provides far more benefit than an occasional 45-minute session followed by days of recovery. Your liver heals through sustained, gentle support — not through dramatic effort. Show up for your walk today, tomorrow, and the day after. The cumulative effect of those regular, gentle sessions is what will make the difference in your liver health, your energy levels, and your quality of life.\\n\\nYou are not asking your body to run a marathon. You are asking it to move, gently and regularly, in a way that supports healing. That is something you can do."}]'::jsonb, 9, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'yoga-pose'),
  (E'myth-liver-disease-only-happens-to-heavy-drinkers', E'Myth: Liver disease only happens to heavy drinkers', E'LIVER_DISEASE', '{"LIVER_DISEASE"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The myth that liver disease only happens to heavy drinkers is one of the most dangerous misconceptions in healthcare today. It stops people from getting tested, delays diagnosis, and allows preventable liver damage to progress silently for years.', E'The myth that liver disease only happens to heavy drinkers is one of the most dangerous misconceptions in healthcare today. It stops people from getting tested, delays diagnosis, and allows preventable liver damage to progress silently for years. The truth is far more complex, and understanding it could save your life or the life of someone you love.\n\nWhat the science actually shows\n\nNon-alcoholic fatty liver disease, known as NAFLD, is now the most common form of liver disease worldwide. It has nothing to do with alcohol. NAFLD is driven by obesity, type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance, and diets high in processed foods and added sugars. When fat accumulates inside liver cells beyond a certain threshold, it triggers inflammation that can eventually lead to scarring, cirrhosis, and even liver cancer. A person who has never touched a drop of alcohol can develop severe liver disease.\n\nWhy this matters in Kenya\n\nKenya is experiencing a rapid rise in lifestyle diseases. Diabetes, hypertension, and obesity rates have climbed steadily over the past two decades, particularly in urban areas. As diets shift from traditional meals based on vegetables, legumes, and whole grains toward processed foods, fried snacks, sugary drinks, and fast food, the liver bears much of the burden. A plate of deep-fried chips and sausage washed down with a 500ml soda puts significant stress on your liver, even if you never visit a bar. The growing availability of cheap packaged snacks and sweetened beverages at every kiosk across the country means more Kenyans are unknowingly putting their livers at risk.\n\nHepatitis B and C are also major causes of liver disease in East Africa. These viral infections attack the liver directly and can lead to chronic liver damage, cirrhosis, and liver cancer over time. Kenya has a significant burden of hepatitis B, and many people carry the virus without knowing it because the early stages produce no symptoms. You can contract hepatitis B through contact with infected blood or bodily fluids, and hepatitis C through contaminated needles or medical equipment. Neither has anything to do with drinking.\n\nOther causes people overlook\n\nCertain medications, including common painkillers like paracetamol taken in high doses over time, can damage the liver. Some herbal supplements and traditional remedies that are widely used in Kenya can also be toxic to the liver, particularly when taken in large quantities or combined with prescription drugs. Autoimmune conditions, where the body''s immune system mistakenly attacks liver cells, are another cause that has absolutely no connection to alcohol.\n\nWhy this myth is harmful\n\nWhen people believe liver disease equals alcoholism, several dangerous things happen. First, non-drinkers assume they are safe and never get screened, even when they have risk factors like diabetes, obesity, or a family history of hepatitis. Second, people who do develop liver disease face intense stigma. Friends, family, and even some healthcare workers may assume the person has been drinking secretly. This shame can prevent people from seeking treatment, disclosing their diagnosis, or asking for support. Third, alcohol-related liver disease itself deserves medical treatment, not moral judgement. Addiction is a medical condition, and people struggling with it need help, not blame.\n\nWhat you should do\n\nIf you have diabetes, are overweight, or have a family history of hepatitis, ask your doctor about liver function tests. A simple blood test measuring ALT and AST enzymes can reveal whether your liver is under stress. An ultrasound can check for fat deposits in the liver. These tests are available at most county and referral hospitals in Kenya and at private facilities. Many are covered under SHA. Early detection of fatty liver disease is critical because the condition is reversible in its early stages through diet changes, exercise, and weight management. Once scarring sets in, the damage becomes permanent.\n\nProtect your liver by reducing your intake of fried foods, sugary drinks, and processed snacks. Choose meals built around vegetables, legumes like lentils and beans, and whole grains. Limit paracetamol use and always follow dosage instructions. Get tested for hepatitis B, and if you have not been vaccinated, ask your doctor about the hepatitis B vaccine, which is safe, effective, and widely available in Kenya.\n\nYour liver works silently every day, filtering toxins, processing nutrients, and producing proteins your body needs. It deserves your attention regardless of whether you drink alcohol or not.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The myth that liver disease only happens to heavy drinkers is one of the most dangerous misconceptions in healthcare today. It stops people from getting tested, delays diagnosis, and allows preventable liver damage to progress silently for years. The truth is far more complex, and understanding it could save your life or the life of someone you love."},{"id":"s2","title":"What the science actually shows","body":"Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, known as NAFLD, is now the most common form of liver disease worldwide. It has nothing to do with alcohol. NAFLD is driven by obesity, type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance, and diets high in processed foods and added sugars. When fat accumulates inside liver cells beyond a certain threshold, it triggers inflammation that can eventually lead to scarring, cirrhosis, and even liver cancer. A person who has never touched a drop of alcohol can develop severe liver disease."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why this matters in Kenya","body":"Kenya is experiencing a rapid rise in lifestyle diseases. Diabetes, hypertension, and obesity rates have climbed steadily over the past two decades, particularly in urban areas. As diets shift from traditional meals based on vegetables, legumes, and whole grains toward processed foods, fried snacks, sugary drinks, and fast food, the liver bears much of the burden. A plate of deep-fried chips and sausage washed down with a 500ml soda puts significant stress on your liver, even if you never visit a bar. The growing availability of cheap packaged snacks and sweetened beverages at every kiosk across the country means more Kenyans are unknowingly putting their livers at risk.\\n\\nHepatitis B and C are also major causes of liver disease in East Africa. These viral infections attack the liver directly and can lead to chronic liver damage, cirrhosis, and liver cancer over time. Kenya has a significant burden of hepatitis B, and many people carry the virus without knowing it because the early stages produce no symptoms. You can contract hepatitis B through contact with infected blood or bodily fluids, and hepatitis C through contaminated needles or medical equipment. Neither has anything to do with drinking."},{"id":"s4","title":"Other causes people overlook","body":"Certain medications, including common painkillers like paracetamol taken in high doses over time, can damage the liver. Some herbal supplements and traditional remedies that are widely used in Kenya can also be toxic to the liver, particularly when taken in large quantities or combined with prescription drugs. Autoimmune conditions, where the body''s immune system mistakenly attacks liver cells, are another cause that has absolutely no connection to alcohol."},{"id":"s5","title":"Why this myth is harmful","body":"When people believe liver disease equals alcoholism, several dangerous things happen. First, non-drinkers assume they are safe and never get screened, even when they have risk factors like diabetes, obesity, or a family history of hepatitis. Second, people who do develop liver disease face intense stigma. Friends, family, and even some healthcare workers may assume the person has been drinking secretly. This shame can prevent people from seeking treatment, disclosing their diagnosis, or asking for support. Third, alcohol-related liver disease itself deserves medical treatment, not moral judgement. Addiction is a medical condition, and people struggling with it need help, not blame."},{"id":"s6","title":"What you should do","body":"If you have diabetes, are overweight, or have a family history of hepatitis, ask your doctor about liver function tests. A simple blood test measuring ALT and AST enzymes can reveal whether your liver is under stress. An ultrasound can check for fat deposits in the liver. These tests are available at most county and referral hospitals in Kenya and at private facilities. Many are covered under SHA. Early detection of fatty liver disease is critical because the condition is reversible in its early stages through diet changes, exercise, and weight management. Once scarring sets in, the damage becomes permanent.\\n\\nProtect your liver by reducing your intake of fried foods, sugary drinks, and processed snacks. Choose meals built around vegetables, legumes like lentils and beans, and whole grains. Limit paracetamol use and always follow dosage instructions. Get tested for hepatitis B, and if you have not been vaccinated, ask your doctor about the hepatitis B vaccine, which is safe, effective, and widely available in Kenya.\\n\\nYour liver works silently every day, filtering toxins, processing nutrients, and producing proteins your body needs. It deserves your attention regardless of whether you drink alcohol or not."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'medical-truth'),
  (E'the-shame-around-liver-disease-and-why-it-is-misplaced', E'The shame around liver disease — and why it is misplaced', E'LIVER_DISEASE', '{"LIVER_DISEASE"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'Few diagnoses carry as much social weight as liver disease. The moment you mention your liver to someone, you can almost see the question forming behind their eyes.', E'Few diagnoses carry as much social weight as liver disease. The moment you mention your liver to someone, you can almost see the question forming behind their eyes. In Kenya, where social gatherings often revolve around food and drink, the assumption is swift and unspoken: this person must be a drinker. That assumption causes real harm, and it is time to address it openly.\n\nThe stigma is real and it hurts\n\nImagine being diagnosed with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease after years of managing diabetes, only to have a relative whisper to others that you have been drinking in secret. Or picture a young mother with hepatitis B, contracted during a medical procedure, who cannot bring herself to tell her family about her liver condition because she fears they will judge her. These are not hypothetical situations. They happen every day across Kenya. The stigma around liver disease is so deeply embedded that some patients avoid picking up their medication from the pharmacy, worried about what the person behind them in the queue might think.\n\nEven within healthcare settings, bias exists. Some patients report feeling that their concerns were dismissed or that they received less empathetic care once liver disease appeared on their file. This is unacceptable. Every patient deserves to be treated with dignity, regardless of what caused their condition.\n\nWhen alcohol is part of the story\n\nLet us also be honest about cases where alcohol did contribute to liver damage. Addiction is a medical condition with genetic, psychological, and social components. It is not a character flaw. In Kenya, the availability of cheap illicit brews, combined with economic stress, social pressure, and limited access to mental health services, means that many people struggle with alcohol without adequate support. A person whose liver disease is related to drinking is not less deserving of compassion, medical treatment, or emotional support. Shame does not cure addiction. In fact, shame is one of the biggest barriers to recovery. When someone feels judged and isolated, they are more likely to continue harmful behaviour, not less.\n\nIf alcohol has played a role in your liver condition, you do not need to carry guilt alone. Speak with your doctor about support options. Many county hospitals now have substance use counsellors, and organisations like NACADA offer resources. Recovery is possible, and seeking help is a sign of strength.\n\nYou do not owe anyone an explanation\n\nHere is something important to understand: your medical diagnosis is your private information. You are not obligated to explain to anyone why you have liver disease. You do not need to prove to relatives, colleagues, or neighbours that you are not a drinker. If you choose to share your diagnosis, do it on your terms, with people you trust, when you are ready.\n\nFor those moments when someone makes an assumption, it can help to have a simple response prepared. Something like, "Liver disease has many causes, and mine is not related to alcohol" is enough. You do not need to educate everyone, but having a calm, clear response ready can reduce the emotional toll of repeated assumptions.\n\nBuilding your support system\n\nLiving with liver disease is easier when you have people in your corner. Identify one or two trusted individuals, whether a spouse, sibling, close friend, or your Jireh Circle, and share what you are going through. Let them know how they can help, whether that means accompanying you to appointments, helping you stick to dietary changes, or simply listening when you need to talk.\n\nIf the emotional weight feels too heavy, consider speaking with a professional counsellor. Many hospitals in Kenya offer counselling services as part of chronic disease management programmes. Your Jireh app can also connect you with support resources. There is no shame in needing help. Managing a chronic condition is demanding, and doing it while carrying the additional burden of stigma makes it even harder.\n\nPractical steps for emotional wellbeing\n\nKeep a simple journal where you note how you are feeling each day, even just one or two words. Over time, this helps you recognise patterns, such as feeling low after certain social interactions or anxious before clinic visits, and develop strategies to cope. Stay connected to activities that bring you joy, whether that is cooking a favourite meal, attending your place of worship, spending time with your children, or taking an evening walk. Your diagnosis is part of your life, but it is not your entire life.\n\nRemind yourself regularly that you are doing something meaningful by following your treatment plan and taking care of your health. That takes courage, especially in the face of misunderstanding. You deserve respect, support, and quality care. Do not let anyone''s assumptions make you feel otherwise.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Few diagnoses carry as much social weight as liver disease. The moment you mention your liver to someone, you can almost see the question forming behind their eyes. In Kenya, where social gatherings often revolve around food and drink, the assumption is swift and unspoken: this person must be a drinker. That assumption causes real harm, and it is time to address it openly."},{"id":"s2","title":"The stigma is real and it hurts","body":"Imagine being diagnosed with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease after years of managing diabetes, only to have a relative whisper to others that you have been drinking in secret. Or picture a young mother with hepatitis B, contracted during a medical procedure, who cannot bring herself to tell her family about her liver condition because she fears they will judge her. These are not hypothetical situations. They happen every day across Kenya. The stigma around liver disease is so deeply embedded that some patients avoid picking up their medication from the pharmacy, worried about what the person behind them in the queue might think.\\n\\nEven within healthcare settings, bias exists. Some patients report feeling that their concerns were dismissed or that they received less empathetic care once liver disease appeared on their file. This is unacceptable. Every patient deserves to be treated with dignity, regardless of what caused their condition."},{"id":"s3","title":"When alcohol is part of the story","body":"Let us also be honest about cases where alcohol did contribute to liver damage. Addiction is a medical condition with genetic, psychological, and social components. It is not a character flaw. In Kenya, the availability of cheap illicit brews, combined with economic stress, social pressure, and limited access to mental health services, means that many people struggle with alcohol without adequate support. A person whose liver disease is related to drinking is not less deserving of compassion, medical treatment, or emotional support. Shame does not cure addiction. In fact, shame is one of the biggest barriers to recovery. When someone feels judged and isolated, they are more likely to continue harmful behaviour, not less.\\n\\nIf alcohol has played a role in your liver condition, you do not need to carry guilt alone. Speak with your doctor about support options. Many county hospitals now have substance use counsellors, and organisations like NACADA offer resources. Recovery is possible, and seeking help is a sign of strength."},{"id":"s4","title":"You do not owe anyone an explanation","body":"Here is something important to understand: your medical diagnosis is your private information. You are not obligated to explain to anyone why you have liver disease. You do not need to prove to relatives, colleagues, or neighbours that you are not a drinker. If you choose to share your diagnosis, do it on your terms, with people you trust, when you are ready.\\n\\nFor those moments when someone makes an assumption, it can help to have a simple response prepared. Something like, \\"Liver disease has many causes, and mine is not related to alcohol\\" is enough. You do not need to educate everyone, but having a calm, clear response ready can reduce the emotional toll of repeated assumptions."},{"id":"s5","title":"Building your support system","body":"Living with liver disease is easier when you have people in your corner. Identify one or two trusted individuals, whether a spouse, sibling, close friend, or your Jireh Circle, and share what you are going through. Let them know how they can help, whether that means accompanying you to appointments, helping you stick to dietary changes, or simply listening when you need to talk.\\n\\nIf the emotional weight feels too heavy, consider speaking with a professional counsellor. Many hospitals in Kenya offer counselling services as part of chronic disease management programmes. Your Jireh app can also connect you with support resources. There is no shame in needing help. Managing a chronic condition is demanding, and doing it while carrying the additional burden of stigma makes it even harder."},{"id":"s6","title":"Practical steps for emotional wellbeing","body":"Keep a simple journal where you note how you are feeling each day, even just one or two words. Over time, this helps you recognise patterns, such as feeling low after certain social interactions or anxious before clinic visits, and develop strategies to cope. Stay connected to activities that bring you joy, whether that is cooking a favourite meal, attending your place of worship, spending time with your children, or taking an evening walk. Your diagnosis is part of your life, but it is not your entire life.\\n\\nRemind yourself regularly that you are doing something meaningful by following your treatment plan and taking care of your health. That takes courage, especially in the face of misunderstanding. You deserve respect, support, and quality care. Do not let anyone''s assumptions make you feel otherwise."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'community-care'),
  (E'signs-your-liver-needs-urgent-attention', E'Signs your liver needs urgent attention', E'LIVER_DISEASE', '{"LIVER_DISEASE"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'Your liver is one of the most resilient organs in your body, but when it is struggling, it sends signals. Learning to recognise these signals and knowing when to act can be the difference between catching a problem early and ending up in an emergency ward.', E'Your liver is one of the most resilient organs in your body, but when it is struggling, it sends signals. Learning to recognise these signals and knowing when to act can be the difference between catching a problem early and ending up in an emergency ward. Between your scheduled clinic visits, you are the first line of defence for your own health.\n\nWarning signs to watch for daily\n\nGet into the habit of checking yourself briefly each morning. It only takes a minute and can alert you to changes before they become serious.\n\nJaundice is one of the most visible signs that your liver is not coping. Look at the whites of your eyes in natural daylight, not under artificial light which can distort colour. If they appear yellowish, or if your skin takes on a yellow tint, this means bilirubin is building up in your blood because your liver cannot process it properly. Jaundice that develops suddenly or worsens over days needs medical attention within 24 hours.\n\nMonitor the colour of your urine and stools. Dark brown or tea-coloured urine, when you know you have been drinking enough water, can indicate liver stress. Pale, clay-coloured, or greyish stools suggest that bile is not reaching your digestive system as it should. Keep a mental note of any changes and mention them at your next appointment, or seek care sooner if the change is dramatic.\n\nAbdominal swelling and fluid retention\n\nAscites, the medical term for fluid accumulation in the abdomen, is a sign of advancing liver disease. If your belly feels increasingly tight, swollen, or uncomfortable, and especially if it is growing larger over days or weeks, contact your doctor. You might notice that your clothes no longer fit around the middle, or that pressing on your abdomen leaves a temporary dent. Swelling in your ankles and feet can also occur as fluid builds up in the body. Measure your waist at the same time each morning using a tape measure and record the number. An increase of more than two centimetres over a week warrants a call to your healthcare provider.\n\nItching that will not stop\n\nPersistent, widespread itching without a visible rash can be caused by bile salts accumulating under the skin when the liver is not functioning well. This kind of itching is different from a mosquito bite or a skin allergy. It tends to be worse at night and can affect your sleep. Over-the-counter antihistamines may provide some relief, but the underlying cause needs to be addressed by your doctor. Do not ignore itching that continues for more than a few days.\n\nBruising and bleeding\n\nYour liver produces proteins that help your blood clot. When liver function declines, you may bruise more easily or notice that small cuts take longer to stop bleeding. If you find unexplained bruises appearing on your body, or if your gums bleed frequently when brushing your teeth, note these symptoms and report them. This is important information for your medical team.\n\nThe emergency sign you must never ignore\n\nHepatic encephalopathy is a serious complication where toxins that the liver normally filters out of the blood accumulate and affect the brain. The early signs are subtle: confusion, difficulty concentrating, forgetfulness, personality changes, or unusual sleepiness during the day. Family members often notice these changes before the patient does. If someone with liver disease suddenly becomes confused, disoriented, or unusually drowsy, treat it as a medical emergency. Do not wait to see if it passes. Get to the nearest hospital immediately, whether that is Kenyatta National Hospital, your county referral hospital, or the closest facility with an emergency department. Call an ambulance if available, or arrange the fastest transport you can.\n\nKeeping a symptom log\n\nBuy a small notebook, or use the notes app on your phone, to keep a brief daily log. Each day, record in just a few words: how you feel overall, any symptoms you noticed, what you ate, and whether you took your medication. This log becomes incredibly valuable at your doctor''s appointments. Instead of trying to remember weeks of symptoms, you can show the doctor a clear record. Doctors at busy facilities like those in Nairobi, Mombasa, or Kisumu often have limited time per patient, so a written summary helps them make faster, better decisions about your care.\n\nWhen to call your doctor versus going to hospital\n\nCall your doctor or visit the clinic within a day or two if you notice: mild jaundice, changes in urine or stool colour, new or worsening itching, increased bruising, or mild ankle swelling. Go to hospital immediately if you experience: sudden confusion or disorientation, vomiting blood or passing black tarry stools, rapid abdominal swelling, high fever with abdominal pain, or severe jaundice that appeared suddenly.\n\nYour regular check-ups and blood tests\n\nIn addition to daily self-monitoring, keep all your scheduled appointments for liver function tests. These blood tests, which typically cost between KES 1,500 and KES 3,000 at private labs or are available at subsidised rates at public hospitals, measure enzymes and proteins that reveal how well your liver is working. Your doctor will track trends over time. Even if you feel fine, these numbers can detect problems before symptoms appear. SHA may cover some or all of these tests depending on your plan, so ask at your facility.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Your liver is one of the most resilient organs in your body, but when it is struggling, it sends signals. Learning to recognise these signals and knowing when to act can be the difference between catching a problem early and ending up in an emergency ward. Between your scheduled clinic visits, you are the first line of defence for your own health."},{"id":"s2","title":"Warning signs to watch for daily","body":"Get into the habit of checking yourself briefly each morning. It only takes a minute and can alert you to changes before they become serious.\\n\\nJaundice is one of the most visible signs that your liver is not coping. Look at the whites of your eyes in natural daylight, not under artificial light which can distort colour. If they appear yellowish, or if your skin takes on a yellow tint, this means bilirubin is building up in your blood because your liver cannot process it properly. Jaundice that develops suddenly or worsens over days needs medical attention within 24 hours.\\n\\nMonitor the colour of your urine and stools. Dark brown or tea-coloured urine, when you know you have been drinking enough water, can indicate liver stress. Pale, clay-coloured, or greyish stools suggest that bile is not reaching your digestive system as it should. Keep a mental note of any changes and mention them at your next appointment, or seek care sooner if the change is dramatic."},{"id":"s3","title":"Abdominal swelling and fluid retention","body":"Ascites, the medical term for fluid accumulation in the abdomen, is a sign of advancing liver disease. If your belly feels increasingly tight, swollen, or uncomfortable, and especially if it is growing larger over days or weeks, contact your doctor. You might notice that your clothes no longer fit around the middle, or that pressing on your abdomen leaves a temporary dent. Swelling in your ankles and feet can also occur as fluid builds up in the body. Measure your waist at the same time each morning using a tape measure and record the number. An increase of more than two centimetres over a week warrants a call to your healthcare provider."},{"id":"s4","title":"Itching that will not stop","body":"Persistent, widespread itching without a visible rash can be caused by bile salts accumulating under the skin when the liver is not functioning well. This kind of itching is different from a mosquito bite or a skin allergy. It tends to be worse at night and can affect your sleep. Over-the-counter antihistamines may provide some relief, but the underlying cause needs to be addressed by your doctor. Do not ignore itching that continues for more than a few days."},{"id":"s5","title":"Bruising and bleeding","body":"Your liver produces proteins that help your blood clot. When liver function declines, you may bruise more easily or notice that small cuts take longer to stop bleeding. If you find unexplained bruises appearing on your body, or if your gums bleed frequently when brushing your teeth, note these symptoms and report them. This is important information for your medical team."},{"id":"s6","title":"The emergency sign you must never ignore","body":"Hepatic encephalopathy is a serious complication where toxins that the liver normally filters out of the blood accumulate and affect the brain. The early signs are subtle: confusion, difficulty concentrating, forgetfulness, personality changes, or unusual sleepiness during the day. Family members often notice these changes before the patient does. If someone with liver disease suddenly becomes confused, disoriented, or unusually drowsy, treat it as a medical emergency. Do not wait to see if it passes. Get to the nearest hospital immediately, whether that is Kenyatta National Hospital, your county referral hospital, or the closest facility with an emergency department. Call an ambulance if available, or arrange the fastest transport you can."},{"id":"s7","title":"Keeping a symptom log","body":"Buy a small notebook, or use the notes app on your phone, to keep a brief daily log. Each day, record in just a few words: how you feel overall, any symptoms you noticed, what you ate, and whether you took your medication. This log becomes incredibly valuable at your doctor''s appointments. Instead of trying to remember weeks of symptoms, you can show the doctor a clear record. Doctors at busy facilities like those in Nairobi, Mombasa, or Kisumu often have limited time per patient, so a written summary helps them make faster, better decisions about your care."},{"id":"s8","title":"When to call your doctor versus going to hospital","body":"Call your doctor or visit the clinic within a day or two if you notice: mild jaundice, changes in urine or stool colour, new or worsening itching, increased bruising, or mild ankle swelling. Go to hospital immediately if you experience: sudden confusion or disorientation, vomiting blood or passing black tarry stools, rapid abdominal swelling, high fever with abdominal pain, or severe jaundice that appeared suddenly."},{"id":"s9","title":"Your regular check-ups and blood tests","body":"In addition to daily self-monitoring, keep all your scheduled appointments for liver function tests. These blood tests, which typically cost between KES 1,500 and KES 3,000 at private labs or are available at subsidised rates at public hospitals, measure enzymes and proteins that reveal how well your liver is working. Your doctor will track trends over time. Even if you feel fine, these numbers can detect problems before symptoms appear. SHA may cover some or all of these tests depending on your plan, so ask at your facility."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Track your key health indicators effectively at home","Understand what your numbers mean","Know when to contact your healthcare provider"}', E'medication-tracking'),
  (E'living-fully-with-a-liver-condition', E'Living fully with a liver condition', E'LIVER_DISEASE', '{"LIVER_DISEASE"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'Hearing that you have a liver condition can feel like the ground has shifted beneath you. The liver is one of those organs that most people never think about until something goes wrong, and suddenly it is at the centre of everything.', E'Hearing that you have a liver condition can feel like the ground has shifted beneath you. The liver is one of those organs that most people never think about until something goes wrong, and suddenly it is at the centre of everything. You may feel frightened, confused, or even numb. All of those reactions are completely valid, and they are the starting point of a journey that millions of people navigate successfully.\n\nThe fear behind the diagnosis\n\nLiver disease sounds severe, and in some cases it is. But the spectrum is wide. Some people have early-stage fatty liver that is entirely reversible with lifestyle changes. Others are managing chronic hepatitis with medication that keeps the virus suppressed. Some are living with compensated cirrhosis, where the liver is scarred but still functioning well enough to support a normal life. Where you fall on this spectrum matters, and your doctor can help you understand your specific situation. Do not let fear of the worst-case scenario prevent you from learning the facts about your particular condition.\n\nIn Kenya, where chronic disease management is still developing in many areas, you might feel that the system is not set up to support you. Specialist hepatologists are concentrated in Nairobi and a few other major cities. Follow-up care can be inconsistent. Medications can be expensive. These are real challenges, but they are not reasons to give up. Many county hospitals now have trained physicians who can manage liver conditions, and referral pathways to specialists are improving. SHA coverage continues to expand, and your Jireh Wallet and Care Saver can help bridge gaps in treatment costs.\n\nYour liver is remarkable\n\nHere is something that might give you hope: the liver is the only organ in the human body that can regenerate. If you have early to moderate liver damage, your liver can actually repair itself when you remove the source of harm and give it the right conditions. This means that the lifestyle changes you make today are not just slowing damage, they are actively enabling healing. Stopping alcohol, losing excess weight, managing your blood sugar, eating nourishing food, and taking your medications as prescribed all contribute to this process.\n\nEven in cases of more advanced disease, the liver often retains enough function to support a good quality of life for many years. The key is consistent management, regular monitoring, and a collaborative relationship with your healthcare team.\n\nPractical daily adjustments\n\nLiving with a liver condition does require some changes, but they do not have to be dramatic. Start with your diet. Your liver processes everything you eat and drink, so giving it less work to do is one of the best things you can do. Reduce fried foods, and when you do cook, use small amounts of healthy oils like sunflower or olive oil. Build your meals around vegetables like sukuma wiki, spinach, managu, and cabbage. Choose lean proteins such as fish, chicken, or beans over fatty cuts of meat. Limit your intake of nyama choma, which is high in fat, to occasional treats rather than weekly staples. Reduce sugar by cutting back on sodas, sweetened tea, and mandazi.\n\nIf your doctor has advised you to stop alcohol completely, take that advice seriously even if you were not a heavy drinker. A damaged liver processes alcohol much more slowly, and even small amounts can cause disproportionate harm. If cutting out alcohol feels socially difficult, especially in a culture where sharing a drink is part of hospitality, prepare yourself with alternatives. Order a soda water with lemon, a fresh juice, or simply say you are on medication that does not mix with alcohol. Most people will respect that without pressing further.\n\nStay active within your limits. A daily walk of 20 to 30 minutes helps reduce fat in the liver, improves insulin sensitivity, and lifts your mood. You do not need a gym membership. Walking in your neighbourhood, doing light stretching at home, or gardening all count. Listen to your body and rest when you need to.\n\nBuilding your long-term plan\n\nWork with your doctor to establish a clear monitoring schedule. This typically includes blood tests for liver enzymes every three to six months, periodic ultrasound scans, and regular check-ups. Write down the dates and set reminders on your phone. Keep a folder, physical or digital, with all your test results so you and your doctor can track trends over time.\n\nTake your medications exactly as prescribed. If cost is a concern, discuss this openly with your doctor. There may be alternative medications, patient assistance programmes, or ways to use your Jireh benefits to manage the expense. Never skip doses or stop treatment without consulting your doctor first, even if you feel well. Liver disease can progress silently.\n\nYou are not your diagnosis\n\nA liver condition is something you have, not something you are. You are still a parent, a professional, a friend, a community member. You still have goals, dreams, and a future worth planning for. Many people with liver conditions live full, productive, and joyful lives for decades. The adjustment period can be hard, and there will be difficult days. But each day that you show up for your health, take your medication, make a good food choice, or attend an appointment, you are building a life that is defined by resilience, not by illness.\n\nTake it one day at a time. Focus on what you can control. Let your medical team handle the complexities. And remember that asking for help, whether from family, friends, your Jireh Circle, or a professional counsellor, is not weakness. It is wisdom.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Hearing that you have a liver condition can feel like the ground has shifted beneath you. The liver is one of those organs that most people never think about until something goes wrong, and suddenly it is at the centre of everything. You may feel frightened, confused, or even numb. All of those reactions are completely valid, and they are the starting point of a journey that millions of people navigate successfully."},{"id":"s2","title":"The fear behind the diagnosis","body":"Liver disease sounds severe, and in some cases it is. But the spectrum is wide. Some people have early-stage fatty liver that is entirely reversible with lifestyle changes. Others are managing chronic hepatitis with medication that keeps the virus suppressed. Some are living with compensated cirrhosis, where the liver is scarred but still functioning well enough to support a normal life. Where you fall on this spectrum matters, and your doctor can help you understand your specific situation. Do not let fear of the worst-case scenario prevent you from learning the facts about your particular condition.\\n\\nIn Kenya, where chronic disease management is still developing in many areas, you might feel that the system is not set up to support you. Specialist hepatologists are concentrated in Nairobi and a few other major cities. Follow-up care can be inconsistent. Medications can be expensive. These are real challenges, but they are not reasons to give up. Many county hospitals now have trained physicians who can manage liver conditions, and referral pathways to specialists are improving. SHA coverage continues to expand, and your Jireh Wallet and Care Saver can help bridge gaps in treatment costs."},{"id":"s3","title":"Your liver is remarkable","body":"Here is something that might give you hope: the liver is the only organ in the human body that can regenerate. If you have early to moderate liver damage, your li