 your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'new-beginning'),
  (E'liver-enzymes-trending-down', E'Liver enzymes trending down', E'LIVER_DISEASE', '{"LIVER_DISEASE"}', E'MILESTONE', E'Your latest blood test shows that your ALT and AST levels have come down since the last time they were checked. This is genuinely good news, and you should take a moment to recognise what it means.', E'Your latest blood test shows that your ALT and AST levels have come down since the last time they were checked. This is genuinely good news, and you should take a moment to recognise what it means. These numbers are not just figures on a lab report. They are a direct reflection of the care you have been giving your body, and right now, they are telling you that it is working.\n\nWhat ALT and AST actually measure\n\nALT (alanine aminotransferase) and AST (aspartate aminotransferase) are enzymes found inside your liver cells. When liver cells are damaged or inflamed, these enzymes leak into your bloodstream in higher amounts. A blood test picks up these elevated levels, which is why your doctor orders liver function tests regularly. High ALT and AST numbers mean your liver is actively being harmed. Lower numbers mean less damage is occurring. It is that straightforward.\n\nNormal ALT levels are generally below 40 units per litre for men and below 32 for women, though your doctor may use slightly different reference ranges. AST follows a similar pattern. If your levels were elevated before and are now trending downward, your liver cells are experiencing less injury. The inflammation is cooling down. This is exactly what treatment and lifestyle changes are designed to achieve.\n\nWhat brought your numbers down\n\nThis improvement did not happen by accident. It is the result of specific choices you have been making, even on the days when those choices felt difficult. If you reduced or stopped alcohol, your liver has had the chance to recover without the constant assault of processing ethanol. If you changed your diet, cutting back on fried foods, reducing sugar, eating more vegetables and lean proteins, your liver has had less fat to process and store. If you started exercising, even just walking for 20 to 30 minutes a day, you have improved your body''s insulin sensitivity and helped reduce fat deposits in the liver.\n\nIf you have been taking your prescribed medications consistently, those drugs have been working to reduce inflammation, suppress a virus, or address whatever underlying cause is affecting your liver. Consistency with medication is one of the most powerful tools you have, and the fact that you have maintained it deserves real credit.\n\nWhy trending matters more than a single number\n\nOne blood test is a snapshot. Two or more blood tests showing a consistent direction tell a story. If your liver enzymes have come down across two or more tests, that trend is more meaningful than any single result. It suggests that the improvement is sustained, not a random fluctuation. Your doctor will be looking at this trend to assess whether your current treatment plan is working and whether any adjustments are needed.\n\nKeep all your lab results in one place. A simple folder, whether a physical file at home or photos on your phone, makes it easy to see how your numbers have changed over time. When you visit your doctor, bring these records. At busy hospitals in Kenya, patient files sometimes get misplaced or test results from different facilities do not connect. Being your own record-keeper ensures continuity of care.\n\nWhat this means for your liver long-term\n\nEvery month that your liver enzymes remain lower is a month where your liver is healing rather than deteriorating. The liver has a remarkable ability to repair itself when given the chance. Reduced inflammation means less risk of fibrosis, the scarring that occurs when damage is ongoing. Less fibrosis means a lower risk of progressing to cirrhosis, which is the advanced stage of scarring where liver function becomes seriously compromised. You are actively slowing or stopping this progression through your daily actions.\n\nFor people with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, sustained improvement in liver enzymes often correlates with fat reduction in the liver, which can be confirmed with an ultrasound. If your doctor orders one and it shows less fat than before, that is another concrete sign of improvement.\n\nKeep building on this momentum\n\nDo not let this good news make you complacent. The choices that brought your numbers down are the same choices that will keep them down. Continue eating balanced meals built around local foods like sukuma wiki, beans, lentils, fish, and whole grains. Keep your portions reasonable, especially with starchy foods like ugali and rice. Stay active with whatever form of movement works for you. Take your medication every day at the same time.\n\nIf you have been avoiding alcohol, stay the course. The temptation might increase when you feel better, but remember that your liver is still recovering. Reintroducing alcohol now would undo weeks or months of healing. If social situations make this challenging, lean on your support system, whether that is family, friends, or your Jireh Circle.\n\nYour next blood test is the next checkpoint. Set a reminder for the date your doctor recommended, and keep the appointment even if you feel well. Feeling good and having good numbers is the best possible combination, and confirming it with a test gives you and your doctor confidence that you are on the right path.\n\nCelebrate this progress. You earned it. Not everyone who gets this advice follows through, and you did. That discipline, that commitment to your own health, is something to be genuinely proud of.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Your latest blood test shows that your ALT and AST levels have come down since the last time they were checked. This is genuinely good news, and you should take a moment to recognise what it means. These numbers are not just figures on a lab report. They are a direct reflection of the care you have been giving your body, and right now, they are telling you that it is working."},{"id":"s2","title":"What ALT and AST actually measure","body":"ALT (alanine aminotransferase) and AST (aspartate aminotransferase) are enzymes found inside your liver cells. When liver cells are damaged or inflamed, these enzymes leak into your bloodstream in higher amounts. A blood test picks up these elevated levels, which is why your doctor orders liver function tests regularly. High ALT and AST numbers mean your liver is actively being harmed. Lower numbers mean less damage is occurring. It is that straightforward.\\n\\nNormal ALT levels are generally below 40 units per litre for men and below 32 for women, though your doctor may use slightly different reference ranges. AST follows a similar pattern. If your levels were elevated before and are now trending downward, your liver cells are experiencing less injury. The inflammation is cooling down. This is exactly what treatment and lifestyle changes are designed to achieve."},{"id":"s3","title":"What brought your numbers down","body":"This improvement did not happen by accident. It is the result of specific choices you have been making, even on the days when those choices felt difficult. If you reduced or stopped alcohol, your liver has had the chance to recover without the constant assault of processing ethanol. If you changed your diet, cutting back on fried foods, reducing sugar, eating more vegetables and lean proteins, your liver has had less fat to process and store. If you started exercising, even just walking for 20 to 30 minutes a day, you have improved your body''s insulin sensitivity and helped reduce fat deposits in the liver.\\n\\nIf you have been taking your prescribed medications consistently, those drugs have been working to reduce inflammation, suppress a virus, or address whatever underlying cause is affecting your liver. Consistency with medication is one of the most powerful tools you have, and the fact that you have maintained it deserves real credit."},{"id":"s4","title":"Why trending matters more than a single number","body":"One blood test is a snapshot. Two or more blood tests showing a consistent direction tell a story. If your liver enzymes have come down across two or more tests, that trend is more meaningful than any single result. It suggests that the improvement is sustained, not a random fluctuation. Your doctor will be looking at this trend to assess whether your current treatment plan is working and whether any adjustments are needed.\\n\\nKeep all your lab results in one place. A simple folder, whether a physical file at home or photos on your phone, makes it easy to see how your numbers have changed over time. When you visit your doctor, bring these records. At busy hospitals in Kenya, patient files sometimes get misplaced or test results from different facilities do not connect. Being your own record-keeper ensures continuity of care."},{"id":"s5","title":"What this means for your liver long-term","body":"Every month that your liver enzymes remain lower is a month where your liver is healing rather than deteriorating. The liver has a remarkable ability to repair itself when given the chance. Reduced inflammation means less risk of fibrosis, the scarring that occurs when damage is ongoing. Less fibrosis means a lower risk of progressing to cirrhosis, which is the advanced stage of scarring where liver function becomes seriously compromised. You are actively slowing or stopping this progression through your daily actions.\\n\\nFor people with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, sustained improvement in liver enzymes often correlates with fat reduction in the liver, which can be confirmed with an ultrasound. If your doctor orders one and it shows less fat than before, that is another concrete sign of improvement."},{"id":"s6","title":"Keep building on this momentum","body":"Do not let this good news make you complacent. The choices that brought your numbers down are the same choices that will keep them down. Continue eating balanced meals built around local foods like sukuma wiki, beans, lentils, fish, and whole grains. Keep your portions reasonable, especially with starchy foods like ugali and rice. Stay active with whatever form of movement works for you. Take your medication every day at the same time.\\n\\nIf you have been avoiding alcohol, stay the course. The temptation might increase when you feel better, but remember that your liver is still recovering. Reintroducing alcohol now would undo weeks or months of healing. If social situations make this challenging, lean on your support system, whether that is family, friends, or your Jireh Circle.\\n\\nYour next blood test is the next checkpoint. Set a reminder for the date your doctor recommended, and keep the appointment even if you feel well. Feeling good and having good numbers is the best possible combination, and confirming it with a test gives you and your doctor confidence that you are on the right path.\\n\\nCelebrate this progress. You earned it. Not everyone who gets this advice follows through, and you did. That discipline, that commitment to your own health, is something to be genuinely proud of."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise and celebrate the progress you have made","Understand what this milestone means for your health","Set meaningful goals for the next phase of your journey"}', E'wellness-journey'),
  (E'drinking-enough-water-on-a-busy-day', E'Drinking enough water on a busy day', E'GENERAL', '{"GENERAL"}', E'DIETARY', E'Water is the most underrated medicine available to you, and it costs almost nothing. Your body is roughly 60 percent water, and every system depends on it.', E'Water is the most underrated medicine available to you, and it costs almost nothing. Your body is roughly 60 percent water, and every system depends on it. Your kidneys use water to flush out waste products. Your blood needs water to flow smoothly and maintain healthy pressure. Your brain needs water to think clearly. When you are even mildly dehydrated, everything works harder and worse.\n\nWhy dehydration is especially risky with chronic conditions\n\nIf you are managing diabetes, dehydration concentrates sugar in your blood, making your readings higher than they need to be. If you have hypertension, dehydrated blood is thicker and your heart has to pump harder, raising your blood pressure. If you have kidney disease, your kidneys are already working under strain, and dehydration forces them to do more with less. Whatever condition you are managing, adequate water intake makes your medications work better and your body cope better.\n\nHow much water do you actually need\n\nAim for six to eight glasses per day, roughly 1.5 to 2 litres. If you are physically active, live in a hot area like the coast or northern Kenya, or take medications that increase urination such as some blood pressure drugs, you may need more. A simple check is the colour of your urine. Pale yellow means you are well hydrated. Dark yellow or amber means you need more water.\n\nPractical strategies for busy days\n\nThe biggest barrier to drinking enough water is not forgetting that it matters. It is forgetting to actually do it when you are busy with work, commuting, caring for children, or running errands.\n\nStart your day with a full glass of water before anything else. Keep it on your bedside table so it is the first thing you reach for in the morning. This replaces the fluid you lost overnight and gets your body going.\n\nCarry a reusable water bottle with you everywhere. A 500ml bottle fits easily into a bag, and your goal is to finish it three to four times during the day. You can buy a durable water bottle for around KES 200 to 500 at most supermarkets. Having water within arm''s reach makes you far more likely to drink it.\n\nLink water to your daily routines. Drink a glass before each meal, that is three glasses without thinking about it. Have a glass when you arrive at work and another when you leave. If you take medication at specific times, always take it with a full glass of water rather than just a sip.\n\nIf you use a matatu for your daily commute, carry your water bottle. Long rides, especially in Nairobi traffic, can leave you dehydrated without you noticing.\n\nMaking water more enjoyable\n\nIf plain water feels boring, slice a lemon, lime, or cucumber and add it to your water bottle. Fresh mint leaves also work well. These add subtle flavour without any sugar. You can prepare a jug of infused water in the morning and pour from it throughout the day.\n\nAvoid reaching for sodas, packaged fruit juices, or sweetened yoghurt drinks when you are thirsty. A 500ml bottle of soda contains about 12 teaspoons of sugar and does almost nothing for thirst. Packaged juices marketed as healthy often contain just as much sugar. If you enjoy fruit flavour, eat the actual fruit instead. A whole orange or a slice of watermelon gives you water, fibre, and vitamins without the sugar overload.\n\nWhat about tea and coffee\n\nGood news for tea lovers: your daily cups of chai count toward your fluid intake. Despite the old belief that caffeine dehydrates you, moderate consumption of two to three cups per day contributes positively to hydration. The key is to watch what you add. If you are used to three teaspoons of sugar per cup and drink four cups a day, that is 12 extra teaspoons of sugar daily. Gradually reduce to one teaspoon per cup, then try half. After a few weeks, your taste buds adjust.\n\nSigns you are not drinking enough\n\nBeyond dark urine, watch for frequent headaches especially in the afternoon, feeling unusually tired even after sleeping well, dry or cracked lips, dizziness when standing up, and constipation. Many people who complain of afternoon fatigue find that simply drinking more water resolves the problem within days.\n\nYour body performs every function better when properly hydrated. It is one of the simplest and most affordable things you can do for your health, and it supports every other effort you are making, from medication adherence to exercise to managing your blood sugar or blood pressure.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Water is the most underrated medicine available to you, and it costs almost nothing. Your body is roughly 60 percent water, and every system depends on it. Your kidneys use water to flush out waste products. Your blood needs water to flow smoothly and maintain healthy pressure. Your brain needs water to think clearly. When you are even mildly dehydrated, everything works harder and worse."},{"id":"s2","title":"Why dehydration is especially risky with chronic conditions","body":"If you are managing diabetes, dehydration concentrates sugar in your blood, making your readings higher than they need to be. If you have hypertension, dehydrated blood is thicker and your heart has to pump harder, raising your blood pressure. If you have kidney disease, your kidneys are already working under strain, and dehydration forces them to do more with less. Whatever condition you are managing, adequate water intake makes your medications work better and your body cope better."},{"id":"s3","title":"How much water do you actually need","body":"Aim for six to eight glasses per day, roughly 1.5 to 2 litres. If you are physically active, live in a hot area like the coast or northern Kenya, or take medications that increase urination such as some blood pressure drugs, you may need more. A simple check is the colour of your urine. Pale yellow means you are well hydrated. Dark yellow or amber means you need more water."},{"id":"s4","title":"Practical strategies for busy days","body":"The biggest barrier to drinking enough water is not forgetting that it matters. It is forgetting to actually do it when you are busy with work, commuting, caring for children, or running errands.\\n\\nStart your day with a full glass of water before anything else. Keep it on your bedside table so it is the first thing you reach for in the morning. This replaces the fluid you lost overnight and gets your body going.\\n\\nCarry a reusable water bottle with you everywhere. A 500ml bottle fits easily into a bag, and your goal is to finish it three to four times during the day. You can buy a durable water bottle for around KES 200 to 500 at most supermarkets. Having water within arm''s reach makes you far more likely to drink it.\\n\\nLink water to your daily routines. Drink a glass before each meal, that is three glasses without thinking about it. Have a glass when you arrive at work and another when you leave. If you take medication at specific times, always take it with a full glass of water rather than just a sip.\\n\\nIf you use a matatu for your daily commute, carry your water bottle. Long rides, especially in Nairobi traffic, can leave you dehydrated without you noticing."},{"id":"s5","title":"Making water more enjoyable","body":"If plain water feels boring, slice a lemon, lime, or cucumber and add it to your water bottle. Fresh mint leaves also work well. These add subtle flavour without any sugar. You can prepare a jug of infused water in the morning and pour from it throughout the day.\\n\\nAvoid reaching for sodas, packaged fruit juices, or sweetened yoghurt drinks when you are thirsty. A 500ml bottle of soda contains about 12 teaspoons of sugar and does almost nothing for thirst. Packaged juices marketed as healthy often contain just as much sugar. If you enjoy fruit flavour, eat the actual fruit instead. A whole orange or a slice of watermelon gives you water, fibre, and vitamins without the sugar overload."},{"id":"s6","title":"What about tea and coffee","body":"Good news for tea lovers: your daily cups of chai count toward your fluid intake. Despite the old belief that caffeine dehydrates you, moderate consumption of two to three cups per day contributes positively to hydration. The key is to watch what you add. If you are used to three teaspoons of sugar per cup and drink four cups a day, that is 12 extra teaspoons of sugar daily. Gradually reduce to one teaspoon per cup, then try half. After a few weeks, your taste buds adjust."},{"id":"s7","title":"Signs you are not drinking enough","body":"Beyond dark urine, watch for frequent headaches especially in the afternoon, feeling unusually tired even after sleeping well, dry or cracked lips, dizziness when standing up, and constipation. Many people who complain of afternoon fatigue find that simply drinking more water resolves the problem within days.\\n\\nYour body performs every function better when properly hydrated. It is one of the simplest and most affordable things you can do for your health, and it supports every other effort you are making, from medication adherence to exercise to managing your blood sugar or blood pressure."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'fresh-vegetables'),
  (E'smart-swaps-at-the-market-that-work-for-any-condition', E'Smart swaps at the market that work for any condition', E'GENERAL', '{"GENERAL"}', E'DIETARY', E'Eating well does not have to mean buying expensive imported foods or following diets designed for people in other countries. The healthiest changes you can make are often simple swaps using foods you already know from your local market.', E'Eating well does not have to mean buying expensive imported foods or following diets designed for people in other countries. The healthiest changes you can make are often simple swaps using foods you already know from your local market. These swaps cost the same or sometimes even less, and they benefit everyone at your table, not just the person managing a health condition.\n\nSwap white potatoes for sweet potatoes\n\nSweet potatoes, known as viazi vitamu, are one of the best carbohydrate choices at any Kenyan market. They release sugar into your bloodstream more slowly than white potatoes, meaning less of a spike in blood sugar after eating. Sweet potatoes are also rich in fibre, vitamin A, and potassium, which helps regulate blood pressure. A medium sweet potato costs between KES 10 and KES 30 depending on season. Boil or roast them instead of frying, and you have an affordable, nutritious staple.\n\nChoose brown flour over refined white flour\n\nWhen making chapati or ugali, consider switching to brown or whole wheat flour. Refined white flour has been stripped of its fibre and nutrients, leaving mostly starch that converts quickly to sugar. Whole wheat flour retains the bran and germ, giving you fibre that slows digestion and helps manage blood sugar. The price difference is minimal, often only KES 10 to 20 more per kilogram. If your family resists the change, start by mixing half brown and half white flour and gradually increase the proportion.\n\nFor ugali, try mixing regular maize flour with millet or sorghum flour. These traditional flours are lower on the glycemic index and add a pleasant, slightly nutty flavour. Wimbi, finger millet flour, makes an excellent breakfast porridge that is far better for blood sugar than white bread or mandazi.\n\nBuy whole fruits instead of juice boxes\n\nJuice boxes and packaged fruit drinks are marketed as healthy, but most contain concentrated sugar with little fibre. A 250ml juice box can contain six to eight teaspoons of sugar. Compare that to eating an actual mango, which gives you natural sugar alongside fibre, vitamins, and minerals. Seasonal fruits like mangoes, pawpaw, bananas, oranges, and passion fruit are widely available and affordable from local markets. A whole pawpaw might cost KES 50 to 100 and provide multiple servings, while the same money buys just two or three juice boxes.\n\nChoose omena and beans over processed meats\n\nOmena, the small dried fish from Lake Victoria, is one of the most nutritious and affordable protein sources in Kenya. Rich in protein, calcium, iron, and omega-3 fatty acids, a heap of omena costing KES 50 to 100 provides excellent nutrition. Compare this to processed meats like sausages and canned meat, which are high in sodium, unhealthy fats, and preservatives linked to heart disease and high blood pressure.\n\nBeans, lentils, green grams, and chickpeas are other excellent affordable protein choices. A kilogram of beans costs around KES 120 to 180 and provides many meals worth of protein and fibre. Githeri, the traditional combination of maize and beans, is actually a nutritionally balanced meal providing carbohydrates, protein, and plenty of fibre. If you grew up eating githeri and moved away from it, consider bringing it back to your weekly menu.\n\nSmarter cooking methods\n\nHow you prepare food matters as much as what you buy. Deep frying adds large amounts of unhealthy fat to otherwise nutritious food. When cooking sukuma wiki and other greens, use just one to two tablespoons of oil rather than drowning them in fat. Steaming, boiling, grilling, and stewing are all healthier methods.\n\nWhen preparing pilau or biryani, use measured amounts of oil rather than pouring freely. These dishes can be made with much less oil without sacrificing flavour, especially if you are generous with spices. Turmeric, cumin, ginger, and garlic all add flavour and have their own health benefits.\n\nShopping tips for the market\n\nGo to the market with a list and a budget. Buy vegetables in bulk when they are in season. Sukuma wiki, spinach, cabbage, carrots, and tomatoes form the backbone of a healthy Kenyan kitchen. At open-air markets you can negotiate better prices, especially as a regular customer buying toward end of day.\n\nBuy grains and legumes in bulk when you have the funds, as the per-kilogram price is lower. Store them in airtight containers to prevent weevils. A month''s supply bought in bulk can save you KES 500 to 1,000 compared to buying small packets weekly.\n\nEvery swap you make at the market is a step toward better health for your whole household. Start with one or two swaps this week and build from there.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Eating well does not have to mean buying expensive imported foods or following diets designed for people in other countries. The healthiest changes you can make are often simple swaps using foods you already know from your local market. These swaps cost the same or sometimes even less, and they benefit everyone at your table, not just the person managing a health condition."},{"id":"s2","title":"Swap white potatoes for sweet potatoes","body":"Sweet potatoes, known as viazi vitamu, are one of the best carbohydrate choices at any Kenyan market. They release sugar into your bloodstream more slowly than white potatoes, meaning less of a spike in blood sugar after eating. Sweet potatoes are also rich in fibre, vitamin A, and potassium, which helps regulate blood pressure. A medium sweet potato costs between KES 10 and KES 30 depending on season. Boil or roast them instead of frying, and you have an affordable, nutritious staple."},{"id":"s3","title":"Choose brown flour over refined white flour","body":"When making chapati or ugali, consider switching to brown or whole wheat flour. Refined white flour has been stripped of its fibre and nutrients, leaving mostly starch that converts quickly to sugar. Whole wheat flour retains the bran and germ, giving you fibre that slows digestion and helps manage blood sugar. The price difference is minimal, often only KES 10 to 20 more per kilogram. If your family resists the change, start by mixing half brown and half white flour and gradually increase the proportion.\\n\\nFor ugali, try mixing regular maize flour with millet or sorghum flour. These traditional flours are lower on the glycemic index and add a pleasant, slightly nutty flavour. Wimbi, finger millet flour, makes an excellent breakfast porridge that is far better for blood sugar than white bread or mandazi."},{"id":"s4","title":"Buy whole fruits instead of juice boxes","body":"Juice boxes and packaged fruit drinks are marketed as healthy, but most contain concentrated sugar with little fibre. A 250ml juice box can contain six to eight teaspoons of sugar. Compare that to eating an actual mango, which gives you natural sugar alongside fibre, vitamins, and minerals. Seasonal fruits like mangoes, pawpaw, bananas, oranges, and passion fruit are widely available and affordable from local markets. A whole pawpaw might cost KES 50 to 100 and provide multiple servings, while the same money buys just two or three juice boxes."},{"id":"s5","title":"Choose omena and beans over processed meats","body":"Omena, the small dried fish from Lake Victoria, is one of the most nutritious and affordable protein sources in Kenya. Rich in protein, calcium, iron, and omega-3 fatty acids, a heap of omena costing KES 50 to 100 provides excellent nutrition. Compare this to processed meats like sausages and canned meat, which are high in sodium, unhealthy fats, and preservatives linked to heart disease and high blood pressure.\\n\\nBeans, lentils, green grams, and chickpeas are other excellent affordable protein choices. A kilogram of beans costs around KES 120 to 180 and provides many meals worth of protein and fibre. Githeri, the traditional combination of maize and beans, is actually a nutritionally balanced meal providing carbohydrates, protein, and plenty of fibre. If you grew up eating githeri and moved away from it, consider bringing it back to your weekly menu."},{"id":"s6","title":"Smarter cooking methods","body":"How you prepare food matters as much as what you buy. Deep frying adds large amounts of unhealthy fat to otherwise nutritious food. When cooking sukuma wiki and other greens, use just one to two tablespoons of oil rather than drowning them in fat. Steaming, boiling, grilling, and stewing are all healthier methods.\\n\\nWhen preparing pilau or biryani, use measured amounts of oil rather than pouring freely. These dishes can be made with much less oil without sacrificing flavour, especially if you are generous with spices. Turmeric, cumin, ginger, and garlic all add flavour and have their own health benefits."},{"id":"s7","title":"Shopping tips for the market","body":"Go to the market with a list and a budget. Buy vegetables in bulk when they are in season. Sukuma wiki, spinach, cabbage, carrots, and tomatoes form the backbone of a healthy Kenyan kitchen. At open-air markets you can negotiate better prices, especially as a regular customer buying toward end of day.\\n\\nBuy grains and legumes in bulk when you have the funds, as the per-kilogram price is lower. Store them in airtight containers to prevent weevils. A month''s supply bought in bulk can save you KES 500 to 1,000 compared to buying small packets weekly.\\n\\nEvery swap you make at the market is a step toward better health for your whole household. Start with one or two swaps this week and build from there."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Identify healthier alternatives using locally available foods","Understand portion sizes that support your condition","Plan affordable meals that fit your health goals"}', E'healthy-cooking'),
  (E'starting-an-exercise-habit-when-you-have-never-had-one', E'Starting an exercise habit when you have never had one', E'GENERAL', '{"GENERAL"}', E'EXERCISE', E'If you have never exercised regularly, the idea of starting can feel overwhelming. You might picture people running marathons or lifting heavy weights in a gym and think that is not for you.', E'If you have never exercised regularly, the idea of starting can feel overwhelming. You might picture people running marathons or lifting heavy weights in a gym and think that is not for you. Good news: it does not have to be. The most effective exercise programme is the one you actually do, and that starts with something so small it feels almost silly.\n\nThe power of starting ridiculously small\n\nDo not start with a plan to run five kilometres. Start with putting on your shoes. On day one, your only goal is to walk to your gate and back. If it feels too easy, that is exactly the point. You are not building fitness yet. You are building a habit, and habits form when the action is so easy you cannot say no.\n\nTomorrow, walk to the end of your street. By the end of the first week, try walking for 10 minutes. Add a little distance each week, but never so much that it feels punishing. The goal for the first month is consistency, not intensity.\n\nAttach exercise to an existing habit\n\nOne of the best strategies is attaching exercise to something you already do every day. Walk for 10 minutes after breakfast. Do five minutes of stretching before bed. Walk to the nearby shop for your milk instead of sending someone. Take the stairs instead of the lift. Get off the matatu one stop early and walk the remaining distance. These small additions accumulate throughout the day without requiring extra time set aside.\n\nA simple walking programme for beginners\n\nWeek 1 and 2: Walk for 10 minutes at a comfortable pace, three to four days per week. Walk on flat ground and do not worry about speed.\n\nWeek 3 and 4: Increase to 15 minutes per walk, four to five days per week. Notice your pace naturally increasing as fitness improves.\n\nWeek 5 and 6: Walk for 20 minutes per session. Add gentle inclines or slightly brisker stretches in the middle of your walk.\n\nWeek 7 and 8: Aim for 25 to 30 minutes of walking, five days per week. By now you should feel less winded, have more energy during the day, and sleep more soundly at night.\n\nAfter two months, 30 minutes of daily walking should feel achievable and enjoyable. From here, gradually add variety: faster intervals, bodyweight exercises, or longer weekend walks.\n\nSimple exercises you can do at home\n\nYou do not need a gym or equipment. Here are exercises anyone can do in their living room or compound.\n\nWall push-ups: Stand arm''s length from a wall, palms flat at shoulder height. Bend your elbows to bring your chest toward the wall, then push back. Start with 5 repetitions and build to 15. This strengthens arms and chest without the strain of floor push-ups.\n\nChair squats: Stand in front of a sturdy chair, feet shoulder-width apart. Lower yourself as if sitting down, touch the seat lightly, then stand back up. Start with 5 and build to 15. Strengthens legs and improves balance.\n\nMarching in place: Lift your knees alternately, swinging your arms naturally. Do this for two to three minutes. Gets your heart rate up without joint impact.\n\nStanding calf raises: Hold a chair for balance. Rise on your toes, hold two seconds, then lower. Start with 10 and build to 20. Good for leg circulation.\n\nSafety precautions\n\nIf you have a heart condition, diabetes, or joint problems, talk to your doctor before starting. If you have diabetes, check your blood sugar before exercising. If it is below 5.0 mmol/L, eat a small snack first, such as a banana, to avoid hypoglycemia. Carry a sugary snack during walks.\n\nStop and rest if you feel chest pain, severe dizziness, or unusual shortness of breath. Some mild breathlessness during brisk walking is normal, but pain is not. Exercise in the morning or evening when it is cooler. Wear comfortable clothing and supportive shoes. Stay hydrated.\n\nThe two-week turning point\n\nIt takes about two weeks of consistent daily action for a behaviour to start feeling automatic. After two weeks, your body begins to expect the movement. After a month, you will feel restless on days you miss it. Once exercise becomes something you do rather than something you force yourself to do, the health benefits compound over months and years.\n\nConsistency beats intensity every time. A 15-minute daily walk done seven days a week is far better than one intense 90-minute workout done once and then abandoned. Start small, stay steady, and let the habit carry you forward.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you have never exercised regularly, the idea of starting can feel overwhelming. You might picture people running marathons or lifting heavy weights in a gym and think that is not for you. Good news: it does not have to be. The most effective exercise programme is the one you actually do, and that starts with something so small it feels almost silly."},{"id":"s2","title":"The power of starting ridiculously small","body":"Do not start with a plan to run five kilometres. Start with putting on your shoes. On day one, your only goal is to walk to your gate and back. If it feels too easy, that is exactly the point. You are not building fitness yet. You are building a habit, and habits form when the action is so easy you cannot say no.\\n\\nTomorrow, walk to the end of your street. By the end of the first week, try walking for 10 minutes. Add a little distance each week, but never so much that it feels punishing. The goal for the first month is consistency, not intensity."},{"id":"s3","title":"Attach exercise to an existing habit","body":"One of the best strategies is attaching exercise to something you already do every day. Walk for 10 minutes after breakfast. Do five minutes of stretching before bed. Walk to the nearby shop for your milk instead of sending someone. Take the stairs instead of the lift. Get off the matatu one stop early and walk the remaining distance. These small additions accumulate throughout the day without requiring extra time set aside."},{"id":"s4","title":"A simple walking programme for beginners","body":"Week 1 and 2: Walk for 10 minutes at a comfortable pace, three to four days per week. Walk on flat ground and do not worry about speed.\\n\\nWeek 3 and 4: Increase to 15 minutes per walk, four to five days per week. Notice your pace naturally increasing as fitness improves.\\n\\nWeek 5 and 6: Walk for 20 minutes per session. Add gentle inclines or slightly brisker stretches in the middle of your walk.\\n\\nWeek 7 and 8: Aim for 25 to 30 minutes of walking, five days per week. By now you should feel less winded, have more energy during the day, and sleep more soundly at night.\\n\\nAfter two months, 30 minutes of daily walking should feel achievable and enjoyable. From here, gradually add variety: faster intervals, bodyweight exercises, or longer weekend walks."},{"id":"s5","title":"Simple exercises you can do at home","body":"You do not need a gym or equipment. Here are exercises anyone can do in their living room or compound.\\n\\nWall push-ups: Stand arm''s length from a wall, palms flat at shoulder height. Bend your elbows to bring your chest toward the wall, then push back. Start with 5 repetitions and build to 15. This strengthens arms and chest without the strain of floor push-ups.\\n\\nChair squats: Stand in front of a sturdy chair, feet shoulder-width apart. Lower yourself as if sitting down, touch the seat lightly, then stand back up. Start with 5 and build to 15. Strengthens legs and improves balance.\\n\\nMarching in place: Lift your knees alternately, swinging your arms naturally. Do this for two to three minutes. Gets your heart rate up without joint impact.\\n\\nStanding calf raises: Hold a chair for balance. Rise on your toes, hold two seconds, then lower. Start with 10 and build to 20. Good for leg circulation."},{"id":"s6","title":"Safety precautions","body":"If you have a heart condition, diabetes, or joint problems, talk to your doctor before starting. If you have diabetes, check your blood sugar before exercising. If it is below 5.0 mmol/L, eat a small snack first, such as a banana, to avoid hypoglycemia. Carry a sugary snack during walks.\\n\\nStop and rest if you feel chest pain, severe dizziness, or unusual shortness of breath. Some mild breathlessness during brisk walking is normal, but pain is not. Exercise in the morning or evening when it is cooler. Wear comfortable clothing and supportive shoes. Stay hydrated."},{"id":"s7","title":"The two-week turning point","body":"It takes about two weeks of consistent daily action for a behaviour to start feeling automatic. After two weeks, your body begins to expect the movement. After a month, you will feel restless on days you miss it. Once exercise becomes something you do rather than something you force yourself to do, the health benefits compound over months and years.\\n\\nConsistency beats intensity every time. A 15-minute daily walk done seven days a week is far better than one intense 90-minute workout done once and then abandoned. Start small, stay steady, and let the habit carry you forward."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Follow safe exercise routines suited for your condition","Know when to rest and when to push through","Build a sustainable daily movement habit"}', E'light-exercise'),
  (E'myth-natural-means-safe', E'Myth: Natural means safe', E'GENERAL', '{"GENERAL"}', E'MYTH_BUSTING', E'The belief that anything labelled natural is automatically safe is one of the most widespread and dangerous health myths.', E'The belief that anything labelled natural is automatically safe is one of the most widespread and dangerous health myths. It leads people to take herbal supplements without telling their doctors, mix traditional remedies with prescription medications, and sometimes abandon proven treatments in favour of unregulated products. Understanding why natural does not mean safe could prevent a serious health crisis.\n\nThe flawed logic behind the myth\n\nThe word natural carries an emotional appeal. It suggests purity, gentleness, and harmony with the body. Pharmaceutical drugs, by contrast, are often seen as artificial, harsh, and full of side effects. This framing feels intuitive but does not hold up to scrutiny. Consider this: snake venom is natural. Poisonous mushrooms are natural. Arsenic occurs naturally in the earth. The castor bean plant, which grows across Kenya, contains ricin, one of the most toxic substances known. Something being found in nature says nothing about whether it is safe for human consumption.\n\nConversely, many life-saving medicines were originally derived from natural sources. Aspirin comes from willow bark. The diabetes drug metformin traces its origins to the French lilac plant. Morphine comes from poppies. The difference is that these substances were isolated, studied, tested for safety, standardised in dosage, and regulated. A herbal supplement bought from a market stall or social media vendor has gone through none of these steps.\n\nCommon natural remedies and their risks\n\nIn Kenya, several herbal and traditional remedies are widely used alongside or instead of prescribed medications. Many of these carry real risks that users are unaware of.\n\nMoringa, often called the miracle tree, is promoted for everything from diabetes to high blood pressure to cancer. While moringa leaves contain some beneficial nutrients, they can lower blood sugar unpredictably when taken alongside diabetes medication, potentially causing dangerous hypoglycemia. They can also interact with thyroid medications and blood thinners.\n\nNeem, commonly used for skin conditions and general wellness, can affect liver function and lower blood sugar. For someone already taking liver or diabetes medication, adding neem creates an unpredictable combination that no doctor can safely manage because the dosage of active compounds in neem preparations varies wildly.\n\nAloe vera juice, popular as a health drink, acts as a laxative and can cause dangerous drops in potassium levels. Low potassium is particularly risky for people with heart conditions or those taking blood pressure medications.\n\nSt. John''s Wort, used for depression, reduces the effectiveness of a staggering number of medications including blood thinners like warfarin, HIV antiretrovirals, oral contraceptives, and immunosuppressants. A person taking ARVs who adds St. John''s Wort could see their viral load increase because the herb accelerates the breakdown of the medication in the body.\n\nTurmeric in food quantities is generally safe, but concentrated turmeric supplements can interact with blood thinners and diabetes medications and may worsen gallbladder problems.\n\nThe danger of the unregulated market\n\nIn Kenya, herbal supplements and traditional remedies are largely unregulated. Products sold at markets, by roadside vendors, through social media, and in some health shops do not undergo the rigorous testing that pharmaceutical drugs do. There is no guarantee that what is on the label matches what is in the bottle. Studies have found that some herbal supplements contain none of the advertised ingredient but do contain unlisted pharmaceutical drugs, heavy metals, or other contaminants.\n\nSome products marketed as natural actually contain hidden prescription medications. There have been cases in East Africa of herbal diabetes remedies that secretly contained glibenclamide, a powerful blood sugar-lowering drug that can cause severe hypoglycemia. The person takes what they believe is a gentle herbal supplement and ends up in hospital with dangerously low blood sugar.\n\nWhat you should do\n\nThis is not a call to reject all traditional knowledge or never use a herbal remedy. Many traditional practices have value, and some have led to important pharmaceutical discoveries. The point is that natural substances deserve the same caution as any other substance you put in your body.\n\nAlways tell your doctor about everything you are taking, including herbal teas, supplements, tonics, and traditional preparations. Do not leave anything out because you think it does not count or because you worry the doctor will disapprove. Your doctor needs this information to keep you safe. Drug interactions can be serious and sometimes fatal.\n\nBe especially cautious about products that make dramatic claims, such as curing diabetes, reversing cancer, or eliminating the need for medication. If a product could truly do these things, it would be the biggest medical breakthrough of the century, not sold in unlabelled bottles at a bus station.\n\nIf you want to explore natural or traditional remedies, do so in partnership with your healthcare provider, not as a secret alternative. Some herbs can be safely used alongside conventional medicine, but only when your doctor knows about them and can monitor for interactions.\n\nYour health is too important to gamble on assumptions. Natural deserves respect, but respect means taking it seriously enough to ask questions, demand evidence, and never assume it is harmless just because it grew from the ground.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"The belief that anything labelled natural is automatically safe is one of the most widespread and dangerous health myths. It leads people to take herbal supplements without telling their doctors, mix traditional remedies with prescription medications, and sometimes abandon proven treatments in favour of unregulated products. Understanding why natural does not mean safe could prevent a serious health crisis."},{"id":"s2","title":"The flawed logic behind the myth","body":"The word natural carries an emotional appeal. It suggests purity, gentleness, and harmony with the body. Pharmaceutical drugs, by contrast, are often seen as artificial, harsh, and full of side effects. This framing feels intuitive but does not hold up to scrutiny. Consider this: snake venom is natural. Poisonous mushrooms are natural. Arsenic occurs naturally in the earth. The castor bean plant, which grows across Kenya, contains ricin, one of the most toxic substances known. Something being found in nature says nothing about whether it is safe for human consumption.\\n\\nConversely, many life-saving medicines were originally derived from natural sources. Aspirin comes from willow bark. The diabetes drug metformin traces its origins to the French lilac plant. Morphine comes from poppies. The difference is that these substances were isolated, studied, tested for safety, standardised in dosage, and regulated. A herbal supplement bought from a market stall or social media vendor has gone through none of these steps."},{"id":"s3","title":"Common natural remedies and their risks","body":"In Kenya, several herbal and traditional remedies are widely used alongside or instead of prescribed medications. Many of these carry real risks that users are unaware of.\\n\\nMoringa, often called the miracle tree, is promoted for everything from diabetes to high blood pressure to cancer. While moringa leaves contain some beneficial nutrients, they can lower blood sugar unpredictably when taken alongside diabetes medication, potentially causing dangerous hypoglycemia. They can also interact with thyroid medications and blood thinners.\\n\\nNeem, commonly used for