tal with dangerously low blood sugar."},{"id":"s5","title":"What you should do","body":"This is not a call to reject all traditional knowledge or never use a herbal remedy. Many traditional practices have value, and some have led to important pharmaceutical discoveries. The point is that natural substances deserve the same caution as any other substance you put in your body.\\n\\nAlways tell your doctor about everything you are taking, including herbal teas, supplements, tonics, and traditional preparations. Do not leave anything out because you think it does not count or because you worry the doctor will disapprove. Your doctor needs this information to keep you safe. Drug interactions can be serious and sometimes fatal.\\n\\nBe especially cautious about products that make dramatic claims, such as curing diabetes, reversing cancer, or eliminating the need for medication. If a product could truly do these things, it would be the biggest medical breakthrough of the century, not sold in unlabelled bottles at a bus station.\\n\\nIf you want to explore natural or traditional remedies, do so in partnership with your healthcare provider, not as a secret alternative. Some herbs can be safely used alongside conventional medicine, but only when your doctor knows about them and can monitor for interactions.\\n\\nYour health is too important to gamble on assumptions. Natural deserves respect, but respect means taking it seriously enough to ask questions, demand evidence, and never assume it is harmless just because it grew from the ground."}]'::jsonb, 5, '{"Separate medical facts from common misconceptions","Understand why certain myths can be harmful","Make informed decisions about your treatment"}', E'medical-truth'),
  (E'it-is-normal-to-feel-overwhelmed', E'It is normal to feel overwhelmed', E'GENERAL', '{"GENERAL"}', E'EMOTIONAL', E'If you are reading this and feeling like managing your health condition is just too much, you are not alone and you are not failing. Living with a long-term condition is exhausting in ways that people who do not have one cannot fully understand.', E'If you are reading this and feeling like managing your health condition is just too much, you are not alone and you are not failing. Living with a long-term condition is exhausting in ways that people who do not have one cannot fully understand. The constant attention to medications, diet, appointments, symptoms, and costs takes a toll that goes far beyond the physical. It is completely normal to feel overwhelmed, and acknowledging that feeling is the first step toward managing it.\n\nWhat overwhelm actually looks like\n\nOverwhelm does not always announce itself with dramatic breakdowns. More often, it creeps in quietly. You might notice that you have stopped caring about what you eat, not because you made a decision but because you just cannot think about food choices anymore. You might skip a medication dose and feel a mixture of guilt and relief. You might cancel a doctor''s appointment and tell yourself you will reschedule but never do. You might snap at your family over small things and then feel terrible about it. You might lie awake at night worrying about your health, your finances, or your future, only to feel numb and disconnected during the day.\n\nThese are all signs that you are carrying more than you can manage alone. They are not signs of weakness. They are signs that you are human, and that the burden you are carrying is genuinely heavy.\n\nWhy chronic conditions are emotionally exhausting\n\nWhen you have a cold or a broken bone, there is an end date. You know you will recover and return to normal. Chronic conditions do not offer that. There is no finish line where you can stop thinking about your health. Every meal, every pill, every appointment is part of an ongoing reality that stretches into the indefinite future. That permanence is what makes it so draining.\n\nIn Kenya, additional layers of stress are common. The cost of medication can strain already tight budgets. A single lab test might cost KES 1,500 to 3,000, and some people need tests every few months. Transport to the hospital, time away from work or business, and the long waiting times at public facilities all add up. If you are the primary earner for your family, the fear of what happens if your health deteriorates can be paralysing. And if you are a caregiver managing someone else''s condition as well as your own daily responsibilities, the weight doubles.\n\nSocial pressure and cultural expectations also play a role. In many Kenyan communities, illness is seen as something to endure privately. Admitting that you are struggling emotionally can feel like admitting defeat. Men especially may feel pressure to be strong and silent about their health challenges. But silence does not make the burden lighter. It just means you carry it alone.\n\nYou are already doing something right\n\nHere is what is true right now: you are reading this. That means you engaged with your health today, even if it was just opening this article. That counts. On the days when you take your medication, you are doing something for your health. On the days when you choose a healthier meal, you are doing something. On the days when you just get through the day without giving up, you are doing something. Progress does not require perfection. It requires persistence, and persistence includes bad days.\n\nPractical strategies for managing overwhelm\n\nTalk to someone you trust. This is the single most important step. Holding everything inside multiplies the weight. A spouse, a sibling, a close friend, a pastor, an imam, or a colleague who knows your situation can provide both emotional support and practical help. You do not need to have a formal counselling session. Sometimes just saying out loud what you are feeling to someone who listens without judgement is enough to lighten the load.\n\nSimplify where you can. If managing multiple medications feels complex, ask your pharmacist to help you organise them. A simple pill organiser, available for around KES 200 to 500 at most Kenyan pharmacies, can take the daily guessing out of your medication routine. Set phone alarms for your doses. Prepare meals in bulk on weekends so you are not making food decisions every day when you are tired.\n\nBreak your health management into the smallest possible steps. Instead of thinking about managing a chronic condition for the rest of your life, focus on today. Did you take your morning medication? Good. Did you drink some water? Good. Will you eat something with vegetables today? Good. That is enough for today. Tomorrow is tomorrow''s problem.\n\nGive yourself permission to have bad days without guilt. Missing one dose, eating one unhealthy meal, or skipping one walk does not erase all your progress. What matters is the overall pattern, not any single day. Be as kind to yourself as you would be to a friend going through the same thing.\n\nWhen to seek professional help\n\nIf you feel consistently hopeless, have lost interest in things you used to enjoy, cannot sleep or are sleeping too much, are eating too much or too little, or have thoughts of harming yourself, please reach out to a mental health professional. These are signs of depression, which is a medical condition that is both common among people with chronic diseases and very treatable.\n\nMany Kenyan hospitals now have mental health departments or can refer you to a counsellor. Your Jireh app can help you find nearby services. The Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 offers free crisis support. You deserve to feel better, and help is available.\n\nRemember: managing a chronic condition is one of the hardest things a person can do. The fact that you are still doing it, even imperfectly, even on the hard days, means you are stronger than you think.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"If you are reading this and feeling like managing your health condition is just too much, you are not alone and you are not failing. Living with a long-term condition is exhausting in ways that people who do not have one cannot fully understand. The constant attention to medications, diet, appointments, symptoms, and costs takes a toll that goes far beyond the physical. It is completely normal to feel overwhelmed, and acknowledging that feeling is the first step toward managing it."},{"id":"s2","title":"What overwhelm actually looks like","body":"Overwhelm does not always announce itself with dramatic breakdowns. More often, it creeps in quietly. You might notice that you have stopped caring about what you eat, not because you made a decision but because you just cannot think about food choices anymore. You might skip a medication dose and feel a mixture of guilt and relief. You might cancel a doctor''s appointment and tell yourself you will reschedule but never do. You might snap at your family over small things and then feel terrible about it. You might lie awake at night worrying about your health, your finances, or your future, only to feel numb and disconnected during the day.\\n\\nThese are all signs that you are carrying more than you can manage alone. They are not signs of weakness. They are signs that you are human, and that the burden you are carrying is genuinely heavy."},{"id":"s3","title":"Why chronic conditions are emotionally exhausting","body":"When you have a cold or a broken bone, there is an end date. You know you will recover and return to normal. Chronic conditions do not offer that. There is no finish line where you can stop thinking about your health. Every meal, every pill, every appointment is part of an ongoing reality that stretches into the indefinite future. That permanence is what makes