one who listens without judgement is enough to lighten the load.\\n\\nSimplify where you can. If managing multiple medications feels complex, ask your pharmacist to help you organise them. A simple pill organiser, available for around KES 200 to 500 at most Kenyan pharmacies, can take the daily guessing out of your medication routine. Set phone alarms for your doses. Prepare meals in bulk on weekends so you are not making food decisions every day when you are tired.\\n\\nBreak your health management into the smallest possible steps. Instead of thinking about managing a chronic condition for the rest of your life, focus on today. Did you take your morning medication? Good. Did you drink some water? Good. Will you eat something with vegetables today? Good. That is enough for today. Tomorrow is tomorrow''s problem.\\n\\nGive yourself permission to have bad days without guilt. Missing one dose, eating one unhealthy meal, or skipping one walk does not erase all your progress. What matters is the overall pattern, not any single day. Be as kind to yourself as you would be to a friend going through the same thing."},{"id":"s6","title":"When to seek professional help","body":"If you feel consistently hopeless, have lost interest in things you used to enjoy, cannot sleep or are sleeping too much, are eating too much or too little, or have thoughts of harming yourself, please reach out to a mental health professional. These are signs of depression, which is a medical condition that is both common among people with chronic diseases and very treatable.\\n\\nMany Kenyan hospitals now have mental health departments or can refer you to a counsellor. Your Jireh app can help you find nearby services. The Kenya Red Cross helpline at 1199 offers free crisis support. You deserve to feel better, and help is available.\\n\\nRemember: managing a chronic condition is one of the hardest things a person can do. The fact that you are still doing it, even imperfectly, even on the hard days, means you are stronger than you think."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Recognise emotional challenges of living with a chronic condition","Learn practical coping strategies for difficult days","Know when and how to seek support"}', E'community-care'),
  (E'living-well-with-a-lifelong-condition', E'Living well with a lifelong condition', E'GENERAL', '{"GENERAL"}', E'ACCEPTANCE', E'Receiving a diagnosis that changes your life permanently is one of the most difficult experiences a person can go through.', E'Receiving a diagnosis that changes your life permanently is one of the most difficult experiences a person can go through. Whether it is diabetes, hypertension, kidney disease, a heart condition, or any other chronic illness, the moment your doctor tells you this is something you will manage for the rest of your life, something shifts inside you. The future you imagined changes shape. And for a while, that can feel like a loss.\n\nThe emotions are part of the process\n\nSome people feel angry when they are diagnosed. Why me? I did everything right. Others feel ashamed, especially if they believe the condition is connected to lifestyle choices. Some people go numb, hearing the words but not absorbing them, only for the reality to hit days or weeks later. And many people try to ignore the diagnosis altogether, hoping that if they do not think about it, it might not be real.\n\nNone of these reactions are wrong. They are all part of the human response to a fundamental change in your life. The important thing is not to get stuck in any one of these places. Anger that festers becomes bitterness. Shame that is not addressed becomes isolation. Denial that persists becomes dangerous when it delays treatment. Allow yourself to feel whatever you are feeling, but also keep moving forward, even in small steps.\n\nIn Kenya, chronic disease diagnoses often come with additional weight. There may be fear about the cost of ongoing treatment. Worry about how your family will manage if your health declines. Concern about stigma, especially for conditions that people associate with lifestyle, like diabetes. Cultural beliefs about illness can add layers of guilt or superstition that make acceptance harder. All of these are real pressures, and it is okay to acknowledge them.\n\nYou are not alone in this\n\nMillions of people across Kenya live with chronic conditions and lead full, active, meaningful lives. The mama selling vegetables at the market who takes her blood pressure medication every morning. The teacher who checks his blood sugar before breakfast and still coaches the football team. The grandmother managing her kidney health who has not missed a Sunday at church in years. These people are everywhere around you, and most of them had the same fear and uncertainty you are feeling now.\n\nChronic conditions are common. According to health data, non-communicable diseases account for a growing share of illness in Kenya. You are not unusual, and you are not broken. You are part of a large community of people who have learned to adapt, and you can learn too.\n\nWhat acceptance actually means\n\nAcceptance does not mean giving up or being happy about your diagnosis. It does not mean you have to smile and say everything is fine. Acceptance means acknowledging reality as it is and choosing to work with it rather than against it. It means saying: I have this condition, I did not choose it, but I can choose how I respond to it.\n\nAcceptance is practical. It means filling your prescription and taking the medication as directed. It means showing up for your appointments, even when you would rather not. It means making dietary changes not because you want to, but because you understand that they matter. It means asking for help when you need it, whether from family, friends, your healthcare team, or your Jireh Circle.\n\nAcceptance is also gradual. You do not wake up one morning and suddenly feel at peace with your diagnosis. It comes in layers, over weeks and months and sometimes years. There will be good days when you feel in control and hopeful, and bad days when you feel resentful and tired. Both are part of the process.\n\nStart small and build\n\nDo not try to overhaul your entire life the week after your diagnosis. That approach leads to burnout and frustration. Instead, focus on one thing at a time.\n\nFirst, understand your condition. Ask your doctor to explain it in plain language. What is happening in your body? What does the medication do? What happens if you do nothing? Knowledge reduces fear, and fear is one of the biggest obstacles to acceptance.\n\nNext, establish your medication routine. This is the foundation everything else builds on. Find a time that works for you, set a reminder, and make it non-negotiable. Once taking your medication becomes automatic, it frees up mental energy for other changes.\n\nThen, make one dietary change. Just one. Maybe it is switching from soda to water with meals. Maybe it is adding a serving of vegetables to your dinner. Maybe it is reducing the sugar in your tea from three spoons to one. One change, sustained over weeks, becomes a new normal. Then you add another.\n\nFinally, move your body a little. A short walk after dinner, stretching in the morning, taking the stairs. Any movement is better than none, and the mood-boosting effects of even mild exercise can help with the emotional side of your diagnosis.\n\nRedefining your story\n\nYou are not defined by your condition. A diagnosis is something that happened to you. It is part of your story, but it is not the whole story. You are still a parent, a child, a friend, a professional, a member of your community. You still have things to contribute, goals to pursue, and people who need you.\n\nMany people find that managing a chronic condition, while difficult, also brings unexpected clarity. You may find yourself making healthier choices that improve your overall quality of life. You may deepen relationships as you learn to lean on others. You may discover a resilience in yourself that you did not know was there.\n\nOne refill at a time. One walk at a time. One meal at a time. One day at a time. That is how a full life with a chronic condition is built, and you are already building it.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Receiving a diagnosis that changes your life permanently is one of the most difficult experiences a person can go through. Whether it is diabetes, hypertension, kidney disease, a heart condition, or any other chronic illness, the moment your doctor tells you this is something you will manage for the rest of your life, something shifts inside you. The future you imagined changes shape. And for a while, that can feel like a loss."},{"id":"s2","title":"The emotions are part of the process","body":"Some people feel angry when they are diagnosed. Why me? I did everything right. Others feel ashamed, especially if they believe the condition is connected to lifestyle choices. Some people go numb, hearing the words but not absorbing them, only for the reality to hit days or weeks later. And many people try to ignore the diagnosis altogether, hoping that if they do not think about it, it might not be real.\\n\\nNone of these reactions are wrong. They are all part of the human response to a fundamental change in your life. The important thing is not to get stuck in any one of these places. Anger that festers becomes bitterness. Shame that is not addressed becomes isolation. Denial that persists becomes dangerous when it delays treatment. Allow yourself to feel whatever you are feeling, but also keep moving forward, even in small steps.\\n\\nIn Kenya, chronic disease diagnoses often come with additional weight. There may be fear about the cost of ongoing treatment. Worry about how your family will manage if your health declines. Concern about stigma, especially for conditions that people associate with lifestyle, like diabetes. Cultural beliefs about illness can add layers of guilt or superstition that make acceptance harder. All of these are real pressures, and it is okay to acknowledge them."},{"id":"s3","title":"You are not alone in this","body":"Millions of people across Kenya live with chronic conditions and lead full, active, meaningful lives. The mama selling vegetables at the market who takes her blood pressure medication every morning. The teacher who checks his blood sugar before breakfast and still coaches the football team. The grandmother managing her kidney health who has not missed a Sunday at church in years. These people are everywhere around you, and most of them had the same fear and uncertainty you are feeling now.\\n\\nChronic conditions are common. According to health data, non-communicable diseases account for a growing share of illness in Kenya. You are not unusual, and you are not broken. You are part of a large community of people who have learned to adapt, and you can learn too."},{"id":"s4","title":"What acceptance actually means","body":"Acceptance does not mean giving up or being happy about your diagnosis. It does not mean you have to smile and say everything is fine. Acceptance means acknowledging reality as it is and choosing to work with it rather than against it. It means saying: I have this condition, I did not choose it, but I can choose how I respond to it.\\n\\nAcceptance is practical. It means filling your prescription and taking the medication as directed. It means showing up for your appointments, even when you would rather not. It means making dietary changes not because you want to, but because you understand that they matter. It means asking for help when you need it, whether from family, friends, your healthcare team, or your Jireh Circle.\\n\\nAcceptance is also gradual. You do not wake up one morning and suddenly feel at peace with your diagnosis. It comes in layers, over weeks and months and sometimes years. There will be good days when you feel in control and hopeful, and bad days when you feel resentful and tired. Both are part of the process."},{"id":"s5","title":"Start small and build","body":"Do not try to overhaul your entire life the week after your diagnosis. That approach leads to burnout and frustration. Instead, focus on one thing at a time.\\n\\nFirst, understand your condition. Ask your doctor to explain it in plain language. What is happening in your body? What does the medication do? What happens if you do nothing? Knowledge reduces fear, and fear is one of the biggest obstacles to acceptance.\\n\\nNext, establish your medication routine. This is the foundation everything else builds on. Find a time that works for you, set a reminder, and make it non-negotiable. Once taking your medication becomes automatic, it frees up mental energy for other changes.\\n\\nThen, make one dietary change. Just one. Maybe it is switching from soda to water with meals. Maybe it is adding a serving of vegetables to your dinner. Maybe it is reducing the sugar in your tea from three spoons to one. One change, sustained over weeks, becomes a new normal. Then you add another.\\n\\nFinally, move your body a little. A short walk after dinner, stretching in the morning, taking the stairs. Any movement is better than none, and the mood-boosting effects of even mild exercise can help with the emotional side of your diagnosis."},{"id":"s6","title":"Redefining your story","body":"You are not defined by your condition. A diagnosis is something that happened to you. It is part of your story, but it is not the whole story. You are still a parent, a child, a friend, a professional, a member of your community. You still have things to contribute, goals to pursue, and people who need you.\\n\\nMany people find that managing a chronic condition, while difficult, also brings unexpected clarity. You may find yourself making healthier choices that improve your overall quality of life. You may deepen relationships as you learn to lean on others. You may discover a resilience in yourself that you did not know was there.\\n\\nOne refill at a time. One walk at a time. One meal at a time. One day at a time. That is how a full life with a chronic condition is built, and you are already building it."}]'::jsonb, 6, '{"Develop a positive relationship with your health journey","Find practical ways to adapt your daily routine","Build resilience for the long term"}', E'sunrise'),
  (E'making-the-most-of-your-doctors-appointment', E'Making the most of your doctor''s appointment', E'GENERAL', '{"GENERAL"}', E'SELF_MONITORING', E'Your doctor''s appointment is one of the most valuable tools in managing your health condition. But if you walk in unprepared, you will walk out wishing you had asked more questions.', E'Your doctor''s appointment is one of the most valuable tools in managing your health condition. But if you walk in unprepared, you will walk out wishing you had asked more questions. In Kenya, where clinic wait times can stretch for hours and actual face time with a doctor might be only 10 to 15 minutes, making the most of that brief window is essential. A little preparation can transform a routine visit into a genuinely useful conversation about your health.\n\nPrepare before you go\n\nThe single most important thing you can do before a doctor''s appointment is write things down. Your memory in a clinical setting is unreliable. The stress of being in the hospital, the formality of the interaction, the beeping machines and busy corridors, all of it conspires to push your carefully rehearsed questions right out of your head. A written list solves this problem entirely.\n\nPrepare a list of three things before every visit. First, your top concern, the one thing that is bothering you most or that you most need answered. Maybe it is a new symptom, a side effect of your medication, or anxiety about a test result. Second, any changes since your last visit, including new symptoms, changes in how you feel, changes in your diet or activity level, or any new medications or supplements you started, including herbal remedies. Third, your questions, such as what your test results mean, whether your medication is working, or what you should watch for before the next visit.\n\nWrite this list the night before your appointment when you have time to think clearly. Use a notebook, the notes app on your phone, or even the back of an envelope. The format does not matter. What matters is that it is written down and you bring it with you.\n\nBring your medications\n\nCarry all your current medications with you, ideally the actual containers or packets, not just a list of names. This is important for several reasons. Your doctor can verify that you are taking the correct doses. They can check for interactions if you are seeing multiple specialists or taking supplements. The pharmacist at the hospital can confirm that your refills match what was prescribed. And if you are at a facility where your previous records are not easily accessible, which is common in Kenya, the medication containers are a physical record of your current treatment.\n\nIf carrying the actual medications is impractical, take clear photos of each label with your phone. Make sure the photos show the medication name, dose, and prescribing instructions.\n\nKeep a symptom diary\n\nBetween appointments, keep a brief daily or weekly log of how you are feeling. This does not need to be elaborate. A few words each day are enough. For example: Monday, felt dizzy in the afternoon, blood sugar 8.2 before lunch. Thursday, headache that lasted all day, took paracetamol. Saturday, felt good, walked for 30 minutes.\n\nThis diary gives your doctor real information to work with rather than vague recollections. Patterns that you might not notice become visible in the log. Your doctor might spot that your dizziness always occurs on the same day of the week, or that your blood sugar spikes after certain meals, or that your blood pressure readings are consistently higher in the morning.\n\nIf you are monitoring numbers at home, such as blood sugar readings, blood pressure, or weight, record them consistently. Even if you only check once a week, that is a data point. Bring these records to your appointment.\n\nDuring the appointment\n\nWhen you sit down with the doctor, start by stating your top concern clearly. Do not wait for them to ask the right question. Say something like, "Doctor, the thing I most want to discuss today is the swelling in my ankles that started two weeks ago." This ensures the most important issue gets attention even if time runs short.\n\nPresent your symptom diary or notes. You can simply hand it to the doctor and say, "I have been keeping notes since my last visit." Most doctors appreciate this because it saves time and provides reliable information.\n\nWhen the doctor discusses test results, ask them to explain the key numbers in simple terms. It is your right to understand your own health data. Ask: "What is the normal range? Where do my numbers fall? Are they getting better, worse, or staying the same?" Write the answers down during the appointment. Do not rely on remembering them later.\n\nIf the doctor uses a medical term you do not understand, ask them to explain it. There is no such thing as a stupid question about your own health. A good doctor will welcome your curiosity.\n\nAsk about costs\n\nIn Kenya, where healthcare costs can be a significant burden, it is practical and appropriate to discuss finances with your doctor. If a prescribed medication is expensive, ask if there is an equally effective but more affordable alternative. Many conditions can be managed with generic medications that cost a fraction of brand-name versions. If a test is recommended, ask whether it is urgent or can be scheduled for a time when you have saved enough, especially if it is not covered by SHA.\n\nYour Jireh Wallet and Care Saver can help plan for these expenses. If you know your next appointment will likely include lab work, start setting aside funds in advance.\n\nBefore you leave\n\nBefore walking out of the consultation room, confirm three things with your doctor. First, what should I do next? This might be a new medication, a dietary change, a test to schedule, or simply continuing what you are doing. Second, what warning signs should I watch for before my next visit? This tells you when to come back sooner. Third, when should I come back for my next appointment? Get a specific timeframe: two weeks, one month, three months.\n\nBook your next appointment before leaving the facility if possible. If you leave intending to call later and schedule, life often gets in the way. Walk to the reception desk and secure the date before you head home.\n\nMaking the system work for you\n\nThe Kenyan healthcare system is stretched, and the reality is that you cannot always get as much time as you need with your doctor. But a prepared patient makes the most of whatever time is available. When you walk in with a clear concern, a written record, your medications, and specific questions, you are not just a passive recipient of care. You are an active partner in managing your health. And that partnership produces better outcomes than any amount of time in a waiting room.', E'[{"id":"s1","title":"Part 1","body":"Your doctor''s appointment is one of the most valuable tools in managing your health condition. But if you walk in unprepared, you will walk out wishing you had asked more questions. In Kenya, where clinic wait times can stretch for hours and actual face time with a doctor might be only 10 to 15 minutes, making the most of that brief window is essential. A little preparation can transform a routine visit into a genuinely useful conversation about your health."},{"id":"s2","title":"Prepare before you go","body":"The single most important thing you can do before a doctor''s appointment is write things down. Your memory in a clinical setting is unreliable. The stress of being in the hospital, the formality of the interaction, the beeping machines and busy corridors, all of it conspires to push your carefully rehearsed questions right out of your head. A written list solves this problem entirely.\\n\\nPrepare a list of three things before every visit. First, your top concern, the one thing that is bothering you most or that you most need answered. Maybe it is a new symptom, a side effec